window.WHOLE_FOREST = {"people":[{"after":"there isn't a turning point to be after. she didn't fall and get up. what she did was keep moving, sixty years of trains and ashrams and sitting with whoever came, prime ministers and widows and seekers from france, and treating all of them about the same.\nat the end, in 1982, the accounts say she stopped eating and nearly stopped speaking for months. she died that august. most hindus are cremated. they didn't cremate her. they carried her to kankhal and buried her by the ganges in a shrine, the way hindus bury a saint.","basically":"a village girl from bengal with almost no schooling who went into trances when people sang, and spent sixty years riding trains around india while people everywhere called her mother. a prime minister came to see her and so did widows with nothing, and she treated them about the same. think of the person at a party everybody ends up standing near without knowing why. take from her that joy can be the ground you stand on, even in a life that's run by other people.","born":1896,"died":1982,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 188 116 C 176 124 172 146 166 160 M 212 116 C 224 124 230 146 236 160' stroke-width='4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 200 180 C 176 200 170 230 176 246 L 226 246 C 232 230 226 200 200 180 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='200' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 200 134 L 200 180 M 200 148 L 178 118 M 200 148 L 222 118'/><path d='M 195 125 Q 200 129 205 125'/><path d='M 150 170 C 146 160 152 150 146 140 M 250 170 C 254 160 248 150 254 140' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='70' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 70 192 L 70 234'/><path d='M 70 234 C 50 234 40 250 56 250 L 84 250 C 100 250 90 234 70 234'/><path d='M 70 208 L 56 234 M 70 208 L 84 234'/><rect x='92' y='226' width='50' height='22' rx='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 102 226 L 102 248 M 132 226 L 132 248' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='320' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 320 192 L 320 234'/><path d='M 320 234 C 300 234 290 250 306 250 L 334 250 C 350 250 340 234 320 234'/><path d='M 320 208 L 292 218 M 320 208 L 348 218'/><circle cx='290' cy='210' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='350' cy='210' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 290 196 L 290 190 M 350 196 L 350 190 M 282 200 L 278 196 M 358 200 L 362 196' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(117,205.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a bengali village girl with almost no schooling, married at thirteen, and her husband ended up as her disciple.","key":"anandamayi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamayi_Ma","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7452","why":"autobiography of a yogi, the first edition; chapter 45 is about her"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing ugly i know of. one thing to know. her ashrams kept strict brahmin rules about food, who could cook it and who could eat with whom, and westerners and people of lower caste often ate apart. she didn't change it. put that next to [[nanak]], the founder of the sikhs, whose whole idea was a free kitchen where everybody sits on the same floor and eats the same food.","moments":["the name anandamayi came from her early devotees in dhaka in the twenties. she didn't pick it and didn't use it. she said this body.","yogananda wrote her up as chapter 45 of autobiography of a yogi, his 1946 memoir, and called her the bengali joy-permeated mother. for a lot of americans that chapter was the first they heard of her.","she's photographed laughing more than almost any saint of her century, long hair loose, in plain white."],"name":"anandamayi ma","place":"east bengal, then dhaka, then every train line in india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i was the same as a little girl. i grew into womanhood, and still i was the same.","where":"to yogananda, in his memoir autobiography of a yogi, chapter 45, paraphrased"},{"q":"this body.","where":"what she nearly always called herself, instead of i. it's in every record of her talks"},{"q":"this body has no plans. it goes where the kheyala takes it.","where":"her usual answer about where she'd go next, paraphrased; kheyala is a bengali word for a sudden, unwilled impulse"}],"start":"autobiography of a yogi, chapter 45, for a first look. then alexander lipski's short life and teaching of sri anandamayi ma.","status":"approved","story":"nirmala sundari was born in 1896 in a village in east bengal, now bangladesh. her family were brahmins, the priest caste. caste is the inherited ranking hindu society was built on, which decided what work you did and who you could eat with, and brahmins were at the top of it, though her family had very little money. she had almost no schooling. at about thirteen, as the custom was, she was married to ramani mohan chakrabarti. they never slept together, and the marriage stayed that way for life. her husband ended up taking initiation from her, the ceremony where you formally take someone as your spiritual teacher. her people called him bholanath, and he followed her for the rest of his life.\nin her twenties she went through years of intense spiritual practice with no teacher at all, and in 1922 initiated herself. then came the kirtan, a room of people singing the names of god over and over to drums and cymbals, faster and louder, closer to a gospel revival than a church choir. when it started she'd go into states nobody could reach her in, body rigid, or swaying, or down on the floor. in dhaka, now bangladesh's capital, people started coming to watch, then to stay. they named her anandamayi ma, the mother full of joy.\nshe never wrote anything and never set up a system. for sixty years she traveled, rarely staying anywhere long, and ashrams, communities built around a teacher, went up where she'd been, in varanasi, the holy city on the ganges, in dehradun in the himalayan foothills, and dozens more places. kamala nehru, the wife of india's first prime minister, was devoted to her, and later their daughter indira gandhi, prime minister herself and no relation to mohandas gandhi, came to her for years.\nin 1936 paramahansa yogananda, the indian monk who'd been teaching yoga in america since 1920, came to see her. he asked about her life, and she told him she'd been the same before she was born, the same as a little girl, the same as a wife, and the same now. she died in 1982 near dehradun and is buried at kankhal, next to haridwar, a holy town where the ganges comes down out of the mountains.","struggle":"in her teens and twenties, in her husband's family's house, she'd go stiff and stop answering. the relatives thought she was sick or possessed, and the story goes they called in exorcists. she was a young wife who wouldn't behave like one, in a house that had expected one.\nfor long stretches later on, the accounts say, her hand wouldn't go to her mouth, and attendants fed her. whatever you make of that, it meant she lived her whole adult life in other people's hands.","threads":["joy","wandering","family"],"years":"1896 to 1982"},{"after":"he broke off the engagement and never married. after the garden he went to a friend's country house at cassiciacum, outside milan, with his mother, his son, and a few friends, and spent the months before his baptism talking philosophy and reading the psalms out loud, the old hebrew prayer songs in the middle of the bible. then, a decade later, he wrote it all down, the pears, the woman, the lust, the lies to his mother, in a book addressed to god that anyone could read.\nmonica died at ostia, rome's port, on the way home in 387, and adeodatus died a couple of years later, still a teenager. augustine went back to africa with nothing left of that life and became the bishop who wrote about restlessness better than anyone. when he was dying, says his friend possidius, who wrote the first life of him, he had the penitential psalms copied out and hung on the wall where he could read them from bed, and he wept. those are seven psalms that are all about being sorry.","basically":"a brilliant, horny grad student from north africa who partied through his twenties, had a kid with a woman he never married, then got religion hard and became a bishop. then he wrote the first tell-all memoir in history and put all of it in. if you've ever done something shitty for no reason and lain awake wondering why, he's your guy.","born":354,"died":430,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 100 250 L 100 140'/><ellipse cx='100' cy='120' rx='60' ry='46' fill='var(--sage)'/><g fill='var(--lavender)'><circle cx='72' cy='132' r='5'/><circle cx='128' cy='110' r='5'/><circle cx='92' cy='92' r='5'/><circle cx='140' cy='140' r='5'/><\/g><circle cx='170' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 170 192 L 170 234'/><path d='M 170 234 C 150 234 140 250 156 250 L 184 250 C 200 250 190 234 170 234'/><path d='M 170 208 L 156 234 M 170 208 L 184 234'/><path d='M 214 226 L 270 226 M 220 226 L 220 250 M 264 226 L 264 250'/><path d='M 226 226 L 226 218 C 234 214 240 216 242 220 C 244 216 250 214 258 218 L 258 226' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 242 220 L 242 226'/><path d='M 296 250 L 296 180 L 390 180 L 390 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 205 L 390 205 M 296 228 L 390 228 M 330 180 L 330 205 M 360 205 L 360 228 M 330 228 L 330 250' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='340' cy='168' r='10'/><path d='M 335 171 Q 340 175 345 171'/><path d='M 328 180 L 330 175 M 352 180 L 350 175'/><path d='M 320 156 C 312 150 306 156 298 150 M 320 166 C 312 162 306 168 298 164' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='290' cy='146' r='3' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 293 146 L 293 134'/><g transform='translate(106,128.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he prayed for chastity, but not yet, and then wrote down every bit of it in a book people are still reading sixteen hundred years later.","key":"augustine","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_of_Hippo","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3296","why":"the confessions on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"plainly. in 385 his mother arranged a marriage for him to a girl from a good family who was about two years too young to marry. the woman he'd lived with for fifteen years, the mother of his son, was sent back to africa so she wouldn't be in the way. he says she vowed never to be with another man. he kept the boy. he never tells us her name. all adults, all consensual, and a cold thing to have done, and he knew it.\nlater, as a bishop, he argued that the roman state should use force to bring the donatists back into line. they were a rival christian church in north africa that said priests who'd caved during the last roman persecution weren't real priests anymore. he leaned on a line from a story jesus tells in luke, about a man whose dinner guests don't show, so he sends his servant out to the roads to compel people to come in. augustine read it as permission to force people into the church. later churchmen leaned on his reading for centuries to justify forcing people back into line, and that argument had a long, ugly life after him.","moments":["a bishop told monica, when she begged him to talk sense into her son, that the child of so many tears could not be lost. augustine put it in book 3 of the confessions.","in book 9, at a window in ostia looking out on a garden, he and monica talked about what eternal life might be like and for a moment, he says, they touched it. she was dead within two weeks.","about the woman he sent away, he writes that his heart, where it clung to her, was torn and wounded and bleeding. she's in the book because he loved her."],"name":"augustine of hippo","place":"north africa, then rome and milan, then north africa again","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 34 40 L 38 8 L 60 22 L 82 8 L 86 40 C 70 34 50 34 34 40 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 22 L 60 36 M 53 28 L 67 28' opacity='.7'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.","where":"the confessions, his memoir, book 1, chapter 1, the first paragraph"},{"q":"give me chastity and continence, but not yet.","where":"confessions, book 8, chapter 7, the prayer he says he prayed as a young man. chastity and continence mean no sex"},{"q":"late have i loved you, beauty so ancient and so new.","where":"confessions, book 10, chapter 27"}],"start":"the confessions, books 1 through 9, which are the memoir part; after that it turns into philosophy. book 8 has the garden. a modern translation, like maria boulding's or sarah ruden's.","status":"approved","story":"born in 354 in thagaste, a small town in what's now algeria, back when north africa was part of the roman empire, to a pagan father who kept the old roman gods and a christian mother, monica. brilliant and ambitious, and in his twenties a teacher of rhetoric, which meant public speaking and argument, the skill you needed back then to get anywhere in law or government. think debate coach crossed with speechwriter. at sixteen he and some friends snuck into a neighbor's orchard at night and stole a load of pears. they weren't hungry. the pears weren't even good. they threw most of them to the pigs. what got him, and what he spends pages on thirty years later, is that they did it for no reason except that it was wrong and they were together. it's the first honest look anyone wrote at why we do dumb shit just because. keying a car you don't even hate.\nat around eighteen, in carthage, the big city of roman africa, he took a woman as his concubine, a live-in partner from a lower class, the kind of arrangement an ambitious roman could have without marrying. the confessions, the tell-all memoir he wrote at forty three, never gives her name. they were together about fifteen years and had a son, adeodatus, which means given by god. for nine of those years augustine belonged to the manichees, a religion out of persia that taught the world was a war between a good power of light and an evil power of darkness, and that the body and its appetites belonged to the dark. he says later he liked that it let him believe the bad in him wasn't really him.\nhe moved to rome and then to milan, where he'd landed the job of the city's official teacher of rhetoric, and where the preaching of the bishop, ambrose, one of the most powerful churchmen in the empire, got under his skin. in 386, in a garden in milan, weeping under a fig tree, he heard a child's voice from the next house chanting take up and read, take up and read. he picked up the letters of [[paul]], the early christian whose letters make up a big part of the new testament, opened them at random, and read two lines from chapter 13 of the letter to the romans telling him to quit the partying and the sleeping around. that was it. he was done fighting. ambrose baptized him and adeodatus at easter 387, the ceremony that made them christians.\nhe went home to africa, was made a priest in hippo, a port town in what's now algeria, in 391, more or less against his will, and bishop a few years later. he wrote the confessions around 397, then the city of god, a huge book written after the goths sacked rome in 410, arguing that christians didn't need rome to last. then a mountain of everything else. he's shaped how the west thinks about sin and sex more than almost anyone after paul. he died in 430 with the vandals, a germanic tribe that had crossed over from spain, besieging the city.","struggle":"book 8 of the confessions is a man at war with his own will. he says he wanted to change and didn't want to, both at once, and that the chains were his own habits. he'd been praying give me chastity, but not yet, for years, meaning give me the strength to stop sleeping around, and meaning the second half.\nafter his mother got him engaged and the woman he lived with was sent away, which is down in the mess, he got worse, not better. he took another mistress while he waited for his fiancée to come of age. he writes that the wound from losing the first one didn't heal. it festered, and the pain went colder and more desperate.","threads":["the body","love","family"],"years":"354 to 430"},{"after":"he didn't get bitter and he didn't go quiet. he started a podcast, wrote books nobody in evangelicalism would touch, did a speaking tour with oprah winfrey, did shows with comedians, and kept saying the same thing he'd said in grand rapids, just without the sound system.\nthe 2018 documentary about him is called the heretic and he's fine with it. he says he's more interested in what's true than in who's allowed to say it.","basically":"an evangelical pastor from michigan who built one of the hottest churches in america before he was thirty five, then wrote a book asking whether god really sends most people to burn forever. the answer he gave, probably not, got him run out of the whole world he grew up in. now he lives in los angeles, does a podcast, and tours with comedians. if you were raised on hell and quietly stopped buying it, he's the guy who said it out loud from the stage and paid for it.","born":1970,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><ellipse cx='150' cy='232' rx='90' ry='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 80 240 L 70 270 M 220 240 L 230 270 M 150 246 L 150 270'/><path d='M 150 232 C 140 232 130 226 150 220 C 170 226 160 232 150 232' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='150' cy='80' r='14'/><path d='M 150 94 L 150 142'/><path d='M 150 142 L 136 176 M 150 142 L 164 176'/><path d='M 150 108 L 130 84 M 150 108 L 170 84'/><path d='M 145 85 Q 150 89 155 85'/><path d='M 150 176 L 150 216' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 290 270 L 290 170 L 380 170 L 380 270' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 195 L 380 195 M 290 220 L 380 220 M 290 245 L 380 245 M 335 170 L 335 195 M 312 195 L 312 220 M 358 195 L 358 220 M 335 220 L 335 245 M 312 245 L 312 270 M 358 245 L 358 270'/><rect x='336' y='221' width='21' height='23' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(352,143.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a megachurch pastor who wrote that hell might not be the point, and got told farewell by the whole industry.","key":"bell","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rob_Bell","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nooma","why":"the short films that made him famous in youth groups"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing gross i know of. the fights were all about doctrine. some people who worked for him at mars hill felt left behind when he walked out, and he's talked about that.","moments":["the first nooma film, rain, from 2002, is him carrying his crying son home in a storm and saying that's what god is like. it's ten minutes long and it wrecked a generation of youth groups.","john piper's farewell tweet came before the book was even in stores. bell's response was basically to shrug and keep the tour dates.","his book what is the bible? says the bible is a library of arguing voices and the argument is the point. it's the most useful thing anybody in a church has written for people who love the book and can't stand the rulebook."],"name":"rob bell","place":"michigan, then california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"a brick wall is only as strong as its weakest brick. a trampoline gets stronger the more you jump.","where":"velvet elvis, his first book, paraphrased"},{"q":"the bible isn't one book, it's a library, and the books argue with each other. that's the point.","where":"what is the bible?, his 2017 book, paraphrased"},{"q":"love wins. that's it. that's the whole title.","where":"love wins, 2011, the book that got him thrown out"}],"start":"velvet elvis. then love wins. then his podcast, the robcast, which is where he's loosest.","status":"approved","story":"he built mars hill bible church in grand rapids, michigan into ten thousand people a week by his early thirties. a megachurch is exactly that, a church the size of an arena, with a band and big screens. he also made short films called nooma, ten minute videos of him walking around talking about god, and half the youth groups in america watched them. for about five years he was the future of evangelicalism, the born again, bible first side of american protestant christianity, the side with the megachurches and the christian radio.\nvelvet elvis, his first book, from 2005, has the trampoline. he said faith should be a trampoline, springs you can pull out and look at and bounce on, not a brick wall where if you pull one brick the whole thing comes down. to the people running evangelicalism, questioning the bricks was already the problem. that book was the warning shot.\nlove wins, from 2011, asked whether a loving god sends billions of people to hell, conscious eternal torment, awake and burning with no end, and answered, probably not. to evangelicals that isn't a side question. it's the floor. before the book was even in stores, john piper, one of the most powerful pastors in that world, tweeted three words: farewell rob bell. that was the excommunication, the old word for a church formally throwing you out. this one was a tweet, and it worked. he left mars hill the next year.\nhe's been in los angeles since, doing a podcast, touring with comedians, writing about the bible as a library of arguing voices instead of a rulebook. he's the easiest christian on these pages to love because he never stopped being funny about it.","struggle":"after love wins he was forty one with young kids, no church, no denomination to fall back on the way most pastors have, and half the people who'd loved him calling him a heretic on the internet, meaning somebody teaching what the church calls false. he's talked about the weirdness of walking away from ten thousand people who'd shown up to hear you every week.\nhe moved to los angeles and, by his own account, spent a lot of time surfing badly and figuring out who he was without a building.","threads":["doubt","fame"],"years":"born 1970"},{"after":"after wounded knee he went on living on the reservation, raising a family, healing people. after he became a catholic he gave himself to it for about forty years. he also kept the old stories, and when neihardt and later joseph epes brown, a young white scholar, came asking, he told them.\nafter black elk speaks came out, he signed a statement in 1934 saying he was a catholic and regretted the book leaving that out. scholars argue about how much of that statement was his and how much the priests'. some of his descendants have said he held both and didn't feel he had to choose. that's the most honest reading anyone's come up with.","basically":"a lakota holy man who had a huge vision as a sick nine year old, was a boy in the fight where custer's men were wiped out, toured europe with buffalo bill's wild west show, rode into the aftermath of the wounded knee massacre, and then spent decades as a catholic teacher without ever dropping the old ways. the famous book about him is partly ghostwritten, by the white poet who interviewed him. take from him that you can carry two faiths at once and not owe anyone a choice between them.","born":1863,"died":1950,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 280 L 150 180 L 280 280 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='150' cy='84' r='14'/><path d='M 150 98 L 150 146'/><path d='M 150 146 L 136 180 M 150 146 L 164 180'/><path d='M 150 112 L 130 88 M 150 112 L 170 88'/><path d='M 125 44 Q 125 26 142 28 Q 151 10 169 22 Q 183 14 187 32 Q 195 42 183 44 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 140 50 L 136 62 M 156 50 L 152 64 M 172 50 L 168 62' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 20 280 L 380 280'/><path d='M 310 280 L 310 240 L 350 240 L 350 280' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 242 L 330 222 L 356 242'/><path d='M 330 222 L 330 206 M 324 212 L 336 212'/><path d='M 324 280 L 324 262 L 336 262 L 336 280' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 204 236 C 206 224 222 224 226 236 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(208,210.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he had a great vision at nine, fought at the little bighorn at twelve, and spent the last half of his life as a catholic teacher, and he never said he'd given up either.","key":"blackelk","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Elk","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Elk_Speaks","why":"the famous book, and the argument about how much of it is his"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["in 1931 he had neihardt take him up harney peak, the highest mountain in the black hills, the lakota's holy ground, where in the vision he'd stood at the center of the world. he was old and nearly blind, and he prayed for his people. neihardt wrote that a little rain fell out of a nearly clear sky. the mountain has been called black elk peak since 2016.","in 1947 he told the lakota rites to joseph epes brown, the seven sacred ceremonies, the sweat lodge and the vision quest among them, and the sun dance, the big summer ceremony of fasting and dancing around a sacred tree, and that became the sacred pipe, in 1953. it's the other book, and it's closer to his own voice."],"name":"black elk","place":"the northern plains, then the pine ridge reservation in south dakota","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 60 100 L 60 118 M 54 106 L 66 106' stroke-width='2.5'/><path d='M 48 96 C 50 100 56 100 60 100 C 64 100 70 100 72 96' stroke-dasharray='0.1 4' stroke-width='3'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"anywhere is the center of the world.","where":"black elk speaks, the chapter on the great vision; the english is neihardt's"},{"q":"it is the story of all life that is holy and good to tell.","where":"black elk speaks, the opening; neihardt's english, from what black elk told him"},{"q":"the nation's hoop is broken and scattered.","where":"black elk speaks, the last pages. the scholar raymond demallie showed these lines are neihardt's, not in the interview notes"}],"start":"black elk speaks, the chapter on the great vision, read knowing neihardt's hand is on it. then raymond demallie's the sixth grandfather, which prints the interview notes. then damian costello's black elk: colonialism and lakota catholicism.","status":"approved","story":"heȟáka sápa, black elk, was born in 1863, by his own count, into the oglala, one of the seven bands of the lakota, the plains nation white americans called the sioux. crazy horse, the war leader, was his second cousin. at nine he fell sick for days, and while he lay there he had the great vision. in it he was taken up to the six grandfathers, the powers of the four directions and the sky and the earth, and shown the sacred hoop of his people, their life as one nation, with a flowering tree at its center. he told almost no one for years.\nat twelve he was at the battle of the little bighorn in june 1876, where the lakota and cheyenne wiped out custer's command, the fight white americans called custer's last stand. then the buffalo were gone and the people were on reservations. in 1886 he joined buffalo bill's wild west, a traveling show of cowboys, sharpshooters and real indians acting out battles, to see the white world for himself, and in 1887 performed in london for queen victoria, whom the lakota called grandmother england. he got separated from the show, traveled on through europe, and made it home in 1889.\non december 29, 1890, at wounded knee creek in south dakota, the us army opened fire on a band of lakota and killed hundreds, many of them women and children. black elk rode there as the killing was happening and was wounded in the fighting that followed. he'd been healing people since his late teens and went back to it. in 1904 he was baptized a catholic, took the name nicholas, and became a catechist, a lay teacher who travels around teaching the faith, going from reservation to reservation, and he did that for decades.\nin 1931 john neihardt, nebraska's poet laureate, came to pine ridge and black elk told him his life and his vision, through his son ben as interpreter. black elk speaks came out in 1932. neihardt shaped it and in places rewrote it, including its most famous lines, and he left out the catholic half of black elk's life. black elk died in 1950. in 2017 the catholic church opened his cause for sainthood, the first step toward declaring him a saint.","struggle":"the vision sat on him for years. he was told it was for his people and he didn't know how to give it to them, and he said later he'd been afraid of it, and sick, until he acted part of it out in his late teens in the horse dance, a ceremony where the vision was staged in front of the whole camp with riders on horses.\nand then everything the vision was about went under. the buffalo gone, the land taken, the people starving on the reservation, and wounded knee in december 1890. he was twenty seven.","threads":["the land","war","doubt"],"years":"1863 to 1950"},{"after":"when the german church folded to hitler, he didn't write essays about it. in 1935 he started an illegal seminary, a school for training pastors that the nazi-approved church didn't allow, at finkenwalde, a village in what's now poland, and trained young pastors in a big old house until the gestapo, hitler's secret police, shut it down in 1937. life together is the book about that house.\nwhen the july 1944 bomb plot against hitler failed and the gestapo found papers tying him to the conspiracy, it was over. he'd already been in a cell for more than a year and he spent the rest of his time there writing letters that are more alive than most people's freedom. the camp doctor who watched him hanged said he'd never seen a man die so submissive to the will of god. he was thirty nine.","basically":"a german pastor in the 1930s who saw what hitler was the minute he took over and said so, while most of the churches in germany went along. he was safe in new york, went home on purpose, joined the plot to kill hitler, and was hanged a month before the war in europe ended. when people ask what they'd have done in 1933, he's the answer nobody wants to live. if you've ever had to choose between keeping your hands clean and doing what's needed, he chose, and he never pretended it was clean.","born":1906,"died":1945,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 L 90 200 L 90 230 L 20 230' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 200 L 30 170 L 50 170 L 50 200 M 60 200 L 60 150 L 78 150 L 78 200' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 250 C 60 240 100 260 140 250 S 220 240 260 250 S 340 260 380 250'/><path d='M 150 236 L 170 262 L 330 262 L 350 236 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 236 L 250 180'/><path d='M 250 186 L 300 210 L 250 210 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 290 236 L 290 205 L 320 205 L 320 236' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 305 205 L 305 190'/><path d='M 305 182 C 312 170 320 175 328 168' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 212'/><path d='M 200 212 L 186 246 M 200 212 L 214 246'/><path d='M 200 178 L 214 202 M 200 178 L 174 172'/><path d='M 110 140 C 130 130 150 130 165 138' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(150,209.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was safe in new york in 1939 and went back to nazi germany on one of the last boats.","key":"bonhoeffer","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dietrich_Bonhoeffer","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cost_of_Discipleship","why":"the book with cheap grace in it"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of. he was engaged to a woman eighteen years younger, maria von wedemeyer, and they barely got to see each other before his arrest. the mess in his story is the country's, not his.","moments":["in 1931 he took a confirmation class, the class where teenagers learn the faith before they join the church, of fifty rough kids in wedding, a working class berlin district, and moved into the neighborhood to be near them. the older pastors had given up on the class.","his twin sister sabine married gerhard leibholz, a law professor the nazis counted as jewish, and in 1938 bonhoeffer helped them drive across the border to switzerland at night. it was the first illegal thing he did.","he brought recordings of black spirituals, the songs enslaved people made in america, back from harlem in 1931 and played them for his german students, who'd never heard anything like it."],"name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","place":"berlin, harlem, berlin, prison","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"cheap grace is the grace we give ourselves.","where":"the cost of discipleship, chapter 1"},{"q":"i will have no right to participate in the reconstruction of christian life in germany after the war if i do not share the trials of this time with my people.","where":"a letter to reinhold niebuhr, the american theologian, 1939"},{"q":"who am i? they mock me, these lonely questions of mine. whoever i am, thou knowest, o god, i am thine.","where":"letters and papers from prison, the poem who am i, paraphrased"}],"start":"the cost of discipleship, the first chapter, on cheap grace. then letters and papers from prison, the poems especially. then life together, which is short, about the little underground school for pastors he ran.","status":"approved","story":"a german pastor and theologian, meaning someone who studies and writes about god for a living, brilliant early, with a doctorate at twenty one. in 1930 he went to new york to study at union seminary, a big school for training ministers, and ended up spending his sundays in harlem at abyssinian baptist church, one of the biggest black churches in america, teaching sunday school. the black church's music and grief got into him in a way the lecture halls never did.\nwhen hitler took power in 1933 he was one of the first pastors to say out loud that the church had to oppose him. most of the german protestant church went along. a breakaway called the confessing church didn't, and he was in it. the cost of discipleship, from 1937, his book about what it actually costs to follow [[jesus]], has the line everyone quotes. cheap grace, he says, is forgiveness you hand yourself without changing anything, grace without the cross, meaning without any of the price jesus paid, and it's the deadly enemy of the church. it's the apology with nothing behind it.\nfriends got him a job in new york in june 1939 to keep him safe. he lasted a few weeks. he wrote to reinhold niebuhr, the best known american theologian of the day, that he had no right to help rebuild christian life in germany after the war if he didn't go through the trials of that time with his people. he sailed back in july. he joined the conspiracy to kill hitler, a circle of officers inside german military intelligence who tried more than once, was arrested in april 1943, and was hanged at flossenbürg, a nazi concentration camp, on april 9, 1945, two weeks before the americans got there.\nthe letters he wrote from prison, mostly to his best friend eberhard bethge and to his family, came out after the war as letters and papers from prison, and they're the best thing he did. he was thirty nine.","struggle":"in prison he wrote a poem called who am i. the guards saw a calm, cheerful, confident man. inside, he wrote, he was restless and longing and sick, like a bird in a cage, struggling for breath. he asked which one was true and answered, whoever i am, you know me.\nthe deeper trouble was joining a plot to kill a man. he'd been a pacifist, someone who won't use violence at all, and had wanted to go study with gandhi, the indian leader who fought the british without guns. he decided that if a madman was driving a car into a crowd, the job wasn't to bandage the victims, it was to wrestle the wheel away. he never called it innocent. he accepted the guilt and did it anyway.","threads":["prison","war","dying"],"years":"1906 to 1945"},{"after":"his after is what he did with other people's messes. a serial killer named angulimala, whose name means finger necklace because he wore one made from his victims' fingers, came at him on a road. the buddha kept walking calmly and the killer ran and couldn't catch him, and finally yelled stop. the buddha said i've stopped, you haven't. angulimala became a monk. villagers threw rocks at him for years and he'd come back bleeding. the buddha told him to bear it, that this was what he'd done coming back to him. he did.\nhis cousin devadatta tried to kill him three times, once by setting a drunk elephant loose on him, and split his community of monks. his own clan, the shakyas, was massacred by a neighboring king in his lifetime. he taught for forty five years through all of it and died of a bad meal, telling his cousin and attendant ananda to make sure nobody blamed the man who'd cooked it.","basically":"the son of a rich ruling family in what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago, who had everything and walked out on it at twenty nine to figure out why everybody suffers. six years later, sitting under a tree, he figured it out, and spent the next forty five years walking around northern india teaching it. he didn't ask anyone to believe him. he told them to try it and see, which is why so much of it sits comfortably in a therapist's office today. if you want a method more than a belief, start here.","born":-480,"died":-400,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 120 250 L 120 150'/><ellipse cx='120' cy='126' rx='70' ry='53' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='120' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 120 184 L 120 226'/><path d='M 120 226 C 100 226 90 242 106 242 L 134 242 C 150 242 140 226 120 226'/><path d='M 120 200 L 106 226 M 120 200 L 134 226'/><path d='M 115 175 Q 120 179 125 175'/><path d='M 200 250 L 220 200 M 214 202 L 222 198 L 224 208' /><path d='M 300 250 C 300 236 340 236 340 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 110 C 260 100 280 110 290 100' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(150,121.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a rich kid who saw a sick man, an old man, and a corpse, and couldn't unsee them.","key":"buddha","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Buddha","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the dhammapada, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/kn/dhp/index.html","why":"his sayings in verse, in english"},{"label":"the kalama sutta, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/an/an03/an03.065.than.html","why":"the test-it-yourself talk, in english"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"he left his wife, yasodhara, and a newborn son in the middle of the night without saying goodbye. the tradition frames it as necessary. his wife might have had a different word for it. years later the son, rahula, joined his order as a boy. that's the mess and it's a real one.","moments":["his last words were roughly: all things that come together fall apart. work it out with care. then he lay down on his right side between two trees and stopped.","he refused to answer whether the universe goes on forever or whether a self survives death, and said so, because the answer didn't help anyone stop hurting. the poisoned arrow story is his answer.","kushinagar, where he died, is where mingyur rinpoche, the tibetan monk on these pages, got food poisoning on his years as a wandering beggar and nearly died, twenty five centuries later. mingyur knew exactly where he was lying."],"name":"the buddha","place":"northern india and nepal","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 50 34 C 50 22 70 22 70 34 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 33 58 C 30 66 32 76 36 74 M 87 58 C 90 66 88 76 84 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"don't believe it because it's tradition, or scripture, or because i said it. test it in your own life.","where":"the kalama sutta, his talk to the confused villagers, paraphrased. it's in the anguttara nikaya, the numbered talks, book 3, number 65, part of the pali canon, the oldest full set of his talks, kept in an old indian language called pali"},{"q":"a man shot with an arrow who won't be treated until he knows who shot it will die with the arrow in him.","where":"the shorter malunkya sutta, the talk to the monk who wanted answers first, in the majjhima nikaya, the middle-length talks, number 63, paraphrased"},{"q":"my teaching is a raft for crossing the river. you don't carry the raft on your head afterward.","where":"the alagaddupama sutta, the talk about grabbing a snake by the wrong end, majjhima nikaya 22, paraphrased"}],"start":"the dhammapada, a short book of his sayings in verse, the one most people start with. then the kalama sutta and the sona sutta. that one's about a monk named sona who pushed so hard at walking meditation that his feet bled, and the buddha asked him, you used to play the lute, didn't you? when the strings were too tight, did it sound right? too loose? practice is the same. in the buddha's words, a single book by bhikkhu bodhi, an american monk who's spent his life translating the talks, is the best collection.","status":"approved","story":"siddhartha gautama was a prince, or at least a rich man's son, in the foothills of the himalayas, and his father tried to keep him from ever seeing anything sad. it didn't work. out in the town he saw a sick man, then an old man, then a corpse being carried off, and then a wandering monk, a holy man who'd given up everything and lived on handouts, who looked at peace. at twenty nine he left his wife and newborn son in the night to find out why we suffer and whether it can stop.\nhe tried six years of starving himself, the way the holy men of his day did, and got nowhere but thin. then he ate a bowl of rice, sat down under a fig tree, and decided not to get up until he understood. the story says he woke up that night, which is what buddha means, the one who woke up. what he found is called the four noble truths. life hurts. the hurt comes from craving, from grabbing for things to be other than they are. the craving can stop. and here's how. then he taught for forty five years.\nhe didn't ask to be believed. his talks were memorized by his monks and written down centuries later, and each one is called a sutta. in one, the kalama sutta, the kalamas, the people of a town called kesaputta, tell him that every teacher who passes through trashes all the others, so how are they supposed to know who's right. he tells them not to take anything on tradition, or scripture, or because a teacher said so, including him, but to test it in their own lives and keep what actually makes them better. another time a monk demanded answers about the afterlife and the edges of the universe before he'd practice, and the buddha said he was like a man shot with a poisoned arrow refusing treatment until he knew who shot it and what the bow was made of.\nhe died at about eighty at kushinagar, a small town in northern india, after a meal a blacksmith named cunda served him made him sick. [[mingyur|mingyur rinpoche]] nearly died of food poisoning in the same place twenty five centuries later. his last words were roughly: everything falls apart. keep going.","struggle":"six years of starving himself, down to a spine you could feel through his stomach, and it got him nothing. then he ate a bowl of rice, and the five holy men who'd been starving alongside him walked off in disgust, sure he'd gone soft.\nunder the tree, the story goes, everything came at him. mara, the demon in buddhist stories who stands for everything that keeps you stuck, threw fear at him, then desire, then doubt, his whole army. the last thing was a voice asking who he thought he was to sit there. he reached down and touched the ground. the earth was his witness. that's the answer to the doubt. no argument, just, i'm here.\nand after he woke up, he almost didn't teach. he thought nobody would get it. the story has a god named brahma come down and beg him to try, and he looked out at people like lotus flowers at different heights in a pond and figured a few had their heads above the water.","threads":["family","dying","wandering"],"years":"fifth century bc, give or take"},{"after":"after the surrender he spent twenty seven years asking to go home. in 1879 he went to washington and met president rutherford hayes and gave a speech to a big crowd of congressmen and officials. he kept writing and petitioning and talking to anyone who'd listen, and the answer was always no.\nhe never got back to the wallowa. white settlers were farming it. he died at colville, and the doctor there said he died of a broken heart. i can't improve on that.","basically":"a nez perce leader from a green valley in oregon who, in 1877, when the government ordered his band off their land, ended up leading about seven hundred and fifty men, women and kids on a fighting retreat of more than a thousand miles toward canada, beating the army again and again, and gave up forty miles from the border because the children were freezing. his surrender line is one of the most quoted sentences in american history. then the government broke every promise it made him, and he spent the rest of his life asking to go home. think of a refugee column with a leader who became famous for saying he was done. take from him that saying enough, out loud, for the sake of the people behind you, is its own kind of courage.","born":1840,"died":1904,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 220 L 70 150 L 130 200 L 210 120 L 290 200 L 340 160 L 400 210 L 400 300 L 0 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 220 C 250 196 290 196 290 220' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='150' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 150 164 L 150 212'/><path d='M 150 212 L 136 246 M 150 212 L 164 246'/><path d='M 150 180 L 172 190 M 150 176 L 172 180'/><path d='M 140 146 C 138 136 162 136 160 146' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 160 176 L 214 180' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 318 250 L 340 210 L 362 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 334 200 L 340 210 L 346 200'/><path d='M 332 250 L 340 226 L 348 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='60' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='110' cy='40' r='2'/><circle cx='360' cy='70' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(70,135.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he led his people eleven hundred miles away from the army and surrendered forty miles short of canada.","key":"chiefjoseph","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Joseph","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the national park","url":"https://www.nps.gov/nepe/index.htm","why":"nez perce national historical park, with the sites of the 1877 flight"},{"label":"the nez perce tribe","url":"https://nezperce.org/","why":"the nation's own site"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["about two hundred and thirty of his people, his own daughter among them, slipped through the army lines the night of the surrender and made it to sitting bull's camp in canada.","the historian mark brown wrote that the famous picture of joseph handing over his rifle with that little speech doesn't have a word of truth in it. the last line is the part everybody keeps anyway."],"name":"chief joseph","place":"the wallowa valley in northeastern oregon, then the long flight through idaho, wyoming and montana, then kansas, indian territory, and the colville reservation in washington","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 48 38 C 46 20 74 20 72 38 C 66 30 54 30 48 38 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"from where the sun now stands, i will fight no more forever.","where":"the last line of his surrender speech, october 5, 1877, as written down by lieutenant charles erskine scott wood"},{"q":"never sell the bones of your father and your mother.","where":"his father's dying words to him, as he told it"},{"q":"let me be a free man, free to travel, free to stop, free to work, free to think and talk and act for myself.","where":"an indian's view of indian affairs, his account published in the north american review, 1879, paraphrased"}],"start":"chief joseph's own account, an indian's view of indian affairs, from 1879. then kent nerburn's chief joseph and the flight of the nez perce.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on march 3, 1840, in the wallowa valley of northeastern oregon. his name was hin-mah-too-yah-lat-kekt, thunder rolling down the mountain. he was nez perce, a name french traders gave them. their own name is nimíipuu, the people. his father, old joseph, refused to sign an 1863 treaty that gave the wallowa away, and dying, told his son never to sell the bones of his father and mother.\nin 1877 the government ordered the band onto a small reservation in idaho, fast. while they were moving, some young men, angry over old killings, killed white settlers, and the army came. on june 17, 1877, at white bird canyon in idaho, the nez perce beat the cavalry badly. then they ran.\nabout seven hundred and fifty people, most of them not fighters, went more than eleven hundred miles over the mountains through idaho, wyoming and montana, through the new yellowstone national park, heading for canada, where [[sittingbull|sitting bull]] and his lakota had found refuge. they beat or outran several armies. historians now say joseph was more the camp's leader than its war chief, looking after the old people and the children while others led the fighting.\nat the bear paw mountains in montana, about forty miles from canada, the army caught them. after days of fighting in the cold, on october 5, 1877, joseph surrendered to colonel nelson miles. an officer named charles erskine scott wood wrote down the speech, and historians argue over how much of it is joseph's words and how much is wood's. it ends, i will fight no more forever. they promised him his people could go back to idaho. instead they were shipped to kansas and then to indian territory, now oklahoma, where many died. in 1885 he was sent to the colville reservation in washington, never back to the wallowa. he died there on september 21, 1904.","struggle":"the flight itself. four months running with families, old people and babies, through mountains, burying people along the way, with armies coming from every side. by the bear paw it was snowing, they'd lost their horses, and people were freezing in pits they'd dug in the ground.\nthen the exile. in indian territory, a hot, low country nothing like their mountains, his people got sick and died, the children especially. he'd surrendered to save them, and then had to watch them die anyway, in a place he'd been lied into.","threads":["exile","war","grief","the land"],"years":"1840 to 1904"},{"after":"the after is the tour. seventy three and robbed, he sued, won a judgment for millions he never collected, and went back to work. he rehearsed the band for months, walked out in a suit and a fedora, knelt on the stage for the songs he loved, and thanked the audience every night for coming out.\nand at the end he wasn't dodging it. in the summer of 2016 marianne ihlen was dying in norway, and he wrote to her, roughly, that he was right behind her, close enough to take her hand. he died that november. the last album is a man in his eighties telling god he's ready.","basically":"a jewish poet from montreal who became one of the great songwriters, the one who wrote hallelujah, and who spent his whole life circling god and fighting a heavy depression, with a lot of women and wine along the way. in his sixties he lived five years in a zen monastery on a mountain above los angeles, came down to find his manager had taken most of his money, and went back on tour at seventy three and found a bigger audience than he'd ever had. he's for anyone who's tried everything on the dark and is still standing there in a good suit, making something out of it.","born":1934,"died":2016,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 130 80 L 190 160 L 240 110 L 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 250 250 L 250 206 L 330 206 L 330 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 242 208 L 290 180 L 338 208'/><path d='M 280 250 L 280 222 L 300 222 L 300 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 316 210 L 316 213'/><path d='M 309 224 C 309 216 311 213 316 213 C 321 213 323 216 323 224 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 302 226 C 310 222 322 222 330 226' stroke-width='3.5'/><path d='M 238 222 L 250 184'/><circle cx='236' cy='227' r='7' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='232' cy='240' r='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='233' cy='236' r='2.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='170' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 170 192 L 170 234'/><path d='M 170 234 C 150 234 140 250 156 250 L 184 250 C 200 250 190 234 170 234'/><path d='M 170 208 L 156 234 M 170 208 L 184 234'/><path d='M 162 176 C 164 178 166 178 168 176 M 172 176 C 174 178 176 178 178 176'/><g transform='translate(281,160.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he spent five years as a zen monk, came down to find his manager had taken his money, and went back on the road at seventy three.","key":"cohen","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_Cohen","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his official site","url":"https://www.leonardcohen.com/","why":"the official leonard cohen site"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You_Want_It_Darker","why":"his last album"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"sloppy, all adults. a lot of women, openly, over decades. a lot of drink, and on tour in the nineties he said he was drinking three bottles of wine before a show. a lot of drugs, acid and speed on hydra and pills later. he never married, and his kids, adam and lorca, grew up with a father who was often somewhere else. nothing in the other category.","moments":["hallelujah took him years and, he said, dozens of verses. his record company wouldn't release the album it was on in the united states in 1984. john cale, from the velvet underground, recorded it, then jeff buckley, then it turned up in shrek, the cartoon about an ogre, and then everyone sang it.","the house on hydra cost him about fifteen hundred dollars in 1960, money his grandmother had left him.","accepting a prize in spain in 2011 he told a story about a young spanish guitarist in montreal who taught him a six chord flamenco progression over a few lessons and then didn't come back, because he'd taken his own life. cohen said all his songs were built on those chords."],"name":"leonard cohen","place":"montreal, hydra, new york, a mountain above los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 36 43 C 36 22 84 22 84 43 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 26 44 C 40 40 80 40 94 44 C 84 48 36 48 26 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 37 38 L 83 38' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='3'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"there is a crack in everything.","where":"the song anthem, 1992, just the start of the line; the rest is about how the light gets in"},{"q":"i'm not looking for a new religion, i'm happy with the old one.","where":"interviews in the nineties about mount baldy, paraphrased"},{"q":"hineni, here i am.","where":"you want it darker, the title song of his last album, 2016; hineni is the hebrew word abraham answers god with in the bible, and he sang it with the choir of the montreal synagogue he grew up in"}],"start":"book of longing, the poems and drawings from the mountain years. then the album you want it darker, start to finish. then the favourite game, his first novel, if you want him young.","status":"approved","story":"born in montreal in 1934 into a prominent jewish family in westmount, the well-off english-speaking part of the city. his mother's father was a rabbi. his father died when he was nine, and he said he cut open one of his father's bow ties, put a note inside, and buried it in the garden, and that was the first thing he ever wrote.\nhe was a poet first. let us compare mythologies, his first book of poems, in 1956, then novels, then a house on hydra, a small greek island with no cars, in 1960, where he lived with marianne ihlen, the norwegian woman the song so long, marianne is about, and wrote. in his early thirties, broke, he decided to try songs. the folk singer judy collins recorded his suzanne, and his first album came out in 1967.\nhe knew his zen teacher, joshu sasaki, for decades before moving up to the mount baldy zen center, a bare-bones monastery high in the mountains above los angeles, in the mid nineties. zen is the japanese school of buddhism that's mostly sitting still and paying attention. he cooked for the old man, sat in the cold, and in 1996 was ordained a monk under the name jikan, usually given as the silent one. he said he wasn't looking for a new religion. he was a jew who sat.\nhe came down in 1999. in 2004 he found out his manager had taken most of his retirement money, millions, while he was up on the mountain. in 2008, at seventy three, he went back on tour, played hundreds of shows over the next five years, and came out the other side with his money back and a new audience. his last album, you want it darker, came out in october 2016. he died seventeen days later.","struggle":"depression, for most of his life, the heavy kind, the background of everything. he tried drink, drugs, women, fame, zen, and prayer on it. he said in interviews that it finally started to lift in his sixties, and he wasn't sure which thing did it, or whether it just wore out.\nand the mountain wasn't peaceful. he got up before dawn to sit in the cold and told interviewers he was bad at it. after he came down he went to mumbai in 1999 to study with ramesh balsekar, a retired indian banker who taught that there's no separate self doing anything, still looking. and his own teacher on the mountain, sasaki, was accused starting in 2012 by many former students of sexual misconduct going back decades. sasaki died in 2014. as far as i know cohen never spoke about it publicly.","threads":["madness","money","silence","dying"],"years":"1934 to 2016"},{"after":"after the shooting he healed, with a scar by his nose for the rest of his life. black buffalo woman went back to no water. crazy horse didn't try to get the title back. he stopped sitting in the councils and went back to being what he was good at, a fighter who took nothing for himself. a year later he married black shawl.\nafter his daughter died he kept fighting, and he kept to himself more than before. there's no redemption arc. there's a man who got it wrong once, publicly, took the loss, and went on being useful to his people until they killed him for it.","basically":"the oglala lakota war leader of the 1870s, quiet, strange, and so brave the men who rode with him talked about it for the rest of their lives. he never signed a treaty, never went to washington, and never let anyone make him into a picture. he also ran off with another man's wife and got shot in the face for it. picture the guy in your unit who never talked about himself and who everybody would have followed anywhere. take from him that you don't have to explain yourself to be trusted.","born":1840,"died":1877,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 80 250 L 100 180 L 120 250 M 100 180 L 100 250'/><rect x='82' y='150' width='40' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='122' cy='165' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 150 C 70 160 70 180 76 192' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='250' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 250 164 L 250 210'/><path d='M 250 210 L 234 246 M 250 210 L 268 242'/><path d='M 250 178 L 236 200 M 250 178 L 270 186'/><path d='M 256 144 L 266 124' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 280 200 C 280 186 340 184 346 198 L 360 172 L 372 178 L 356 202 C 350 212 284 214 280 200 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 288 208 L 286 250 M 300 210 L 302 250 M 338 210 L 336 250 M 348 206 L 350 250'/><path d='M 280 200 C 270 204 268 216 272 224'/><path d='M 270 186 C 290 180 300 184 330 192' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(102,133.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"nobody ever got a confirmed photograph of him, and he's the one they're carving into a mountain.","key":"crazyhorse","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crazy_Horse","why":"the plain overview, including what's legend and what isn't"},{"label":"the battlefield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/libi/index.htm","why":"the national park service site at the little bighorn"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"around 1870 a woman named black buffalo woman, whom he'd loved since they were young, left her husband, a man named no water, and rode off with crazy horse on a war party. no water came after them, found them in a lodge, and shot crazy horse in the face from close range. the bullet broke his upper jaw. it nearly set off a feud between whole bands, and older men had to settle it with gifts of horses.\nit cost him. he'd been made a shirt wearer, one of four men chosen to keep the peace in the camp and put the people ahead of themselves, and they took it away from him for this. he'd put himself first, over a married woman, and everybody knew it. adults being stupid about love, and it had a price.","moments":["the story goes that when someone wanted to take his photograph, he asked why they'd want to shorten his life by taking his shadow. that's legend. what's true is that valentine mcgillycuddy, the army doctor who knew him at fort robinson, doubted any picture of him was ever made.","in 1948 a sculptor named korczak ziółkowski started blasting a huge figure of him on horseback out of a mountain in the black hills, the lakota's holy ground. the head was finished in 1998. plenty of lakota think carving up a sacred mountain to honor a man who wouldn't be photographed is exactly backwards."],"name":"crazy horse","place":"the powder river country and the black hills, then fort robinson in nebraska","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 80 34 C 86 22 92 12 98 4 C 98 16 92 28 84 36 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 35 L 97 7' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"it is no good for the people to depend on me any longer.","where":"his dying words at fort robinson, september 1877, to his father, as recorded by those in the room"},{"q":"hoka hey, it's a good day to die.","where":"attributed to him at the little bighorn. hoka hey means something like let's go, and the line was a common lakota rallying cry, not his alone"},{"q":"one does not sell the earth the people walk on.","where":"attributed; the source is shaky, and it gets quoted anyway because it sounds like what he did"}],"start":"joseph marshall iii's the journey of crazy horse, a life of him told by a lakota writer from lakota stories. then the chapters in black elk speaks about the killing of crazy horse.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1840, near the black hills in what's now south dakota, into the oglala, one of the seven bands of the lakota. as a boy he was called curly, for his light wavy hair. his father was a holy man named crazy horse, and when the son proved himself as a young fighter, the father gave him that name and took another one, worm. [[blackelk|black elk]], the holy man whose vision became a famous book, was his second cousin, and was a boy in the same camps.\nthe story goes that as a young man he fasted alone and had a vision of a rider coming out of a lake, dressed plain, a small stone behind one ear and a single hawk feather in his hair, riding untouched through a storm of bullets and hail. he was told to ride like that, and to take nothing for himself. the man who first told that story to white writers wasn't lakota, and chips, the holy man who actually worked with crazy horse, said the part about the lake wasn't true. so it's a story. what's certain is that he did ride into fights plain, took no scalps or loot for himself, and gave away what he had.\nin december 1866 he led the decoys who drew captain william fetterman and eighty soldiers out of their fort in wyoming and into an ambush. none of them came back. in june 1876 he fought at the rosebud and then at the little bighorn, where custer's command was wiped out. the army hunted the free bands all that winter, and his people froze and starved.\non may 6, 1877, he led about nine hundred people into fort robinson, in nebraska, and surrendered. there were rumors, jealousies among the lakota leaders, and nervous officers who believed he'd break out. on september 5 they walked him to the guardhouse. when he saw the cell he fought, and a soldier ran a bayonet into him. he died that night, in his thirties. his parents took the body away and buried it where nobody has ever found it.","struggle":"in 1871 he married a woman named black shawl, and they had one child, a daughter named they are afraid of her. she died in 1873, a little girl, while he was away fighting. he found her burial scaffold, the raised platform the lakota laid the dead on, and lay on it beside her body for three days and nights.\nthen the winter of 1876 into 1877. soldiers burning the camps, the buffalo nearly gone, children dying of cold. he held out as long as he could, and surrendered to keep the people alive. he knew what it would cost him.","threads":["war","love","grief","silence"],"years":"around 1840 to 1877"},{"after":"he didn't end a grief observed with an answer. he ended it with something quieter. he wrote that maybe his own frantic shouting had deafened him to the voice he wanted to hear, and that the less he clawed at her memory the nearer she seemed. he kept teaching, looked after joy's two sons, and went back to church.\nhe died three years later, a week before his sixty fifth birthday, at home outside oxford. hardly anyone noticed for a day or two, because it was the day kennedy was shot.","basically":"the oxford professor who wrote the narnia books, the ones with the wardrobe and the lion, and who before that was a hard-headed atheist until his friend tolkien, the lord of the rings guy, talked him the rest of the way into christianity on a walk one night. he became the most famous defender of christianity in the english speaking world, with an answer for everything. then he married late, lost his wife to cancer four years later, and wrote a small, furious notebook saying god felt like a door slammed in his face. if you've ever had your neat answers fall apart when somebody died, he wrote the answers and then watched them fall apart.","born":1898,"died":1963,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 200 80 L 172 94 L 172 252 L 200 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 320 80 L 348 94 L 348 252 L 320 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='200' y='80' width='120' height='180' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 194 80 L 326 80'/><rect x='212' y='94' width='96' height='156' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 212 236 C 240 228 280 240 308 232 L 308 250 L 212 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 260 236 L 260 140 M 254 236 L 266 236'/><path d='M 252 124 L 268 124 L 266 140 L 254 140 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><g fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'><circle cx='228' cy='120' r='2.5'/><circle cx='290' cy='110' r='2.5'/><circle cx='240' cy='170' r='2.5'/><circle cx='292' cy='190' r='2.5'/><circle cx='226' cy='210' r='2.5'/><circle cx='280' cy='150' r='2.5'/><\/g><circle cx='100' cy='164' r='14'/><path d='M 100 178 L 100 226'/><path d='M 100 226 L 86 260 M 100 226 L 114 260'/><path d='M 100 192 L 86 216 M 100 192 L 126 186'/><path d='M 106 170 L 116 174'/><path d='M 114 168 L 122 168 L 121 176 L 115 176 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 118 164 C 114 154 122 150 118 140' stroke-dasharray='2 5' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(250,53.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"an atheist oxford don got talked into christ on a night walk with tolkien, and then his wife died and he wrote that god had bolted the door.","key":"cslewis","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._S._Lewis","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Grief_Observed","why":"the notebook he kept after joy died"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"sloppy at most. the thirty years with janie moore were private and odd, and he hid the arrangement from his father for years. whether they were ever lovers nobody knows for sure, and he never said. all adults, and his business.","moments":["narnia started, he said, with a picture he'd had in his head since he was about sixteen, a faun, a little goat-legged man out of the old myths, carrying an umbrella and parcels through a snowy wood.","tolkien read him the lord of the rings in pieces for years at the inklings, a group of oxford friends who met to read their work out loud to each other. tolkien said later that he owed lewis an unpayable debt, mostly for the sheer encouragement.","joy davidman first wrote to him as a reader in 1950, from new york, a jewish-born former communist who'd become a christian partly through his books."],"name":"c.s. lewis","place":"belfast, then oxford, then cambridge","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 70 78 L 86 86' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 84 78 L 96 78 L 94 92 L 86 92 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"no one ever told me that grief felt so like fear.","where":"a grief observed, the notebook he kept after joy died, the first line"},{"q":"i believe in christianity as i believe that the sun has risen, not only because i see it, but because by it i see everything else.","where":"is theology poetry?, a 1945 essay"},{"q":"the most dejected and reluctant convert in all england.","where":"surprised by joy, his memoir of how he came to believe, chapter 14, about 1929"}],"start":"a grief observed, which you'll read in one night. then surprised by joy, his memoir, chapters 14 and 15, for the conversion.","status":"approved","story":"clive staples lewis, jack to everyone, was born in belfast in 1898. his mother died of cancer when he was nine. his father sent him to english boarding schools he hated, and by his teens he was an atheist.\nhe went to the trenches of the first world war as a young officer and was wounded in france in april 1918 by a british shell that fell short. he'd made a pact with a friend in training, paddy moore, that if one of them died the other would look after the dead man's family. paddy was killed. lewis took in paddy's mother, janie moore, twenty six years older than him, and lived with her for about thirty years, until she died in 1951.\nhe became an oxford don, which is what oxford calls its teachers, teaching english literature at magdalen college. in 1929 he gave in and admitted god existed, the most dejected and reluctant convert in all england, he said. on the night of september 19, 1931, he walked addison's walk, a path along the river behind the college, with j.r.r. tolkien, who'd go on to write the lord of the rings, and hugo dyson, another literature teacher, into the small hours. lewis had always loved the old myths, the norse gods and the pagan gods who die and come back. tolkien argued that the story of christ was that same kind of story, except this one had actually happened. a few days later, riding to the zoo in the sidecar of his brother's motorbike, he found he believed it.\nthen the books. the screwtape letters, a senior devil's letters coaching a junior devil on how to ruin a man. mere christianity, which started as radio talks during the second world war explaining the faith to ordinary people. the chronicles of narnia, seven children's books about kids who get into another world. a pile of others. in 1956 he married joy davidman, an american writer, in a registry office, a quick civil ceremony at a government office, so she could stay in britain. months later she had cancer. in 1957 they were married again, by a priest this time, at her hospital bed. she went into remission and they got a few good years. she died in 1960. he died on november 22, 1963, the same day as [[huxley|aldous huxley]], the novelist who wrote brave new world, and president john kennedy.","struggle":"a grief observed is the struggle, written in notebooks after joy died. he says that when you're happy god seems to be there with open arms, and when you're desperate you get a door slammed in your face and the sound of bolting and double bolting on the inside. he wondered on paper whether god was a cosmic sadist. the man who'd explained suffering to the english in the problem of pain, his 1940 book on why a good god lets people hurt, couldn't stand what it did to him.\nhe published it in 1961 under a made-up name, n.w. clerk, so nobody would know the famous defender of the faith had come apart. the story goes that friends who didn't know started handing him copies to help with his grief.","threads":["grief","doubt","love"],"years":"1898 to 1963"},{"after":"nathan the prophet came and told him a story. a rich man with huge flocks had a guest to feed, and instead of killing one of his own sheep he took a poor man's only lamb, the one that ate off the poor man's plate and slept in his arms like a daughter. david was furious and said the man deserved to die. nathan said, you are the man. david didn't argue. he said i have sinned against the lord. the baby got sick, and david lay on the ground and fasted for seven days, and the child died anyway. he got up, washed, ate, and said he'd go to the child someday and the child wouldn't come back to him.\npsalm 51 is the after, and it doesn't make one excuse. nothing about her being where a king could see, nothing about the war. wash me, make me a clean heart, a broken spirit is what you want. it does say against you, you only, have i sinned, and uriah would have had something to say about that. but he didn't excuse it, and he didn't get to undo it. nathan told him the sword would never leave his house, and it didn't.","basically":"the shepherd boy who killed goliath with a sling, became israel's greatest king about three thousand years ago, and wrote a lot of the psalms, the prayer songs people still read at funerals. at the height of his power he had a soldier's wife brought to him, a woman who had no way to say no to a king, got her pregnant, and had her husband killed to cover it up. then a prophet looked him in the eye and said you're the man, and he didn't argue. the worst of it is on the page, and if you've ever done something you can't undo and had somebody name it to your face, psalm 51 is what he wrote after.","born":null,"died":-970,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 70 L 380 70 L 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 294 70 L 386 70'/><rect x='326' y='100' width='26' height='30' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 44 250 C 36 222 40 206 48 200 M 72 250 C 80 222 76 206 68 200 M 44 250 L 72 250 M 42 212 L 74 212 M 52 212 L 52 250 M 58 212 L 58 250 M 64 212 L 64 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 120 192 L 120 234'/><path d='M 120 234 C 100 234 90 250 106 250 L 134 250 C 150 250 140 234 120 234'/><path d='M 120 208 L 106 234 M 120 208 L 134 234'/><path d='M 114 186 L 126 186'/><path d='M 158 250 L 158 236 L 164 243 L 170 232 L 176 243 L 182 236 L 182 250 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 194 236 C 188 226 200 218 208 224 C 214 216 228 222 224 232 C 230 238 220 246 212 242 C 204 248 192 244 194 236 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='230' cy='226' rx='6' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 202 243 L 202 250 M 216 243 L 216 250'/><circle cx='262' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 262 168 L 262 216'/><path d='M 262 216 L 248 250 M 262 216 L 276 250'/><path d='M 262 182 L 276 206 M 262 182 L 236 176'/><g transform='translate(330,45.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the shepherd who killed the giant and wrote the psalms took another man's wife, had the man killed, and then wrote psalm 51.","key":"david","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David","why":"the plain overview, the story and the history"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"here it is plainly. in the spring, when kings went out to war, david stayed home and sent the army. from the roof of the palace he saw a woman bathing, bathsheba, the wife of uriah the hittite, one of his own soldiers, who was off at the siege. david sent men to bring her and he slept with her. he was the king and she was his subject, and the text never says she had any say in it. then she sent him word that she was pregnant.\nhe called uriah home from the front so he'd sleep with his wife and the baby would pass as his. uriah wouldn't go home while his comrades were sleeping in the field. david got him drunk and he still wouldn't. so david wrote a letter to his general, joab, telling him to put uriah in the front line where the fighting was worst and then pull back from him, and he sent the letter by uriah's own hand. uriah died. when the mourning was over david married bathsheba. it's sex and power and murder, all of it one man's doing. a king who took a woman who couldn't say no and then had her husband killed. that's what he did. everything after has to be read with that sitting there.","moments":["on the run, he and his men were hiding at the back of a cave at en-gedi, an oasis by the dead sea, when saul came in alone to relieve himself. david crept up and cut off a corner of his robe instead of killing him, and then felt bad about even that. 1 samuel 24.","when jonathan, saul's son and his closest friend, was killed in battle, david wrote a lament that says jonathan's love for him was more wonderful than the love of women. 2 samuel 1.","running from absalom, a man named shimei followed him along the road throwing stones and cursing him. one of david's men offered to go take his head off. david said leave him alone, maybe the lord told him to. 2 samuel 16.","the line about a man after god's own heart is from 1 samuel 13, said before any of this happened. the new testament repeats it in acts 13, in a sermon of paul's. the book knew what it was saying and kept the rest in anyway."],"name":"david","place":"bethlehem, the hills of judah, jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 36 42 L 36 20 L 47 30 L 60 16 L 73 30 L 84 20 L 84 42 C 70 36 50 36 36 42 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have sinned against the lord.","where":"2 samuel 12:13, the whole of his answer to nathan the prophet"},{"q":"create in me a clean heart, o god.","where":"psalm 51:10; the psalm is headed as his, from when nathan came to him"},{"q":"o my son absalom, my son, my son absalom, would i had died instead of you.","where":"2 samuel 18:33, paraphrased slightly"}],"start":"2 samuel 11 and 12 in one sitting, in the old testament, the first and bigger part of any bible. then psalm 51 straight after. then 2 samuel 18 for absalom. 1 samuel 17 for goliath if you want the part everyone knows.","status":"approved","story":"the youngest of jesse's sons in bethlehem, the small town where jesus would be born a thousand years later. he was out with the sheep when the prophet samuel came looking for a king. a prophet in the bible is someone who speaks for god, and samuel was the one who made and unmade israel's kings. they had to send for him. samuel poured oil on his head in front of his older brothers, which is how you made someone king.\nthen goliath. the philistines were the people on the coast, israel's enemies, and their champion was a giant who stood in the valley of elah for forty days daring anyone to fight him. a kid with a sling and five smooth stones from the brook walked out. one stone. king saul, israel's first king, took him in, got jealous of him, and threw a spear at him while he played the lyre, a small harp. david spent years on the run in the hills with a band of men who were in debt or in trouble, and he had saul at his mercy twice and let him go.\nhe became king, first at hebron and then in jerusalem, and when they brought the ark of the covenant into the city, the gold-covered chest that held the stone tablets of the law and was the holiest thing israel had, he danced in front of it so wildly that his wife michal said he'd made a fool of himself. about half the psalms, the hundred and fifty prayer songs in the middle of the bible, carry his name. scholars argue over how many he wrote. the twenty third, the lord is my shepherd, the one they read at funerals, carries it. so does the twenty second, the one that starts my god, my god, why have you forsaken me, which [[jesus]] quoted from the cross.\nthen 2 samuel 11, the chapter where it all goes wrong, which is in the mess below. then his son absalom took the kingdom from him, and david walked out of jerusalem barefoot and weeping up the mount of olives, the hill just east of the city. he got the kingdom back. absalom died in the fighting, and when the news came david went up to the room over the gate and cried, my son absalom, would i had died instead of you.","struggle":"most of the psalms with his name on them are a man in trouble. hiding in caves, hunted, betrayed by friends, asking god how long. psalm 13 asks it four times in two verses. these are the prayers of somebody who was scared a lot and said so.\nand then the family. his son amnon raped his half sister tamar, and 2 samuel says david was very angry, and it doesn't say he did anything. absalom, tamar's brother, waited two years and killed amnon, ran, came back, and turned the country against his father. the king who could handle a giant couldn't handle his own house.","threads":["forgiveness","family"],"years":"around 1000 bc"},{"after":"after the books made him famous, he didn't just stay a pundit. he taught, for decades, and a lot of the native lawyers who fought for treaty rights and tribal courts in the eighties and nineties sat in his classes. he wrote on law, on treaties, on religion, over twenty books.\nand he kept the argument with christianity going instead of walking away from it. that's what god is red is, a man who took his father's religion seriously enough to fight with it for his whole life.","basically":"a standing rock sioux writer, lawyer and professor, the son and grandson of episcopal priests, who went to seminary himself and then wrote the funniest, angriest books ever aimed at the people who'd been managing native americans. custer died for your sins, in 1969, went after the anthropologists, the missionaries, and the government, and made white readers laugh while it cut them. then god is red, in 1973, argued that native religion was not a primitive version of christianity but a different and in some ways better way of being religious. think of a stand-up comic with a law degree and a theology degree, who knows the church from inside. take from him that you can argue with the faith you were raised in, all the way down, and still take religion seriously.","born":1933,"died":2005,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 120 174 L 120 216'/><path d='M 120 216 C 100 216 90 232 106 232 L 134 232 C 150 232 140 216 120 216'/><path d='M 120 190 L 106 216 M 120 190 L 134 216'/><rect x='150' y='196' width='50' height='26' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 222 L 210 222 L 214 232 L 146 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='160' y='176' width='30' height='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 130 170 L 140 172' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 144 166 C 148 158 142 152 148 144' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 280 250 L 280 170 L 340 170 L 340 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 272 172 L 310 120 L 348 172'/><path d='M 300 130 L 300 80 L 320 80 L 320 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 82 L 310 58 L 324 82'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 214 L 320 214 L 320 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.5'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><rect x='176' y='170' width='14' height='6'/><rect x='196' y='238' width='20' height='4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(310,43.1) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his father and grandfather were episcopal priests, and he wrote the book that told the missionaries to go home.","key":"deloria","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vine_Deloria_Jr.","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custer_Died_for_Your_Sins","why":"the 1969 book that made him"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_Is_Red","why":"his book arguing with christianity"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"in red earth, white lies, in 1995, he went after mainstream science itself, the idea that native people came over a land bridge from asia and the scientists' dates for the ice age, and argued for native oral history over them. scientists said he got a lot of it wrong, and a lot of his admirers wince at that book. he was a fighter, and sometimes he picked a fight he couldn't win.","moments":["an original copy of custer died for your sins sits in the permanent exhibit at the smithsonian's national museum of the american indian.","in 1979 he wrote the introduction to a new edition of [[blackelk|black elk speaks]], calling it a religious classic for native people."],"name":"vine deloria jr.","place":"martin, south dakota, near pine ridge, then washington, and universities in arizona and colorado","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 68 76 L 86 80' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 90 76 C 94 70 90 66 94 60' opacity='.4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"indians have been cursed above all other people in history. indians have anthropologists.","where":"custer died for your sins, 1969, the chapter anthropologists and other friends"},{"q":"christianity is a religion of time. native religions are religions of place.","where":"god is red, 1973, paraphrased; it's the spine of the whole book"},{"q":"the churches should put their theology aside and help the tribes, instead of trying to convert them.","where":"custer died for your sins, the chapter on missionaries, paraphrased"}],"start":"custer died for your sins, the chapters on anthropologists and on missionaries. then god is red, the first few chapters.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on march 26, 1933, in martin, south dakota, near the pine ridge reservation, and was an enrolled member of the standing rock sioux tribe. his grandfather, philip deloria, a yankton dakota leader, had become an episcopal priest, the american branch of the church of england. his father, vine deloria sr., was an episcopal archdeacon and missionary on the reservations. his aunt ella deloria was a well known scholar of the dakota language.\nhe served in the marines from 1954 to 1956, then went to seminary, and in 1963 earned a theology degree from augustana, a lutheran seminary in rock island, illinois. he looked like he'd be the third generation in the pulpit. instead, from 1964 to 1967 he ran the national congress of american indians, the biggest native political organization in the country. then he went to law school, and finished at the university of colorado in 1970.\nin 1969 he published custer died for your sins: an indian manifesto. custer was the cavalry commander wiped out at the little bighorn in 1876, and the title throws the country's own religion back at it. the book is eleven essays, and it's funny. the chapter on anthropologists, the scholars who kept coming out to study native people, opens by saying indians have been cursed above all other people, because indians have anthropologists. the chapter on the churches says the missionaries should stop converting indians and start helping the tribes.\nin 1973 came god is red. its argument is that christianity is a religion of time, of history marching toward an end, and native religions are religions of place, tied to particular land. and that a religion that forgot the land had helped wreck it. he taught for years at the university of arizona and the university of colorado, and trained a generation of native lawyers and scholars. he died in golden, colorado, on november 13, 2005.","struggle":"he was raised inside the church, by men who'd given their lives to it, and he went to seminary to join them. then he spent the rest of his life arguing that the whole missionary project had been a disaster for his people. i don't know what that cost at home. the books are what's left, and they don't sound like a man who found it easy.\nand he was writing into a country that mostly thought of native people as sad history. being funny about it was a choice, and it didn't come from nowhere.","threads":["anger","doubt","the land"],"years":"1933 to 2005"},{"after":"what she did was the works of mercy, literally, for forty seven years. that's an old church list that comes mostly from a speech jesus gives in matthew 25, where he says whatever you did for the least of these, you did for me. feed the hungry, shelter the homeless, visit the prisoner. she lived in the houses with the people who came, ate the same soup, and wore donated clothes.\nand she kept going to jail. in the fifties for refusing to take shelter during new york's civil defense drills, the practice runs where the whole city had to hide underground as if a nuclear bomb were coming. the last time was 1973, in california, with the farmworkers' union led by cesar chavez, the grape pickers striking for decent pay, at seventy five. there's a photograph of her sitting on a folding stool facing two armed police officers, a small old woman who isn't going anywhere.","basically":"a radical young journalist in 1910s new york, drinking with playwrights and getting arrested with the women fighting for the vote, who had an abortion, then a baby with a man she never married, and then became catholic and lost him over it. in 1933 she started a penny newspaper and a string of soup kitchens and shelters that are still running, and she lived in them, with whoever came through the door, for almost fifty years. picture a new york activist who never took a salary, never moved out of the shelter, and was still getting arrested at seventy five. she's for anyone who thinks faith should look like somebody getting fed tonight.","born":1897,"died":1980,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='36' y='228' width='50' height='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 235 L 86 235 M 36 242 L 86 242' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='150' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 150 168 L 150 216'/><path d='M 150 216 L 136 250 M 150 216 L 164 250'/><path d='M 150 184 L 172 194 M 150 180 L 172 184'/><path d='M 145 159 Q 150 163 155 159'/><path d='M 166 212 L 262 212 M 174 212 L 174 250 M 254 212 L 254 250'/><path d='M 184 212 L 188 182 L 238 182 L 242 212 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 182 182 L 244 182'/><path d='M 172 180 L 200 176'/><path d='M 202 176 C 198 166 206 160 202 150 M 222 176 C 226 166 218 160 222 150' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='290' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 290 168 L 290 216'/><path d='M 290 216 L 276 250 M 290 216 L 304 250'/><path d='M 290 182 L 304 206 M 290 182 L 264 176'/><path d='M 256 174 C 256 182 272 182 272 174 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='335' cy='154' r='11'/><path d='M 335 168 L 335 216'/><path d='M 335 216 L 321 250 M 335 216 L 349 250'/><path d='M 335 182 L 319 206 M 335 182 L 351 206'/><circle cx='372' cy='154' r='10'/><path d='M 372 168 L 372 216'/><path d='M 372 216 L 358 250 M 372 216 L 386 250'/><path d='M 372 182 L 356 206 M 372 182 L 388 206'/><\/g><g transform='translate(46,203.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she had an abortion, a common-law husband, and a jail record before she was thirty, and then she started feeding people and never stopped.","key":"dorothyday","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothy_Day","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"the catholic worker","url":"https://www.catholicworker.org/","why":"the movement she started, still running, with her writings"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Long_Loneliness","why":"her autobiography"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the abortion, the short marriage, the common-law years. she told it herself, in her own books, and called herself a sinner without making a show of it. later there were people at the catholic worker who found her bossy and hard to work for, and on a bad day she'd have agreed.","moments":["the first issue of the catholic worker, may 1, 1933, was 2,500 copies. it cost a penny. it still does.","in the fifties, when the sirens went for the air raid drills, she and a handful of others sat on benches in city hall park instead of going underground, and got arrested for it, year after year, until the city quietly stopped the drills.","[[merton|thomas merton]], the famous monk in kentucky, wrote to her for years and sent the paper his essays against war. she printed them."],"name":"dorothy day","place":"brooklyn, chicago, the lower east side of new york","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 31 48 C 36 34 84 34 89 48 C 84 42 36 42 31 48 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 43 L 44 38 M 50 40 L 54 36 M 60 39 L 64 35 M 70 40 L 74 36 M 80 43 L 84 38' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"don't call me a saint. i don't want to be dismissed so easily.","where":"attributed; she said something like it more than once"},{"q":"we have all known the long loneliness, and the only solution is love, and love comes with community.","where":"the long loneliness, her autobiography, the last pages, paraphrased"},{"q":"i really only love god as much as i love the person i love the least.","where":"attributed to her"}],"start":"the long loneliness, her autobiography. then loaves and fishes, her book about the catholic worker itself, the houses and the people in them.","status":"approved","story":"a bohemian journalist in greenwich village, new york's artists' and radicals' neighborhood, in the late teens, writing for socialist papers and drinking with eugene o'neill, the playwright, before he was famous. in 1917 she was arrested with the suffragists, the women fighting for the right to vote, for picketing the white house, and she went on a hunger strike in the workhouse, a prison farm in virginia. she was twenty.\nafter an affair with a newspaperman went bad she had an abortion, around 1919. then a short marriage. then, on staten island, a common-law life with forster batterham, an anarchist, a man who thought there shouldn't be governments at all, who loved the beach and didn't believe in marriage or god. she was happier than she'd ever been. when their daughter tamar was born in 1926 the joy turned her toward god, and she had tamar baptized, the ceremony that makes a baby a member of the church.\nforster wouldn't marry, and she wouldn't stay without the church, so in 1927 she was baptized herself and they split. it broke her heart, and she said so for the rest of her life.\nin december 1932 peter maurin turned up at her apartment with a plan. he was a french drifter from a peasant family, twenty years older than her, who walked around new york lecturing anybody who'd listen about how to rebuild the world. on may 1, 1933, they sold the first issue of the catholic worker, a newspaper saying the church belonged on the side of the poor and the unions, in union square, the manhattan plaza where the radicals gave their speeches, for a penny. then came the soup lines, then the houses of hospitality, which were shelters where anyone who came got a bed and a meal and nobody made them earn it, then the farms. it's still going, a movement of houses all over the world run by volunteers who live in them. she stayed a pacifist, someone who won't support any war, through the second world war, even against hitler, and lost most of her readers for it. she died in 1980 at maryhouse, one of her own houses, in new york.","struggle":"in 1924 she wrote a thinly disguised novel about the years before, the eleventh virgin, and later regretted it. she didn't dress up that part of her life. she put it in the long loneliness, her autobiography, and let it stand.\nthe loneliness in the title was real. she gave up the man she loved for a church he couldn't stand, and raised tamar mostly on her own in the middle of a soup kitchen full of drunks, cranks, and saints. her diaries are full of being tired, sharp with people, and sick of everyone in the house.","threads":["work","loneliness","prison"],"years":"1897 to 1980"},{"after":"after wounded knee he kept practicing medicine, and when the reservation job went bad he found other ways to work. he started thirty two native ymca chapters, the young men's christian associations, in the 1890s, and lectured all over the country. he kept writing, calm and clear, for people who'd never heard a native person explain anything.\nafter the separation he mostly lived apart from his family, and kept lecturing and writing. he said what he had to say in the books and didn't add a public reckoning about the marriage. that's his after.","basically":"a santee dakota boy raised the old way in canada until he was fifteen, when his father, whom everyone thought was dead, walked into camp in white man's clothes and took him off to school. he became one of the first native doctors in america, and his first job was on the pine ridge reservation weeks before the massacre at wounded knee. after that he spent his life explaining native religion to white america in plain, calm books. think of the kid who gets out, gets the degree, and comes home to the worst day in his people's history. take from him that you can carry two worlds and say out loud what each one got wrong.","born":1858,"died":1939,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 170 L 130 170 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 172 L 85 126 L 140 172'/><path d='M 85 126 L 85 104 M 77 112 L 93 112'/><path d='M 70 250 L 70 206 L 100 206 L 100 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.25'/><path d='M 85 212 L 76 240 L 94 240 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='250' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 250 164 L 250 210'/><path d='M 250 210 L 266 246 M 250 210 L 232 242'/><path d='M 250 178 L 264 200 M 250 178 L 230 186'/><ellipse cx='230' cy='186' rx='12' ry='8' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='262' y='202' width='22' height='16' rx='3' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='180' cy='60' r='3'/><circle cx='240' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='320' cy='80' r='3'/><circle cx='360' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='200' cy='110' r='3'/><circle cx='300' cy='130' r='3'/><\/g><path d='M 160 250 C 200 244 320 244 380 250' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(85,89.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was the new doctor at pine ridge when wounded knee happened, and he went out in the snow and brought back the living.","key":"eastman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Eastman","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the soul of the indian, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340","why":"his short book on native religion, at project gutenberg"},{"label":"indian boyhood, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/337","why":"his memoir of growing up dakota, at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of. he and elaine separated in 1921, and neither of them said much in public about why. that's grief, not a scandal.","moments":["his first name, hakadah, the pitiful last, was a kind of grief for his mother. the second, ohiyesa, the winner, he earned as a small boy at a ball game.","in the soul of the indian he says the old dakota didn't argue about religion. they thought each person's way to the holy was their own."],"name":"charles eastman, ohiyesa","place":"minnesota, then canada, then dartmouth and boston, then the pine ridge reservation, then everywhere lecturing","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 46 98 C 44 112 54 118 60 118 C 66 118 76 112 74 98'/><circle cx='60' cy='121' r='3.5' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all this was a severe ordeal for one who had so lately put all his faith in the christian love and lofty ideals of the white man.","where":"from the deep woods to civilization, his 1916 memoir, the chapter on the ghost dance war"},{"q":"the worship of the great mystery was silent, solitary, free from all self-seeking.","where":"the soul of the indian, 1911, the first chapter, paraphrased. the great mystery is wakan tanka, the lakota and dakota name for the holy"},{"q":"i am an indian, and though i've learned a lot from civilization, i've never lost my indian sense of right and justice.","where":"from the deep woods to civilization, the last pages, paraphrased"}],"start":"the soul of the indian, which is short and free online. then from the deep woods to civilization, the chapters on pine ridge, for wounded knee in his own words.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in february 1858 near redwood falls, minnesota, into the santee, the eastern dakota, one of the three great branches of the people called the sioux. his mother died soon after, so they called him hakadah, the pitiful last. his grandmother raised him. in 1862, with the government months late on the food and money it owed them, and the dakota starving, the dakota war of 1862 broke out in minnesota. it was crushed, and on december 26, 1862, thirty eight dakota men were hanged together at mankato, the biggest mass execution in american history. his family fled to canada. his father had been taken prisoner, and they believed he'd been hanged.\nhe grew up in canada as a hunter, living the old way. the story goes he got the name ohiyesa, the winner, when his band won a lacrosse match. at fifteen his father showed up alive. he'd become a christian, taken the name jacob eastman, and was farming in dakota territory, and he took the boy to school. ohiyesa became charles eastman, went through dartmouth college, graduated in 1887, and finished medical school at boston university in 1890, one of the first native americans to become a doctor.\nthat fall he went to the pine ridge reservation in south dakota as the government doctor. on december 29, 1890, soldiers killed hundreds of lakota at wounded knee creek. the wounded were brought to the episcopal mission chapel, where the christmas tree was still standing. eastman and the missionaries tore out the pews, covered the floor with hay and quilts, and laid them in rows. on the third day, when the blizzard cleared, he went out with a search party to the field and found bodies strewn as far as three miles away, and a few still alive under the snow, among them a baby girl about a year old, wrapped up and unhurt. he carried her back.\nat pine ridge he met elaine goodale, a white teacher who ran the government schools in the dakotas, and they married in 1891. they had six children, and she helped shape the books he wrote, among them indian boyhood in 1902 and the soul of the indian in 1911, his short plain explanation of native religion for people who'd never heard it explained. he helped start the society of american indians in 1911, the first national group run by native people, and helped get the boy scouts going. he died in detroit on january 8, 1939.","struggle":"he lost everyone at four. his mother was already gone, his father taken and presumed hanged, and his people driven out of minnesota for good. he grew up a refugee in another country.\nthen wounded knee, weeks into his first job, and it shook the thing he'd built his life on. he'd become a christian and a doctor and believed what his teachers told him about civilization, and he spent that week pulling children out from under the snow where soldiers of that civilization had left them. he never stopped being a christian. he did stop believing that the white way was the higher one.\nmuch later his daughter irene died of influenza in 1918, and not long after, the marriage came apart.","threads":["war","grief","doubt","family"],"years":"1858 to 1939"},{"after":"the dominicans, his order, kept reading him quietly for seven hundred years. copies survived in convents. in the twentieth century d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who brought zen to america, reached for him, thomas merton, the kentucky monk on these pages, read him, and he became the bridge between christians and buddhists that the church had tried to shut down.\nin 1992 the dominican order formally asked the vatican to lift the condemnation. it's still technically on the books. nobody cares. that's a kind of after too.","basically":"a german friar around the year 1300, one of the dominicans, an order of preaching monks, who was among the top professors in europe and then started telling ordinary people in their own language that god was nearer to them than they were to themselves. the church put him on trial for it and he died before the verdict. picture a star professor who drops the jargon, starts preaching at the laundromat, and gets reported to headquarters. if every idea you've got about god has started getting in the way of god, he's the one who told you to drop those too.","born":1260,"died":1328,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 212'/><path d='M 200 212 L 186 246 M 200 212 L 214 246'/><path d='M 200 178 L 180 154 M 200 178 L 220 154'/><path d='M 140 70 C 170 40 230 40 260 70 C 230 100 170 100 140 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='200' cy='70' r='13' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='200' cy='70' r='5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 200 90 L 200 132' stroke-dasharray='2 7' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 20 260 C 100 258 150 262 380 260'/><path d='M 300 260 L 300 220 L 340 220 L 340 260 M 310 220 L 320 200 L 330 220' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(360,230.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a dominican friar who prayed to be rid of god, got put on trial for heresy, and died before the verdict.","key":"eckhart","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meister_Eckhart","why":"the plain overview of his life and trial"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"none. the church thought his ideas were the mess. history has come around.","moments":["meister means master, the german version of the title he earned at the university of paris, where he held the top chair in theology twice. only thomas aquinas, the dominican whose big system the catholic church still teaches, had done that before him.","his sermon on martha and mary flips a story from luke's gospel that everyone in church knows. jesus visits two sisters. martha runs around doing all the cooking and complains, mary sits at his feet listening, and jesus says mary picked the better part. eckhart says martha was the one who'd arrived, because she could be in the kitchen and with god at once. mary was still a beginner.","he preached in german to nuns and ordinary people when theology was done in latin, the church's language. that's why the wild stuff survived. the women wrote it down."],"name":"meister eckhart","place":"thuringia, paris, cologne","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 33 42 40 38 46 40 L 44 52 Z M 88 50 C 87 42 80 38 74 40 L 76 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the eye through which i see god is the same eye through which god sees me.","where":"his german sermons, number 57 in maurice walshe's english edition"},{"q":"i pray god to rid me of god.","where":"his sermon on poverty of spirit, known by its latin first line, beati pauperes spiritu, blessed are the poor in spirit, which is how jesus opens his most famous sermon"},{"q":"if the only prayer you ever say in your life is thank you, it will be enough.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; the idea is his, the sentence probably isn't"}],"start":"the german sermons, in maurice walshe's or oliver davies's english. if you only read one, read the sermon on poverty of spirit, beati pauperes spiritu, where he says the truly poor person wants nothing, knows nothing, and has nothing, not even god. the talks of instruction are the practical side, advice he gave the young friars when he was in charge of them.","status":"approved","story":"he was one of the most senior dominicans in europe. the dominicans are an order of friars, which means monks who live out in the towns and preach instead of staying behind monastery walls. he held a professor's chair at the university of paris, the harvard of medieval europe, twice, which almost nobody got. then he started preaching in german to nuns and ordinary townspeople, and what came out of him in plain speech was wilder than anything he'd written in latin, the language of scholars and the church, which most people couldn't read.\nhe said there's a spark in the soul that's never been separate from god. he said the eye you see god with is the same eye god sees you with. he said he prayed to god to rid him of god, meaning every idea of god you've got is in the way of the real thing, like a map you keep staring at while the road goes by. and the line everyone quotes, which is his idea if not his sentence, says that if the only prayer you ever said was thank you, it would be enough.\nin 1326 the archbishop of cologne, the top churchman in that part of germany, put him on trial for heresy. heresy means teaching something the church has ruled false, and it could get you burned. sixteen years before, a french mystic named marguerite porete had been burned alive in paris for a book about the soul losing itself in god. eckhart appealed over the archbishop's head to the pope, who lived in avignon, in southern france, back then, not rome, and went there to defend himself. he died there before it was settled. in 1329 the pope condemned a list of his statements anyway. the dominicans quietly kept reading him for seven hundred years.\nwhen d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who did more than anyone to bring zen buddhism to america, wanted to explain zen to christians in the fifties, eckhart was the one he reached for. zen is the japanese school of buddhism built on sitting still and seeing straight through. [[merton]] read him. [[rohr]] reads him. [[huxley]] put him all over the perennial philosophy, his 1945 book arguing that all the religions share one core. he's what christianity sounds like when it stops explaining.","struggle":"he was in his sixties when the archbishop of cologne charged him with heresy, teaching what the church had ruled false, and he went to avignon, where the pope lived then, to argue his own case in front of the pope's men. he wrote that he could be wrong but he couldn't be a heretic, because being wrong is a matter of the mind and heresy is a matter of the will. he meant he'd never set out to go against the church, and if he'd made a mistake, show him and he'd take it back.\nhe died before they finished. the pope's condemnation came a year later, so the last months of a man who'd taught that god was nearer to you than you are to yourself were spent in rooms full of church lawyers picking through his sentences one at a time.","threads":["silence","doubt"],"years":"around 1260 to 1328"},{"after":"in nicopolis he lived in a small house with almost nothing in it, a pallet and a mat and a lamp, and taught for the rest of his life. he didn't write, he didn't go into politics, he didn't ask for anything. he sat in a room and argued with young men about how to be free.\na greek scholar named simplicius, writing a few hundred years later, says that in old age epictetus took in a baby whose parents were going to leave it out to die, which romans did with babies they didn't want, legally, all the time. he got a woman to help him raise it. that's all we know about it. a man who'd been owned took in someone nobody wanted.","basically":"a slave with a busted leg who became the most useful teacher rome ever had. everything on a therapist's whiteboard about controlling what you can and letting go of the rest, he said first, and he said it as a man who'd been property. think of him as the original 'not my circus' guy, except he'd earned it.","born":50,"died":135,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 246 L 380 246'/><circle cx='140' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 140 164 L 140 212'/><path d='M 140 212 L 126 246 M 140 212 L 154 246'/><path d='M 140 178 L 124 202 M 140 178 L 156 202'/><path d='M 135 155 Q 140 159 145 155'/><path d='M 162 176 L 170 246 M 154 174 L 170 176' stroke-width='3.5'/><path d='M 190 210 L 250 210 M 196 210 L 196 246 M 244 210 L 244 246'/><path d='M 206 210 C 206 200 234 200 234 210 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 234 204 L 242 202'/><path d='M 220 200 C 214 192 220 186 220 182 C 224 188 228 192 220 200 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='280' y='80' width='90' height='90' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 325 80 L 325 170' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(338,100) scale(.42)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 60'/><path d='M 0 60 L -16 96 M 0 60 L 18 92'/><path d='M 0 28 L -14 50 M 0 28 L 20 36'/><\/g><path d='M 350 108 L 358 118 L 350 118 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g transform='translate(292,148.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was born a slave, walked with a limp, never wrote a word, and an emperor carried his lectures around with him.","key":"epictetus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epictetus","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the handbook, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45109","why":"the enchiridion at project gutenberg, twenty minutes to read"},{"label":"the discourses, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10661","why":"a selection from his class notes at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. the handbook has some cold lines, the one that tells you when you kiss your child to remind yourself you're kissing someone who'll die, and people reasonably find that hard to take. he meant it as protection. it can read like armor.","moments":["someone stole his iron lamp. he said he'd lost it because the thief was better at staying awake than he was, and bought a clay one. after he died, says lucian, a satirist of the time, a collector paid three thousand drachmas, a small fortune, for the clay lamp, hoping some of the wisdom would come with it.","a father told him he'd run out of the house when his little daughter was sick because he couldn't stand watching. epictetus took him apart gently for a whole chapter, asking whether love was the thing that made him leave her. discourses, book 1, chapter 11.","the teacher he learned from, musonius rufus, used to tell students that if they were comfortable in his lectures he was doing it wrong. epictetus kept that."],"name":"epictetus","place":"hierapolis in phrygia, then rome, then nicopolis in northwest greece","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 104 130 L 108 40' stroke-width='4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"some things are up to us and some are not.","where":"the enchiridion, his short handbook, section 1, the first line"},{"q":"it isn't things that upset people, it's what they think about things.","where":"the enchiridion, section 5, paraphrased"},{"q":"a philosopher's school is a hospital. you shouldn't walk out feeling good, you should walk out having been treated.","where":"the discourses, his student's notes of his classes, book 3, chapter 23, paraphrased"}],"start":"the enchiridion, which you can read in twenty minutes. then the discourses, book 1, chapter 1. robin hard's or robin waterfield's translations are plain.","status":"approved","story":"he was born a slave in hierapolis, in what's now turkey, around the year 50. his name just means acquired. his owner in rome was epaphroditus, himself a former slave who'd risen to be one of the secretaries of nero, the emperor remembered for murdering his own mother.\nhe was lame in one leg. the story goes that his master was twisting it to see what he'd do, and epictetus said calmly, you're going to break it, and when it broke he said, i told you you would. a later writer says it was rheumatism. either way he walked with a limp his whole life.\nwhile still a slave he was allowed to study with musonius rufus, the leading stoic teacher in rome. the stoics were a greek school of philosophy that said you can't control what happens to you, only what you make of it, and that that's enough to live free and decent. modern cognitive behavioral therapy borrowed straight from them, and its founders said so. at some point he was freed and started teaching himself. around 93 the emperor domitian, who was paranoid about critics, threw the philosophers out of rome, and epictetus went to nicopolis on the greek coast and opened a school. people came from all over to sit in it.\nhe wrote nothing. a young student named arrian, who later governed a province and wrote the history of alexander the great, took down what he said as close to word for word as he could. that's the discourses, four books of class notes surviving, and the enchiridion, which means handbook, a short summary you can read in twenty minutes. the first line of the handbook is the whole program. some things are up to us and some are not.","struggle":"he spent the first part of his life as property. someone else decided where he slept, what he did, and what happened to his body, and the leg is the proof. everything he taught about what's up to you and what isn't, he learned in a house where almost nothing was.\nthen freedom, and then exile. he'd built a life teaching in rome and in his forties an emperor emptied the city of philosophers and he had to start over on a foreign coast with nothing. he never went back.","threads":["exile","the body"],"years":"around 50 to around 135"},{"after":"after fannie died he kept working, married again, and kept praying for people. after wounded knee in 1973 he didn't cash in on being the famous elder. he went back to pine ridge and kept doing ceremonies and hosting the treaty talks that came out of the occupation.\nat his funeral in 1989, russell means, one of the american indian movement's leaders, gave the eulogy and said fools crow was the reason the occupation ended without a massacre. that's not a bad thing to have said over you.","basically":"an oglala lakota holy man and ceremonial chief, born around 1890 and living to about ninety nine, who turned up at the center of almost every big lakota moment of the twentieth century. he quit school in third grade, toured with buffalo bill's wild west as a young man, led the sun dance for decades, stood between the militants and the federal government at wounded knee in 1973, prayed to open the united states senate in lakota, and told congress to give back the black hills. think of the old grandfather at the table everybody listens to, the cops included. take from him that the quiet old man is sometimes the one holding the whole thing together.","born":1890,"died":1989,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 230 250 L 230 80 M 230 110 L 210 90 M 230 104 L 254 86'/><path d='M 210 90 L 206 104 M 254 86 L 258 100 M 230 84 L 240 96' stroke='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='140' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 140 164 L 140 212'/><path d='M 140 212 L 126 246 M 140 212 L 154 246'/><path d='M 140 180 L 162 190 M 140 176 L 162 180'/><path d='M 150 180 L 200 168' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='196' y='162' width='9' height='9' rx='2' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(300,196) scale(.35)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -20 4 M 0 28 L 20 4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(340,196) scale(.35)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -20 4 M 0 28 L 20 4'/><\/g><path d='M 40 250 C 40 220 80 220 80 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 222 L 60 212'/><g transform='translate(254,72.5) scale(0.5)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"black elk's nephew kept the sun dance going at pine ridge, and in 1973 he helped talk the guns down at wounded knee.","key":"foolscrow","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Fools_Crow","why":"the plain overview of his life"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the senate prayer in 1975 was in lakota, start to finish. he said what he had to say in his own language, and let them catch up.","the books about him were made by thomas mails, a white writer and illustrator who interviewed him in the 1970s. fools crow, in 1979, and fools crow: wisdom and power, in 1990."],"name":"frank fools crow","place":"the pine ridge reservation in south dakota, near wounded knee and kyle","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 18 120 L 72 106' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='70' y='100' width='9' height='9' rx='2' fill='var(--peach)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"survival of the world depends on our sharing what we have and working together.","where":"attributed, quoted in accounts of his life"},{"q":"we are little hollow bones for the higher powers to work through.","where":"fools crow: wisdom and power, the book thomas mails made with him, paraphrased"},{"q":"the black hills are not for sale.","where":"the message of his testimony to congress in september 1976, paraphrased"}],"start":"thomas mails's fools crow, from 1979, built from interviews with him, a white writer's book in his words. then fools crow: wisdom and power.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1890 on the pine ridge reservation, near wounded knee creek, into the oglala lakota. [[blackelk|black elk]], the holy man whose vision became a famous book, was his uncle. he quit school in the third grade to help support his family, and as a young man he traveled around the country with buffalo bill's wild west.\nhe became a holy man and healer, and in time the ceremonial chief of the lakota, the one who leads the ceremonies for the whole people rather than one family. for years he was the intercessor at the sun dance, the great summer ceremony, meaning the holy man who prays for the people and for the dancers. the government had banned the sun dance for decades, and native religion wasn't protected by law until the american indian religious freedom act of 1978. he was one of the men who kept it going and brought it back into the open.\non february 27, 1973, members of the american indian movement, a young native activist group, and local oglala took over the village of wounded knee, the site of the 1890 massacre. they were protesting the tribal chairman, dick wilson, and the government's broken treaties. federal agents surrounded them, and it went on seventy one days, with gunfire most nights. fools crow, the senior elder, backed the protest, went to the united nations in new york to ask for recognition of an independent oglala nation, and helped negotiate the end in may.\non september 5, 1975, he gave the opening prayer in the united states senate, in lakota. on september 10, 1976, he spoke to a congressional subcommittee and asked for the black hills to be returned to his people. the fort laramie treaty of 1868 had promised the hills to the lakota forever. in 1980 the supreme court agreed they'd been taken illegally and awarded money instead. the lakota have refused the money ever since. he died near kyle, on pine ridge, on november 27, 1989.","struggle":"he lived his whole life on a reservation that was, for most of it, one of the poorest places in the united states, and he prayed and doctored for people there who mostly couldn't pay him. the ceremonies he was trained in were outlawed when he was young, and he carried them anyway.\nhis first wife, fannie afraid, died in 1954. his second wife, kate, died in october 1988, and he died a year later.","threads":["nonviolence","the land","grief"],"years":"around 1890 to 1989"},{"after":"he didn't fight for the order. he went off to pray, and at the end, sick and nearly blind, in a hut by san damiano, he wrote the canticle of the sun. brother sun, sister moon, brother wind, sister water, brother fire, mother earth. a praise song for the light from a man who couldn't stand to look at it.\nnear the end he added one more verse, praising god for sister bodily death. then he asked to be laid naked on the bare ground at the portiuncula, the little chapel below assisi where it had all started, and he died there on october 3, 1226.","basically":"a rich merchant's kid from a small italian town around 1200 who wanted to be a knight, came home from a war and a year in prison not quite right, and gave away everything he had down to his clothes. he started a band of barefoot beggar brothers who owned nothing, and the stories have him preaching to birds and making a deal with a wolf. picture a trust fund kid who walks out of the family business to live under bridges on purpose, and turns out to be the happiest person anyone's ever met. he's for anyone who suspects the stuff is what's in the way.","born":1181,"died":1226,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='200' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 200 168 L 200 216'/><path d='M 200 216 L 186 250 M 200 216 L 214 250'/><path d='M 200 182 L 180 158 M 200 182 L 220 158'/><path d='M 195 159 Q 200 163 205 159'/><path d='M 226 250 L 230 240 L 276 240 L 280 250 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 230 240 L 234 232 L 272 232 L 276 240 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='264' cy='226' r='6' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 260 220 L 264 222 L 268 220'/><circle cx='50' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 50 164 L 50 210'/><path d='M 50 210 L 66 246 M 50 210 L 32 242'/><path d='M 50 178 L 64 200 M 50 178 L 30 186'/><path d='M 76 170 L 88 166 M 78 182 L 90 182' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 70 Q 306 64 312 70 Q 318 64 324 70 M 330 96 Q 335 91 340 96 Q 345 91 350 96 M 280 104 Q 285 99 290 104 Q 295 99 300 104'/><circle cx='140' cy='56' r='16' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 164 56 L 174 56'/><path d='M 116 56 L 106 56'/><path d='M 140 80 L 140 90'/><path d='M 140 32 L 140 22'/><path d='M 157 73 L 164 80'/><path d='M 123 73 L 116 80'/><path d='M 157 39 L 164 32'/><path d='M 123 39 L 116 32'/><g transform='translate(234,211.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a rich kid took off every stitch in the town square, handed the clothes back to his father, and walked out naked.","key":"francis","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_of_Assisi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canticle_of_the_Sun","why":"the praise song he wrote near the end"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fioretti_di_San_Francesco","why":"the little flowers, the old book of stories about him"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of, unless you count what he did to himself. he fasted and went cold and slept on the ground until his body broke. near the end, the story goes, he apologized to it, to brother ass, which was his name for his body, for how he'd treated it.","moments":["at christmas 1223, at greccio, a hill town south of assisi, he set up a manger with a real ox and donkey so people could see how poor the birth of jesus was. every nativity scene on every lawn comes from that.","the story goes that he preached to a flock of birds near bevagna, told them to be grateful for their feathers, and they stayed to listen.","the wolf of gubbio, from the little flowers, a book of stories about him collected about a century after he died: a wolf was eating people, francis walked out and made a deal with it, the town fed it, and it died of old age. legend, and a pretty good plan."],"name":"francis of assisi","place":"assisi, italy, and the roads out of it","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 33 42 40 38 46 40 L 44 52 Z M 88 50 C 87 42 80 38 74 40 L 76 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 82 106 C 84 96 96 94 100 102 C 104 100 108 100 110 102 C 106 104 102 106 100 106 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='98' cy='101' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"praised be you, my lord, through brother sun.","where":"the canticle of the sun, the praise song he wrote near the end, 1225"},{"q":"what seemed bitter to me was turned into sweetness of soul and body.","where":"his testament, the last words he left his brothers, 1226, about the lepers"},{"q":"preach the gospel at all times, and if necessary use words.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; there's no record he said it"}],"start":"the little flowers of st francis, the old book of stories about him, for the legends. then the canticle of the sun, which is one page. then eager to love, richard rohr's book, for a modern franciscan reading him.","status":"approved","story":"francesco bernardone was the son of a rich cloth merchant in assisi, and he spent his youth spending it. parties, songs, good clothes. he wanted to be a knight. in 1202 assisi went to war with perugia, the bigger town next door, he was captured, and he spent about a year in a perugian prison. he came home sick and not quite the same.\na few years later, riding outside assisi, he met a leper. leprosy is a disease that eats away at skin and nerves, and lepers were kept outside the town and nobody touched them. instead of riding past he got down and kissed him. he said at the end of his life that what had seemed bitter was turned into sweetness. in the crumbling little church of san damiano outside town he heard the painted cross over the altar tell him to go and repair its house, which as he could see was falling down. he took it literally, sold some of his father's cloth, and tried to give the money to the priest.\nhis father hauled him before the bishop to get the money back. in front of the town francis gave back the money, took off all his clothes, handed them to his father, and said that from now on he'd only say our father who art in heaven, the first line of the lord's prayer. god was the only father he had now. the bishop covered him with his own cloak.\nbrothers gathered around him, owning nothing, begging and preaching. they became the franciscans, the friars, which is from the word for brothers. friars are like monks except they don't stay behind walls; they're out on the roads. in 1219, in the middle of the crusades, the christian wars to take the holy land, he walked across the battle lines in egypt to talk to the sultan, al-kamil, the muslim ruler the crusaders were fighting, and came back alive. in 1224 on la verna, a mountain in tuscany, he received the stigmata, which means wounds showed up in his hands and feet and side like the nail and spear wounds of jesus on the cross. he died in 1226. [[rohr|richard rohr]] is one of his friars, eight hundred years on.","struggle":"the order got away from him. thousands of men joined within ten years, and a lot of them wanted buildings, books, rules, and a little security. he wanted none of it. around 1220 he handed the running of the order to someone else and watched his rule, the short set of instructions the brothers lived by, which had been mostly jesus's own words about taking nothing for the road, get rewritten into something the lawyers could live with.\nand his body gave out. his stomach, and eyes so bad that by the end he was nearly blind and couldn't bear the light. the doctors burned his temples with a hot iron to treat them. the story goes that he talked to the fire first, asked brother fire to be gentle, and said afterward he hadn't felt it.","threads":["money","the body","joy"],"years":"1181 or 1182 to 1226"},{"after":"he went back to work. by 1946 he was running the neurology department. in 1947 he married elly schwindt and they had a daughter, gabriele. he spent fifty years telling people who'd never been near a camp that their suffering could mean something if they could find what it was for, and he meant their small suffering too.\nhe wouldn't blame the austrians as a people, which angered some other survivors. he said there were two races of men, the decent and the indecent, and that both were everywhere, including among the guards and among the prisoners. he stayed in vienna, the city that had sent his family to die, for the rest of his life.","basically":"a jewish psychiatrist from vienna who survived four nazi camps, came home to find his wife, his parents, and his brother all dead, and then wrote a short book in nine days saying a person can bear almost anything if they have a reason to. it's sold millions and it's on every therapist's shelf, and a lot of the modern talk about finding your why traces back to him. he earned every word of it. take from him that you don't always get to pick what happens to you, and you can still pick what it's for.","born":1905,"died":1997,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='60' cy='50' r='2.5'/><circle cx='120' cy='80' r='2.5'/><circle cx='200' cy='40' r='2.5'/><circle cx='250' cy='90' r='2.5'/><circle cx='40' cy='120' r='2.5'/><\/g><path d='M 90 250 C 96 282 184 282 190 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='140' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 140 190 L 140 238'/><path d='M 140 238 L 126 272 M 140 238 L 154 272'/><path d='M 140 204 L 124 228 M 140 204 L 156 228'/><path d='M 170 250 L 190 190'/><path d='M 176 184 C 186 182 198 188 204 196'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 222 L 344 222 L 344 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 294 224 L 322 204 L 350 224'/><rect x='314' y='230' width='12' height='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 294 232 L 282 228 M 296 242 L 284 244' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(313,188.1) scale(0.7)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he lost his wife, his parents, and his brother to the camps, and then wrote the book about meaning in nine days.","key":"frankl","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Frankl","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27s_Search_for_Meaning","why":"the book"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing sexual. the honest criticism is about the book. he was in auschwitz itself only a few days before he was moved on, which the book doesn't make obvious, and in the last camp he worked as a doctor, which was hard but better than most had it. some survivors and scholars, lawrence langer, who writes about holocaust testimony, among them, think the book makes survival sound like a matter of attitude, which is unfair to the millions who had the right attitude and died anyway. that's fair to say. it doesn't unmake what happened to him.","moments":["by his own telling, in 1941 he had a visa for america and let it lapse so he wouldn't leave his parents. he says he found a piece of marble at home from a destroyed synagogue, carved with part of the commandment to honor your father and mother, one of the ten commandments, and took it as his answer.","digging in a ditch at dawn, thinking about tilly without knowing if she was alive, he saw a light come on in a farmhouse on the horizon. he wrote that it felt like an answer.","a fellow prisoner told him he'd dreamed the war would end on march 30. when the day came and it didn't, the man got sick, and on march 31 he died. frankl thought losing the date is what killed him."],"name":"viktor frankl","place":"vienna, the camps, vienna again","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"everything can be taken from a man but one thing, the last of the human freedoms, to choose one's attitude in any given set of circumstances.","where":"man's search for meaning, part one"},{"q":"he who has a why to live can bear almost any how.","where":"a line from nietzsche, the german philosopher, which frankl quotes in man's search for meaning and made famous"},{"q":"between stimulus and response there is a space.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; it isn't in his books, and he probably never said it"}],"start":"man's search for meaning, part one, in one sitting. then the page about the farmhouse window again. then recollections, his short memoir, for what came after.","status":"approved","story":"a viennese psychiatrist and neurologist, born in 1905. as a teenager he wrote to sigmund freud, the father of psychoanalysis, and freud wrote back. in the thirties he set up free counseling for students around report card time, when student suicides spiked, and ran a ward for women who'd tried to kill themselves, and he kept working on the idea that what people need most is a reason.\nin 1942 he, his new wife tilly, and his parents were deported to theresienstadt, a ghetto and camp the nazis ran in czechoslovakia. his father died there. in 1944 he and tilly were sent to auschwitz, the biggest of the death camps, and from there he went on to kaufering and türkheim, work camps attached to dachau in bavaria. his mother was killed in the gas chamber at auschwitz. his brother walter died in the camps. tilly died at bergen-belsen, a camp in northern germany. he learned all of it after the americans freed him in april 1945.\nback in vienna, in 1946, he dictated man's search for meaning in nine days. he wanted it to come out anonymously, under his prisoner number. his name went on it in the end, and it's sold millions. the first half is the camps. the second half is logotherapy, the kind of therapy he built, from the greek word logos, meaning. freud had said people were driven by pleasure, and alfred adler, another viennese psychiatrist, said power. frankl said the deepest drive is the need for a reason, and the therapist's job is to help you find yours.\nhe ran the neurology department at the vienna poliklinik, a big city hospital, for twenty five years, remarried, had a daughter, kept climbing mountains, and got a pilot's license at sixty seven. he died in vienna in 1997, at ninety two.","struggle":"the manuscript of his first book was sewn into the lining of his coat when he got to auschwitz, and it was taken with everything else. in the last camp, sick with typhus, a fever carried by lice that killed huge numbers in the camps, he started rebuilding it in shorthand on scraps of paper, partly to keep himself awake at night so he wouldn't die in his sleep.\nthen he came home. tilly, his mother, his brother, all dead. he wrote to friends that year, roughly, that he was terribly tired and terribly lonely, and that only the work kept him going. the book came out of that year as much as out of the camps.","threads":["prison","grief","war"],"years":"1905 to 1997"},{"after":"what he did with his failures was publish them. the autobiography is called the story of my experiments with truth, and it's mostly a list of experiments that didn't work. the meat he ate in secret as a teenager, which in his family was close to unthinkable. the brothel a friend took him to, where he froze and got shown the door. the night his father died. he thought hiding it was worse than doing it.\nwhat he did with the killings was fast, twice, at seventy eight, until the killing stopped in calcutta and then in delhi. it stopped. two weeks after the second fast a hindu nationalist shot him for it.","basically":"a skinny indian lawyer who figured out how to beat the british empire without firing a shot, by getting millions of ordinary people to break unfair laws on purpose and take the beating without hitting back. every protest you've seen where people sit down in the road and let themselves get dragged off runs on his method. he was also hard on the people closest to him and did something in old age that's genuinely creepy, and he'd tell you to look at all of it. take from him that you can refuse to hate someone and still refuse to obey them.","born":1869,"died":1948,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 260 230 C 290 220 320 240 350 230 S 390 225 400 230'/><path d='M 270 250 C 300 240 330 260 360 250 S 395 245 400 250'/><path d='M 20 270 L 260 270'/><circle cx='200' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 200 174 L 200 220'/><path d='M 200 220 L 184 256 M 200 220 L 218 252'/><path d='M 200 188 L 186 210 M 200 188 L 220 196'/><path d='M 222 196 L 222 270'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='140' cy='178' r='10'/><path d='M 140 192 L 140 238'/><path d='M 140 238 L 124 274 M 140 238 L 158 270'/><path d='M 140 206 L 126 228 M 140 206 L 160 214'/><circle cx='90' cy='186' r='9'/><path d='M 90 200 L 90 246'/><path d='M 90 246 L 74 282 M 90 246 L 108 278'/><path d='M 90 214 L 76 236 M 90 214 L 110 222'/><circle cx='45' cy='192' r='8'/><path d='M 45 206 L 45 252'/><path d='M 45 252 L 29 288 M 45 252 L 63 284'/><path d='M 45 220 L 31 242 M 45 220 L 65 228'/><\/g><path d='M 300 270 L 320 250 L 340 270 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='340' cy='70' r='22' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 370 70 L 380 70'/><path d='M 310 70 L 300 70'/><path d='M 340 100 L 340 110'/><path d='M 340 40 L 340 30'/><path d='M 361 91 L 368 98'/><path d='M 319 91 L 312 98'/><path d='M 361 49 L 368 42'/><path d='M 319 49 L 312 42'/><g transform='translate(240,250.5) scale(1.3)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he read a hindu scripture and jesus's big sermon side by side every day, and took the british empire apart with both.","key":"gandhi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahatma_Gandhi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Story_of_My_Experiments_with_Truth","why":"his autobiography"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"this one's real. in his late seventies he had young women, including his nineteen year old grandniece manu, sleep naked beside him, calling it a test of his celibacy, his vow to give up sex. several people close to him quit over it. and in south africa as a young man he wrote things about black africans that are flatly racist, and fought for indians to be treated better than them rather than for everyone to be treated the same. he grew out of the second one. he never gave up the first. it's on the page because he asked to be judged whole. so judge him whole.","moments":["the salt march in 1930. the british made it a crime for indians to make their own salt so they'd have to buy it taxed, and he walked two hundred and forty miles to the sea to pick up a handful of it. it took twenty four days and by the end there were tens of thousands walking with him.","he read the gita first in english, in london, because two theosophists, westerners who were into eastern religion, asked him about it and he was embarrassed not to know his own scripture.","he was the first person to be thrown out of a first class carriage and turn it into a fifty year career."],"name":"mohandas gandhi","place":"gujarat, london, south africa, india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my life is my message.","where":"his answer when a reporter asked for one"},{"q":"the sermon on the mount went straight to my heart. the gita and the sermon said the same thing.","where":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth, paraphrased"},{"q":"be the change you want to see in the world is something he never said. what he said was that if we could change ourselves, the world would change too.","where":"his collected works, the hundred volume set of everything he wrote, and a lot of posters that got it wrong"}],"start":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth. then the gita, chapter 2, which is the one he lived on, especially the verses at the end about a person who stays steady no matter what happens. then the sermon on the mount, which is matthew chapters 5 through 7 in any bible.","status":"approved","story":"a shy young lawyer who got thrown off a train in south africa in 1893, at a station called pietermaritzburg, for being brown in a first class seat, and spent a freezing night in the waiting room deciding whether to go home or fight. what he did about it took fifty years.\nhe first read the [[krishna|bhagavad gita]], the hindu scripture where the god krishna talks a prince through his terror on the morning of a battle, in london, in english, because some theosophists asked him about it and he was embarrassed not to know it. theosophists were a club of westerners who were into eastern religion. it became his daily bread. around the same time he read the sermon on the mount, the three chapters in matthew where [[jesus]] says turn the other cheek and love your enemies, and said it went straight to his heart. later, in south africa, he read [[tolstoy]]'s the kingdom of god is within you, the russian novelist's book arguing that jesus meant all that literally, and it overwhelmed him.\nput the three together and you get satyagraha, which means truth force, the whole method. you break the unjust law in the open and take whatever they do to you without hitting back. then you keep coming, until the other side can't stand what it's doing to you, and neither can anyone watching.\nhe and tolstoy wrote to each other in the last year of tolstoy's life. he named his commune in south africa, a farm where protesters and their families lived together, tolstoy farm. decades later [[king|martin luther king]] read him and went to india to see what was left.\nin 1930 he walked two hundred and forty miles to the sea to make salt because the british said he couldn't. the british taxed salt and made it a crime for indians to make their own, in a hot country where everyone sweats it out. he picked up a lump of salty mud on the beach, and people all over india started making salt too. in 1947 india was free and immediately tore itself in half along religious lines, when the british split it into mostly hindu india and mostly muslim pakistan and neighbors started killing each other by the hundreds of thousands. he fasted nearly to death to stop the killing. a hindu nationalist, a man who thought gandhi had gone soft on muslims, shot him on january 30, 1948, on his way to evening prayers.","struggle":"when he was sixteen his father was dying and gandhi, already married, as boys often were in india then, left the sickbed to go to bed with his wife. his father died while he was gone. he never forgave himself and he tells it in the autobiography without flinching. he traced the vow of celibacy he took at thirty seven back to that night.\nhis oldest son harilal became an alcoholic, converted to islam to spite him, and turned up at gandhi's funeral so wrecked that almost nobody recognized him. the man who could bring an empire to the table couldn't reach his own kid.\nand then, as independence came, india started killing itself, hindus and muslims, hundreds of thousands dead. he'd spent his life on this. he walked into the worst of it, village to village in bengal, on foot, an old man, and said he'd rather die than watch.","threads":["nonviolence","the body","family"],"years":"1869 to 1948"},{"after":"he used what time he had. he did interviews about dying, gave talks, and helped raise money for an endowed professorship at hopkins to keep the research going after him.\nhe didn't describe his death as a tragedy or pretend it wasn't coming. he said he felt more awake and more grateful than he'd ever been. that's the most useful data he left.","basically":"a johns hopkins pharmacologist who spent thirty years studying things like caffeine addiction, the least glamorous research there is, then ran the 2006 psilocybin study that got psychedelic science taken seriously again. he later gave it to people with cancer who were terrified of dying, and it helped. then he got stage four cancer himself and faced it the way his volunteers had. if you've wondered whether any of this is real or just hype, he's the careful one who checked.","born":1946,"died":2023,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 200 L 230 200 L 230 250' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 50 200 L 50 176 C 50 168 64 168 64 176 L 64 200' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='80' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 72 184 L 88 184' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 66 176 C 70 168 90 168 94 176'/><path d='M 94 190 L 160 194 L 220 196 M 130 192 L 140 176 M 160 194 L 176 178'/><circle cx='300' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 300 174 L 300 216'/><path d='M 300 216 C 280 216 270 232 286 232 L 314 232 C 330 232 320 216 300 216'/><path d='M 300 190 L 272 200 M 300 190 L 328 200'/><rect x='316' y='188' width='16' height='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 250 L 244 226 L 264 226 L 258 250 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 254 226 L 250 206 M 254 226 L 262 204'/><circle cx='250' cy='204' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='262' cy='202' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><g transform='translate(20,225.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a careful drug scientist restarted psychedelic research in 2006, then got stage four cancer and called it a call to celebration.","key":"griffiths","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roland_R._Griffiths","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"npr","url":"https://www.npr.org/2023/05/14/1175762261/cancer-patients-psychedelics-medical-spirituality","why":"the 2023 story on his own diagnosis and what he said about it"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. critics of the field say the early studies were small, that people guess whether they got the drug, and that the hype has run ahead of the evidence. he said many of those things himself. and the risks are real even in careful studies. some volunteers had hours of fear, anxiety, or paranoia, which is why the guides are there.","moments":["his 2006 study was designed partly to answer walter pahnke's good friday experiment from 1962, the harvard study where divinity students got psilocybin in a chapel.","the setup in his sessions was deliberately cozy, with a couch, eyeshades, headphones with music, two guides in the room, and flowers.","his team also ran a study using psilocybin to help people quit smoking. in the small first study, most of them quit."],"name":"roland griffiths","place":"baltimore, at johns hopkins","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"psilocybin can occasion mystical-type experiences having substantial and sustained personal meaning and spiritual significance.","where":"the title of his 2006 paper in psychopharmacology, a science journal"},{"q":"it has been a call to celebration.","where":"on his stage four diagnosis, speaking to npr, the american public radio network, may 2023"},{"q":"i'm truly grateful for the diagnosis.","where":"the same interview"}],"start":"the 2006 paper is readable if you skip the statistics. then michael pollan's how to change your mind, chapter 1, which is about him and his volunteers.","status":"approved","story":"he was a psychopharmacologist, a scientist who studies how drugs act on the mind, at johns hopkins, one of the top medical schools in america, in baltimore. for decades he did the dull, respected work, on caffeine withdrawal, sedatives, and what makes a drug addictive. he was also a longtime meditator, and he got curious about what meditators reported and whether you could study it.\nin 2006 he published the study that restarted the field. thirty six healthy adults, most of whom had never taken a psychedelic, got psilocybin in a living room style setup with a trained guide, eyeshades, and music, and a comparison drug on another day. two months later about two thirds rated the psilocybin day among the five most meaningful experiences of their lives, and about a third said it was the single most meaningful. the paper's title said psilocybin can occasion mystical-type experiences with lasting personal meaning. the careful journals ran it. nobody had done that in thirty five years.\nto measure what people went through he used the mystical experience questionnaire, a checklist going back to a 1962 harvard study, that asks things like whether you felt unity with everything and whether it felt more real than real. then his team gave psilocybin to people with life threatening cancer who were depressed and afraid. the 2016 study found the fear and depression dropped sharply and mostly stayed down six months later. in 2019 hopkins opened a whole center for psychedelic research with him running it.\nat the end of 2021 he was diagnosed with stage four colon cancer. he spent the last year and a half of his life talking about it in public, calm and grateful, and raising money for research. he died in october 2023, at seventy seven.","struggle":"he was fifty something and at the top of a respectable field when he decided to study the drugs that had wrecked careers in the sixties. colleagues thought he'd lost it. it took years to get approval, and every step had to be twice as careful as normal research because one bad headline could close it all down again.\nthen the cancer. he said the first reaction was shock, the kind anyone would have. what he said after that surprised people. he said he'd been handed the same test his volunteers had been handed, and that meditation, and his own experience with psychedelics, which he went public about in 2022, had helped him meet it.","threads":["psychedelics","dying","joy"],"years":"1946 to 2023"},{"after":"after lsd was banned he didn't go underground and didn't quit. he built something legal, taught thousands of people to guide it, and kept writing.\nwhen the research came back in the 2000s, the new scientists at johns hopkins and elsewhere were reading his old notes. he lived to see the thing he'd been doing in prague in 1960 become a front page story again. he married again in 2016, at eighty four.","basically":"a psychiatrist from communist czechoslovakia who ran supervised lsd therapy for years, first in prague and then in a state hospital in maryland, and probably watched more people on psychedelics than anyone alive. when the drug was outlawed he and his wife built a way to get somewhere similar by breathing hard to loud music for hours. picture the most experienced guide in the room, the one who's seen every kind of night and doesn't panic. if you want the long view from someone who stayed sober and took notes, he's it.","born":1931,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 250 L 250 250 L 250 240 L 60 240 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='80' cy='226' r='14'/><path d='M 94 228 L 170 230 L 236 234 M 130 229 L 150 214 M 170 230 L 190 214'/><circle cx='280' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 280 184 L 280 226'/><path d='M 280 226 C 260 226 250 242 266 242 L 294 242 C 310 242 300 226 280 226'/><path d='M 280 200 L 266 226 M 280 200 L 294 226'/><path d='M 275 175 Q 280 179 285 175'/><path d='M 320 250 L 320 196 L 360 196 L 360 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='340' cy='212' r='7'/><circle cx='340' cy='236' r='10'/><path d='M 310 176 C 302 166 302 156 310 146 M 296 184 C 284 168 284 150 296 134' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 90 200 C 100 180 120 190 130 170 C 140 150 160 160 170 140' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(334,174.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a czech psychiatrist sat through thousands of lsd sessions as the sober one in the room, and when the drug was banned he found a way in with breathing.","key":"grof","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanislav_Grof","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holotropic_Breathwork","why":"the breathwork page, including the practice he built after lsd was banned and its risks"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. critics say his maps of the mind, especially his claim that people relive their own birth, go further than the evidence. and the breathwork isn't harmless. breathing that hard for that long can bring on panic, and it's not for anyone with heart trouble, epilepsy, serious mental illness, or a pregnancy.","moments":["his first session, in 1956, involved a stroboscope, a flashing light the researchers were testing on him. he said he was catapulted out of his body.","at esalen he gave workshops next to the famous names of the human potential movement, the sixties and seventies scene of encounter groups and bodywork.","in 1977 he helped start the international transpersonal association, for people studying experiences beyond the ordinary sense of self."],"name":"stanislav grof","place":"prague, then baltimore, then big sur, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"psychedelics, used responsibly, could be for psychiatry what the microscope is for biology or the telescope is for astronomy.","where":"lsd psychotherapy, his 1980 book, and many talks, paraphrased"},{"q":"a spiritual crisis can be a breakthrough, if it's supported instead of suppressed.","where":"spiritual emergency, the 1989 book he edited with christina grof, paraphrased"},{"q":"you don't need the drug to get there. breath and music will take you a long way.","where":"his talks on holotropic breathwork, paraphrased"}],"start":"the way of the psychonaut, his 2019 two volume summing up, or for something shorter, when the impossible happens, from 2006. read spiritual emergency if someone you love is going through something psychiatry can't name.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in prague in 1931 and trained as a doctor and psychiatrist. in 1956, as a young resident, he volunteered for an lsd session in a study at his clinic, supplied by [[hofmann|albert hofmann]]'s company, sandoz, and it changed the direction of his life.\nat the psychiatric research institute in prague in the sixties he led a program giving lsd to psychiatric patients, many sessions each, in a careful clinical setup, and wrote down everything. in 1967 he came to the united states, to johns hopkins, the big medical school in baltimore, and then became chief of psychiatric research at the maryland psychiatric research center. there he ran lsd therapy for alcoholics, heroin addicts, and people dying of cancer. between prague and maryland he said he'd been part of more than four thousand sessions.\nwhen the controlled substances act of 1970 put lsd in schedule i, the strictest category in us drug law, and research dried up in the early seventies, he moved to esalen, a retreat center on the cliffs of big sur in california, and lived there as scholar in residence from 1973 to 1987. there he and his wife christina worked out holotropic breathwork, which means breathing toward wholeness. you breathe fast and deep for a long time, lying down, with loud music and a partner sitting with you. people report experiences a lot like the ones he'd seen with lsd.\nhis big idea is that what psychiatry calls a breakdown is sometimes a breakthrough trying to happen, and that people in that state need a guide more than a sedative. he wrote more than thirty books, and he's still at it in his nineties.","struggle":"his career was cut in half twice. first by the soviet invasion of czechoslovakia in 1968, which meant he couldn't safely go home, and then by the american ban on psychedelics, which ended the maryland program in the seventies. he'd spent almost twenty years building a method, and two governments took it away.\nchristina, his wife and partner in all of it, went through what they came to call a spiritual emergency of her own, years of overwhelming states that ordinary psychiatry would have medicated. she also struggled with alcohol. they founded the spiritual emergence network in 1980 to help people in her position. she died in 2014.","threads":["psychedelics","the body","madness"],"years":"born 1931"},{"after":"there's no conversion in his story and no fall. he stayed in shiraz, wrote, outlasted the puritan, and got old. the after is what the book did once he was gone.\nsix hundred years on, on yalda, the longest night of the year, a winter party older than islam, iranian families sit up late and open his divan to read their fortunes. people quote him in ordinary conversation the way english speakers quote shakespeare without noticing. goethe built a whole book on him. the poet the clerics didn't want to bury is in every house.","basically":"a fourteenth century persian poet who wrote short love poems full of wine and longing that might be about god and might be about the bar, and six hundred years later iranians still keep his book next to the quran and flip it open to tell their fortunes. he's quoted in iran the way shakespeare is quoted in english, by people who don't know they're doing it. he also went after preachers who drank in private and scolded in public. and most of the soft, sweet hafiz quotes on your feed were written by an american in 1999.","born":1315,"died":1390,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 342 30 C 322 38 318 70 342 82 C 314 86 300 50 320 34 C 326 30 334 28 342 30 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 242 L 380 242'/><path d='M 60 242 L 60 226 M 60 226 C 30 190 44 110 60 60 C 76 110 90 190 60 226 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='190' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 190 184 L 190 226'/><path d='M 190 226 C 170 226 160 242 176 242 L 204 242 C 220 242 210 226 190 226'/><path d='M 190 200 L 170 176 M 190 200 L 210 176'/><path d='M 185 175 Q 190 179 195 175'/><path d='M 204 170 L 222 170 C 222 182 204 182 204 170 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 132 240 L 152 232 L 152 242 Z M 152 232 L 172 240 L 152 242 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 286 242 C 280 222 294 206 312 212 C 330 204 348 222 340 242' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='296' cy='216' r='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='334' cy='222' r='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(316,193.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"half the hafiz on the internet isn't hafiz, and the real one is better and meaner.","key":"hafiz","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hafez","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/hafez","why":"a short life, with some real translations"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divan_of_Hafez","why":"his book, the one families open for fortunes"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. the wine might have been real. persians have argued about it for six centuries, and he'd have liked that they couldn't settle it. the mess is ours. ladinsky's poems sold under his name, and the whole internet quoting them. read the real ones.","moments":["the story goes that when timur, the central asian conqueror known in english as tamerlane, took shiraz, he summoned hafiz and asked how a beggar poet dared give away samarkand and bukhara, the cities timur had conquered half the world to adorn, for a mole on somebody's cheek. hafiz said, sire, that kind of generosity is why i'm so poor. it almost certainly never happened. it's too good to leave out.","the last couplet of nearly every ghazal has his own name in it. hafiz, stop complaining. hafiz, don't tell anyone. the poet talking to himself in front of everybody.","people go to his tomb in shiraz, put a hand on the stone, and read to each other. young couples, old men, whole families."],"name":"hafiz","place":"shiraz, persia, which he hardly ever left","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 28 54 C 26 24 94 24 92 54 C 84 40 36 40 28 54 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 50 34 70 34 84 44 M 32 50 C 46 38 74 38 88 50' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 88 104 L 110 104 C 110 117 88 117 88 104 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 99 115 L 99 124 M 92 125 L 106 125'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"o cupbearer, bring the cup around and pass it, for love looked easy at first and then came the trouble.","where":"the first ghazal of the divan, his collected poems, paraphrased; it opens in arabic before it turns to persian"},{"q":"if that beauty of shiraz would take my heart in hand, i'd give samarkand and bukhara for the mole on that cheek.","where":"the ghazal that opens agar an turk-e shirazi, paraphrased. samarkand and bukhara were two of the richest cities in central asia"},{"q":"even after all this time the sun never says to the earth, you owe me.","where":"not hafiz. daniel ladinsky, the gift, 1999. it's on a million posters with hafiz's name under it"}],"start":"faces of love, dick davis's translations, which have the real hafiz with notes. read ladinsky too, if you like him, knowing whose poems they are.","status":"approved","story":"shams ad-din muhammad was born around 1315 in shiraz, a city in the south of what's now iran, and lived nearly his whole life there. hafiz is a title. it means someone who has the whole quran, the muslim holy book, by heart. he did, and he says in one poem he knew it in fourteen different traditional ways of chanting it.\nhe was a court poet, which meant he was paid by whoever ruled the city, and in fourteenth century shiraz that meant a string of rulers taking it from each other. one prince would drink with him and the next would close the taverns. in 1353 a hard puritan named mubariz al-din took the city and shut the wine shops, and hafiz wrote about preachers who say one thing from the pulpit and do another in private.\nwhat he left is the divan, which just means collected poems. about five hundred ghazals, a persian kind of poem, a handful of couplets that each stand on their own and all ring on the same end sound. his are about wine and love and the beloved, the one the poet longs for, and you can't tell whether the wine is wine or god or both. iranians have argued about that for six hundred years. families keep the divan next to the quran and open it at random to tell their fortunes. his tomb in shiraz is a garden, and people go at dusk and read him to each other.\ngoethe, germany's great poet, read him in german in 1814, in a translation by an austrian scholar named joseph von hammer, and it set him writing again in his sixties. the west-eastern divan, 1819, a whole book of poems written back to hafiz, is his answer. ralph waldo emerson, the american essayist, translated him into english, from the german. then in 1999 an american named daniel ladinsky published the gift, poems by hafiz. they aren't. they're ladinsky's own poems, and they're what almost everybody quotes.","struggle":"he lived through a long war for his own city, and the poets rose and fell with whoever held it. the stories say he fell out with more than one ruler and spent some time away from shiraz. his poems complain about closed taverns, jealous rivals, patrons who didn't pay, and hypocrites in turbans.\nand the clerics never trusted him. the story goes that when he died some of them didn't want him buried with prayers, because of the wine. somebody opened the divan at random, the way people still do, and the line said don't hold back your steps from hafiz's funeral, because though he's drowning in sin he's on his way to paradise. they buried him.","threads":["love","joy"],"years":"around 1315 to 1390"},{"after":"after the witch hunts people turned away from him, and he lost the standing he'd had. he spent his last years moving between seneca towns, preaching sobriety and the ceremonies to whoever would listen, less of a big man than he'd been. i know of no record that he took back what he'd done.\nwhat his followers did after him was keep the code and drop the witch hunting. the gaiwiio is still recited in longhouses, over several mornings, by people trained to carry it. the sobriety part held. a lot of haudenosaunee families today will tell you it's why they're still here.","basically":"a seneca man, one of the six nations of the haudenosaunee, the people the english called the iroquois, who spent years drinking while his whole world fell apart after the american revolution. in 1799, flat on his back and near death, he had visions, got sober, and started preaching a code that told his people to quit whiskey, keep the old ceremonies, and hold on to their land. it worked, and it's still practiced two hundred years later. it also set off witch hunts that got people killed. think of the founder of a recovery program that saved thousands, who also had a paranoid streak nobody should copy.","born":1735,"died":1815,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 30 250 L 30 170 L 130 170 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 172 L 80 132 L 140 172'/><path d='M 30 190 L 130 190 M 30 210 L 130 210 M 30 230 L 130 230' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 150 226 L 250 226 M 156 226 L 156 250 M 244 226 L 244 250' /><circle cx='166' cy='214' r='10'/><path d='M 176 216 L 236 218'/><circle cx='200' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 200 124 L 200 172'/><path d='M 200 172 L 186 206 M 200 172 L 214 206'/><path d='M 200 138 L 174 140 M 200 138 L 226 140'/><circle cx='250' cy='116' r='14'/><path d='M 250 130 L 250 178'/><path d='M 250 178 L 236 212 M 250 178 L 264 212'/><path d='M 250 144 L 234 168 M 250 144 L 266 168'/><circle cx='300' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 300 124 L 300 172'/><path d='M 300 172 L 286 206 M 300 172 L 314 206'/><path d='M 300 138 L 284 162 M 300 138 L 316 162'/><path d='M 150 250 C 150 238 170 236 176 244 L 186 240 L 188 246 L 176 250 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(80,115.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a dying seneca drunk woke up with a message from the creator, and his people still stand up and recite it in the longhouse.","key":"handsomelake","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handsome_Lake","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longhouse_Religion","why":"the longhouse religion that came from him, still practiced"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"his first vision told him witchcraft was behind his people's troubles, and he went looking for witches. he demanded confessions from people he accused. some who refused to confess were killed. this wasn't a rumor about him. it's in the record, and people at the time turned against him for it.\na man who'd just found his own way out of the dark decided the dark lived in his neighbors. that's the part nobody should copy.","moments":["quakers, the pacifist christians, had come to cornplanter's town in 1798, invited by cornplanter to teach farming and trades. one of them, henry simmons, wrote down handsome lake's first vision in his journal the summer it happened.","a full recitation of the code takes three days, mornings only, and it's done at the big midwinter ceremony and at a gathering of the six nations in the fall.","cornplanter's town, where the visions happened, is under water now. the kinzua dam flooded it in 1965."],"name":"handsome lake","place":"seneca towns in western new york and on the allegheny river in pennsylvania, then onondaga","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the creator is sad about four things: whiskey, witchcraft, love charms, and medicine to end a pregnancy.","where":"the code of handsome lake, the section on the four words, as written down by the seneca anthropologist arthur c. parker in 1913, paraphrased"},{"q":"parents shouldn't beat their children. talk to them.","where":"the code of handsome lake, paraphrased"},{"q":"keep the thanksgiving ceremonies. the creator made these for you.","where":"the code of handsome lake, paraphrased"}],"start":"the code of handsome lake, the seneca prophet, arthur c. parker's 1913 version of the text. then anthony wallace's the death and rebirth of the seneca, which tells the whole story plainly, the witch hunts included.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1735 at a seneca town on the genesee river in western new york. the seneca are the westernmost of the six nations of the haudenosaunee, a league of peoples in what's now new york, and they called themselves the keepers of the western door. he was a half brother of cornplanter, a seneca war chief. the seneca fought on the british side in the american revolution, and in 1779 american troops burned their towns and crops. after the war they lost most of their land and were squeezed onto small reservations.\nby the 1790s he was living at cornplanter's town on the allegheny river in pennsylvania. whiskey came up the river by the barrel, and the towns were drowning in it. he'd been drinking hard for years. early in 1799 he got sick and stayed in bed for months. in june 1799 he collapsed, and his family thought he'd died.\nhe woke up and said messengers sent by the creator had come to him. they told him the creator was grieved by what the people were doing, whiskey and witchcraft first of all, and that he had to tell them. more visions came that summer, including a journey through the sky to see where the good and the wicked go after they die. he stopped drinking and started preaching.\nwhat he preached came to be called the gaiwiio, the good message, or the code of handsome lake. no drinking. no witchcraft. don't beat your wife, don't abandon your family, don't sell your land. keep the old thanksgiving ceremonies for the corn and the maple and the new year. it pulled a broken people back together. he died at onondaga, the league's central town near what's now syracuse, on august 10, 1815. his followers kept the code, and the longhouse religion that grew from it is still practiced by haudenosaunee people today.","struggle":"years of drinking, while his people lost a war, their towns, and their land, and white traders sold them the thing that finished the job. by 1799 he was a sick old man in bed, and nothing about him suggested anybody would ever listen to him about anything.\nand the visions themselves were frightening. the sky journey showed him punishments for drinkers and wife beaters and witches, in detail. he came back from it believing the danger was everywhere around him, and that belief is where the worst of what he did came from.","threads":["addiction","madness","the land"],"years":"around 1735 to 1815"},{"after":"after she got out, she built a life out of the thing that saved her. painting, then poems, then music, and teaching others how. she didn't write about the bad house to get even. she wrote about it to find the way through it, and then kept going.\nafter the laureateship she went back to tulsa, near where she started, and kept writing and playing. being the national poet didn't turn her into a monument. she's still showing up with a saxophone.","basically":"a muscogee poet and musician from tulsa who got out of a hard childhood through art, and in 2019 became the united states poet laureate, the country's official poet, the first native american ever to hold the job. she kept it three terms and used it to put native poets from all over the country on a map. she also plays saxophone in a band. her poems read like prayers you'd say driving at night. think of the kid in the house where it wasn't safe who found the one thing that could carry her out, and then was handed the national microphone and passed it around.","born":1951,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='150' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 150 154 L 150 202'/><path d='M 150 202 L 136 236 M 150 202 L 164 236'/><path d='M 150 170 L 172 180 M 150 166 L 172 170'/><path d='M 172 170 L 182 206 C 184 216 196 216 198 206 L 196 196' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 190 212 C 186 222 206 224 204 210' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 138 146 C 130 170 132 190 136 204 M 162 146 C 170 170 168 190 164 204' stroke-width='4'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='60' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='120' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='200' cy='30' r='3'/><circle cx='260' cy='70' r='2'/><circle cx='330' cy='36' r='3'/><circle cx='370' cy='90' r='2'/><circle cx='40' cy='110' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 350 120 C 340 110 346 96 358 98 C 348 102 346 114 356 120 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 260 200 C 260 186 320 184 326 198 L 340 172 L 352 178 L 336 202 C 330 212 264 214 260 200 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 268 208 L 266 250 M 280 210 L 282 250 M 318 210 L 316 250 M 328 206 L 330 250'/><path d='M 260 200 C 250 204 248 216 252 224'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 214 M 34 214 L 46 214'/><g transform='translate(40,199.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the first native american poet laureate picked up the saxophone at forty.","key":"harjo","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joy_Harjo","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"her site","url":"https://www.joyharjo.com/","why":"her own site, with her books and music"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/joy-harjo","why":"a life and a lot of poems"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["she started the saxophone at forty, which is late for anyone, and stuck with it. there's something in that for everybody who thinks it's too late to start the thing.","as laureate she built an online map where you can click on a place and hear a native poet from there read."],"name":"joy harjo","place":"tulsa, oklahoma, then santa fe, albuquerque, and back to tulsa","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 130 L 98 100 C 100 92 94 88 92 94' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 86 124 C 82 132 100 134 98 122' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"remember the sky that you were born under,","where":"remember, the first line, in she had some horses, 1983"},{"q":"to pray, you open your whole self to the sky, the earth, the sun, the moon.","where":"eagle poem, the opening, paraphrased"},{"q":"the horses in the poem are all the parts of one woman, the scared ones and the brave ones, and at the end they turn out to be the same horses.","where":"she had some horses, the title poem, paraphrased"}],"start":"she had some horses, the 1983 book, the title poem and remember. then crazy brave, her memoir.","status":"approved","story":"she was born on may 9, 1951, in tulsa, oklahoma. she's a citizen of the muscogee nation, also called the creek, a people whose homeland was alabama and georgia until the government forced them west to what's now oklahoma on the trail of tears in the 1830s. she's descended from monahwee, a muscogee leader who fought against andrew jackson.\nthe house she grew up in wasn't safe, and she's written about it plainly in her memoir crazy brave. at sixteen she left for the institute of american indian arts, a native art school in santa fe, to be a painter. at the university of new mexico she turned to poems. she finished there in 1976 and got a master's in writing at the university of iowa in 1978. her first book, the last song, came out in 1975, and she had some horses in 1983.\nat forty she picked up the alto saxophone. she's played in bands ever since, first one called poetic justice, and put out several albums, reading and singing and playing, because to her the poems were always music.\nin 2019 she was named the twenty third poet laureate of the united states, the first native american to hold the job, and served three terms, until 2022. her project was a map of native poets and poems across the country, called living nations, living words. since then she's won the bollingen prize and the frost medal, two of the biggest prizes in american poetry. she lives in tulsa.","struggle":"the childhood. crazy brave is about growing up in a house where violence was part of the weather, and about a girl who felt everything too much and had nowhere to put it. she got out at sixteen, and the art school was the door.\nand the long middle, which she's also written about, of trying to make a life as a young mother, a poet, and a native woman in a country that didn't expect any of those to amount to anything.","threads":["joy","family","the land"],"years":"born 1951"},{"after":"what he did with the fire was go toward other people's. during the vatican council he pushed the cardinals to drop language hoping for the conversion of the jews, and said he'd rather go to auschwitz than give up his faith. he went to selma. he went against the war. a man who'd watched his world burned for being jewish spent his american years showing up for black americans and vietnamese villagers.\nnear the end, in the interview with carl stern, he said he'd asked for wonder and got it, and that just to be is a blessing, just to live is holy. he died not long after. he never pretended the fire hadn't happened. he just wouldn't give it the last word.","basically":"a rabbi from a long line of hasidic holy men in poland who got out of warsaw six weeks before the nazis came, lost his mother and sisters to them, and ended up in new york writing books about wonder. then he marched in the front row at selma with martin luther king, and came out against the vietnam war, when plenty of people thought a rabbi should stay in his lane. picture an old-world rabbi with a huge white beard at the front of a civil rights march with a hawaiian flower lei around his neck. take from him that prayer can be something you do with your feet.","born":1907,"died":1972,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='40' cy='176' r='9'/><circle cx='66' cy='172' r='9'/><circle cx='92' cy='176' r='9'/><path d='M 40 185 L 40 250 M 66 181 L 66 250 M 92 185 L 92 250'/><\/g><circle cx='170' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 170 168 L 170 214'/><path d='M 170 214 L 154 250 M 170 214 L 188 246'/><path d='M 170 182 L 156 204 M 170 182 L 190 190'/><path d='M 158 160 C 154 190 186 190 182 160 C 176 168 164 168 158 160 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 157 148 L 148 142 M 160 142 L 154 132 M 180 142 L 186 132 M 183 148 L 192 142'/><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='160' cy='188' r='3'/><circle cx='170' cy='192' r='3'/><circle cx='180' cy='188' r='3'/><\/g><circle cx='236' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 236 168 L 236 214'/><path d='M 236 214 L 220 250 M 236 214 L 254 246'/><path d='M 236 182 L 222 204 M 236 182 L 256 190'/><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='228' cy='172' r='3'/><circle cx='236' cy='176' r='3'/><circle cx='244' cy='172' r='3'/><\/g><path d='M 352 250 L 352 216'/><rect x='334' y='204' width='36' height='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(340,182.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a hasidic rabbi from warsaw marched at selma next to king and said afterward his legs were praying.","key":"heschel","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Joshua_Heschel","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/heschel-abraham-joshua","why":"stanford's page on him and king"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. some jewish leaders thought he went too far with the catholics during the second vatican council, the big meeting in rome in the early sixties where the catholic church rethought a lot of things, including what it said about jews. some thought a rabbi had no business marching or talking about vietnam. he went anyway.","moments":["in january 1963, at a national conference on religion and race in chicago, he opened by saying the first such conference had been between pharaoh, the king of egypt, and moses, the man who told him let my people go, and that it still wasn't over.","at selma the front row wore flower leis, brought by marchers from hawaii, and the photograph of him next to king, white beard and a lei around his neck, is the one the country saw.","his phrase for where faith starts is radical amazement, the plain shock that there's anything at all. it's all through god in search of man."],"name":"abraham joshua heschel","place":"warsaw, berlin, then new york","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 44 34 C 48 22 72 22 76 34 C 66 31 54 31 44 34 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i felt my legs were praying.","where":"his notes on the selma march, in moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, a collection of his essays"},{"q":"the sabbaths are our great cathedrals.","where":"the sabbath, his short book about the day of rest, the prologue"},{"q":"i did not ask for success, i asked for wonder, and you gave it to me.","where":"his tv interview with carl stern for nbc, filmed shortly before he died, paraphrased"}],"start":"the sabbath, which is short. then god in search of man, the early chapters on wonder. then moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, his collected essays, for selma and vietnam.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in warsaw in 1907 into a long line of hasidic rebbes, named after one of them, and everyone expected him to become one. hasidism is a joyful, mystical, intensely devout kind of judaism that started in eastern europe in the 1700s. each community gathers around a rebbe, a holy man and teacher, and the job often passes from father to son. instead he went to berlin, wrote a doctorate on the hebrew prophets, the bible's fire-breathing spokesmen for god like isaiah and amos, and kept praying the way he'd been raised.\nin october 1938 the nazis threw him out of germany along with thousands of other jews who held polish papers. he got out of warsaw about six weeks before the germans invaded, went to london, and reached new york in 1940. his mother and his sisters who were still in europe were killed. he called himself a brand plucked from the fire, a line from the prophet zechariah that means a stick yanked out of the flames just before it burns up.\nfrom 1945 until he died he taught at the jewish theological seminary in new york, where conservative rabbis are trained, conservative being the middle road in american judaism between orthodox and reform. the books came fast. man is not alone, the sabbath, god in search of man, the prophets. the sabbath is about the jewish day of rest, friday sundown to saturday sundown, and says the jews built their cathedrals in time, and the seventh day is one of them.\nhe met [[king|martin luther king]] in chicago in 1963 and they became friends. in march 1965 he marched in the front row out of selma, alabama, on the march to montgomery for black americans' right to vote. he spoke against vietnam beside king. ten days before king was killed, heschel introduced him to a room full of rabbis. heschel died in his sleep on the sabbath, december 23, 1972.","struggle":"he mostly didn't write about the murders directly. he wrote about wonder, and a god who comes looking for people, and he wrote it as a man whose mother and sisters had been killed by the country where he'd earned his degree. in 1965, opening a lecture at union theological seminary, a protestant school in new york, called no religion is an island, he said he'd left warsaw six weeks before the disaster, a brand plucked from the fire where his people were burned.\nhis first years in america, at hebrew union college in cincinnati, where reform rabbis are trained, reform being the liberal, modernized branch of judaism that had dropped a lot of the old prayer customs, he was a hasid from warsaw among people who mostly didn't pray the way he did, and he was lonely there. it took him five years to get to new york.","threads":["exile","grief","nonviolence"],"years":"1907 to 1972"},{"after":"he didn't disown the drug and he didn't cheerlead it. he kept publishing chemistry, retired from sandoz in 1971, and wrote the problem child book to say plainly what he thought. real value in psychiatry and in the kind of looking the mystics do, real danger in careless use, and young people shouldn't be taking it at parties.\nat a hundred he was still giving interviews, and he lived to see the first new lsd therapy study in decades approved in switzerland, in 2007. he said the discovery hadn't been his to choose. it had found him.","basically":"a careful swiss chemist at a drug company who, in 1943, found out by accident that a compound he'd made five years earlier could knock your whole sense of reality loose. he spent the next sixty five years watching it get used for therapy, spy experiments, and the sixties, and he called it his problem child. he lived to a hundred and two. if you've ever made something that got away from you, he's the man who knew exactly how that feels.","born":1906,"died":2008,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 150 250 C 190 250 200 236 230 246 C 260 256 280 232 310 244 C 340 256 360 238 380 246'/><path d='M 30 250 L 30 170 L 110 170 L 110 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 170 L 90 140 L 102 140 L 102 170' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='46' y='190' width='18' height='18' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='76' y='190' width='18' height='18' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='190' cy='232' r='18'/><circle cx='250' cy='232' r='18'/><path d='M 190 232 L 212 206 L 240 206 L 250 232 M 212 206 L 220 232 L 190 232 M 220 232 L 240 206 M 240 206 L 238 196 L 248 194 M 212 206 L 210 200'/><circle cx='214' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 214 174 L 212 206 L 220 232 M 212 206 L 234 214 M 214 186 L 240 196'/><path d='M 240 120 C 250 110 262 124 272 112 M 180 130 C 170 118 188 110 178 100 M 290 150 C 300 140 312 150 322 138' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='330' cy='70' r='16' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 354 70 L 364 70'/><path d='M 306 70 L 296 70'/><path d='M 330 94 L 330 104'/><path d='M 330 46 L 330 36'/><path d='M 347 87 L 354 94'/><path d='M 313 87 L 306 94'/><path d='M 347 53 L 354 46'/><path d='M 313 53 L 306 46'/><g transform='translate(88,118.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a swiss chemist made lsd looking for a heart and lung drug, then rode his bike home through the most famous afternoon in drug history.","key":"hofmann","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Hofmann","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"his book, free online","url":"https://maps.org/images/pdf/books/lsdmyproblemchild.pdf","why":"lsd: my problem child, his own account, hosted by maps"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing gross i know of. he was a company chemist, and the company sent his drugs to the psychiatrists and researchers who asked, some of whom, it turned out, were working for governments doing ugly things with them. he wasn't one of them.","moments":["in august 1961 [[huxley|aldous huxley]], the novelist who'd written about mescaline, rang him at the lab out of the blue while passing through zurich and took him and his wife to lunch.","in september 1971 he met timothy leary, then a fugitive in switzerland, at a railway station snack bar in lausanne. he found him charming, sincere, and blind to the dangers.","in 1978 he co-wrote the road to eleusis with gordon wasson and a classics professor, carl ruck, arguing that the secret drink at an ancient greek religious festival had ergot in it."],"name":"albert hofmann","place":"basel, switzerland","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 96 L 92 106 L 84 124 L 108 124 L 100 106 L 100 96 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 90 96 L 102 96'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"an uninterrupted stream of fantastic pictures, extraordinary shapes with intense, kaleidoscopic play of colors.","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 1, quoting his own lab report on the accident of april 16, 1943"},{"q":"wrong and inappropriate use has caused lsd to become my problem child.","where":"lsd: my problem child, his 1979 memoir of the drug"},{"q":"it's medicine for the soul.","where":"interviews around his hundredth birthday in 2006, paraphrased"}],"start":"lsd: my problem child. the first chapter is the bicycle day story in his own words, and chapter 6 is his trip to maría sabina's town.","status":"approved","story":"he was a chemist at sandoz, a big drug company in basel, switzerland, working on ergot, a fungus that grows on rye and other grain. for centuries ergot in bread had poisoned whole villages, and drug companies were pulling useful medicines out of it. in november 1938 he made a new compound from it, number twenty five in a series, lysergic acid diethylamide, lsd-25, hoping for a drug to perk up breathing and circulation. the animal tests were boring. it went on the shelf.\nin april 1943 he had a hunch and made it again. on april 16 some of it got into him by accident, probably through his fingertips, and he had to go home in the middle of the afternoon with, as he wrote later, an uninterrupted stream of fantastic pictures behind his eyes. three days later, on april 19, he took some on purpose to find out. it hit much harder than he'd planned. wartime switzerland had no gas for cars, so he rode his bicycle home with his lab assistant alongside, sure he was losing his mind. people who take lsd now call april 19 bicycle day.\nafter that he went on with his work. in 1958 he took mushrooms from mexico, the ones [[sabina|maría sabina]] used, grown in a lab in paris from samples [[wasson|gordon wasson]] had brought back, and pulled out their active chemical and named it psilocybin. sandoz sold both drugs to researchers for years. in 1962 he went up to her town himself.\nhe watched lsd go from a psychiatry tool to a cia experiment to a street drug to a banned substance. in 1979 he wrote a book about it and called it lsd: my problem child. he kept saying, into his hundreds, that it was medicine for the soul when used with care and a disaster when used carelessly. he died at home in the swiss countryside in april 2008, at a hundred and two.","struggle":"the bicycle ride was not fun. he wrote that he thought a demon had taken him over, that the neighbor lady bringing him milk looked like a witch in a mask, and that he was afraid he'd die and leave his wife and kids without knowing what he'd done to himself. a doctor came and found nothing wrong except big pupils. by the next morning he felt fine, clear, even new.\nthe long struggle was the next thirty years. his discovery got given to people without their knowledge in the cia's secret mind control program, mkultra, and it got handed out at rock concerts. he didn't control any of it and he took it personally. he broke with timothy leary's way of doing things in writing, while still liking the man.","threads":["psychedelics","work"],"years":"1906 to 2008"},{"after":"he wrote island after the fire. it's the book where he tried to build the good version of brave new world, a society organized around attention and kindness and knowing you'll die. it's not a great novel and it's a great answer.\nand on his last afternoon, dying of cancer, too weak to talk, he wrote a note to his second wife laura asking for lsd, a hundred micrograms, as a shot. she gave it to him and read to him from the tibetan book of the dead, the same guide for the dying he'd read to maria, while the news of kennedy's assassination was on every television in the country. he went out the way he'd told maria to.","basically":"an english novelist who wrote brave new world, the 1932 book about a future where everybody's kept happy with a pill and cheap entertainment and nobody wants anything real, which still feels uncomfortably close. then he moved to los angeles, got serious about the mystics, and took a psychedelic in his living room to see what they'd seen. he's the one who first made the case, for regular readers in english, that all the religions are pointing at the same thing underneath. if you've ever said all paths go up the same mountain, he wrote the book on it.","born":1894,"died":1963,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 20 90 L 380 90 L 380 260' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 260 L 150 120 L 250 120 L 250 260' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 165 200 L 175 180 M 190 160 L 205 140 M 220 190 L 235 170' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='210' cy='150' r='12' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 230 150 L 240 150'/><path d='M 190 150 L 180 150'/><path d='M 210 170 L 210 180'/><path d='M 210 130 L 210 120'/><path d='M 224 164 L 231 171'/><path d='M 196 164 L 189 171'/><path d='M 224 136 L 231 129'/><path d='M 196 136 L 189 129'/><circle cx='120' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 120 164 L 120 212'/><path d='M 120 212 L 106 246 M 120 212 L 134 246'/><path d='M 120 178 L 106 202 M 120 178 L 146 172'/><circle cx='113' cy='150' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='129' cy='150' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 120 150 L 122 150'/><path d='M 20 200 L 150 200 M 20 230 L 150 230 M 250 200 L 380 200 M 250 230 L 380 230' opacity='.35'/><g transform='translate(300,63.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he went almost blind at sixteen, wrote brave new world, and then decided all the mystics were describing the same thing and made a book to prove it.","key":"huxley","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldous_Huxley","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Perennial_Philosophy","why":"the book that lines up all the mystics"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing gross i know of. he and his first wife maria had an open marriage, which was their business.","moments":["he taught french at eton, the most famous boys' school in england, for a year around 1918, and one of his students was eric blair, later george orwell, who wrote 1984. brave new world and 1984 had the same schoolteacher.","the perennial philosophy came out in 1945, the same year america dropped the atomic bomb on japan, from a pacifist who'd left england for california partly to get away from the coming war.","he took mescaline for the first time in may 1953 in his living room, with a researcher taking notes, and spent the afternoon looking at the folds of his trousers."],"name":"aldous huxley","place":"england, then los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the ground of all being is the same whether you get there by the christian door or the buddhist one, and the doorway isn't the room.","where":"the perennial philosophy, paraphrased from its argument"},{"q":"it's a little embarrassing that after forty five years of research the best advice i have is to be a bit kinder to each other.","where":"a late talk, widely quoted, paraphrased"},{"q":"the man who comes back through the door in the wall will never be quite the same as the man who went out.","where":"the doors of perception, his short 1954 book about taking mescaline"}],"start":"the perennial philosophy, chapter 1, which is the whole thesis. the doors of perception is an afternoon. island, his last novel, from 1962, is where he tried to build a good version of brave new world.","status":"approved","story":"an english novelist from a famous family of scientists. his grandfather was darwin's loudest defender. he was nearly blinded by an eye infection as a teenager. brave new world came out in 1932, the novel where babies are grown in bottles and adults are kept content with a happy pill called soma, and he was famous. then in 1937 he moved to california and got quietly religious in a way that fit no church.\nthe perennial philosophy, from 1945, is an anthology with his commentary. perennial as in the plant that keeps coming back every spring. he took [[eckhart|meister eckhart]], a german priest from around 1300, [[rumi]], the persian poet, the [[krishna|gita]], the hindu scripture, [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the chinese sage, the buddhist sutras, which are the buddha's recorded talks, and the christian mystics, and cut them up and laid them side by side to show they're all saying one thing. there's a ground under everything, you're not separate from it, and the whole job is to find that out. mystics, here and everywhere on these pages, are the people who want to experience god directly instead of just believing things about god. it's the book that made the idea respectable in english.\nin may 1953 he took mescaline, the drug in the peyote cactus, in his living room and wrote the doors of perception about it. the title is from william blake, the english poet, who wrote that if those doors were cleaned, we'd see everything as it is, infinite. jim morrison named his band, the doors, after the book. in 1960 he visited timothy leary, the harvard psychologist who became the face of lsd, and the whole psychedelic decade partly runs through that visit. [[ramdass|ram dass]] got to god by a road huxley helped pave.\nhe died on november 22, 1963, the same afternoon kennedy was shot in dallas, after asking his wife laura to inject him with lsd. she did. [[merton|thomas merton]], the american monk, who'd become a monk partly because of one of huxley's early books, had argued with him by letter about whether the drugs got you anywhere real.","struggle":"at sixteen an eye infection left him nearly blind for about eighteen months and he read braille, the raised-dot alphabet, and taught himself to type. his eyes never fully came back. the man who wrote the doors of perception spent his life squinting through them.\nhis first wife maria died of cancer in 1955 and he sat by the bed reading her instructions from the tibetan buddhist tradition for dying, the words you say into a dying person's ear to guide them through, telling her to go toward the light. he wrote it into island, his last novel.\nin 1961 a wildfire in the hills above los angeles took his house and every paper he'd ever kept, letters, manuscripts, forty years. he said afterward it was a clean sweep and he felt light, which is either enlightenment or shock.","threads":["psychedelics","dying"],"years":"1894 to 1963"},{"after":"the best what-happens-next story in the book isn't his own, it's what he did with peter's. peter, a fisherman and one of his closest friends, swore he'd die for him. then, the night jesus was arrested, people in a courtyard asked peter three times if he was with jesus, and three times he said he didn't know the man, before the rooster crowed at dawn. after the resurrection, on a beach, over a fire, jesus asked peter three times, do you love me. once for each denial. then he gave him the job. no lecture. that's the whole model.\nand the story about the woman caught in adultery, sleeping with a man who wasn't her husband, which under the old law could get her stoned to death. the religious men dragged her in front of him and asked what to do. he wrote in the dirt, said whoever's never fucked up can throw the first rock, and waited until the crowd left, oldest first. then he said, neither do i condemn you. go, and don't do it again. that's what he did with other people's messes, every time, and it's why the professionals wanted him dead.","basically":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel who, around the year 30, spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided, until the roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him. strip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off. if what you got in church felt like rules, go read what he actually said.","born":-4,"died":30,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 200 L 180 200 M 50 200 L 50 250 M 170 200 L 170 250' /><rect x='40' y='192' width='140' height='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><g opacity='.8'><circle cx='70' cy='168' r='10'/><circle cx='110' cy='166' r='10'/><circle cx='150' cy='169' r='10'/><\/g><path d='M 70 178 L 70 192 M 110 176 L 110 192 M 150 179 L 150 192'/><circle cx='90' cy='188' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='130' cy='186' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='260' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 260 164 L 260 210'/><path d='M 260 210 L 244 246 M 260 210 L 278 242'/><path d='M 260 178 L 246 200 M 260 178 L 280 186'/><path d='M 260 178 L 286 160 M 260 178 L 288 186' /><circle cx='345' cy='158' r='12'/><path d='M 345 172 L 345 218'/><path d='M 345 218 L 361 254 M 345 218 L 327 250'/><path d='M 345 186 L 359 208 M 345 186 L 325 194'/><path d='M 300 100 C 310 90 330 92 335 105' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(180,165.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a carpenter from nowhere who ate with the wrong people, told stories with no moral, and got executed for it.","key":"jesus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesus","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"mark, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/mar.htm","why":"the shortest gospel, in the king james version"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"none in the record. plenty in what's been done in his name, from wars and burnings to a lot of kids told they were going to hell, which he'd have had things to say about.","moments":["his first miracle, in john's gospel, was turning water into wine at a wedding in a village called cana because the party was running dry. his mother made him.","he told stories with no moral attached and walked off. a father throwing a party for the son who blew the inheritance, while the good son who stayed home stands outside furious. the story stops there. he never says whether the brother goes in.","he said the sabbath, the weekly day of rest, was made for people, not people for the sabbath, and healed on it to make the point."],"name":"jesus","place":"galilee, then jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the kingdom of god is within you.","where":"luke 17:21, which means the gospel of luke, chapter 17, verse 21. the greek can mean within you or among you, depending on the translation; both are true"},{"q":"the sabbath was made for people, not people for the sabbath.","where":"mark 2:27; read chapter 2"},{"q":"don't worry about tomorrow. tomorrow has its own problems. today has enough.","where":"matthew 6:34, paraphrased; read all of the sermon on the mount, matthew chapters 5 through 7"}],"start":"mark, the shortest of the four gospels, in one sitting. then matthew 5 through 7, the sermon on the mount. then luke 15, the three lost things, a lost sheep, a lost coin and a lost son, and the party every time one gets found.","status":"approved","story":"he came from nazareth, a farming village in galilee, the hill country in the north, that nobody had a good word for. in one of the gospels, the four books that tell his life, a man hears where he's from and says, can anything good come out of nazareth? he worked with his hands until he was about thirty. then he walked into the jordan river, where his cousin john, a wild preacher living out in the desert, was dunking people under the water as a sign they were starting over, which is what baptizing means. the story says that when jesus came up, the sky opened and a voice called him son. he came out with a job.\nfor maybe three years he walked around galilee healing people and telling stories, mostly stories. a father who runs down the road to meet the son who took his inheritance early and blew all of it; people call that one the prodigal son. a samaritan, from a people the jews of his day despised, who stops for a man beaten half to death in a ditch after a priest and a temple worker walked right past him; that's the good samaritan. workers hired at five in the afternoon paid the same as the ones who started at dawn. the stories don't explain themselves. that's the method.\nhe ate with tax collectors, locals who collected money for the roman occupiers and skimmed off the top, the most hated guys in town, and with prostitutes. he told the rich to give it away. he said the kingdom of god was inside you. he said the sabbath, the weekly day of rest in jewish law when work was forbidden, was made for people and not the other way around, and he healed people on it. the religious professionals, the temple priests and the strictest teachers, hated him for it. the romans crucified him for it, nailing him to a wooden cross outside jerusalem to die slowly in public, which is what they did to rebels and runaway slaves. his friends said they saw him alive on the third day.\neverybody on these pages who came after has a version of him. [[rohr]]'s christ, bigger than the man, showing up in everything. [[bell]]'s jesus, who's funny. [[tolstoy]]'s teacher, from the gospel tolstoy rewrote himself with the miracles cut out. [[gandhi]]'s sermon on the mount, the three chapters in matthew where jesus says turn the other cheek and love your enemies. [[ramakrishna]]'s vision of him walking up the road. the man himself wrote nothing down.","struggle":"in a garden called gethsemane, the night before he died, he asked god to take the cup away, meaning what was coming for him. sweating, the text says, like drops of blood, with his friends asleep. he asked three times. he didn't get it.\non the cross he quoted psalm 22, an old hebrew prayer-song from the bible's book of psalms, that starts my god, my god, why have you left me. people spend a lot of energy explaining that one away. don't. the man said it.\nhis own family, in mark 3, came to take him home because they thought he was out of his mind. he wept at the grave of his friend lazarus. he was tired enough to sleep through a storm in a boat while the fishermen with him thought they were going to drown.","threads":["dying","love"],"years":"around 4 bc to around 30"},{"after":"after the whirlwind he says, i'd heard about you with my ears. now my eye sees you. and he sits down in the dust. that's not defeat. he asked for god and got god, and that turned out to be the answer, not the explanation he'd been demanding.\nand then the part everybody skips. god tells the friends to go ask job to pray for them, because they got it wrong and he got it right. and job does. he prays for the three men who spent thirty chapters telling him he deserved it. the book says his fortunes turned when he prayed for his friends. not before. read that twice.","basically":"the main character of a book in the bible, probably a story and not a history, about a good man who loses everything, his ten kids included, and then breaks out in sores from head to foot. his friends spend most of the book telling him he must have done something to deserve it. he refuses to buy it and demands that god explain himself, and god shows up and doesn't. if anyone's ever told you everything happens for a reason while you were at the bottom, job is on your side, and so, the book says, is god.","born":-550,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='110' cy='180' r='14'/><path d='M 110 194 L 110 236'/><path d='M 110 236 C 90 236 80 252 96 252 L 124 252 C 140 252 130 236 110 236'/><path d='M 110 210 L 96 236 M 110 210 L 124 236'/><path d='M 96 236 L 84 230 L 90 224' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 60 250 C 80 240 100 246 120 250 S 150 240 170 250' opacity='.5'/><g opacity='.8'><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='10'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 212'/><path d='M 200 212 L 186 246 M 200 212 L 214 246'/><path d='M 200 178 L 186 202 M 200 178 L 226 172'/><circle cx='240' cy='156' r='10'/><path d='M 240 170 L 240 218'/><path d='M 240 218 L 226 252 M 240 218 L 254 252'/><path d='M 240 184 L 226 208 M 240 184 L 266 178'/><circle cx='280' cy='152' r='10'/><path d='M 280 166 L 280 214'/><path d='M 280 214 L 266 248 M 280 214 L 294 248'/><path d='M 280 180 L 266 204 M 280 180 L 306 174'/><\/g><path d='M 205 130 C 200 118 214 112 214 122 M 245 136 C 240 124 254 118 254 128 M 285 132 C 280 120 294 114 294 124' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 340 250 C 320 220 360 200 340 170 C 325 145 355 130 345 100 C 340 85 352 70 350 55' /><path d='M 330 200 C 350 190 360 200 355 215 M 335 130 C 350 120 360 130 356 145' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(60,223.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he lost everything for no reason, his friends spent thirty chapters explaining why, and god showed up and said they were full of it.","key":"job","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Job_(biblical_figure)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Job","why":"the book itself, and what scholars make of it"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/job.htm","why":"the book of job in the king james version"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the book opens with god making a bet with the accuser and letting a man's children die to win it, and then giving him ten new children at the end like that fixes it. that's the mess and it's god's, not job's. the book knows it. it leaves it there on purpose.","moments":["the friends sat with him on the ground for seven days and seven nights without saying a word, because they saw his suffering was very great. it's the best thing anybody does in the book. then they opened their mouths.","his wife gets one line: curse god and die. she'd lost ten children too. nobody writes her a chapter.","god's speech from the whirlwind spends a lot of time on the hippopotamus. behemoth, the text calls it. the answer to why do good people suffer includes an extended riff on how great hippos are. sit with that."],"name":"job","place":"the land of uz, wherever that was","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 L 30 100 L 45 106 L 60 94 L 75 106 L 90 100 L 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.5'><circle cx='34' cy='112' r='1.5'/><circle cx='42' cy='120' r='1.5'/><circle cx='84' cy='116' r='1.5'/><circle cx='90' cy='124' r='1.5'/><circle cx='50' cy='30' r='1.5'/><circle cx='72' cy='27' r='1.5'/><\/g><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"naked i came from my mother's womb and naked i'll go back. the lord gave and the lord took away.","where":"job 1:21, which means the book of job, chapter 1, verse 21; read the first two chapters"},{"q":"i wish i'd never been born.","where":"job 3, the whole chapter, paraphrased; it's the first thing he says after the silence"},{"q":"you're not the ones who spoke rightly about me. job is.","where":"god to the friends, job 42:7, paraphrased"}],"start":"job 1 and 2, then skip to 38 through 42, where god finally talks. then go back and read the friends if you want to hear every bad thing anyone's ever said to you at a funeral.","status":"approved","story":"job was rich and good and had ten kids. up in heaven god pointed him out to the accuser, the satan, which in hebrew just means the accuser, a kind of prosecutor in god's court, not yet the devil of later stories. the accuser said job was only good because he had it good. take it away and he'll curse you to your face. god let him try. in one day raiders took his animals, fire from the sky burned up his sheep, and a wind brought the house down on his kids and killed all ten. then the accuser covered him in sores. his wife told him to curse god and die. he sat down in the ashes and scraped his sores with a broken piece of pot.\nthree friends came and sat with him on the ground in silence for a week, which is the best thing anybody does in the book. then they opened their mouths. for thirty chapters they explain that he must have done something. suffering is punishment. god is fair. look harder. job says no. he never did anything. he wants god to show up and answer for it.\ngod shows up out of a whirlwind and doesn't answer. he asks job where he was when the earth was made. he shows him the ocean, the stars, the war horse that laughs at fear, the hippo, and leviathan, the sea monster nobody can hook. it's the same move as [[krishna]] showing arjuna what he really is in the gita, the hindu scripture. no explanation. just scale.\nthen god turns to the friends and says he's angry with them, because they didn't speak the truth about him the way his servant job did. the guy who yelled at god got it right. the guys who defended god got it wrong. read that twice.","struggle":"the struggle is the whole book. chapter 3 is a man cursing the day he was born, wishing the night he was conceived had been wiped off the calendar. it's the first thing he says after a week of silence and it's not polite.\nthen thirty chapters of his friends explaining that he must have deserved it, and him saying no, over and over, and demanding that god show up and answer for himself. he doesn't get comfort. he gets a whirlwind.","threads":["grief","anger","doubt"],"years":"the book is maybe 2500 years old"},{"after":"what he did with toledo was write it as a love poem. in the dark night a lover slips out of a sleeping house at night to meet the beloved, and the dark is what gets her out the door unseen. he spent years afterward writing commentaries saying the dark is where god works on you while you can't feel a thing.\nwhat he did with úbeda was die there, gently. the story goes that the prior who'd made it hard came around before the end and asked his pardon. john had never given him anything to forgive.","basically":"a tiny, dirt-poor spanish friar in the 1500s who helped [[teresaavila|teresa of avila]] drag their order back to a hard, simple life, and got kidnapped for it by his own brothers, locked in a closet-sized cell, and whipped. in the dark he made up love poems to god in his head, then escaped out a window on a rope of torn blankets. the phrase dark night of the soul comes from him, and he meant something more exact than a bad week: the long stretch where god goes silent and you keep going anyway. if you've ever felt nothing where you used to feel everything, he drew the map.","born":1542,"died":1591,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 272 C 80 262 140 282 200 272 S 320 262 380 272'/><path d='M 150 272 L 150 50 L 250 50 L 250 272' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 50 L 150 38 L 164 38 L 164 50 M 186 50 L 186 38 L 200 38 L 200 50 M 236 50 L 236 38 L 250 38 L 250 50'/><rect x='214' y='92' width='14' height='22' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 221 114 C 224 150 218 190 222 232'/><circle cx='222' cy='140' r='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='220' cy='170' r='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='221' cy='205' r='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='234' cy='176' r='8'/><path d='M 234 184 L 234 212 M 234 190 L 222 178 M 234 190 L 223 188 M 234 212 L 226 228 M 234 212 L 242 226'/><circle cx='330' cy='70' r='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='322' cy='64' r='17' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'><circle cx='60' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='100' cy='110' r='2'/><circle cx='300' cy='140' r='2'/><circle cx='360' cy='180' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(208,28.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his own order kidnapped him and locked him in a cell the size of a closet for nine months, and he came out with some of the best poems in spanish.","key":"johncross","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_the_Cross","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Night_of_the_Soul","why":"the poem and the phrase"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the story goes that when teresa first got him she told a friend she now had a friar and a half to start the men's reform. john was the half.","while he was confessor at the convent in avila, the priest the nuns told their sins to, he made a small ink sketch of christ on the cross seen from above, the way god would see it. in 1951 salvador dalí, the surrealist painter with the waxed mustache, painted christ of saint john of the cross from it.","he died just after midnight on december 14, 1591, as the bell rang for matins, the night prayers the friars sang, and is supposed to have said he'd be singing them in heaven."],"name":"john of the cross","place":"castile, a cell in toledo, andalusia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 33 42 40 38 46 40 L 44 52 Z M 88 50 C 87 42 80 38 74 40 L 76 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 98 98 L 98 124 M 90 106 L 106 106' stroke-width='3'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"in the evening of life we will be judged on love.","where":"sayings of light and love, a little collection of his short sayings, paraphrased"},{"q":"where there is no love, put love, and you will draw love out.","where":"a letter to a carmelite nun, 1591, paraphrased"},{"q":"on a dark night, kindled in love with yearnings, oh happy chance, i went out unseen.","where":"the dark night, the poem, first stanza"}],"start":"the dark night, the poem first, which is eight short stanzas. then the spiritual canticle. the commentaries, his long prose explanations, are for later. kieran kavanaugh's translation.","status":"approved","story":"juan de yepes was born in 1542 in a small town in castile, the heart of old spain. his father died when he was small and the family was desperately poor. he grew up in medina del campo, worked in a hospital, studied with the jesuits, an order of scholarly priests, and at twenty one joined the carmelites, an order of friars and nuns that traced itself back to hermits on mount carmel in the holy land. by his day a lot of their houses had gone comfortable. he was tiny, under five feet by most accounts.\nin 1567, newly ordained and thinking about leaving for the carthusians, the strictest monks around, who live alone in cells in silence, he met [[teresaavila|teresa of avila]]. she was fifty two and trying to take the carmelite nuns back to the poor, hard, prayerful life they'd started with, and she wanted friars to do the same. she talked him out of leaving. in 1568 he and another friar opened the first house of the reform for men, in a farmhouse at duruelo, a hamlet in the middle of nowhere.\nthe reformers were called the discalced, which just means shoeless, because they went in rough sandals as a sign of being poor. the carmelites who hadn't signed on hated the reform. in december 1577 a group of them seized him in avila, took him to their friary in toledo, and locked him in a tiny cell with one slit of light high up. they brought him out to be whipped in front of the other friars. he was there about nine months, and he composed most of the spiritual canticle in his head, a long love poem between a woman and her lover, modeled on the song of songs, the bible's book of love poetry.\nin august 1578 he cut his blankets into strips, tied them together, and went out a window at night. he hid with the discalced nuns in toledo until it was safe. the dark night, the poem that gave us the phrase dark night of the soul, is one of his, and he wrote a long commentary on it later explaining it line by line. he died in 1591 at úbeda, pushed out of every post by his own reformed brothers.","struggle":"toledo was nine months in the dark, through winter into summer, in the same clothes, with lice, on bread and water and scraps of sardine. they whipped him in the dining hall while the friars ate. the friars talked outside his door about how the reform was finished.\nthe end was the same thing in slow motion. in 1591 the reform he'd helped build, now run by a hard man named nicolás doria, stripped him of his posts, and there was talk of sending him off to mexico. he got sick, went to úbeda for treatment, and the prior there, the man in charge of the house, resented him and made his last months hard. he died with his leg covered in sores.","threads":["prison","silence","love"],"years":"1542 to 1591"},{"after":"there isn't one. the book ends on god's question and jonah doesn't answer. no repentance scene, no lesson learned, no line about him walking back into the city. as far as we know he's still sitting outside it in the heat.\nthat's the point. the question's left open because it's aimed at whoever's reading. jewish tradition reads the whole book aloud on the afternoon of yom kippur, the day of atonement, the holiest day of the jewish year, when everyone fasts and asks to be forgiven. it's a good day to be asked whether you want your enemies forgiven too.","basically":"the prophet in a four chapter bible story who gets told to go warn the capital of the empire everyone hates, buys a ticket the other way, gets thrown overboard and swallowed by a huge fish, finally goes and preaches, and the whole city actually listens and god spares them, and he's furious about it. it's a comedy, and the joke is on him. picture being told to go help the country you hate most, and then sulking when it works. he's for anyone who, if they're honest, wants mercy for themselves and fire for everybody else.","born":null,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='70' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 96 60 L 106 60'/><path d='M 44 60 L 34 60'/><path d='M 70 86 L 70 96'/><path d='M 70 34 L 70 24'/><path d='M 88 78 L 95 85'/><path d='M 52 78 L 45 85'/><path d='M 88 42 L 95 35'/><path d='M 52 42 L 45 35'/><path d='M 280 250 L 280 206 L 300 206 L 300 190 L 320 190 L 320 210 L 340 210 L 340 196 L 362 196 L 362 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='140' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 140 192 L 140 234'/><path d='M 140 234 C 120 234 110 250 126 250 L 154 250 C 170 250 160 234 140 234'/><path d='M 140 208 L 126 234 M 140 208 L 154 234'/><path d='M 134 189 Q 140 184 146 189'/><path d='M 190 250 C 200 200 180 140 140 128'/><path d='M 176 150 C 186 150 190 162 184 170 C 178 164 174 158 176 150 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 150 130 C 144 138 146 150 140 156 C 134 148 138 136 150 130 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 192 196 C 204 196 206 210 198 214 C 192 208 190 202 192 196 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 196 250 C 199 244 203 250 206 244 C 209 250 213 244 216 248'/><g transform='translate(343,174.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"god told him to go preach to the enemy, he ran the other way, and when it worked he was furious.","key":"jonah","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Jonah","why":"the four chapter book itself, explained"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"cowardice first. he ran, and nearly took a ship full of sailors down with him, and they behaved better than he did the whole time. then the sulk. a city of a hundred and twenty thousand people repents and lives, and he's angry enough to want to die about it, and then angrier about a plant. the book isn't hiding him. he's the joke, and the joke is on anybody who's ever wanted god to hit the people they hate.","moments":["the sailors in chapter 1 are pagans, men who worship other gods, and they come off better than the prophet. they pray, they try to save him, and at the end they're making vows to his god.","the hebrew says a great fish. the whale came later. matthew's gospel, written in greek, uses a word for a sea monster, and the king james bible, the english translation from 1611, made it a whale.","when people asked [[jesus]] for a sign he said they'd get none but the sign of jonah, three days in the heart of the earth, and that nineveh repented and they wouldn't. matthew 12.","the king of nineveh put the animals in sackcloth too. the cows repented. it's supposed to be a little funny."],"name":"jonah","place":"israel, a ship, a fish, nineveh","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 L 30 100 L 45 106 L 60 94 L 75 106 L 90 100 L 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 42 30 C 48 18 62 18 60 30 C 54 28 48 28 42 30 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 42 30 L 36 36'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i knew you were gracious and merciful, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love.","where":"the book of jonah, 4:2, said as a complaint, and the reason he ran"},{"q":"it is better for me to die than to live.","where":"jonah 4:3, because a city was spared"},{"q":"i do well to be angry, angry enough to die.","where":"jonah 4:9, about the plant"}],"start":"the book of jonah, one of the short books near the end of the old testament, all four chapters, which takes about ten minutes. then chapter 4 again.","status":"approved","story":"jonah, son of amittai, a prophet, someone who carries a message from god. god told him to go to nineveh, the great city of assyria, the empire that was the terror of the region, in what's now northern iraq, and cry out against it. he went down to joppa, a port on the coast, and paid for a ship to tarshish, probably in spain, which was about as far the other way as a ship went.\na storm came up. the sailors prayed to their gods and threw the cargo over. jonah was asleep below deck. the sailors cast lots, a kind of drawing straws, to find out whose fault the storm was, and it fell on him. he told them to throw him in, and they tried to row for shore first rather than do it. then they did it, and the sea went quiet, and a great fish swallowed him. three days and three nights. he prayed from inside it and it threw him up on dry land.\nthe second time god said go, he went. he walked into nineveh and said one sentence, forty days and nineveh will be overthrown. the whole city repented, from the king down. the king sat in ashes and put on sackcloth, rough goat-hair cloth people wore to show they were sorry, and put even the cattle in sackcloth. god saw it and didn't destroy them.\nthat's where the book should end and doesn't. jonah went out past the city and sat down to watch, still hoping. god grew a plant to shade him and jonah loved the plant. god sent a worm to kill it and a hot east wind, and jonah said he'd rather die. then god asks, you cared about a plant you didn't even grow, and shouldn't i care about nineveh, with a hundred and twenty thousand people who don't know their right hand from their left, and all those animals. the book ends on the question. jonah never answers.","struggle":"the prayer from inside the fish, in chapter 2, is the bottom. the water closed over him, weeds wrapped around his head, he went down to the roots of the mountains. it's the most sincere he gets in the whole book, and it comes while he's being digested.\nand then the thing he can't beat, which is that god is kinder than he wants god to be. he says it straight out in chapter 4. he ran because he knew nineveh would be forgiven and he didn't want it forgiven. assyria would later wipe out the northern kingdom of israel, ten of the twelve tribes, and carry its people off, in 722 bc. he'd rather die than watch them get mercy.","threads":["anger","forgiveness"],"years":"set in the eighth century bc, written down later"},{"after":"she didn't found anything or go anywhere. she stayed in the room and thought about what she'd seen for twenty years, and the long version is what twenty years of thinking looks like. people came to the street window for advice, and she gave it.\nat the very end of the book she says she'd wanted to know what it all meant, and more than fifteen years after the showings she got an answer. love was his meaning. who showed it to you? love. what did he show you? love. why? for love. it took her fifteen years to get one word, and she thought that was about right.","basically":"an englishwoman in the 1300s who nearly died of an illness at thirty, saw sixteen visions of jesus on what she thought was her deathbed, and then spent the rest of her life shut in a single room on the side of a church, thinking about what she'd seen. she wrote the first book in english we know was written by a woman, and the line everybody knows from it is all shall be well. she lived through the black death, the plague that killed a third or more of europe, so it isn't a greeting card. think of the longest lockdown you can imagine, by choice, for decades, and what came out of it was hope.","born":1343,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 70 L 80 70 L 80 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 L 44 62 L 50 70 L 56 62 L 62 70 L 68 62 L 74 70 L 80 62'/><path d='M 80 250 L 80 140 L 172 140 L 172 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 76 142 L 126 110 L 176 142'/><path d='M 172 250 L 172 150 L 270 172 L 270 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 168 148 L 278 174'/><rect x='194' y='184' width='44' height='32' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 201 208 C 197 184 227 184 223 208' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='212' cy='200' r='9'/><path d='M 220 210 L 228 204'/><circle cx='231' cy='202' r='3.5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 188 216 L 262 216'/><circle cx='340' cy='70' r='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 362 70 L 372 70'/><path d='M 318 70 L 308 70'/><path d='M 340 92 L 340 102'/><path d='M 340 48 L 340 38'/><path d='M 355 85 L 362 92'/><path d='M 325 85 L 318 92'/><path d='M 355 55 L 362 48'/><path d='M 325 55 L 318 48'/><g transform='translate(240,195.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she spent her life walled into one room beside a church, and came out with all shall be well.","key":"julian","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_of_Norwich","why":"the plain overview of her life and the cell"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52958","why":"revelations of divine love on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["around 1413 margery kempe came to julian's window for advice. margery was a townswoman from king's lynn, a mother of fourteen, who went on pilgrimage as far as jerusalem and sobbed out loud in church so often that people wanted her gone. julian told her to trust what she'd been given and not be afraid of what people said. margery put it in her own book, which is often called the first autobiography in english.","the ancrene wisse, an old rulebook for anchoresses, allowed them one animal, a cat, for the mice. so every modern picture of julian has a cat. nobody knows if she had one.","the church of st julian was bombed in 1942 and rebuilt, with a room on the side where the cell was. people go and sit in it."],"name":"julian of norwich","place":"norwich, england, in one room","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 34 70 C 36 84 44 92 60 92 C 76 92 84 84 86 70 L 88 108 L 32 108 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well.","where":"revelations of divine love, her book, the long version, chapter 27"},{"q":"sin is behovely, but all shall be well.","where":"the long version, chapter 27. behovely means something like necessary, or fitting"},{"q":"as truly as god is our father, so truly is god our mother.","where":"the long version, chapter 59, paraphrased into modern english"}],"start":"revelations of divine love, the long version, chapters 5 and 27. start with the hazelnut. a modern translation, like elizabeth spearing's.","status":"approved","story":"we don't know her name. julian comes from the church of st julian in norwich, a city in the east of england, where she lived as an anchoress. an anchoress was a woman enclosed for life in a small room built onto the side of a church, to pray. hers had one window into the church, so she could see the altar, and one onto the street, where people could come and talk to her. she chose it. the service that put anchoresses in their cells borrowed from the service for the dead, because as far as the world was concerned she was gone.\nshe grew up through the black death, the plague that killed somewhere between a third and half of everyone in europe. it hit norwich when she was a small child and came back more than once. in may 1373, when she was thirty and a half, she got so sick that a priest came to be with her at the end and held a crucifix, a cross with jesus's body on it, in front of her face. over the next hours she had sixteen showings, what she called revelations of divine love, visions of jesus on the cross and things shown to her about god.\nin one of them she was shown something small, the size of a hazelnut, lying in the palm of her hand, and understood that it was everything that is made. she wondered how it could last, it was so small. the answer was that it lasts because god loves it.\nshe wrote it down twice. a short version soon after, and a long one twenty years or so later, after thinking about it the whole time. it's the first book we know of in english written by a woman. she was still alive in 1416, when someone left her money in a will. after that, nothing.","struggle":"she asked for it. as a young woman she prayed to see the crucifixion as if she'd been standing there, and for a sickness at thirty so bad she'd think she was dying. she got it, and it nearly killed her.\nand then, the same day, she doubted it. a religious man came by and she told him she'd been raving. he laughed. then she told him the crucifix had seemed to bleed, and he went quiet and serious, and she was ashamed that she'd called it raving to a man who believed her. that night she dreamed the devil had her by the throat. she wrote all of that down too, against herself.","threads":["dying","silence"],"years":"around 1343 to sometime after 1416"},{"after":"he kept weaving. that's the whole of it. there's no conversion and no crisis in the legend. the loom is in the songs because it was what he did with his hands while the songs came, and he never pretended the songs made him better than the loom.\nthe flowers under the sheet that covered his body are the after. both sides got half. he'd have laughed at that too.","basically":"a weaver in fifteenth century india, a guy who ran a loom all day in a holy city packed with priests, who made up songs telling the hindu priests and the muslim clerics they'd both lost the plot. the songs are short and rude, and village people still sing them five hundred years later. think of a guy on a factory line who writes the sharpest lyrics in town and roasts every church and mosque on the block, and then when he dies every one of them wants the body. if you've ever felt closer to god doing the dishes than in a pew, he's with you.","born":1440,"died":1518,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 150 L 180 150 L 180 250' /><path d='M 60 170 L 180 170 M 60 190 L 180 190 M 60 210 L 180 210 M 60 230 L 180 230' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 80 150 L 80 250 M 100 150 L 100 250 M 120 150 L 120 250 M 140 150 L 140 250 M 160 150 L 160 250' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='230' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 230 174 L 230 216'/><path d='M 230 216 C 210 216 200 232 216 232 L 244 232 C 260 232 250 216 230 216'/><path d='M 230 190 L 202 200 M 230 190 L 258 200'/><path d='M 225 165 Q 230 169 235 165'/><path d='M 180 230 C 240 250 280 220 320 240 S 380 250 400 240'/><path d='M 300 228 C 306 220 320 220 326 228 C 320 236 306 236 300 228 Z M 326 228 L 334 222 M 326 228 L 334 234' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='306' cy='227' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 310 210 C 312 204 316 204 318 210' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(72,123.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a muslim weaver who mocked the hindus and the muslims equally, and both of them claimed his body when he died.","key":"kabir","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabir","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the songs, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519","why":"songs of kabir, tagore's 1915 translation, public domain"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. the dates and half the stories are legend, and people kept writing new songs and putting his name on them for centuries, the way every clever quote online gets pinned on einstein. pretend they're all his; the point holds.","moments":["the story of how he got a guru: ramananda, a famous hindu teacher in varanasi, wouldn't take a muslim boy as a student. so kabir lay down before dawn on the ghat, the stone steps down into the ganges where people bathe, and ramananda, coming down in the dark to bathe, stepped on him and cried out ram, ram, a name of god, the way you'd say oh god when you trip over something. kabir got up and said that's my mantra now, the holy word you repeat to pray, and you gave it to me. ramananda kept him.","there are more of his verses in the guru granth sahib, the sikh holy book, than from anyone else who wasn't a sikh guru. the sikhs decided the weaver was theirs too.","five hundred years later his songs are still sung in villages across north india by people who can't read them, the way you know songs by heart you've never once seen written down."],"name":"kabir","place":"varanasi, india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 C 34 30 86 30 90 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 L 90 50'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 20 118 C 30 108 46 108 56 118 C 46 128 30 128 20 118 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i laugh when i hear that the fish in the water is thirsty.","where":"songs of kabir, the english version by rabindranath tagore, the indian poet who won the nobel prize in 1913, and robert bly's american reworking of it"},{"q":"if god is in the mosque, then who owns the rest of the world?","where":"his songs, paraphrased; the bijak, the book of his songs kept by his own followers, has the sharpest versions"},{"q":"the breath inside the breath. look there.","where":"a phrase that runs through his songs, in robert bly's rendering"}],"start":"the kabir book, robert bly's versions, which is short and loose. then the bijak, the collection his own followers kept, if you want the sharp edges. the hindi songs are still sung across north india and you can find recordings.","status":"approved","story":"he was a weaver in varanasi, the holiest city in hinduism, on the ganges river, where pilgrims come to bathe and the dying come to be burned on the stone steps by the water. he was raised muslim, in a family of weavers near the bottom of the caste system. caste is the old hindu order that ranks every family at birth and keeps them there for life, deciding who you can marry and who you can eat with. the story says he took a hindu guru anyway, a spiritual teacher, and he wrote songs in plain hindi, the everyday language, that peasants could sing.\nhe had no patience for priests of any kind. the brahmin, the hindu priest born into the top caste, wears a sacred thread over his shoulder to show it. kabir asked him, if you're so special, why weren't you born some other way than the rest of us? the mullah, the muslim cleric, climbs the tower of the mosque and shouts the call to prayer. kabir asked whether he thought god was deaf. god wasn't in the building, he said.\nthe songs say the thing you're looking for is already inside you, and every ritual you do to find it is a trip in the wrong direction. he laughs at the fish in the water who's thirsty, which is you, swimming in god and complaining you can't find a drink. you'll find god in the breath inside the breath, he says, not in the temple or the mosque.\nhe's in the guru granth sahib, the holy book of the sikhs, a religion founded around 1500 in punjab, in northern india, by a man named guru nanak. he's in there more than any other writer who wasn't one of the sikh gurus. hindus call him a saint. muslims call him a sufi, one of their mystics. he called himself a weaver.\nthe story goes that when he died the hindus wanted to burn the body, which is how hindus do it, and the muslims wanted to bury it, which is how muslims do it. while they argued, somebody lifted the sheet and found only flowers. they split them. the hindus burned their half and the muslims buried theirs. that's the whole point of him in one story.","struggle":"he was a low caste weaver, near the bottom of the ladder, in a city run by brahmins, the hindu priests, and mullahs, the muslim clerics, and he told both of them their god wasn't in the building. the stories have him hauled in front of the sultan, the muslim king in delhi, and mocked in the street. he wrote about it with the shrug of a man who'd already decided the argument was beneath him.\nthe songs have loneliness in them too. in some he's a bride sitting up all night for a husband who doesn't come, and the husband is god. in others the soul is a swan, and he keeps asking it where it came from and where it's going to fly. he wasn't only laughing.","threads":["work"],"years":"around 1440 to 1518, nobody's sure"},{"after":"there wasn't a good after. he never got sober. he never made it right with jan. he took care of his mother after her stroke, and married stella sampas, the sister of his boyhood friend, partly so there'd be someone to look after her. that's the whole of the last chapter, and it's not a redemption.\nwhat he left behind is the part of him that was real. the sutras he wrote in notebooks, the life of the buddha, the dharma bums. near the end he told interviewers he was a catholic and always had been. both were true. the tragedy is he couldn't live in either one.","basically":"a working class kid from a french canadian family in a massachusetts mill town who wrote on the road, the book that made a whole generation want to quit everything and drive west. then he couldn't handle being famous and drank himself to death at forty seven. in between he fell hard for buddhism, and never stopped being catholic. he more or less wrote the script for every kid who's backpacked through asia to find themselves, and he couldn't follow it. read him for the hunger, and read what the hunger did to him.","born":1922,"died":1969,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='70' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 70 192 L 70 234'/><path d='M 70 234 C 50 234 40 250 56 250 L 84 250 C 100 250 90 234 70 234'/><path d='M 70 208 L 56 234 M 70 208 L 84 234'/><rect x='112' y='196' width='30' height='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 96 222 L 150 222 L 156 250 L 90 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'><circle cx='104' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='114' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='124' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='134' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='144' cy='238' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 156 244 L 186 244 C 196 244 196 232 188 232 C 180 232 182 244 194 244 L 262 244 L 262 250 L 156 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 250 L 320 130 L 390 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='308' y='112' width='24' height='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 302 114 L 320 100 L 338 114'/><path d='M 40 90 C 50 86 60 94 70 90' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(320,83.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he typed the book that sent a generation onto the highway, then drank himself to death in his mother's living room at forty seven.","key":"kerouac","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Kerouac","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/jack-kerouac","why":"his life, and the poems people forget he wrote"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Dharma_Bums","why":"the buddhist novel"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"in 1952 his second wife, joan haverty, had a daughter, jan, and he said for years she wasn't his. a court finally made him pay a little support in the early sixties, and he met her twice in his life. she grew up and became a writer and died young, in 1996.\nthe drink killed him. and at the end he'd turned into a bitter, angry man who railed against hippies, the left, and the kids who'd read him, said ugly things about jews, and showed up drunk in 1968 on firing line, the tv show hosted by william buckley, the best known conservative in america. the man who wrote about the holy road ended up shouting at it from the couch.","moments":["the scroll of on the road is about a hundred and twenty feet long. the story goes that a dog ate the last few feet. the scroll itself says so in his handwriting at the end.","in october 1955 he was in the room at the six gallery, a little art space in san francisco, when ginsberg first read howl, the long furious poem that later got its publisher hauled into court for obscenity. kerouac passed around a jug of wine and yelled go.","in the summer of 1956, on snyder's advice, he spent two months alone as a fire lookout on desolation peak in washington. he'd expected to meet god up there. he mostly met himself and got bored."],"name":"jack kerouac","place":"lowell, massachusetts, then new york, the road, and his mother's house","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the only people for me are the mad ones.","where":"on the road, part one, chapter one; the sentence goes on a good while after that"},{"q":"beat never meant beaten down. it meant beatific.","where":"his essay the origins of the beat generation, 1959, and interviews, paraphrased. beatific means blissed out, blessed"},{"q":"i see a vision of a great rucksack revolution, thousands of young americans wandering around with packs.","where":"the dharma bums, the speech on the mountain by japhy ryder, the character based on gary snyder, paraphrased"}],"start":"the dharma bums. then some of the dharma, his buddhist notebooks, or wake up, if you want his buddhism raw. then big sur, when you want the truth.","status":"approved","story":"he was born jean-louis kerouac in lowell, massachusetts, in 1922, to french canadian parents, and didn't speak much english until he was about six. his big brother gerard died at nine of rheumatic fever, a heart illness that follows a bad strep infection, when jack was four, and the nuns who sat with the boy said he was a saint. jack believed it his whole life.\nhe went to columbia university in new york on a football scholarship, broke his leg, and drifted out. in new york he fell in with allen ginsberg, a young poet who'd later write howl, and william burroughs, a harvard man and heroin addict who'd later write naked lunch. that circle got called the beat generation, and later just the beats. in the late forties he went back and forth across the country with neal cassady, a fast talking ex car thief from denver. in april 1951 he taped sheets of paper into one long roll so he wouldn't have to stop, and typed on the road, the story of those trips, in about three weeks. it took six years to get it published. when it came out in 1957 he was famous overnight and hated it.\nin 1954 he found a fat collection of buddhist scriptures in english and went at it hard, reading and copying out sutras, the talks the buddha is supposed to have given, and writing his own. the next year, in berkeley, he met the poet gary snyder, a real student of zen, the japanese buddhism of sitting meditation, and the dharma bums, from 1958, is about snyder and climbing a mountain with him. dharma is the buddhist word for the teaching, so the title means something like holy bums. he wrote a life of the buddha called wake up that nobody published until 2008.\nunderneath all of it he was catholic. he'd been raised in it and never really left. by the sixties he was living with his mother, drinking all day, and on october 21, 1969, in st. petersburg, florida, he died bleeding inside from a liver that had given out.","struggle":"fame broke him. he'd spent years writing in poverty and when on the road hit, reporters wanted him to be dean moriarty, the wild young man in the book who was really neal cassady, and he was a shy mama's boy of thirty five who wanted to be left alone to write. he drank to get through the interviews and then he drank all the time.\nbig sur, from 1962, is the book about it. in 1960 he went to a cabin on the california coast that his friend lawrence ferlinghetti, the poet who ran city lights books in san francisco, lent him, to dry out and find the peace he'd written about, and instead cracked up, in detail, on the page. it's the least romantic book about being a famous writer that exists.","threads":["addiction","wandering","fame"],"years":"1922 to 1969"},{"after":"he never fixed it with regine, and he never really tried to take it back. what he did was keep her. in his will he left her everything he had, and wrote that for him an engagement had been as binding as a marriage. she and her husband turned the money down.\nin 1855 her husband was made governor of the danish west indies, the danish islands in the caribbean that are now the u.s. virgin islands, and they were leaving. on the street, the day they sailed, she passed him and said quietly, god bless you, may it go well for you. he stepped back and raised his hat. that was the last time they saw each other. he died that november. she lived until 1904.","basically":"a skinny, brilliant, gloomy danish writer in the 1840s who broke off his engagement to the girl he loved and spent the rest of his short life writing books about faith and dread, most of them under fake names that argue with each other. he's the one who gave us the leap, the idea that faith is a jump you make without proof. picture a guy who dumps the love of his life on purpose, playing the jerk so she'll be the one to walk, and then writes about her in book after book for years. he's for anyone who's ever been frozen in front of a choice.","born":1813,"died":1855,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 300 L 20 230 L 150 230 L 164 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 300 L 262 210 L 380 210 L 380 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='100' cy='225' r='5'/><path d='M 100 212 L 100 206 M 92 216 L 88 212 M 108 216 L 112 212' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 150 228 C 168 186 180 166 194 154' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='206' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 206 124 L 206 170'/><path d='M 206 170 L 190 206 M 206 170 L 224 202'/><path d='M 206 138 L 192 160 M 206 138 L 226 146'/><path d='M 226 146 L 236 92'/><path d='M 212 92 Q 236 64 260 92 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><g transform='translate(320,183.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he got engaged to the girl he loved, broke it off on purpose a year later, and wrote about her for the rest of his life.","key":"kierkegaard","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8ren_Kierkegaard","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/","why":"a longer, serious guide to his ideas"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"what he did to regine. he asked a girl of eighteen to marry him, took a year, then played a cold, heartless man so she'd be the one to leave, and when she wouldn't, he left anyway. she was in pieces. he told himself he was sparing her, and maybe he was. then he spent years publishing books that circle the engagement, repetition and the part of stages on life's way called guilty? not guilty?, where anyone in copenhagen who knew could read it.\nhe also picked the corsair fight himself, and in his last year he went after bishop mynster, the head of the danish church, his father's pastor and his own, right after the man died. sloppy, all of it. nothing in the other category.","moments":["in the hospital he refused communion, the bread and wine of the church service, from a priest, because he said priests were officials of the crown. he'd take it from a layman or not at all. he didn't get it.","at his funeral his nephew henrik lund stood up at the grave and protested that the church was burying a man who'd attacked it.","he walked the streets of copenhagen for hours most days, talking to anyone at all, and called it his people bath."],"name":"søren kierkegaard","place":"copenhagen, with a few trips to berlin","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 38 44 C 38 20 70 12 84 34 C 74 28 54 30 38 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"life can only be understood backwards, but it must be lived forwards.","where":"his journals, 1843"},{"q":"anxiety is the dizziness of freedom.","where":"the concept of anxiety, his book on dread, paraphrased"},{"q":"purity of heart is to will one thing.","where":"the title of an upbuilding discourse from 1847, one of the religious talks he published under his own name"}],"start":"fear and trembling, the opening sections on abraham. then a good selection of the journals. then works of love if you want to see him warm.","status":"approved","story":"he was the youngest of seven children of a rich copenhagen wool merchant, a gloomy, devout old man who, the story goes, had cursed god on a hillside in jutland, the danish mainland, as a poor shepherd boy. by the time søren was twenty one his mother and five of his brothers and sisters were dead, and his father seems to have taken it as punishment.\nin 1840 he got engaged to regine olsen, who was eighteen and in love with him. within a year he'd decided he couldn't go through with it, and he broke it off in a way designed to make her hate him, so she'd be free. she begged. her father begged. he went to berlin.\nthen he wrote, fast, mostly under pseudonyms, made-up authors who argue with each other. either/or, a huge book that sets a pleasure seeker's life next to a married judge's, and fear and trembling, both in 1843, then the concept of anxiety, then the sickness unto death, which is his name for despair. fear and trembling is about abraham. in genesis, the first book of the bible, god tells abraham to take his son isaac up a mountain and sacrifice him, and abraham goes, three days' walk, knife and all, until an angel stops his hand at the last second. kierkegaard admits he can't understand abraham and can't stop looking at him. the leap comes out of those books. everyone remembers it as the leap of faith, though he mostly just called it the leap.\nat the end of 1845 he picked a fight with the corsair, copenhagen's satire and gossip paper, and it made him a laughingstock for years, cartoons of his thin legs and uneven trousers. in his last year he went after the danish state church itself, the lutheran church the government ran and paid for, in pamphlets he paid for, saying the christianity of the new testament didn't exist in denmark. in october 1855 he collapsed in the street, and he died in hospital that november, at forty two.","struggle":"he called it his melancholy, and it's all over the journals from his twenties on. he believed he carried something from his father that made him unfit to marry, and he never said plainly what it was. scholars still guess.\nregine married johan frederik schlegel, a civil servant, in 1847. kierkegaard kept writing about her for the rest of his life. in 1849 he sent schlegel a letter asking to be allowed to write to her, and schlegel sent it back unopened. and the corsair got under his skin. he'd walked all over copenhagen every day talking to anyone, and after the cartoons kids in the street shouted either/or at him.","threads":["love","doubt","loneliness"],"years":"1813 to 1855"},{"after":"after the adviser told her beauty wasn't science, she did the science, all of it, properly, and then came back to the asters and goldenrod decades later and explained, as a scientist, why they look good together. purple and yellow are opposites on the color wheel, and side by side they pull in more bees than either would alone. so the beauty was doing something all along.\nafter the language was lost, she went and got some of it back. she doesn't pretend she's fluent. she tells what she found.","basically":"a potawatomi scientist who studies moss, the tiny plants almost nobody looks at, and wrote braiding sweetgrass in 2013 for a small press. word of mouth made it one of the best selling nonfiction books in america, millions of copies. it says the land is a gift, and gifts come with obligations, and it says it like a science teacher who also knows the old stories. think of the professor who takes you out to the pond and makes you feel like the whole planet is your family, and doesn't let the science or the stories win the argument alone. take from her that paying attention is a way of saying thank you.","born":1953,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='256' rx='80' ry='16' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='150' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 150 184 L 150 222 L 130 250 M 150 222 L 176 238 L 176 250 M 150 196 L 176 214 M 150 196 L 170 218'/><path d='M 170 216 C 186 222 196 232 212 236' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='5' stroke='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 40 250 C 40 226 90 222 96 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 236 C 58 226 80 226 92 238' stroke='var(--sage)' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 230 250 L 232 200 M 244 250 L 248 206 M 258 250 L 254 196'/><g fill='var(--lavender)'><circle cx='232' cy='198' r='6'/><circle cx='254' cy='194' r='6'/><\/g><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='248' cy='204' r='5'/><circle cx='244' cy='196' r='4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(70,211.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a botanist who got told in her first college interview that beauty wasn't science, and spent forty years proving it was.","key":"kimmerer","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin_Wall_Kimmerer","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"the publisher","url":"https://milkweed.org/book/braiding-sweetgrass","why":"milkweed editions' page for braiding sweetgrass"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braiding_Sweetgrass","why":"what's in the book"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["in potawatomi, she writes, there's a word, puhpowee, for the force that pushes mushrooms up out of the ground overnight. english doesn't have one.","she writes that in potawatomi a bay isn't a thing, it's a verb, wiikwegamaa, to be a bay. the water is doing something, it's alive. english makes it a noun, a dead thing.","her newer book, the serviceberry, in 2024, is short and about gift economies, how a berry bush shares, and what it would take for people to."],"name":"robin wall kimmerer","place":"upstate new york, then wisconsin, then syracuse","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 16 128 C 24 114 36 104 46 100' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='5' stroke='var(--sage)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all flourishing is mutual.","where":"braiding sweetgrass, 2013"},{"q":"take only what you need, never the first one or the last, and give something back.","where":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapter on the honorable harvest, paraphrased"},{"q":"the land knows you, even when you are lost.","where":"braiding sweetgrass"}],"start":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapters skywoman falling and learning the grammar of animacy.","status":"approved","story":"she was born on september 13, 1953, in upstate new york. she's a citizen of the potawatomi nation, a great lakes people who were forced west in the 1830s, many of them on a march in 1838 called the trail of death, and ended up in kansas and oklahoma. her grandfather was sent to carlisle, the government boarding school built to erase native kids' languages and ways. she grew up without the potawatomi language, and as an adult she set out to learn it.\nthe story she tells in braiding sweetgrass is that when she started college, the adviser asked why she wanted to study botany, and she said she wanted to know why asters and goldenrod, a purple flower and a yellow one, look so beautiful together. he told her that wasn't science. she studied botany anyway, at the state university of new york college of environmental science and forestry in syracuse, then got a master's and a doctorate at the university of wisconsin, by 1983.\nshe went back to that college in syracuse to teach, and directed its center for native peoples and the environment. her first book, gathering moss, in 2003, is about mosses, and won the john burroughs medal, the big prize for nature writing.\nbraiding sweetgrass came out in 2013 from milkweed editions, a small nonprofit press. it's built around sweetgrass, a plant sacred to many native nations, and it braids three strands together, the science, the native stories, and her own life as a mother and teacher. it opens with skywoman, a creation story of a woman falling from the sky onto a turtle's back, which is why so many native people call this continent turtle island, and goes through pecans, strawberries, and the honorable harvest, the old rules for taking from the land. it sold over two million copies in ten years. she got a macarthur fellowship, the so-called genius grant, in 2022.","struggle":"the language. her grandfather lost it at carlisle, and the family grew up without it. she started learning potawatomi as a grown woman, one of a handful of students with a few elderly speakers, and she's written honestly about how slow and humbling it was.\nand science itself, the version that told her at eighteen that what she loved about plants didn't count. she spent a career inside that world, publishing, teaching, getting tenure, while carrying the other way of knowing that it had told her to leave at the door.","threads":["the land","joy","family"],"years":"born 1953"},{"after":"he never got a next chapter to make right what he'd done wrong. what he did instead, the night before he died, was stand up in memphis and say he'd been to the mountaintop. that's moses in the bible, who led his people forty years through the desert and got to see the promised land from a mountain but died before he could go in. king said he might not get there with them and wasn't worried about anything. thirty nine years old, with the fbi's tapes in a drawer somewhere, and he said it like a man who'd already set the weight down.\nthe after was left to everyone else. that's the honest version.","basically":"the baptist preacher from atlanta who led the civil rights movement, the fight in the fifties and sixties to end the laws that kept black americans segregated and kept them from voting. you know him from the i have a dream speech in washington in 1963, but he was twenty six when it started, and in the thirteen years he had he got stabbed, jailed, wiretapped by his own government, dropped by his allies, and shot dead at thirty nine. take from him that you can refuse to hate the people hurting you and still refuse to let them do it.","born":1929,"died":1968,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 L 20 140 L 130 140 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 55 165 L 95 165 L 95 200 L 55 200 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 65 165 L 65 200 M 75 165 L 75 200 M 85 165 L 85 200'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><path d='M 110 150 L 128 146 L 130 160 L 112 164 Z'/><path d='M 150 120 L 170 114 L 173 130 L 153 136 Z'/><path d='M 200 100 L 222 96 L 224 112 L 202 116 Z'/><\/g><circle cx='280' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 280 154 L 280 200'/><path d='M 280 200 L 264 236 M 280 200 L 298 232'/><path d='M 280 168 L 266 190 M 280 168 L 300 176'/><path d='M 275 145 Q 280 149 285 145'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='330' cy='156' r='10'/><path d='M 330 170 L 330 216'/><path d='M 330 216 L 314 252 M 330 216 L 348 248'/><path d='M 330 184 L 316 206 M 330 184 L 350 192'/><circle cx='365' cy='166' r='9'/><path d='M 365 180 L 365 226'/><path d='M 365 226 L 349 262 M 365 226 L 383 258'/><path d='M 365 194 L 351 216 M 365 194 L 385 202'/><\/g><g transform='translate(105,113.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a baptist preacher who got his method from a hindu who got it from a russian novelist who got it from a jewish carpenter.","key":"king","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_Luther_King_Jr.","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/","why":"stanford's archive of his letters, sermons, and speeches"},{"label":"the nobel site","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1964/king/facts/","why":"his 1964 peace prize"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the fbi, under j. edgar hoover, wiretapped him for years and recorded extramarital affairs, and he had them. boston university, where he got his doctorate, found in 1991 that parts of his dissertation, the long research paper you write for a phd, were copied from other people's work without credit. both true. both with adults, and both his own to carry.","moments":["in 1958 a woman stabbed him in the chest with a letter opener at a book signing in harlem. the blade sat against his aorta, the main artery out of the heart. the surgeons told him if he'd sneezed he'd have died. he put that in his last speech.","the letter from birmingham jail was written on the margins of a newspaper and scraps his lawyers smuggled out. it's addressed to eight white clergymen who'd asked him to be patient.","in 1959 he went to india and slept in a room where gandhi had stayed. he said afterward he was more convinced than ever that nonviolent resistance was the most potent weapon available to oppressed people."],"name":"martin luther king jr","place":"atlanta, montgomery, memphis","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i come to india as a pilgrim, not a tourist.","where":"his 1959 trip, said on arrival"},{"q":"injustice anywhere is a threat to justice everywhere.","where":"letter from birmingham jail, 1963"},{"q":"i have decided to stick with love. hate is too great a burden to bear.","where":"where do we go from here, a 1967 speech to the civil rights group he led, paraphrased"}],"start":"letter from birmingham jail, which takes twenty minutes. then the riverside speech, beyond vietnam. then strength to love, a book of his sermons.","status":"approved","story":"he was a twenty six year old pastor in montgomery, alabama in 1955 when rosa parks, a black seamstress who'd been organizing for years, refused to give up her bus seat to a white man. the boycott that followed, black montgomery walking to work for a year rather than ride segregated buses, needed a face. he didn't ask for it. the night his house got bombed, with his wife and baby daughter inside, he stood on the porch and told the angry crowd outside, some of them armed, to put their guns away and go home.\nthe method was [[gandhi]]'s, and he said so. in 1959 he went to india and said he was going as a pilgrim, not a tourist. he walked where gandhi walked. what he brought back was the certainty that you could break a system without becoming it.\nin 1966 a vietnamese buddhist monk named [[thay|thich nhat hanh]] sat with him in chicago and asked him to speak against the vietnam war. in 1967 king nominated him for the nobel peace prize, and on april 4 at riverside church in new york came out against the war in a speech called beyond vietnam. it cost him most of his allies in washington, the president included. exactly one year later he was shot on a motel balcony in memphis, where he'd gone to stand with striking garbage workers. he was thirty nine.\nthe letter from birmingham jail, written on scraps in a cell in 1963 after he was arrested for marching without a permit, is the best thing he wrote. it's addressed to the moderate white clergy, eight local religious leaders, a rabbi among them, who'd put out a statement in the paper telling him to wait. it doesn't ask them for anything.","struggle":"january 27, 1956, midnight, montgomery. a phone call telling him to get out of town or die. he sat at his kitchen table over a cup of coffee and, in his own words, told god he was at the end of his powers, he had nothing left, he couldn't face it alone. he says he heard an inner voice tell him to stand up for righteousness and he'd never be alone. three days later his house was bombed.\nby 1967 he was exhausted in a way that scared the people around him. the movement had split, the white liberals had left over vietnam, the young black power activists thought he was finished. he checked into hospitals for exhaustion more than once and kept going anyway.","threads":["nonviolence","prison","dying"],"years":"1929 to 1968"},{"after":"he forgave the hunter. that's the last thing he does in the story. the man is horrified and krishna tells him it's fine, this was owed, go in peace. then he leaves the body.\nthe gita's own answer to what you do after is chapter 18, the last one. he lays it all out and then says, i've told you everything, reflect on it fully, then do what you want. the god gives the man the choice back. arjuna picks up the bow.","basically":"one of the most loved gods in hinduism, the blue-skinned one with the flute, and the voice of the bhagavad gita, a seven hundred verse poem where he talks a terrified soldier through the morning of a battle. picture a guy frozen in the passenger seat, and the driver turns around, and the driver is god, and instead of telling him it'll be fine he says get up, do your job, and let go of how it turns out. gandhi read it every day. if you're stuck in front of something you have to do, that's the whole book.","born":-200,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 150 250 L 150 200 L 260 200 L 260 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='170' cy='250' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='240' cy='250' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='190' cy='160' r='12'/><path d='M 190 174 L 190 216'/><path d='M 190 216 C 170 216 160 232 176 232 L 204 232 C 220 232 210 216 190 216'/><path d='M 190 190 L 176 216 M 190 190 L 204 216'/><path d='M 178 152 L 176 146 M 200 152 L 202 146' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='250' cy='120' r='13'/><path d='M 250 134 L 250 182'/><path d='M 250 182 L 236 216 M 250 182 L 264 216'/><path d='M 250 148 L 236 172 M 250 148 L 276 142'/><g opacity='.25'><circle cx='232' cy='108' r='13'/><circle cx='268' cy='108' r='13'/><circle cx='250' cy='96' r='13'/><\/g><path d='M 276 142 C 300 150 320 160 340 180'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 190 L 360 190 L 360 250 M 360 200 L 385 180 L 390 200 M 310 250 L 310 260 M 350 250 L 350 260' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 300 190 C 296 170 306 172 304 150' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(150,173.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a god disguised as a chariot driver, who spends eighteen chapters telling a soldier to get up.","key":"krishna","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krishna","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the gita, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/gita/index.htm","why":"edwin arnold's old english verse version, public domain"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"he's a god in a story. the stories about him outside the gita have him as a kid stealing butter from every kitchen in the village, and stealing the clothes of the village milkmaids while they bathed in the river and climbing a tree with them. the tradition reads all of it as lila, divine play, god enjoying himself in the world. the gita itself is a god telling a man to go fight his own family, and people have used it for war and for nonviolence both.","moments":["the childhood stories have him stealing butter from every kitchen in the village and getting caught with it all over his face, a mother scolding a god. the tradition calls it lila, divine play. every indian grandmother has a painting of it.","in chapter 11 arjuna asks to see him as he really is, and gets every face, every mouth, all of time, everything being born and eaten at once, and begs him to please put the friendly face back on.","oppenheimer, who ran the atomic bomb project, remembered a line from chapter 11 at the first test in the new mexico desert. thoreau read the gita in his cabin by walden pond. the book gets around."],"name":"krishna","place":"the battlefield of kurukshetra","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 60 36 C 62 26 66 16 74 10' /><ellipse cx='76' cy='9' rx='5' ry='7' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='76' cy='9' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 40 L 60 32 L 80 40' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 20 118 L 78 92' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 30 113 L 30 113 M 40 109 L 40 109 M 50 104 L 50 104' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you have a right to your work, never to its fruit.","where":"bhagavad gita 2:47, which means chapter 2, verse 47; read all of chapter 2"},{"q":"however people come to me, that's how i meet them. every road is mine.","where":"bhagavad gita 4:11, paraphrased"},{"q":"i've told you everything. now think it over, and do as you choose.","where":"bhagavad gita 18:63, paraphrased; the last thing he says before the end"}],"start":"the bhagavad gita, chapter 2, then 11, then 18. eknath easwaran's translation is warm; he was an indian english professor who ended up teaching meditation in california. barbara stoler miller's is clean; she was an american scholar of sanskrit, the old language it's written in. ram dass's paths to god is the gita as he taught it.","status":"approved","story":"the bhagavad gita, which means the song of god, is one conversation from the middle of the mahabharata, india's giant old epic, one of the longest poems ever written. it's set on a battlefield called kurukshetra just before a war between two halves of the same royal family, cousins fighting over a kingdom. the greatest archer alive, a prince named arjuna, looks across at the other army, sees his cousins and his old teachers and the great-uncle he loved, drops his bow, sits down in his chariot, and says he won't fight.\nhis chariot driver is krishna. in that world a chariot was a two-wheeled war cart, and the driver handled the horses while the warrior fought from the back, so the two of them were in it together, close enough to talk. krishna is also god, though arjuna half forgets that. they're old friends, and in-laws too.\nthe first thing krishna says is get up. don't be a coward, it doesn't suit you. then the philosophy. you have a right to your work but never to its fruit, meaning the results. do the thing in front of you and let go of how it comes out. the self doesn't die when the body does. whatever road people take to me, i meet them on it.\nin chapter 11 arjuna asks to see krishna as he really is, and krishna shows him every face, every mouth, all of time, everything being born and dying at once. arjuna begs him to put the disguise back on. it's the same move as god answering [[job]], the man in the bible who lost everything, out of a whirlwind. no explanation, just scale.\n[[gandhi]] read it every day. [[ramdass|ram dass]] called it his bible. henry david thoreau, the american who lived alone in a cabin by walden pond and wrote the book about it, read it there. robert oppenheimer, the physicist who ran the project that built the atomic bomb, said a line from chapter 11 came into his head at the first bomb test in 1945: now i am become death, the destroyer of worlds. it's seven hundred verses and the whole of it is get up, do your work, don't grip.","struggle":"outside the gita, in the rest of the mahabharata, the giant epic it comes from, krishna's own people destroy themselves. after the war, gandhari, the mother of the hundred brothers who died on the other side, cursed him for letting it happen and said his own clan would die the same way. he accepted it. thirty six years later his clan, the yadavas, got drunk at a festival at prabhasa, by the sea, and beat each other to death with reeds that the story says turned to iron clubs in their hands, and he watched it.\nthen he went into the forest to sit, and a hunter named jara mistook his foot for a deer and put an arrow through it. the god who told arjuna the self can't be killed died of an arrow in the heel.","threads":["war","work"],"years":"the gita is roughly two thousand years old"},{"after":"after the wandering he came back to the hill, more or less. he settled into being what the vision had said, a wičháša wakȟáŋ, a holy man, running ceremonies, doctoring people, doing the heyoka's backwards work. his son archie fire lame deer became a holy man too.\nhe didn't pretend the wild years were a mistake he'd outgrown. his whole point was that they were part of the training. you can't help a drunk if you've never been one. you can't talk to a man in jail if you think you're better than him.","basically":"a lakota holy man from the rosebud reservation who spent his twenties and thirties doing about every job and every dumb thing a man can do, and then told all of it, laughing, in a book he made with a white writer in 1972. he was a heyoka, one of the sacred clowns who do everything backwards so people can see themselves. think of the uncle who's been to jail and back, tells the filthiest stories at the table, and is also the one the family calls when somebody's dying. take from him that the holy man doesn't have to be the cleanest person in the room. he has to have been in all the rooms.","born":1903,"died":1976,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='60' y='200' width='44' height='50' rx='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 60 216 L 104 216 M 60 234 L 104 234' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(160,236) rotate(180)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -20 4 M 0 28 L 20 4'/><\/g><path d='M 20 190 L 20 250 M 20 204 L 40 204 M 20 224 L 40 224'/><path d='M 250 250 C 280 190 340 190 380 250' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 300 214 C 304 206 320 206 324 214' /><g transform='translate(20,175.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a lakota holy man who'd been a rodeo clown, a sheepherder, a sign painter and a jailbird, and figured all of it counted.","key":"lamedeer","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Fire_Lame_Deer","why":"the plain overview of his life"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"the women, mostly. he tells about one night a policeman came home, found him with his wife, and shot at him. he ran. he tells it like a man who knows exactly how that sounds and isn't going to clean it up for you. all adults, a lot of them, a lot of it stupid.\nand the booze and the jail, which he also doesn't clean up. the book is sloppy the way a good long night at the kitchen table is sloppy.","moments":["the name he came down the hill with at sixteen, tahca ushte, lame deer, was his great-grandfather's, a chief.","he learned most of his english on the road in the wandering years, not in school, which you can hear in the book.","erdoes went on to write lakota woman with mary crow dog, a young lakota woman who'd been inside the wounded knee occupation of 1973."],"name":"john fire lame deer","place":"the rosebud reservation in south dakota, and a lot of road","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 18 42 C 28 35 92 35 102 42 C 92 47 28 47 18 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 40 C 38 16 82 16 82 40 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"a clown's work is sacred. it's a medicine man's job.","where":"lame deer, seeker of visions, the chapter on the heyoka"},{"q":"a medicine man ought to have lived all of it, the good and the rotten, drunk and sober, so he knows what the people he's helping are going through.","where":"lame deer, seeker of visions, paraphrased"},{"q":"i had a thirst for women. i loved many girls, more than a hundred.","where":"lame deer, seeker of visions, the chapters on his wandering years"}],"start":"lame deer, seeker of visions, the first chapter, alone on the hilltop, then the chapter on the clowns. remember erdoes wrote the english.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on march 17, 1903, on the rosebud reservation in south dakota, home of the sicangu lakota. his father was hunkpapa and his mother miniconjou, two of the other lakota bands. at sixteen he went alone up a hill for his hanbleceya, the lakota vision quest, fasting and praying for days in a pit dug into the hilltop and waiting for a vision. he came down with one, and took his great-grandfather's name, tahca ushte, lame deer.\nthen came what he called his wandering years. he rode in rodeos and worked as a rodeo clown, the guy in the barrel who distracts the bull. he was a soldier, a sign painter, a potato picker, a tribal policeman, a sheepherder, a singer, and, as he put it, a jail prisoner. he drank. he learned english on the road. he chased women all over the west.\nand he was a heyoka, a sacred clown. among the lakota, a person who dreams of the wakinyan, the thunder beings, has to become a heyoka, and heyokas do everything backwards. they shiver in a heat wave and complain of being full when food runs out. it's funny, and it's supposed to be. the laughing opens people up. he said a clown's work is sacred.\nin 1967 richard erdoes, an austrian-born illustrator, came to the pine ridge reservation on assignment for life magazine and stayed in the lakota world. he and lame deer made lame deer, seeker of visions, published in 1972. lame deer talked and erdoes wrote the english and put it in order, the same kind of partnership john neihardt had with [[blackelk|black elk]]. so the voice in the book is lame deer's, through erdoes's hands. he took part in the protests of the early seventies, and died on december 14, 1976, in south dakota.","struggle":"he had the vision at sixteen and then spent about twenty years running from it. rodeos, bars, jail, other men's wives. he knew what he was supposed to be and he went and was everything else first.\nthe drinking was real. the book doesn't make a sermon of it and doesn't make it cute either. it's in there next to the rodeo stories like it was just the weather in those years.","threads":["addiction","love","wandering","joy"],"years":"1903 to 1976"},{"after":"he wrote it down for the gatekeeper, the border guard, and left. that's the after. he didn't found a school or gather students or come back to check on the book. it's the only scripture in this forest where the author's response to writing it was to disappear.\nthe book's own answer to failure is chapter 76. the stiff and the hard are companions of death, the soft and yielding are companions of life. the tree that won't bend breaks in the wind. he'd already said what to do next before you asked.","basically":"the name given to an old chinese sage who may never have existed, credited with the tao te ching, a thin book of eighty one short poems about living like water. it's one of the most translated books in the world. the whole idea is that forcing things makes them worse, and the soft thing wins in the end. if you've ever tried so hard at something that it fell apart in your hands, this book was written for that exact morning.","born":-500,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 150 250 C 150 200 250 200 250 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 160 220 C 140 210 140 195 155 200 M 240 220 C 260 210 260 195 245 200'/><path d='M 165 250 L 165 268 M 185 250 L 185 268 M 215 250 L 215 268 M 235 250 L 235 268'/><circle cx='200' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 195 165 Q 200 169 205 165'/><path d='M 200 174 L 200 210 M 200 188 L 180 202 M 200 188 L 224 200'/><path d='M 190 148 C 195 138 205 138 210 148' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 120 M 370 250 L 370 120 M 290 120 L 380 120 M 296 108 L 374 108'/><circle cx='335' cy='170' r='11'/><path d='M 335 184 L 335 232'/><path d='M 335 232 L 321 266 M 335 232 L 349 266'/><path d='M 335 198 L 349 222 M 335 198 L 309 192'/><path d='M 306 180 L 322 178' /><rect x='296' y='172' width='14' height='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(376,81.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he opens his book by telling you the book can't do it, then writes eighty more chapters anyway.","key":"laotzu","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laozi","why":"the plain overview of the legend"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216","why":"the tao te ching in an old public domain translation"}],"lineage":"tao","mess":"can't have a mess if you never existed.","moments":["the old story, told by sima qian, china's first great historian, has confucius, the famous teacher of duty and good manners whose sayings shaped china for two thousand years, going to ask him about ritual and coming away shaken. he told his students he'd met a dragon. in china a dragon isn't a monster. it's a thing that rides the wind and clouds up to heaven and can't be caught or pinned down. confucius meant he had no idea what he'd just met.","the tao te ching is eighty one chapters and about five thousand chinese characters, which makes it shorter than most of the introductions written about it.","chapter 11 is the one about empty spaces. thirty spokes meet at the hub, and it's the hole in the middle that makes the wheel work. same with the inside of a pot, and the doors and windows cut into a room."],"name":"lao tzu","place":"china, maybe","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='28' r='7' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the way that can be spoken of is not the eternal way.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 1, first line"},{"q":"we shape clay into a pot, but it's the emptiness inside that holds what we want.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 11, paraphrased"},{"q":"nothing is softer than water, and nothing beats it at wearing down the hard.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 78, paraphrased"}],"start":"the tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4, then 8, 11, 76 and 78. each chapter is a few lines; you can read the whole book in an evening. any translation. stephen mitchell's is loose and pretty, d.c. lau's is a scholar's and plainer.","status":"approved","story":"the story is that he was keeper of the archives, the royal library, for the zhou, the family of kings who ruled china back then. he got sick of the whole thing and rode a water buffalo west toward the mountains. at the last gate out of the country the guard recognized him and wouldn't let him leave until he wrote down what he knew. he wrote eighty one short chapters and rode off and was never seen again.\nthere probably wasn't a lao tzu. the name just means old master. the tao te ching, roughly the book of the way and its power, is likely a collection somebody put together over a couple of centuries. it doesn't matter. the book is the teacher.\nthe first line is the tao that can be spoken isn't the eternal tao. tao means the way, the thing underneath everything that makes it all go, and the book says right up front that once you've put it in words, you've already missed it. every scripture on these pages should start that way and this is the only one that does.\nthe rest is about water, which is soft and beats everything hard, the way a river cuts a canyon. about the empty space in a pot, which is the part you actually use. about doing by not doing, wu wei in chinese, which isn't laziness. it's not forcing, like floating in the ocean, which only works once you stop thrashing. the more you read it the less there is, on purpose.","struggle":"chapter 20 of the tao te ching is the loneliest thing in scripture. everyone else is bright, i alone am dim. everyone has a purpose, i'm like a fool, drifting. everyone else has plenty, i alone seem to have nothing. whoever wrote that, and it wasn't nobody, was not having a good year.\nthe legend has him riding off sick of the court and the whole business of governing people who won't be governed. that's a man who tried and quit, not a man who floated above it.","threads":["silence"],"years":"sixth century bc, or fourth, or never"},{"after":"no redemption, exactly. he got out in 1976, moved to los angeles, and reinvented himself as a futurist and a talk show guest. in the early eighties he went on a paid debate tour with g. gordon liddy, the former prosecutor who'd raided millbrook and later went to prison for watergate. they made money off it and became friends.\nwhen he got his cancer diagnosis in 1995 he treated his own dying as a show too, and a kind of teaching. he talked openly about it, invited people over, and planned his ashes into space. you can call that brave or you can call it more circus. it was probably both.","basically":"a harvard psychologist who ate magic mushrooms in mexico in 1960, came home a true believer, and became the most famous drug preacher in america. turn on, tune in, drop out was his. he was fired, raided, jailed, broke out of prison with the help of a radical underground group, ran to algeria and switzerland, got caught, and ended up talking to the fbi. picture a tenured scientist turning into a cult influencer in about four years. he's why psychedelic research got shut down for thirty years, and he's worth reading so you know what not to do.","born":1920,"died":1996,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 40 250 L 200 250 L 200 230 L 40 230 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='120' cy='114' r='14'/><path d='M 120 128 L 120 176'/><path d='M 120 176 L 106 210 M 120 176 L 134 210'/><path d='M 120 142 L 100 118 M 120 142 L 140 118'/><path d='M 115 119 Q 120 123 125 119'/><path d='M 150 230 L 150 176 M 144 170 C 144 162 156 162 156 170 C 156 178 144 178 144 170' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 300 150 L 318 90 L 336 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 150 L 296 168 L 312 156 M 332 150 L 340 168 L 324 156'/><path d='M 318 160 C 312 190 326 210 316 240' stroke-dasharray='3 7' opacity='.6'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='330' cy='40' r='2'/><circle cx='260' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='370' cy='90' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(46,208.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he had the science in his hands at harvard and turned it into a circus, and he knew it.","key":"leary","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Leary","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Psilocybin_Project","why":"the harvard experiments that got him fired"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he turned a science into a circus, and he knew it. the harvard research could have been careful, and he made it a movement, then a slogan, then a brand. the backlash he helped cause is a big reason lsd and psilocybin were banned and serious research stopped for about thirty years.\nafter his capture he talked to the fbi to get his sentence cut, and the informant question has followed him ever since. his defenders say he warned people and gave the fbi little that hurt anyone. others who knew him never forgave it. what's clear is he went into the program under a code name. the rest of it, drink, five marriages, bad fathering, was all with adults and all his own. the risks of the drugs he was selling as salvation were real then and are now. psychotic breaks in some people, a bad trip that doesn't end when you want it to, rarely hallucinogen persisting perception disorder, where the visuals never fully go away, and prison.","moments":["in the fall of 1960 [[huxley|aldous huxley]] was a visiting professor at mit, just down the road, and met with him that season. huxley's advice, roughly, was go slow and keep it quiet. he did the opposite.","he made the phrase set and setting famous, meaning your mindset going in and the place you're in matter as much as the drug. in 1964 he, alpert, and ralph metzner, a grad student in their circle, wrote the psychedelic experience, a trip manual built on the tibetan book of the dead, the old buddhist guide read to the dying.","in september 1971, a fugitive in switzerland, he met [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], the man who made lsd, at a railway station snack bar in lausanne. hofmann thought he was charming and blind to the dangers."],"name":"timothy leary","place":"harvard, millbrook, prison, algeria, prison, los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"turn on, tune in, drop out.","where":"the human be-in, golden gate park, san francisco, january 14, 1967. he said the phrasing came partly from marshall mcluhan, the media thinker"},{"q":"i learned more in the five hours after taking these mushrooms than in the preceding fifteen years of studying psychology.","where":"his account of cuernavaca, mexico, august 1960, paraphrased"},{"q":"why not?","where":"his last words, according to his son zach, who was there, may 1996"}],"start":"flashbacks, his 1983 autobiography, which is funny and unreliable. then, for the honest version, jay stevens's storming heaven, a history of lsd in america.","status":"approved","story":"he was a clinical psychologist, a good one, with a well known book on personality. in 1959 he got a lecturer's job at harvard, the most famous university in america. in august 1960, on vacation in cuernavaca, mexico, he ate psilocybin mushrooms by a swimming pool and said later he'd learned more in five hours than in fifteen years of psychology.\nback at harvard he started the harvard psilocybin project with his colleague richard alpert, later [[ramdass|ram dass]]. they gave psilocybin, made in switzerland by [[hofmann|albert hofmann]]'s company, to grad students, to prisoners at a massachusetts jail to see if it kept them from reoffending, and in 1962 to divinity students on good friday. [[huxley|aldous huxley]] was an early adviser. the work got loose and sloppy fast. there were parties. the promise to keep it away from undergraduates got broken. harvard fired him in may 1963, officially for skipping his classes.\na rich young family, the hitchcocks, lent him and alpert a sixty four room mansion in millbrook, new york, and it became a psychedelic commune for five years. in january 1967 at the human be-in, a huge hippie gathering in golden gate park in san francisco, he told the crowd to turn on, tune in, drop out. richard nixon, the president, is said to have called him the most dangerous man in america.\narrested for small amounts of marijuana, he got ten years in 1970. that september he climbed out of a california prison along a telephone wire, and the weather underground, a radical group that set off bombs against government buildings, smuggled him out of the country for a fee paid by a hippie drug ring. he went to algeria, fell out with the black panthers in exile there, moved to switzerland, and was caught in kabul, afghanistan, in early 1973. back in prison he cooperated with the fbi. he got out in 1976.\nafter that he was a showman, touring with the watergate conspirator who'd once raided him, selling the idea that computers were the new lsd, making software, talking. he died of prostate cancer in beverly hills in may 1996, and the next year a few grams of his ashes went into orbit on a rocket.","struggle":"in 1955, on his thirty fifth birthday, his first wife marianne killed herself. he was left with two young kids. that's the man who went to mexico five years later.\nthe kids paid for his fame. his daughter susan, who'd been dragged through millbrook, raids, and exile, struggled her whole life and died in jail in 1990. he was all over the news for thirty years, and the people nearest him got the least of him.","threads":["psychedelics","fame","prison","exile"],"years":"1920 to 1996"},{"after":"she went and told them. that's the after in the book, and then the record goes quiet. she isn't named in acts, the book about what the first christians did next. whatever she did with the rest of her life, nobody wrote it down anywhere we can trust.\nthe legends fill it in. an eastern tradition says she went to ephesus, a big greek city in what's now turkey. a french one says she landed in provence, in the south of france, and lived out her life in a cave at sainte-baume. an old story says she went to the emperor tiberius, who ruled rome when jesus died, with an egg to tell him about the resurrection, and that's why orthodox icons, the painted holy images of the eastern church, show her holding a red one. none of it is history. the history is a woman told to go and tell, who went.","basically":"one of the women who followed jesus around galilee and paid for the trip out of her own pocket, who stayed at the cross when the men ran, and who, in john's gospel, is the first person to see him alive again. then in 591 a pope mixed her up with a different woman in a sermon, and for fourteen hundred years she got painted and preached as a reformed prostitute, which the bible never says. think of somebody whose whole reputation got hijacked by one bad take that went viral and stayed that way for centuries. she's for anyone who stayed when everyone else left, and anyone who's been remembered wrong.","born":null,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 22 250 C 22 224 62 224 62 250' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 42 216 L 42 206 M 16 228 L 8 222 M 68 228 L 76 222' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 262 250 C 264 170 372 160 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 306 250 C 306 214 338 214 338 250 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='250' cy='228' r='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='100' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 100 168 L 100 216'/><path d='M 100 216 L 86 250 M 100 216 L 114 250'/><path d='M 100 182 L 86 206 M 100 182 L 126 176'/><path d='M 132 140 L 112 248 L 100 246'/><path d='M 146 250 L 146 236 M 156 250 L 156 240'/><circle cx='146' cy='232' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='156' cy='236' r='4' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 177 150 C 172 166 174 178 180 186 M 203 150 C 208 166 206 178 200 186'/><circle cx='190' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 190 168 L 190 216'/><path d='M 190 216 L 176 250 M 190 216 L 204 250'/><path d='M 190 182 L 204 206 M 190 182 L 164 176'/><path d='M 185 159 Q 190 163 195 159'/><g transform='translate(240,181.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the church spent almost fourteen hundred years calling her a prostitute, and nothing in the book says so.","key":"magdalene","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Magdalene","why":"the plain overview, and how her name got dragged"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of. the mess is what got done to her name. in a sermon in 591, pope gregory the great, one of the most powerful popes of the early middle ages, merged her with the unnamed sinful woman who washes jesus's feet with her tears and anoints them in luke 7, and with mary of bethany, the sister of martha and lazarus. the sinful woman became a prostitute in the preaching, and the prostitute became mary magdalene, in sermons and paintings for fourteen centuries.\nnone of it is in the text. the catholic church quietly separated them again in 1969, when it revised its calendar. the orthodox churches of the east never mixed them up in the first place.","moments":["a second century text, written a hundred years or more after jesus, called the gospel of mary, which survives only in pieces, has peter asking the others whether jesus really spoke privately with a woman and not openly to them. it isn't in the bible. the argument in it sounds real.","in john 20:17 he tells her not to hold on to him. in latin, the old language of the western church, that's noli me tangere, and it's on the wall of half the museums in europe.","the alabaster jar painters give her, a pale stone jar of perfumed oil, belongs to the woman in luke 7 and the mary of bethany in john 12. every picture of her with a jar is a picture of the mix-up."],"name":"mary magdalene","place":"magdala on the sea of galilee, then jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><ellipse cx='96' cy='112' rx='8' ry='10' fill='var(--peach)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"they have taken away my lord, and i don't know where they have laid him.","where":"the gospel of john, 20:13"},{"q":"rabboni.","where":"john 20:16, which john translates for you as teacher"},{"q":"i have seen the lord.","where":"john 20:18, what she told the disciples"}],"start":"john 20:1 to 18, near the end of john's gospel in any bible. then luke 8:1 to 3, the three verses about the money. then luke 24:1 to 11.","status":"approved","story":"mary from magdala, a fishing town on the sea of galilee, the big lake in the north of what's now israel. luke 8, in the gospel of luke, one of the four books that tell jesus's life, says seven demons had gone out of her. nobody knows what that meant, illness, madness, something else, and the text doesn't say.\nthe same passage says she and joanna, whose husband ran the household of herod antipas, the ruler of galilee, and susanna and many other women traveled with [[jesus]] and the twelve, his inner circle, and paid for it out of their own money. the ministry ran on women's money.\nwhen he was arrested, mark says, the disciples all ran. she didn't. she stood near the cross with the other women and watched him die, and then watched where they laid him. john's gospel puts one of the men at the cross too. the rest were gone.\nearly on sunday morning, still dark, john says, she went to the tomb and found the stone rolled away. she ran for [[peter]] and the other disciple, the one john calls the disciple jesus loved. they came, looked, and went home. she stayed outside, crying. a man she took for the gardener asked why, and she said if you carried him off, tell me where. he said mary. she said rabboni, my teacher. he sent her to tell the others, and she went and said i have seen the lord. that's why her old title is apostle to the apostles. an apostle is someone sent out with a message, so she's the one who was sent to the ones who were sent. thomas aquinas, the great medieval catholic thinker, used the title, and the vatican used it again in 2016 when it raised her day on the church calendar to a feast, the same rank the male apostles get.","struggle":"seven demons is the struggle, and it's all we get. whatever it was, it was bad enough to count to seven, and it was over before the story starts. she's in the book as someone who had already been through it.\nthen good friday, the day of the crucifixion. she watched him die slowly and watched them close the tomb, and on sunday even the body was gone. the grief in john 20 is so raw she can't see who's standing in front of her. and when she and the other women told the apostles, luke says it seemed to them like nonsense, and they didn't believe them.","threads":["grief","love"],"years":"the first century"},{"after":"mecca was the after. in april 1964 he made the hajj and wrote a letter home saying he'd eaten from the same plate and prayed beside muslims whose eyes were the bluest of blue and whose hair was the blondest of blond, and it had made him rethink race. he still thought america was racist to the bone. he stopped saying white people were devils, and he said he'd let himself be used to make sweeping indictments of all white people and wouldn't do it again.\nhe had less than a year left. he started two organizations, traveled through africa, talked about taking america to the united nations over human rights, went to selma, and dictated the rest of his life to alex haley, the writer who'd later write roots. he knew they were coming for him. he kept going to the meetings.","basically":"a kid whose father died under a streetcar in what the family always believed was a murder, and whose mother was locked in a state hospital, who became a street hustler in boston and harlem, went to prison at twenty, taught himself words by copying the dictionary, and came out the fiercest voice of black anger in america. for twelve years he preached that white people were devils. then he went on the pilgrimage to mecca, prayed next to blue-eyed muslims, publicly changed his mind, and was shot dead less than a year later. he's for anyone who's had to admit in public that they were wrong about something big, with enemies watching.","born":1925,"died":1965,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='20' y='70' width='170' height='180' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='90' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 90 192 L 90 234'/><path d='M 90 234 C 70 234 60 250 76 250 L 104 250 C 120 250 110 234 90 234'/><path d='M 90 208 L 76 234 M 90 208 L 104 234'/><path d='M 64 228 L 90 234 L 116 228 L 116 244 L 90 250 L 64 244 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 234 L 90 250'/><rect x='134' y='244' width='34' height='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='136' y='238' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='134' y='232' width='34' height='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 70 L 190 70 M 50 70 L 50 250 M 80 70 L 80 250 M 110 70 L 110 250 M 140 70 L 140 250 M 170 70 L 170 250' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 196 150 C 232 110 272 124 300 176' stroke-dasharray='2 9' opacity='.5'/><rect x='290' y='190' width='50' height='60' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><rect x='290' y='204' width='50' height='6' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='256' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='272' cy='236' r='5'/><circle cx='358' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='374' cy='236' r='5'/><path d='M 256 237 L 256 250 M 272 241 L 272 250 M 358 237 L 358 250 M 374 241 L 374 250'/><g transform='translate(140,210.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he preached for twelve years that white people were devils, went to mecca, and wrote home that he'd had to throw out some of his conclusions.","key":"malcolmx","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malcolm_X","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Autobiography_of_Malcolm_X","why":"the book he told to alex haley"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/malcolm-x","why":"stanford's page on him and king"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he was a thief and a hustler and a drug dealer and he robbed houses, and he told all of it himself in the autobiography. then for twelve years he preached the nation's teaching that white people were a race of devils, and meant it. in 1962, when a plane full of white art patrons from atlanta crashed in paris, he called it a beautiful thing and said god had answered their prayers.\nhe said ugly things about jews in those years too, in speeches and interviews. he called king an uncle tom, the old insult for a black man who bows and scrapes to white people. nothing sexual.","moments":["in prison he started at the first page of the dictionary and copied every word. he said later that months passed without his even thinking about being in prison, and that he'd never been so free in his life.","on march 26, 1964, he and [[king|martin luther king]] crossed paths in the capitol during the senate fight over the civil rights act, the law that outlawed segregation in public places. they shook hands, a photographer caught it, and that was the only time they met.","in 2021, fifty six years after the killing, two of the three men convicted of it were exonerated. they'd spent decades in prison for something they didn't do."],"name":"malcolm x","place":"omaha, lansing, boston, harlem, mecca","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have been forced to rearrange much of my thought patterns and to toss aside some of my previous conclusions.","where":"his letter home from mecca, april 1964, paraphrased slightly"},{"q":"by any means necessary.","where":"the founding rally of the organization of afro-american unity, the group he started after he left the nation, june 1964, and a phrase he used often"},{"q":"i'm for truth, no matter who tells it.","where":"the autobiography of malcolm x, the book he told to the writer alex haley, the last chapter"}],"start":"the autobiography of malcolm x, as told to alex haley, the chapter called saved and the chapter called mecca. then manning marable's malcolm x: a life of reinvention, the big 2011 biography, for the corrections.","status":"approved","story":"malcolm little was born in omaha in 1925. his father, earl, was a baptist preacher who organized for marcus garvey, the jamaican-born leader who told black people to be proud of africa and build their own businesses and institutions, and the family was burned out of a house in lansing, michigan. in 1931 his father died under a streetcar. the police called it an accident. the family believed white supremacists killed him. his mother held on for years, then broke down, and in 1939 she was committed to a state mental hospital. the kids went to foster homes.\nhe was smart. when he told a teacher he wanted to be a lawyer, the teacher told him that was no realistic goal for a black kid and he should be a carpenter. by his late teens he was in boston and then harlem, where they called him detroit red, hustling, running numbers, an illegal street lottery, selling drugs, robbing houses. in 1946 he got eight to ten years for burglary.\nin prison his brothers and sisters wrote to him about the nation of islam and its leader, elijah muhammad. the nation was a black american religious movement that used the name of islam but taught its own things, including that white people were a race of devils, and that black people should live separately and build their own world. elijah muhammad said he was god's messenger. malcolm wanted to write to him and couldn't write well enough, so he copied the dictionary out by hand, page by page. he came out in 1952 and took the x for the african family name he'd never know, since little was a name from slavery.\nhe built the nation from a few hundred members into tens of thousands and became the most feared voice in america. in 1964 he broke with elijah muhammad, made the hajj, the pilgrimage to mecca in saudi arabia every muslim who can is supposed to make once in their life, and came back as el-hajj malik el-shabazz. on february 21, 1965, he was shot to death on the stage of the audubon ballroom in upper manhattan, in front of his wife betty and their daughters. he was thirty nine.","struggle":"prison was the floor. twenty years old, in charlestown state prison, in a cell with no plumbing, so angry and so profane that the other inmates called him satan. he tells it himself.\nthen the break. in 1963 he learned that elijah muhammad, the man he'd called god's messenger, had fathered children with women who worked for him as secretaries, and that the nation meant to keep it quiet. after kennedy was killed malcolm said the chickens had come home to roost, meaning america's violence had come back around on it, and elijah banned him from speaking for ninety days. by 1964 he was out, and he knew the nation wanted him dead. his house was firebombed a week before he was killed.","threads":["prison","anger","doubt"],"years":"1925 to 1965"},{"after":"the after is the memoir. old, sick, needing a co-writer to get it on paper, he told the whole thing, the drinking, the lies, the marriage, and called it all is grace. there's no clean ending. he didn't get sober for good and ride off. he just stopped hiding.\nwhich is the ragamuffin gospel with the lights on. he'd spent twenty years telling people god loved them in the gutter, and at the end he let everyone see the gutter he'd been writing from the whole time.","basically":"a funny, charming ex-marine who became a catholic priest, fought the bottle his whole life, left the priesthood to get married, and in 1990 wrote the book that told a generation of church kids that god loved them as screwups, not after they got it together. then, old and sick, he wrote a memoir admitting he'd been drinking far more than anyone knew the whole time he was on the road preaching that, and lying to cover it. if you've ever sat in church feeling like the only fraud in the room, he was the guy on the stage, and at the end he said so.","born":1934,"died":2013,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 250 250 L 250 150 L 370 150 L 370 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 152 L 310 110 L 380 152'/><path d='M 270 250 L 270 196 L 300 196 L 300 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='294' cy='224' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><rect x='320' y='175' width='30' height='26' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 335 175 L 335 201 M 320 188 L 350 188'/><circle cx='285' cy='184' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='170' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 170 192 L 170 234'/><path d='M 170 234 C 150 234 140 250 156 250 L 184 250 C 200 250 190 234 170 234'/><path d='M 170 208 L 156 234 M 170 208 L 184 234'/><path d='M 198 243 L 220 243 L 226 245.5 L 234 245.5 L 234 248.5 L 226 248.5 L 220 250 L 198 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><g opacity='.5'><path d='M 60 40 L 54 56 M 110 30 L 104 46 M 160 50 L 154 66 M 210 34 L 204 50 M 90 90 L 84 106 M 190 100 L 184 116 M 140 120 L 134 136 M 60 140 L 54 156'/><\/g><g transform='translate(90,232.0) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote the book on grace for failures, kept failing, and admitted at the end that he'd lied about it.","key":"manning","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brennan_Manning","why":"the plain overview of his life and the ragamuffin gospel"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"all of it the ordinary kind. drunk, over and over. left the priesthood and broke his vows, including the promise never to marry, and then the marriage broke too. and he lied, to friends and to readers, about how sober he was. he put the lying in his own memoir, which is how anybody knows. nothing in the other category.","moments":["in the late sixties he spent time with the little brothers of jesus, a small religious order that follows charles de foucauld, a french soldier who became a hermit in the sahara. they work ordinary jobs and pray among the poor. he writes about months alone in a cave in the spanish desert.","the title all is grace comes from the diary of a country priest, a 1936 french novel by georges bernanos about a sick young priest in a village that doesn't want him. it's the priest's last words as he dies.","rich mullins loved the ragamuffin gospel so much he called his group the ragamuffin band. half the christian music scene of the nineties carried the book around."],"name":"brennan manning","place":"new york, then new orleans, then the road","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my deepest awareness of myself is that i am deeply loved by jesus christ and i have done nothing to earn it or deserve it.","where":"a line he used in talks and books, paraphrased"},{"q":"the gospel of grace is for the bedraggled, beat-up, and burnt-out.","where":"the ragamuffin gospel, his 1990 book, the opening pages, paraphrased"},{"q":"god loves you as you are, not as you should be, because you're never going to be as you should be.","where":"attributed to him in talks"}],"start":"the ragamuffin gospel, the first chapter. then all is grace, which is the same man telling you what was going on while he wrote the first one.","status":"approved","story":"born richard manning in new york in 1934. a marine in korea, then a franciscan priest, ordained in 1963. the franciscans are the order of friars that goes back to [[francis]] of assisi, men who promise to own nothing. he took the name brennan along the way. he was funny, charming, and a very good preacher.\nhe was also an alcoholic. he went into treatment in 1975, got sober, relapsed, got sober, relapsed. in 1982 he left the franciscans and the priesthood to marry a woman named roslyn, which a catholic priest isn't allowed to do. the marriage ended in divorce years later.\nthe ragamuffin gospel came out in 1990 and found everybody who'd been told they weren't good enough for church. a ragamuffin is a dirty, ragged kid. its whole argument is that grace, god's love that you can't earn and can't lose, is for the bedraggled, the beat up, and the burnt out, first. it was huge among evangelicals, the born again, bible-first protestants, a lot of whom had grown up being told to try harder. the singer rich mullins, who wrote the worship song awesome god, named his band after it.\nhe spent the next twenty years on the road preaching that to crowds. in 2011, old and failing, he put out a memoir called all is grace, which says he'd been drinking more than anyone knew and lying to cover it. he died in asheville, north carolina, in 2013.","struggle":"the drinking never let go of him. he'd preach grace to a room of thousands and go back to the hotel and drink alone. all is grace, the memoir, is frank about relapses he hid for years while the books kept selling.\nand under that, the shame about the shame. the man famous for telling everyone god loved them as they were couldn't stand himself as he was, and he says so in the memoir, more than once.","threads":["addiction","forgiveness"],"years":"1934 to 2013"},{"after":"when cassius was killed, cassius dio, a roman historian who lived through those years, says marcus was sorry he hadn't had the chance to pardon him, and burned the man's letters without reading them so he wouldn't have to know who else had been involved. he forgave the cities that had backed the revolt.\nand he kept writing, and he kept going back to the front. there's no turn in his story. he got up every morning and did the job, which is what the notebook keeps telling him to do, and he died doing it. the notebook doesn't pretend he was good at being who he wanted to be. that's why it's still useful.","basically":"a roman emperor who spent the last ten years of his life in cold army camps running a war he never wanted, and at night wrote in a private notebook telling himself to be patient, be decent, and remember he'd be dead soon. he never meant anyone to read it, and now it's on every nightstand from locker rooms to silicon valley. picture the most powerful person on earth keeping a notes app full of pep talks to himself, and the pep talks turn out to be good. take from him that the people running the world have to talk themselves out of bed too.","born":121,"died":180,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 268 C 60 258 100 278 140 268 S 220 258 260 268 S 340 278 380 268'/><path d='M 20 242 L 380 242'/><path d='M 50 242 L 110 130 L 170 242 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 110 130 L 110 242' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 96 242 L 110 200 L 124 242' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='230' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 230 184 L 230 226'/><path d='M 230 226 C 210 226 200 242 216 242 L 244 242 C 260 242 250 226 230 226'/><path d='M 230 200 L 216 226 M 230 200 L 244 226'/><path d='M 214 222 L 246 214 L 250 226 L 218 234 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 280 242 C 280 232 300 232 300 242 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 232 C 286 226 290 220 290 216 C 294 222 296 226 290 232 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='250' cy='40' r='2'/><circle cx='200' cy='80' r='2'/><circle cx='370' cy='110' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(110,108.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the most powerful man in the world kept a notebook telling himself to get out of bed.","key":"marcus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Aurelius","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the meditations, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2680","why":"an old translation of the notebook at project gutenberg"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/","why":"the stoic ideas he was living by, laid out properly"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"christians were killed under him. in 177 at lyon, in gaul, which is roughly france, a mob and then the authorities turned on the christian community. a slave girl named blandina and an old bishop named pothinus were among the dead. blandina was tortured from morning to night, and in the end thrown to a wild bull in the arena. the governor wrote to the emperor asking what to do with the ones who were roman citizens, and the answer, as the church historian eusebius preserves it about a hundred and fifty years later, was that the ones who denied the faith should go free and the ones who confessed should be executed. that's his answer, not somebody else's.\nand commodus. marcus made his teenage son co-emperor in 177 and left him everything. the four emperors before marcus had each picked the best man they could find and adopted him. marcus had a son, and picked him. commodus was a disaster, vain and cruel, who fought in the arena as a gladiator and was strangled in 192. he's the villain in the movie gladiator, loosely.","moments":["the first book of the meditations is just thanks. he goes through his grandfather, his mother, his teachers, one at a time, and says what each of them taught him. he thanks his teacher rusticus for lending him his copy of the lectures of [[epictetus]], the old slave turned teacher.","the bronze statue of him on horseback in rome survived the middle ages because people thought it was constantine, the first christian emperor, and didn't melt it down.","a line in book 11 sneers at people who die for a cause out of stubbornness, like the christians. some scholars think a later copyist added the word christians. either way, he didn't think much of them."],"name":"marcus aurelius","place":"rome, then army camps on the danube","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 32 50 C 36 36 48 30 60 30 C 72 30 84 36 88 50' opacity='.7'/><g fill='var(--sage)'><ellipse cx='36' cy='42' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(-40 36 42)'/><ellipse cx='44' cy='35' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(-60 44 35)'/><ellipse cx='53' cy='31' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(-80 53 31)'/><ellipse cx='84' cy='42' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(40 84 42)'/><ellipse cx='76' cy='35' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(60 76 35)'/><ellipse cx='67' cy='31' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(80 67 31)'/><\/g><path d='M 16 112 L 46 104 M 16 112 C 12 116 20 120 20 114 M 46 104 C 50 100 44 98 44 104' /><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"at dawn, when you don't want to get up, tell yourself, i'm getting up to do the work of a human being.","where":"the meditations, his private notebook, book 5, section 1, paraphrased"},{"q":"first thing in the morning, tell yourself you'll meet meddling, ungrateful, arrogant people today, and none of them can actually hurt you.","where":"meditations, book 2, section 1, paraphrased"},{"q":"see that you don't turn into a caesar, that you don't get dyed purple.","where":"meditations, book 6, section 30, paraphrased. purple was the emperor's color, and he's warning himself not to let the job soak in"}],"start":"meditations, book 2, section 1, then all of book 5. gregory hays's translation reads like it was written this year.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in rome in 121 to a rich family and noticed young by the emperor hadrian, the one who built the wall across northern england. roman emperors often picked their heirs by adoption instead of blood, and hadrian arranged it so marcus would be emperor one day. he wanted to be a philosopher. he got the empire instead, in 161, and for the first eight years he shared it with his adoptive brother lucius verus.\nnothing went right. a war with parthia, the rival empire in what's now iran. the soldiers came home carrying a plague, probably smallpox, that killed people across the empire for years. then the germanic tribes came over the danube, the river that was rome's northern border, and he spent most of the last decade of his life in army camps along it, a sick, tired man in his fifties running a war he never wanted.\nin those camps he wrote in greek, the language educated romans did their thinking in, a notebook for himself that we call the meditations. he never meant anyone to read it. book two is headed among the quadi, one of the tribes he was fighting, on the river gran, in what's now slovakia. book three, at carnuntum, a legion base near what's now vienna. it's mostly him talking himself down. be kind to the people who'll annoy you today. you're going to die soon. do the next thing well.\nhe died on campaign in 180 and left the empire to his son commodus. the notebook survived by luck and wasn't printed until the sixteenth century.","struggle":"his family died around him. his wife faustina bore him at least thirteen children and most of them died young, before him. faustina herself died in 175 at a village in cappadocia, in the middle of what's now turkey, while traveling with him in the east. he renamed the place after her.\nthe same year his best general in the east, avidius cassius, heard a false rumor that marcus was dead and declared himself emperor. his own officers killed him three months later. marcus spent the last years of his life sick, at war, and grieving, and the notebook is what that sounds like from inside.","threads":["war","grief","dying","work"],"years":"121 to 180"},{"after":"there wasn't time for much of an after. he got sick, and he spent the last year doing what he'd always done, talking, a little more quietly.\nhis brother dennis wrote the brotherhood of the screaming abyss, in 2012, and told the whole story, the genius and the con, with love. that book is the honest after. terence never wrote it himself.","basically":"a funny, brilliant, fast talking guy from colorado who went up the amazon with his brother in 1971, came back with wild ideas, and became the voice of psychedelics in the nineties. his talks were passed around on cassettes and now fill youtube. he said magic mushrooms made us human and that time would end in 2012. neither is true. picture the most charismatic podcaster you've ever heard, mixing real knowledge with things he made up, and never letting you see the seam. enjoy him, and check everything.","born":1946,"died":2000,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 C 100 240 180 260 260 250 S 360 244 380 250 L 380 290 L 20 290 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 60 240 L 60 115'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='103' rx='35' ry='26' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 340 236 L 340 126'/><ellipse cx='340' cy='116' rx='30' ry='23' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='170' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 170 134 L 170 182'/><path d='M 170 182 L 156 216 M 170 182 L 184 216'/><path d='M 170 148 L 144 150 M 170 148 L 196 150'/><path d='M 165 125 Q 170 129 175 125'/><path d='M 200 110 C 214 100 222 114 236 104 C 250 94 258 110 272 100 M 204 126 C 222 120 232 132 250 124' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 230 268 C 250 280 300 280 320 268 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(46,128.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the best talker psychedelics ever had spent the nineties telling people to take huge doses alone in the dark, while privately too scared to take them himself.","key":"mckenna","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terence_McKenna","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoned_ape_theory","why":"his idea about human evolution, and why scientists don't accept it"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he's sloppy with the truth, and that's the mess. he mixed real botany and history with things that were just wrong, like the stoned ape idea and the 2012 prediction, and delivered all of it in the same confident voice. people took his word for it and still do.\nworse, all through the nineties, while he told crowds to take heroic doses alone in the dark, he'd stopped taking mushrooms himself after a trip that scared him. he didn't tell them that. his brother did, twelve years after he died. the risks he talked around are real. terror, psychotic breaks in people prone to them, and the danger of doing it alone with no one there.","moments":["in 1985 he and his partner kathleen harrison started botanical dimensions, a garden in hawaii to save medicinal plants from the rainforest.","people recorded his talks on cassette and traded them, and a lot of them are still circulating, sometimes set to space footage."],"name":"terence mckenna","place":"colorado, berkeley, the amazon, hawaii","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"nature loves courage.","where":"his talks in the nineties, attributed; it's the line people quote most"},{"q":"culture is not your friend.","where":"his talks, attributed"},{"q":"the mushroom was the catalyst that made us human.","where":"food of the gods, 1992, paraphrased. this is the stoned ape idea, and it's not accepted science"}],"start":"the brotherhood of the screaming abyss, his brother dennis's memoir, before anything terence wrote. then food of the gods if you want to see how he argued, with the warning that the big idea in it is wrong.","status":"approved","story":"he grew up in a small town in colorado, got into science fiction and plants, and studied ecology and shamanism at berkeley in the late sixties. shamanism is the old practice, in many cultures, of a healer who goes into altered states for the community.\nin 1971 he and his younger brother dennis, with a few friends, went to la chorrera in the colombian amazon looking for a plant drug. what happened there, by their account, was mostly mushrooms and a strange experiment built around mushrooms and sound, after which dennis was in a kind of breakdown for days. the brothers wrote a book about it, the invisible landscape, in 1975. dennis became a respected ethnopharmacologist, a scientist who studies plant drugs. terence became a talker.\nby the late eighties he was the star of the lecture circuit, at esalen, the retreat center on the big sur coast, at raves, on radio. he pushed what he called the heroic dose, his phrase for taking a lot, alone, in silent darkness. his book food of the gods, from 1992, put forward the stoned ape idea, that early humans eating mushrooms on the african plains gave us language and consciousness. it's not accepted science. there's no evidence for it. he also built a theory that time itself would climax in december 2012. it didn't.\nin 1999 he had a seizure, and it was a brain tumor, glioblastoma, the worst kind. he died in california in april 2000, at fifty three, talking about death to the end.","struggle":"at the end of the eighties, by his brother's account, he had a bad trip on mushrooms, a terrifying vision of total meaninglessness, and he was badly shaken. dennis wrote in 2012 that terence never took mushrooms again after it, and only rarely and nervously took ayahuasca or dmt.\nthen the tumor in 1999. he'd spent his life talking about death as the great adventure, and he got about a year to find out. in his last interviews he was honest that he was frightened and also curious.","threads":["psychedelics","fame","dying","wandering"],"years":"1946 to 2000"},{"after":"he ended it. in september 1966 he made a formal, permanent commitment to live as a hermit, alone in a cinder block cabin in the woods up the hill from the monastery, and kept it. he didn't burn the journals. he ordered them published twenty five years after his death, all of it, so nobody could turn him into a plaster saint.\nand then he went east, which was the answer to the other doubt. the last big thing he wrote in his journal came at polonnaruwa, a ruined old city in sri lanka with enormous buddhas carved out of the rock. he wrote that he'd finally seen what he'd been looking for and the whole thing was clear. a week later he was dead.","basically":"a wild young guy in the 1930s, drinking and chasing women through two universities, who walked into a silent monastery in the kentucky woods at twenty six and wrote a memoir about it that became a bestseller. he spent the rest of his life as the most famous monk in america, writing against war and racism from a place he wasn't supposed to leave, and falling in love once when he wasn't supposed to. he's the catholic who went looking for god in buddhism without leaving his own church. if you want to know whether you can go all the way into one tradition and still learn from the others, he's the proof.","born":1915,"died":1968,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 170 L 150 170 L 150 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 172 L 105 130 L 160 172'/><path d='M 92 250 L 92 200 L 120 200 L 120 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='106' cy='200' r='9'/><path d='M 106 214 L 106 256'/><path d='M 106 256 C 86 256 76 272 92 272 L 120 272 C 136 272 126 256 106 256'/><path d='M 106 230 L 92 256 M 106 230 L 120 256'/><path d='M 150 250 C 200 245 240 255 290 250 S 360 245 390 250' stroke-dasharray='3 9' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 250 120 L 290 108 L 296 132 L 256 144 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 120 L 273 132 L 290 108'/><path d='M 200 150 C 220 130 235 125 250 122' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='340' cy='70' r='20' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 368 70 L 378 70'/><path d='M 312 70 L 302 70'/><path d='M 340 98 L 340 108'/><path d='M 340 42 L 340 32'/><path d='M 360 90 L 367 97'/><path d='M 320 90 L 313 97'/><path d='M 360 50 L 367 43'/><path d='M 320 50 L 313 43'/><g transform='translate(35,220.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the most famous monk in america wrote down everything, including the parts a monk isn't supposed to have.","key":"merton","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Merton","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the merton center","url":"https://merton.org/","why":"the thomas merton center at bellarmine university in louisville, which keeps his papers"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Storey_Mountain","why":"the memoir that made him famous"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"in 1966, at fifty one and a monk for twenty five years, he fell in love with a student nurse half his age while in the hospital for back surgery. he saw her secretly for months and wrote it all in his journal, which he ordered published after his death. it was with an adult, he ended it, and he told on himself. the child he fathered in england and never acknowledged is the part he never fixed.","moments":["in 1937 he bought a book on medieval philosophy, saw the imprimatur inside the cover, the catholic church's official stamp saying a book is approved, and nearly threw it out the train window. he read it instead. it was the beginning.","he entered the monastery on december 10, 1941, three days after pearl harbor, the japanese attack that pulled america into world war two, and died on december 10, 1968. he'd have noticed.","joan baez, the folk singer, drove out to see him at the hermitage in december 1966 and they talked for an afternoon. he was in the middle of the worst year of his life and didn't tell her."],"name":"thomas merton","place":"france, new york, a monastery in kentucky","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have no idea where i am going. i do not see the road ahead of me. but i believe the desire to please you does in fact please you.","where":"thoughts in solitude, his short 1958 book of reflections, the famous prayer, paraphrased"},{"q":"at the corner of fourth and walnut i suddenly realized i loved all these people, that they were mine and i theirs.","where":"conjectures of a guilty bystander, his 1966 book of notebook entries, paraphrased. fourth and walnut is a busy street corner in downtown louisville"},{"q":"nhat hanh is my brother. he is more my brother than many who are nearer to me by race and nationality.","where":"his 1966 essay, nhat hanh is my brother"}],"start":"the seven storey mountain if you want the story. new seeds of contemplation, his short book on what praying quietly actually is, if you want the teaching. the asian journal, his notebooks from the last trip, if you want the end.","status":"approved","story":"orphaned young, his mother dead when he was six and his father when he was sixteen. he drank and slept around through cambridge in england, where he fathered a child he never met, and then columbia in new york. then in 1941 he walked into a trappist monastery in kentucky, the abbey of gethsemani, and stayed twenty seven years. trappists are the strictest kind of catholic monk, up in the middle of the night to pray, mostly silent, farming, owning nothing. the seven storey mountain, his memoir of getting there, named for the mountain souls climb to get clean in dante's old poem, came out in 1948 and became a bestseller, and a lot of young men joined monasteries because of it.\nhe never stopped writing, which trappists mostly don't do. seventy books, thousands of letters. by the early sixties he was writing against the bomb, against racism, against the war, and his order kept telling him to knock it off.\nthen he turned east. he read the zen masters, the chinese and japanese buddhist teachers famous for sitting still and saying short strange things. he wrote letters to d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who more or less introduced zen to the west, and met him in new york in 1964. he met [[thay|thich nhat hanh]], a vietnamese buddhist monk, at his monastery in 1966 and called him his brother. in 1968 he finally got permission to go to asia. he met the dalai lama, the head of tibetan buddhism, living in exile in india, three times in dharamsala that november. on december 10 in bangkok, after giving a talk, he was found dead in his room, apparently electrocuted by a faulty fan. same day of the year he'd entered the monastery.\nhe's the bridge. almost every christian on these pages who talks to the east walked across him.","struggle":"he spent years wanting out. not out of the church, out of gethsemani, his monastery, to a stricter and lonelier order, the carthusians, monks who live alone in separate cells and barely see each other. his abbot, the head of the monastery, kept saying no. he wrote about it in his journal like a man scratching at a locked door.\nthe affair in 1966 wasn't a slip. it was months. he wrote that he'd never been loved like that and didn't know what to do with it. he was fifty one and he'd been a monk for twenty five years and he was, by his own account, a fool in love.\nunder all of it was a doubt he never fully put down. was the famous monk, the bestseller, the letters from everywhere, anything to do with god at all, or had he just found a more interesting way to be thomas merton.","threads":["silence","love","fame"],"years":"1915 to 1968"},{"after":"the after is the whole rest of his life, sixty years of it. he didn't build a monastery or write a rulebook. he sat in caves and sang, and people came up the mountain to hear a murderer explain how it feels to put it down.\nat the end, the story goes, a jealous geshe, a tibetan monk with the equivalent of a doctorate in religion, who couldn't stand a cave singer getting all the devotion, had him poisoned. milarepa knew, took the poison anyway, and forgave the man to his face before he died. that's the last song.","basically":"a tibetan kid in the eleventh century whose uncle robbed his family blind after his father died, so his mother sent him off to learn black magic, and he used it to kill thirty five people. then the guilt ate him alive. he spent years hauling rocks for a brutal teacher and decades alone in mountain caves, half starved, singing what he'd learned. he's tibet's favorite saint. if you think you've done something too bad to come back from, he's the one they point to.","born":1052,"died":1135,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 L 90 120 L 150 180 L 210 100 L 280 190 L 340 130 L 380 180' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 120 260 C 110 190 190 170 230 200 C 260 220 250 260 250 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='185' cy='202' r='12' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 185 216 L 185 258'/><path d='M 185 258 C 165 258 155 274 171 274 L 199 274 C 215 274 205 258 185 258'/><path d='M 185 232 L 171 258 M 185 232 L 199 258'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 300 260 L 300 200 L 340 200 L 340 260 M 300 220 L 340 220 M 300 240 L 340 240 M 320 200 L 320 220 M 310 220 L 310 240 M 330 220 L 330 240'/><path d='M 306 200 L 306 186 L 322 186' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(60,239.0) scale(1.4)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he killed thirty five people with a hailstorm, and tibet's favorite saint is what he did about it.","key":"milarepa","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milarepa","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marpa_Lotsawa","why":"the teacher who made him build the towers"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"thirty five people, killed on purpose, as a young man. it doesn't get worse than that, and he spent sixty years on it. the story is the point. nobody's too far gone, and the proof is a murderer.","moments":["every painting of him has one hand cupped at his ear. some say he's listening to the songs coming. some say he's listening for the echo of his own.","his two great students were rechungpa, who kept wandering off, and gampopa, a doctor who became a monk after his wife died, and who turned the cave songs into an actual lineage, a line of teachers passed hand to hand. it's called the kagyu, one of the four big schools of tibetan buddhism, and mingyur rinpoche comes out of it.","he wore a single cotton robe through the himalayan winter, kept warm by a meditation practice for making heat inside the body. the tibetan word for someone who lives like that in cotton is repa. that's the second half of his name. mila was his family's."],"name":"milarepa","place":"the mountains of western tibet","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 60 C 104 52 110 62 106 72 C 102 80 92 78 90 70' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my religion is to live and die without regret.","where":"the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, the big collection of his songs his followers passed down, paraphrased"},{"q":"in the beginning nothing comes. in the middle nothing stays. in the end nothing goes.","where":"attributed to him in the songs; the sequence is his"},{"q":"i have no need for a big temple. the cave is fine and the sky doesn't leak.","where":"the life of milarepa, his life story as tibetans tell it, paraphrased from the cave songs"}],"start":"the life of milarepa, written down around 1488, about three hundred and fifty years after he died, by tsangnyön heruka, a wandering tibetan yogi, somebody who meditates full time out in the wild, known as the madman of tsang. read it in lobsang lhalungpa's or andrew quintman's translation. then dip into the hundred thousand songs.","status":"approved","story":"his father died when he was a boy, and an uncle and aunt took the family's land and everything in it and worked his mother and sister like servants. his mother never forgave it. she sent him off to learn black magic for revenge, the kind of curse-for-hire sorcery tibetans took completely seriously then. he learned it. the story says he brought a house down on his uncle's family during a wedding feast and killed thirty five people, then wrecked the village's harvest with a hailstorm. he was maybe twenty.\nthe guilt got him. he went looking for a teacher who'd take a murderer and found marpa, a farmer and family man with a temper who'd made the dangerous trek over the himalayas to india more than once to bring buddhist teachings back to tibet and translate them. marpa refused to teach him anything. instead he made him build a stone tower by hand, alone, then tear it down and carry the stones back where they came from, then build another somewhere else, again and again. milarepa's back was one open sore. he nearly gave up. marpa finally gave him the teachings, the meditation instructions he'd carried back from india, and told him the towers had been the teachings. all that hauling had been working off his karma, the weight of what he'd done coming back to him.\nhe went into the caves in the mountains and stayed. there was no food but the nettles growing outside, and he ate so many that his skin turned green. he sang everything he understood, and the songs are still sung. hunters and villagers came across him and he sang to them, and some of them stayed.\nwhen the songs talk about how good it feels to sit alone in a cave with nothing, they're coming from a man who had a mass killing to work off. that's the whole reason tibet loves him. nobody's too far gone. imagine the most beloved preacher in your town being a man who'd killed people and told you so every time he opened his mouth.","struggle":"the guilt was the engine. after the killings he couldn't sleep, and the story has him going from teacher to teacher looking for anyone who'd take on a murderer.\nmarpa took him and broke him. build the tower, tear it down, build it over there, tear it down, his back an open sore, and every time he asked for the teachings marpa cursed him and threw him out. he walked away more than once. marpa's wife, dagmema, felt sorry for him, snuck him food, and forged a letter in marpa's name sending him to another of marpa's students, a teacher named ngokpa. milarepa meditated there and nothing happened, because it wasn't the teachings that needed fixing.\nthen years alone in caves with nothing. his sister found him once, green and skeletal from living on nettles, and screamed.","threads":["anger","forgiveness","work"],"years":"around 1052 to 1135"},{"after":"what he did with the panic was stop running from it. he let it in, looked at it, and it came apart, because it had needed the running. that's the whole teaching and it came from a scared kid, not a sage.\nwhat he did with the shame at the train station was hold the bowl out anyway. what he did with the dying at kushinagar, where the buddha died, was let go of the last thing, being a rinpoche, being somebody, and he says the joy that showed up was the point of the whole trip. then he walked back into the monastery four and a half years later, sat down, and went back to work.","basically":"a tibetan monk born in 1975 who had panic attacks as a kid and figured out how to stop them by sitting still and letting them in. now he teaches that all over the world with a big grin. in his thirties he walked out on his own monasteries in the night to live as a beggar, and nearly died doing it. if you've ever had your chest lock up at 3am, he's the one on these pages who actually knows what that's like.","born":1975,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='200' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 200 134 L 200 176'/><path d='M 200 176 C 180 176 170 192 186 192 L 214 192 C 230 192 220 176 200 176'/><path d='M 200 150 L 186 176 M 200 150 L 214 176'/><path d='M 195 125 Q 200 129 205 125'/><path d='M 165 60 C 150 40 190 30 200 50 C 215 25 250 40 235 60 C 255 70 230 90 215 75 C 200 95 170 85 180 68 C 160 75 150 70 165 60' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 45 L 270 32 M 262 62 L 285 60 M 245 25 L 258 12' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 250 L 380 250 M 300 262 L 380 262 M 315 250 L 315 262 M 335 250 L 335 262 M 355 250 L 355 262 M 375 250 L 375 262'/><g transform='translate(56,220.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he had panic attacks as a kid, got sent to a three year retreat at thirteen, and it got worse before it got better.","key":"mingyur","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yongey_Mingyur_Rinpoche","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his community","url":"https://tergar.org/","why":"tergar, the meditation network he runs, with short guided meditations"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of. he did abandon a lot of responsibilities and a lot of people to go on the wandering retreat, and he's honest that it scared everyone who worked for him.","moments":["his father, tulku urgyen rinpoche, taught him to meditate at nine by pointing at his own mind and saying, roughly, look, that's it, you're done.","in 2002 he let scientists at the university of wisconsin, in richard davidson's lab, put him in a brain scanner while he meditated, and the results helped kick off the whole science-of-meditation era you see in the headlines now. he thinks it's funny that people needed the machine.","he tells the story about his own panic in almost every talk, with a grin, like a guy showing you a scar."],"name":"yongey mingyur rinpoche","place":"nubri, nepal, then everywhere","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the harder you chase calm, the faster it runs.","where":"the joy of living, his first book, paraphrased"},{"q":"you can't stop the waves but you can learn to surf. actually you already know how, you just forgot.","where":"his talks, paraphrased"},{"q":"i was lying in the dirt dying and i'd never been happier.","where":"in love with the world, his book about the wandering years, paraphrased"}],"start":"the joy of living, his first book, which is about the panic and the brain science. then in love with the world, which is the wandering retreat and the food poisoning. tergar, the network of meditation groups he runs, has guided meditations that are short and plain.","status":"approved","story":"his father was tulku urgyen rinpoche, one of the great tibetan meditation teachers of the last century. rinpoche is a title tibetans give their important teachers, it means precious one, and mingyur was named one as a small boy, recognized as the rebirth of a teacher who'd died long before. he grew up in the himalayas with panic attacks so bad he thought he was dying. real ones. the kind that put you on the floor.\nat thirteen he entered a three year retreat, which is what it sounds like. three years in a closed building with a small group, meditating from before dawn into the night, not coming out. the first year the panic got worse. then he stopped fighting it and let it in and watched it, and it fell apart in a few days. it had needed the fight to stay alive.\nin 2011, running a network of monasteries and a teaching schedule on several continents, he walked out of his monastery in the middle of the night with almost no money and no plan, to do the old wandering retreat. that's an ancient practice in tibet and india where a monk leaves everything and lives on the road, sleeping wherever and eating whatever people give him. think of a ceo leaving a note on the desk and walking out with a toothbrush. not long after, at kushinagar, the town in india where [[buddha|the buddha]] died, he got food poisoning so bad he lay on the ground for days and nearly died. he says that was the best thing that ever happened to him. he came back four and a half years later.\nhe teaches that awareness, the plain part of you that notices things, is already there under whatever you're feeling, and you don't have to fix the feeling to find it. panic was his teacher. he says that without irony.","struggle":"the panic attacks started around age six. he describes lying in bed in the mountains certain he was dying, night after night, and nobody around him with a name for it. this was nepal in the early eighties. nobody was saying panic disorder.\nthe first year of the three year retreat was worse, not better. he was thirteen and shut in a building with the thing he was most afraid of and no way out but through. he says he sat there and hated it.\non the wandering retreat, the first night, sleeping on the floor of the train station in varanasi, a big crowded city on the ganges, he was so ashamed of begging that he couldn't do it. a famous teacher with monasteries on three continents, unable to hold out a bowl for food the way monks have for twenty five hundred years. he went hungry rather than ask.","threads":["madness","wandering","joy"],"years":"born 1975"},{"after":"she left. that's the after. no reconciliation with the palace, no triumphant return. the story goes that the family later sent brahmin priests to dwarka to bring her back, and she went into the temple and didn't come out.\nand the songs outlived the kingdom. five centuries on they're sung by women at weddings and by beggars on trains. m.s. subbulakshmi, the great singer from south india, played her in a film in 1945 and sang her songs for the rest of her life. [[gandhi]]'s ashram sang them at prayers, and when a british admiral's daughter, madeleine slade, came to live there, he named her mirabehn, sister mira.","basically":"a sixteenth century indian princess who decided as a little girl that the god krishna was her husband, and never backed off it, not for her in-laws, not for the palace, not when, the stories say, they tried to poison her. when her husband died she wouldn't play the royal widow and went off singing and dancing in the temples with beggars instead. she's the patron saint of every woman who was told to be quiet and wasn't. her songs are still sung all over india, at weddings and on trains.","born":1498,"died":1546,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 L 20 150 L 130 150 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 150 L 20 140 L 32 140 L 32 150 M 48 150 L 48 140 L 60 140 L 60 150 M 76 150 L 76 140 L 88 140 L 88 150 M 104 150 L 104 140 L 116 140 L 116 150'/><path d='M 50 140 C 50 118 90 118 90 140' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 70 120 L 70 110'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 214 C 60 200 80 200 80 214 L 80 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 140 256 C 200 262 300 254 390 258' stroke-dasharray='3 8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 222 172 C 196 196 190 226 196 244 L 262 244 C 268 226 262 196 236 172 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='229' cy='118' r='14'/><path d='M 224 123 Q 229 127 234 123'/><path d='M 213 112 C 214 96 244 96 245 112 C 248 124 246 136 244 144' fill='none'/><path d='M 229 132 L 229 172 M 229 146 L 206 120 M 229 146 L 252 120'/><path d='M 214 244 L 210 250 M 244 244 L 250 250'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'><circle cx='206' cy='250' r='2'/><circle cx='212' cy='252' r='2'/><circle cx='248' cy='252' r='2'/><circle cx='254' cy='250' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 174 234 C 192 236 194 252 176 254 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 175 244 L 160 245 M 160 238 L 160 252'/><g transform='translate(104,118.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a rajput princess said krishna was her husband, and when her in-laws sent poison she drank it and kept singing.","key":"mirabai","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirabai","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/mirabai","why":"a short life and some of her songs in english"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing i know of. the history is thin and the legend is thick. we don't really know what the family did to her, only what the songs say, and the songs were on her side.","moments":["the story goes that a great scholar monk in vrindavan wouldn't see her, because he didn't meet women. she sent back word that she'd thought the only man in vrindavan was krishna, and everyone else was one of his girls. he came out.","the tradition says her guru was ravidas, a cobbler, from the people at the bottom of the hindu caste ranking, the ones higher castes called untouchable and wouldn't eat with. some of her songs name him. historians aren't sure the dates line up.","she's usually shown with an ektara, a one stringed instrument wandering singers carry, and bells on her ankles."],"name":"mirabai","place":"merta and chittor in rajasthan, then the road to vrindavan and dwarka","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 38 46 C 44 34 76 34 82 46 C 74 41 46 41 38 46 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 29 110 C 24 64 32 30 60 28 C 88 30 96 64 91 110' opacity='.4' stroke-dasharray='1 5'/><path d='M 106 130 L 110 72' stroke-width='3'/><ellipse cx='104' cy='120' rx='8' ry='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"mine is the mountain lifter, gopal, and nobody else.","where":"the song mere to giridhar gopal, paraphrased. giridhar, mountain lifter, is krishna, from the story where he holds a whole hill up on one finger to keep a storm off his village. gopal means cowherd"},{"q":"mira tied bells on her ankles and danced.","where":"the song pag ghunghroo bandh mira nachi, the first line"},{"q":"the rana sent a cup of poison, and mira laughed as she drank it.","where":"the same song, a later verse, paraphrased; the verses change from singer to singer"}],"start":"for love of the dark one, andrew schelling's translations, which are short and alive. then find the songs sung, m.s. subbulakshmi's first.","status":"approved","story":"mira was born around 1498 into the rathore royal family of merta, in rajasthan, the desert country of northwest india. the rathores were rajputs, the warrior clans who ruled that country from hill forts. the story goes that as a little girl she saw a wedding procession go by and asked her mother who her husband would be, and her mother, to shut her up, pointed at a small image of [[krishna]], the blue-skinned cowherd god hindus love like a sweetheart. she took it seriously for the rest of her life.\naround 1516 she was married to bhoj raj, crown prince of mewar, the proudest of the rajput houses, at chittor, their hill fortress. within a few years he was dead. a royal widow in that world could be expected to commit sati, to climb onto her husband's funeral fire and burn with him. the story goes she refused, and said her husband was alive, and it was krishna.\na widow of the royal house singing and dancing in temples in front of wandering holy men was a scandal, and the new rana, the new king, wanted it stopped. the stories say they tried to kill her. a cup of poison sent to her as holy water, and she drank it and nothing happened. a snake in a flower basket, and when she opened it, a garland.\nshe left the palace and walked. to vrindavan, the town where krishna grew up, and then to dwarka on the western coast, the city he ruled as a grown king. the story goes that there, in the temple, she went into the image of krishna and never came out. how many of the songs sung as hers are really hers nobody knows. they're sung all over india today.","struggle":"a young widow in a court that wanted her dead or quiet. the songs are full of it. the rana is angry, the family calls her shameless, the town talks. lok laj, what people will say, runs all through her songs, and she keeps saying she's thrown it off.\nand the songs aren't all ecstasy. a lot of them are a woman waiting for a lover who doesn't come. she can't sleep, she can't eat, she asks the rain clouds for news of him. the ache of separation is half of what she sang.","threads":["love","family","wandering"],"years":"around 1498 to 1546"},{"after":"after the pulitzer he could have written the same book again. he didn't. he taught at berkeley, stanford and the university of arizona, painted, wrote poems and plays and memoirs, and kept going back to the same few things, the rock tree, his grandmother, the bear, the land.\nin his old age he talked about the land as sacred in the plainest way, in lectures and interviews, like it wasn't a controversial thing to say. he'd spent sixty years making it sound obvious.","basically":"a kiowa writer with a huge voice and a huge mustache, whose first novel won the pulitzer prize in 1969, the first time the prize went to a native american. it cracked the door open for every native writer since, louise erdrich, sherman alexie, tommy orange, all of them. then he wrote a small book about his grandmother and his people's long walk from montana to oklahoma that's one of the most beautiful things in american prose. think of the first indigenous name on the bestseller shelf, and the one the others still thank. take from him that your people's story is also your own, and you're allowed to tell it slowly.","born":1934,"died":2024,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 180 260 L 196 110 C 200 96 244 96 248 110 L 264 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 206 260 L 212 108 M 222 260 L 222 102 M 238 260 L 232 108' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='100' cy='164' r='14'/><path d='M 100 178 L 100 226'/><path d='M 100 226 L 86 260 M 100 226 L 114 260'/><path d='M 100 192 L 84 216 M 100 192 L 116 216'/><path d='M 92 172 C 96 168 104 168 108 172' stroke-width='3'/><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='290' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='310' cy='46' r='3'/><circle cx='330' cy='44' r='3'/><circle cx='346' cy='54' r='3'/><circle cx='350' cy='74' r='3'/><circle cx='372' cy='76' r='3'/><circle cx='372' cy='56' r='3'/><\/g><path d='M 300 260 C 310 246 330 246 340 260' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(222,83.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"as a baby they took him to a giant rock tower in wyoming and named him after it, and he spent his life writing his way back.","key":"momaday","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N._Scott_Momaday","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/n-scott-momaday","why":"a life and some poems"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Way_to_Rainy_Mountain","why":"the small book most people should start with"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the kiowa story says devils tower was a tree that grew up into the sky to save seven sisters from a bear, and they became the stars of the big dipper. he tells it in the way to rainy mountain.","the delight song of tsoai-talee, his most loved poem, is a long list of all the things he is, a feather, a fish, a shadow, and it ends by saying he is alive."],"name":"n. scott momaday","place":"lawton, oklahoma, then reservations in arizona and new mexico, then stanford, arizona and santa fe","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"an indian is an idea which a given man has of himself.","where":"his essay the man made of words, 1970"},{"q":"a single knoll rises out of the plain in oklahoma, north and west of the wichita range.","where":"the way to rainy mountain, the first line of the introduction"},{"q":"we are what we imagine.","where":"the man made of words, 1970"}],"start":"the way to rainy mountain, the introduction, which is short and might be the best thing he wrote. then house made of dawn.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on february 27, 1934, in lawton, oklahoma. his father, al momaday, was a kiowa painter, and his mother, natachee scott, was a writer of mostly white and some cherokee descent. the kiowa are a plains people who came down from montana to the southern plains, and were forced onto a reservation in oklahoma in 1867. as a baby he was taken to devils tower in wyoming, a huge rock tower the kiowa call tsoai, the rock tree, and given the name tsoai-talee, rock tree boy.\nhis parents taught school on reservations across arizona and new mexico, among navajo, apache and pueblo people, and from twelve he grew up at jemez pueblo in new mexico. he went on to stanford and finished a doctorate in english in 1963.\nhis first novel, house made of dawn, came out in 1968. it follows abel, a young man from a new mexico pueblo who comes home from world war ii and can't fit back in. he kills a man, goes to prison, drifts in los angeles and drinks, and finally goes home, where his grandfather is dying, and runs at dawn with the runners of the old ceremony. it won the pulitzer prize for fiction in 1969, and critics started calling what came after it the native american renaissance.\nthe same year came the way to rainy mountain. it follows the kiowa migration from montana to oklahoma, told three ways at once on facing pages, the old story, the history, and his own memory, and it's built around the death of his grandmother aho and his trip back to her grave at rainy mountain, a hill in oklahoma. he taught for decades, painted, wrote poems, and got the national medal of arts in 2007. he died in santa fe on january 24, 2024, at eighty nine.","struggle":"he didn't put his private troubles on display, and i don't know much about them. what he wrote about was loss. the kiowa had lost the buffalo, the land, and the sun dance, and in the way to rainy mountain he says the last kiowa sun dance was held in 1887, and his grandmother was there as a child.\nand house made of dawn is about a veteran who can't find his way back into his own life, and drinks, and ends up in prison. it's not a book about him. it's a book by somebody who'd watched that happen to people he knew.","threads":["the land","grief","family"],"years":"1934 to 2024"},{"after":"after he killed the egyptian, he ran, and spent forty years as a shepherd for his father in law. the bush found him there, an eighty year old man who said he couldn't talk, tending someone else's sheep. the after was four decades of nothing, and then everything.\nafter the rock, when he was told he'd never enter the land, he didn't argue. deuteronomy, the fifth book of the bible, is him at the border, retelling the whole story to the kids who'd make it in, giving them the law one more time, and then walking up the mountain alone to look at what he wouldn't get. nobody knows where he's buried. the book says god did it himself.","basically":"the central figure of the hebrew bible, the jewish scriptures that christians call the old testament, the man who, as the story goes, led a nation of slaves out of egypt and came down a mountain with the ten commandments. he was also a fugitive who'd killed a man, and a bad talker who tried five different excuses to get out of the job. after forty years walking his people through the desert, he was told he couldn't go into the land he'd been walking them to. if you've ever been handed something way too big and said please, pick someone else, he said it first, to god's face.","born":-1300,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 120 154 L 120 202'/><path d='M 120 202 L 106 236 M 120 202 L 134 236'/><path d='M 120 168 L 106 192 M 120 168 L 146 162'/><path d='M 96 250 C 97 244 107 244 108 250 M 82 250 C 83 244 93 244 94 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 250 C 180 250 170 220 190 210 C 180 190 210 180 215 195 C 225 175 250 185 240 205 C 260 210 250 240 230 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 200 235 C 195 220 210 215 206 200 M 220 240 C 228 225 216 215 226 200 M 212 250 C 208 238 220 232 214 220' stroke='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 300 250 L 350 140 L 400 250' opacity='.7'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='350' cy='122' r='7'/><path d='M 350 136 L 350 150'/><path d='M 350 184 L 336 218 M 350 184 L 364 218'/><path d='M 350 150 L 336 174 M 350 150 L 376 144'/><\/g><g transform='translate(216,168.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a stuttering fugitive who argued with a burning bush, asked god for a name, and got told i am who i am.","key":"moses","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moses","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"exodus, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/exo.htm","why":"the book of exodus in the king james version"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he killed a man in egypt and hid the body in the sand. later the book has him ordering a lot of killing in god's name. while he was up the mountain the people made a golden calf to worship, and when he came down, about three thousand of them were killed in a day on his orders. the book doesn't flinch. neither should you.","moments":["his father in law jethro, a priest of midian, a desert people, not an israelite, watched him judging everybody's disputes from morning to night and told him he'd wear himself out, delegate. moses did. the first management consultant was an outsider who worshipped other gods.","he asked to see god's face and was told nobody sees that and lives, but he could stand in a crack in the rock and see god's back going by. he came down the mountain glowing and had to wear a veil.","in numbers 12 his own brother and sister, aaron and miriam, complained about his wife, a woman from cush, the kingdom south of egypt. god took moses's side and struck miriam with a skin disease for a week. the text calls him the most humble man on the face of the earth, which is a strange thing to find in a book tradition says he wrote himself."],"name":"moses","place":"egypt, then the desert for forty years","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 104 130 L 108 40' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 60 18 L 60 8 M 42 24 L 36 16 M 78 24 L 84 16' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"who am i to go to pharaoh?","where":"exodus 3:11, which means the book of exodus, chapter 3, verse 11. exodus is the second book of the bible, the escape from egypt. read the whole of chapters 3 and 4, the argument"},{"q":"show me your glory.","where":"exodus 33:18; read the whole chapter, he only gets the back"},{"q":"please, lord, send someone else.","where":"exodus 4:13"}],"start":"exodus 3 and 4 for the bush and the argument. exodus 33 for the back of god. deuteronomy 34 for the ending, which is short and it's the best thing in the torah, the first five books of the bible, which tradition says he wrote himself.","status":"approved","story":"the story goes that the israelites, the hebrew tribes who became the jewish people, were slaves in egypt, and pharaoh, the king, ordered every newborn israelite boy thrown in the nile. moses's mother hid him in a basket in the reeds by the river, and pharaoh's own daughter found him and raised him in the palace. as a young man he saw an egyptian overseer beating a hebrew slave, killed the overseer, hid the body in the sand, and ran for the desert. years later he was tending his father in law's sheep on a mountain when a bush caught fire and didn't burn up, and a voice told him to go back and get his people out.\nhe tried to get out of it five times. i'm nobody. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk. send someone else. god got annoyed and sent his brother aaron along to do the speaking. when moses asked for a name, he got i am who i am, which isn't a name. it's a refusal to be one.\nhe led them out, and the story says the sea split and they walked across on dry ground with the egyptian army behind them. then into the desert and up mount sinai, where he got the law, the ten commandments on stone tablets and hundreds of other rules for how to live. he asked to see god's face and was told nobody sees that and lives, but he could see god's back going by. he came down glowing and had to wear a veil because it scared people.\nhe wandered forty years with people who complained the entire time. once they were dying of thirst and god told him to speak to a rock and water would come out. moses was so fed up he yelled at them and hit the rock with his staff instead, twice. the water came anyway. but for that, he was shown the promised land, canaan, roughly where israel is now, from a mountaintop, and told he couldn't go in. he died up there. nobody knows where he's buried.","struggle":"he didn't want the job and said so five times. i'm nobody. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk. please send someone else. god got angry and sent him anyway, with his brother aaron to do the speaking.\nthen forty years in the desert with people who complained about everything, and in numbers 11, the book of the bible about the desert years, when they were all crying for meat, he finally breaks. why did you lay the burden of all these people on me? did i give birth to them? if this is how you're going to treat me, kill me now. that's in the bible. a prophet asking god to kill him because he's tired of everybody.","threads":["exile","wandering"],"years":"the story is set maybe 3300 years ago"},{"after":"she kept going. every day for about fifty years she got up before dawn, prayed into a dark she couldn't feel anything in, and went and washed people. she didn't tell the crowds. she told her confessors, in writing, in detail, and let them see all of it.\nin 1961 joseph neuner, the jesuit she finally told the whole thing to, told her the darkness might be part of the work, the same abandonment the people on the street felt, the feeling of being wanted by nobody. she wrote back that for the first time in eleven years she'd come to love it. it didn't lift. she just stopped reading it as proof she'd failed. she never made the other things right, the keating money or the pain in the homes. both halves of her stand.","basically":"an albanian nun who walked out of a comfortable convent school in calcutta in 1948 to live in the slums and pick dying people up off the street, and became one of the most famous women on earth doing it. after she died her private letters showed that for almost fifty years she felt nothing when she prayed, no god, no comfort, nothing, and she kept going anyway. she also took money from crooks and a dictator and ran homes where the dying got very little for their pain, so she's not a poster. if you've ever kept showing up to something long after the feeling left, she did it longer than anybody.","born":1910,"died":1997,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 200 222 L 280 222 L 280 232 L 200 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 204 232 L 204 250 M 276 232 L 276 250'/><circle cx='214' cy='213' r='8'/><path d='M 222 222 C 230 208 270 208 278 222' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 300 222 L 380 222 L 380 232 L 300 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 232 L 304 250 M 376 232 L 376 250'/><circle cx='314' cy='213' r='8'/><path d='M 322 222 C 328 210 342 210 346 222' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 282 250 C 282 242 298 242 298 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 242 C 286 236 290 230 290 228 C 292 232 296 236 290 242 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 180 250 C 180 242 196 242 196 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 188 242 C 184 236 188 230 188 228 C 190 232 194 236 188 242 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 92 186 C 84 120 136 120 128 186 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='110' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 110 168 L 110 216'/><path d='M 110 216 L 96 250 M 110 216 L 124 250'/><path d='M 110 184 L 132 194 M 110 180 L 132 184'/><path d='M 130 182 C 130 192 150 192 150 182 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 142 178 C 138 170 146 166 142 158' stroke-dasharray='2 5' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(350,197.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the most famous saint of the century felt god had left her for almost fifty years, and went to work every morning anyway.","key":"motherteresa","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother_Teresa","why":"the plain overview of her life, the letters and the criticism included"},{"label":"the nobel prize","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1979/teresa/facts/","why":"her 1979 nobel peace prize page, with her lecture"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Missionary_Position:_Mother_Teresa_in_Theory_and_Practice","why":"hitchens's book against her, for the other side"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"this one's real. in 1981 she flew to haiti, accepted an honor from the duvaliers, the father and son dictators who ran the country for almost thirty years with a private militia that tortured and killed people, praised the family, and took their money. she took over a million dollars from charles keating, an american banker whose savings and loan, a kind of savings bank, collapsed and wiped out the savings of thousands of ordinary people, a lot of them retirees. when he was tried for fraud she wrote to the judge asking for mercy. a prosecutor wrote back asking her to return the money to the people keating had robbed. she never answered him and never gave it back.\nchristopher hitchens, the british journalist and famous atheist, and others, including the editor of the lancet, one of the big medical journals, in 1994, reported what went on in the homes for the dying. little real pain relief, needles reused, suffering treated as something to offer up to christ. that's the old catholic idea that your pain, handed over to god, gets joined to jesus's pain on the cross and does some good, and it's a hard thing to hear when it's somebody else's pain and there's medicine for it. she spoke hard against contraception and divorce, and in her nobel lecture called abortion the greatest destroyer of peace. nothing sexual, ever. the mess is money, pain, and being very sure.","moments":["before she started, in 1948, she went to patna, a city in northern india, for a few months to learn basic nursing from the medical mission sisters, an american order of nuns who were doctors and nurses. a teacher of twenty years, back to being a student.","when she won the nobel in 1979 she asked them to cancel the banquet and give her the money for the poor. they did.","she died on september 5, 1997, five days after princess diana. at the state funeral in calcutta her body rode through the streets on the same gun carriage, a wagon built for hauling a cannon, that had carried [[gandhi]]'s."],"name":"mother teresa","place":"skopje, then calcutta for the rest of it","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 27 116 C 22 62 31 26 60 26 C 89 26 98 62 93 116 L 89 116 C 94 64 86 31 60 31 C 34 31 26 64 31 116 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"if i ever become a saint, i will surely be one of darkness.","where":"a letter from 1962, in come be my light, the book of her private letters"},{"q":"for the first time in eleven years i have come to love the darkness.","where":"a letter to joseph neuner, a jesuit priest she confided in, 1961, in come be my light, paraphrased. the jesuits are an order of catholic priests known for being scholars"},{"q":"we can't all do great things, but we can do small things with great love.","where":"attributed to her everywhere; the idea runs through her talks"}],"start":"come be my light, her private letters, edited by brian kolodiejchuk, the priest who argued her case for sainthood. start with the letters from the fifties on. then the missionary position, christopher hitchens's short, furious book against her, for the other side. read both.","status":"approved","story":"she was born anjezë gonxhe bojaxhiu to an albanian family in skopje, which is now the capital of north macedonia. at eighteen she joined the sisters of loreto, an order of nuns based in ireland that ran schools, and they sent her to india. for almost twenty years she taught at a girls' school the order ran in calcutta, behind a wall, and by her own account she was happy there.\non september 10, 1946, on a train up to darjeeling, a hill town in the himalayas, she heard what she called a call within a call. the first call had been to become a nun. this one said leave the convent and go live with the poorest of the poor, and serve them right where they were. it took two years to get permission from the church. in 1948 she walked out in a cheap white cotton sari with a blue border and started an open air school in a slum.\nrome approved her new order of nuns, the missionaries of charity, in 1950. in 1952 she opened a home for the dying in an old pilgrims' hostel next to the temple of kali, the hindu goddess, at kalighat, so people picked up off the street wouldn't die on it. think of a hospice with almost no money, for people nobody was coming to look for. the nobel peace prize came in 1979. by the time she died there were homes in over a hundred countries.\nthen in 2007 her private letters came out, in a book called come be my light, and everyone found out what her confessors had known for decades. confessors are the priests a catholic tells her sins and her inner life to, and she told hers everything. almost from the moment the work began she felt nothing when she prayed. no presence, no voice, no comfort, for close to fifty years. she'd asked for the letters to be destroyed. the church kept them, and in 2016 it made her a saint.","struggle":"the letters are specific. she writes of feeling unwanted by god, of god not being god, of god maybe not existing. she writes that when she tries to lift her thoughts to heaven there's such emptiness that the thoughts come back like sharp knives. she smiled for the cameras through all of it and called the smile a cloak.\nit started around 1949, right after the work began, the same work she'd been so sure god had asked for. the voice on the train went quiet and never really came back. she'd been certain she was called and then she was certain of nothing, and she kept the daily routine of her order anyway, up before dawn, prayer, then the streets, every single day.","threads":["doubt","money","dying"],"years":"1910 to 1997"},{"after":"he stopped walking. after all those years on the road he settled at kartarpur, took off the traveling clothes, the story goes, put on ordinary ones, and farmed. the man who'd been to mecca went back to the plow and ran the kitchen.\nand when it came to who would carry it on, he passed over his own sons and picked a disciple named lehna, who'd done the dirty work, and renamed him angad. his son sri chand went his own way and founded an order of wandering holy men who gave up home and family. nanak died in 1539.","basically":"the founder of sikhism, a religion that now counts around twenty five million people, most of them from the punjab. he was a village accountant's son who walked out of a river saying there's no hindu and no muslim, spent years walking across asia with a muslim musician, singing, and ended up a farmer running a free kitchen where rich and poor sat on the same floor. every sikh temple on earth still feeds anyone who walks in, no questions, which is a pretty good test of whether an idea worked. take from him that what you believe shows up where you eat and who you eat with.","born":1469,"died":1539,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 262 L 380 262'/><g transform='translate(40 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='52' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(90 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='102' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(140 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='152' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='202' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(240 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='252' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='300' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 300 164 L 300 212'/><path d='M 300 212 L 286 246 M 300 212 L 314 246'/><path d='M 300 180 L 322 190 M 300 176 L 322 180'/><path d='M 295 155 Q 300 159 305 155'/><path d='M 322 190 L 348 222'/><path d='M 326 222 L 378 222 C 380 250 368 262 352 262 C 336 262 324 250 326 222 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 322 222 L 382 222'/><path d='M 338 212 C 334 202 342 196 338 186 M 364 212 C 368 202 360 196 364 186' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(190,237.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he went into the river to bathe, didn't come out for three days, and when he did he said there is no hindu and there is no muslim.","key":"nanak","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guru_Nanak","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the scriptures, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/skh/","why":"sikh texts in english at the internet sacred text archive"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. the stories about him were written down generations later and they grew in the telling. the hymns are his.","moments":["when he died, the story goes, hindus wanted to cremate him and muslims wanted to bury him. he'd told them to lay flowers on either side of him, and whoever's flowers were still fresh in the morning could have the body. in the morning both sets were fresh and the body was gone. it's the same story they tell about kabir, almost exactly. two men who said the same thing got the same ending.","nearly everything he taught, he sang. the sikh scripture is arranged by raga, the scale and mood a piece of indian music is built on, not by author or subject.","the langar at the golden temple in amritsar, the holiest place sikhs have, feeds tens of thousands of people a day, for free, sitting on the floor."],"name":"guru nanak","place":"the punjab, and on foot across half of asia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 28 54 C 26 24 94 24 92 54 C 84 40 36 40 28 54 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 50 34 70 34 84 44 M 32 50 C 46 38 74 38 88 50' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 44 98 C 48 116 72 116 76 98' stroke-dasharray='0.1 5' stroke-width='5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"there is no hindu, there is no muslim.","where":"the janamsakhis, the collections of stories of his life written down after he died, what he said coming out of the river"},{"q":"ik onkar. there is one.","where":"the first words of the guru granth sahib, the sikh holy book, at the start of the mul mantar, the short creed it opens with"},{"q":"truth is high, but higher still is truthful living.","where":"sri rag, one of the musical sections of the guru granth sahib, paraphrased"}],"start":"japji sahib, his long morning hymn, which opens the scripture. read it in any translation, then find it sung.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in 1469 in talwandi, a village in the punjab, the land of five rivers that's now split between india and pakistan. the village is on the pakistan side now and called nankana sahib. his family was hindu. his father was the village accountant and wanted him in business. the boy kept giving things away.\nas a young man he worked at the government storehouse in sultanpur lodhi. one morning, the story goes, he went to bathe in the river bein and didn't come back. they searched the river. on the third day he walked out, and the first thing he said was na koi hindu, na koi musalman. there is no hindu, there is no muslim.\nthen he walked. four long journeys over many years, with a muslim musician named mardana playing the rabab, a kind of lute, while he sang. the accounts put him in the himalayas and tibet, in sri lanka, in baghdad and mecca. at mecca, the story goes, he lay down to sleep with his feet toward the kaaba, the black shrine at the center of mecca that every muslim on earth faces to pray. an angry man told him to move them, and he said, then turn my feet toward where god isn't.\nin his last years he settled at kartarpur on the ravi river, farmed, and built the community around a kitchen. the langar. free food, cooked by volunteers, and everyone sits on the floor in rows to eat it, brahmin priest next to the man who sweeps the street, rich next to poor. every gurdwara, every sikh temple in the world, still does it. [[kabir]], a weaver poet from varanasi who'd been saying a lot of the same things, ended up with his songs in the same holy book as nanak's, the guru granth sahib, which sikhs treat as their living teacher.","struggle":"his father thought he was useless. the story goes that he gave the boy twenty rupees to go make a good deal, and nanak met a group of hungry holy men on the road and spent all of it feeding them. he came home and said he'd made the true bargain. the stories say his father hit him.\naround 1520 babur, a warlord from central asia who went on to found the mughal empire, the dynasty that ruled most of india for the next two hundred years, came down into the punjab and sacked the town of saidpur. the tradition says nanak was there and was taken prisoner. what's certain is the hymns he wrote about it. he asks god straight out, so much beating, so much screaming, and you felt no pain? it's in the scripture. a guru calling god to account.","threads":["wandering","work"],"years":"1469 to 1539"},{"after":"the after is what his people did. [[ramdass|ram dass]] spent fifty years on the four instructions. larry brilliant, the young doctor he kept pushing toward the world health organization, the united nations' health agency, worked on the campaign that ended smallpox. krishna das, a kid from long island who sat at his feet, went home lost after he died and spent years falling apart. he came back by leading kirtan, call and response singing of the names of god, and he's been filling halls with strangers singing along for decades now.\nhe left no book, no system, and no successor. the temples at kainchi and vrindavan are still there, and the crowds still come to kainchi every june for the anniversary of the temple.","basically":"an old man in a plaid blanket in the hills of north india who fed everybody, gave almost no talks, and seemed to know what you were thinking before you did. a fired harvard professor, a hippie doctor who helped wipe out smallpox, and later a kid named steve jobs all went looking for him. picture the grandpa the whole neighborhood goes to, except the neighborhood turned out to be half the world. take from him the short list he gave out. love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth.","born":null,"died":1973,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 236 L 380 236 M 20 246 L 380 246'/><path d='M 40 236 L 40 246 M 70 236 L 70 246 M 100 236 L 100 246 M 130 236 L 130 246 M 160 236 L 160 246 M 190 236 L 190 246 M 220 236 L 220 246 M 250 236 L 250 246 M 280 236 L 280 246 M 310 236 L 310 246 M 340 236 L 340 246 M 370 236 L 370 246' opacity='.5'/><rect x='40' y='170' width='130' height='50' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='130' y='140' width='40' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 64 170 L 64 146 L 80 146 L 80 170'/><path d='M 64 140 C 60 130 70 124 64 114' opacity='.4' stroke-dasharray='2 6'/><circle cx='70' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='110' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='150' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 290 C 244 236 264 222 280 222 C 296 222 316 236 310 290 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 262 232 L 262 290 M 280 222 L 280 290 M 298 232 L 298 290 M 254 250 L 306 250 M 250 270 L 310 270' opacity='.45'/><circle cx='280' cy='206' r='16'/><path d='M 275 211 Q 280 215 285 211'/><g transform='translate(110,115.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a fat old man in a plaid blanket who read ram dass's mind and told a young doctor to go end smallpox.","key":"neemkaroli","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neem_Karoli_Baba","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his ashram in america","url":"https://www.nkbashram.org/","why":"the temple his western devotees built in taos, new mexico"},{"label":"ram dass's foundation","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/","why":"where ram dass's stories of him live"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing ugly i'm sure of. the miracles, the mind reading, being in two places at once, all come from people who loved him, and none of it can be checked. read them that way.","moments":["steve jobs went to india in 1974 with his friend dan kottke, hoping to meet him, and found out he'd died months before. jobs stayed on in india for months anyway and came home changed.","the first thing people remember about his temples is the food. whoever you were, you got fed before you got anything else.","he called larry brilliant doctor america, and kept telling him, as brilliant tells it, that he'd go to work for the united nations and help get rid of smallpox."],"name":"neem karoli baba","place":"the hills of uttarakhand and the plains of north india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 18 130 C 18 102 34 90 60 94 C 86 90 102 102 102 130 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 32 102 L 32 130 M 46 95 L 46 130 M 74 95 L 74 130 M 88 102 L 88 130 M 22 112 L 98 112 M 19 122 L 101 122' opacity='.45'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth.","where":"the instructions ram dass brought home; in be here now, ram dass's 1971 book, and miracle of love, the collection of stories about the old man"},{"q":"sub ek. it's all one.","where":"one of his most repeated phrases, in miracle of love"},{"q":"love is the strongest medicine.","where":"attributed, in miracle of love; ram dass quoted it for years"}],"start":"miracle of love, compiled by ram dass, the stories of him from people who knew him. then be here now, the first section, which is ram dass's own story of meeting him.","status":"approved","story":"almost nothing about his early life is certain. he went by different names in different towns, came and went without warning, and didn't talk about himself. the story goes that a conductor threw him off a train for having no ticket, near a village called neeb karori, and the train wouldn't start again until they let him back on. the village name stuck.\nby the sixties he was maharaj-ji, which just means great king and is what people call a holy man they love, to thousands. a heavy old man wrapped in a plaid blanket on a wooden bench, joking, feeding people, and mostly refusing to give talks. he built temples to hanuman, the monkey god from the ramayana, india's great epic, who's the devoted servant of the god-king ram. he built ashrams, communities that grow up around a teacher, at kainchi in the himalayan foothills near the hill town of nainital, and at vrindavan, the town where the god krishna is said to have grown up.\nin 1967 [[ramdass|richard alpert]], a harvard psychologist who'd been fired for his drug experiments, was brought to him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. alpert hadn't told anyone. he cried for two days and went home as ram dass. the westerners who followed him got a short list of instructions and a lot of food.\nhe died in the early hours of september 11, 1973, in a hospital in vrindavan, after leaving kainchi on a night train. the next year a nineteen year old named steve jobs, who'd go on to start apple, went to india to find him and got there too late.","struggle":"we don't have his struggle. he wrote nothing, gave almost no talks, and the people around him recorded what he did to them, not what went on in him. whatever it cost him, he kept it under the blanket.\nwhat the stories do say is that he wouldn't stay put. he'd vanish from an ashram without a word, turn up in some other town days later, and leave again. the devotees spent a lot of time looking for him.","threads":["love","wandering"],"years":"died in 1973; nobody's sure when he was born"},{"after":"he came back. that's the after. he'd tried the romantic version, the robe and the road, and turned around and went home to the shop, the family, the smoke. the rest of his life happened in a small room up a steep stair over a street in bombay.\nand he didn't make the dying into a performance. visitors asked him about it and he answered with the same flat impatience as everything else. the body was going. he'd never thought he was the body. he kept smoking.","basically":"a guy who ran a cigarette stand in bombay, got one instruction from a teacher, and practiced it for three years until he was done. then he kept the stand, and talked to visitors in a tiny room upstairs, chain smoking, losing his temper, and throwing people out. imagine the most awake person in your city is the guy at the corner store, and he's rude to you. take from him that you don't need a mountain. you need to notice that you exist, and stay there.","born":1897,"died":1981,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='70' y='70' width='150' height='180' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 70 170 L 220 170 M 60 70 L 230 70'/><path d='M 64 176 L 226 176 L 214 190 L 76 190 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 84 216 L 206 216'/><g stroke-width='2'><path d='M 150 206 L 150 216 M 156 206 L 156 216 M 162 206 L 162 216 M 180 206 L 180 216 M 186 206 L 186 216 M 192 206 L 192 216'/><\/g><circle cx='130' cy='96' r='12'/><path d='M 130 110 L 130 152'/><path d='M 130 152 C 110 152 100 168 116 168 L 144 168 C 160 168 150 152 130 152'/><path d='M 130 126 L 116 152 M 130 126 L 144 152'/><path d='M 140 100 L 154 104' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 157 100 C 162 92 154 88 160 80 C 164 74 158 70 162 62' stroke-dasharray='2 5' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 222 250 L 222 170 M 244 250 L 244 170 M 222 234 L 244 234 M 222 218 L 244 218 M 222 202 L 244 202 M 222 186 L 244 186'/><circle cx='320' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 320 168 L 320 214'/><path d='M 320 214 L 336 250 M 320 214 L 302 246'/><path d='M 320 182 L 334 204 M 320 182 L 300 190'/><g transform='translate(106,194.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a cigarette seller in bombay was told he wasn't the body, believed it, and went back to selling cigarettes.","key":"nisargadatta","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nisargadatta_Maharaj","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Am_That","why":"the book of his talks"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"sloppy stuff. a temper, and he didn't hide it. he'd shout at visitors, mock their questions, and throw people out of the room, people who'd flown halfway around the world included. he smoked his own product to the end. and he left his family in 1937 to go wandering, briefly. nothing worse than that. no stories of him using anybody, for money or for sex.","moments":["the talks went on between rounds of devotional singing he kept up every day, which surprises people who know him only from the book.","maurice frydman, who translated i am that from marathi, the language of the bombay region, died in 1976, three years after it came out.","his teacher gave him the instruction and he said he gave it three years, and that it was enough. he never claimed it was complicated."],"name":"nisargadatta maharaj","place":"a small shop and the loft above it, bombay","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 66 76 L 82 81' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 85 78 C 90 72 83 68 88 60 C 92 54 86 50 90 44' stroke-dasharray='2 4' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"wisdom tells me i am nothing. love tells me i am everything.","where":"i am that, the book of his talks; the next line is that between the two his life flows"},{"q":"stay with the i am, and let go of everything else.","where":"his teacher's instruction, repeated all through i am that, paraphrased"},{"q":"you are not what you take yourself to be.","where":"i am that, paraphrased; he says versions of it on nearly every page"}],"start":"i am that, in frydman's translation. one conversation a morning, anywhere in the book.","status":"approved","story":"maruti kambli was born in 1897 and grew up in a farming family. as a young man he came to bombay, clerked for a while, and opened small shops. the one that did best sold beedis, tobacco rolled up in a leaf, the cheapest smoke in india. he married and had children.\nin 1933 a friend took him to siddharameshwar maharaj, a teacher in the navnath line, an old chain of teachers said to go back to nine legendary yogis. the teacher told him he wasn't the body and he wasn't the mind. he was only the bare sense i am, the plain feeling of existing that's there before you think anything about who you are, and he should stay with that and nothing else. nisargadatta did exactly that, every spare minute, for three years. in 1936 the teacher died.\nthe next year he walked out of bombay to be a wandering monk and headed for the himalayas. on the road a fellow disciple talked him out of it and told him the giving up was supposed to happen inside. he came back. most of the shops had gone under by then. he kept one.\nhe talked to whoever climbed the steep stair to a tiny loft room in his house in khetwadi, a crowded neighborhood of bombay, and for decades that wasn't many people. then maurice frydman, a polish engineer who'd lived in india for decades, taped the talks and translated them, and i am that, the big book of those conversations, came out in 1973. after that people came from everywhere, and he chain smoked and answered them. he died of throat cancer in 1981.","struggle":"in 1937 he walked away from a wife, children, and a business to go be holy on the road. it lasted months. he came back to a family that had managed without him and a business that mostly hadn't.\nthe cancer came in his early eighties, in the throat, from the cigarettes he'd sold all his life. he kept the room open and kept talking to visitors as long as his voice would carry.","threads":["work","family","dying"],"years":"1897 to 1981"},{"after":"in winnipeg, nearly every day, he wrote himself a short note, what he called a spiritual imperative, an order to himself, from the part of him that still knew something to the part that didn't. he kept them private for eight years and let them out as the inner voice of love in 1996, the year he died. it's the most naked thing he wrote.\nthen he went back to daybreak and back to the bathroom with adam in the morning, running the water. the friendship with nathan survived. he didn't come out of it cured. he came out of it back at work, with the same wound and a better idea of where it went.","basically":"a dutch catholic priest who taught at yale and harvard and wrote small, warm books that sold millions, while privately he was lonely, depressed, and gay, and kept that last part mostly to himself as a priest sworn to celibacy. at fifty four he quit harvard to be the pastor of a group home for people with intellectual disabilities outside toronto, and spent his mornings bathing and shaving a man who couldn't speak. picture a famous professor walking off the lecture circuit to work the early shift at a group home, and saying it was the best thing that ever happened to him. he's for anyone who's done plenty and still isn't sure anybody's glad they exist.","born":1932,"died":1996,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 40 280 L 40 96 M 360 280 L 360 96'/><rect x='24' y='88' width='52' height='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='324' y='88' width='52' height='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 276 10 L 276 104 M 306 10 L 306 104 M 270 104 L 312 104'/><path d='M 282 104 L 291 124 M 300 104 L 291 124 M 291 124 L 291 166'/><circle cx='291' cy='180' r='12'/><path d='M 291 160 L 262 150 M 291 160 L 264 164'/><g transform='rotate(70 190 170)'><circle cx='190' cy='130' r='14'/><path d='M 190 144 L 190 192'/><path d='M 190 192 L 176 226 M 190 192 L 204 226'/><path d='M 190 158 L 170 134 M 190 158 L 210 134'/><path d='M 185 135 Q 190 139 195 135'/><\/g><path d='M 76 90 C 110 110 130 150 150 180' stroke-dasharray='2 9' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 30 262 C 140 280 260 280 370 262' stroke-dasharray='4 6'/><g transform='translate(44,63.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a harvard professor quit to go live with people who couldn't read his books, and one of them became his teacher.","key":"nouwen","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_Nouwen","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his foundation","url":"https://henrinouwen.org/","why":"the henri nouwen society, with his writings and a free daily meditation"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Return_of_the_Prodigal_Son_(Rembrandt)","why":"the painting his best book is about"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of. he was exhausting to his friends, needy, phoning at all hours, and he'd have told you so himself. that's a weight, not a wrong.","moments":["in 1974 he spent seven months living as a monk at the abbey of the genesee in upstate new york, a monastery of trappists, the strict, mostly silent order [[merton|thomas merton]] belonged to. he baked bread and pulled rocks out of fields, and wrote the genesee diary about how bad he was at it.","in 1991 he got to know a trapeze troupe, the flying rodleighs, at a circus in germany, and traveled with them. their leader told him the flyer never catches the catcher. you let go, stretch out your arms, and wait for the catcher to grab you. he meant to write a book about it and died first.","adam died in february 1996. nouwen wrote a short book about him, adam, god's beloved, and died that september before it came out."],"name":"henri nouwen","place":"the netherlands, then yale and harvard, then a house outside toronto","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i am the prodigal son every time i search for unconditional love where it cannot be found.","where":"the return of the prodigal son, his book about the rembrandt painting"},{"q":"being the beloved is the core truth of who you are, and the other voices are trying to talk you out of it.","where":"life of the beloved, a short book he wrote for a friend who didn't share his faith, paraphrased"},{"q":"the minister's wounds, looked at honestly, become a source of healing for other people.","where":"the wounded healer, his 1972 book about people who help people, paraphrased"}],"start":"the return of the prodigal son. then the inner voice of love, the notes he wrote to himself in winnipeg, if it's a bad month. the wounded healer is short.","status":"approved","story":"a dutch catholic priest, ordained in 1957, who came to america to study psychology and stayed to teach. notre dame, then yale divinity school, where they train ministers, for ten years, then harvard. his books were short and warm and sold by the million. the wounded healer, from 1972, says the thing a minister really has to give people is his own wounds, the places he's been hurt, looked at honestly, because that's where he can meet them.\nhe was lonely, restless, and never at home in the competition of university life. in 1985 he spent a year in france at l'arche, which is french for the ark. it's a network of homes started in 1964 by jean vanier, a canadian philosopher, where people with intellectual disabilities and the assistants who help them share a house and a life, cooking, eating, and praying together. in 1986 he left harvard for good and moved to l'arche daybreak, the one outside toronto, as its pastor. years after nouwen died, in 2020, an investigation found that vanier had sexually abused women who came to him for spiritual guidance. nouwen never knew. nobody outside a small circle did.\nthey gave him adam arnett to care for. adam couldn't speak, needed help with everything, and had seizures. every morning nouwen bathed him, shaved him, dressed him, and fed him breakfast, slowly, and badly at first. he said later that adam was the one taking care of him.\nthe return of the prodigal son, from 1992, came out of a painting. in the gospel of luke jesus tells a story about a younger son who takes his share of the inheritance early, blows all of it, and comes home broke hoping to be hired on as a servant, and his father runs down the road to hug him. the older son, the one who stayed home and did everything right, sulks outside the party. rembrandt, the dutch master, painted the homecoming near the end of his own life. nouwen sat in front of it for hours in 1986 at the hermitage, the enormous art museum in what was then leningrad. he found himself in the younger son, the one who ran off, then in the older one, the resentful good boy, and then a friend at daybreak told him the job was to become the father. he died of a heart attack in the netherlands in 1996. he was sixty four.","struggle":"in late 1987 he fell apart. a close friendship at daybreak with a younger man, nathan ball, had become the center of his emotional life, and when nathan pulled back nouwen couldn't function. he left daybreak and spent about six months with therapists in winnipeg, in what he called anguish and what most people now would call a breakdown.\nunder that was the longer thing. he was gay and a celibate priest, a man who'd promised never to have sex or marry, and he kept it mostly private his whole life; the people close to him knew, and the biographers said it after he died. he fought depression for years, and a need to be wanted that he described as bottomless.","threads":["loneliness","love","work"],"years":"1932 to 1996"},{"after":"after the childhood she went to the woods and didn't come back. that's close to literal. she built her whole life around the walk, the notebook, and the one person she loved, and she kept that shape for over half a century. it looks simple. it was a choice she made every morning.\nafter molly, she kept walking. new woods, new ponds, florida instead of the cape. the late books are plainer and more openly about god and grief than the early ones. she didn't pretend to be done grieving and she didn't stop going out in the morning.","basically":"a poet who walked the same woods and ponds and beaches at the tip of cape cod nearly every morning for decades with a little notebook, writing down what she saw, and turned it into some of the best loved poems in america. she's the one people read at funerals and graduations, the one wild and precious life poet. she was also tougher and more private than the tote bags make her look. take the walk from her. go outside, look at one thing until you actually see it, and write it down.","born":1935,"died":2019,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 70 250 L 70 135'/><ellipse cx='70' cy='123' rx='35' ry='26' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 330 250 L 330 120'/><ellipse cx='330' cy='106' rx='40' ry='30' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 250 250 L 250 158'/><ellipse cx='250' cy='148' rx='28' ry='21' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 250 196 L 262 190' stroke-width='3.5'/><circle cx='160' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 160 168 L 160 214'/><path d='M 160 214 L 144 250 M 160 214 L 178 246'/><path d='M 160 182 L 146 204 M 160 182 L 180 190'/><rect x='176' y='186' width='14' height='18' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 120 60 L 130 66 L 140 60 M 150 50 L 160 56 L 170 50 M 180 64 L 190 70 L 200 64 M 210 54 L 220 60 L 230 54'/><path d='M 20 270 C 100 262 200 278 380 266' stroke-dasharray='3 8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 104 250 L 104 232 L 128 232 L 128 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='116' cy='232' rx='12' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(116,212.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she walked into the woods every morning with a notebook for fifty years and came out with poems about paying attention.","key":"oliver","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Oliver","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"her site","url":"https://maryoliver.beacon.org/","why":"the authorized site, with recordings of her reading"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/mary-oliver","why":"a life and some poems"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["she said that when she was walking and found she'd come out without a pencil, it was a problem, so she hid pencils in the trees along the trails.","she'd get a line down in the notebook on the walk and work it up later at the desk. the walk came first, every day.","she loved dogs, and late in life put out a whole book of poems about them, dog songs, in 2013."],"name":"mary oliver","place":"ohio, then provincetown on the tip of cape cod, then florida","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><rect x='86' y='100' width='18' height='24' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 106 L 100 106 M 90 111 L 100 111 M 90 116 L 97 116' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 108 96 L 100 126' stroke-width='2.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"tell me, what is it you plan to do with your one wild and precious life?","where":"the summer day, the last lines, in new and selected poems, volume one"},{"q":"you do not have to be good.","where":"wild geese, the first line, in dream work, 1986"},{"q":"her instructions for living a life come to three things. pay attention. be astonished. tell somebody.","where":"the poem sometimes, in red bird, 2008, paraphrased"}],"start":"new and selected poems, volume one: the summer day, wild geese, when death comes. then upstream, her essays, for how she lived.","status":"approved","story":"she was born in 1935 in maple heights, ohio, outside cleveland. she spent her childhood in the woods to get away from a house she didn't feel safe in. she wrote poems out there from about fourteen.\nas a teenager she wrote to the sister of edna st. vincent millay, one of the most famous american poets of the twenties, who had died a few years before. she ended up living for years at millay's old place, steepletop, in upstate new york, helping sort the papers. she went to ohio state and then vassar and didn't finish at either.\nat steepletop she met the photographer molly malone cook. they moved to provincetown, the old fishing town and artists' colony at the tip of cape cod, and stayed together over forty years, until molly died in 2005. molly handled the world so mary could write. mary walked. every morning, in the woods and the dunes and along the ponds, with a small notebook, writing down what she saw.\namerican primitive won the pulitzer prize, the big american prize for poetry, in 1984. her books sold like almost no poet's in america. she didn't do much publicity and didn't care much for the fuss. after molly died she moved to florida, and died there in january 2019, at eighty three.","struggle":"the childhood is the thing under everything, and she wrote about it only late, and only a little. she said the woods saved her. she said the poems started there as a way to get out.\nmolly's death in 2005 flattened her. she'd been with her since her twenties. she wrote about the grief in thirst and our world, and then left provincetown, where every path had molly on it. in her late seventies she was treated for lung cancer, and kept writing.","threads":["the land","grief","love"],"years":"1935 to 2019"},{"after":"he never scrubbed the record. in his letters he keeps saying it. i persecuted the church. i'm the least of the apostles, the first messengers sent out to spread the word, and i don't deserve the name. he didn't hide it and he didn't let it stop him either. he worked harder than all of them, he said, and then caught himself. not me, the grace in me.\nwhat he did with the thorn was the whole method. weakness was the point. beaten, shipwrecked, jailed, and writing joy from a cell. philippians is that, a short letter from prison that says rejoice twice in one line like he knew you'd argue.","basically":"a strict religious jew in the first century who went after the first followers of jesus, dragging them off to jail, until something knocked him flat on a road and he switched sides completely. then he spent thirty years walking and sailing around the roman empire, starting little churches in people's houses and writing them letters, and those letters make up a big part of the new testament, the christian half of the bible. imagine the loudest guy against something becoming its best organizer overnight, and never once hiding what he'd been. if you've ever done harm and wondered whether you still get to do good, he spent his whole life asking that out loud.","born":5,"died":64,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 C 100 245 300 255 380 250'/><path d='M 210 30 L 200 110 M 230 40 L 210 112 M 190 40 L 192 110' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='210' cy='30' r='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='150' cy='225' r='14'/><path d='M 164 228 L 220 236 M 220 236 L 250 250 M 220 236 L 240 224 M 185 231 L 180 210 M 195 232 L 205 214'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 200 L 350 200 L 350 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 310 218 L 340 218 M 310 232 L 340 232' opacity='.35'/><g transform='translate(305,173.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was hunting christians for a living when the light knocked him off the road, and then he wrote half the new testament.","key":"paul","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_the_Apostle","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"acts, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/act.htm","why":"the book of acts, his road story, in the king james version"},{"label":"romans, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/rom.htm","why":"his letter to the romans in the king james version"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he wrote a few lines about women keeping quiet in church and about slaves obeying their masters that have been used for two thousand years to hurt people, including by american slaveholders quoting him from the pulpit. scholars argue over which of those he actually wrote, because some of the letters with his name on them were probably written by his followers after he died, which was a normal thing to do then. before the road he was hunting people down and dragging them to jail. he never pretended otherwise.","moments":["he and peter, the fisherman who'd been one of jesus's closest friends and was a leader of the first church, had a public fight in antioch, a big city in what's now southern turkey. peter had been eating with christians who weren't jewish, then stopped when some strict men came up from jerusalem, because a lot of jews then wouldn't share a table with non-jews. paul says he called him a hypocrite to his face, in front of everyone. the two founders of the church, going at it over dinner.","he made tents for a living and kept doing it so nobody could say he preached for money.","on the way to rome as a prisoner, to be tried before the emperor, he was shipwrecked on malta, got bitten by a snake on the beach, and shook it off into the fire. the islanders had been waiting for him to swell up and drop dead, and when he didn't, they decided he was a god. acts doesn't say what he told them. but years earlier in a town called lystra, when a crowd tried to worship him, he tore his clothes and shouted that he was just a man like them."],"name":"paul","place":"tarsus, damascus, everywhere the roads went","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 44 86 52 104 60 110 C 68 104 76 86 80 70 C 72 80 48 80 40 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 16 112 L 46 104 M 16 112 C 12 116 20 120 20 114 M 46 104 C 50 100 44 98 44 104' /><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"in him we live and move and have our being, as some of your own poets have said.","where":"acts 17:28, which means the book of acts, chapter 17, verse 28; bible references work that way everywhere on these pages. he's quoting epimenides and aratus. read all of chapter 17"},{"q":"love is patient. love is kind. it doesn't keep score.","where":"1 corinthians 13, from his first letter to the church in corinth, a rowdy port city in greece, paraphrased; read the whole chapter"},{"q":"i don't do the good i want. i do the thing i hate.","where":"romans 7:15, from his letter to the christians in rome, paraphrased; read all of chapter 7"}],"start":"acts 17 for the athens speech. 1 corinthians 13 for love. then philippians, his letter to the church in philippi, a town in northern greece, the whole thing. he wrote it from prison and it's short.","status":"approved","story":"saul of tarsus, a city on the south coast of what's now turkey, was a pharisee, one of the strictest groups of jewish teachers, the ones who carried every detail of god's law into daily life. he was also a roman citizen, a legal status most people in the empire didn't have. he was on his way to damascus, in syria, to arrest followers of jesus, a new group of jews who said their executed teacher had come back from the dead. on the road a light hit him, he fell to the ground, and a voice asked, saul, why are you persecuting me. he got up blind. he spent three days blind in the city, not eating or drinking. when he could see again he was theirs. he's better known by his roman name, paul.\nhe never met [[jesus]] in the flesh. he spent the next thirty years walking and sailing around the mediterranean starting churches, getting beaten, getting shipwrecked, and writing letters back to the churches when they screwed up, which was constantly. those letters are the oldest christian writing there is, older than the gospels, the four accounts of jesus's life that open the new testament.\nacts is the book of the bible about what the first christians did after jesus, and in chapter 17 paul stands up in the marketplace in athens in front of the philosophers and quotes their own poets at them. the athenians had an altar in the city carved to an unknown god, set up in case they'd missed one. paul told them that unknown god was the one he was talking about, and quoted two greek poets from centuries before, epimenides and aratus. in him we live and move and have our being. we are his offspring. that's the move: the old texts talking to each other, and a preacher admitting the other guys had it first.\nhe was a hard man, sure of himself, and he wrote the best thing about love anybody ever has, the love is patient, love is kind passage that gets read at almost every church wedding. tradition says rome cut his head off under nero, the emperor who blamed the christians for the great fire of rome in 64 and had them killed in public, some of them burned alive as torches.","struggle":"he had something wrong with him that he never names. a thorn in the flesh, he calls it, a messenger of satan to keep him from getting too proud. people have been guessing what it was for two thousand years. he says he begged god three times to take it away and got told, my grace is enough for you, my power works best in weakness. he stopped asking.\nand romans 7, in his letter to the christians in rome, is the most honest paragraph in the book. i don't understand what i do. what i want to do, i don't do. what i hate, i do. the man who wrote half the new testament said he couldn't get his own hands to obey him. anybody who's sworn off something at midnight and done it again by lunch knows exactly what he means.","threads":["forgiveness","wandering"],"years":"around 5 to around 64"},{"after":"what she did with the rock was turn around and look at the arm that threw it. her whole teaching is that move. when the feeling comes, don't throw it and don't swallow it, stay with it a little longer than you can stand. the practice she gives out most is tonglen, tibetan for sending and taking. you breathe in the thing you don't want, the fear or the grief or the rage, for everybody who has it, and breathe out some relief for them. it's backwards from the meditation apps, where you breathe in calm and breathe out stress. she says start with your own pain, since it's right there.\nwhat she did with shambhala was leave without a speech and keep teaching, mostly on her own, into her eighties. as far as i know she hasn't written the full reckoning with trungpa. the after on that side is partial, and it's on the page that way.","basically":"an american schoolteacher with two kids and two marriages behind her who hit the worst rage of her life in her thirties, found tibetan buddhism in the middle of it, and became a nun. now she's the one whose book gets handed around hospital waiting rooms and passed between friends going through a divorce. she's the friend who doesn't tell you it'll be fine and just sits down in it with you. the one move to take from her is this. when the bad feeling comes, stay with it a little longer than you can stand.","born":1936,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='90' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 90 168 L 90 216'/><path d='M 90 216 L 76 250 M 90 216 L 104 250'/><path d='M 90 182 L 76 206 M 90 182 L 116 176'/><path d='M 118 172 C 170 90 250 80 300 240' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 214 104 C 212 94 226 90 232 98 C 238 106 228 114 220 112 C 214 112 214 108 214 104 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 196 110 L 206 106 M 198 120 L 208 114' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 320 250 L 320 128 L 380 128 L 380 250'/><path d='M 380 130 L 352 138 L 352 244 L 380 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='338' cy='160' r='10'/><circle cx='334' cy='158' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='340' cy='158' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g transform='translate(268,225.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"her husband came home and told her he was having an affair, she threw a rock at him, and everything she teaches starts there.","key":"pema","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pema_Ch%C3%B6dr%C3%B6n","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"her foundation","url":"https://pemachodronfoundation.org/","why":"the pema chödrön foundation, with her talks and books"},{"label":"the abbey","url":"https://gampoabbey.org/","why":"gampo abbey, the monastery on the cliff she directed"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"her own conduct is clean. the mess is the man she stayed with. chögyam trungpa slept with many of his students and drank all the time, and died in 1987 at forty seven of what was plainly the drinking. in 1975, at a months-long retreat he ran for his senior students in colorado, the poet w.s. merwin, who'd later be america's poet laureate, and his partner dana naone locked themselves in their room rather than join a party. trungpa had his guards break the door down, and the two of them were dragged out and stripped in front of everyone. his chosen successor, an american student named ösel tendzin, knew he was hiv positive and kept having unprotected sex with students, and at least one young man was infected. trungpa doesn't get a page of his own here, because teachers who used their students for sex are left out of this whole collection.\nshe stayed. for decades she called trungpa her root teacher, which in tibetan buddhism means the one teacher who matters most in your life, and she defended him. trungpa's network of meditation centers, shambhala, passed to his son, who went by the sakyong mipham, sakyong being a title that means earth protector. in 2018 reports came out that the son had sexually abused students. she stepped back, and in early 2020, when it looked like he'd return to teaching, she resigned as a senior teacher.","moments":["she says she read working with negativity and felt like the man was talking straight to her, about her, in the middle of the worst of it.","the tibetan word she's made famous is shenpa, the hook, the itch before you scratch it, the half second where you could still not send the text. taking the leap, from 2009, is a short book about nothing else.","she took her novice vows in 1974 from the sixteenth karmapa. the karmapa is the head of trungpa's branch of tibetan buddhism, and tibetans believe it's the same teacher reborn over and over, sixteen times by then, going back to the twelfth century."],"name":"pema chödrön","place":"new york, california, then a monastery on a cliff in nova scotia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"things don't really get solved. they come together and they fall apart.","where":"when things fall apart, her 1997 book, early on, paraphrased"},{"q":"only to the extent that we expose ourselves over and over to annihilation can that which is indestructible be found in us.","where":"when things fall apart, the opening chapters"},{"q":"you are the sky. everything else is just the weather.","where":"attributed to her everywhere; the idea runs through her talks, the sentence is hard to pin down"}],"start":"when things fall apart, the first three chapters. then the places that scare you, from 2001. no time to lose when you want her going through shantideva's handbook line by line.","status":"approved","story":"she was born deirdre blomfield-brown in new york in 1936. she married young, had two kids, divorced, taught elementary school in california, and married again.\none day the second husband came home and told her he was having an affair. she picked up a rock and threw it at him. she's told that story for decades as the moment the floor went out from under her, and the reason she went looking for anything that would hold.\nwhat held was a magazine article called working with negativity, by chögyam trungpa, a tibetan monk who'd fled when china took over tibet, ended up in the west, given up his robes, and was teaching americans in colorado. the article was about exactly the rage she was in. she met him in 1972, and in 1974 took novice vows, the first step of becoming a nun, head shaved and robes on. in 1981 she went to hong kong for full ordination, the complete set of vows a fully ordained monk takes. tibetan buddhism had never kept that line going for women, so she took it from the chinese tradition, which had. she was one of the first american women to do it.\nin the mid eighties she became director of gampo abbey, a monastery trungpa had founded on a cliff over the gulf of st lawrence on cape breton island, way up the nova scotia coast in canada. when things fall apart came out in 1997, edited from talks she'd given there, and it's the book people hand each other in hospital hallways. in 2005 she put out no time to lose, a line by line walk through the way of the bodhisattva, [[shantideva]]'s eighth century handbook on how to live for other people.","struggle":"the rage came first. she's described the anger after the second marriage ended as the most intense thing she'd ever felt, and she had no idea what to do with it. she was in her mid thirties with two kids.\nthen the monastery. she's said gampo abbey was the first place she couldn't run from herself, and that every habit she'd used to hold herself together came apart there. when things fall apart is her report from inside that, not a lecture from after it.","threads":["anger","family"],"years":"1936 to now"},{"after":"john 21, the last chapter of john's gospel, is the after, and it's the best one in the book. seven of them go back to fishing and catch nothing all night. at dawn a man on the shore tells them to throw the net on the right side, and it fills, a hundred and fifty three fish. when someone says it's the lord, peter puts his coat on, which is backwards, jumps into the water, and swims for it. on the beach there's a charcoal fire going with fish on it. the last time peter stood at a charcoal fire he said he didn't know him.\nafter breakfast jesus asks him three times, do you love me. three, for three. the third time peter's hurt, and says you know everything, you know i love you. jesus never says i forgive you. he says feed my sheep, meaning take care of my people, and tells him that when he's old someone else will dress him and lead him where he doesn't want to go. no speech about the denial. the question three times, and a job.","basically":"a fisherman from a lake in northern israel who dropped his nets to follow jesus and became his loudest, most eager friend, and then, the night jesus was arrested, swore three times he'd never met the man. after the execution, jesus found him back at his boat and asked him three times if he loved him, and gave him a job instead of a lecture. he went on to lead the first christians, and the catholic church counts him as the first pope. if you've ever sold out someone you loved right when it counted, he's the one who got asked back.","born":null,"died":64,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 240 L 190 240 C 205 240 215 232 230 230 L 380 230'/><path d='M 240 252 C 255 246 265 258 280 252 S 305 246 320 252 S 345 258 360 252' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 262 272 C 277 266 287 278 302 272 S 327 266 342 272' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 74 240 L 104 230 M 74 230 L 104 240'/><path d='M 89 228 C 80 218 88 208 89 198 C 94 208 100 216 89 228 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 112 236 C 118 230 128 230 134 236 C 128 242 118 242 112 236 Z M 112 236 L 106 231 L 106 241 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='160' cy='144' r='14'/><path d='M 160 158 L 160 206'/><path d='M 160 206 L 146 240 M 160 206 L 174 240'/><path d='M 160 172 L 134 174 M 160 172 L 186 174'/><path d='M 155 149 Q 160 153 165 149'/><circle cx='252' cy='224' r='12'/><path d='M 262 232 L 276 216 L 288 220'/><path d='M 238 222 L 230 214 M 242 214 L 238 204' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 214 L 312 232 L 368 232 L 380 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 334 214 L 334 150'/><path d='M 300 214 C 296 224 298 236 306 246' stroke-dasharray='2 5'/><path d='M 34 240 L 36 230 M 42 240 L 40 230'/><path d='M 26 222 C 26 212 44 210 48 220 C 48 230 30 232 26 222 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='48' cy='212' r='5'/><path d='M 46 207 L 48 203 L 50 207 M 53 212 L 57 213 L 53 215 M 26 222 L 18 212 M 27 219 L 20 206'/><g transform='translate(346,189.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he swore he'd die with him, said he'd never met him before the rooster crowed, and then got asked three times on a beach if he loved him.","key":"peter","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter","why":"the plain overview, the gospels and the traditions"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"cowardice, the plain kind, and a temper to go with it. the sword in the garden, which jesus told him to put away. the rebuke that got him called satan. in acts, the book right after the gospels about what the first christians did next, a couple named ananias and sapphira sell some land, lie about how much of the money they're giving the church, and drop dead at his feet, and he doesn't look sorry.\nand years later at antioch he'd been eating with gentile christians, which strict jews wouldn't do, until some men came from james, jesus's brother, who ran the church in jerusalem and was strict about jewish law. then peter quietly stopped and ate apart. paul says he did it out of fear, and that the other jewish christians followed him, even barnabas, paul's own partner. nothing in the other category.","moments":["fifty days after the crucifixion, at the jewish harvest festival of pentecost, in acts 2, the man who'd folded in front of a servant girl stood up in jerusalem and preached to a crowd. in acts 4 the council, the jewish high court that had handed jesus over, looks at him and john, sees unschooled, ordinary men, and notices they'd been with jesus.","in acts 10 he sees a sheet let down from the sky full of animals jewish law said never to eat, and a voice tells him to eat, three times. then the men of a roman centurion, an army officer, knock on the door, and he goes into a gentile's house, which he'd been raised never to do.","in acts 12 an angel walks him out of the prison of herod agrippa, the local king under rome, at night. he knocks at the gate of the house where the church is praying for him, and the servant girl, rhoda, is so glad she runs in to tell everyone and leaves him standing outside, still knocking.","the story goes, from a second century book called the acts of peter, that he was slipping out of rome ahead of nero and met jesus on the road going the other way. he asked where are you going, in latin quo vadis. to rome, to be crucified again. peter turned around."],"name":"peter","place":"capernaum on the sea of galilee, then jerusalem, antioch, rome","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><circle cx='94' cy='106' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 97 110 L 108 124 M 104 119 L 108 116 M 107 123 L 111 120'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"go away from me, lord, i'm a sinful man.","where":"the gospel of luke, 5:8, after the net nearly broke with fish"},{"q":"i don't know the man.","where":"the gospel of matthew, 26:72, the second time he denied him"},{"q":"lord, you know everything, you know that i love you.","where":"the gospel of john, 21:17, the third time he was asked"}],"start":"john 21, the whole chapter, the last one in the gospel of john, near the start of the new testament in any bible. then mark 14 from verse 27 to the end, the promise, the fire, and the rooster. then galatians 2, one of paul's letters, for antioch.","status":"approved","story":"simon was a fisherman on the sea of galilee, a big freshwater lake in the north of what's now israel, with his brother andrew. he had a wife, and a mother-in-law who gets healed of a fever in the first chapter of mark, the shortest of the gospels, the four books that tell jesus's life. jesus called him cephas, which is aramaic, the language they spoke, for rock. in greek it comes out petros, peter. the nickname was rocky, more or less. the gospels spend the next three years showing you how little like a rock he was.\none night on the lake he saw [[jesus]] walking on the water and asked to come out to him. he got a few steps, looked at the wind, and sank. later he was the first to say out loud that jesus was the messiah, the king the jews had been promised who'd come and set everything right, and a few verses after that he took him aside to tell him he'd got it wrong about having to die. jesus said get behind me, satan, the name for the enemy. matthew, another of the gospels, fits all of it into chapters 14 through 16.\nat the last supper, the final meal jesus ate with his friends the night before he died, peter swore that even if he had to die with him he'd never deny him. that night, in the olive grove outside jerusalem where the soldiers came for jesus, he pulled a sword and cut off the ear of the high priest's servant, a man john names as malchus. the high priest was the top religious official in jerusalem, and the one who'd had jesus arrested. hours later, warming himself at a charcoal fire in the high priest's courtyard, peter told a servant girl and then others that he didn't know the man. the rooster crowed. luke says jesus turned and looked at him. he went out and wept bitterly.\nafter the crucifixion he went back to fishing, and that's where the best morning in the book happens, down in the after. then he preached in jerusalem, took the message into the house of a gentile, a non-jew, a line jews of his day didn't cross, and got into a public fight with [[paul]], the former persecutor who became christianity's great letter writer, at antioch, a big city in what's now turkey. tradition says he was crucified in rome under nero, the emperor who blamed the christians for the great fire of rome, upside down, at his own request, because he didn't think he deserved to die the way jesus had.","struggle":"the denial is the struggle, and it's specific. he'd said out loud, in front of all of them, that the others might fall away but he wouldn't. then a servant girl at a fire asked him a question and he folded. mark says he started cursing and swearing he didn't know him. all four gospels tell it and none of them clean it up.\nthen the three days between the execution and the empty tomb. the man he'd sworn to die with was dead, and the last thing peter had said about him in public was that he'd never known him. nobody wrote down what those days were like for him. you can guess.","threads":["forgiveness","love"],"years":"the first century, died in rome in the sixties by tradition"},{"after":"after the book he kept the tone careful. he pushed for research instead of rushing to legalize, talked about the risks in almost every interview, and wrote this is your mind on plants, from 2021, about caffeine, opium, and mescaline, which is as much about the law as the drugs.\nhe's the one on these pages who found this late and handled it like a grown up. no conversion, no robes, just a man saying it changed how he sees some things and here's exactly how.","basically":"the journalist who told america how to eat, famous for eat food, not too much, mostly plants, who in his late fifties decided to find out what the new psychedelic research was about and ended up trying it himself. how to change your mind, from 2018, is the book most people read first, and it's why a lot of middle aged people who never touched drugs in college started asking about psilocybin. he's a skeptic by temperament and it shows. if you want the careful, grown up tour, start here.","born":1955,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 200 L 170 200 L 170 250 M 40 208 L 170 208' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='100' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 100 164 L 100 200 M 100 176 L 130 196 M 100 176 L 76 196'/><rect x='118' y='192' width='28' height='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 220 250 L 220 60 L 380 60 L 380 250' fill='var(--sage)' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 60 L 300 250'/><path d='M 340 250 L 340 125'/><ellipse cx='340' cy='116' rx='25' ry='19' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 260 250 L 260 234 M 246 234 C 246 216 274 216 274 234 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(236,231.1) scale(0.7)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a food writer in his sixties who'd never tripped went and did it, carefully, and wrote the book that made your aunt curious about mushrooms.","key":"pollan","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Pollan","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/How_to_Change_Your_Mind","why":"the 2018 book, and the netflix series made from it"},{"label":"the publisher","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/529343/how-to-change-your-mind-by-michael-pollan/","why":"penguin's page for how to change your mind"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. the fair criticism is that the book is one comfortable man's view, and that describing his own underground trips in a bestseller is a kind of advertising whether he means it or not. he's said versions of that himself.","moments":["for how to change your mind he interviewed [[griffiths|roland griffiths]] at length and sat in on the world of the hopkins studies.","he tried psilocybin, lsd, and the venom of a desert toad, which has a very strong short acting psychedelic in it. the toad was the one that scared him."],"name":"michael pollan","place":"long island, then connecticut, then berkeley, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"eat food. not too much. mostly plants.","where":"in defense of food, 2008, the first line"},{"q":"i came to it as a skeptic and a journalist, not a seeker.","where":"how to change your mind, 2018, paraphrased from the prologue"},{"q":"spiritual might just mean the opposite of egotistical.","where":"how to change your mind, the chapters on his own trips, paraphrased"}],"start":"how to change your mind. the prologue and chapter 1, then his own trips in chapter 4. or the netflix series if you'd rather watch.","status":"approved","story":"he's a journalist and professor who made his name writing about plants and food. the omnivore's dilemma, from 2006, followed four meals back to where they came from, a fast food meal, organic, and a meal he hunted and gathered himself. in defense of food, from 2008, boiled it down to seven words. he's taught at berkeley and harvard.\nin the early 2010s he got curious about the new research at johns hopkins and nyu, where people with cancer were being given psilocybin and coming out less afraid of dying. he wrote about it for the new yorker, the magazine, in 2015. then he spent years on a book.\nhow to change your mind came out in 2018. it's three things at once. a history of psychedelics, from [[hofmann|albert hofmann]]'s bicycle ride and [[wasson|gordon wasson]]'s trip to [[sabina|maría sabina]]'s town through [[leary|timothy leary]]'s crash. a report on the new science, with [[griffiths|roland griffiths]] at the center, and the brain scans that show the default mode network, the part of the brain that keeps the running story of me going, quieting down on psilocybin. and his own trips, taken around the age of sixty with underground guides, told plainly, including the scary parts. it was a bestseller, and in 2022 netflix made it into a four part series.\nin 2020 he helped start the uc berkeley center for the science of psychedelics. he's careful to say he's not an evangelist, and that these drugs can do real harm.","struggle":"he said he was scared going in. a lifelong worrier in his late fifties, with a family, deciding to take drugs he'd avoided all his life. one of his trips was frightening, and he wrote that the fear was real and that the guides were the reason it didn't turn into something worse.\nhis bigger worry since the book is the hype. he's said plainly that psychedelics are being oversold, and he knows his own book is part of how people got excited.","threads":["psychedelics","doubt"],"years":"born 1955"},{"after":"after the surrender he decided to be useful in the new world instead of dying in the old one. he went to washington again and again for his people, dealt with the ranchers, and opened the star house to anybody who came, white or comanche. whatever he took, he also gave away a lot.\nand he went looking for his mother. he found where she was buried in texas, and in 1910 he had her body brought to oklahoma and reburied near him. a year later he died and was buried next to her. they're both at fort sill now.","basically":"the last war chief of the comanche, the horse nation that ruled the southern plains, and the son of a white woman who'd been kidnapped by them as a child and never wanted to go back. he fought the army until 1875, surrendered, and then turned into a businessman who made deals with texas cattlemen, rode in a president's inaugural parade, and spent nights in a tipi praying with peyote. think of a guerrilla commander who becomes a ceo and a religious founder, and never quite stops being any of the three. take from him that surviving can mean becoming someone nobody expected, on purpose.","born":1852,"died":1911,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 160 L 170 160 L 170 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 162 L 105 118 L 180 162' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 205 L 170 205' opacity='.4'/><rect x='60' y='174' width='18' height='20' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='130' y='174' width='18' height='20' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 96 250 L 96 218 L 116 218 L 116 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 80 146 L 83 138 L 86 146 L 78 141 L 88 141 Z M 120 146 L 123 138 L 126 146 L 118 141 L 128 141 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 235 250 L 290 150 L 345 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 284 140 L 290 150 L 296 140'/><path d='M 282 250 L 290 226 L 298 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='220' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 220 168 L 220 216'/><path d='M 220 216 L 206 250 M 220 216 L 234 250'/><path d='M 220 182 L 204 206 M 220 182 L 236 206'/><path d='M 200 144 C 206 140 234 140 240 144 M 208 142 C 208 128 232 128 232 142 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 300 170 C 296 160 306 152 302 142 M 306 170 C 312 160 304 152 310 140' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(290,133.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the son of a stolen white girl and a comanche war leader, he fought the army to the end, then got rich on cattle and led all-night peyote prayers.","key":"quanahparker","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quanah_Parker","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_American_Church","why":"the peyote church he helped start"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"he had eight wives over his life and several at once, all adults, which was ordinary for a comanche leader and scandalous to the government, which ordered him to give up all but one. the story goes he told the agent he would, as soon as the agent picked which ones to send away and went and told them himself. the agent didn't.\nthe money is the real mess. texas cattlemen who leased comanche land paid him personally on the side, starting in the 1880s, and a lot of comanches believed he was selling out the tribe's grass for his own house. he got richer than anybody around him, and not all of it was clean.","moments":["in march 1905, at theodore roosevelt's inauguration in washington, he rode in the parade with other native leaders, geronimo among them.","at adobe walls a hunter named billy dixon shot a warrior off a horse nearly a mile away. he said himself it was luck."],"name":"quanah parker","place":"the staked plains of texas, then fort sill and cache, in what's now oklahoma","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the white man goes into his church and talks about jesus. the indian goes into his tipi and talks to jesus.","where":"attributed to him, and quoted by peyote people ever since; there's no dated source"},{"q":"no kill my father. he not there.","where":"what he said about the rangers' claim they'd killed peta nocona at the pease river; he said his father died a few years later, sick"},{"q":"my mother loved the indians and the wild life so much, she never wanted to go back to the white people.","where":"attributed, from when he had her body moved to be buried near him in 1910, paraphrased"}],"start":"s. c. gwynne's empire of the summer moon, from 2010, the big readable book about quanah and the comanches. it's by a white journalist, so read it knowing whose telling it is.","status":"approved","story":"in 1836 comanches raided fort parker, a family settlement in texas, and carried off a nine year old girl named cynthia ann parker. she grew up comanche, forgot english, married a war leader named peta nocona, and had three children. quanah was the oldest, born probably around 1852. in december 1860 texas rangers attacked the family's camp on the pease river and took cynthia ann and her baby daughter back to her white relatives. she didn't want to go. she never adjusted, the baby died in 1864, and cynthia ann died in 1871. quanah was a boy when she was taken, and he never saw her again.\nhe grew up with the kwahadi, the most remote comanche band, out on the staked plains of west texas, and became a war leader young. on june 27, 1874, he led hundreds of warriors against a post of white buffalo hunters at a place called adobe walls, in the texas panhandle. the hunters had long range rifles and held them off, his horse was shot out from under him, and he was hit. the army answered with the red river war, and in june 1875 he brought his band into fort sill, in indian territory, now oklahoma. they were among the last comanches to surrender.\nthen he changed completely. he took his mother's last name. he learned to deal with white cattlemen and leased comanche grazing land to texas ranchers, and got rich doing it. he built a big two story house near cache, oklahoma, with white stars painted on the roof, which everybody called the star house. in 1905 he rode in theodore roosevelt's inaugural parade, and that april roosevelt came to hunt wolves with him.\nand he found peyote, a small cactus with a mind-altering drug in it, mescaline. the story goes that after a bull gored him in texas, a mexican healer gave him peyote tea, and he got well. he became a leader of the peyote road, the all-night prayer meetings in a tipi led by a road man, where people eat peyote, sing, and pray until morning. he died of heart failure at the star house on february 23, 1911. the native american church, the legal home of that way of praying, was incorporated in oklahoma in 1918, and he's counted as one of its founders.","struggle":"he lost his mother to the rangers when he was a boy, and his father a few years later, from old wounds and grief, by quanah's account. by his teens he was on his own with a band that was being hunted.\nthen surrender. he went from the freest life on the plains to standing in line at an army post for rations. everything he'd been trained for was over, while he was still a young man, and he had to figure out what a comanche man was for now.","threads":["psychedelics","war","money","family"],"years":"probably 1852, some say earlier, to 1911"},{"after":"after the visions he didn't become a saint on a hill. he stayed at the temple by the river and talked to whoever came, in bengali, his own language, with jokes. the gospel of sri ramakrishna is just a diary of those conversations, and it's the one book in this whole forest that's mostly laughing.\nthe last year, dying, he taught from a bed in a rented garden house at cossipore, on the edge of calcutta, and made vivekananda promise to keep the boys together, the handful of young men who'd become his first monks. vivekananda did, and seven years later stood up in chicago and told america that all religions are true. that's the after. a barely literate priest's disciples built the ramakrishna mission, monks and schools and hospitals, on five continents.","basically":"a village kid from bengal, in eastern india, born in 1836, who could barely read, became the priest at a big temple outside calcutta, and spent years so overwhelmed by god that his family thought he'd lost his mind. then he did something almost nobody does. he lived as a muslim for a while, then as a christian, to see where they went, and came back saying they all get you to the same place. his student carried that to america in 1893, and a lot of the all-paths-up-the-same-mountain talk you hear today runs back through him. if you've ever wanted to test a religion instead of just believing it, he's the one who actually did.","born":1836,"died":1886,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 C 80 190 140 210 200 200 S 320 190 380 200 L 380 300 L 20 300 Z' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 C 80 190 140 210 200 200 S 320 190 380 200'/><path d='M 60 240 C 90 232 120 240 150 232 M 240 250 C 270 242 300 250 330 242' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='150' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 150 134 L 150 182'/><path d='M 150 182 L 136 216 M 150 182 L 164 216'/><path d='M 150 150 L 172 160 M 150 146 L 172 150'/><path d='M 145 125 Q 150 129 155 125'/><path d='M 172 150 L 190 175'/><circle cx='200' cy='190' r='13'/><path d='M 200 203 L 215 235 M 190 220 L 175 250 M 205 214 L 232 226'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='215' cy='180' r='4'/><circle cx='224' cy='168' r='5'/><circle cx='236' cy='154' r='6'/><\/g><path d='M 300 200 L 300 150 L 350 150 L 350 200 M 310 150 L 325 120 L 340 150' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(355,123.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a temple priest who tried being a muslim, then a christian, to check, and came back saying it's all the same water.","key":"ramakrishna","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramakrishna","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gospel_of_Sri_Ramakrishna","why":"the record of his conversations"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing gross i know of. when he was twenty three his family married him to a five year old girl, which was the custom then and in that place. she stayed with her own family until she was grown, they lived together as celibates, meaning no sex, ever, and she became a teacher in her own right, sarada devi, whom his followers call the holy mother. it reads badly now and it's on the page so you know.","moments":["his first real teacher was a woman, the bhairavi brahmani, a wandering tantric, which means a follower of the hidden side of hinduism that works through the body and ritual. she showed up at the temple in 1861 and told him his madness was the highest state, that the old texts described it, and that she'd seen it before. nobody had said that to him.","then a naked wandering monk named totapuri taught him advaita vedanta, the hindu teaching that you and god and everything are one thing and the separate self is a mistake. ramakrishna went into a state so deep he didn't move for three days. totapuri had taken forty years to get there.","the story goes that his hand would curl up and refuse if anyone put money in it. he tested it. it kept happening."],"name":"ramakrishna","place":"dakshineswar, outside calcutta","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"as many faiths, so many paths.","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the record of his conversations kept by a schoolteacher named mahendranath gupta, who signed it only m; it's the refrain of the whole book"},{"q":"when you want god the way a drowning man wants air, you'll find him.","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the story of the teacher holding a student's head under the water, paraphrased"},{"q":"the winds of god's grace are always blowing. you have to raise the sail.","where":"his sayings, as collected by his disciples, paraphrased"}],"start":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, in swami nikhilananda's translation, done in new york in the 1940s by a monk of his order. it's a diary of his conversations and it runs over a thousand pages. open it anywhere.","status":"approved","story":"he was a barely literate village boy who became the priest of a kali temple on the ganges at dakshineswar, just north of calcutta. kali is the hindu mother goddess, drawn black with her tongue out and a necklace of heads, terrifying to look at and, to the people who love her, a mother. a temple priest in india takes care of the god every day. he wakes the statue, bathes it, dresses it, feeds it and waves lamps in front of it, the way you'd care for someone you loved. ramakrishna did that and then couldn't stop. he spent years in states of god-drunk ecstasy that his relatives took for insanity. he'd fall down in the road. he'd weep for the goddess like a kid crying for his mother. people came to see him the way they'd come to see a fire.\nthen he did something nobody does. in 1866 he lived as a muslim for a stretch under a muslim teacher, saying the muslim prayers and eating the food, and had a vision. in 1874 he had someone read him the bible, did the same with christianity, and had a vision of [[jesus]] walking toward him. he came back and said what he'd say for the rest of his life: as many faiths, so many paths. his picture for it was a lake with steps down to the water on every side. hindus come down one side and call what they drink jal. muslims come down another and call it pani. the english come down a third and call it water. it's all the same water.\nthe best story about him is one he told, about a teacher and a student, not about himself. a young man asked his teacher how to see god. the teacher took him to the water and held his head under until he fought for air. when you want god the way you just wanted air, the teacher said, you'll see him.\nhis star disciple was vivekananda, a sharp, skeptical college kid from calcutta who'd walked in demanding to know if ramakrishna had actually seen god, and got told yes, more clearly than i see you. vivekananda became a monk and took the message to chicago in 1893, to the parliament of the world's religions, a big gathering held alongside the world's fair. he opened with sisters and brothers of america, and the crowd, thousands of people, applauded for minutes before he could go on. everything on these pages that assumes the traditions are talking about one thing runs partly through a barely literate priest who checked.","struggle":"for years his family thought he was insane. he'd fall down in the road. he'd stop in the middle of the temple worship and stare. he stopped sleeping. they married him off at twenty three to a five year old girl to settle him, which tells you what the eighteen hundreds thought a cure looked like.\nthe ecstasy wasn't gentle. he describes the longing for the goddess as a physical pain that made him rub his face in the dirt and weep, and it went on for years.\nhe died of throat cancer at fifty, and in the last months his disciples begged him to ask the divine mother, his name for kali, to cure him. he said he couldn't. she'd put it there.","threads":["madness","joy"],"years":"1836 to 1886"},{"after":"his mother came back years later to live with him on the hill, and he looked after her until she died in 1922. they buried her at the foot of arunachala and he moved down to the grave, and the ashram grew up around it. the boy who wouldn't go home ended up building a home around his mother.\nat the end, with the arm eaten through, people begged him not to leave them. he said they gave too much importance to the body. on the evening of april 14, 1950, the people outside his room started singing arunachala shiva, a hymn to the hill, and he died while they were singing.","basically":"a teenager from south india who was hit out of nowhere by a terror of dying, lay down on the floor and went all the way into it, and never got scared again. he spent the next fifty four years on one hill, mostly sitting quietly, and people came from all over the world just to be in the room with him. picture the calmest person you've ever met, then imagine that's all they did, for half a century. take one question from him, who am i, and don't take anybody's word for the answer, his included.","born":1879,"died":1950,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 30 250 C 90 210 150 70 210 60 C 262 70 300 180 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 210 58 C 204 48 210 40 210 34 C 214 40 218 48 210 58 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='96' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 96 192 L 96 234'/><path d='M 96 234 C 76 234 66 250 82 250 L 110 250 C 126 250 116 234 96 234'/><path d='M 96 208 L 82 234 M 96 208 L 110 234'/><path d='M 90 176 C 92 178 94 178 96 176 M 98 176 C 100 178 102 178 104 176'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='224' rx='34' ry='15' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 280 236 L 278 250 M 292 238 L 292 250 M 310 238 L 310 250 M 322 236 L 324 250 M 334 220 C 342 230 340 240 336 244'/><ellipse cx='262' cy='212' rx='10' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 256 205 L 250 198 M 266 204 L 270 197'/><g transform='translate(252,78.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"at sixteen he lay down on the floor and practiced being dead, and got up knowing the thing that dies isn't him.","key":"ramana","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramana_Maharshi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ramana-Maharshi","why":"a short, sober encyclopedia entry"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-enquiry_(Ramana_Maharshi)","why":"his one method, the question who am i"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["a cow named lakshmi came to the ashram in the twenties and more or less joined. he treated her like one of the devotees. when she died in 1948 she was buried with full rites in the ashram grounds, and he said she'd been liberated, meaning free of the round of being born and dying again.","the story goes that at the moment he died a big shooting star crossed the sky toward the hill. henri cartier-bresson, the french photographer, one of the most famous of the century, was at the ashram and saw it.","the english novelist somerset maugham visited in 1938. his 1944 novel the razor's edge is about a young american who goes to india looking for something, and the holy man he finds there is at least partly ramana."],"name":"ramana maharshi","place":"madurai, then the hill of arunachala at tiruvannamalai, for fifty four years","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 104 130 L 108 40' stroke-width='4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the question who am i will burn up every other thought, and in the end it burns up too, like the stick used to stir a funeral pyre.","where":"who am i?, a short booklet of his answers to a follower named sivaprakasam pillai, paraphrased. the stick that stirs a cremation fire gets thrown in at the end"},{"q":"they say i'm dying, but i'm not going anywhere. where would i go?","where":"said to his attendants in his last days, 1950, as they remembered it; paraphrased"},{"q":"happiness is your own nature, and it's not wrong to want it. the mistake is looking for it outside.","where":"talks with sri ramana maharshi, the notes of conversations in his hall, paraphrased"}],"start":"who am i?, which is about twenty pages. then be as you are, the collection of his talks edited by david godman.","status":"approved","story":"venkataraman was an ordinary schoolboy from the tamil-speaking south of india, living at his uncle's house in madurai, a big temple city down there. in the summer of 1896, when he was sixteen, alone in an upstairs room and perfectly healthy, a terror of death came over him out of nowhere.\nhe didn't run from it. he lay down, stiffened his body like a corpse, held his breath, and asked what dies. the body would be carried off and burned. something was still there, watching the whole thing. he said the fear left that day and never came back.\nsix weeks later he left a note saying he'd gone in search of his father, which meant god, and not to spend money looking for him, and took the train toward arunachala. that's a hill at the town of tiruvannamalai that hindus have treated for centuries as the god shiva himself, in the form of a mountain. he got there on september 1, 1896, and never left. for years he barely spoke. he sat in a dark vault under the big temple in town, then in caves on the hill.\nhis whole method was one question. who am i? whatever thought comes up, ask who it's coming to, and follow the i back to where it starts. mostly he taught it by sitting in a room and saying nothing, and people said that's where it happened. in the thirties the westerners started coming. he died in 1950 at the ashram at the foot of the hill, ashram being the hindu word for a community that grows up around a teacher, part retreat house and part commune.","struggle":"the struggle in his story is the body, and what other people wanted from it. in the vault under the temple, an underground shrine room called the patala lingam, insects ate sores into his thighs and he didn't notice. local boys threw stones down at him. an older holy man, seshadri swamigal, chased them off, and in the end people carried him out.\nin 1898 his mother found him and begged him to come home. he wouldn't speak. finally he wrote on a scrap of paper that what's meant to happen will happen however hard you try to stop it, and what isn't won't, and the best thing is to stay quiet. she went home without him.\nin 1949 a growth came up on his left elbow. it was a sarcoma, a cancer of the flesh and bone. there were operations, and his people begged him to try every other cure too, and it kept coming back. he let them try. it didn't trouble him, and that troubled them.","threads":["dying","the body","silence"],"years":"1879 to 1950"},{"after":"in 1976 he wrote a piece in yoga journal, the main magazine of the american yoga world, called egg on my beard. he said in print that he'd been conned, that he'd conned others along with him, and that the guru who'd told him to tell the truth had apparently meant it. no spin, no wisdom-of-the-lesson, just i was full of shit, and here's how.\nthen he went and did the least glamorous things a famous guru could do. in 1978 he co-founded seva, a charity that pays for cataract surgeries so poor people in nepal and india who've gone blind can see again. he spent years with stephen levine, a poet and meditation teacher, sitting with dying people. he backed a project that mailed meditation books to people in prison. he said the stroke finally made him what he'd been pretending to be, somebody who had to be helped.","basically":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.","born":1931,"died":2019,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 240 L 380 240'/><path d='M 90 150 C 60 150 50 240 90 240 L 150 240 C 190 240 180 150 150 150 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='120' cy='138' r='14'/><path d='M 115 143 Q 120 147 125 143'/><path d='M 178 190 C 210 140 250 130 285 150' stroke-dasharray='2 9'/><circle cx='292' cy='152' r='9' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 292 143 L 295 137'/><circle cx='320' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 320 164 L 320 212'/><path d='M 320 212 L 306 246 M 320 212 L 334 246'/><path d='M 320 178 L 300 154 M 320 178 L 340 154'/><path d='M 315 155 Q 320 159 325 155'/><path d='M 285 60 Q 285 42 308 44 Q 319 26 341 38 Q 360 30 365 48 Q 375 58 360 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 300 78 L 292 100 M 340 78 L 350 100' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(40,210.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was a harvard psychologist named richard alpert until a man in a blanket knew what he was thinking.","key":"ramdass","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ram_Dass","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his foundation","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/","why":"the love serve remember foundation, with his talks, podcasts, and books"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Be_Here_Now_(book)","why":"the 1971 book that made him"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"he got fired from harvard partly for giving psilocybin, the mushroom drug, to an undergraduate. the one promise he and leary had made the university was that they'd keep it away from undergraduates, and he broke it. he was honest about it later. the drugs were the door, he'd say for decades, but they weren't the room. they could show you the place and they couldn't let you live there.","moments":["his father george alpert was a boston lawyer and president of the new haven railroad, one of the big east coast rail lines, and called him rum dum for years. eventually the old man came around. ram dass took care of him at the end.","he gave the royalties from be here now to the hanuman foundation, the nonprofit he set up, named for the monkey god in hindu stories who's the perfect servant, and never took them back. that's why a man with a two million copy bestseller lived pretty small until friends bought him the house in maui.","in his last years on maui he ended nearly every talk the same way. i'm loving awareness, and so are you, and we're just walking each other home."],"name":"ram dass","place":"boston, then india, then maui","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 44 98 C 48 116 72 116 76 98' stroke-dasharray='0.1 5' stroke-width='5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"we're all just walking each other home.","where":"a line he said in talks so often nobody's sure of the first time"},{"q":"i can do nothing for you but work on myself. you can do nothing for me but work on yourself.","where":"be here now, his 1971 book"},{"q":"the stroke did what forty years of meditation couldn't.","where":"still here, the book he finished after the stroke, and fierce grace, the 2001 documentary about him, paraphrased"}],"start":"be here now, the square brown book with the hand-drawn middle; read the first part, which is his own story. then still here, which he wrote after the stroke, about getting old. then any talk on ram dass's own archive.","status":"approved","story":"richard alpert had the whole thing. a psychology professorship at harvard, his own small plane, a mercedes, a sailboat, an apartment full of antiques. he was miserable and drank too much and knew it.\nthen in 1961 timothy leary, a harvard colleague who'd go on to become the most famous drug evangelist in america, handed him psilocybin, the stuff in magic mushrooms, and the floor fell out. alpert watched every part he played, professor, pilot, the whole list, peel away one after another, until there was nothing left but somebody watching, and that somebody was fine. harvard fired them both in 1963. alpert spent the next few years trying to get the drug to say something it would keep saying, and it wouldn't. he'd go up, see everything, and come back down, every time.\nso he went to india in 1967 with no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to meet his own teacher, a fat old man wrapped in a blanket named neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to the people around him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. he hadn't told anyone. nobody in india could have known. alpert cried for two days. the old man gave him a new name, ram dass, servant of god, and told him to love everyone and tell the truth.\nbe here now, his account of all that, came out in 1971. it's a strange square brown book, part memoir and part hand-drawn pages you turn sideways, and it sold a couple million copies. for a lot of kids in the seventies it was the first spiritual book that sounded like them. in 1997 a stroke took his right side and most of his speech. he called it fierce grace, eventually, meaning a gift that nearly kills you. he spent his last twenty years in maui, slower, kinder, and by his own account further along than he'd ever been when he could talk.","struggle":"the fame after be here now went to his head and he knew it. he'd stand up in front of a thousand people talking about surrender, about letting the self go, and go back to the hotel and be a guy who'd just given a great talk about letting the self go.\nin 1974 a brooklyn housewife named joyce green, who went by joya, convinced him she was channeling maharaj-ji, his guru, who'd died the year before. he fell for it completely, moved his whole practice around her, and told everyone she was the real thing. it took him two years to see it. it's the spiritual version of getting pulled into a con you'd have spotted in a second if it happened to a friend.\nthe stroke in 1997 put him in a wheelchair, took his words, and for a while took his faith. he said he'd spent forty years talking about the soul and when the body broke he found out he'd been mostly body.","threads":["psychedelics","the body","fame","love"],"years":"1931 to 2019"},{"after":"there's no after with the family. he didn't go back. what he did with the ten dry years was keep sitting at the desk, and when the elegies finally came he wrote to his friends in a kind of shock, saying it had come through him like a storm, and got it down.\nand at the end, he wrote his own epitaph and chose his own grave, in the churchyard at raron in the swiss mountains, under a line that starts rose, oh pure contradiction.","basically":"a thin, charming, usually broke poet from prague who lived off rich admirers and wrote the letters every writing student gets handed sooner or later, the ones that say be patient with what you don't understand yet and live the questions. he also walked out on his wife and baby to do it, and never really went back. he's the guy whose advice about art is perfect and whose life you shouldn't look at too closely. take the advice anyway. it's good.","born":1875,"died":1926,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 300 L 0 150 L 200 150 C 206 200 214 250 224 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='30' y='100' width='60' height='50' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='52' y='70' width='22' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 100 L 30 94 L 38 94 L 38 100 M 82 100 L 82 94 L 90 94 L 90 100'/><circle cx='160' cy='54' r='14'/><path d='M 160 68 L 160 114'/><path d='M 160 114 L 144 150 M 160 114 L 178 146'/><path d='M 160 82 L 146 104 M 160 82 L 180 90'/><path d='M 150 76 C 138 84 132 92 124 104' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 240 70 L 300 62 M 250 96 L 330 90 M 236 124 L 290 120' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 230 250 C 260 240 290 260 320 250 S 360 240 400 250 M 240 280 C 270 270 300 290 330 280 S 370 270 400 280'/><circle cx='190' cy='144' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 190 149 L 190 150'/><g transform='translate(63,48.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote the most famous advice ever given to a young poet, and he'd left his own baby to go write it.","key":"rilke","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainer_Maria_Rilke","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/rainer-maria-rilke","why":"a life and some poems in english"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letters_to_a_Young_Poet","why":"the letters, where most people start"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he walked out on his wife and his baby daughter within about a year of the wedding. clara went to paris to work too, and ruth was left with clara's parents. they never divorced and they wrote to each other, but he chose the work over the family and kept choosing it. there was a long line of women after that, all adults.\nhe lived most of his life on other people's money. princesses, countesses, rich admirers. muzot was bought by a swiss businessman, werner reinhart, so rilke could live in it. he was very good at being taken care of, and not much good at taking care of anyone.","moments":["the story goes that in the autumn of 1926 he pricked his hand on a rose thorn cutting flowers for a visitor, and the wound wouldn't heal. that part's legend. the leukemia was real and was diagnosed only weeks before he died.","the first letter to kappus says the only question that matters is whether you'd die if you weren't allowed to write. if the answer is yes, build your life around it. he did.","at duino, the story comes from the castle's owner, his patron princess marie von thurn und taxis. he was walking on the cliffs in a hard wind and the first line of the first elegy came to him out of the air. he wrote it down in his notebook and went back inside."],"name":"rainer maria rilke","place":"prague, munich, paris, a castle on the adriatic, a tower in the swiss valais","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 80 C 52 92 68 92 70 80 C 66 84 54 84 50 80 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"live the questions now.","where":"letters to a young poet, the fourth letter, july 1903"},{"q":"you must change your life.","where":"the last line of archaic torso of apollo, a poem about a broken ancient statue with no head, in new poems, 1908"},{"q":"who, if i cried out, would hear me among the angels?","where":"the duino elegies, the first line of the first elegy, paraphrased"}],"start":"letters to a young poet, the first and fourth letters. then the first duino elegy. stephen mitchell's translations are the ones most people read.","status":"approved","story":"he was born rené rilke in prague in 1875, when it belonged to the austrian empire. his mother had lost a baby daughter before he was born, and for his first years she dressed him as a girl and kept his hair long. then his father sent him to military school, where he was miserable for five years.\nin his early twenties he fell in with lou andreas-salomé, a russian-born writer fourteen years older than him, married, brilliant, who'd turned down the philosopher nietzsche and would later train with freud. she got him to change rené to rainer and took him to russia twice. in 1901 he married the sculptor clara westhoff, and their daughter ruth was born that december. within a year he'd gone to paris to write a book on rodin, the most famous sculptor alive, the man who made the thinker. for the rest of his life he mostly lived apart from them.\nfrom 1903 to 1908 he wrote ten letters to a young military cadet named franz kappus who'd sent him some poems and asked if they were any good. they were published after rilke died, as letters to a young poet, and they're the thing most people know him for. be patient with everything unresolved in your heart. live the questions now.\nin january 1912, staying as a guest at duino castle, on the adriatic coast near trieste, he started a set of long poems he called elegies, which means poems of mourning, here mourning and praise at once. then he stalled for ten years. in february 1922, alone in a little stone tower called muzot in switzerland, it all came at once. he finished the duino elegies and wrote the sonnets to orpheus, fifty five of them, in a few weeks. orpheus is the singer in greek myth who went down to the land of the dead to bring his wife back. rilke died of leukemia in december 1926, at fifty one.","struggle":"the ten years between the first elegies and the last were a wall. he wrote bits and fragments and letters, hundreds of letters, and couldn't finish the thing he knew he was supposed to write. he said he was stuck and scared that he'd never get it back.\nthe first world war made it worse. in 1916 the austrian army drafted him, and after a few weeks of training he was put at a desk in the war archive in vienna, doing paperwork until friends got him out that summer. he'd lived his whole life for the inner life, and the world had no use for it.","threads":["family","work"],"years":"1875 to 1926"},{"after":"he didn't leave. that's the thing about rohr. every reason to walk out of the church and he stayed in it, built a center at the edge of it, and kept the door propped open so people who were halfway out could come and go.\nwhen the letters come he answers them, politely, and goes back to work. he calls his position an alternative orthodoxy. orthodoxy is the official version of what the church believes, and his point is that franciscans have always lived inside it, doing it their own way. i'm inside the house, he's saying. i just use the side door.","basically":"a catholic priest in new mexico, in his eighties now, who's spent fifty years telling people their faith isn't dead just because the version they grew up with stopped working. his big idea is that life has two halves, and the second one only starts when something you built falls apart. what most people call a midlife crisis, he calls the door. if you grew up in church and quietly walked out, he's the one saying you might be closer now than you were in the pew.","born":1943,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='170' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 170 134 L 170 182'/><path d='M 170 182 L 156 216 M 170 182 L 184 216'/><path d='M 170 150 L 192 160 M 170 146 L 192 150'/><path d='M 165 125 Q 170 129 175 125'/><path d='M 196 150 C 190 150 186 165 190 180 L 226 180 C 230 165 226 150 220 150 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 206 152 L 210 166 L 204 178'/><path d='M 214 160 L 250 130 M 216 168 L 262 160 M 214 176 L 250 196' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 300 244 C 306 236 316 236 320 244 M 320 244 L 328 240 M 306 250 L 306 244 M 314 250 L 314 244'/><g transform='translate(60,220.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a franciscan priest who says the first half of your life builds the box and the second half starts when something breaks it.","key":"rohr","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Rohr","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his center","url":"https://cac.org/","why":"the center for action and contemplation, where you can sign up for the free daily meditation"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing gross i know of. plenty of conservative catholics think he's a heretic, meaning somebody teaching what the church says is false, and he's fine with that.","moments":["in 1971 in cincinnati he founded the new jerusalem community, a lay community, meaning ordinary people, not priests or nuns, that at its height had hundreds of people living close and sharing money. it's where he learned that community is hard and worth it.","the center for action and contemplation started in 1987 in albuquerque. the name was a fight he wanted to end. the activists thought sitting in silent prayer was navel gazing, and the people who did the silent prayer thought activism was ego. he said do both or you're doing neither.","he keeps a dog, and the dogs show up in the books. venus, then opie. he says a dog will teach you more about unconditional presence than most theologians."],"name":"richard rohr","place":"kansas, then albuquerque","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"we don't think ourselves into a new way of living. we live ourselves into a new way of thinking.","where":"a line he uses constantly, in books and talks"},{"q":"if you don't transform your pain, you'll transmit it.","where":"falling upward, his 2011 book about the second half of life, and elsewhere, paraphrased"},{"q":"the first half of life is discovering the script. the second half is writing it and owning it.","where":"falling upward, paraphrased"}],"start":"falling upward. then the universal christ if you want the big argument. his daily meditations, a short email the center for action and contemplation sends out every morning, are short and free.","status":"approved","story":"he's a franciscan, a priest ordained in 1970. franciscans are the catholic order that follows francis of assisi, a rich merchant's kid from italy in the 1200s who gave away everything he owned, preached to birds, and, the story goes, got off his horse and kissed a leper, a man with the disease everybody was terrified to touch. rohr took his vows to a guy like that.\nin 1987 he founded the center for action and contemplation in albuquerque, a teaching and retreat center. action means working for justice out in the world. contemplation means sitting quiet with god. the name is the whole program. you do both, or neither one works.\nfalling upward, from 2011, is his best known book and it's one point. the first half of life is about building a container, identity, career, rules, a self. the second half only begins when that container gets cracked by something you didn't sign up for. a divorce, say, or a diagnosis. nobody chooses to fall. you get thrown.\nhe reads the bible the way the mystics read it. mystics are the people in every religion who want to experience god directly instead of just believing things about god, and they read scripture looking for what it's pointing at instead of what it's saying. the universal christ, from 2019, argues that christ was never [[jesus]]'s last name. it's a title, it means the anointed one, and rohr says it's the name for god showing up in everything, rocks and dogs and strangers, with jesus as the clearest example.\nhe doesn't get everybody all the way there, and that's fine. he's the guy holding the door open between the church a lot of us grew up in and everything outside it.","struggle":"he's spent fifty years as a priest in a church that keeps writing letters about him. complaints to rome, meaning the vatican, the pope's headquarters. cardinals, the church's top officials under the pope, asking questions. bishops uninviting him from their dioceses. he says he's had to learn to hold the institution loosely and the vows tight.\nin his own telling the harder struggle was quieter. somewhere in midlife he found that the first half of life's answers had run out on him too, and that the book he'd write about falling was going to be about himself.","threads":["doubt","silence"],"years":"born 1943"},{"after":"he stopped looking. he said, roughly, why should i seek? i am the same as he. his essence speaks through me. that's not a man who got over it. that's a man who decided the missing person was inside him now, and wrote sixty thousand lines to prove it.\nthen he found two more friends, a goldsmith named salah al-din and then a young man named husam al-din, and dictated the masnavi, his six volume poem, to husam over the last twelve years of his life, walking around konya while husam wrote it down. the wound never closed. it became the mouth.","basically":"a muslim scholar in thirteenth century turkey, a respected preacher with a following, whose whole life got blown open at thirty seven by a scruffy wandering holy man he met in the street. the two of them were inseparable, his students got jealous, and then the friend disappeared, probably murdered. rumi spent the rest of his life turning that loss into poems, tens of thousands of lines of them. today he's all over wedding programs and instagram, usually in soft american versions with the islam taken out. if you've ever lost somebody and found them still talking inside you, that's his whole subject.","born":1207,"died":1273,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 230 150 C 170 260 290 260 230 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='230' cy='108' r='14'/><path d='M 230 122 L 230 150 M 230 132 L 196 112 M 230 132 L 264 114'/><ellipse cx='230' cy='258' rx='58' ry='7' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 150 200 C 140 170 170 160 180 190 M 290 200 C 300 170 270 160 262 190' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='70' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 70 164 L 70 210'/><path d='M 70 210 L 54 246 M 70 210 L 88 242'/><path d='M 70 178 L 56 200 M 70 178 L 90 186'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='20' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 300 90 C 320 80 340 100 360 90' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(345,230.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a respectable professor of religion until a wild man walked into his life, and then he couldn't stop singing.","key":"rumi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the masnavi, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/isl/masnavi/index.htm","why":"whinfield's old abridged english version of his big poem, public domain"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mevlevi_Order","why":"the whirling order his followers started"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. the popular english versions by coleman barks are loose and strip out most of the islam. in the originals the god he's in love with is allah, and the lines are full of the quran and the prophet muhammad. the american versions make him sound like he came from nowhere in particular, which is its own kind of mess, and worth knowing when you read him.","moments":["his family fled the mongols, the armies from the east that were flattening whole cities, out of balkh, in what's now afghanistan, when he was a boy. the story says the old poet attar, the great persian mystic who wrote the conference of the birds, met the child on the road and gave him a book and said this one will set fire to the world.","the whirling comes from him. the story is that he heard the goldsmiths hammering in the bazaar, the market street, and started turning right there. his followers made it a practice, a slow spin with one palm up to take from heaven and one palm down to give it to the earth. the dervishes still do it in konya every december, on the anniversary of his death.","when he died in december 1273, the story goes, jews and christians walked in the funeral with the muslims, and when someone asked why, they said he'd been theirs too."],"name":"rumi","place":"balkh, then konya, in what's now turkey","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 28 54 C 26 24 94 24 92 54 C 84 40 36 40 28 54 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 50 34 70 34 84 44 M 32 50 C 46 38 74 38 88 50' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"out beyond ideas of wrongdoing and rightdoing there's a field. i'll meet you there.","where":"quatrain 158, one of his four line poems, as coleman barks renders it. barks is an american poet who doesn't read persian and reworks older scholars' english translations into loose free verse. the persian is rougher"},{"q":"the wound is the place where the light enters you.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; probably barks squeezing a longer passage from the masnavi, rumi's six volume poem, into one line"},{"q":"you were born with wings. why crawl through life?","where":"the divan, his big collection of short poems, as widely translated"}],"start":"the essential rumi, coleman barks's collection, for the doorway, knowing it's a doorway. then the masnavi, book one, in jawid mojaddedi's translation, which goes straight from the persian and keeps the rhymes, for the real thing.","status":"approved","story":"jalal ad-din rumi was a muslim scholar and preacher in konya, a city in the middle of what's now turkey. he was a serious man with students, the kind of religious professor people came to for rulings on how to live. then in 1244 a wandering dervish named shams of tabriz stopped him in the street. a dervish was a muslim holy man who'd given up everything, owned nothing, and drifted from town to town living on what people gave him. shams asked rumi a question, and nobody agrees what it was.\none version goes like this. shams asked who was greater, the prophet muhammad or bayazid, an earlier muslim mystic, somebody who wants to meet god directly and not only follow the rules, famous for saying wild things like glory be to me. rumi said muhammad, obviously. then why, shams asked, did muhammad say he'd never known god as well as he should, while bayazid talked like he'd swallowed god whole? rumi answered that bayazid's thirst was small and one gulp filled him, and muhammad's thirst never ended. in some tellings rumi fainted. whatever really happened, the scholar fell apart and came back a poet.\nshams and rumi were inseparable for a couple of years, shut away together talking for days on end, and rumi's students hated it. their teacher had stopped teaching them. then shams vanished for good, probably murdered, possibly by people close to rumi. rumi wrote thousands of poems to him, in his voice, as him, and signed them with shams's name. the divan, rumi's giant collection of short love poems, is named for shams, not for rumi.\nthe masnavi, his big book, is six volumes of stories inside stories in rhyming couplets, dirty jokes next to prayers. it opens with a reed flute crying because it was cut from the reed bed, and every sad song it plays is that one ache. you're the flute. all of it points at one thing: you and god are lovers who've been apart too long. sufis, the people inside islam who go after god directly like that, call it the quran in persian, the quran being islam's holy book.\nthe whirling dervishes, the men in tall felt hats and white skirts who spin in slow circles as a way of praying, come from him. his son organized them into an order after he died. and he's the best selling poet in america, which would have made him laugh.\nwhen he died, the story goes, christians and jews came to his funeral alongside the muslims, and when asked why, they said he'd been theirs too.","struggle":"losing shams broke him open twice. the first time shams left, in 1246, rumi sent his own son, sultan walad, all the way to damascus, in syria, to bring him back, and he came. the second time, about a year later, shams vanished for good, and the rumor was that rumi's other son and some jealous students had killed him. rumi went looking for him for two years anyway.\nthe poems that came out of that are grief pretending to be love poems, or love poems that had to go through grief to get here. he wrote in shams's name because he couldn't stand to write in his own. it's like losing your best friend and still talking to him out loud in the car, for years, and meaning every word.","threads":["love","grief"],"years":"1207 to 1273"},{"after":"there wasn't a comeback. she kept healing the people in her own town who still came to her, for as long as she could, and she kept saying plainly that the foreigners had ruined it. she worked with álvaro estrada, a man from huautla, to get her own life down in her own words, and that book came out in 1977. it's the only reason we have her side at all.\nafter she died she became a symbol, her face on murals and souvenirs in mexico and far beyond, including in huautla. the town that burned her house sells her picture now. that's not a redemption. it's just what happened.","basically":"a mazatec healer in a mountain town in southern mexico who couldn't read or write and spent her life using mushrooms at night to heal sick people. in 1955 she let an american banker watch, he wrote it up, and within ten years her town was full of hippies and rock fans looking for a trip. her neighbors blamed her, her house got burned, and she died poor. every psilocybin study at johns hopkins, every microdosing podcast, runs back through her kitchen. take from her what it costs when the thing you hold sacred becomes somebody else's weekend.","born":1894,"died":1985,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 270 C 80 250 120 200 170 190 C 230 180 300 220 380 210 L 380 290 L 20 290 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 120 206 L 120 166 L 190 166 L 190 196' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 112 168 L 155 136 L 198 168'/><rect x='140' y='174' width='18' height='16' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 149 186 L 149 180'/><path d='M 149 178 C 146 176 149 172 149 172 C 149 172 152 176 149 178' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='322' cy='55' r='15' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='262' cy='180' r='5'/><path d='M 262 185 L 262 202 M 262 202 L 257 212 M 262 202 L 267 212'/><circle cx='284' cy='186' r='5'/><path d='M 284 191 L 284 208 M 284 208 L 279 218 M 284 208 L 289 218'/><circle cx='306' cy='190' r='5'/><path d='M 306 195 L 306 212 M 306 212 L 301 222 M 306 212 L 311 222'/><circle cx='328' cy='190' r='5'/><path d='M 328 195 L 328 212 M 328 212 L 323 222 M 328 212 L 333 222'/><circle cx='350' cy='188' r='5'/><path d='M 350 193 L 350 210 M 350 210 L 345 220 M 350 210 L 355 220'/><circle cx='372' cy='186' r='5'/><path d='M 372 191 L 372 208 M 372 208 L 367 218 M 372 208 L 377 218'/><g transform='translate(160,128.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she let one american banker sit in on a healing night, and the whole world followed him up the mountain.","key":"sabina","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar%C3%ADa_Sabina","why":"the plain overview of the life"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. she let a stranger in because she trusted him, or because a local official asked her to, and the stranger told the world. that's his mess, and it's on [[wasson|his page]].","moments":["wasson recorded her chanting through a velada in 1956, and the record came out in 1957. the chants go on for pages, her naming who she is over and over. poets later translated them and printed them as poetry. she never read them.","in 1962 the swiss chemist [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] came up the mountain with pills of the drug he'd made from her mushrooms in his lab. she took them and said the pills had the same power as the mushrooms.","she said she first ate the mushrooms as a small girl, out in the hills, long before anyone taught her what they were for."],"name":"maría sabina","place":"huautla de jiménez, in the mountains of oaxaca, southern mexico","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 36 70 C 34 86 36 100 40 112 M 84 70 C 86 86 84 100 80 112' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 104 L 80 104 M 44 110 L 48 116 L 52 110 L 56 116 L 60 110 L 64 116 L 68 110 L 72 116 L 76 110' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"from the moment the foreigners arrived, the saint children lost their purity.","where":"maría sabina: her life and chants, the book of her own story as told to álvaro estrada, a mazatec writer from her town, 1977; paraphrased from the english translation"},{"q":"before wasson, nobody took the mushrooms just to find god. they were always for the sick, to get well.","where":"the same book, paraphrased"},{"q":"the language belongs to the saint children. they speak, and i have the power to translate.","where":"the same book, paraphrased"}],"start":"maría sabina: her life and chants, the book she told to álvaro estrada. read her before anyone who wrote about her.","status":"approved","story":"she was a mazatec, one of the indigenous peoples of the mountains in oaxaca, a state in the far south of mexico, and she lived her whole life in and around huautla de jiménez, a town on a steep ridge. she never learned to read. she said she'd forgotten what year she was born, only that her mother told her it was on the feast of mary magdalene. she was widowed twice and raised her kids on what she could grow and sell.\nshe was a curandera, the spanish word for a healer, and her work was the velada, which means a night vigil. somebody sick, or somebody in trouble, came to her, and she sat up with them through the night with the mushrooms, which the mazatecs called the little saints or the saint children. the velada was for healing and for finding out what was wrong. she said the mushrooms did the talking and she only translated. what happened inside those nights belonged to her people, and this page leaves it there.\nin june 1955 an american named gordon wasson, a vice president at the bank j.p. morgan and an amateur mushroom scholar, came up the mountain and asked to sit in. she let him. in may 1957 he published the story in life magazine, the biggest picture magazine in america, under the title seeking the magic mushroom. in the magazine he changed her name. in his private two volume book that same year, and in what he wrote after, he used her real name and the name of her town. imagine a stranger posting your grandmother's address on a travel site as the best place in the world to get high.\nthe seekers came. by the early sixties young americans and europeans were climbing to huautla, then hippies by the hundreds, plus scientists and writers and people who were famous for other things. most of them didn't want healing. they wanted a trip. the mexican government eventually sent in police and soldiers and deported foreigners. her neighbors blamed her for bringing all of it. her house was burned. she was jailed for a short time. one of her sons was murdered. she said the foreigners had spoiled the little saints and they'd lost their purity.\nshe died on november 22, 1985, poor, in the town she'd never left. she didn't get rich off any of what came after.","struggle":"she'd already had a hard life before anybody from outside knew her name. two husbands dead, kids to feed, and a town that went to her when it was sick and gossiped about her the rest of the time. that was just the job.\nwhat broke her was the sixties. her own practice, the thing she'd done since she was a girl, became the reason people drove twenty hours from mexico city and flew in from california. the veladas stopped being about sick people. she said the saint children didn't work the same anymore, and she said it as a fact about the world, the way you'd say a well went bad.","threads":["psychedelics","fame","grief"],"years":"about 1894 to 1985"},{"after":"after he rose into the air and vanished, so the story goes, the monks later found him far to the south, living as a layman, an ordinary guy out of robes, and he told them where he'd hidden two other books in his room. then he stayed gone. he didn't come back to be congratulated.\nthe book he left is still the one tibetan teachers hand you when you ask how to actually live. the dalai lama, the exiled leader of tibetan buddhism, has taught it more than any other text. the slob's homework became the syllabus.","basically":"an indian monk around the year 700 whose fellow monks had him down as a total slacker, and who turned out to have written the most practical guide to being a decent person in all of buddhism. tibetan teachers still hand it to you when you ask how to actually live, and the dalai lama has taught from it more than any other book. think of the guy at work everybody's written off who turns in the best thing the place ever made, then quits. if anger's your problem, his chapter on patience is the one that talks you down.","born":685,"died":763,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 170 250 L 170 220 L 230 220 L 230 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='200' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 200 124 L 200 166'/><path d='M 200 166 C 180 166 170 182 186 182 L 214 182 C 230 182 220 166 200 166'/><path d='M 200 140 L 172 150 M 200 140 L 228 150'/><path d='M 195 115 Q 200 119 205 115'/><path d='M 175 190 C 190 200 210 200 225 190' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 180 205 C 195 212 205 212 220 205' opacity='.3'/><g opacity='.7'><circle cx='70' cy='210' r='10'/><path d='M 70 224 L 70 266'/><path d='M 70 266 C 50 266 40 282 56 282 L 84 282 C 100 282 90 266 70 266'/><path d='M 70 240 L 56 266 M 70 240 L 84 266'/><circle cx='120' cy='216' r='9'/><path d='M 120 230 L 120 272'/><path d='M 120 272 C 100 272 90 288 106 288 L 134 288 C 150 288 140 272 120 272'/><path d='M 120 246 L 106 272 M 120 246 L 134 272'/><circle cx='300' cy='212' r='10'/><path d='M 300 226 L 300 268'/><path d='M 300 268 C 280 268 270 284 286 284 L 314 284 C 330 284 320 268 300 268'/><path d='M 300 242 L 286 268 M 300 242 L 314 268'/><circle cx='350' cy='218' r='9'/><path d='M 350 232 L 350 274'/><path d='M 350 274 C 330 274 320 290 336 290 L 364 290 C 380 290 370 274 350 274'/><path d='M 350 248 L 336 274 M 350 248 L 364 274'/><\/g><path d='M 235 250 C 236 244 246 244 247 250 M 252 250 C 253 244 263 244 264 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(200,193.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the other monks thought he was a lazy slob, so they set him up to recite scripture in public and he recited a masterpiece.","key":"shantideva","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shantideva","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodhicaryavatara","why":"his one big book"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bud/tpol/index.htm","why":"the path of light, an old public domain english version of the way of the bodhisattva"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"none that history recorded, besides the sleeping.","moments":["the nickname the monks gave him, bhusuku, is a squashed-together word for eat, sleep, and shit. that's who wrote the way of the bodhisattva.","the exchange of self and other in chapter 8 is the hardest practice in the book. you take the other person's happiness as if it were yours and your own as if it were theirs, and then you actually want theirs more. he says it's the only thing that's ever worked.","pema chödrön, the american buddhist nun, wrote a whole book walking through it, called no time to lose. she says she teaches it because it's the book that made her laugh at herself."],"name":"shantideva","place":"nalanda, india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"where would i find enough leather to cover the earth? with leather on my feet, the earth is covered.","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, his one big book, chapter 5, verses 13 and 14"},{"q":"if there's a fix, why be upset? if there's no fix, what's the use of being upset?","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6, the one on patience, verse 10"},{"q":"all the happiness in the world comes from wanting others to be happy. all the misery comes from wanting it for yourself.","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 8, paraphrased"}],"start":"the way of the bodhisattva in the padmakara translation, done by a group of western translators working with tibetan teachers in france. read chapter 6 first, then 8. pema chödrön, an american buddhist nun whose book when things fall apart gets passed around by people having the worst year of their lives, walks through the whole thing in no time to lose.","status":"approved","story":"he was a monk at nalanda, in northeast india, for centuries the biggest buddhist university in the world, a sprawling campus where thousands of monks lived and studied, the closest thing that age had to harvard. the other monks thought he did nothing but eat, sleep, and go to the toilet. they gave him a nickname that meant exactly that.\nso they set him up. they asked him to recite scripture in front of the whole assembly, figuring he'd stand there with nothing and be humiliated. he asked whether they wanted something old or something new. they said new. he recited the way of the bodhisattva, ten chapters of verse nobody had ever heard, from memory. the legend says partway through the chapter on wisdom he rose into the air and vanished, and his voice kept going.\nthe book is about how to become a bodhisattva. in buddhism that's someone who could take their own final peace and walk away from the whole mess of life, and won't, until everybody else gets out too. like a lifeguard who won't leave the beach while anyone's still in the water. chapter 6, on patience, is the hardest thing in buddhist writing. he says one flash of anger burns up the good you've done over a thousand eons, ages so long they're lifetimes stacked on lifetimes. and he says the person hurting you is a treasure, because without them you'd have nowhere to practice patience.\nthe famous image is from chapter 5. you'll never find enough leather to cover the whole earth so it stops cutting your feet. put leather on the soles of your feet and it's the same thing. you don't fix the world. you fix where you stand on it.","struggle":"the legend says he practiced at night, alone, and let the whole monastery think he was a slob during the day. if that's true, the struggle was years of being the guy everyone had written off, and choosing not to correct them.\nthe text itself is a struggle written down. the chapter on patience isn't a calm man's chapter. it's somebody talking himself out of anger line by line, out loud, because he needed to hear it.","threads":["anger"],"years":"the eighth century"},{"after":"after the raid he didn't hide. he kept teaching, kept writing, gave talks, and in 2010 was the subject of a documentary, dirty pictures. friends and fans called him the godfather of ecstasy, which he disliked.\nann spent her last years making the case for mdma therapy, which by then was in serious clinical trials paid for by maps, the multidisciplinary association for psychedelic studies, and speaking about marriage and the shadow, the parts of yourself you don't want to see. she outlived him by eight years and kept the hill going.","basically":"sasha was a chemist who worked for dow chemical, then built a lab behind his house and spent forty years making new psychedelic compounds and trying them himself, carefully, with a small group of friends. he's the one who got mdma, now known as ecstasy or molly, into the hands of therapists in the seventies. ann was his wife, a lay therapist who used those drugs with clients before they were banned. together they wrote two huge books that are half memoir and half chemistry. think of the nerdiest couple you know, with a license from the government to do the one thing it hates most.","born":1925,"died":2022,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 C 120 230 260 230 380 260 L 380 290 L 20 290 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 280 240 L 280 180 L 360 180 L 360 240' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 272 182 L 320 150 L 368 182'/><path d='M 120 236 L 120 206 L 230 206 L 230 236 M 110 206 L 240 206'/><path d='M 150 206 L 150 190 L 142 180 L 164 180 L 156 190 L 156 206' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 190 206 L 190 176 M 200 206 L 200 186' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='90' cy='132' r='14'/><path d='M 90 146 L 90 194'/><path d='M 90 194 L 76 228 M 90 194 L 104 228'/><path d='M 90 160 L 76 184 M 90 160 L 116 154'/><circle cx='260' cy='138' r='14'/><path d='M 260 152 L 260 200'/><path d='M 260 200 L 246 234 M 260 200 L 274 234'/><path d='M 260 166 L 274 190 M 260 166 L 234 160'/><path d='M 74 124 L 106 124 M 76 118 C 80 110 100 110 104 118' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(208,194.0) scale(0.8)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a chemist tested hundreds of new mind drugs on himself in a shed on his hill, and his wife helped him write it all down as a love story.","key":"shulgin","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Shulgin","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ann_Shulgin","why":"ann's life, the therapist half of the pair"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PiHKAL","why":"their first book together"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of in how they treated people. the real argument is about the work. publishing detailed methods for making new drugs put them in the hands of people who were never going to be careful, and some of the compounds from his notebooks showed up years later in the street drug trade. he believed in information being free. the cost of that was paid by strangers.\nand mdma is not harmless. people have died from overheating and from drinking too much water at raves, and the questions about what heavy use does to the brain aren't settled. in 2024 the us food and drug administration turned down an application to approve mdma therapy for ptsd, asking for more study.","moments":["they tested new compounds with a small circle of old friends, the same people for decades, who met at the house and wrote careful notes.","the shulgin rating scale, a simple plus and minus system he invented for how strong an effect was, is still used by researchers.","pihkal stands for phenethylamines i have known and loved, a family of chemicals that includes mescaline. tihkal is the same joke for tryptamines, the family with psilocybin and dmt in it."],"name":"sasha and ann shulgin","place":"a hilltop farm in lafayette, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 96 L 92 106 L 84 124 L 108 124 L 100 106 L 100 96 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 90 96 L 102 96'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i learned there was a great deal inside me.","where":"sasha, on his first mescaline experience in 1960, told in pihkal and in interviews"},{"q":"it's my low-calorie martini.","where":"sasha, describing mdma in his notes from the seventies, as later quoted"},{"q":"i publish so the information is out there and can't be taken away.","where":"sasha, in interviews about why they printed the chemistry, paraphrased"}],"start":"the first half of pihkal, the love story, which is book one. skip book two.","status":"approved","story":"alexander shulgin, sasha to everyone, was born in berkeley in 1925, served in the navy, and got a phd in biochemistry. at dow chemical in the fifties he invented a profitable pesticide, and the company let him do more or less what he wanted. in 1960 he took mescaline, the psychedelic in the peyote cactus, and said he learned there was a great deal inside him. he spent the rest of his life on it. he left dow in 1966 and built a lab behind his house on a hill in lafayette, east of san francisco.\nhe held a license from the dea, the us drug enforcement administration, to work with banned substances, consulted for them, and testified as an expert. meanwhile he designed and made hundreds of new compounds and tested them on himself first, then with a small circle of friends, and rated what happened. in 1976 he made mdma, a drug first made by the german company merck in 1912 and forgotten, and gave it to a psychotherapist friend, leo zeff, who was about to retire and instead spent his last years teaching it to hundreds of other therapists. they said it helped people talk about things they'd never been able to say.\nann, born in new zealand in 1931, married him in 1981. she was a lay therapist, trained by doing rather than by a degree, and worked with clients using mdma and other drugs while that was still legal. together they wrote pihkal: a chemical love story, in 1991, and tihkal: the continuation, in 1997. the first half of each is their life, a love story told in turns. the second half is the chemistry. this page stays out of that half.\nin 1994, two years after pihkal came out, the dea raided the lab, fined him twenty five thousand dollars over samples people had sent him anonymously, and took the license back. he kept working and teaching anyway. sasha had a stroke in 2010 and died of liver cancer in 2014 at eighty eight. ann died in 2022.","struggle":"sasha's first wife, nina, died in 1977, after a stroke. he met ann not long after, and the love story in pihkal starts from that grief.\nthe government that licensed him turned on him. the pihkal book, with its recipes in the back, embarrassed the dea, and the 1994 raid ended the legal side of his lab. in his last years a stroke took most of his speech and some of his memory. ann looked after him on the hill, with friends raising money for his care.","threads":["psychedelics","love","work"],"years":"sasha 1925 to 2014, ann 1931 to 2022"},{"after":"after the surrender he didn't pretend it hadn't happened and he didn't pretend to be converted either. he went home to a cabin on the grand river, near where he was born, raised horses and kids, and argued with the agent. in 1889, when a government commission came to break up the great sioux reservation and buy the pieces, he spoke against it. they got the signatures anyway.\nafter the wild west show he came home too. he'd seen the cities and the crowds. the story goes that he gave most of his show money away to hungry kids in the streets, and couldn't understand how a country that rich let its own children beg. he never went back on tour.","basically":"the hunkpapa lakota holy man who became the face of the plains nations saying no. he wasn't a general. he was the one people trusted to pray, to dream, and to decide, and in 1876 he saw the biggest native victory over the us army in a vision before it happened. then he spent four hungry years in canada, four months in a wild west show, and was shot by his own people's police on his own doorstep. think of the one elder in town everybody, the government included, knows will not sign. take from him that you can lose everything and still be the person who didn't sell.","born":1831,"died":1890,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 30 250 L 80 160 L 130 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 74 150 L 80 160 L 86 150'/><path d='M 72 250 L 80 226 L 88 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 292 250 L 330 180 L 368 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 324 170 L 330 180 L 336 170'/><path d='M 322 250 L 330 226 L 338 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 200 250 L 200 110 M 200 140 L 184 118 M 200 132 L 218 112'/><path d='M 184 118 L 180 132 M 218 112 L 224 126' stroke='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='236' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 236 192 L 236 234'/><path d='M 236 234 C 216 234 206 250 222 250 L 250 250 C 266 250 256 234 236 234'/><path d='M 236 208 L 222 234 M 236 208 L 250 234'/><g transform='translate(110,40) rotate(180) scale(.28)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -16 52 M 0 28 L 16 52'/><\/g><g transform='translate(160,70) rotate(170) scale(.28)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -16 52 M 0 28 L 16 52'/><\/g><g transform='translate(270,50) rotate(190) scale(.28)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -16 52 M 0 28 L 16 52'/><\/g><path d='M 104 50 L 100 60 M 154 80 L 150 90 M 264 60 L 262 70' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(330,163.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he danced until he dropped, saw soldiers falling into camp like grasshoppers, and a few weeks later custer rode in.","key":"sittingbull","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sitting_Bull","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the battlefield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/libi/index.htm","why":"the national park service site at the little bighorn, which now honors the lakota and cheyenne who fought there"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of. he was proud and stubborn, and the agent at standing rock called it arrogance in every report. that isn't a mess. that was the job.","moments":["in 1884, at a show in st. paul, minnesota, he saw a young sharpshooter named annie oakley hit everything she aimed at. he adopted her as a daughter, the lakota way, named her little sure shot, and offered sixty five dollars for a photograph of the two of them. she used the name the rest of her life.","the story goes that when the shooting started at his arrest, the gray horse buffalo bill had given him, trained for the show, heard the gunfire and sat down and started doing its tricks.","his son crow foot, the boy who handed over the rifle at fort buford, was killed with him at the cabin in 1890.","in 2016 thousands of people camped at standing rock, where he lived and died, against the dakota access pipeline, an oil line under the missouri river upstream of the reservation's water."],"name":"sitting bull","place":"the grand river and the yellowstone country on the northern plains, then canada, then the standing rock reservation on the border of the dakotas","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 80 34 C 86 22 92 12 98 4 C 98 16 92 28 84 36 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 35 L 97 7' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"let us put our minds together and see what life we can make for our children.","where":"attributed; widely quoted and hard to pin to a date or place"},{"q":"i wish it to be remembered that i was the last man of my tribe to give up my rifle.","where":"reported from his surrender at fort buford, july 1881, paraphrased"},{"q":"the voice in the vision said, i give you these because they have no ears.","where":"the sun dance vision, june 1876, as his relatives told it later, paraphrased. no ears meant the soldiers wouldn't listen"}],"start":"ernie lapointe's sitting bull: his life and legacy, written by his great-grandson from the family's own stories. then robert utley's the lance and the shield, a careful life by a white historian.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1831 on the grand river in what's now south dakota, into the hunkpapa, one of the seven bands of the lakota, the plains nation white americans called the sioux. hunkpapa means head of the circle, because when the bands camped together, their lodges stood at the entrance. as a boy they called him slow, because he took his time with everything. at fourteen he rode into a fight with the crow, a rival nation, and counted coup, which means he touched an enemy with his hand or a stick, a braver thing than killing him. his father gave him his own name for it. tatanka iyotake. sitting bull.\nhe became a wičháša wakȟáŋ, a holy man, which is the lakota word for someone who prays and dreams on behalf of the people. outsiders say medicine man. by the 1870s, the lakota who refused to go live on reservations looked to him as their leader. in early june 1876, in a huge camp on rosebud creek in montana, he held a sun dance, the great summer ceremony where people fast and dance for days around a sacred tree to pray for everyone. the story his relatives told is that he had fifty small pieces of skin cut from each arm as an offering, then danced until he passed out. when he came to, he said he'd seen soldiers falling into the camp upside down, like grasshoppers, and heard a voice say they were being given to the people.\non june 25, 1876, george custer and the seventh cavalry attacked the big camp on the little bighorn river. custer and every man with him died. sitting bull was in his forties and didn't lead the charge. he was the holy man, and people said the vision had come true. the army's answer was a war that didn't stop, and in may 1877 he led his people north across the border into canada, grandmother's land, the lakota called it, after queen victoria. the buffalo ran out there too. on july 19, 1881, at fort buford, an army post in dakota territory, he surrendered, and had his young son crow foot hand over his rifle.\nthe army held him as a prisoner of war at fort randall, on the missouri, for about twenty months, then sent him to the standing rock reservation. in 1885 he toured for four months with buffalo bill's wild west, the traveling show of cowboys and real indians, for about fifty dollars a week, and sold signed photographs of himself. in 1890 the ghost dance, a new religion from nevada promising the dead would come back, reached standing rock. the government agent there, james mclaughlin, had hated him for years and was sure he'd lead an uprising. before dawn on december 15, 1890, lakota police came to arrest him at his cabin. a crowd gathered, somebody fired, and lieutenant bull head shot him in the chest and sergeant red tomahawk shot him in the head. two weeks later came wounded knee.","struggle":"canada was four years of watching his people get hungrier. the buffalo herds that had fed the lakota for generations were being shot out by white hunters for their hides, and there was nothing left to follow. the canadian government wanted him gone and wouldn't give him land. by 1881 families were slipping back south to surrender one after another, and he went last, with the ones who were left.\nthen twenty months as a prisoner, and then a reservation where an agent's job was to make his kind of leader stop existing. he was a man whose whole life had been deciding things for his people, and now a clerk decided when they ate.","threads":["war","exile","fame","the land"],"years":"around 1831 to 1890"},{"after":"after the curse he didn't write back. no answer to the synagogue survives, no attack on the people who threw him out. he went to work at the grinding wheel, kept his friends, answered letters from all over europe, and spent years on a book that ends in joy, what he called the intellectual love of god, the happiness of seeing how everything fits.\nthe story goes that when his father died his sister fought him over the inheritance, and he took her to court and won, and then gave her all of it except one bed. he died in a rented room in the hague on a sunday afternoon while his landlord's family was at church. he'd already arranged for his desk, with the ethics in it, to be sent to his publisher in amsterdam.","basically":"a jewish kid in 1600s amsterdam who got thrown out of his community at twenty three with a curse so thorough nobody was allowed to talk to him or stand within six feet of him, and who spent the rest of his short life alone in rented rooms grinding lenses and working out that god and nature are the same thing. einstein said spinoza's god was the only one he believed in. picture someone cut off from his whole family and world before he's twenty four who never publicly hits back, and ends his big book on joy. he's for anyone who got kicked out for thinking and kept thinking.","born":1632,"died":1677,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='290' y='50' width='70' height='70' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 325 50 L 325 120 M 290 85 L 360 85'/><circle cx='343' cy='67' r='9' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='170' y='190' width='130' height='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 180 200 L 180 250 M 290 200 L 290 250'/><circle cx='220' cy='172' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='220' cy='172' r='3' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 220 188 L 220 190'/><ellipse cx='262' cy='184' rx='10' ry='6' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 310 120 L 264 178' stroke-dasharray='2 6' opacity='.6'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.4'><circle cx='204' cy='160' r='1.2'/><circle cx='198' cy='150' r='1.2'/><circle cx='210' cy='146' r='1.2'/><circle cx='194' cy='164' r='1.2'/><\/g><path d='M 117 150 C 112 170 114 182 120 186 M 143 150 C 148 170 146 182 140 186'/><circle cx='130' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 130 168 L 130 216'/><path d='M 130 216 L 116 250 M 130 216 L 144 250'/><path d='M 130 182 L 116 206 M 130 182 L 156 176'/><g transform='translate(222,233.5) scale(1.1)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his own synagogue cursed him at twenty three and told everyone to stay four cubits away, and he went off and ground lenses.","key":"spinoza","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baruch_Spinoza","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/","why":"a longer, serious guide to his ideas"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3800","why":"the ethics on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of. his enemies called him an atheist for a hundred years, and he was careful and cagey about what he published, which is what you do when books get banned. his theological political treatise, which argued for freedom of thought and read the bible as a book written by people, came out anonymously in 1670 and was banned in 1674.","moments":["leibniz came to see him in the hague in november 1676, a few months before he died, and they talked more than once. afterward leibniz played it down.","in 1929 a rabbi in new york cabled einstein to ask whether he believed in god. einstein cabled back that he believed in spinoza's god, who shows himself in the harmony of what exists, and not in a god who concerns himself with what people do.","the story goes that around the time of the herem someone came at him with a knife, and he kept the torn coat afterward as a reminder."],"name":"baruch spinoza","place":"amsterdam, then rented rooms near leiden and the hague","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><circle cx='98' cy='110' r='9' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 93 106 C 95 103 98 102 101 102' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have taken care not to mock human actions, or mourn them, or hate them, only to understand them.","where":"his political treatise, an unfinished book on government, chapter 1, paraphrased"},{"q":"a free man thinks of nothing less than of death, and his wisdom is a meditation on life.","where":"the ethics, his big book, part 4, proposition 67, paraphrased. he numbered everything like geometry"},{"q":"all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.","where":"the ethics, the last line of part 5"}],"start":"the ethics, the appendix to part 1, where he takes apart the idea of a god who acts like a person. then part 5. steven nadler's spinoza: a life, the standard biography, for the man.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in amsterdam in 1632 into a family of portuguese jews who had been forced to live as christians back in portugal, where the inquisition, the church's heresy court, hunted down converts who kept jewish ways in secret. he went to the community's schools, was very good at them, and started asking the questions he'd later put in print, like whether moses really wrote the books with his name on them, and whether god is the kind of being who makes choices at all.\non july 27, 1656, the community put him under herem, the hebrew word for a ban, the jewish version of excommunication, being cut off completely. it was one of the harshest curses the community ever issued, read out in the synagogue. cursed by day and cursed by night, lying down and rising up. no one was to speak with him, write to him, stay under a roof with him, come within four cubits of him, or read anything he wrote. a cubit is about a forearm, so four is about six feet, the same distance we all learned in 2020. he was twenty three. the document says abominable heresies and doesn't say which.\nhe left, went by benedictus, the latin form of his name, both meaning blessed, and made his living grinding glass lenses for microscopes and telescopes, first in rijnsburg near leiden, then outside the hague, then in it. he was good at it. in 1673 he turned down a professorship at heidelberg, the big university in germany, because he wanted to stay free to think.\nthe ethics took him years and he didn't dare publish it. it's laid out like a geometry textbook, definitions and axioms and proofs, and it says there's one substance, one basic stuff that everything is, which you can call god or nature, deus sive natura in latin, and everything is in it. no god outside the world making choices. it came out after he died in 1677, of a lung disease, at forty four, probably made worse by years of breathing glass dust.","struggle":"he opens an early unfinished book, on the improvement of the understanding, by saying experience had taught him that the things people usually chase, money and honor and pleasure, were empty, and that he went looking for something that would give lasting joy. he says he felt like a sick man with a deadly illness looking for a remedy, however uncertain.\nthen being alone. cut off from his family and his people at twenty three, he lived in rented rooms for the rest of his life. in 1672 a mob in the hague tore apart the de witt brothers, johan and cornelis. johan had run the dutch republic for nearly twenty years, and spinoza admired him. the story goes, from leibniz, the german philosopher and mathematician, that he wrote a sign calling them the lowest of barbarians and meant to post it at the scene, and his landlord locked him in the house to keep him from getting killed.","threads":["exile","joy","work"],"years":"1632 to 1677"},{"after":"after carlisle he spent the rest of his life slowly taking it back. he'd been the model student, and in land of the spotted eagle he said the schools had it backwards, that native children should be taught by their own people, their own language and their own ways, and that white america had plenty to learn from them.\nafter the wreck he went home, tried being a chief, and found reservation politics under a government agent weren't a chief's life anymore. so he left for los angeles and made a living playing the thing he was, badly written by others, while he pushed to change who did the writing. then he wrote it himself.","basically":"an oglala lakota kid who got on a train in 1879 to go to carlisle, the first big government boarding school for native children, where they cut his hair and had him pick a new name off a list on the wall. he came out the model student, toured with buffalo bill's wild west, got smashed up in a train wreck, became a chief, quit, and went to hollywood to play indians in the movies and fight to get real ones hired. then in his sixties he wrote the books that said, calmly, that his people had been right about the land all along. think of someone who went through the program designed to erase him and came out with the clearest account of what it tried to take.","born":1868,"died":1939,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 300 250 L 320 176 L 340 250 M 320 176 L 320 250'/><rect x='296' y='146' width='50' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='308' cy='136' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='334' cy='136' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 162 L 280 158 L 280 170 L 296 166'/><circle cx='160' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 160 164 L 160 212'/><path d='M 160 212 L 146 246 M 160 212 L 174 246'/><path d='M 160 178 L 174 202 M 160 178 L 134 172'/><ellipse cx='139' cy='140' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(285 139 140)'/><ellipse cx='143' cy='133' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(308 143 133)'/><ellipse cx='149' cy='127' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(331 149 127)'/><ellipse cx='160' cy='124' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(359 160 124)'/><ellipse cx='170' cy='127' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(388 170 127)'/><ellipse cx='177' cy='132' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(411 177 132)'/><ellipse cx='181' cy='140' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(434 181 140)'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 216 L 100 216 L 100 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 216 L 98 204 L 100 212' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g transform='translate(321,109.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was in the first class at the school built to kill the indian in him, and fifty years later he wrote that the white man was the one who'd made the land wild.","key":"standingbear","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luther_Standing_Bear","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlisle_Indian_Industrial_School","why":"the school he was in the first class of"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["on may 24, 1883, the day the brooklyn bridge opened in new york, a lakota kid who'd been on the plains four years earlier led the carlisle band across it.","he picked the name luther by pointing at squiggles on a wall he couldn't read. he kept it his whole life."],"name":"luther standing bear","place":"the lakota country in dakota territory, carlisle in pennsylvania, the wild west show, then los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><ellipse cx='24' cy='43' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(290 24 43)'/><ellipse cx='30' cy='33' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(308 30 33)'/><ellipse cx='38' cy='25' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(325 38 25)'/><ellipse cx='49' cy='20' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(342 49 20)'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='18' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(360 60 18)'/><ellipse cx='71' cy='20' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(378 71 20)'/><ellipse cx='82' cy='25' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(395 82 25)'/><ellipse cx='90' cy='33' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(412 90 33)'/><ellipse cx='96' cy='43' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(430 96 43)'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"only to the white man was nature a wilderness.","where":"land of the spotted eagle, 1933, the introduction; the lakota, he says, never thought of the plains and hills as wild"},{"q":"the old lakota knew that a man's heart away from nature becomes hard.","where":"land of the spotted eagle, paraphrased"},{"q":"i went east to show my father i was brave, and i thought i was going to my death.","where":"my people the sioux, 1928, the chapters on going to carlisle, paraphrased"}],"start":"land of the spotted eagle, the introduction and the chapters on nature and on schooling. then my people the sioux for the carlisle years.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in december 1868 into the oglala lakota, and named ota kte, plenty kill. in october 1879, at about ten, he was one of the first children at the carlisle indian industrial school in pennsylvania, run by an army officer named richard henry pratt whose motto was kill the indian, save the man. the school meant to make native kids into white americans, no language, no hair, no ceremonies. he went, he said later, to show his father he was brave, sure he'd never come back.\nat carlisle they cut his hair, put him in a uniform, and told him to choose a name from a list of english names written on the wall. he pointed at one without being able to read it. luther. he became pratt's model student, and in may 1883 he marched with the carlisle band across the brooklyn bridge on the day it opened.\nback on the reservation he taught school and clerked. in 1902 and 1903 he toured with buffalo bill's wild west, the traveling show of cowboys and real indians, including in england. in april 1903 the show's train wrecked outside chicago, three native performers died, and he was broken nearly everywhere a person can be, hips, leg, arm, ribs, collarbone, nose. in 1905 the oglala chose him as a chief, and he left that same year.\nin 1912 he moved to california and went into the movies, playing indians in westerns, and pushing hollywood to hire native actors for native parts instead of white men in makeup. in his sixties he wrote my people the sioux, in 1928, and land of the spotted eagle, in 1933. he died of the flu on february 20, 1939, while working on a movie called union pacific.","struggle":"carlisle took his language, his hair, and his name in the first week, and he was ten. more than a hundred and eighty children who went there are buried in the school cemetery. he was one of the kids who lived, and he did it by becoming exactly what they wanted, for years.\nthen the wreck in 1903. he was in his thirties and a performer, and his body was wrecked in a single night. he spent months healing and never toured again.","threads":["exile","family","the land","fame"],"years":"1868 to 1939"},{"after":"what he did after japan, as far as anyone can tell, was start over. he went to a country that had been at war with his fifteen years earlier, at an age when most priests are settling in, and sat down every morning with whoever came in the door.\nand he died the way he'd taught. he told his students that if he suffered at the end, that was all right, that would be a suffering buddha, buddha being just the word for someone who's woken up, and there'd be nothing wrong in it. thin and sick, he got through the ceremony handing over the seat, and two weeks later he was gone. he was sixty seven.","basically":"a small, cheerful japanese priest who came to san francisco at fifty five to do funerals and weddings for japanese american families, and ended up teaching a generation of hippies how to sit still. a lot of american zen runs back through him, and so does the phrase beginner's mind, which you've probably seen on a slide in some office mindfulness session. take from him that you never have to become an expert at your own life. you get to start fresh every morning.","born":1904,"died":1971,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 300 44 A 40 40 0 1 0 322 52' stroke-width='7' opacity='.8'/><ellipse cx='80' cy='248' rx='26' ry='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='80' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 80 190 L 80 232'/><path d='M 80 232 C 60 232 50 248 66 248 L 94 248 C 110 248 100 232 80 232'/><path d='M 80 206 L 66 232 M 80 206 L 94 232'/><path d='M 74 174 C 76 176 78 176 80 174 M 82 174 C 84 176 86 176 88 174'/><ellipse cx='180' cy='248' rx='24' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='180' cy='180' r='12'/><path d='M 180 194 L 180 236'/><path d='M 180 236 C 160 236 150 252 166 252 L 194 252 C 210 252 200 236 180 236'/><path d='M 180 210 L 166 236 M 180 210 L 194 236'/><path d='M 170 172 C 164 184 166 196 170 200 M 190 172 C 196 184 194 196 190 200' opacity='.7'/><ellipse cx='250' cy='248' rx='24' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='250' cy='182' r='12'/><path d='M 250 196 L 250 238'/><path d='M 250 238 C 230 238 220 254 236 254 L 264 254 C 280 254 270 238 250 238'/><path d='M 250 212 L 236 238 M 250 212 L 264 238'/><ellipse cx='320' cy='248' rx='24' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='320' cy='180' r='12'/><path d='M 320 194 L 320 236'/><path d='M 320 236 C 300 236 290 252 306 252 L 334 252 C 350 252 340 236 320 236'/><path d='M 320 210 L 306 236 M 320 210 L 334 236'/><ellipse cx='372' cy='248' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(372,221.1) scale(0.85)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a small japanese priest came to san francisco to do funerals for a congregation, and the hippies started showing up at dawn to sit.","key":"suzuki","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shunry%C5%AB_Suzuki","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the zen center","url":"https://www.sfzc.org/","why":"san francisco zen center, the community his students started"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen_Mind,_Beginner%27s_Mind","why":"his one book, where to start"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of. in 1983, twelve years after he died, his successor richard baker was pushed out of the zen center over an affair with a friend's wife and how he'd been running the place. that was baker's mess, not suzuki's.","moments":["trudy dixon, the student who did most of the editing on zen mind, beginner's mind, died of cancer in 1969, before the book came out. she was thirty. her introduction is still in it.","at tassajara he'd spend afternoons moving big stones around for the garden, a little man in robes with a pry bar, students hauling on the other end.","his teacher's nickname for him became the title of the biography, crooked cucumber, by his student david chadwick, which is the best way into the man."],"name":"shunryu suzuki","place":"japan, then san francisco and a canyon in the mountains inland from big sur","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 48 98 L 60 114 L 72 98' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='49' y='114' width='22' height='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"in the beginner's mind there are many possibilities, but in the expert's there are few.","where":"zen mind, beginner's mind, his one book, the prologue"},{"q":"when you do something, you should burn yourself completely, like a good bonfire, leaving no trace of yourself.","where":"zen mind, beginner's mind, the talk called no trace"},{"q":"each of you is perfect the way you are, and you can use a little improvement.","where":"attributed; his students tell it, it isn't in the book"}],"start":"zen mind, beginner's mind, the prologue and then any talk that grabs you. crooked cucumber, david chadwick, for the man.","status":"approved","story":"he was the son of a zen priest and became one himself as a boy. zen is the japanese branch of buddhism that cares less about scripture and more about sitting still and seeing straight. his family's school was soto, the quieter of zen's two big schools, the one that says just sit, no riddles to solve, no goal. he spent the first half of his life running country temples in japan. his teacher called him crooked cucumber, a cucumber too bent to be any use, and he never argued.\nin 1959, at fifty five, he came to san francisco to look after sokoji, a japanese american soto temple in japantown. the job was funerals, weddings, and a congregation of families. then a few americans turned up asking about zazen, which is soto meditation, sitting facing a wall with your back straight and your eyes half open, doing nothing on purpose. he told them he sat every morning and they could sit with him. more came.\nthe students became san francisco zen center in 1962. in 1967 they bought tassajara, an old hot springs resort at the end of a dirt road in the mountains inland from big sur, and made it the first zen monastery outside asia. he was a small, quiet man who laughed a lot and moved rocks for the gardens with his students.\nzen mind, beginner's mind came out in 1970, talks his students taped and edited, and it's still the book people start with. in 1971 he had cancer. in late november he handed the zen center over to his american student richard baker in a ceremony, and he died on december 4. he's no relation to d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who spent decades writing the books that first explained zen to westerners. people mix them up all the time.","struggle":"in 1952, back in japan, his wife chie was killed at his temple by a mentally ill man who'd been staying there. suzuki was away. he'd let the man stay. that's the kind of thing a person carries, and he didn't talk about it much.\nin san francisco he had two congregations under one roof and they didn't get along. the japanese american board at sokoji wanted a priest for their families, and got a man whose mornings belonged to a room full of long haired americans. he tried to serve both for ten years. in 1969 he left sokoji and moved in with the students.","threads":["grief","dying"],"years":"1904 to 1971"},{"after":"after the statue she didn't become serene. she became busy. convent after convent over twenty years, with no money and bad health, arguing with men who could have had her locked up, and writing books in between letters about leaky roofs and debts.\nshe wrote the interior castle in a few months in 1577, in the middle of the worst fights over the reform, the same year her friend and fellow reformer [[johncross|john of the cross]] was kidnapped by his own brothers. the book says you get to god by going in, one room at a time, and that humility is the floor of every room. she wrote it while the building around her was on fire.","basically":"a spanish nun in the 1500s, funny, chatty, and vain as a girl, who coasted through convent life for almost twenty years, then broke down in front of a statue at thirty nine and turned into a force of nature. she started seventeen convents on terrible roads from the back of a covered cart, fought bishops and her own order, had her book seized by the church's heresy court, and wrote a book about prayer that's still one of the best maps of the inside of a person. think of someone who starts a chain of tiny shelters with no money, bad health, and every official in the country against her, and wins. she's for anyone who figures they already missed their chance.","born":1515,"died":1582,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 C 80 244 140 256 200 250 S 320 244 380 250'/><path d='M 50 252 C 60 240 140 240 150 252 C 130 262 70 262 50 252 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 82 176 C 76 116 124 116 118 176 L 112 176 C 116 132 84 132 88 176 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='100' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 100 164 L 100 212'/><path d='M 100 212 L 86 246 M 100 212 L 114 246'/><path d='M 100 178 L 80 154 M 100 178 L 120 154'/><path d='M 80 70 Q 80 52 100 54 Q 110 36 130 48 Q 147 40 151 58 Q 160 68 147 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='200' cy='205' rx='24' ry='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 186 216 L 184 244 M 196 218 L 196 246 M 206 218 L 206 246 M 216 216 L 218 244 M 180 200 L 170 188 M 224 202 L 232 214 M 224 206 L 244 212'/><ellipse cx='164' cy='186' rx='9' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 166 180 L 164 166 M 170 181 L 172 168'/><g transform='rotate(-12 290 220)'><path d='M 240 222 L 340 222 L 340 190 L 240 190 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 190 C 240 150 340 150 340 190' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='262' cy='232' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='318' cy='232' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(281,139.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she was a lukewarm nun for almost twenty years, woke up at thirty nine, and then founded convents all over spain from the back of a cart.","key":"teresaavila","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teresa_of_%C3%81vila","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior_Castle","why":"her book on prayer"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of. she was vain as a girl, loved romances and perfume and her own looks, and she tells you so. as a founder she could be ruthless in a negotiation, and got her way with bishops by being charming and not letting go.","moments":["in 1576 a friar painted her portrait, the only one done from life. she looked at it and said, the story goes, god forgive you, brother juan, for painting me ugly and bleary eyed.","she met [[johncross|john of the cross]] in 1567, when he was twenty five and about to leave for the carthusians, the strictest monks around, and talked him into her reform instead.","the river story. crossing a flooded stream, her cart tipped and dumped her in the mud, and she heard god say this is how he treats his friends. she said that's why he has so few. it's attributed, not documented, and it's the most teresa thing she may never have said."],"name":"teresa of avila","place":"ávila, and every bad road in castile","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 34 70 C 36 84 44 92 60 92 C 76 92 84 84 86 70 L 88 108 L 32 108 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 96 128 C 98 112 104 98 114 88 C 112 102 106 116 98 126' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"god walks among the pots and pans.","where":"the book of her foundations, her account of starting the convents, chapter 5, paraphrased"},{"q":"let nothing disturb you, let nothing frighten you, all things pass, god does not change.","where":"a short poem found in her breviary, the prayer book nuns carried, after she died"},{"q":"if this is how you treat your friends, no wonder you have so few.","where":"attributed; the story is she said it to god after her cart dumped her in a river"}],"start":"the book of her life, her autobiography, chapters 8 and 9, the lukewarm years and the statue. then the interior castle, the first dwelling places, which is what she calls the rooms.","status":"approved","story":"teresa de ahumada was born in avila, a walled city in castile, in 1515. her grandfather was a converso, a jew whose family had converted to christianity, and the spanish inquisition, the church's court for hunting down heresy, including people it suspected of secretly still being jewish, made him do public penance in toledo. the family worked hard afterward to pass as old christians, spaniards with no jewish blood. at seven she and her brother ran away from home to get martyred, killed for their faith, by the moors, the muslims of north africa, so they could go straight to heaven. an uncle caught them on the road.\nat twenty she entered the convent of the incarnation in avila, a house of carmelite nuns, an order that traced itself back to hermits on mount carmel in the holy land. it was big, comfortable, and chatty, with visitors in the parlor, the visiting room, all day, and she liked the chatting. then she got so sick she lay in a coma for days and they dug her grave. she was paralyzed for about three years after.\nfor almost twenty years she was, by her own account, a middling nun. in 1554, at about thirty nine, she walked past a statue of christ covered in wounds and broke down in front of it. she counted her real life from there.\nin 1562 she founded a small, poor, strict convent, st joseph's in avila, where the nuns went in rough sandals instead of shoes, discalced, the old word for shoeless, as a sign they were poor. the town fought it. then she founded more, seventeen in all, traveling in covered carts on terrible roads, sick most of the time, fighting bishops and her own order. she wrote the interior castle in 1577, a book about the soul as a castle with god in the innermost room. she died in 1582 at alba de tormes, on the road.","struggle":"the twenty years were the struggle. she describes being pulled between god and the parlor, wanting both, doing neither well. she gave up mental prayer, which means quiet prayer in your own words, just talking to god as a friend, for about a year because she thought she was too bad to pray, and she calls it one of the worst things she ever did.\nthen, once the visions came, nobody believed her. her confessors, the priests who heard her sins and guided her, told her it was the devil. one told her to make an obscene gesture at the visions to drive them off, the fig, a rude hand sign about like giving the finger, and she did, and apologized to christ while she did it. the inquisition took her autobiography and kept it for years.","threads":["work","wandering"],"years":"1515 to 1582"},{"after":"he built plum village out of a farm in southwest france in 1982, with a handful of vietnamese refugees, and turned exile into a home that took in everybody. tens of thousands of people came through. he taught them to wash the dishes.\nwhen his own country finally let him back in 2005, he went, taught, and left again. in 2018, after the stroke, he went home to the temple in hue where he'd been a teenage novice and stayed until he died. nobody had to explain the ending to him.","basically":"a vietnamese buddhist monk who spent the vietnam war telling both sides to stop, got locked out of his own country for it, and spent the next forty years in france teaching people to wash the dishes like it mattered. a lot of what's in the meditation app on your phone traces back to him, usually with the hard parts sanded off. if you've ever tried to be present and felt like it was one more thing to fail at, he's the one who makes it small enough to actually do.","born":1926,"died":2022,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='220' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 220 154 L 220 200'/><path d='M 220 200 L 204 236 M 220 200 L 238 232'/><path d='M 220 168 L 206 190 M 220 168 L 240 176'/><path d='M 215 145 Q 220 149 225 145'/><g fill='var(--peach)'><circle cx='150' cy='250' r='5'/><circle cx='120' cy='250' r='5'/><circle cx='90' cy='250' r='5'/><circle cx='60' cy='250' r='5'/><\/g><path d='M 150 245 L 150 238 M 120 245 L 120 238 M 90 245 L 90 238 M 60 245 L 60 238'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 205 L 370 205 L 370 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='335' cy='205' rx='35' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='335' cy='200' rx='16' ry='4' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 330 190 C 328 180 334 176 332 168 M 340 190 C 342 178 336 174 340 164' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(378,178.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he got banned from his own country for saying the war should stop, and spent forty years teaching people to wash dishes.","key":"thay","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thich_Nhat_Hanh","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his community","url":"https://plumvillage.org/","why":"plum village, with his talks, guided meditations, and retreats"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["he wrote a poem in 1978 called please call me by my true names, after hearing about a twelve year old girl on one of those refugee boats who'd been raped by a pirate and drowned herself. in the poem he is the girl and he is the pirate, because if he'd been born in the pirate's village he might have been the pirate. that's the whole of his teaching in one poem.","martin luther king called him an apostle of peace and nonviolence in the nobel nomination letter. they'd met once.","at plum village a bell rings every so often and everybody stops, mid-sentence, mid-step, for three breaths. visitors find it annoying for about a day."],"name":"thich nhat hanh","place":"hue, vietnam, then exile, then home","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 30 52 C 32 28 88 28 90 52 C 80 44 40 44 30 52 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 30 52 L 90 52' /><path d='M 40 44 L 40 34 M 50 40 L 50 30 M 60 38 L 60 28 M 70 40 L 70 30 M 80 44 L 80 34' opacity='.35'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"wash the dishes to wash the dishes. not to get them done.","where":"the miracle of mindfulness, his small 1975 book on paying attention, paraphrased"},{"q":"there's no way to peace. peace is the way.","where":"a line he used often. a.j. muste, an american pacifist minister, said it first and he knew that"},{"q":"people say walking on water is a miracle. walking on the earth is the miracle.","where":"the miracle of mindfulness, paraphrased"}],"start":"the miracle of mindfulness. then peace is every step, which is even shorter. any of his short talks on breathing will do more than a long book.","status":"approved","story":"a vietnamese zen monk. zen is the branch of buddhism built around sitting meditation and paying close attention to right now. he became a novice monk at sixteen. in the sixties, with the war tearing his country apart, the communist north against the american-backed south, he refused to take a side and organized monks and nuns to rebuild bombed villages instead. he called it engaged buddhism, buddhism that gets its hands dirty out in the world instead of staying in the temple. both sides hated him for it.\nin 1966 he came to america to ask for peace. he met [[merton|thomas merton]], the most famous monk in america, who wrote an essay calling him his brother. he met [[king|martin luther king]], who nominated him for the nobel peace prize the next year and then came out against the war himself, partly because of him. vietnam responded by refusing to let him come home. that lasted almost forty years.\nhe settled in france and built plum village, a monastery and retreat center on old farmland in the southwest. his books are short, plain, and mostly about breathing, walking, and doing the dishes with your whole attention. the miracle of mindfulness, from 1975, is where half the mindfulness industry started, the apps and the corporate workshops, and he'd be the first to say the industry missed the point.\na stroke in 2014 took his speech. in 2018 he went home to the temple in hue, the old imperial city in central vietnam, where he'd started as a teenager, and died there in january 2022, at ninety five.","struggle":"exile was the struggle. in his journal from the early sixties, published as fragrant palm leaves, he writes about a loneliness so total he'd sit and watch a single leaf for hours because it was the only thing that made sense.\nthe young workers he'd trained to rebuild bombed villages kept getting killed, by one side or the other. he had to write letters to their families from another country.\nin 1976 he chartered a ship to pull boat people out of the south china sea. boat people were the vietnamese who fled after the war in small overcrowded boats, and huge numbers of them drowned or were attacked by pirates. singapore expelled him for the rescue and it collapsed. he called it one of the hardest failures of his life.","threads":["war","exile","nonviolence"],"years":"1926 to 2022"},{"after":"after the fire, he became the man in concord who knew the woods better than anyone alive. he spent the rest of his life surveying them, measuring them, and writing down which plant flowered on which day, year after year, pages and pages of it. scientists use his records now to track how the climate's changed.\nafter john, he wrote the book, even if it flopped. and after walden he didn't stay a hermit. he said he left for as good a reason as he went, that he had several more lives to live. he went back to the house in town and lived there until he died, looking after the pencil business and his mother.","basically":"a stubborn new england oddball who built a tiny house by a pond, lived in it for two years to find out what he actually needed, and wrote the book everybody who's ever fantasized about quitting and moving to the woods is quoting, whether they know it or not. he also spent a night in jail rather than pay a tax that went to a government backing slavery, and the essay he wrote about it got read by gandhi and martin luther king. take from him that you find out what you need by doing without the rest for a while.","born":1817,"died":1862,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 240 L 220 240'/><path d='M 60 240 L 60 170 L 150 170 L 150 240' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 172 L 105 134 L 160 172'/><path d='M 130 150 L 130 128 L 142 128 L 142 158'/><path d='M 94 240 L 94 196 L 118 196 L 118 240' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='106' cy='196' r='9'/><path d='M 106 210 L 106 252'/><path d='M 106 252 C 86 252 76 268 92 268 L 120 268 C 136 268 126 252 106 252'/><path d='M 106 226 L 92 252 M 106 226 L 120 252'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='250' rx='90' ry='24' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 290 246 C 296 238 310 238 314 246 C 308 250 296 250 290 246 Z M 314 246 L 322 240' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 20 270 C 60 262 100 276 150 268' stroke-dasharray='3 8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 30 262 L 48 262 L 46 272 L 32 272 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='176' cy='234' r='6'/><circle cx='190' cy='234' r='6'/><circle cx='183' cy='222' r='6'/><\/g><path d='M 360 200 L 360 105'/><ellipse cx='360' cy='96' rx='25' ry='19' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(183,199.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he spent two years in a cabin by a pond reading the gita, and one night in jail that changed the twentieth century.","key":"thoreau","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_David_Thoreau","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the books, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/205","why":"walden and civil disobedience at project gutenberg"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walden_Pond","why":"the pond itself, a state park now"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"in april 1844 he and a friend were cooking fish in the woods near concord in a dry spring, and the fire got away from them. it burned about three hundred acres. for years people in town called him a woods burner. he wrote about it in his journal six years later, and admitted that while it burned he'd gone up a hill and watched it.\nthe story that his mother did his laundry while he was playing hermit gets told as a gotcha. it's partly true. walden was a mile and a half from town, he walked in every day or two, and he ate at his family's table and emerson's. he never claimed otherwise. the book says so, if you read it.","moments":["in 1859 he stood up in the concord town hall and defended john brown, the abolitionist who'd led an armed raid on the federal armory at harpers ferry, virginia, to start a slave uprising, and was about to be hanged for it. almost nobody in the north would say his name. the town officials wouldn't ring the bell for the meeting, so he rang it himself.","near the end, a friend tried to get him talking about the next world, and the story goes he said, one world at a time."],"name":"henry david thoreau","place":"concord, massachusetts, and a cabin a mile and a half out of town","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i went to the woods because i wished to live deliberately.","where":"walden, the chapter where i lived, and what i lived for"},{"q":"in the morning i bathe my intellect in the stupendous and cosmogonal philosophy of the bhagvat geeta.","where":"walden, the chapter the pond in winter"},{"q":"i did not know we had ever quarreled.","where":"attributed; said on his deathbed to an aunt who asked if he'd made his peace with god"}],"start":"walden, the chapters where i lived, and what i lived for, and the pond in winter. then civil disobedience, which is short.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in concord, massachusetts, in 1817, went to harvard, and came home. he taught school, quit after two weeks rather than whip the children, worked in the family pencil business and made better pencils, surveyed land, and walked. every day, for hours. ralph waldo emerson lived down the road and took him in as a sort of younger brother. emerson was the most famous writer in america, the center of a circle called the transcendentalists, who believed god shows up in nature and in your own gut more than in any church.\non july 4, 1845, he moved into a one room cabin he'd built himself on emerson's land at walden pond. he stayed two years, two months, and two days. he planted beans, read, wrote, watched ice, and took notes on everything. walden came out of it in 1854. he said he went to the woods to live deliberately.\nhe read the [[krishna|bhagavad gita]] out there, the hindu scripture where the god krishna talks a prince through his terror on the morning of a battle, and said so. in the morning, he wrote, he bathed his intellect in its stupendous and cosmogonal philosophy, cosmogonal meaning about how the universe got made. then he went down to the pond for water and found men cutting the ice into blocks to ship south, and thought the pure walden water was mingling with the sacred water of the ganges, india's holy river.\nin july 1846 the tax collector stopped him on his way to the cobbler and asked for six years of back poll tax, a flat tax every man paid just for being there. he refused, because the government backed slavery and was fighting a war with mexico that he and plenty of others saw as a land grab to spread slavery. he spent a night in the concord jail until somebody, probably his aunt, paid it. he turned the night into an essay, resistance to civil government, in 1849. it's usually called civil disobedience now. [[gandhi]] read it. he died of tuberculosis in 1862, at forty four.","struggle":"in january 1842 his older brother john cut his finger shaving, got lockjaw, which is tetanus, and died in henry's arms eleven days later. henry then came down with the symptoms of lockjaw himself, with no wound at all, and was sick for weeks. he could barely say john's name for years afterward.\nhis first book, a week on the concord and merrimack rivers, was about a boat trip he'd taken with john. it came out in 1849 and flopped. he'd paid for the printing. in 1853 the publisher sent back the unsold copies and he wrote in his journal that he now had a library of nearly nine hundred volumes, over seven hundred of which he'd written himself.","threads":["the land","prison","grief"],"years":"1817 to 1862"},{"after":"he did the things a rich man could do. he set up soup kitchens during the famine of 1891 and fed thousands. he gave up the copyright on everything he wrote after 1881 so anyone could print it. he made his own boots, badly. he used the money from resurrection, his last big novel, to ship a whole persecuted sect to canada, the doukhobors, peasant christians the government was punishing for refusing to serve in the army.\nand at eighty two, in the night, he finally walked out to go live as he'd preached. he got as far as a railway station and died there in the stationmaster's bed with reporters outside. it's not a clean ending. it's an honest one.","basically":"a russian count, an aristocrat with a big country estate and peasants working his land, who wrote two of the greatest novels ever written and then fell into a depression so deep he hid the rope from himself. what got him out was reading jesus literally, especially the part about never meeting violence with violence, and that one idea went from him to gandhi to martin luther king. picture the most famous writer alive walking away from his own career at fifty to find out what any of it was for. he's for anyone who knows exactly how they should live and keeps not doing it.","born":1828,"died":1910,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 L 20 150 L 140 150 L 140 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 10 152 L 80 105 L 150 152'/><path d='M 40 180 L 60 180 L 60 200 L 40 200 Z M 100 180 L 120 180 L 120 200 L 100 200 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='240' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 240 164 L 240 210'/><path d='M 240 210 L 224 246 M 240 210 L 258 242'/><path d='M 240 178 L 226 200 M 240 178 L 260 186'/><path d='M 260 164 L 300 130'/><path d='M 292 122 C 300 112 316 118 312 130 C 316 138 298 140 292 132 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='340' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='332' cy='55' r='15' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 330 250 L 330 226 L 390 226 L 390 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='345' cy='250' r='6'/><circle cx='375' cy='250' r='6'/><g transform='translate(60,123.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote war and peace, decided none of it mattered, and spent thirty years trying to live like a peasant with a house full of servants.","key":"tolstoy","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_Tolstoy","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/43302","why":"the kingdom of god is within you, the book gandhi read, public domain"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he was hell to be married to. he made his wife sophia read his diaries of his sexual past the week before their wedding, then spent decades fighting her over money, the children, and his desire to give everything away. she bore thirteen children and copied war and peace out by hand seven times. the mess is real and it's cruelty to his wife, told straight. it's not the kind of thing, abuse of someone in his care, that gets a person cut from these pages.","moments":["in 1901 the russian orthodox church, the country's official church, excommunicated him. crowds turned out to cheer him in the street the next day.","he ran a school for peasant children on his estate in the 1860s with no grades, no punishment, and kids who could leave whenever they wanted. it was a hundred years early.","in his last year he put out a little book of sayings by lao tzu, the ancient chinese sage behind the tao te ching, for russian readers. the count had stopped caring which country god came from."],"name":"leo tolstoy","place":"yasnaya polyana, russia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"everyone thinks of changing the world. no one thinks of changing himself.","where":"a line attributed to him everywhere. the idea is all through his late diaries"},{"q":"the kingdom of god is within you. it's not in a building.","where":"the kingdom of god is within you, 1894, paraphrased from luke 17:21, where jesus says it"},{"q":"if you want to be happy, be.","where":"attributed to him in a hundred places, likely a compression of his diaries"}],"start":"a confession, which is short. then the kingdom of god is within you. skip the novels unless you have a winter.","status":"approved","story":"count leo tolstoy had written the two biggest novels in the russian language by fifty. war and peace follows a handful of families through napoleon's invasion of russia. anna karenina is about a married woman's affair, and it ends with her under a train. and he was so depressed he hid the rope so he wouldn't hang himself. a confession, from around 1882, is him trying to explain why a man with everything wanted out.\nwhat pulled him back was the gospels, the four books in the bible that tell [[jesus]]'s life, stripped of everything the church had added. he wrote his own version that stitched the four into one, cut out the miracles, and kept the sermon on the mount, the part where jesus says don't resist evil with violence and love your enemies. he decided that was the whole thing and everything else was furniture.\nthe kingdom of god is within you, from 1894, is that argument at book length. jesus meant it, so no armies and no violence from anybody, for any reason, ever. the russian orthodox church, the country's official church, excommunicated him in 1901, which means it formally threw him out. a young indian lawyer in south africa read the book and said it overwhelmed him. they wrote to each other the last year of tolstoy's life, and the lawyer, [[gandhi]], named his commune after him. tolstoy also put together a little book of sayings by [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the ancient chinese sage behind the tao te ching, and wrote about [[buddha|the buddha]] with respect. he'd stopped caring which door the truth came through.\nat eighty two he finally walked out of his house in the night to go live as he preached, got as far as a little railway station at astapovo, and died there of pneumonia while reporters waited outside.","struggle":"in 1869, in a hotel room in arzamas, a small town east of moscow, he woke up in the night with a terror of death so complete that he described it forty years later like it had just happened. no reason, no illness. just you're going to die and none of this means anything.\nby fifty, with war and peace and anna karenina behind him, he was hiding the rope from himself and not going hunting alone with his gun. a confession is that. it's short and it's the most honest thing a famous man has ever written about wanting to quit.\nthen, having found his answer, he spent thirty years unable to live it. he preached poverty from a manor house. he preached chastity and kept getting his wife pregnant. he knew it, everyone knew it, and it ate him.","threads":["money","family","doubt"],"years":"1828 to 1910"},{"after":"after the overdose he turned his life around, married, and went to school, all the way to a doctorate. after the churches told him to drop the drum, he picked it back up and brought it into their buildings.\nhe didn't end the anger by forgetting it. he turned it into an argument, a long polite one, with the churches, and kept it up until he died. his last book, rescuing the gospel from the cowboys, was finished by others and came out in 2015.","basically":"a sicangu lakota activist who hated christianity for what it had done to his people, found jesus anyway during an overdose on a hawaiian beach in 1974, and then spent forty years insisting you could follow jesus without giving up the drum, the dance, and being lakota. the churches that had told native converts their ceremonies were the devil's didn't love hearing it. think of the guy who gets saved and comes back to church and tells them their missionaries owe his grandmother an apology. take from him that you don't have to pick between where you came from and what saved you.","born":1954,"died":2013,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 230 C 60 220 120 240 200 230 S 340 222 400 232 L 400 300 L 0 300 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 0 214 L 400 214' opacity='.3'/><path d='M 320 214 C 316 170 318 130 328 100'/><path d='M 328 100 C 310 88 290 92 282 104 M 328 100 C 344 84 366 88 374 100 M 328 100 C 322 80 330 66 340 62 M 328 100 C 340 96 356 110 360 124' stroke='var(--sage)' stroke-width='5'/><circle cx='170' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 170 164 L 170 198 L 150 214 M 170 198 L 190 214 M 170 176 L 150 146 M 170 176 L 190 146'/><ellipse cx='80' cy='200' rx='26' ry='8' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 54 200 L 54 212 C 60 220 100 220 106 212 L 106 200'/><g transform='translate(318,85.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a lakota kid who'd helped take over the bureau of indian affairs overdosed on a beach in maui, yelled at jesus, and spent the rest of his life telling churches they'd done it wrong.","key":"twiss","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Twiss","why":"the plain overview of his life"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["he'd been inside the occupation of the bureau of indian affairs in 1972. forty years later he died in the same city.","he led christian worship in powwow regalia, dancing, which some of the churches he spoke at thought was the devil. he kept doing it."],"name":"richard twiss","place":"the rosebud reservation in south dakota, then maui, then the portland area in the pacific northwest","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"jesus, if you are real, would you forgive me, would you come into my life?","where":"his account of the night on maui in 1974, in interviews"},{"q":"god loves you, but he doesn't like you. no more drumming, no more powwows, no more ceremonies.","where":"his summary of what the missionaries had told native people, in interviews"},{"q":"you don't have to stop being lakota to follow jesus.","where":"the message of one church many tribes and his last book, paraphrased"}],"start":"rescuing the gospel from the cowboys, his last book, finished after he died. then one church many tribes.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in 1954 on the rosebud reservation in south dakota, among his mother's people, the sicangu lakota. sicangu means burnt thighs, a name that goes back, the story goes, to a band running through a prairie fire.\nin 1972, at eighteen, he was part of the american indian movement's trail of broken treaties, a caravan to washington, d.c., that ended with activists occupying the headquarters of the bureau of indian affairs, the federal agency that runs a lot of native life. he came out of it hating white people, he said later, and hating their religion.\nby 1974 he was a beach bum on maui, doing drugs, partying, sleeping on the sand and living on food stamps, by his own description. he overdosed. he said that at the bottom of it he yelled at the top of his lungs, jesus, if you're real, would you forgive me and come into my life, and felt more peace than he ever had.\nin 1976 he married katherine kroshus, and in 1997 they started wiconi international, a native christian ministry, wiconi being the lakota word for life. he led worship with a powwow drum and danced in full regalia, and wrote one church many tribes, about native christians keeping their cultures. he finished a doctorate at asbury theological seminary in 2011. on february 6, 2013, in washington, d.c., he had a heart attack, and he died three days later with his family around him.","struggle":"the anger first. he'd grown up on a reservation that was one of the poorest places in america and learned the history, and the only thing that made sense to him at eighteen was fury. then the drugs, and a beach, and an overdose, at twenty.\nthen the churches. after he converted, a lot of the christians around him told him his drum and his dances and his lakota ways were pagan and had to go. he spent years believing them before he decided they were wrong.","threads":["addiction","anger","forgiveness"],"years":"1954 to 2013"},{"after":"the morning after his father died, with the house full of creditors, he didn't get religion. he got a temper and a job hunt and a year of doubt, and at the end of it he walked into the temple to ask for money and couldn't. he took that as the answer, and it shaped everything. when ramakrishna died two years later and the boys had nothing, he didn't scatter them. he kept them in the haunted house, and then he walked away from them for five years, which looked like abandoning them and turned out to be the plan. the one who'd asked for proof spent the rest of his life on the part of religion that doesn't need any, feeding people. his phrase for it was daridra narayana, god as the poor man.\nthe fame didn't fix him and it didn't ruin him either. he came home from chicago in 1897 a celebrity and used it to buy land on the ganges for a monastery and a mission whose stated work was hospitals, schools, and famine relief, and that's what it still does. then he went west again, came back sick, handed the work to the others, and spent his last months at belur teaching the young monks and feeding the animals. on the last day he meditated, walked, taught a sanskrit class, and died that evening. no redemption story needed. he'd already done the thing.","basically":"a sharp calcutta college kid who walked into a temple in 1881 and asked the priest, [[ramakrishna]], have you seen god. the priest said yes, more clearly than i see you, and the kid spent the next twenty years turning that yes into monks and hospitals and the first hindu teaching in america. think of a young lawyer with a philosophy degree who meets a barely literate saint, can't shake him, ends up in a borrowed orange turban in chicago in 1893 telling a hall full of americans that every religion is true, and gets a two minute standing ovation for calling them sisters and brothers. yoga in america, and most of the idea that the traditions are talking about one thing, runs through him.","born":1863,"died":1902,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 118 C 60 112 120 116 180 114 C 230 112 300 116 400 112'/><path d='M 330 112 L 330 92 L 340 80 L 350 92 L 350 112'/><circle cx='70' cy='62' r='18'/><path d='M 120 250 C 110 224 128 206 150 200 C 190 190 240 188 268 196 C 290 202 296 226 284 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 214 C 170 210 200 208 230 210'/><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 194'/><path d='M 200 194 C 184 194 170 200 168 204 L 232 204 C 230 200 216 194 200 194'/><path d='M 200 176 L 178 198 M 200 176 L 222 198'/><path d='M 0 236 C 20 228 40 244 60 236 S 100 228 118 236'/><path d='M 290 240 C 310 232 330 248 350 240 S 385 232 400 238'/><path d='M 0 270 C 24 262 48 278 72 270 S 120 262 144 270 S 192 278 216 270 S 264 262 288 270 S 336 278 360 270 S 390 264 400 268'/><path d='M 30 202 C 50 194 70 210 90 202'/><path d='M 320 196 C 340 188 360 204 380 196'/><path d='M 330 154 L 338 164 L 372 164 L 380 154 Z'/><path d='M 355 154 L 355 132'/><g transform='translate(250,169) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the college kid who asked a priest if he'd seen god, and got told yes.","key":"vivekananda","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Vivekananda","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"belur math","url":"https://belurmath.org/","why":"the monastery he built on the ganges, still the home of the ramakrishna mission"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_World%27s_Religions","why":"the chicago gathering where he stood up"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"he smoked, cigars and a pipe, and he ate meat, and he said so in public in a country where a monk doing either was a scandal, and he defended it with a temper. the temper was real. his brother monks got scorched regularly, and so did his american hosts.\nhe said a lot of things, fast, to a lot of rooms, and they don't all agree. he praised islam in one place and said things about hindu greatness in another that hindu nationalists have been waving around ever since, and they didn't have to bend them much. he took money from rajas and rich american widows to build what he built. and he worked himself to death at thirty nine, which he'd have called a choice and the people who loved him called something else.","moments":["1881, in an english literature class at his college, the scottish principal was explaining a wordsworth poem about a trance and said if you want to know what that is, go see ramakrishna at dakshineswar. so he went.","august 1893, at a farmhouse in massachusetts, a harvard professor named john henry wright listened to him for a few hours and then wrote the parliament that asking this man for credentials was like asking the sun to state its right to shine, and bought him a train ticket to chicago.","1896, new york. nikola tesla, the inventor, met him at a party and got interested in the hindu ideas of prana and akasha, energy and matter, and told him he thought he could show they were the same thing mathematically. he couldn't, then. somebody else did a few years later.","december 1892, kanyakumari. no boat fare, so he swam out to the rock through water the fishermen warned him had sharks, sat there three days, and came back with a plan."],"name":"swami vivekananda","place":"calcutta, then all of india on foot, then america and england, then belur","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 102 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 102 102 104 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 30 116 C 44 106 76 106 92 118'/><path d='M 36 122 L 60 110 M 84 122 L 60 110'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 C 28 24 46 16 60 16 C 76 16 94 24 90 50 C 80 40 66 36 60 36 C 52 36 40 40 30 50 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 38 30 C 52 26 70 26 84 32'/><path d='M 34 44 C 30 36 24 34 20 40 C 22 46 28 48 34 44 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='50' cy='58' r='2.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='58' r='2.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 44 50 C 48 47 53 47 56 50 M 64 50 C 67 47 72 47 76 50'/><path d='M 52 72 L 68 72'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"each soul is potentially divine. the goal is to bring that out.","where":"raja yoga, 1896, the opening lines of the preface, paraphrased"},{"q":"arise, awake, and stop not till the goal is reached.","where":"his line, lifted from the katha upanishad, which he used in lectures and letters for the rest of his life"},{"q":"help and not fight. assimilation and not destruction. harmony and peace and not dissension.","where":"the last words of his closing address at the parliament of the world's religions, chicago, september 27, 1893"}],"start":"raja yoga, the first chapter, and then the chicago addresses, which are short and can be read in an hour.","status":"approved","story":"he was born narendranath datta on january 12, 1863, in calcutta, the son of a lawyer, in a house with money and books. he went to college, read the english philosophers, boxed, sang, and joined the brahmo samaj, a modern hindu reform group that had cut out the idols and the miracles. he wanted proof of god, not a story, and he went around asking religious men if they'd seen him. nobody said yes.\nin 1881 he went up the river to the kali temple at dakshineswar and asked [[ramakrishna]], the temple priest everybody said was either a saint or insane. ramakrishna said yes, i see him more clearly than i see you, and i can show you. narendra thought the man was mad, and kept coming back for five years. in 1884 his father died suddenly and the family went from comfortable to hungry. he asked ramakrishna to pray to kali for money for them. ramakrishna told him to go in and ask her himself. he went in three times and each time all he could ask for was knowledge and love of god. that's the story he told about how he lost the argument.\nramakrishna died of throat cancer in august 1886, in a rented house at cossipore, and left a dozen young men with no money and a promise to stay together. they moved into a broken down house at baranagar that was supposed to be haunted, took the vows of monks, and went hungry a lot. then narendra left and walked india for five years, mostly alone, carrying a water pot and a couple of books, sleeping wherever, seeing how poor the country actually was. at the end of 1892 he got to kanyakumari, the rock at the southern tip where three seas meet, swam out to it, sat for three days, and decided the poor needed food and schools before they needed philosophy, and that he'd go to america to get the money.\na raja in rajasthan, ajit singh of khetri, backed him and, the story goes, gave him the name vivekananda. his madras students raised the fare. he sailed in may 1893 and got to chicago for the parliament of the world's religions, a big gathering of religions held with the world's fair, and found out he was early and had no credentials. a harvard professor he'd met by chance, john henry wright, wrote a letter saying that asking this man for credentials was like asking the sun for its right to shine. he still lost the address in chicago, slept in a boxcar in a freight yard, and got taken in the next morning by a woman who saw him from her window. on september 11, 1893, he stood up and said sisters and brothers of america, and the hall applauded for minutes before he could go on. he closed the parliament two weeks later with help and not fight, assimilation and not destruction.\nhe stayed in the west almost four years. he lectured everywhere, got fleeced by a lecture bureau, started the vedanta society in new york in 1894, and dictated raja yoga, the first book that explained hindu meditation to americans in their own words. william james read it and quoted him in the varieties of religious experience. harvard offered him a chair in eastern philosophy in 1896 and he said no, he was a monk. he went home in january 1897 to crowds at every station, founded the ramakrishna mission that may, built belur math on the ganges as its home, and put an irish schoolteacher named margaret noble to work as sister nivedita running a school for girls. he went west once more, came home sick, and died at belur on july 4, 1902. he was thirty nine. he'd said he wouldn't see forty.","struggle":"1884. his father dropped dead and it turned out there was nothing left, and creditors. he was twenty one, the oldest son, walking calcutta barefoot looking for a job, lying to his mother about having eaten so his brothers and sisters could. he wrote later that he doubted god existed that year, and told ramakrishna so to his face. that's the year of the three trips into the temple, where he couldn't get the word money out of his mouth.\n1886 to 1893. ramakrishna dead, a dozen boys in a haunted house with no income, and then five years on foot across india with nothing, watching a whole country starve. he said afterward that he'd wept at what he saw. in chicago in the summer of 1893 he was a monk with no address in a city he didn't know, sleeping in a freight car, being turned away from doors because of his color and his clothes.\nthe body. diabetes and asthma, bad insomnia, by his mid thirties. he worked through all of it and he knew what it was doing. he told people he wouldn't live to forty, and he didn't.","threads":["wandering","doubt","work","money"],"years":"1863 to 1902"},{"after":"he admitted it, at least partly. in his later writing he said he was held responsible for ending a practice that went back thousands of years, and that he was afraid she was right about him. he also stood by publishing. he didn't give back what he'd taken, because there wasn't a way to.\nhe spent the rest of his life on scholarship, the soma book and the eleusis book, and died in connecticut in december 1986, a year after her. there's no redemption arc here. there's a man who said sorry in a book and meant it about halfway.","basically":"a wall street banker, head of public relations at j.p. morgan, who spent his vacations chasing the history of mushrooms. in 1955 he became one of the first outsiders to take part in a mazatec mushroom ceremony, and in 1957 he told the whole country about it in the biggest magazine in america. that article is where the sixties' magic mushroom craze starts. he meant it as scholarship. the woman who trusted him paid for it. think of the travel writer whose viral post about a hidden village ends with the village overrun.","born":1898,"died":1986,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 170 L 130 170 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 172 L 85 132 L 140 172'/><path d='M 72 250 L 72 200 L 98 200 L 98 250' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='230' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 230 154 L 230 202'/><path d='M 230 202 L 216 236 M 230 202 L 244 236'/><path d='M 230 168 L 244 192 M 230 168 L 204 162'/><rect x='190' y='160' width='20' height='14' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='196' cy='167' r='4'/><path d='M 280 250 L 360 250 L 370 226 L 290 226 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='300' y='232' width='24' height='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 180 162 L 140 196' stroke-dasharray='2 6' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(250,233.5) scale(1.1)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a j.p. morgan vice president ate the mushrooms with maría sabina, went home, and put her town in life magazine.","key":"wasson","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R._Gordon_Wasson","why":"the plain overview of the life"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"this is the mess, and it's not small. a woman who couldn't read, in a town with no road, let him into the most sacred thing she did because he asked, and he went home and published it in a magazine read by millions. the false name in life magazine didn't last the year. his own book named her and her town. then came the hippies, the rock tourists, the soldiers, the fire.\nand one of his trips, in 1956, was partly paid for in secret by the cia's mind control program, mkultra, through a front. the documents say he didn't know. he was a banker who liked mushrooms and he became the door the whole mess came through.","moments":["the life magazine story ran with a photo spread and made his name. it made hers a target.","in 1956 he brought back recordings of her chanting through a velada, and they came out as a record the next year.","in 1962 he went back up the mountain with [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], who brought her the psilocybin he'd made from her mushrooms in pill form."],"name":"gordon wasson","place":"montana, then new york, and the mountains of oaxaca","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><rect x='82' y='104' width='24' height='16' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='94' cy='112' r='5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"we were the first white men in recorded history to eat the divine mushrooms.","where":"seeking the magic mushroom, his life magazine article, may 13, 1957, paraphrased"},{"q":"i am held responsible for the end of a religious practice in mesoamerica that goes back for millennia.","where":"his later writing on maría sabina, in the 1970s, paraphrased"},{"q":"my only intention was to add to the sum of what people know.","where":"his defense of himself when asked about her, paraphrased"}],"start":"read maría sabina's own book first, the one she told to álvaro estrada. then, if you want his side, the life magazine article, seeking the magic mushroom, from may 1957.","status":"approved","story":"he grew up in montana, became a newspaper reporter, and ended up at j.p. morgan, one of the most powerful banks in the world, rising to vice president for public relations. in 1927 on his honeymoon in the catskills, the mountains north of new york city, his russian wife valentina, a children's doctor, ran into the woods and came back with an armful of wild mushrooms to cook. he was horrified. she was delighted. the two of them spent the next thirty years studying why some cultures love mushrooms and some fear them.\nthat trail took him to mexico. in june 1955, in huautla de jiménez, a town high in the mountains of oaxaca, [[sabina|maría sabina]], a mazatec healer, let him and a photographer friend, allan richardson, sit through one of her all-night healing ceremonies, the velada, and eat the mushrooms. he wrote that it was the first time white men in recorded history had done it.\non may 13, 1957, life magazine, the giant weekly picture magazine that was in half the living rooms in america, ran his story, seeking the magic mushroom, with photographs. he gave her a false name in the magazine. the same year, in a two volume book called mushrooms, russia and history, which he and valentina printed in a small expensive edition, he used her real name and the name of her town. after that, anyone who wanted to could find her.\nhe kept at it. soma, from 1968, argued that the holy drink in the rig veda, the oldest hindu scriptures, was made from a red and white mushroom, fly agaric. the road to eleusis, from 1978, with the swiss chemist [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] and a classics professor, carl ruck, argued that an ancient greek religious festival used a drink made with ergot, a fungus. scholars still argue about both.","struggle":"valentina died of cancer in 1958, the year after the life article. the whole mushroom project had been the two of them, and he carried on alone.\nby the seventies he knew what had happened in huautla. the hippies, the army checkpoints, a burned house, a woman blamed by her own town. he wrote about it with regret, and he also kept saying knowledge was worth it. both of those lived in him at once until he died.","threads":["psychedelics","fame"],"years":"1898 to 1986"},{"after":"there isn't a redemptive after. he kept teaching, kept drinking, and died in his sleep at fifty eight in a cabin on mount tamalpais, just north of san francisco, in november 1973, with the talks still pouring out.\nwhat he leaves is the honesty of the gap, which is its own kind of teaching. he said the ego, the separate little me we all think we are, is a hoax, and he couldn't get out from under his. both true. the talks still land because he never pretended the second part wasn't.","basically":"an englishman with a great voice who moved to california and explained zen and taoism, the big eastern traditions about letting go, to americans on the radio in the fifties and sixties, and made it sound like the best joke you'd ever heard. his old lectures are all over youtube now, cut up and set to ocean footage. he taught letting go better than almost anybody and couldn't let go of the bottle, and it killed him at fifty eight. listen to him for the teaching, and keep the second part in mind while you do.","born":1915,"died":1973,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 C 80 250 120 200 160 200 C 230 200 250 130 240 110 C 232 92 200 100 208 122 C 214 138 240 140 250 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 130 C 280 180 330 210 380 200 L 380 260 L 20 260' fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 250 130 C 280 180 330 210 380 200'/><path d='M 60 240 C 90 232 120 240 150 232 M 280 240 C 310 232 340 240 370 232' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='300' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 300 124 L 300 172'/><path d='M 300 172 L 286 206 M 300 172 L 314 206'/><path d='M 300 138 L 274 140 M 300 138 L 326 140'/><path d='M 295 115 Q 300 119 305 115'/><path d='M 282 206 L 322 206' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 60 246 L 60 232 L 64 226 L 72 226 L 76 232 L 76 246 Z' fill='var(--paper)' opacity='.8'/><g transform='translate(200,173.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"an episcopal priest who quit, became the voice of zen on the radio, called himself a spiritual entertainer, and drank himself to death at fifty eight.","key":"watts","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Watts","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Way_of_Zen","why":"his 1957 book on zen"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"alcoholic, badly, for his last decade. three marriages, seven children, most of whom he left. sloppy and sad, and he knew it. nothing that would get him cut from these pages, no abuse of anyone in his care, that i know of.","moments":["he published his first book, the spirit of zen, at twenty one, in 1936, in london, having never been to japan.","he lived for years on a converted ferryboat, the vallejo, moored in sausalito, the houseboat town across the bay from san francisco, with a rotating cast of artists and drunks and a view of the water. the book was mostly written there.","his 1959 series on kqed, san francisco's public tv station, called eastern wisdom and modern life, put a smiling englishman explaining the tao into american living rooms nearly a decade before the beatles went to india and made it fashionable."],"name":"alan watts","place":"england, then san francisco","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 80 C 52 92 68 92 70 80 C 66 84 54 84 50 80 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you didn't come into this world. you came out of it, like a wave from the ocean.","where":"the book, his 1966 book about the self, paraphrased"},{"q":"trying to define yourself is like trying to bite your own teeth.","where":"a line he used in talks"},{"q":"a myth is an image in terms of which we try to make sense of the world.","where":"the way of zen, his 1957 history of zen"}],"start":"the book. then any of his talks, which are all over the internet and better than the books. the way of zen if you want the history.","status":"approved","story":"an english kid who read every buddhist book in london by his teens, moved to america in 1938, became an episcopal priest for about five years, and left the church in 1950. episcopalians are the american branch of the church of england. he left over his marriage falling apart and his growing sense that he didn't believe the furniture anymore, the creeds and the rules the church had built around the thing itself.\nhe landed in san francisco and became the guy who explained zen and taoism to america in plain english. zen is the branch of buddhism built around sitting still and noticing right now. taoism is the old chinese way of going with the grain of things instead of forcing them, from [[laotzu|lao tzu]]. he did it on kpfa, a listener-funded radio station in berkeley, in lectures, and in the way of zen, his 1957 history of it. he had a voice like a friendly uncle and a laugh you could hear from the next room. the serious zen teachers and scholars were not amused. he called himself a spiritual entertainer, which was honest.\nhe ran with the psychedelic crowd. the joyous cosmology, from 1962, is his book about lsd, and timothy leary and richard alpert, the two harvard psychologists running drug experiments, alpert later [[ramdass|ram dass]], wrote the foreword. the book, from 1966, is his best. its full title is the book on the taboo against knowing who you are, and the thing we're not supposed to know is that you're not a separate ego in a bag of skin. you're the whole thing, pretending.\nhe was a wonderful teacher of not gripping who couldn't stop gripping a bottle. he died in his sleep in 1973 in a cabin on mount tamalpais, the big mountain just north of san francisco.","struggle":"he was a beautiful explainer of letting go and couldn't. the drinking got serious in his fifties, a bottle of vodka a day by the end according to people who loved him. he was supporting two families on lectures and getting on planes hungover to give talks about the present moment.\nin his autobiography, in my own way, from 1972, he's frank about the gap. he said he was a spiritual entertainer and not a saint and never claimed to be. that's true and it's also the sound of a man letting himself off the hook.","threads":["addiction","fame","family"],"years":"1915 to 1973"},{"after":"after he got fired he got another government job within days, in the attorney general's office, and kept going to the hospitals. after the stroke he moved into his brother's house in camden and later bought a small house on mickle street, and people came from england and all over to sit with the old man in the cluttered front room.\nhe never stopped working on the one book. there were several editions in his lifetime. the last, in 1891 and 1892, he approved from his bed, and it's the one called the deathbed edition. the book grew from twelve poems to about four hundred and he kept all of it.","basically":"a brooklyn newspaperman who paid to print a book of poems in 1855 that sounded like nothing before it, loose and huge and sexy and in love with everybody, and then wrote rave reviews of it himself under no name. then during the civil war he spent about three years sitting with wounded and dying soldiers in the army hospitals in washington. he's the grandfather of every poet who writes the way people talk, and of everybody who ever made a fake account to hype their own work. take from him that you can be full of contradictions and still show up for people every single day.","born":1819,"died":1892,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='110' cy='230' r='13'/><path d='M 116 234 C 124 252 146 252 152 236 C 140 240 128 240 116 234 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 124 238 L 200 240 M 200 240 L 240 226 L 260 244 M 200 240 L 250 246'/><path d='M 150 240 L 170 200 L 176 176'/><ellipse cx='170' cy='170' rx='5' ry='8' fill='var(--marigold)' transform='rotate(-25 170 170)'/><ellipse cx='182' cy='170' rx='5' ry='8' fill='var(--lavender)' transform='rotate(25 182 170)'/><path d='M 176 164 L 176 178'/><path d='M 50 250 L 46 214 M 56 250 L 60 208 M 64 250 L 62 220 M 300 250 L 296 196 M 306 250 L 312 210 M 330 250 L 326 220 M 340 250 L 346 204'/><path d='M 270 250 C 266 232 290 232 286 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='274' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='283' cy='230' r='5'/><\/g><g transform='translate(312,231.1) scale(0.7)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he published his own book, reviewed it himself, and then spent the war holding the hands of dying boys.","key":"whitman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walt_Whitman","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/walt-whitman","why":"a life and a lot of the poems"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1322","why":"leaves of grass at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"when leaves of grass came out he wrote at least three glowing reviews of it himself and got them printed without his name on them. that's pretty funny. less funny, in 1890 the english writer john addington symonds, who was gay himself, asked him straight out whether the poems about love between comrades meant what they seemed to mean, and whitman wrote back that he had six illegitimate children. there's no evidence any of them existed.\nsome of what he wrote as a newspaper editor and said in old age about black americans is ugly. the poems are bigger than the man was on some days.","moments":["when lincoln was shot in april 1865 whitman wrote when lilacs last in the dooryard bloom'd, a long poem of grief, and o captain! my captain!, the one schoolkids still recite. he'd watched lincoln ride past in the streets of washington and they'd nodded to each other.","there's a photograph of him as an old man with a butterfly perched on his finger. the butterfly was cardboard. it turned up in his papers in the library of congress.","the last lines of song of myself tell you that if you want him again, look for him under your boot soles."],"name":"walt whitman","place":"long island, brooklyn, the army hospitals in washington, camden, new jersey","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 22 44 C 30 38 90 38 98 44 C 90 48 30 48 22 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 42 C 38 20 82 20 82 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"do i contradict myself? very well then i contradict myself.","where":"song of myself, the long poem at the heart of leaves of grass, section 51; the next line is the one about multitudes"},{"q":"i am large, i contain multitudes.","where":"song of myself, section 51"},{"q":"go over everything you've been told at school or church or in any book, and throw out whatever insults your own soul.","where":"the preface to the 1855 leaves of grass, paraphrased"}],"start":"song of myself, the 1855 version, sections 1, 6 and 52. then specimen days, his prose memoir, for the war hospitals.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on long island in 1819 and grew up in brooklyn. he left school at eleven and worked as a printer's boy, a teacher, a newspaperman, and an editor. at thirty six he paid to print a thin book of poems with no author's name on the title page, just a picture of himself in work clothes with his shirt open. leaves of grass, july 1855.\nalmost nobody noticed. he sent a copy to ralph waldo emerson, the most respected writer in america, who wrote back that it was the most extraordinary piece of wit and wisdom america had yet produced, and greeted him at the beginning of a great career. whitman printed the letter in the next edition without asking.\nin december 1862 his brother george was wounded at fredericksburg, virginia, one of the worst union defeats of the civil war, and walt went south to find him. george was fine. walt stayed in washington for the rest of the war and went to the army hospitals nearly every day, sitting with wounded and dying soldiers, north and south, bringing oranges and tobacco and paper, and writing letters home for the men who couldn't. by his own count he saw tens of thousands of them.\nhe was gay, as surely as anyone can be said to be about a man in the nineteenth century. his great love was peter doyle, a young streetcar conductor he met in washington in 1865. he had a stroke in 1873, moved to camden, new jersey, and kept revising the same book until he died there in 1892.","struggle":"in 1865 he had a clerk's job at the department of the interior. the new secretary, james harlan, went through his desk, found a copy of leaves of grass with whitman's notes in it, read it, and fired him for it. a friend wrote an angry pamphlet calling him the good gray poet, and the name stuck.\nthe hospitals wore him down. he'd sit up all night with a boy who died in the morning, and do it again the next night. his health went in those years and never came back. the stroke in 1873 left him partly paralyzed, and his mother died four months later. he said it was the great cloud of his life.","threads":["war","dying","love"],"years":"1819 to 1892"},{"after":"after wounded knee he didn't go on tour, didn't start a church, and didn't denounce the lakota or the dance. he went on living in mason valley and yerington, doing ranch work, sometimes working as a healer for people who asked, and receiving the visitors who kept coming to see him for years.\nhe lived another forty years. he died in yerington on september 20, 1932, and was buried at schurz, on the walker river paiute reservation. the round dance he taught still gets danced. the world didn't end. he'd have told you that was never the point.","basically":"a northern paiute man in nevada who worked on a white family's ranch, took their name, jack wilson, and heard their bible stories at the table. on new year's day 1889, during a solar eclipse, he had a vision. work, don't lie, don't fight, dance the round dance, and the dead will come back and the world will be made new. the ghost dance spread across the west like wildfire, the lakota heard it their own way, and the government's panic over it ended in the massacre at wounded knee. think of someone whose message of peace goes viral and gets turned into something he never said, with a body count. take from him how little control you have over a thing once it leaves your hands.","born":1856,"died":1932,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='300' cy='70' r='30' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='300' cy='70' r='38' stroke-dasharray='2 6'/><g transform='translate(90,150) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(125,140) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(160,146) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(195,140) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(230,150) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><path d='M 330 250 C 322 236 320 226 330 220 C 336 226 346 224 346 234 C 352 236 352 246 344 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(338,207.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a paiute ranch hand saw god during an eclipse and told everyone to dance and be kind, and the army heard war.","key":"wovoka","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wovoka","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dance","why":"the movement he started, and what happened to it"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of. he let people believe he could control the weather, and his neighbors mostly did believe it. that's not a mess, that was the job his father had too.","moments":["the story goes that he could make ice fall out of a clear summer sky, and that he ended a drought. his authority among the paiute rested on it as much as on the vision.","twenty years before him, in 1869, another paiute holy man named wodziwob had a similar vision and started a dance, and wovoka's father helped spread it. the 1889 dance was the second one.","in january 1892 james mooney, the smithsonian researcher, came out to nevada to find him, and got an interview. that report is still the main account in his own words."],"name":"wovoka","place":"smith valley and mason valley, near yerington, nevada","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 18 42 C 28 35 92 35 102 42 C 92 47 28 47 18 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 40 C 38 16 82 16 82 40 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"do not tell lies. do not hurt anybody. do right always.","where":"the messiah letter, the message he gave to cheyenne and arapaho visitors in 1891, written down by one of them and printed by james mooney, a smithsonian researcher, paraphrased"},{"q":"when the earth shakes, do not be afraid. it will not hurt you.","where":"the messiah letter, 1891, paraphrased"},{"q":"i went up to the other world and saw god, and all the people who had died long ago, happy and young.","where":"his account of the vision to james mooney, paraphrased"}],"start":"james mooney's the ghost-dance religion and the sioux outbreak of 1890, the smithsonian report from 1896, the chapters on wovoka. then michael hittman's wovoka and the ghost dance.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1856 in smith valley, nevada, into the northern paiute, a people of the great basin, the dry country of lakes and sagebrush between the rockies and the sierra. his father was a weather doctor, a man people believed could bring rain.\nfrom about eight years old until nearly thirty he worked for a white rancher near yerington named david wilson. the family called him jack wilson, and he heard their bible stories and prayers. he knew both worlds before he was grown.\nhe got sick with a fever, and on january 1, 1889, the day of a total eclipse of the sun over nevada, he fell into something like a coma. when he came out of it he said he'd been taken up to heaven and seen god, and the dead living happily there. he was told to tell the people to love one another, not to fight, not to lie or steal, to work, and to dance a traditional round dance. if they did, the dead would come back and the earth would be made new. he never left nevada to spread it. people came to him.\nthey came by train from nations all over the west. the lakota sent men, among them kicking bear and short bull, in 1889 and 1890. back home the message grew harder edges. dancers wore ghost shirts they believed would stop bullets, and some said the whites would be swept away. the government saw an uprising coming. on december 15, 1890, police killed sitting bull while arresting him, partly over the dance, and on december 29 the army killed hundreds of lakota at wounded knee creek. he hadn't wanted any of it.","struggle":"wounded knee gutted him. he'd preached a dance and a way of living that said don't hurt anybody, and it came back to him as a mass grave in the snow in south dakota. and people who'd believed in him had to reckon with what the dance had led to.\nand the dead didn't come back. the new world he'd promised didn't arrive in 1890 or 1891 or ever. he had to go on living in the old one, in the same valley, as the man whose prophecy hadn't happened.","threads":["nonviolence","grief","fame"],"years":"around 1856 to 1932"},{"after":"his answer to huizi about the pot was that at first he grieved like anybody. then he looked back to before she was born, before she had a body, before she even had breath, and saw that she'd changed into life and now changed out of it, the way the seasons go round. she's asleep in the big room now, he said, meaning the whole world, and if i sat here bawling it would mean i didn't understand. so i stopped.\nand when huizi died, he walked past his grave and told a story. a plasterer got a speck of mud on the tip of his nose and a carpenter named shi took it off with a whirling axe, without touching skin. shi said he could only do it with that one plasterer. since huizi died, zhuangzi said, i've had no one to work with. no one to talk to. the man who drummed at his wife's funeral missed his friend.","basically":"a chinese thinker from about twenty three hundred years ago who wrote the funniest serious book there is, full of talking trees, happy fish, a cook who never sharpens his knife, and a man who turns down a kingdom because he'd rather be a turtle in the mud. he's the patron saint of everyone who ever turned down the promotion and never regretted it. take from him that being useless to the machine might be how you get to have a life.","born":-369,"died":-286,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 86 250 C 98 214 70 196 94 164 C 108 146 84 128 100 104 M 112 250 C 118 220 100 200 118 172 C 128 156 112 134 120 110'/><path d='M 104 132 C 128 126 146 130 168 120'/><path d='M 40 100 C 30 70 60 40 96 46 C 120 26 170 40 166 74 C 190 90 170 120 146 112 C 130 124 100 120 92 110 C 70 120 40 116 40 100 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='66' cy='78' r='4' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='130' cy='66' r='5' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='104' cy='92' r='3' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='200' cy='234' r='12'/><path d='M 194 233 C 196 235 198 235 200 233 M 202 233 C 204 235 206 235 208 233'/><path d='M 212 238 L 270 240 M 270 240 L 306 246 M 270 240 L 302 232 M 230 239 L 214 226'/><path d='M 218 214 C 222 210 226 210 228 214 M 226 204 C 230 200 234 200 236 204' opacity='.4'/><ellipse cx='262' cy='150' rx='9' ry='14' fill='var(--marigold)' transform='rotate(-30 262 150)'/><ellipse cx='280' cy='150' rx='9' ry='14' fill='var(--lavender)' transform='rotate(30 280 150)'/><path d='M 271 142 L 271 162 M 271 142 L 266 132 M 271 142 L 276 132'/><path d='M 240 200 C 250 186 246 176 258 168' stroke-dasharray='2 7' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(150,104.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he dreamed he was a butterfly and woke up not sure which one he was, and thought that was funny.","key":"zhuangzi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuang_Zhou","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuangzi_(book)","why":"the book with his name on it, and the butterfly dream"}],"lineage":"tao","mess":"nothing i know of. we barely know he existed, and he'd have found the question funny.","moments":["when he was dying, his disciples wanted to give him a big funeral. he said heaven and earth were his coffin and the stars were his jewels, what more did they want. they said they were afraid the crows would eat him. he said above ground the crows get me, below ground the ants. why rob one to feed the other?","if an empty boat drifts into yours on the river, you don't get angry, even if you've got a temper. if there's a man in it, you shout. make yourself the empty boat. that's chapter 20.","chapter 26 ends with him asking where he can find a man who's forgotten words, so he can have a word with him."],"name":"zhuangzi","place":"the state of song, in north china","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='28' r='7' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><ellipse cx='94' cy='102' rx='6' ry='9' fill='var(--marigold)' transform='rotate(-25 94 102)'/><ellipse cx='106' cy='102' rx='6' ry='9' fill='var(--lavender)' transform='rotate(25 106 102)'/><path d='M 100 95 L 100 110 M 100 95 L 97 89 M 100 95 L 103 89'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"was it zhuang zhou dreaming he was a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming it was zhuang zhou?","where":"the zhuangzi, the book named after him, chapter 2, the end, paraphrased"},{"q":"everyone knows the use of the useful. nobody knows the use of the useless.","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 4, the last line"},{"q":"the fish trap is for catching fish; once you've got the fish you can forget the trap.","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 26, where it goes on to say words are for catching meaning"}],"start":"the inner chapters, burton watson's translation, chapters 1 through 7. then the way of chuang tzu, thomas merton's loose versions, which are short.","status":"approved","story":"zhuang zhou lived in the fourth century bc in song, one of the small warring kingdoms china was broken into back then, before it was one country. sima qian, china's first great historian, writing about two hundred years later, says he was once a minor official in charge of a lacquer garden, a grove of the trees whose sap makes varnish. that's about all we know. the book with his name on it has thirty three chapters, and scholars think he wrote the first seven, the inner chapters, and his followers wrote the rest.\nthe book is jokes. talking trees and skulls. a cook named ding who's used the same knife for nineteen years without sharpening it, because he doesn't hack at the ox. he feels for the gaps between the joints and lets the blade slide through where the animal is already open. hunchbacks and cripples who turn out to be the wise ones. confucius, china's great teacher of duty and good manners, who'd died about a century before, gets made to say things he'd never say, about the tao, the way, the natural grain of things that life goes better when you stop fighting. and the best tree in the forest is the useless one, so knotted nobody bothers to cut it down, so it gets old.\nthe king of chu, the biggest kingdom in the south, sent two officials to offer him the job of running the state. he was fishing and didn't look up. he asked them whether a sacred tortoise, dead three thousand years and wrapped in silk in the king's temple, would rather be dead and honored or alive and dragging its tail in the mud. alive, they said. go away, he said. i'll drag my tail in the mud.\nhis best friend was huizi, a logician who argued with him about everything. on a bridge over the hao river zhuangzi said look how happy the fish are. huizi said you're not a fish, how do you know. zhuangzi said you're not me, how do you know i don't know. it goes around once more, and nobody wins, and that's the point. the tradition puts him right next to [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the legendary old master whose tao te ching, a short strange book of eighty one little poems, is where the whole tradition starts.","struggle":"he was poor. chapter 20 has him going to see the king of wei in patched clothes, shoes tied on with string. when the king asks why he's so wretched, he says he's poor, not wretched. chapter 26 has him going to borrow grain from an official who says sure, as soon as the taxes come in, and zhuangzi tells him a story about a fish flopping in the puddle of a wheel rut, who needs a bucket of water today, and gets promised a whole river someday.\nand his wife died. huizi came to pay his respects and found him sitting on the floor, legs sprawled, drumming on a pot and singing. huizi said she lived with you, raised your children, grew old with you, and you won't even cry. this is too much.","threads":["grief","money","joy"],"years":"the fourth century bc"},{"after":"after pratt threw her out, she didn't stop writing, and she didn't go quiet. she went to the reservation in utah and turned her anger into work, an opera, organizing, letters. and when she got to washington she stayed twenty years, testifying, writing reports, and pushing congress on citizenship and land and money stolen from native families.\nshe never got the credit. in 1938, the year she died, hanson rewrote the sun dance opera for broadway and left her name off it. she's buried at arlington, under her married name, and the name she gave herself is on the books.","basically":"a yankton dakota girl who went east at eight because missionaries promised her red apples and a train ride, and got her hair cut off at a boarding school in indiana. she grew up to teach at carlisle, the flagship government indian school, and then wrote essays in the atlantic monthly, the most respected magazine in the country, telling everybody what the schools did to children. the school's founder fired her. she wrote an opera, then spent the rest of her life in washington lobbying congress. think of the whistleblower who writes the exposé while she's still on the payroll. take from her that you can use the tools they trained you with to tell the truth about them.","born":1876,"died":1938,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 150 200 L 290 200 M 160 200 L 160 250 M 280 200 L 280 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 120 134 L 120 182'/><path d='M 120 182 L 106 216 M 120 182 L 134 216'/><path d='M 120 148 L 106 172 M 120 148 L 146 142'/><path d='M 110 128 C 104 146 106 160 108 170 M 130 128 C 136 146 134 160 132 170' stroke-width='5'/><rect x='176' y='186' width='40' height='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 178 L 216 190' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='240' y='184' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='240' y='190' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='240' y='178' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 320 246 C 330 240 344 242 356 246' stroke-width='6'/><circle cx='318' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='330' cy='232' r='5'/><path d='M 321 236 L 340 222 M 327 236 L 308 222'/><g transform='translate(255,163.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"they cut off her braids at eight, she taught at their flagship school, and then she told the whole country what those schools did.","key":"zitkalasa","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zitkala-%C5%A0a","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"american indian stories, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376","why":"her childhood and school essays, at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"she campaigned hard against peyote, the cactus used as a sacrament in the native american church, the church [[quanahparker|quanah parker]] helped build. in 1918 she testified to congress for banning it, alongside white reformers she otherwise fought. plenty of native people then and now think she was flat wrong and helped the government go after a native religion. that's the one thing on her record that people still argue about.","moments":["zitkala-ša, red bird, is a name she gave herself as a young woman. the government knew her as gertrude simmons bonnin, and that's the name on her grave.","the osage report she co-wrote in 1923 is about the same murders that became the book and the movie killers of the flower moon."],"name":"zitkala-ša","place":"the yankton reservation in dakota territory, indiana, carlisle, the uintah reservation in utah, then washington, d.c.","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"now my long hair was shingled like a coward's!","where":"the school days of an indian girl, 1900, in the atlantic monthly and later in american indian stories"},{"q":"if this is paganism, then at present, at least, i am a pagan.","where":"why i am a pagan, the atlantic monthly, december 1902, the last line"},{"q":"my indian nature is a moaning wind, but it comes out like the low voice of a seashell, only for those who lean in to hear it.","where":"an indian teacher among indians, 1900, paraphrased"}],"start":"american indian stories, which is free online. the three pieces about her childhood and school days, then why i am a pagan, which is short.","status":"approved","story":"she was born on february 22, 1876, on the yankton reservation in dakota territory, now south dakota. yankton dakota are one of the seven council fires, the seven nations of the people called the sioux. her mother raised her. her name means red bird. in 1884, at eight, she went off with missionaries to white's manual labor institute, a quaker boarding school in wabash, indiana, because they'd told her about red apples in the east and the train ride sounded exciting. her mother didn't want her to go.\nthe school cut her long hair. among the dakota, she'd been taught, only mourners and cowards had their hair cut short. she hid under a bed, and they dragged her out. she went home and came back, went to earlham college in indiana, and in 1897 started teaching music at carlisle, the big government indian school in pennsylvania run by richard henry pratt.\nin 1900, while she was still connected to carlisle, the atlantic monthly printed three of her essays, impressions of an indian childhood, the school days of an indian girl, and an indian teacher among indians. they told what it was like to be the kid on the other end of the program. pratt called one of her stories trash, and in 1901 she was out. in 1902 she married raymond bonnin, who worked for the indian service, and they spent fourteen years on the uintah and ouray reservation in utah.\nthere, with a white composer named william hanson, she wrote the sun dance opera, which opened in vernal, utah, in february 1913, the first american indian opera, about the sun dance, a ceremony the government had outlawed. in 1916 she became secretary of the society of american indians and moved to washington, d.c. in 1924 she co-wrote oklahoma's poor rich indians, a report on how white guardians were robbing and killing osage people for their oil money. in 1926 she started the national council of american indians and ran it until she died, on january 26, 1938. she's buried at arlington national cemetery, under the name gertrude simmons bonnin.","struggle":"the school took her hair and her language, and when she went home to her mother she couldn't be all the way there either. she wrote that she was neither a wild indian nor a tame one. she spent her life in between, and wrote about it without pretending it had resolved.\nand her mother. she'd gone against her mother's wishes to go east at eight, and she wrote about the distance that opened between them, and never closed.","threads":["exile","family","anger","work"],"years":"1876 to 1938"}],"meetings":[{"also":["barnabas","james, the brother of jesus"],"key":"antioch","lines":"three years after he was knocked flat on the road to damascus and went from hunting jesus's followers to being one, [[paul]] went up to jerusalem to see [[peter|cephas]], which is peter's aramaic name, and stayed with him fifteen days. paul says so himself in galatians 1, one of his letters. they were on the same side of the big question, whether gentiles, non-jews, had to become jews to follow jesus.\nthen at antioch, a big city in what's now turkey, peter had been eating with gentile christians, which strict jews wouldn't do, until men came from james, jesus's brother, who led the church in jerusalem. then peter quietly pulled back and ate apart, and the others followed him, even barnabas, paul's partner on the road. paul says he opposed him to his face in front of everyone and called it hypocrisy. we only have paul's side. we don't know what peter said back.\nin acts 15, at a meeting of the church leaders in jerusalem, peter stands up and says god made no distinction between us and them, which is the thing paul was shouting about. a later letter under peter's name, which most scholars doubt he wrote, calls paul our dear brother and admits some of his letters are hard to understand. somebody wanted the two of them at peace.","status":"approved","title":"paul calls peter a hypocrite to his face.","when":"around the year 49, antioch","where":"galatians 1:18 and 2:11 to 14, in paul's letters, in the new testament. acts 15. 2 peter 3:15 and 16.","who":["peter","paul"],"year":49},{"also":["the dalai lama"],"key":"asia","lines":"after twenty seven years in a kentucky monastery, [[merton]] got permission to go to asia. in november, in dharamsala, the town in the indian himalayas where the tibetans live in exile, he met the dalai lama, the head of tibetan buddhism, three times. they talked about meditation, marxism, and the life of a monk, and each came away saying the other was the real thing. the dalai lama doesn't get a page of his own here, after a 2023 incident where he asked a young boy to suck his tongue on camera, but this meeting happened and it mattered.\nat polonnaruwa, a ruined old city in sri lanka, merton stood barefoot in front of the giant buddhas carved out of the rock and wrote that he'd finally seen what he'd been looking for, and that the whole thing was clear. a week later, in bangkok, on december 10, after giving a talk, he was found dead in his room, apparently electrocuted by a faulty fan.\ndecember 10 was the day he'd entered the monastery in 1941. twenty seven years to the day.","status":"approved","title":"the monk goes east and doesn't come back.","when":"november and december 1968","where":"the asian journal of thomas merton, his notebooks from the trip, the last thirty pages.","who":["merton"],"year":1968},{"also":["epimenides","aratus"],"key":"athens","lines":"in athens, standing in front of the philosophers on the areopagus, a rocky hill where the city's council met, [[paul]] didn't quote scripture. they'd never heard of it. he quoted their own poets. in him we live and move and have our being, that's epimenides, an old poet from crete. we are his offspring, that's aratus, a greek poet who wrote about the stars.\nhe pointed at an altar they'd built to an unknown god, a sort of just-in-case altar, and said, that one, i'll tell you about that one.\nit's the oldest example in the bible of somebody saying you already know this. you already wrote it down. you just called it something else.","status":"approved","title":"paul quotes the pagans to the pagans.","when":"around the year 50","where":"acts 17:16 through 34. read the whole chapter.","who":["paul"],"year":50},{"also":["joshu sasaki"],"key":"baldy","lines":"[[cohen|leonard cohen]] met joshu sasaki, a japanese teacher of rinzai zen, the strict school known for koans, the riddles with no logical answer that teachers give students, in los angeles in the seventies, and kept sitting with him for decades. in the mid nineties he moved up to the zen center on mount baldy, in the mountains above los angeles, cooked for him, drove him around, and in 1996 was ordained a monk.\nsasaki is not in the forest. starting in 2012, many former students accused him of sexual misconduct going back decades. cohen's years on the mountain are his own, and so is whatever he made of that.","status":"approved","title":"a songwriter cooks for an old zen master.","when":"the seventies to the nineties, mount baldy","where":"book of longing, cohen's poems from the mountain years. sylvie simmons's i'm your man, the big biography, the chapters on mount baldy.","who":["cohen"],"year":1996},{"also":[],"key":"beach","lines":"[[peter]] had gone back to fishing on the sea of galilee, the lake where jesus first found him. all night, nothing. at dawn a man on the shore told them to throw the net on the right side, and it filled so full they couldn't haul it in. peter put his coat on and jumped into the water.\n[[jesus]] had a charcoal fire going on the beach with fish on it, and bread. peter had last stood at a charcoal fire in the courtyard of the high priest, the night jesus was arrested, telling people three times he'd never known him. after breakfast jesus asked him three times, do you love me, and three times gave him a job. he never brought up the courtyard.\nno lecture. the same fire, the same number, and a different answer.","status":"approved","title":"three times, for three times.","when":"a morning after the crucifixion, the sea of galilee","where":"john 21, the last chapter of the gospel of john, in any bible. then john 18:15 to 27 for the other fire.","who":["peter","jesus"],"year":30},{"also":["gary snyder","allen ginsberg"],"key":"berkeley-1955","lines":"in the fall of 1955 [[kerouac]], who'd written on the road but couldn't get it published yet, met gary snyder, a young poet who'd studied chinese and japanese and was about to go to kyoto to train in zen, the japanese buddhism of sitting meditation. that october they were both at a reading at the six gallery, a little art space in san francisco, the night their friend allen ginsberg first read howl, his long furious poem. a few weeks later snyder took kerouac and a friend up matterhorn peak in the sierra nevada.\nkerouac wrote it all up as the dharma bums, with snyder as japhy ryder. snyder didn't love being turned into a character. but the book sent a lot of young americans off with a rucksack and a copy of the sutras, the buddha's talks, and the next summer, on snyder's advice, kerouac went up to the fire lookout on desolation peak alone.","status":"approved","title":"a drinker follows a zen student up a mountain.","when":"october 1955, berkeley and the high sierra","where":"the dharma bums. snyder's side of it is in his interviews.","who":["kerouac"],"year":1955},{"also":["bill wilson","gerald heard"],"key":"billw-lsd","lines":"bill wilson, who'd started alcoholics anonymous after a flash of white light in a detox bed in 1934, took lsd in los angeles starting in 1956 with researchers there. gerald heard, an english writer on mysticism who'd come to california with [[huxley|aldous huxley]] in 1937, and huxley himself were among the people he did it with.\nhe said it brought back something like the light of 1934, and he hoped it could help drinkers who couldn't reach a spiritual experience on their own. most of aa was appalled that the founder was taking a mind altering drug. he never told members to try it.","status":"approved","title":"the founder of aa tries lsd.","when":"1956 into the late fifties, los angeles","where":"pass it on, aa's biography of bill wilson, the chapter on lsd.","who":["huxley"],"year":1956},{"also":["neem karoli baba"],"key":"blanket","lines":"richard alpert, the harvard psychologist who'd been fired for giving students psychedelics, came to india with a bag of lsd and no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to see his own guru, a fat old man wrapped in a plaid blanket called neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to his people.\nthe old man told alpert he'd been thinking about his mother the night before, and that she'd died of her spleen. she had. nobody in india could have known that. alpert cried for two days.\nhe was given the name [[ramdass|ram dass]], servant of god. the instruction was to love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, and tell the truth. he spent fifty years on it.","status":"approved","title":"the man in the blanket knows about his mother.","when":"1967, the himalayan foothills","where":"be here now, the first section. miracle of love, ram dass's collection of stories about maharaj-ji.","who":["ramdass"],"year":1967},{"also":[],"key":"bonhoeffer-gandhi","lines":"[[bonhoeffer]], the german pastor who'd later die fighting hitler, wrote to friends that he wanted to go to india and sit at [[gandhi]]'s feet and learn how nonviolent resistance actually worked, because he thought the german church was going to need it. gandhi wrote back and said come.\nhe never went. the situation in germany got worse and he stayed. within a decade the pacifist was in a conspiracy to kill hitler.\nthe trip that didn't happen is one of the great what-ifs on these pages. the german lutheran and the hindu, in the same room, in 1935.","status":"approved","title":"bonhoeffer wants to go learn nonviolence from gandhi, and never gets there.","when":"1934","where":"bonhoeffer's letters from 1934, in the english edition of his collected works, volume 13.","who":["bonhoeffer","gandhi"],"year":1934},{"also":["bronson alcott"],"key":"brooklyn-1856","lines":"in november 1856 [[thoreau]] and bronson alcott, a teacher and dreamer from their concord circle and the father of louisa may alcott, who wrote little women, went to brooklyn to meet [[whitman]] in the house he shared with his mother. thoreau came away shaken. he wrote to a friend that whitman was the greatest democrat the world had seen, meaning a man of the common people, nothing to do with the party, and that some of the poems were very brave and a few made him uncomfortable.\nthoreau told him the poems sounded like the old hindu scriptures and asked if he'd read them. whitman said no, tell me about them.\ntwo men who'd never read each other's minds and ended up in the same place, one from a pond and one from a crowded street.","status":"approved","title":"thoreau asks whitman about the gita, and whitman says tell me.","when":"november 1856, brooklyn","where":"thoreau's letter to his friend harrison blake, december 7, 1856.","who":["thoreau","whitman"],"year":1856},{"also":["paul brunton"],"key":"brunton","lines":"paul brunton was an english journalist touring india in 1930 and 1931, hunting holy men, skeptical and a bit of a showman. at tiruvannamalai, the temple town in south india at the foot of the holy hill arunachala, he came into the hall where [[ramana]] sat with visitors and meant to fire off his questions. by his own account he couldn't. he sat there and the questions went quiet.\na search in secret india came out in london in 1934 and sold. it's the book that sent the westerners to the hill. the man who hadn't left arunachala in thirty five years started getting visitors from everywhere, because a journalist wrote a few chapters about sitting in a room.","status":"approved","title":"a london journalist sits in the hall and his questions go quiet.","when":"1931, tiruvannamalai, then london, 1934","where":"a search in secret india, paul brunton, the chapters on the hill.","who":["ramana"],"year":1931},{"also":[],"key":"buddha-laotzu","lines":"tradition puts [[buddha|the buddha]], the indian prince who walked out of his palace and founded buddhism, and [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the chinese sage said to have written the tao te ching, a short book of poems about going with the grain of things, in the same century. one in india, one in china, neither aware of the other. that's probably tidy nonsense. it doesn't matter.\na thousand years later buddhism walked into china and met taoism, lao tzu's way, and what came out of that marriage was chan, which the japanese call zen. zen is the branch of buddhism that's mostly sitting still and paying attention. the sitting is the buddha's. the not-forcing, the water, the laughing at rules, that's lao tzu.\nwhen [[watts|alan watts]], the englishman who explained zen on california radio in the fifties, explained zen to america, half of what he was explaining was the tao te ching with a shaved head.","status":"approved","title":"they never met, and their kid is zen.","when":"the sixth century bc, by tradition","where":"the tao te ching, chapter 1. the dhammapada, a short early collection of the buddha's sayings. then the way of zen, alan watts's 1957 book, chapter 1, for how the two met.","who":["buddha","laotzu"],"year":-480},{"also":["dwight goddard"],"key":"buddhist-bible","lines":"in 1954 [[kerouac]] found a buddhist bible, a fat anthology of sutras, the talks the buddha is supposed to have given, put into english by an american named dwight goddard in 1932. it took him over. he read it over and over, carried it around, and started the notebooks that became some of the dharma, hundreds of pages of notes on [[buddha|the buddha]] and his own head.\nin 1955 he wrote wake up, a life of the buddha, retelling the story straight out of goddard's pages. it sat unpublished until 2008. he never stopped crossing himself either.","status":"approved","title":"a catholic finds the sutras in a library.","when":"1954","where":"wake up: a life of the buddha. some of the dharma. goddard's a buddhist bible.","who":["kerouac","buddha"],"year":1954},{"also":["coretta scott king"],"key":"capitol","lines":"[[malcolmx|malcolm x]] and [[king|martin luther king]] both came to the capitol to watch the senate fight over the civil rights act, the law that outlawed segregation in public places and discrimination in hiring. after king's press conference malcolm walked up, they shook hands and smiled, the cameras went off, and it was over in about a minute. it's the only time they met.\nin february 1965 malcolm went to selma while king was in jail there, and he told coretta scott king, king's wife, he hadn't come to make things harder for her husband. he said that if white people knew what the alternative was, meaning him, they might be more willing to hear king. seventeen days later malcolm was dead. three years after that, king was.","status":"approved","title":"one handshake, one photograph.","when":"march 26, 1964, washington, then february 1965, selma","where":"the photographs from that day. coretta scott king's my life with martin luther king jr. peniel joseph's the sword and the shield, which is about the two of them.","who":["malcolmx","king"],"year":1964},{"also":["richard henry pratt"],"key":"carlisle-three","lines":"the carlisle indian industrial school opened in 1879, founded by an army officer, richard henry pratt, to kill the indian and save the man, meaning erase the native child and keep the body. more than ten thousand children went through it before it closed in 1918.\n[[standingbear|luther standing bear]] was in the first class, in 1879, and picked his name off a wall. [[zitkalasa|zitkala-ša]] taught music there from 1897, then wrote about schools like it in the atlantic, and pratt threw her out. [[kimmerer|robin wall kimmerer]]'s grandfather was sent there, and she grew up without the potawatomi language because of it, and went back to learn it as a grown woman.\na student who lived through it, a teacher who told on it, and a granddaughter who went looking for what it took. that's what one school does across a hundred years.","status":"approved","title":"one school, three lives.","when":"1879 to the present, carlisle, pennsylvania","where":"luther standing bear's my people the sioux. zitkala-ša's american indian stories. robin wall kimmerer's braiding sweetgrass.","who":["standingbear","zitkalasa","kimmerer"],"year":1879},{"also":[],"key":"civil-disobedience","lines":"[[thoreau]] spent one night in the concord jail in 1846 for refusing to pay a tax to a government that backed slavery, and wrote an essay about it. in 1907 [[gandhi]], then a lawyer in south africa, was leading indians there against a new law that made them register with the police and give their fingerprints. he read the essay and printed extracts in his newspaper, indian opinion. he used the phrase civil disobedience for the rest of his life. he said different things about it at different times. once he said he'd taken the name from thoreau. later he said the resistance in south africa had already started before he ever read the essay.\nin the forties, a student at morehouse college, a black men's college in atlanta, named [[king|martin luther king jr.]] read the same essay. in stride toward freedom, his book about the montgomery bus boycott, he says it was his first contact with the idea of refusing to cooperate with an evil system.\na night in jail over a small tax. it traveled to durban and back to alabama.","status":"approved","title":"one night in a small town jail goes a long way.","when":"1907, south africa; then 1944, atlanta","where":"civil disobedience, all of it. gandhi's satyagraha in south africa. king's stride toward freedom, the chapter pilgrimage to nonviolence.","who":["thoreau","gandhi","king"],"year":1907},{"also":[],"key":"concord","lines":"the rabbinical assembly, the association of conservative rabbis in america, met at the concord hotel, a big resort in the catskill mountains north of new york city, and invited [[king|martin luther king]]. [[heschel]] introduced him. he asked where in america you could hear a voice like the voice of the prophets of israel, and said king was a sign that god hadn't forsaken the country.\nking spoke and took questions from the rabbis. ten days later he was shot and killed on a motel balcony in memphis. heschel lived four more years.","status":"approved","title":"ten days before memphis.","when":"march 25, 1968, the catskills","where":"the transcript was printed as conversation with martin luther king in the journal conservative judaism, spring 1968.","who":["heschel","king"],"year":1968},{"also":["john neihardt"],"key":"crazy-horse-cousin","lines":"[[blackelk|black elk]] was [[crazyhorse|crazy horse]]'s second cousin, and grew up in the same camps. in black elk speaks, the book john neihardt made from his interviews in 1931, he talks about crazy horse the way a boy talks about the family hero. he says crazy horse was a strange man who went about by himself and didn't talk much.\nblack elk also tells of the killing of crazy horse at fort robinson in september 1877, and of how the people mourned. the chapter is one of the main native accounts of it that we have, filtered through neihardt's english.\nblack elk himself had a vision at nine that he told almost nobody about for years. he knew what it was to carry something you couldn't explain.","status":"approved","title":"the boy watched his cousin.","when":"the 1870s, the lakota camps and fort robinson, nebraska","where":"black elk speaks, the chapters on crazy horse.","who":["crazyhorse","blackelk"],"year":1877},{"also":["surendranath mitra"],"key":"dakshineswar-1881","lines":"narendranath datta was eighteen, a calcutta college student who read the english philosophers and belonged to the brahmo samaj, a reform group that had thrown out the idols and the miracles. in november 1881 a neighbor, surendranath mitra, needed a singer for a gathering at his house, and the kid next door could sing. the guest of honor was [[ramakrishna]], the priest from the kali temple at dakshineswar, a man who could barely read. he heard the boy sing and asked him to come up the river. narendra went, and asked what he'd been asking every religious man in the city: have you seen god. yes, ramakrishna said, more clearly than i see you.\nhe didn't buy it, and he kept coming back for almost five years to check. the story goes he once hid a coin under the old man's mattress, because ramakrishna said he couldn't bear the touch of money, and watched him sit down and jump back up like he'd been stung. he laughed out loud at advaita, the teaching that everything is god, this jug, this cup, us. ramakrishna didn't argue. he told him to test him the way a money changer tests a coin, and not to take his word until he had.\nthe testing ended in august 1886 in a rented house at cossipore, with the old man dying of throat cancer and handing the other boys over to the one who'd doubted him hardest. narendra became [[vivekananda|swami vivekananda]]. seven years later he stood up at the parliament of the world's religions in chicago and said what the priest had taught him.","status":"approved","title":"a college skeptic spends five years testing a priest.","when":"november 1881 to august 1886, calcutta and dakshineswar","where":"swami nikhilananda's vivekananda: a biography, the first chapters. the gospel of sri ramakrishna has the two of them talking, from the diary of a man who was in the room.","who":["vivekananda","ramakrishna"],"year":1881},{"also":[],"key":"damascus","lines":"[[paul]] was a strict, brilliant jewish religious scholar hunting down the first followers of [[jesus]], a few years after the romans had executed him. he was on the road to damascus, in syria, to arrest more of them when a light put him on the ground and a voice asked why he was persecuting it. he'd never met the man. he'd only hunted his friends.\nhe spent the rest of his life saying he'd met him anyway, and writing letters to the little churches he started that are older than any of the four gospels, the four accounts of jesus's life in the bible. every account of jesus we have was written after paul had already told everyone what it meant.\nthe guy who never met him shaped how everyone who reads about him meets him. that's worth sitting with.","status":"approved","title":"they never met, and then they did.","when":"a few years after the crucifixion","where":"acts 9, in the book right after the gospels. then galatians 1, one of paul's own letters, where he tells it himself.","who":["paul","jesus"],"year":34},{"also":[],"key":"day-merton","lines":"[[merton|thomas merton]] and [[dorothyday|dorothy day]] wrote to each other for years. he was a trappist, a monk in a strict, mostly silent order, at a monastery in the kentucky woods. she was running soup lines on the lower east side of new york.\nwhen merton started writing against nuclear weapons and the war, the catholic worker, her penny newspaper, printed him. his essay the root of war is fear ran in the paper in october 1961, and the heads of his order were not pleased. she gave him a place to say it when his own order wanted him quiet.\ntwo catholics who'd both lived hard before they converted, and both ended up as the church's bad conscience.","status":"approved","title":"the monk sends his war essays to the soup kitchen.","when":"the late fifties and sixties, by mail","where":"merton's letters to her are in the hidden ground of love, a collection of his letters. hers are in all the way to heaven, her collected letters.","who":["dorothyday","merton"],"year":1961},{"also":["vine deloria sr.","philip deloria"],"key":"deloria-and-the-churches","lines":"[[deloria|vine deloria jr.]] came from a church family. his grandfather philip deloria, a yankton dakota leader, became an episcopal priest, the american branch of the church of england. his father, vine deloria sr., was an episcopal archdeacon who spent his life as a missionary on the dakota and lakota reservations. deloria went to seminary himself.\nthen he wrote custer died for your sins in 1969, with a whole chapter telling the churches that their missions had done damage and that they should stop converting indians and start helping the tribes. in 1973 god is red went further, and argued that native religion wasn't an early draft of christianity but a different way of being religious, rooted in land instead of history.\nit's an argument with his own family's life's work, carried out in public, by a man who knew that work from the inside. he never said it was easy.","status":"approved","title":"the priest's son answers the church.","when":"1969 to 1973","where":"custer died for your sins, the chapter on missionaries. god is red.","who":["deloria"],"year":1969},{"also":["john neihardt"],"key":"deloria-on-black-elk","lines":"[[deloria|vine deloria jr.]] spent his career suspicious of white writers telling native stories. he'd written a whole chapter mocking anthropologists. but in 1979 he wrote the introduction to a new edition of [[blackelk|black elk speaks]], the book john neihardt made in 1932 from black elk's account of his vision and his life.\nhe knew neihardt had shaped it. he called it a religious classic anyway, and said it had become a kind of bible for a younger generation of native people who were trying to find their way back to their own religion.","status":"approved","title":"the skeptic blesses the book.","when":"1979","where":"vine deloria jr.'s introduction to the 1979 edition of black elk speaks.","who":["deloria","blackelk"],"year":1979},{"also":[],"key":"desert","lines":"before he starts teaching, [[jesus]] goes into the desert alone for forty days, and the devil shows up with offers. turn these stones into bread. jump off the top of the temple and let the angels catch you. every time he answers with a line from deuteronomy, one of the five books that carry the name of [[moses]], the old lawgiver. he never says anything of his own out there. he just quotes.\nlater he takes three friends up a mountain and they see him lit up, blazing, and moses and elijah, the great fire-and-thunder prophet of the old stories, standing there with him, talking. peter, one of the friends, wants to build tents and make it permanent. a cloud shuts him up.\nthe new testament, the christian half of the bible, keeps reaching back into the old testament, the jewish scriptures that came first. that's how these books work. nobody starts from scratch.","status":"approved","title":"moses shows up on the mountain.","when":"around the year 29","where":"matthew 4 for the desert. matthew 17 for the mountain. deuteronomy 6 and 8 for what he's quoting.","who":["jesus","moses"],"year":29},{"also":["larry brilliant","girija brilliant"],"key":"doctor-america","lines":"larry brilliant was a young american doctor who'd come to india in the early seventies, by his own telling on a bus trip with the hog farm, a traveling hippie commune, and ended up at the feet of [[neemkaroli|neem karoli baba]], maharaj-ji to his people, at kainchi, his ashram in the himalayan foothills, with his wife girija. the old man called him doctor america and kept telling him he'd go to work for the united nations and help rid the world of smallpox, a disease that killed hundreds of millions of people in the twentieth century alone.\nhe got a job, in the world health organization's smallpox program in india, going village to village hunting cases. the world health organization is the united nations' health agency. the last natural case of the deadly form of smallpox anywhere on earth was a little girl in bangladesh in 1975. the world was declared free of it in 1980.\nin 1978 the brilliants, [[ramdass|ram dass]], and a few others started seva, which means service, a charity that has paid for millions of sight saving eye operations since. the man in the blanket never gave a talk on public health. he just kept telling a doctor where to go.","status":"approved","title":"the old man tells a hippie doctor to go end smallpox.","when":"the early seventies, kainchi, then the villages of india","where":"larry brilliant's memoir, sometimes brilliant. miracle of love, the collection of stories about the old man, has stories from him too.","who":["neemkaroli","ramdass"],"year":1973},{"also":["carl ruck"],"key":"eleusis","lines":"[[wasson|gordon wasson]], the banker who'd taken the mushrooms with maría sabina, and [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], the chemist who'd made lsd, wrote the road to eleusis with carl ruck, a classics professor at boston university. eleusis was a town near athens where for about two thousand years greeks went through a secret initiation, the mysteries, and came out saying they no longer feared death.\nthe book argues that the drink everyone took there had ergot in it, the grain fungus lsd came from. it's a guess. scholars still argue about it. but it's the two men who'd done the most to bring mushrooms and lsd into the modern world, trying to prove it had all happened before.","status":"approved","title":"a banker and a chemist go looking for an ancient drink.","when":"1978","where":"the road to eleusis, 1978.","who":["wasson","hofmann"],"year":1978},{"also":["ralph waldo emerson"],"key":"emerson-letter","lines":"[[whitman]] sent a copy of his self published book, leaves of grass, to ralph waldo emerson, the most respected writer in america. emerson wrote back on july 21, 1855, that he found it the most extraordinary piece of wit and wisdom america had yet contributed, and greeted him at the beginning of a great career.\nwhitman had the letter reprinted in a newspaper without asking, and put a line of it in gold on the spine of the second edition, like a blurb on a movie poster. emerson was not delighted. he kept up with him anyway, and later tried to get him to cut the sex poems, walking with him on boston common, the big park in the middle of the city. whitman listened and said no.","status":"approved","title":"a famous man writes back.","when":"july 1855, concord to brooklyn","where":"emerson's letter to whitman, july 21, 1855. whitman tells the boston common story in specimen days, his prose memoir.","who":["whitman"],"year":1855},{"also":[],"key":"flowers","lines":"[[kabir]] was a weaver and poet in varanasi, india, who spent his life telling hindus and muslims that god wasn't in the temple or the mosque. when he died, the story goes, the hindus wanted to burn the body, which is how hindus handle their dead, and the muslims wanted to bury it, which is how muslims do, and they argued over the shroud.\nsomeone lifted it and there was no body. just flowers. they split the flowers and each did their own rite.\nit's a legend. it's also the best answer anyone's given to the question of who owns a saint.","status":"approved","title":"both sides claim the body and find flowers.","when":"1518, or thereabouts","where":"any life of kabir tells it. the kabir book, robert bly's english versions of the poems, has it in the introduction.","who":["kabir"],"year":1518},{"also":[],"key":"fools-crow-black-elk","lines":"[[foolscrow|frank fools crow]] was [[blackelk|black elk]]'s nephew. both were oglala lakota holy men on the pine ridge reservation, both toured with buffalo bill's wild west as young men, and both carried the old ceremonies through the decades when the government had banned them.\nblack elk rode into wounded knee creek on the day of the massacre in 1890. fools crow was born around then, near the same creek. eighty three years later, in 1973, when activists occupied wounded knee, fools crow was the elder at the center of it, and helped end it without another massacre.","status":"approved","title":"the uncle and the nephew, both holy men, both at wounded knee.","when":"the twentieth century, pine ridge","where":"thomas mails's fools crow. black elk speaks.","who":["foolscrow","blackelk"],"year":1930},{"also":[],"key":"francis-rohr","lines":"[[rohr|richard rohr]], the franciscan priest in new mexico who writes about the second half of life, was ordained in 1970. that means he took vows in the order [[francis]] started around 1209 with a handful of men and nothing in their pockets, promising, like them, to own nothing.\nrohr's whole program is francis's life with the labels on. he named his place the center for action and contemplation, meaning praying and working for the poor as one thing, not two. in 2014 he wrote eager to love, a book about francis as an alternative way, a man who stayed inside the church and lived at its edge, which is also a fair description of rohr.\nthe bird on the ground in rohr's drawing is there for a reason.","status":"approved","title":"a friar in albuquerque takes vows to a man who kissed lepers.","when":"eight centuries apart","where":"rohr's eager to love: the alternative way of francis of assisi.","who":["francis","rohr"],"year":1970},{"also":["maurice frydman"],"key":"frydman","lines":"maurice frydman was a polish jewish engineer who came to india in the thirties, took a hindu monk's vows for a while, worked with [[gandhi]]'s people on getting indian villages to make their own cloth and tools, and sat with [[ramana]] at his hill in the south.\ndecades later, an old man himself, he found [[nisargadatta]], the cigarette seller, teaching in the loft over his house in bombay. frydman taped the talks, translated them out of marathi, the local language, and made them into i am that, in 1973. he died three years later.\nwithout him the cigarette seller stays a neighborhood teacher in khetwadi, his crowded corner of bombay. frydman's the reason the loft filled up.","status":"approved","title":"one polish engineer, two rooms.","when":"the thirties to 1973, the hill and the loft","where":"i am that, the translator's note at the front.","who":["ramana","nisargadatta","gandhi"],"year":1973},{"also":[],"key":"gandhi-gita","lines":"two theosophists, members of a society of westerners who were into eastern religion, asked [[gandhi]], a twenty year old indian law student in london, to read the [[krishna|gita]] with them, in edwin arnold's english translation. he was embarrassed he'd never read his own scripture in the original. it became the book he read every day for the rest of his life.\naround the same time somebody handed him a bible. he couldn't get through the old testament. then he hit the sermon on the mount, the three chapters where jesus says turn the other cheek and love your enemies, and said it went straight to his heart, and that the [[jesus|sermon]] and the gita were saying the same thing.\na hindu who found his own scripture through the english, and his method through a jewish carpenter. the empire that educated him should have seen it coming.","status":"approved","title":"a shy law student meets the gita in english.","when":"1889, london","where":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth, part 1, chapter 20. the gita, chapter 2. the sermon on the mount is matthew 5 through 7.","who":["gandhi","krishna","jesus"],"year":1889},{"also":[],"key":"garden-milan","lines":"[[augustine]] was thirty one, a star teacher of public speaking in milan with a fiancée lined up and a habit he couldn't quit. he was in a garden, sick of himself, crying under a fig tree, when a child's voice from the next house started chanting take up and read. he went back to where he'd left a copy of the letters of [[paul]], the early christian whose letters make up a big part of the new testament, and opened it at random.\nit fell open at paul's letter to the christians in rome, chapter 13. not in partying and drunkenness, not in sleeping around, put on the lord jesus christ and stop making plans for the flesh. he says he didn't want to read any further, and didn't need to.\na letter a tentmaker wrote to rome three hundred years earlier, landing on exactly the one man it was written for.","status":"approved","title":"a child sings, and augustine opens paul.","when":"august 386, milan","where":"the confessions, augustine's memoir, book 8, chapter 12. then romans 13:13 and 14, in the new testament, the christian second half of the bible.","who":["augustine","paul"],"year":386},{"also":["kicking bear","short bull"],"key":"ghost-dance-reaches-lakota","lines":"after [[wovoka]], a paiute holy man in nevada, had his vision on new year's day 1889, people came from all over the west to hear him. the lakota sent men, among them kicking bear and short bull, who brought the ghost dance home to the reservations in south dakota. it promised that if people danced and lived right, the dead would come back and the old world would return.\non the lakota reservations it took on harder edges, like the ghost shirts, painted shirts some believed would stop bullets. [[blackelk|black elk]] danced it, had visions in it, and says in black elk speaks that he made some of the first ghost shirts from what he saw. kicking bear brought it to [[sittingbull|sitting bull]]'s camp at standing rock in october 1890, and sitting bull let the dancers gather there.\nthe government read it as a coming war. police were sent to arrest sitting bull on december 15, 1890, and killed him. two weeks later came wounded knee. wovoka, who'd told people not to hurt anybody, was heartsick.","status":"approved","title":"a peace dance from nevada came east and turned into a reason to kill people.","when":"1889 and 1890, nevada and the lakota reservations","where":"james mooney's the ghost-dance religion and the sioux outbreak of 1890. black elk speaks, the chapters on the messiah and the ghost dance.","who":["wovoka","sittingbull","blackelk"],"year":1890},{"also":["walter pahnke","huston smith"],"key":"good-friday","lines":"on good friday 1962 walter pahnke, a harvard grad student working with [[leary|timothy leary]]'s group, gave psilocybin to half of twenty divinity students, and a placebo to the rest, during a service at marsh chapel at boston university. most who got the drug reported a mystical experience. to measure that, pahnke built a questionnaire from the philosopher w. t. stace's list of what mystics in every religion report, the same idea [[huxley|aldous huxley]] had made popular in the perennial philosophy, and william james, the harvard psychologist, had laid out in 1902.\nin 2006 [[griffiths|roland griffiths]] at johns hopkins ran the careful version, with a proper comparison drug and follow up, and used a descendant of pahnke's questionnaire, now called the mystical experience questionnaire. his results looked a lot like the chapel's. what leary's circle did loosely, he did slowly, and it held.","status":"approved","title":"a chapel full of divinity students, and the study that answered it forty years later.","when":"april 1962, boston; then 2006, baltimore","where":"griffiths's 2006 paper in psychopharmacology. rick doblin's 1991 follow up of the good friday volunteers.","who":["leary","griffiths"],"year":1962},{"also":["guru arjan"],"key":"granth","lines":"[[nanak]], the founder of the sikhs, was born in 1469, when [[kabir]], a weaver poet who mocked hindu priests and muslim clerics alike, was probably still alive a few hundred miles away in varanasi, the holy city on the ganges. there's no good evidence they met, though later stories say they did. what they share is the message. one god, no caste, no ranking of people by birth, the temple and the mosque both beside the point.\nin 1604 guru arjan, the fifth sikh guru, fifth in the line of teachers after nanak, compiled the book that became the guru granth sahib, the sikh holy book, and put hundreds of kabir's verses in it next to nanak's hymns. so a weaver from a muslim family has his songs read out every day in every gurdwara, every sikh temple, on earth.\nand they share a death. both stories end with hindus and muslims arguing over the body, one side wanting to cremate it and the other to bury it, then a sheet lifted, and flowers where the man had been. it's the same legend told twice, which tells you what everybody wanted to be true about both of them.","status":"approved","title":"a weaver's songs go into the sikh book, and both men get the same ending.","when":"1604, amritsar, and two old stories about flowers","where":"the guru granth sahib, the section of songs by saints who weren't sikhs, where kabir's verses are. any life of nanak for the flowers.","who":["nanak","kabir"],"year":1604},{"also":["cornplanter","henry simmons"],"key":"handsome-lake-quakers","lines":"in 1798 cornplanter, a seneca war chief, invited quakers, the pacifist christians from philadelphia, to his town on the allegheny river to teach his people farming and trades. a few young quakers came and lived there. one of them, henry simmons, kept a journal.\nin june 1799 cornplanter's half brother [[handsomelake|handsome lake]], a sick old drinker, collapsed, and woke up with his first vision. simmons wrote it down that summer. that's part of why we know as much as we do about how the code of handsome lake began.\nthe quakers had been preaching sobriety, hard work, and farming, and so did handsome lake. historians have argued ever since about how much he took from them. what's clear is that the code he preached was his own, and seneca, and that it kept the old seneca ceremonies the quakers didn't care about. he may have borrowed their broom. he swept his own house.","status":"approved","title":"the quakers wrote down the vision.","when":"1798 and 1799, cornplanter's town on the allegheny river, pennsylvania","where":"anthony wallace's the death and rebirth of the seneca. the code of handsome lake, as written down by arthur c. parker.","who":["handsomelake"],"year":1799},{"also":["abyssinian baptist church","adam clayton powell sr"],"key":"harlem","lines":"[[bonhoeffer]], a young german theologian, came to union seminary, a big school for ministers in new york, on a fellowship and found the classes shallow. what he found instead was abyssinian baptist church in harlem, one of the biggest black churches in the country, where he taught sunday school for a year and heard the spirituals, the songs enslaved people made, and preaching that came out of grief and got to joy without skipping anything.\nhe took recordings of the spirituals back to germany and played them for his students. it's the same black church world that raised [[king|martin luther king]], though king was a baby in atlanta at the time.\na lot of what he later said about costly grace, faith that actually costs you something, he first heard sung by people who'd paid for it.","status":"approved","title":"a german theologian finds god in harlem.","when":"1930 to 1931, new york","where":"any biography. eric metaxas's bonhoeffer and charles marsh's strange glory both tell it. his own letters from that year.","who":["bonhoeffer"],"year":1930},{"also":["timothy leary","ralph metzner"],"key":"harvard","lines":"[[huxley]], the novelist who'd written the doors of perception about his mescaline trip, visited timothy leary, a harvard psychology professor, in 1960 and pointed him at the mystics. in march 1961 leary gave his colleague richard alpert psilocybin, the drug in magic mushrooms, in his kitchen, and alpert, the future [[ramdass|ram dass]], watched his whole identity come apart and something else stay. harvard fired them both in 1963.\n[[watts|alan watts]], the voice of zen on california radio, was in it too. his lsd book, the joyous cosmology, came out in 1962 with a foreword by leary and alpert. in 1964 leary, alpert, and ralph metzner, a grad student in their circle, published the psychedelic experience, a trip manual built on the tibetan book of the dead, the old buddhist guide read to the dying.\nthe drugs were the door, for all three of them. huxley died on lsd. watts drank himself to death. alpert went to india and found the room behind the door, and spent fifty years saying the door was never the point.","status":"approved","title":"a novelist, a psychologist, and a radio host find the same door.","when":"1960 to 1964, cambridge and beyond","where":"the doors of perception. the joyous cosmology. be here now, the first section, which is alpert telling this himself.","who":["huxley","ramdass","watts"],"year":1961},{"also":["allan richardson"],"key":"huautla-1955","lines":"[[wasson|gordon wasson]], a j.p. morgan vice president who studied mushrooms on the side, and allan richardson, a photographer friend, made it up to huautla de jiménez, a mazatec town high in the mountains of southern mexico. [[sabina|maría sabina]], a curandera, a healer, agreed to let them sit in on a velada, an all-night healing vigil, and eat the mushrooms with her.\nfor her it was the work she'd always done. for him it was the find of his life. two years later he put it in life magazine, the biggest picture magazine in america, and the next ten years of her town went the way you'd expect.","status":"approved","title":"a banker sits up all night in a healer's house.","when":"june 1955, huautla de jiménez, oaxaca","where":"maría sabina: her life and chants, her own account as told to álvaro estrada. then wasson's life magazine article, seeking the magic mushroom, may 13, 1957.","who":["wasson","sabina"],"year":1955},{"also":[],"key":"huxley-leary","lines":"[[huxley|aldous huxley]], who'd written the doors of perception about his mescaline afternoon, spent the fall of 1960 as a visiting professor at mit, down the road from harvard. [[leary|timothy leary]] had just come back from mexico on fire about mushrooms, and the two of them met and talked about the new project that season.\nhuxley's idea was to go quietly, starting with artists, writers, and thinkers, and let it spread slowly. leary went the other way. huxley died three years later, the same year harvard fired leary.","status":"approved","title":"the old novelist tells the young professor to go slow.","when":"the fall of 1960, cambridge, massachusetts","where":"flashbacks, leary's autobiography, which tells it his way. huxley's letters from 1960 to 1962.","who":["leary","huxley"],"year":1960},{"also":["paramahansa yogananda"],"key":"joy-permeated","lines":"paramahansa yogananda was an indian monk who'd been teaching yoga and meditation in america since 1920, out of a headquarters in los angeles. in 1935 and 1936 he went home to india, and in bengal he met [[anandamayi|anandamayi ma]], the village girl whose trances had made her a saint. he asked her about her life. she told him her awareness had never tied itself to the body, and she'd been the same before she was born, the same as a little girl, the same as a wife, and the same now.\nhe wrote it up as chapter 45 of autobiography of a yogi, his 1946 memoir, and called her the joy-permeated mother. it's the book steve jobs arranged to have handed out at his own memorial. for a lot of americans that chapter was the first time they heard her name.","status":"approved","title":"a yogi from los angeles meets the mother full of joy.","when":"1936, bengal","where":"autobiography of a yogi, chapter 45.","who":["anandamayi"],"year":1936},{"also":[],"key":"kushinagar","lines":"[[mingyur|mingyur rinpoche]], a tibetan meditation teacher with monasteries on three continents, walked out of his monastery in june 2011 with almost no money to do the old wandering retreat, leaving everything and living on the road as a beggar monk. not long after he was at kushinagar, the town in india where [[buddha|the buddha]] died, and he got food poisoning from a cheap meal and lay on the ground for days, sure he was dying.\nthe buddha died at kushinagar after a bad meal, twenty five centuries earlier. mingyur knew it. he says that lying there, letting go of everything, was the happiest he'd ever been, and it's the reason the four and a half years that followed worked.\nthe story rhymes on purpose. he'd tell you he didn't plan it.","status":"approved","title":"he nearly dies where the buddha died, of the same thing.","when":"2011, kushinagar","where":"in love with the world, his book about the wandering years, the first hundred pages.","who":["mingyur","buddha"],"year":2011},{"also":["qin shi"],"key":"lao-dan","lines":"in the book of [[zhuangzi]], the chinese joker philosopher, [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the old master behind the tao te ching, shows up as a character called lao dan. in the later chapters he lectures confucius, china's great teacher of duty and manners, more than once. putting the country's most respected teacher in the student's chair was a taoist joke at confucius's expense.\nat the end of chapter 3, lao dan dies. a friend named qin shi comes to mourn, cries three times, and leaves. a disciple asks if that's any way to mourn a friend. qin shi says the old man came when it was his time and went when it was his time, and if you're at peace with that, grief can't get in. the ancients, he says, called it being cut down from the hanging, as if life strings you up and death lets you down.\nthe chinese say lao-zhuang, the two names run together like one. one wrote the short strange book. the other wrote the one with the jokes.","status":"approved","title":"lao tzu dies in zhuangzi's book, and a friend cries three times and leaves.","when":"the fourth century bc, in a book","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 3, the end. the tao te ching next to it.","who":["zhuangzi","laotzu"],"year":-300},{"also":[],"key":"lausanne-1971","lines":"[[leary|timothy leary]] had broken out of a california prison the year before and was living in switzerland. his lawyer set up a meeting with [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], and they met at the snack bar in the lausanne railway station.\nhofmann liked him. he found him charming and sure of his mission, and also blind to practical dangers and unpleasant facts. he'd already written that leary's publicity had pushed lsd onto young people. he said all of it in his book, kindly, and didn't take any of it back.","status":"approved","title":"the father of lsd meets its loudest salesman.","when":"september 3, 1971, lausanne, switzerland","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 5, meeting with timothy leary.","who":["hofmann","leary"],"year":1971},{"also":["george custer"],"key":"little-bighorn-1876","lines":"in june 1876 thousands of lakota and cheyenne were camped together in montana. early that month, at a sun dance on rosebud creek, [[sittingbull|sitting bull]], the hunkpapa holy man, saw soldiers falling into the camp upside down. on june 25 george custer's seventh cavalry attacked the camp on the little bighorn river, and custer's command was wiped out.\n[[crazyhorse|crazy horse]], the oglala war leader, fought there. so, at twelve, did his second cousin [[blackelk|black elk]], who'd later tell john neihardt about it for black elk speaks, including scalping a soldier who wasn't quite dead.\nfor the lakota it was the high point and the beginning of the end. within a year crazy horse had surrendered and sitting bull was in canada.","status":"approved","title":"the holy man, the fighter, and a twelve year old boy, in the same camp.","when":"june 1876, montana","where":"black elk speaks, the chapters on the battle. robert utley's the lance and the shield. the national park service site at the battlefield.","who":["sittingbull","crazyhorse","blackelk"],"year":1876},{"also":["john c.h. wu"],"key":"merton-chuang","lines":"[[merton|thomas merton]] was a trappist monk, the strictest kind of catholic monk, known for keeping silence, at an abbey in kentucky, and the most famous monk in america after his memoir became a bestseller. he couldn't read chinese. he worked from several translations of [[zhuangzi]] side by side, with help from john wu, a chinese catholic scholar, over years, and in 1965 published the way of chuang tzu, his own loose versions of the stories.\nin the note at the front he says, roughly, that he enjoyed writing it more than any other book he could remember. the empty boat is in it, where you don't get mad at a boat with nobody in it. so is the useless tree that lived because nobody wanted its wood. that same year he moved out of the monastery into a cinderblock hermitage in the woods.\na monk in kentucky, translating a man who turned down a kingdom to drag his tail in the mud. he knew exactly why he liked him.","status":"approved","title":"a trappist spends years with a chinese joker.","when":"the early sixties to 1965, kentucky","where":"the way of chuang tzu, thomas merton, the note to the reader first.","who":["merton","zhuangzi"],"year":1965},{"also":[],"key":"merton-huxley","lines":"in 1937 [[merton]], then a hard-drinking columbia student and not yet a christian, read ends and means, a book of essays by [[huxley]], the english novelist who'd written brave new world. it was the first book that told him mysticism, experiencing god directly, was real and worth wanting. he says so in the seven storey mountain, his memoir. four years later he was a trappist, the strictest kind of catholic monk.\ntwenty years on, huxley had taken mescaline, a psychedelic from the peyote cactus, and written the doors of perception about it. merton wrote to him and said, politely, that he didn't think a drug got you to the same place as prayer. huxley wrote back. neither convinced the other.\nthe book that made the monk, and the monk telling the author he'd taken a wrong turn. they stayed on good terms.","status":"approved","title":"a novelist's book makes a monk, then they argue about drugs.","when":"1937, then 1958","where":"the seven storey mountain, part 2. merton's letters to huxley are in the hidden ground of love, a collection of his letters.","who":["merton","huxley"],"year":1937},{"also":["billy hitchcock","g. gordon liddy"],"key":"millbrook","lines":"after harvard fired them in 1963, [[leary|timothy leary]] and richard alpert, later [[ramdass|ram dass]], moved into a sixty four room mansion on a big estate in millbrook, new york, lent to them by billy hitchcock and his siblings, heirs to a banking fortune. it became a psychedelic commune, part research center, part party, part religious experiment.\nthe local assistant district attorney, g. gordon liddy, later famous for the watergate burglary, raided it in 1966. alpert went to india in 1967 and came back ram dass. leary kept going. the two of them stayed friends and disagreed for the rest of their lives about what the drugs were for.","status":"approved","title":"after harvard, a mansion.","when":"1963 to 1967, millbrook, new york","where":"be here now, the first section, alpert's own story. storming heaven, jay stevens's history of lsd in america, on millbrook.","who":["leary","ramdass"],"year":1963},{"also":["madeleine slade"],"key":"mirabehn","lines":"madeleine slade was the daughter of a british admiral. she read a book about [[gandhi]] by romain rolland, a french novelist who'd won the nobel prize, and in 1925 came to live at his ashram at sabarmati, a community on a riverbank outside the city of ahmedabad. he named her mirabehn, sister mira, after [[mirabai]], the sixteenth century princess who walked out of the palace for the god krishna.\nit was the right name. she'd left an admiral's house for a mud floor, learned to spin cotton on a wheel, which was gandhi's way of getting india to make its own cloth instead of buying british, and went to prison with the movement more than once. and mira's songs were in the ashram's hymnbook, sung at the prayers she knelt through every day.","status":"approved","title":"gandhi names an admiral's daughter after a princess who left the palace.","when":"1925, the sabarmati ashram","where":"mirabehn's autobiography, the spirit's pilgrimage. bapu's letters to mira, for gandhi's side. bapu, father, is what his people called him.","who":["mirabai","gandhi"],"year":1925},{"also":["roger heim"],"key":"mushrooms-basel","lines":"mushrooms from the mazatec country, where [[sabina|maría sabina]] worked, were brought out by [[wasson|gordon wasson]] and the french mushroom expert roger heim, who grew them in his lab in paris. heim sent a batch to [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] at sandoz, the drug company in basel, switzerland.\nin 1958 hofmann pulled out the active chemical, named it psilocybin, and then made it from scratch in the lab. within a couple of years sandoz was shipping it to researchers, including timothy leary's group at harvard. the thing a healer used at night for sick neighbors became a white pill in a bottle.","status":"approved","title":"her mushrooms go into a swiss lab.","when":"1957 to 1958, paris and basel","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 6, the sacred mushroom teonanácatl.","who":["hofmann","wasson","sabina"],"year":1958},{"also":[],"key":"my-friend-walt","lines":"as a girl in ohio, getting out of a house she didn't feel safe in, [[oliver|mary oliver]] took [[whitman]], the poet of leaves of grass, into the woods with her. she wrote an essay about it much later, called my friend walt whitman, about him being the friend she needed then.\nthe two of them have more in common than you'd expect, a lot of grass and a lot of walking.","status":"approved","title":"a kid in the woods with whitman in her pocket.","when":"the late forties and fifties, ohio","where":"her essay my friend walt whitman, in her book blue pastures and again in upstream.","who":["oliver","whitman"],"year":1950},{"also":["white bird"],"key":"nez-perce-to-canada","lines":"when the nez perce fled in 1877, the place they were running to was canada, because [[sittingbull|sitting bull]] and his lakota had crossed there that spring and the army couldn't follow. [[chiefjoseph|chief joseph]] and his people got to within about forty miles.\njoseph surrendered at the bear paw mountains on october 5, 1877. that night about two hundred and thirty nez perce, joseph's daughter among them, slipped through the army lines with a leader named white bird and made it across. sitting bull's people took them in.\ntwo nations that had often been enemies, sheltering together in grandmother's land because it was the last place left.","status":"approved","title":"the nez perce were running to sitting bull.","when":"october 1877, the bear paw mountains in montana and the canadian border","where":"the national park service pages on the nez perce flight. kent nerburn's chief joseph and the flight of the nez perce.","who":["chiefjoseph","sittingbull"],"year":1877},{"also":[],"key":"nobel","lines":"in january [[king]] wrote to the nobel committee in norway, the people who pick the peace prize, nominating [[thay|thich nhat hanh]] and saying he knew of nobody more worthy. nominations are supposed to stay secret. he said it publicly anyway. no prize was given that year.\non april 4 at riverside church in new york, king came out against the vietnam war in full, in a speech called beyond vietnam. the newspapers, the white house, and most of his own allies turned on him. exactly a year later, to the day, he was shot in memphis.\nnhat hanh's government answered by not letting him come home for thirty nine years. they'd both paid. neither of them took it back.","status":"approved","title":"king nominates a buddhist monk for the peace prize, then pays for it.","when":"january and april 1967","where":"king's nomination letter is published by the king institute at stanford. the riverside speech, beyond vietnam, is the thing to read.","who":["king","thay"],"year":1967},{"also":[],"key":"nouwen-merton","lines":"[[nouwen]] came to america in the sixties and read [[merton]], the kentucky monk whose memoir made him the most famous monk in america, the way a lot of young priests did, as the man who'd shown them a way to pray without leaving the world's trouble outside the door.\na few years after merton died in 1968, nouwen wrote a short book about him, pray to live, later reissued as thomas merton: contemplative critic. nouwen's own later path runs straight through merton's. the genesee diary, about his seven months living as a trappist monk. the writing that told on himself.","status":"approved","title":"the young dutch priest writes a book about the monk.","when":"the early seventies","where":"nouwen's pray to live, or thomas merton: contemplative critic.","who":["nouwen","merton"],"year":1972},{"also":["john f. kennedy"],"key":"november22","lines":"on the afternoon of november 22, 1963, [[cslewis|c.s. lewis]], who wrote the narnia books, died at home outside oxford, a week before his sixty fifth birthday. that same day in los angeles [[huxley|aldous huxley]], who wrote brave new world, died of cancer, with lsd in his veins at his own request, while his wife read to him. in dallas, president john kennedy was shot.\nthe papers had room for one of them. lewis spent his life as an apologist, which means a professional defender of the christian faith. huxley spent his arguing that all the great religions point at the same thing underneath, what he called the perennial philosophy. they'd come at nearly the same question from opposite ends, and they went out quietly on the same afternoon.\npeter kreeft, a catholic philosophy professor, later wrote a little book imagining the three of them arguing somewhere on the other side.","status":"approved","title":"three men die on the same day, and the news only has room for one.","when":"november 22, 1963, oxford and los angeles","where":"peter kreeft's between heaven and hell. then a grief observed, lewis's notebook after his wife died, and island, huxley's last novel, side by side.","who":["cslewis","huxley"],"year":1963},{"also":[],"key":"pema-shantideva","lines":"[[shantideva]] was a monk at nalanda, the great buddhist university in northern india, around the eighth century. the story goes the other monks thought he was a slob who did nothing but eat, sleep and take a shit, and set him up to give a public talk so he'd embarrass himself. he got up and recited a long poem nobody had heard before, the way of the bodhisattva, a handbook for becoming a bodhisattva, someone who puts off their own peace until everybody else gets there too. thirteen hundred years later [[pema|pema chödrön]], an american nun, taught it for years, and in 2005 put out no time to lose, her line by line walk through it.\nchapter 6 is about patience. it says the person who hurts you is where you get to practice, and that one flash of anger burns up a lot of good you've done. she'd thrown a rock at a husband who told her he was cheating. you can see why she kept coming back to it.\nthe lazy monk and the furious divorcee, and the book in between that told both of them to stay put.","status":"approved","title":"a nun who threw a rock teaches the book on patience.","when":"the eighth century and 2005, nalanda and nova scotia","where":"no time to lose, pema chödrön. the way of the bodhisattva, the padmakara translation, open next to it.","who":["pema","shantideva"],"year":2005},{"also":[],"key":"perennial","lines":"the perennial philosophy is an anthology. [[huxley]] took [[eckhart]], the german priest from around 1300, [[rumi]], the persian poet, the [[krishna|gita]], the hindu scripture, [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the chinese sage, the [[buddha|buddhist]] sutras, the buddha's recorded talks, and the christian mystics, and cut them into pieces and laid the pieces next to each other under headings, so you could see them saying the same thing in different accents.\nthe argument is that there's a ground under everything, you're not separate from it, and every tradition at its deepest is about finding that out. the doorway isn't the room.\nit's the book this whole forest is quietly a copy of. huxley did it first, in 1945, with better sources.","status":"approved","title":"huxley puts everyone on the same page.","when":"1945","where":"the perennial philosophy, chapter 1, and then whichever chapter title grabs you.","who":["huxley","eckhart","rumi","laotzu","krishna","buddha"],"year":1945},{"also":[],"key":"pilgrim","lines":"[[gandhi]] had been dead eleven years. [[king]] went anyway, for a month, with his wife coretta, and said on landing that other countries he might visit as a tourist but to india he came as a pilgrim.\nhe met nehru, india's first prime minister, sat with gandhi's people, and saw the places gandhi had lived. what he brought home was the certainty that the method worked at scale, and a discipline about not hating the people you're fighting that he kept until memphis, where he was killed nine years later.\nhe never met gandhi. gandhi never met tolstoy. tolstoy never met jesus. the thread doesn't need them in the room.","status":"approved","title":"king goes to india as a pilgrim.","when":"february 1959","where":"king's essay my trip to the land of gandhi, in ebony, the big black-owned magazine, july 1959. it's in the published king papers.","who":["king","gandhi"],"year":1959},{"also":["anita hofmann"],"key":"pills-huautla","lines":"[[hofmann|albert hofmann]] and his wife anita went to mexico with [[wasson|gordon wasson]] in 1962, and in huautla he gave [[sabina|maría sabina]] synthetic psilocybin, the chemical he'd made from her mushrooms, as pills, for a velada, her all-night healing vigil.\nafterward she told him the pills had the same power as the mushrooms, that there was no difference. he took that as the highest compliment his chemistry ever got. hofmann tells it himself in his book. it's also the moment the little saints could be made in a factory.","status":"approved","title":"the chemist brings the mushroom back in a pill.","when":"the fall of 1962, huautla de jiménez","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 6, a mushroom ceremony.","who":["hofmann","sabina","wasson"],"year":1962},{"also":[],"key":"pollan-hopkins","lines":"when [[pollan|michael pollan]], the journalist who'd written about food and plants, started looking into psychedelics, the place he went first was [[griffiths|roland griffiths]]'s lab at johns hopkins. he interviewed griffiths and the volunteers from the cancer studies.\nhow to change your mind, from 2018, opens on that work. griffiths is the steady center of the book, the careful scientist who makes the rest of it credible, and pollan's book is how a lot of people first heard his name.","status":"approved","title":"the food writer sits down with the scientist.","when":"the mid 2010s, baltimore","where":"how to change your mind, chapter 1, a renaissance.","who":["pollan","griffiths"],"year":2015},{"also":[],"key":"psalm22","lines":"on the cross, mark's gospel says, [[jesus]] cried out in aramaic, the everyday language of his time and place, eloi, eloi, lema sabachthani, my god, my god, why have you forsaken me. it's the first line of psalm 22, one of the old hebrew prayer songs, which is headed as a psalm of [[david]], the shepherd king. the people standing there thought he was calling elijah, the old fire-and-thunder prophet people expected to come back.\nthe psalm goes on to people mocking, bones out of joint, and clothes divided up by casting lots, a kind of rolling dice, and john's gospel says the soldiers did exactly that. it also ends somewhere else entirely, in praise, and people have argued for two thousand years about whether quoting the first line meant the whole thing.\nluke, another of the gospels, gives him a different last line, into your hands i commit my spirit. that's psalm 31, also headed as david's. a man dying in the words of a king who'd been at the bottom more than once.","status":"approved","title":"the last thing he said was a psalm.","when":"a psalm from around 1000 bc, a cross around the year 30","where":"mark 15:33 to 37. psalm 22, all of it, in the middle of any bible. then luke 23:46 and psalm 31:5.","who":["david","jesus"],"year":30},{"also":[],"key":"ramakrishna-doors","lines":"[[ramakrishna]] was a priest of kali, the fierce hindu mother goddess, at a temple on the river outside calcutta. in 1866 he lived as a muslim for a while, under a muslim teacher, prayed the prayers, and had a vision. in 1874 he had someone read him the bible, got obsessed with a painting of mary holding the baby [[jesus]], and had a vision of jesus walking toward him across the temple garden.\nhe came back and said the thing he'd say until he died. as many faiths, so many paths. water is water, whatever word you use.\nhe didn't argue it. he tested it. that's why it landed.","status":"approved","title":"a hindu priest tries the other doors to check.","when":"1866 and 1874, dakshineswar","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the huge record of his conversations a follower kept. swami nikhilananda's introduction to the english version tells both episodes.","who":["ramakrishna","jesus"],"year":1874},{"also":["auguste rodin","clara westhoff"],"key":"rodin-paris","lines":"in 1902 [[rilke]] went to paris to write a short book on auguste rodin, the most famous sculptor alive, the man who made the thinker. clara, rilke's wife, had studied with rodin. rilke stood in the old man's studio and watched him work every day, whether he felt like it or not, and decided that was the secret.\nthe story goes that rodin told him to go to the zoo at the jardin des plantes, the botanical garden in paris, and look at one animal until he really saw it. rilke went and wrote the panther, the poem about a panther pacing behind bars until the bars are all it sees.\nin 1905 he became rodin's secretary and moved into a cottage at meudon, rodin's place outside paris. in may 1906 rodin fired him, for answering letters on his own. they made it up later. the new poems, from those years, are the ones about things, looked at the way rodin looked at them.","status":"approved","title":"the poet goes to learn how to work.","when":"1902 to 1906, paris and meudon","where":"rilke's book auguste rodin. the panther, in new poems.","who":["rilke"],"year":1902},{"also":[],"key":"rohr-bell","lines":"[[rohr]], the catholic priest in albuquerque, built his whole approach on [[merton]], the trappist monk, and says so. the quiet, looking-underneath way of reading the bible, the refusal to pick between working for justice and sitting in prayer, the willingness to say the east got there too. merton's the ghost in rohr's books.\n[[bell]], who'd built a church of ten thousand in michigan, got pushed out of evangelicalism in 2011 for doubting hell, the same year rohr published falling upward, his book about the second half of life. bell has had rohr on his podcast, and the two of them are the main reason a guy raised in church can read ram dass without feeling like a traitor.\na trappist monk, a franciscan priest, and a former megachurch pastor with a podcast. that's the christian side's chain of custody, if you grew up in church and drifted.","status":"approved","title":"the franciscan and the ex-megachurch pastor hold the door open.","when":"the 2010s","where":"falling upward. velvet elvis, bell's first book. the robcast episodes with richard rohr.","who":["rohr","bell","merton"],"year":2011},{"also":["junius rusticus","arrian"],"key":"rusticus-notes","lines":"when [[marcus|marcus aurelius]] was a young man being trained to be emperor, his teacher junius rusticus, a stoic philosopher and senator, lent him his own copy of the notes of [[epictetus]]'s lectures, the ones his student arrian wrote down. epictetus was a former slave with a lame leg who taught that you control what you think about things and nothing else. marcus thanks rusticus for it in the first book of the meditations, his private notebook, right there in the list of the people who made him.\nthen he quotes epictetus all through the notebook, by name. you're a little soul carrying around a corpse, as epictetus used to say. that's book four.\na man who'd been owned, lame, exiled to a provincial town, and the most powerful man in the world reading him in a tent on the danube, rome's northern frontier. neither of them would've found that surprising. that was the point of what epictetus taught.","status":"approved","title":"the emperor learned from a slave.","when":"the 140s, rome","where":"meditations, book 1, section 7, and book 4, section 41.","who":["marcus","epictetus"],"year":145},{"also":["ralph abernathy","ralph bunche"],"key":"selma","lines":"[[heschel]] and [[king|martin luther king]] met at the national conference on religion and race in chicago in january 1963, where heschel opened by saying the first such conference was between pharaoh, the king of egypt, and moses, who told him let my people go. they became friends.\non march 21, 1965, heschel flew to alabama and marched out of selma in the front row, next to king, on the march to montgomery for black americans' right to vote, both of them wearing flower leis. afterward he wrote that even without words the march was worship, and that he felt his legs were praying.\ntwo years later, in april 1967, they both spoke at riverside church in new york against the war in vietnam, at a night sponsored by clergy and laymen concerned about vietnam, an antiwar group heschel had helped start. the hasid from warsaw, the devout mystical jew, and the baptist preacher from atlanta ended up saying the same thing about the same hebrew prophets, the ones like amos who said god wants justice more than worship.","status":"approved","title":"a rabbi in the front row.","when":"january 1963, chicago, then march 1965, selma","where":"moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, heschel's essays, edited by his daughter susannah heschel. taylor branch's at canaan's edge, the third volume of his history of king's years, for selma.","who":["heschel","king"],"year":1965},{"also":["shams of tabriz"],"key":"shams","lines":"[[rumi]], who'd become the best loved poet in the persian language, was then a respected islamic scholar in konya, in what's now turkey, with students and a reputation. shams of tabriz was a wandering dervish, a muslim mystic who lived on the road, and nobody could stand him. shams stopped him in the street and asked him a question, and the versions of the question disagree, and rumi fell off his mule or fainted or just stood there, depending who tells it.\nfor about two years they were inseparable and everyone around rumi hated it. then shams vanished. rumi's students may have killed him. rumi spent the rest of his life writing to him.\nevery poem you've read on a mug is a love letter to a missing friend who might have been murdered by the people who loved the poet. that's the mess under the beauty and it's why the beauty holds.","status":"approved","title":"a wild man stops a professor in the street.","when":"1244, konya","where":"the divan-e shams, the huge book of rumi's poems, which he signed with shams's name. franklin lewis's rumi: past and present, east and west, a scholar's biography, for what actually happened.","who":["rumi"],"year":1244},{"also":["carlos montezuma"],"key":"society-of-american-indians","lines":"in 1911 [[eastman|charles eastman]] helped found the society of american indians, the first national organization run by native people themselves, mostly doctors, lawyers, teachers, and writers who'd been through the white schools. [[zitkalasa|zitkala-ša]] joined, and in 1916 became its secretary and moved to washington.\nthe thing that tore the society apart was peyote, the cactus used as a sacrament by the peyote road that [[quanahparker|quanah parker]] had helped spread. eastman and zitkala-ša both wanted it suppressed. other members said it was a native religion and deserved the same protection as any church. in 1918 zitkala-ša testified to congress for a ban. the society broke up in 1923.\ntwo of the best native writers of their time, on the side that lost the argument, as it turned out. the native american church is legal and has hundreds of thousands of members.","status":"approved","title":"the doctor and the writer in the first native national group, and the fight over peyote.","when":"1911 to 1923, the society of american indians","where":"the society of american indians' history. zitkala-ša's american indian stories for her side of the school years.","who":["eastman","zitkalasa"],"year":1911},{"also":[],"key":"spring66","lines":"[[thay|thich nhat hanh]], a vietnamese buddhist monk, came to america to ask for an end to the war in his country. in late may he drove to the abbey of gethsemani, the monastery in kentucky, and spent two days with [[merton]], the most famous monk in america, who wrote afterward that nhat hanh was his brother, more his brother than many nearer by race and nationality.\non may 31 he sat down in chicago with [[king|martin luther king]] and told him what the war looked like from a village. king came out against the war publicly the next spring.\nthree men from three traditions, none of them in charge of anything, and the most important conversation in american religion that year happened in a guest room and a hotel.","status":"approved","title":"a vietnamese monk meets a trappist and a baptist in the same month.","when":"may 1966, kentucky and chicago","where":"merton's essay nhat hanh is my brother. nhat hanh's own account in at home in the world, a late book of his stories.","who":["thay","merton","king"],"year":1966},{"also":["d.t. suzuki"],"key":"suzuki-eckhart","lines":"d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who taught the west what zen was, wrote a book in 1957 called mysticism: christian and buddhist, and the christian he used was [[eckhart]]. a dominican friar, a member of a catholic preaching order, from around 1300 was the closest thing in europe to what suzuki was trying to describe.\n[[merton]], the american trappist monk, read it and started writing to suzuki. they met in new york in 1964, the monk's one trip out of the monastery in years. [[watts|alan watts]], the englishman who'd go on to explain zen on american radio, had been reading suzuki since the thirties, met him in london in 1936, and built most of his zen on him.\nso the line runs from a german mystic, some of whose teachings the pope condemned, to a japanese scholar, and on to a trappist in kentucky and a radio host in california. the church buried eckhart. zen dug him up.","status":"approved","title":"eckhart, seven hundred years dead, explains zen to christians.","when":"1957 to 1964","where":"suzuki's mysticism: christian and buddhist. merton's zen and the birds of appetite, his 1968 book of essays on zen, has the suzuki dialogue.","who":["eckhart","merton","watts"],"year":1957},{"also":[],"key":"teresa-john","lines":"in 1567 [[teresaavila|teresa]] was looking for friars to start a men's branch of her reform, her push to take the carmelites, an order of nuns and friars, back to a poor, strict, prayerful life. in medina del campo she met [[johncross|john]], twenty five, newly ordained, and about to leave for the carthusians, the strictest monks around. she talked him out of it. the next year he opened the first house, a farmhouse at duruelo.\nfrom 1572 he was confessor at her convent of the incarnation in avila, the priest the nuns went to with their sins and their prayer lives, for about five years. the story goes that once, talking through the iron grille that separated the nuns from visitors about the trinity, the christian idea that god is one god and also father, son, and spirit, they were both caught up in ecstasy at the same moment. when john was kidnapped by his own order in december 1577 she wrote to philip ii, the king of spain, begging him to do something.\nshe was the talker and the fixer. he was the poet in the cell. the reform needed both, and so did the two of them.","status":"approved","title":"a fifty two year old nun recruits a tiny friar.","when":"1567 to 1582, medina del campo and avila","where":"teresa's letters, including the one to philip ii in december 1577. the book of her foundations, her account of starting the convents, the chapters on duruelo.","who":["teresaavila","johncross"],"year":1567},{"also":[],"key":"tolstoy-gandhi","lines":"[[gandhi]], then a lawyer fighting for indians' rights in south africa, read the kingdom of god is within you by [[tolstoy]], the russian novelist, and said it overwhelmed him. tolstoy had cut the miracles out of the gospels and kept one thing. don't resist evil with violence. love your enemies. [[jesus]] with the furniture removed.\nin 1908 tolstoy wrote a letter to a hindu, a long reply to an indian editor who'd written him, arguing that india could end british rule without firing a shot. gandhi got hold of it, printed it, and wrote to tolstoy. they wrote back and forth until tolstoy died in 1910. gandhi named his commune in south africa, a farm where protesters and their families lived, tolstoy farm.\na jewish carpenter, read by a russian novelist, read by a hindu lawyer, who then got read by [[king|a black baptist preacher in atlanta]]. the thread doesn't care about the labels.","status":"approved","title":"a russian count and an indian lawyer write letters.","when":"1909 to 1910","where":"tolstoy's a letter to a hindu, which is short. the kingdom of god is within you if you have the patience.","who":["tolstoy","gandhi","jesus"],"year":1909},{"also":[],"key":"tolstoy-laotzu","lines":"in his last year [[tolstoy]] put out a little book of sayings by [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the ancient chinese sage behind the tao te ching, chosen and arranged by him. he'd also written about the buddha with respect. by then the man who'd been thrown out of the russian orthodox church didn't much care which door the truth came in through.\nlao tzu's big idea is not forcing things, the soft thing outlasting the hard, water wearing down stone. it's the same idea as tolstoy's refusal to meet violence with violence. he knew it.","status":"approved","title":"the count, at the end, publishes lao tzu.","when":"1910","where":"tolstoy's calendar of wisdom, a book he put together with a reading for every day of the year, has lao tzu all through it.","who":["tolstoy","laotzu"],"year":1910},{"also":["charles wilkins"],"key":"walden-gita","lines":"[[thoreau]] had the [[krishna|bhagavad gita]] with him at walden, the hindu scripture where the god krishna talks a prince through his terror on the morning of a battle. his copy was charles wilkins's translation from 1785, the first one into english, and he read it in the mornings. he said so in walden, in the chapter on the pond in winter.\nthat winter men came to cut the ice off the pond in blocks to ship it south. thoreau heard it went as far as india, and he imagined the water of walden melting into the ganges, india's holy river, where a hindu priest was sitting under a tree with the same book he was reading.\na yankee in a cabin and a two thousand year old conversation on a battlefield. the book gets around.","status":"approved","title":"the ice from walden goes to india.","when":"the winter of 1846 and 1847, walden pond","where":"walden, the chapter the pond in winter. the gita, chapter 2.","who":["thoreau","krishna"],"year":1846},{"also":[],"key":"whirlwind","lines":"[[job]] is the man in the old hebrew book who loses his children, his money, and his health in about a week, for no reason he's ever given. he spends thirty chapters sitting in the ashes demanding that god answer for it. god finally speaks out of a whirlwind and answers nothing. he shows job the ocean, the stars, the war horse, the sea monster. scale instead of reasons.\nin the [[krishna|gita]], the hindu scripture that's one long conversation on a battlefield, the warrior prince arjuna asks his chariot driver, who turns out to be the god krishna, to show himself as he really is. krishna does. every face, every mouth, all of time eating itself. arjuna begs him to put the disguise back on.\ntwo books that never heard of each other, from opposite ends of asia, and the same answer to the same question. the question was why. the answer was look.","status":"approved","title":"god shows up and doesn't explain.","when":"two old books, one move","where":"job 38 through 42, near the middle of any bible. bhagavad gita chapter 11.","who":["job","krishna"],"year":-300},{"also":["buffalo bill","annie oakley"],"key":"wild-west-show","lines":"buffalo bill's wild west was william cody's traveling show of cowboys, sharpshooters, stagecoach robberies and real native people acting out battles, and it played to huge crowds in america and europe. a lot of lakota men took the job. it paid, and it got you off the reservation.\n[[sittingbull|sitting bull]] toured with it for four months in 1885, for about fifty dollars a week, and sold signed photographs of himself. [[blackelk|black elk]] joined in 1886 to see the white world, performed for queen victoria in london in 1887, got stranded in europe, and made it home in 1889. [[standingbear|luther standing bear]] toured in 1902 and 1903, including in england, until the show's train wrecked outside chicago and nearly killed him. [[foolscrow|frank fools crow]], black elk's nephew, traveled with it as a young man.\nnone of them were in the show at the same time, as far as i know. but it's the same show, and it's strange to think of the white world getting its picture of the lakota from men who were also, on the side, some of the most serious holy men and writers their people had.","status":"approved","title":"four lakota men worked for buffalo bill, in different years, and each came home.","when":"1885 to 1903, the united states and europe","where":"black elk speaks, the chapters on the show and grandmother england. luther standing bear's my people the sioux. thomas mails's fools crow.","who":["sittingbull","blackelk","standingbear","foolscrow"],"year":1885},{"also":["russell means"],"key":"wounded-knee-1973","lines":"on february 27, 1973, the american indian movement and local oglala took over the village of wounded knee on the pine ridge reservation, where the massacre had happened in 1890, and federal agents surrounded them for seventy one days.\n[[foolscrow|frank fools crow]], the oglala ceremonial chief, was the senior elder behind the protest, took their case to the united nations, and helped negotiate the end. [[lamedeer|john fire lame deer]], the sicangu holy man and sacred clown, took part in the protests of those years too, wounded knee among them.\ntwo men born before the first world war, who'd both grown up in the shadow of the 1890 massacre, standing with kids a third their age at the same creek.","status":"approved","title":"two old holy men at the second wounded knee.","when":"february to may 1973, wounded knee, south dakota","where":"the wikipedia pages on the wounded knee occupation. thomas mails's fools crow.","who":["foolscrow","lamedeer"],"year":1973},{"also":["elaine goodale"],"key":"wounded-knee-chapel","lines":"on december 29, 1890, soldiers killed hundreds of lakota at wounded knee creek on the pine ridge reservation, many of them women and children. [[blackelk|black elk]], then twenty seven, heard the shooting and rode toward it, and saw the dead in the gulch. he was wounded in the fighting that followed.\n[[eastman|charles eastman]], a santee dakota doctor fresh out of medical school, was the government physician at pine ridge. the wounded were brought to the episcopal mission chapel, where the christmas tree was still up, and he and the missionaries pulled out the pews, laid down hay, and worked. when the blizzard cleared he rode out with the search party and found a few still alive under the snow, a baby girl among them.\nelaine goodale, the white teacher eastman married the next year, worked beside him. i don't know that black elk and eastman ever met. they were two dakota and lakota men of the same generation standing in the same snow, one a holy man who'd stay on the reservation all his life, the other a christian doctor who'd spend his explaining that holy man's world to white america.","status":"approved","title":"one of them rode into it, and the other took the wounded in.","when":"december 29, 1890, to january 1891, pine ridge, south dakota","where":"charles eastman's from the deep woods to civilization, the chapters on pine ridge. black elk speaks, the chapters on wounded knee.","who":["eastman","blackelk"],"year":1890},{"also":["laura huxley"],"key":"zurich-1961","lines":"one morning in august 1961 [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] got a phone call at the lab. it was [[huxley|aldous huxley]], passing through zurich with his wife, inviting the hofmanns to lunch at a hotel. huxley, who'd written the doors of perception about mescaline, knew exactly who had made lsd.\ntwo years later huxley, dying of cancer in los angeles, asked his wife laura for lsd, and she gave it to him. hofmann wrote about the lunch warmly in his book.","status":"approved","title":"the novelist calls the chemist at work.","when":"august 1961, zurich","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 8, meeting with aldous huxley.","who":["hofmann","huxley"],"year":1961}],"terms":[{"aliases":["baranagar math","baranagar"],"key":"baranagar","kind":"place","lines":"the first monastery of ramakrishna's monks, which was really just a half ruined rented house in baranagar, north of calcutta, that people said was haunted. the boys moved in after ramakrishna died in 1886 because it was cheap, took their monastic vows there at the start of 1887, and lived on almost nothing for a few years.\nvivekananda later described days of eating boiled rice and bitter leaves, and being happy. it's where the ramakrishna order starts, before the money, before belur.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baranagar_Math","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"baranagar math","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["belur math","belur"],"key":"belurmath","kind":"place","lines":"the monastery vivekananda built in 1898 on the west bank of the ganges at belur, across the river from calcutta, as the home of the ramakrishna mission. he moved the monks in at the start of 1899 and died there in 1902.\nthe main temple, finished in 1938, was designed on purpose to look hindu, christian, muslim and buddhist at once, depending on which side you're standing on. that was the whole idea of the place.\nit's still the headquarters of the order, with monks, a museum, and a steady stream of people who come to sit by the river. think of it as the home office of a worldwide charity that happens to be a monastery.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belur_Math","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"belur math","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["brahmo samaj","brahmos"],"key":"brahmosamaj","kind":"group","lines":"a hindu reform movement started in calcutta in 1828 by ram mohan roy, who wanted a hinduism with one god, no idols, no caste, no child marriage, and no burning of widows. it drew from the upanishads and from unitarian christianity and was the religion of a lot of educated bengalis in the 1800s.\nvivekananda joined it as a college student, before he met ramakrishna. it gave him the habit of asking for reasons. ramakrishna, who worshipped kali as a statue with her tongue out, was everything the brahmos had cut out, which is part of why the kid kept going back.\nthe poet tagore's family were brahmos. it still exists, small.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmo_Samaj","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the brahmo samaj","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cossipore","cossipore garden house"],"key":"cossipore","kind":"place","lines":"a neighborhood on the north edge of calcutta where ramakrishna's followers rented a garden house for him in december 1885, when his throat cancer got bad and he needed quiet and a doctor nearby. he taught from bed there for eight months and died there on august 16, 1886.\nthe young monks lived in the house with him and it was there he made vivekananda promise to keep them together. the house is a shrine now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cossipore","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cossipore","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john henry wright","professor wright"],"key":"johnwright","kind":"person","lines":"a professor of greek at harvard who met vivekananda in august 1893 at a farm in massachusetts, a few weeks before the parliament of the world's religions in chicago, and spent a few hours talking with him.\nwhen he heard the monk had no credentials to speak at the parliament, he wrote to the organizers that asking this man for credentials was like asking the sun to state its right to shine, and bought him the train ticket to chicago. without him there's no speech.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Henry_Wright","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john henry wright","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kanyakumari","cape comorin"],"key":"kanyakumari","kind":"place","lines":"the town at the very southern tip of india, where the bay of bengal, the indian ocean and the arabian sea run together. the british called it cape comorin. it's named for a virgin goddess whose temple is there, and pilgrims have been going for a very long time.\nin december 1892 vivekananda, at the end of five years of walking, swam out to a rock just offshore and sat on it for three days. that's where he decided to go to america. there's a memorial on the rock now and ferries run out to it all day.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanyakumari","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kanyakumari","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["khetri","ajit singh","raja of khetri"],"key":"khetri","kind":"place","lines":"a small princely state in rajasthan, in the north of india, whose ruler, raja ajit singh, met vivekananda on his wanderings in 1891 and became his patron and friend.\nthe raja paid for part of the trip to america, sent money to vivekananda's mother when he heard she was struggling, and, the story goes, suggested the name vivekananda just before the monk sailed in 1893. viveka means discernment, the ability to tell what's real from what isn't, and ananda means bliss.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Singh_of_Khetri","why":"the raja himself"}],"name":"khetri","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["narendranath datta","narendranath","narendra"],"key":"narendra","kind":"person","lines":"the name vivekananda was born with, in calcutta in 1863. he was narendra for the first thirty years of his life, which covers the whole time he knew ramakrishna. the old man never once called him vivekananda. he died before the name existed.\nthe monk's name came later, around the time he sailed for america in 1893. so in any story set at dakshineswar, narendra is the college kid asking hard questions, and vivekananda is who he turned into.\nsame man. think of it like a fighter's name before he went pro.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Vivekananda","why":"the plain overview of his life, early years included"}],"name":"narendranath datta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["swami nikhilananda","nikhilananda"],"key":"nikhilananda","kind":"person","lines":"a monk of the ramakrishna order, born in bengal in 1895, who was sent to america in 1931 and started the ramakrishna-vivekananda center in new york in 1933. he died in 1973.\nhe's a big part of why english readers have these stories at all. in 1942 he published his translation of the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the thousand page record of the old man's conversations, and he also wrote a plain biography of vivekananda. joseph campbell, the mythology writer, helped him with the english of that translation.\nhe's a monk writing about his own family, so he's warm and he's inside it. read him for the stories the way the order tells them, and know that's what you're getting.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Nikhilananda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"swami nikhilananda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sister nivedita","nivedita","margaret noble"],"key":"nivedita","kind":"person","lines":"margaret noble, an irish schoolteacher in london, who heard vivekananda speak there in 1895 and couldn't let it go. she followed him to india in 1898, took vows, and he named her nivedita, the dedicated one.\nshe opened a school for girls in a poor part of calcutta that same year, nursed people through a plague outbreak in 1899, and wrote books about indian life and about her teacher. she also got involved in indian independence politics, which the monks kept at arm's length.\nshe died in darjeeling in 1911, at forty three. she's the reason a lot of indians first heard the case for educating girls made out loud.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sister_Nivedita","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sister nivedita","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["raja yoga"],"key":"rajayoga","kind":"book","lines":"vivekananda's 1896 book, put together from lectures he gave in new york and a translation of the yoga sutras of patanjali, the old indian manual on stilling the mind. raja means royal, so, the royal path: meditation.\nit's the first book that explained hindu meditation to americans in plain english, step by step, with the claim that religion is something you can test yourself, like an experiment, instead of taking on faith. that claim is why william james read it and why a hundred years of american yoga and meditation books descend from it.\nit's short and blunt and still in print. the preface opens with the line about every soul being potentially divine.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raja_Yoga_(book)","why":"what's in it and where it came from"}],"name":"raja yoga","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["psilocybin"],"key":"psilocybin","kind":"word","lines":"the chemical in magic mushrooms that does the tripping. about two hundred kinds of mushroom make it, and people in mexico were eating them in ceremonies long before anyone at harvard heard of it.\nfor an afternoon it scrambles the brain's usual filing system. colors, time, and the sense of being a separate me all go loose. some people call it the most important day of their lives. some people have the worst day of their lives.\nit was banned in the us in 1970. now it's back in hospital trials for depression and end-of-life fear, which is roughly where timothy leary and richard alpert thought it belonged in 1961, minus the parties.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psilocybin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"psilocybin","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["lsd","acid"],"key":"lsd","kind":"word","lines":"a drug a swiss chemist named albert hofmann made in a lab in 1938 and accidentally dosed himself with in 1943. a speck of it, less than a grain of salt, knocks you into ten or twelve hours of changed perception.\nin the fifties the cia and a lot of psychiatrists experimented with it. by the sixties it was the drug of the counterculture, and the us outlawed it in 1968.\non these pages it's the thing huxley took on his deathbed, the thing watts wrote a book about, and the thing ram dass carried to india in a bag. every one of them ended up saying the same thing about it, more or less. it shows you the door. it doesn't walk you through.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lysergic_acid_diethylamide","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lsd","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["mescaline"],"key":"mescaline","kind":"word","lines":"the psychedelic in peyote, a small spineless cactus from mexico and texas. native american churches have used peyote in all-night prayer ceremonies for a long time, and still do, legally.\naldous huxley took a dose of the pure chemical in his los angeles living room in may 1953 and spent the afternoon staring at the folds in his trousers like they were the face of god. the doors of perception is his report on that day.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mescaline","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mescaline","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["peyote"],"key":"peyote","kind":"word","lines":"a small, round, button-shaped cactus that grows in the desert in texas and northern mexico. it contains mescaline.\nindigenous people in mexico have used it in ceremonies for thousands of years, and the native american church uses it as a sacrament today, a bit like communion wine, with legal protection to do it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peyote","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"peyote","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["psychedelics","psychedelic","psychedelic drugs"],"key":"psychedelics","kind":"word","lines":"the family of drugs that change how you perceive everything for a few hours. lsd, psilocybin from mushrooms, and mescaline from cactus are the big ones. the word was coined in 1956 and means mind-revealing.\nthey're not like alcohol or opioids. they don't make you numb. they tend to make you feel like the walls between you and everything else have gone thin, which is why so many of the people on these pages took them looking for god.\nbanned in the late sixties, they're back now in serious medical research for depression, ptsd, and addiction. the old argument on these pages, whether a drug can show you the real thing or only a picture of it, is still going.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychedelic_drug","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"psychedelics","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["ralph metzner","metzner"],"key":"metzner","kind":"person","lines":"a harvard grad student in timothy leary and richard alpert's circle in the early sixties. with them he wrote the psychedelic experience in 1964, the trip manual built on the tibetan book of the dead.\nhe stayed a psychologist and writer on consciousness for the rest of his life, quieter than leary. he died in 2019.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Metzner","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ralph metzner","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["the psychedelic experience","psychedelic experience"],"key":"psychedelicexperience","kind":"book","lines":"a 1964 guidebook for taking lsd, written by timothy leary, ralph metzner, and richard alpert, who'd later be ram dass. they took the tibetan book of the dead, the old buddhist text read aloud to dying people, and rewrote it as instructions for a trip.\nthe idea was that a trip is a kind of small death of the ego, and the old tibetan map for dying would get you through it. john lennon lifted lines from it for the beatles song tomorrow never knows.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Psychedelic_Experience","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the psychedelic experience","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["harvard","harvard university"],"key":"harvard","kind":"place","lines":"the oldest and most famous university in america, founded in 1636 in cambridge, massachusetts, across the river from boston. getting a job teaching there is about as high as an american academic career goes.\non these pages it matters because of 1960 to 1963, when two of its psychologists, timothy leary and richard alpert, ran experiments giving psilocybin to students and prisoners and divinity students. harvard fired them both in 1963, and the sixties sort of started there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_University","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Psilocybin_Project","why":"the drug experiments that got leary and alpert fired"}],"name":"harvard","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["guru","gurus"],"key":"guru","kind":"word","lines":"in india, a guru is a spiritual teacher you commit to, the person whose job is to get you from where you are to where you need to be. the word means something like the one who removes darkness.\nit's personal. you don't take a class from a guru. you give yourself to one, and they're supposed to see you better than you see yourself. that's why it can go so right, and why it goes so wrong when the guru is a fraud.\nin english the word got watered down to mean any expert, a marketing guru, a fitness guru. on these pages it means the real old thing.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guru","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"guru","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ashram","ashrams"],"key":"ashram","kind":"place","lines":"in india, an ashram is a place where people live around a teacher to practice. somewhere between a monastery and a commune. people sleep there, eat together, work, meditate, and listen when the teacher talks.\nsome are huge and organized. some are a few huts around one old man. you usually don't pay rent. you help out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashram","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ashram","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mantra","mantras"],"key":"mantra","kind":"word","lines":"a word or short phrase you repeat over and over to pray or meditate. it comes from hinduism, and buddhists use them too. om is the most famous one.\nthe idea is that the sound itself does something, and saying it again and again keeps your mind from wandering off. a rosary does the same job for catholics.\nin english it's slid into meaning any slogan you repeat to yourself, like a work motto. the old kind is closer to a lifeline than a slogan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mantra","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mantra","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hanuman"],"key":"hanuman","kind":"person","lines":"the monkey god of hindu stories. in the ramayana, india's great old epic, he's the servant of the god-king rama, strong enough to leap across the ocean to find rama's kidnapped wife and humble enough to want nothing for it.\nhe's the picture of devotion without ego, the perfect servant. that's why neem karoli baba built temples to him, and why ram dass, whose name means servant of god, named his foundation after him. you'll see his statue all over india, often painted orange.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanuman","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hanuman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["theosophists","theosophist","theosophy","theosophical society"],"key":"theosophy","kind":"group","lines":"a movement started in new york in 1875 by a russian woman named helena blavatsky and an american colonel named henry olcott. they mixed hindu and buddhist ideas with séances and secret masters and a lot of their own imagination.\nthey were westerners who were into eastern religion before it was a thing. some of it was nonsense. but they also got a lot of english-speaking people, and even some indians, to take india's own scriptures seriously. that's how a young gandhi ended up reading the gita in english in london.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theosophy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"theosophy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["edwin arnold"],"key":"edwinarnold","kind":"person","lines":"an english poet and newspaper editor in the 1800s who translated eastern scriptures into victorian english verse. his book on the buddha, the light of asia, was a huge bestseller.\nhis english version of the gita, called the song celestial, is the one a young gandhi read in london with two theosophists. he read his own country's scripture first in an englishman's poetry.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwin_Arnold","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"edwin arnold","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["varanasi","benares","banaras"],"key":"varanasi","kind":"place","lines":"one of the oldest cities on earth, on the ganges river in northern india. it's the holiest city in hinduism. people come there to die, because dying there is said to free you from being reborn.\nthe riverbank is lined with stone steps where people bathe and where the dead are burned on open fires day and night. it's crowded and loud and smoky and nobody who goes forgets it. the buddha gave his first teaching just outside it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Varanasi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"varanasi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ganges","the ganges","ganga"],"key":"ganges","kind":"place","lines":"the big river of northern india, flowing out of the himalayas and across the plains to the bay of bengal. hindus treat it as a goddess, ganga, and bathing in it is said to wash away wrongs.\nit's also one of the most polluted rivers in the world, which is part of what makes it india. holy and filthy in the same stretch of water.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganges","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ganges","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dakshineswar"],"key":"dakshineswar","kind":"place","lines":"a temple complex on the river just north of calcutta, built in the 1850s by a rich widow and dedicated to kali, the fierce mother goddess. ramakrishna was a priest there most of his life.\nit's where he did his experiments with other religions, and where the young men who'd carry his ideas to the west came to sit with him. it's still a busy temple.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakshineswar_Kali_Temple","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dakshineswar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the gospel of sri ramakrishna","gospel of sri ramakrishna"],"key":"gospelramakrishna","kind":"book","lines":"a huge record of ramakrishna's conversations from the last few years of his life, written down by a schoolteacher follower called mahendranath gupta, who signed himself m. it's in bengali, and the standard english version is a thick book from the 1940s.\nit reads like a transcript. people show up, ask him things, he sings, jokes, falls into trances, and says the same big thing a hundred ways. it's closer to a podcast archive than a scripture.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gospel_of_Sri_Ramakrishna","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tibetan buddhism","tibetan buddhist"],"key":"tibetanbuddhism","kind":"group","lines":"the kind of buddhism that grew up in tibet from the 700s on, and spread across the himalayas and mongolia. it keeps everything in the older indian buddhism and adds a lot. chanting, visualizing, big colorful paintings, rituals, and a huge emphasis on having a teacher.\nit's the buddhism with the red robes and the dalai lama. since china took over tibet in 1950 and the dalai lama fled in 1959, its teachers have scattered all over the world, which is why a lot of westerners' first buddhist teacher was tibetan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tibetan_Buddhism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tibetan buddhism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tulku","tulkus"],"key":"tulku","kind":"word","lines":"in tibetan buddhism, a tulku is a child recognized as the rebirth of a teacher who died. the teacher's students go looking, sometimes following clues or dreams, find a kid, test him, and bring him to the monastery to be raised for the job.\nthe dalai lama is one. so is mingyur rinpoche. imagine being told at four that you're the continuation of a famous dead man, and then being raised to live up to it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulku","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tulku","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rinpoche"],"key":"rinpoche","kind":"word","lines":"a tibetan title for an important teacher. it means precious one. it goes after the name, the way you'd say doctor before one.\nit's usually given to tulkus, reborn teachers, and to senior lamas. when a tibetan says it, it's honest respect. when you see it on a flyer in a strip mall, check twice.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rinpoche","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rinpoche","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tulku urgyen rinpoche","tulku urgyen"],"key":"tulkuurgyen","kind":"person","lines":"a tibetan meditation master who lived from 1920 to 1996, mostly in nepal after he fled tibet. he was famous among teachers for making the deepest thing simple, pointing out the mind to students in a few plain sentences.\nhe's mingyur rinpoche's father, and he taught his son to meditate. a lot of the western buddhist teachers of the last fifty years went through his little hermitage above kathmandu at some point.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulku_Urgyen_Rinpoche","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tulku urgyen rinpoche","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kagyu"],"key":"kagyu","kind":"group","lines":"one of the four big schools of tibetan buddhism. the name means something like the oral lineage, a line of teachers each passing it hand to hand to the next.\nits famous ancestors are milarepa, the cave-dwelling poet, and his teacher marpa. it's known for long retreats and a meditation practice called mahamudra, which is about resting in plain awareness. mingyur rinpoche and chögyam trungpa, pema chödrön's teacher, both come out of it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kagyu","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kagyu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["karmapa","the karmapa"],"key":"karmapa","kind":"person","lines":"the head of the karma kagyu, the biggest branch of the kagyu school of tibetan buddhism. like the dalai lama, each karmapa is found as a child, the rebirth of the one before. there have been seventeen so far, going back to the 1100s.\nthe sixteenth karmapa fled tibet in 1959 and set up in sikkim in india. he's the one who gave pema chödrön her vows. the choice of the seventeenth has been disputed between two candidates, which is its own long story.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karmapa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the karmapa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dalai lama","the dalai lama"],"key":"dalailama","kind":"person","lines":"the most famous teacher in tibetan buddhism and for centuries the ruler of tibet. like other tulkus, each one is found as a child, the rebirth of the one before. the current one, the fourteenth, was born in 1935.\nchina took over tibet in 1950 and he fled over the mountains to india in 1959, with thousands following. he's lived in dharamsala ever since, as the face of the tibetan exile, and won the nobel peace prize in 1989.\nin 2023 a video went around of him asking a young boy to suck his tongue at a public event. he apologized. this forest doesn't give him a page because of that, but the meetings with other people here happened and they're told as they happened.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14th_Dalai_Lama","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dalai lama","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dharamsala","dharamshala"],"key":"dharamsala","kind":"place","lines":"a hill town in the himalayas in northern india where the dalai lama has lived since 1960. the tibetan government in exile runs from there, and the part of town above it, mcleod ganj, is full of monks, refugees, and backpackers.\nthink of it as tibet's capital while tibet can't have one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dharamshala","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dharamsala","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tergar"],"key":"tergar","kind":"group","lines":"the network of monasteries and meditation groups mingyur rinpoche runs, with a main monastery in bodh gaya, india, where the buddha woke up, and practice groups in dozens of countries.\nthe meditations it puts out are short and plain, made for people with jobs and kids. that's the point.","links":[{"label":"the foundation","url":"https://tergar.org/","why":"mingyur rinpoche's own organization, with guided meditations"}],"name":"tergar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the joy of living","joy of living"],"key":"joyofliving","kind":"book","lines":"mingyur rinpoche's first book, from 2007. it's about his panic attacks as a kid and what fixed them, mixed with the brain science from when he let researchers scan his head while he meditated.\nit's an easy read. if you've ever had your heart pound at 3am for no reason, the early chapters are going to feel personal.","links":[],"name":"the joy of living","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["in love with the world"],"key":"inlovewiththeworld","kind":"book","lines":"mingyur rinpoche's 2019 book about walking out of his monastery in 2011 to live as a wandering beggar, and nearly dying of food poisoning in the town where the buddha died.\nit reads like a memoir more than a teaching. a famous teacher sleeping on a train station floor, too ashamed to beg, and figuring out what's left when you take everything away.","links":[],"name":"in love with the world","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["richard davidson"],"key":"davidson","kind":"person","lines":"an american neuroscientist at the university of wisconsin who's spent decades putting meditating monks in brain scanners. mingyur rinpoche was one of his early subjects in 2002.\nhis lab is one reason you now see headlines like meditation rewires your brain. he's the serious science under a lot of the hype.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Davidson","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"richard davidson","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["retreat","retreats","three year retreat","wandering retreat"],"key":"retreat","kind":"word","lines":"in religion, a retreat is time you take out of ordinary life to practice, usually in silence, usually somewhere away from home. it can be a weekend at a monastery or years.\nthe tibetan three year retreat is the extreme version. three years, three months, in a closed building with a small group, meditating from before dawn into the night. the wandering retreat is the opposite, walking out with nothing and living as a beggar on the road.\na silent weekend at a retreat center is the gentle modern version, and it's harder than it sounds.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiritual_retreat","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"retreat","status":"approved","threads":["silence"]},{"aliases":["meditation","meditate","meditating","meditated"],"key":"meditation","kind":"word","lines":"training your attention on purpose. most kinds start with sitting still and noticing something, your breath, a word, a sound, and bringing your mind back when it wanders, which it will, constantly.\nevery big tradition has some version. buddhists made it central. christian monks call theirs contemplation or prayer. the goal isn't to empty your head. it's to stop being dragged around by it.\ntoday it's in apps and corporate wellness programs. the old versions take longer and ask more.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meditation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"meditation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mindfulness","mindful"],"key":"mindfulness","kind":"word","lines":"paying attention to what's happening right now, on purpose, without fighting it. the word is an english translation of an old buddhist term that also means remembering, as in remembering to be here.\nthich nhat hanh made it plain for westerners in the seventies with washing dishes and breathing. in 1979 an american doctor, jon kabat-zinn, built a hospital program around it, and from there it went everywhere, apps, schools, office trainings.\na lot of the modern version dropped the buddhist ethics that came with it. thich nhat hanh would tell you that's like keeping the steering wheel and throwing out the car.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mindfulness","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mindfulness","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["zen","chan","zen buddhism"],"key":"zen","kind":"group","lines":"a kind of buddhism that started in china around the 500s, where it was called chan, then moved to korea, vietnam, and japan. it's mostly sitting meditation and paying close attention to right now, with less interest in scriptures and theory.\nit's famous for its teachers saying short strange things, like what was your face before your parents were born, to knock students out of their usual thinking. a lot of it came from buddhism meeting the chinese taoist love of not forcing things.\nzen is where the minimalism, the rock gardens, and the word zen on a spa sign all come from. the real thing is a lot of sitting with sore knees.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"zen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["d.t. suzuki","d. t. suzuki","daisetz suzuki"],"key":"dtsuzuki","kind":"person","lines":"a japanese scholar who lived from 1870 to 1966 and more or less introduced zen to the english-speaking world. he wasn't a monk. he was a writer and professor who spent years in the us and england.\nhis books in the twenties and thirties were how alan watts, thomas merton, the beat poets, and a lot of artists first heard about zen. he taught at columbia university in the fifties, and merton met him in new york in 1964.\nhe's a different man from shunryu suzuki, the zen priest who started the san francisco zen center. same last name, no relation.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._T._Suzuki","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"d.t. suzuki","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mysticism: christian and buddhist","mysticism christian and buddhist"],"key":"mysticismchristianbuddhist","kind":"book","lines":"a 1957 book by d.t. suzuki comparing zen to meister eckhart, a german priest from around 1300 whose sermons sound strikingly like zen. suzuki argued they were describing the same thing from two sides of the world.\nit's one of the books that got christians in the fifties and sixties to look east without feeling they had to leave home.","links":[],"name":"mysticism: christian and buddhist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["engaged buddhism"],"key":"engagedbuddhism","kind":"word","lines":"a phrase thich nhat hanh made popular in the sixties for buddhism that gets its hands dirty. monks and nuns out rebuilding bombed villages, running clinics, speaking against the war, instead of only sitting in the temple.\nit's now the name for a whole movement of buddhists working on war, prisons, poverty, and the climate. the idea is that meditation you never take outside is only half done.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engaged_Buddhism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"engaged buddhism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["plum village"],"key":"plumvillage","kind":"place","lines":"the monastery and retreat center thich nhat hanh started in 1982 on old farmland in the dordogne, in southwest france. it began with a few refugees and some plum trees and now has hundreds of monks and nuns and thousands of visitors a year.\nat plum village a bell rings now and then and everyone stops what they're doing for three breaths. there are sister monasteries in the us, germany, and asia.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plum_Village_Monastery","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"its own site","url":"https://plumvillage.org/","why":"the community's home page, with talks and retreats"}],"name":"plum village","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the miracle of mindfulness","miracle of mindfulness"],"key":"miracleofmindfulness","kind":"book","lines":"a short book thich nhat hanh wrote in 1975, first as a long letter to a young monk working in the war zone in vietnam. it's about breathing, walking, and washing the dishes with your full attention.\nit's one of the books the western mindfulness world grew out of. you can read it in an evening.","links":[],"name":"the miracle of mindfulness","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["peace is every step"],"key":"peaceiseverystep","kind":"book","lines":"a small 1991 book by thich nhat hanh, even simpler than the miracle of mindfulness. short chapters on breathing, smiling, answering the phone, driving, and being angry.\nit's the one people hand to friends who say they can't meditate.","links":[],"name":"peace is every step","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["please call me by my true names","call me by my true names"],"key":"callmetruenames","kind":"book","lines":"a poem thich nhat hanh wrote in 1978 after hearing that a young girl on a refugee boat had been raped by a sea pirate and had thrown herself into the ocean. in the poem he is the girl, and he is also the pirate.\nhis point is that if he'd been born in the pirate's village, poor and without help, he might have become the pirate. it's the hardest thing he ever asked anybody to feel.","links":[],"name":"please call me by my true names","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["fragrant palm leaves"],"key":"fragrantpalmleaves","kind":"book","lines":"thich nhat hanh's journals from 1962 to 1966, when he was in his thirties, split between vietnam and new york, lonely, and watching his country slide into war. it's him before he was the calm old monk everyone knows.","links":[],"name":"fragrant palm leaves","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["novice","novices"],"key":"novice","kind":"word","lines":"a beginner monk or nun, someone who's joined but hasn't taken full, permanent vows yet. it's a trial period, sometimes a year, sometimes many years.\nnovices shave their heads, wear the robes, do the chores, and learn the rules. it's a bit like an apprenticeship with no guarantee of the job.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novice","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"novice","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["boat people"],"key":"boatpeople","kind":"group","lines":"the vietnamese who fled their country by sea after the communist north won the war in 1975. between the late seventies and the early nineties, hundreds of thousands of them crammed into small fishing boats and pushed out into the south china sea.\na lot of them drowned. pirates robbed, raped, and killed many more. the ones who made it ended up in camps and then in the us, australia, france, and elsewhere. a lot of vietnamese american families today started on one of those boats.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnamese_boat_people","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"boat people","status":"approved","threads":["exile","war"]},{"aliases":["hue"],"key":"hue","kind":"place","lines":"a city in central vietnam that was the country's capital under its last royal family, from 1802 to 1945. it's full of old palaces and temples along the perfume river.\nthich nhat hanh became a novice monk at a temple there at sixteen, and went back to that same temple to die in 2022.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huế","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hue","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vietnam war","the vietnam war","the war in vietnam"],"key":"vietnamwar","kind":"event","lines":"the war in vietnam between the communist north and the american-backed south. the us sent combat troops in 1965, and at the peak had more than half a million soldiers there. it ended when the north took the south in 1975.\nabout fifty eight thousand americans died, and somewhere between one and three million vietnamese. at home it split america down the middle, the draft, the protests, the body counts on the evening news.\non these pages it's the war thich nhat hanh wouldn't take a side in, the war martin luther king broke with the president over, and the war thomas merton wrote against from his monastery.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_War","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the vietnam war","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["exile","exiled","in exile"],"key":"exile","kind":"word","lines":"being forced to live outside your own country and not allowed back. sometimes it's a law or a court order. sometimes it's just knowing you'd be arrested or killed at the border.\nthich nhat hanh lived in exile from vietnam for almost forty years for asking for peace. the dalai lama has been in exile in india since 1959. it's a kind of grief that doesn't end, because home is still there and you can't go.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exile","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"exile","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["the perennial philosophy","perennial philosophy"],"key":"perennialphilosophy","kind":"book","lines":"the idea that all the big religions, underneath their different stories and rules, are pointing at the same thing. usually that there's one ground under everything, you're not really separate from it, and the whole point of life is to find that out.\nit's also the title of aldous huxley's 1945 book making that case. he laid quotes from christian mystics, sufis, hindu scriptures, taoists, and buddhists side by side under headings so you could see them agree.\nif you've ever said all paths lead up the same mountain, you were talking perennial philosophy. scholars argue hard about whether it's true. this forest is built on the hope that it's at least partly true.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perennial_philosophy","why":"the idea"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Perennial_Philosophy","why":"huxley's book"}],"name":"the perennial philosophy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tibetan book of the dead","the tibetan book of the dead"],"key":"tibetanbookofthedead","kind":"book","lines":"a tibetan buddhist text, from around the 1300s, meant to be read aloud to a person who is dying or just died. the tibetan name is bardo thodol, liberation through hearing in the in-between.\nit describes what the mind goes through after death, frightening lights, gods and demons, and keeps telling the dead person that all of it is their own mind, so don't be afraid. the english translation of 1927 got a big following in the west.\nhuxley read from it to his dying wife in 1955, and his second wife read it to him in 1963. leary and alpert rewrote it as a manual for lsd trips.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardo_Thodol","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the tibetan book of the dead","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["catholic","catholics","catholic church","roman catholic","catholicism"],"key":"catholic","kind":"group","lines":"the biggest christian church in the world, with more than a billion members, run from the vatican in rome by the pope and a structure of bishops and priests that goes back to the early centuries.\ncatholics have the mass, the saints, mary, confession, nuns and monks and priests who don't marry. until the 1500s it was basically the only church in western europe. then the protestants split off.\na lot of the christians in this forest are catholic, and a lot of them spent their lives arguing with it from the inside.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"catholic","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["protestant","protestants","protestantism"],"key":"protestant","kind":"group","lines":"the christians who broke away from the catholic church starting in 1517, when a german monk named martin luther protested what he saw as its corruption. lutherans, baptists, methodists, presbyterians, and hundreds of others all come from that split.\nthe short version of the difference is that protestants go by the bible and personal faith more than by the church's authority. no pope, married pastors, fewer rituals. most american christians are some kind of protestant.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestantism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"protestant","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lutheran","lutherans","lutheranism"],"key":"lutheran","kind":"group","lines":"the protestant church that follows martin luther, the german monk who started the protestant split in 1517. it's the main church in germany and scandinavia.\nbonhoeffer was a lutheran pastor. under hitler most of the lutheran church in germany went along with the nazis, which is why he helped start a breakaway church that didn't.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lutheranism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lutheran","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["baptist","baptists","baptist church","baptist preacher"],"key":"baptist","kind":"group","lines":"a big family of protestant churches that baptize adults who choose it, not babies, usually by dunking them all the way under water. each church runs itself, with no bishop over it.\nin america there are white baptist churches and black baptist churches, and for most of history they were separate. the black baptist church was the backbone of the civil rights movement. martin luther king, his father, and his grandfather were all baptist preachers in atlanta.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baptists","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"baptist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["episcopal","episcopalian","episcopalians","episcopal church"],"key":"episcopal","kind":"group","lines":"the american branch of the church of england. it looks a lot like the catholic church, with priests in robes and a formal service, but it's protestant, and its priests can marry.\nalan watts was an episcopal priest for about five years before he quit in 1950.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Episcopal_Church_(United_States)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"episcopal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["church of england","anglican","anglicans"],"key":"churchofengland","kind":"group","lines":"england's official church, which split from the catholic church in the 1530s when king henry the eighth wanted a divorce the pope wouldn't give him. the king or queen is still its official head.\nit's the anglican church, and its branches around the world, the episcopal church in america among them, have tens of millions of members.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_England","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the church of england","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["evangelical","evangelicals","evangelicalism"],"key":"evangelical","kind":"group","lines":"the born-again, bible-first side of american protestant christianity. evangelicals stress a personal decision to follow jesus, reading the bible as true in a strong sense, and telling other people about it.\nit's the world of megachurches, christian radio, youth groups, and worship bands. it's huge, a quarter of americans or so, and since the late seventies it's been tied closely to conservative politics.\nrob bell was raised in it and was one of its rising stars until he wrote a book doubting hell. being pushed out of evangelicalism usually means losing your job, your friends, and your whole social world at once.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evangelicalism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"evangelical","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["megachurch","megachurches"],"key":"megachurch","kind":"group","lines":"a protestant church with more than two thousand people coming on an average weekend, and some get tens of thousands. they usually meet in something the size of an arena, with a rock band, big screens, a coffee bar, and a parking lot with traffic cops.\nthey took off in america in the eighties and nineties. the pastor is often a celebrity, which makes it a big job to hold and a long way to fall.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megachurch","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"megachurch","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mars hill bible church","mars hill"],"key":"marshill","kind":"group","lines":"the megachurch rob bell started in 1999 in grand rapids, michigan, in an old shopping mall. within a few years ten thousand people were coming on sundays.\nit's named after the hill in athens where the apostle paul argued with the greek philosophers. it's not the seattle church called mars hill, which was a different church with a different scandal.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_Hill_Bible_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mars hill bible church","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nooma"],"key":"nooma","kind":"book","lines":"a series of short films rob bell made between 2002 and 2009, each around ten to fifteen minutes, just him walking around talking about god and some ordinary thing, rain, a chair, a traffic jam.\nchurch youth groups all over america played them. for a lot of kids it was the first time christianity looked like it had been made in their own decade.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nooma","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nooma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["velvet elvis"],"key":"velvetelvis","kind":"book","lines":"rob bell's first book, from 2005. the title is a painting of elvis on black velvet in his basement, and the point is that the painter didn't make the last painting ever. christians get to keep painting too.\nit's where the trampoline image is. faith as springs you can take out and look at and still bounce on, instead of a brick wall that falls if you pull one brick.","links":[],"name":"velvet elvis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["love wins"],"key":"lovewins","kind":"book","lines":"rob bell's 2011 book asking whether a loving god really sends most people to be tortured forever. his answer was probably not, that love gets the last word.\nnothing in the book was new. christians have argued it for two thousand years. but coming from a megachurch star it blew up. it made the cover of time magazine, and it ended his life as a pastor.","links":[],"name":"love wins","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john piper"],"key":"piper","kind":"person","lines":"an american pastor and writer, born in 1946, one of the most powerful voices in a strict kind of evangelicalism that stresses god's total control over who gets saved and who doesn't.\nin february 2011, before rob bell's love wins was even out, piper tweeted farewell rob bell. it was three words, and in that world it worked like a sentence being passed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Piper_(theologian)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john piper","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["excommunication","excommunicated","excommunicate"],"key":"excommunication","kind":"word","lines":"a church formally throwing you out. in the catholic and orthodox churches it means you can't take communion, the bread and wine at the center of their worship, until you make it right.\nfor most of history it was a big deal. it could cost you your friends, your job, and in some centuries your safety. the russian orthodox church excommunicated tolstoy in 1901. rob bell's version was a tweet, which shows how the thing has changed and how it hasn't.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Excommunication","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"excommunication","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["denomination","denominations"],"key":"denomination","kind":"word","lines":"one of the separate branches of christianity, each with its own name and rules and headquarters. baptists, methodists, lutherans, presbyterians, and so on.\na denomination is also a safety net for a pastor, a pension and somewhere to go if one church fires you. rob bell's church wasn't part of one, so when he left, there was nothing under him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_denomination","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"denomination","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pastor","pastors"],"key":"pastor","kind":"word","lines":"the minister who runs a protestant church, preaching on sundays, marrying and burying people, visiting the sick. the word is latin for shepherd.\na catholic would say priest. a pastor can usually marry, and in a lot of american churches the pastor is also basically the ceo.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastor","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pastor","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["theologian","theologians","theology"],"key":"theologian","kind":"word","lines":"someone who studies and writes about god for a living, usually at a university or seminary. theology is to religion roughly what political science is to politics.\nthe good ones think hard about what a faith actually says and whether it holds up. bonhoeffer was a brilliant one by twenty one. rohr says a dog will teach you more than most of them, and he means it kindly.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theology","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"theologian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["seminary","seminaries"],"key":"seminary","kind":"place","lines":"a school that trains priests, pastors, or rabbis. usually three or four years after college, with courses on scripture, history, languages like hebrew and greek, and how to run a church.\nthe one bonhoeffer ran at finkenwalde was illegal. the nazis had shut the official church schools to anyone who wouldn't go along, so he trained young pastors in secret.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seminary","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"seminary","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["union seminary","union theological seminary"],"key":"unionseminary","kind":"place","lines":"a protestant seminary in upper manhattan, next to columbia university, founded in 1836. it's always been on the liberal end of things, the place where theology meets politics.\nbonhoeffer studied there in 1930 and 1931 and was mostly bored by the classes. what changed him was the church a few blocks away in harlem.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_Theological_Seminary","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"union seminary","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["abyssinian baptist church","abyssinian baptist"],"key":"abyssinian","kind":"place","lines":"one of the oldest and biggest black churches in america, founded in 1808 in new york and in harlem since the 1920s. in the thirties it had thousands of members.\nbonhoeffer taught sunday school there in 1930 and 1931. the preaching and the spirituals he heard there got into him in a way nothing in his german schooling had.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abyssinian_Baptist_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"abyssinian baptist church","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["adam clayton powell sr","adam clayton powell"],"key":"powellsr","kind":"person","lines":"the pastor of abyssinian baptist church in harlem from 1908 to 1937, who moved the church uptown and built it into one of the biggest in the country. he preached about the poor and about racism from the pulpit.\nbonhoeffer heard him preach in 1930 and 1931. his son, adam clayton powell jr, took over the church and later became a congressman.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_Clayton_Powell_Sr.","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"adam clayton powell sr","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["spirituals","spiritual songs","black spirituals"],"key":"spirituals","kind":"word","lines":"the religious songs enslaved black people made in america, songs like swing low, sweet chariot and go down, moses. they mixed african singing with bible stories, and a lot of them had double meanings about escape.\nthey're the root of gospel music, and through gospel, of the blues, soul, and most of american pop. bonhoeffer carried records of them back to germany in 1931.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiritual_(music)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"spirituals","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["confessing church","the confessing church"],"key":"confessingchurch","kind":"group","lines":"a breakaway movement of german protestant pastors who refused to let hitler take over the church. it started in 1934, when most of the official church had signed on to the nazis.\nbonhoeffer was in it from the start. it wasn't as brave as later legend makes it. mostly it defended the church's own independence and said little about the jews. but its pastors got arrested, drafted, and killed, and it's the best the german church managed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confessing_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the confessing church","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["finkenwalde"],"key":"finkenwalde","kind":"place","lines":"a village near stettin, then in germany and now in poland, where bonhoeffer ran an underground seminary from 1935 to 1937. about two dozen young pastors at a time lived together in an old house, prayed, studied, and played music.\nthe gestapo shut it down in 1937. bonhoeffer's short book life together is about what they learned living there.","links":[],"name":"finkenwalde","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the cost of discipleship","cost of discipleship"],"key":"costofdiscipleship","kind":"book","lines":"bonhoeffer's 1937 book about what it really costs to follow jesus. it goes line by line through the sermon on the mount and asks whether anybody actually means it.\nthe first chapter is the famous one, about cheap grace, forgiveness without change. it's a hard book, and it was written by a man about to pay the cost he was describing.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cost_of_Discipleship","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the cost of discipleship","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cheap grace","costly grace"],"key":"cheapgrace","kind":"word","lines":"bonhoeffer's phrase for forgiveness you hand yourself without changing anything. saying sorry to god on sunday and going on exactly the same way on monday.\ncostly grace is the opposite, forgiveness that turns your life around and asks everything of you. he meant it for a german church that was blessing hitler. it works just as well for the apology with nothing behind it.","links":[],"name":"cheap grace","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["reinhold niebuhr","niebuhr"],"key":"niebuhr","kind":"person","lines":"an american theologian who lived from 1892 to 1971 and taught at union seminary in new york. he was the most famous thinker in american protestantism in the middle of the century, and he wrote the serenity prayer, the one people say in aa meetings.\nhe got bonhoeffer a job in new york in 1939 to keep him safe from the nazis. bonhoeffer wrote him a letter a few weeks later saying he had to go back.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinhold_Niebuhr","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"reinhold niebuhr","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nazi","nazis","nazism","nazi germany","third reich"],"key":"nazi","kind":"group","lines":"hitler's party, which ran germany from 1933 to 1945. they built a police state, started the second world war, and murdered six million jews along with millions of others in the holocaust.\nthe word nazi gets thrown around a lot now. on these pages it means the actual thing, a government that jailed and killed people like bonhoeffer while most of the churches went along.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nazi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gestapo"],"key":"gestapo","kind":"group","lines":"the nazis' secret police. they could arrest anyone, hold them without trial, torture them, and send them to camps. people in germany were afraid to talk in front of their own neighbors because of them.\nthey shut down bonhoeffer's seminary in 1937 and found the papers that tied him to the plot against hitler in 1944.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gestapo","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gestapo","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["conspiracy","bomb plot","july 1944 bomb plot"],"key":"conspiracy","kind":"event","lines":"a secret circle of german officers, officials, and others who spent years trying to kill hitler and end the war. many were in german military intelligence, the abwehr, where bonhoeffer had been given cover as an agent.\nthe most famous try was july 20, 1944, when an officer named claus von stauffenberg left a bomb in a briefcase at hitler's headquarters. it went off. hitler lived. thousands were arrested and around two hundred were executed, bonhoeffer among them in april 1945.\nfor a pastor who'd been a pacifist, joining was the hardest thing he did. he never claimed it was clean.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/20_July_plot","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the plot against hitler","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["flossenbürg","flossenburg"],"key":"flossenburg","kind":"place","lines":"a nazi concentration camp in bavaria, near the czech border. around thirty thousand prisoners died there.\nbonhoeffer was hanged there at dawn on april 9, 1945, on hitler's personal order. the americans reached the camp about two weeks later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flossenbürg_concentration_camp","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"flossenbürg","status":"approved","threads":["prison","dying"]},{"aliases":["concentration camp","concentration camps"],"key":"concentrationcamp","kind":"place","lines":"the prison camps the nazis built to lock up anyone they didn't want, political enemies, jews, roma, gay men, pastors who spoke up, without trial. people were worked, starved, beaten, and killed there.\nthe death camps, like auschwitz, were the worst of them, built to kill as many people as fast as possible. over the war the nazis ran more than a thousand camps of different kinds.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazi_concentration_camps","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"concentration camp","status":"approved","threads":["prison","war"]},{"aliases":["letters and papers from prison"],"key":"lettersandpapers","kind":"book","lines":"the letters, poems, and notes bonhoeffer wrote from his cell in berlin between 1943 and 1945, mostly to his best friend eberhard bethge and his parents. bethge put them together and published them after the war.\nit's where bonhoeffer starts asking what christianity looks like in a world that doesn't need god to explain anything anymore. it's also just a man in a cell writing to people he loves, and that's why it still works.","links":[],"name":"letters and papers from prison","status":"approved","threads":["prison"]},{"aliases":["eberhard bethge","bethge"],"key":"bethge","kind":"person","lines":"bonhoeffer's student at finkenwalde and then his closest friend. most of the prison letters were written to him. he married bonhoeffer's niece.\nafter the war he spent his life collecting, editing, and publishing bonhoeffer's writing and wrote the big biography. without him there'd be almost nothing to read.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eberhard_Bethge","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eberhard bethge","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["confirmation","confirmation class"],"key":"confirmation","kind":"word","lines":"in many churches, the ceremony where a teenager, usually around thirteen or fourteen, says the faith out loud for themselves and becomes a full member. the class before it is a year or two of learning the basics.\nbonhoeffer's confirmation class in 1931 was fifty rowdy boys from a poor part of berlin who'd run off the pastor before him. he moved into their neighborhood.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confirmation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"confirmation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vatican","the vatican"],"key":"vatican","kind":"place","lines":"the pope's headquarters, a tiny walled country inside rome, the smallest in the world. it's where the catholic church is run from, with offices that watch over what priests teach.\nwhen a catholic says rome is looking into somebody, this is what they mean. rohr has had complaints sent there about him for decades.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vatican_City","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the vatican","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cardinals","cardinal"],"key":"cardinal","kind":"word","lines":"the catholic church's top officials under the pope, usually bishops of big cities or heads of vatican offices. they wear red, and when a pope dies they're the ones who lock themselves in a chapel and vote for the next one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinal_(Catholic_Church)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cardinal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["orthodoxy","alternative orthodoxy"],"key":"orthodoxy","kind":"word","lines":"the official version of what a religion believes, the teaching that counts as correct. the word is greek for right opinion.\nrohr calls his own position an alternative orthodoxy. he's saying he's inside the official faith, just living it the way the franciscans always have, from the edge, with the side door open.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orthodoxy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"orthodoxy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["christ","the anointed one"],"key":"christ","kind":"word","lines":"not jesus's last name. it's a title, from the greek word for the anointed one, the translation of the hebrew messiah. to call jesus the christ is to say he's the one god chose.\nrohr's argument in the universal christ is that christ means something even bigger, god showing up in everything, and jesus is the clearest case. plenty of christians find that thrilling. plenty find it wrong.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christ_(title)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"christ","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sermon on the mount","the sermon on the mount"],"key":"sermononthemount","kind":"book","lines":"the three chapters in the gospel of matthew, chapters 5 through 7, where jesus sits on a hillside and lays out how to actually live. love your enemies. turn the other cheek. don't worry about tomorrow. don't make a show of praying.\nit's the most read, most argued-over speech in the bible. tolstoy decided it was the whole of christianity. gandhi said it went straight to his heart. bonhoeffer wrote a book asking why christians who claim it don't do it.\nif you read one thing in the bible, a lot of people on these pages would tell you to read this. it takes about fifteen minutes.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sermon_on_the_Mount","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sermon on the mount","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["elijah"],"key":"elijah","kind":"person","lines":"a prophet in the hebrew bible, from around the 800s bc, the loud fire-and-thunder one. he calls down fire from heaven in a showdown with the prophets of another god, runs for his life, and hears god not in the wind or the earthquake but in a quiet whisper.\nhe doesn't die in the story. he's taken up in a chariot of fire. that's why jewish families still set out a cup for him at passover, and why he shows up on the mountain with jesus.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elijah","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"elijah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["franciscan","franciscans"],"key":"franciscan","kind":"group","lines":"the catholic religious order that follows francis of assisi, the rich kid from italy who gave everything away in the 1200s. franciscans take vows of poverty and are supposed to live simply among ordinary people, especially the poor.\nthey're the brown robes and rope belts, and they're famously less interested in rules than in how you treat people and animals. there are tens of thousands of them today. richard rohr is one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franciscans","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"franciscan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["trappist","trappists"],"key":"trappist","kind":"group","lines":"the strictest kind of catholic monk. trappists get up around 3am to pray, pray and chant together seven times a day, work with their hands, own nothing, and mostly keep silent.\nthe order took its name from la trappe, a french monastery where a tough abbot reformed things in the 1600s. they support themselves by farming and making things, which is why trappist beer, cheese, and fruitcake exist.\nthomas merton was one for twenty seven years at the abbey of gethsemani in kentucky.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trappists","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"trappist","status":"approved","threads":["silence"]},{"aliases":["abbey of gethsemani","gethsemani"],"key":"gethsemani","kind":"place","lines":"a trappist monastery in the kentucky countryside, about an hour south of louisville, founded in 1848. it's the oldest trappist monastery in the us that's still going.\nthomas merton lived there from 1941 until he died in 1968, and he's buried there. the monks still make fruitcake and bourbon fudge to pay the bills, and they still take in people for silent retreats.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbey_of_Our_Lady_of_Gethsemani","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the abbey of gethsemani","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["carthusians","carthusian"],"key":"carthusians","kind":"group","lines":"an even stricter catholic order than the trappists, founded in 1084 in the french alps. each monk lives alone in his own little house inside the monastery walls and only comes out to pray with the others a few times a day.\nmerton wanted for years to leave the trappists for them. his abbot kept saying no. the 2005 film into great silence, nearly three hours with almost no talking, is about them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carthusians","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"carthusians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["monastery","monasteries","abbey"],"key":"monastery","kind":"place","lines":"a place where monks or nuns live together under a rule, praying, working, and eating together, usually for life. christian ones are often called abbeys. buddhist ones are everywhere in asia.\nsome are silent, some aren't. some are huge, some are three people in a farmhouse. most have a guesthouse, and you can go stay for a few days even if you're not religious.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monastery","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"monastery","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["monk","monks","monastic"],"key":"monk","kind":"word","lines":"a man who's left ordinary life to live by religious vows, usually giving up marriage and personal money, and spending his days in prayer or meditation, often in a monastery with others.\nchristian monks, buddhist monks, and hindu monks all exist, with different rules. the ones on these pages range from a trappist in kentucky who couldn't stop writing to a tibetan who walked out of his monastery in the night with no money.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monk","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"monk","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nun","nuns"],"key":"nun","kind":"word","lines":"a woman who's taken religious vows, usually giving up marriage and personal money, and lives in a community of other nuns under a rule. in the catholic church they're called sisters.\nthey've run most of the schools and hospitals in catholic history. there are buddhist nuns too, like pema chödrön.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nun","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["abbot","abbots"],"key":"abbot","kind":"word","lines":"the head of a monastery, elected by the monks, usually for years or for life. the word comes from abba, father. what he says goes, and the monks have promised to obey him.\nmerton's abbot, dom james fox, spent years saying no to merton's requests to leave for somewhere stricter or to travel. and then, in 1965, the abbot let him move into a hermitage in the woods.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbot","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"abbot","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hermit","hermits"],"key":"hermit","kind":"word","lines":"someone who lives alone for religious reasons, cut off from other people so they can pray. the first christian ones went into the egyptian desert in the 200s and 300s.\nevery tradition has them. tibetan yogis in caves, hindu holy men in the forest, catholic hermits in cabins behind a monastery. it sounds like escaping. the ones who stick it out say it's the opposite, that there's nowhere left to hide.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermit","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hermit","status":"approved","threads":["silence","loneliness"]},{"aliases":["hermitage"],"key":"hermitage","kind":"place","lines":"the place a hermit lives, usually a small hut or cabin. merton's was a one-room cinder block house in the woods up the hill from his monastery, with a fireplace and a porch.\nhe moved in full time in 1965 and lived there until he left for asia in 1968. it's still there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermitage_(religious_retreat)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hermitage","status":"approved","threads":["silence"]},{"aliases":["contemplation","contemplative","contemplatives"],"key":"contemplation","kind":"word","lines":"the christian word for the kind of prayer where you stop talking and just sit with god. no list of requests, no words at all sometimes. it's the christian cousin of meditation.\nmonks have practiced it for fifteen hundred years. merton and rohr both spent their lives trying to hand it back to ordinary people, who'd been told it was only for monks.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contemplation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"contemplation","status":"approved","threads":["silence"]},{"aliases":["mystic","mystics","mysticism","mystical"],"key":"mystic","kind":"word","lines":"someone in any religion who wants to experience god directly instead of only believing things about god. the mystics are the ones who say they've tasted it, not read about it.\nevery tradition has them. sufis in islam, the kabbalists in judaism, eckhart and julian of norwich in christianity, the yogis in hinduism. they often get in trouble with their own religion's officials, and they often sound more like each other than like their own church.\nthat's the whole bet of this forest. if the mystics across the world keep saying the same thing, maybe there's something there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mysticism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mystic","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the seven storey mountain","seven storey mountain"],"key":"sevenstorey","kind":"book","lines":"thomas merton's 1948 memoir of his wild young life and how he ended up in a trappist monastery at twenty six. it sold hundreds of thousands of copies and sent a lot of young american men to monasteries.\nthe title is from dante's purgatorio, where the souls climb a mountain with seven levels to get clean. merton later found it a bit preachy. it still reads like a novel.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Storey_Mountain","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the seven storey mountain","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dante","dante's"],"key":"dante","kind":"person","lines":"dante alighieri, an italian poet who lived from 1265 to 1321. his divine comedy is a long poem about a trip through hell, then up the mountain of purgatory, then into heaven.\nit's one of the most important poems ever written, and where most of our pictures of hell with its circles come from. merton named his memoir after the mountain in the middle part.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dante_Alighieri","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dante","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["imprimatur"],"key":"imprimatur","kind":"word","lines":"latin for let it be printed. it's the stamp a catholic bishop puts in the front of a book to say it contains nothing against church teaching.\nthe young merton saw one in a philosophy book on a train in 1937, felt cheated, and nearly threw the book out the window. he read it instead, and it started him toward the church.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imprimatur","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"imprimatur","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["thoughts in solitude"],"key":"thoughtsinsolitude","kind":"book","lines":"a short book merton put out in 1958, reflections on being alone and on prayer. it ends with the prayer that starts my lord god, i have no idea where i am going.\nthat prayer gets printed on cards and read at funerals. it's the one thing of merton's a lot of people know without knowing it's his.","links":[],"name":"thoughts in solitude","status":"approved","threads":["silence","loneliness"]},{"aliases":["conjectures of a guilty bystander"],"key":"conjectures","kind":"book","lines":"merton's 1966 book made out of his notebooks from the late fifties and early sixties, short entries on war, race, prayer, and whatever he was reading.\nit has the famous moment at fourth and walnut in louisville, when he looked at the crowd on the street and suddenly loved them all.","links":[],"name":"conjectures of a guilty bystander","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["new seeds of contemplation"],"key":"newseeds","kind":"book","lines":"merton's 1961 book on what quiet prayer really is and what it does to you. it's a rewrite of an earlier book, seeds of contemplation, made gentler and wider after he'd lived more.\nit's the one to read if you want his teaching instead of his story.","links":[],"name":"new seeds of contemplation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the asian journal","asian journal"],"key":"asianjournal","kind":"book","lines":"merton's notebooks from his trip to asia in the fall of 1968, published after his death. it's got his meetings with the dalai lama and other teachers, and what he wrote at polonnaruwa in sri lanka a week before he died.\nit breaks off in bangkok. the last pages are what they are.","links":[],"name":"the asian journal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["polonnaruwa"],"key":"polonnaruwa","kind":"place","lines":"a ruined royal city in sri lanka, the island below india, capital about nine hundred years ago. its most famous spot has giant buddhas carved out of a single granite rock face, sitting, standing, and lying down.\nmerton stood in front of them barefoot in december 1968 and wrote that everything had become clear. he was dead a week later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal_Vihara","why":"the carved buddhas merton saw"}],"name":"polonnaruwa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["satyagraha"],"key":"satyagraha","kind":"word","lines":"gandhi's word for his method. it means holding on to truth, or truth force. you break an unjust law openly, you don't hide, you don't hit back, and you take whatever punishment they give you.\nthe point is that you keep showing up until the people doing the unjust thing, and everyone watching, can't stand it anymore. it worked on the british empire. martin luther king used it on the american south.\nit isn't the same as being passive. it takes more nerve than fighting.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satyagraha","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"satyagraha","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["nonviolence","nonviolent","nonviolent resistance","ahimsa"],"key":"nonviolence","kind":"word","lines":"refusing to use violence even against people using it on you. in india the old word is ahimsa, not harming, and jains, hindus, and buddhists have taught it for thousands of years.\ngandhi turned it into a political weapon, and king brought it to america. every protest where people sit in the road and let the police carry them off runs on it. it isn't about being nice. it's about refusing to become the thing you're fighting.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonviolence","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nonviolence","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["salt march","the salt march"],"key":"saltmarch","kind":"event","lines":"in 1930 gandhi walked about two hundred and forty miles, from his ashram to the sea at dandi, to make salt. the british taxed salt and made it illegal for indians to make their own, in a hot country where everyone needs it.\nit took twenty four days. tens of thousands joined him. he picked up a lump of salty mud on the beach, broke the law on purpose, and all over india people started doing the same. sixty thousand were jailed. it's the moment the world saw that an empire could be shamed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_March","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the salt march","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["pietermaritzburg"],"key":"pietermaritzburg","kind":"place","lines":"a town in south africa, now a city, where in 1893 a young indian lawyer named gandhi was thrown off a train for sitting in first class with a ticket for it. he spent the freezing night in the station waiting room.\nhe said later it was the most important night of his life. there's a statue of him in the town now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pietermaritzburg","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pietermaritzburg","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tolstoy farm"],"key":"tolstoyfarm","kind":"place","lines":"a commune gandhi set up in 1910 outside johannesburg in south africa, on land a german architect friend, hermann kallenbach, gave him. families of protesters who'd been jailed lived there together, grew their own food, and kept the movement going.\nhe named it after tolstoy, who he was writing to at the time.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tolstoy_Farm","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tolstoy farm","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the story of my experiments with truth","story of my experiments with truth","experiments with truth"],"key":"experimentswithtruth","kind":"book","lines":"gandhi's autobiography, written in the 1920s as weekly newspaper pieces. it covers his life up to about 1920, and it's mostly about his failures, the meat he ate in secret, the brothel he froze in, the night his father died.\nit's strange and frank and sometimes preachy, and a lot of what's in it about his marriage is hard to read now. he wanted to be seen whole.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Story_of_My_Experiments_with_Truth","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the story of my experiments with truth","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["celibacy","celibate","brahmacharya"],"key":"celibacy","kind":"word","lines":"giving up sex, usually for religious reasons. catholic priests and nuns and most buddhist and hindu monks take a vow of it.\ngandhi took the vow at thirty seven while still married, in the hindu tradition called brahmacharya, and made it into a lifelong project. in his late seventies he tested it in ways that people close to him found disturbing, and some left over it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celibacy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"celibacy","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["partition","partition of india"],"key":"partition","kind":"event","lines":"in august 1947, when britain left india, it split the country in two, mostly hindu india and mostly muslim pakistan. the border was drawn in a few weeks by a british lawyer who'd never been there.\nthen millions of people ran for the right side of the line. hindus and sikhs east, muslims west. neighbors killed neighbors. somewhere between a few hundred thousand and two million people died, and about fifteen million were uprooted. it's one of the biggest forced movements of people in history.\ngandhi, at seventy seven, walked from village to village and fasted to stop the killing. families in india and pakistan still carry it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partition_of_India","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"partition","status":"approved","threads":["war","exile"]},{"aliases":["hindu nationalist","hindu nationalists","hindu nationalism"],"key":"hindunationalism","kind":"group","lines":"the belief that india should be a hindu nation first, rather than a country equally for everyone. it's been around since before independence and it's a powerful force in indian politics today.\nin 1947 hindu nationalists thought gandhi had given too much to muslims during partition. on january 30, 1948, one of them, nathuram godse, shot him three times at close range on his way to evening prayer.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindu_nationalism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hindu nationalism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jawaharlal nehru","nehru"],"key":"nehru","kind":"person","lines":"gandhi's right-hand man in the independence fight, and india's first prime minister, from 1947 until he died in 1964. he was a cambridge-educated lawyer from a rich family who went to jail over and over for the cause.\nmartin luther king met him in delhi in 1959. his daughter indira gandhi, no relation to the mahatma, later became prime minister too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jawaharlal_Nehru","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jawaharlal nehru","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["war and peace"],"key":"warandpeace","kind":"book","lines":"tolstoy's huge novel from 1869 about a handful of russian families living through napoleon's invasion of russia in 1812. balls, battles, marriages, and a burning moscow, over something like twelve hundred pages.\nit's on every list of the greatest novels ever written. his wife sophia copied the whole thing out by hand, several times, as he rewrote it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_and_Peace","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2600","why":"the whole novel in english, public domain"}],"name":"war and peace","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["anna karenina"],"key":"annakarenina","kind":"book","lines":"tolstoy's 1878 novel about a married woman in russian high society who falls in love with an army officer and leaves her husband for him, and what that costs her.\nit opens with the line about all happy families being alike and every unhappy family unhappy in its own way. it ends with her under a train. a lot of people think it's the best novel ever written.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Karenina","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1399","why":"the whole novel in english, public domain"}],"name":"anna karenina","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["a confession"],"key":"aconfession","kind":"book","lines":"a short book tolstoy wrote around 1880 and put out in 1882, about the years in his late forties and early fifties when, at the height of his fame, he couldn't see any reason to live. he hid rope so he wouldn't hang himself.\nit follows him looking for an answer in science, philosophy, and the church, and finding it in the faith of ordinary peasants. it's about fifty pages, and it's one of the most honest things anybody famous ever wrote about wanting to die.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Confession","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a confession","status":"approved","threads":["doubt"]},{"aliases":["the kingdom of god is within you","kingdom of god is within you"],"key":"kingdomwithin","kind":"book","lines":"tolstoy's 1894 book arguing that jesus meant exactly what he said about not resisting evil with violence, so christians can't serve in armies, use force, or back governments that do.\nrussia banned it and it got printed abroad. a young gandhi read it in south africa and said it overwhelmed him. it's the link in the chain between the sermon on the mount and the civil rights movement.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Kingdom_of_God_Is_Within_You","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/43302","why":"the whole book in english, public domain"}],"name":"the kingdom of god is within you","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["a letter to a hindu","letter to a hindu"],"key":"letterhindu","kind":"book","lines":"a long letter tolstoy wrote in 1908 to an indian editor, arguing that a few thousand british could only rule three hundred million indians because the indians went along with it. stop cooperating, he said, and it ends.\ngandhi read it, got permission to print it, and wrote an introduction. it's short and you can hear the whole independence movement in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Letter_to_a_Hindu","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a letter to a hindu","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["calendar of wisdom","a calendar of wisdom"],"key":"calendarofwisdom","kind":"book","lines":"a book tolstoy spent years putting together near the end of his life, a reading for every day of the year, sayings from the bible, the buddha, lao tzu, socrates, and lots of others, plus his own.\nhe considered it one of his most important works. it's a daily devotional from a man who'd been thrown out of his church.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Calendar_of_Wisdom","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a calendar of wisdom","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["russian orthodox church","orthodox church","eastern orthodox","russian orthodox"],"key":"orthodoxchurch","kind":"group","lines":"the eastern half of christianity, which split from the catholic west in 1054. it's the main church in russia, greece, and much of eastern europe, with its own heads instead of a pope, priests who can marry, and services full of candles, icons, and long chanting.\nin tolstoy's russia it was the official state church, tied tight to the czar. when it excommunicated him in 1901 it was the state church throwing out the country's most famous man, and crowds cheered him in the street.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Orthodox_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the orthodox church","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yasnaya polyana"],"key":"yasnayapolyana","kind":"place","lines":"tolstoy's family estate, about a hundred and twenty miles south of moscow. he was born there, wrote war and peace and anna karenina there, and is buried there, in an unmarked grassy mound in the woods.\nit's a museum now. the house is kept the way it was.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yasnaya_Polyana","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"yasnaya polyana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["astapovo"],"key":"astapovo","kind":"place","lines":"a small railway junction in russia where tolstoy, at eighty two, got off the train sick after leaving home in the night in november 1910. he died in the stationmaster's house a week later, with reporters camped outside and his wife kept from his bedside.\nthe town was renamed lev tolstoy after him.","links":[],"name":"astapovo","status":"approved","threads":["dying","wandering"]},{"aliases":["sophia","sophia tolstoy","sofia tolstaya"],"key":"sophiatolstaya","kind":"person","lines":"tolstoy's wife, sofia andreyevna, married to him at eighteen when he was thirty four. she had thirteen children, ran the estate, managed the money, and copied out his novels by hand.\ntheir marriage was a long war, and both kept diaries about it that the other read. she's been treated as the villain of his last years and she wasn't. she was the one holding the house together while he gave it away.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophia_Tolstaya","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sophia tolstoy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["doukhobors"],"key":"doukhobors","kind":"group","lines":"a russian peasant christian sect that rejected the church, priests, and all violence. in 1895 they burned their weapons in protest against military service, and the government came down hard on them.\ntolstoy gave the money from his novel resurrection to help about seventy five hundred of them move to canada in 1899. their descendants are still there, mostly in british columbia and saskatchewan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doukhobors","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"doukhobors","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence","exile"]},{"aliases":["civil rights movement","the civil rights movement","civil rights"],"key":"civilrightsmovement","kind":"event","lines":"the fight, mostly in the fifties and sixties, to end the laws in the american south that kept black people segregated and kept them from voting. bus boycotts, sit-ins at lunch counters, marches, lawsuits, and a lot of people getting beaten and jailed and killed.\nit won the civil rights act of 1964 and the voting rights act of 1965. martin luther king was its best known face, but thousands of ordinary people, most of whose names nobody remembers, did the work.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_rights_movement","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the civil rights movement","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["segregation","segregated","jim crow"],"key":"segregation","kind":"word","lines":"keeping black and white people apart by law. in the american south from the 1870s to the 1960s it covered schools, buses, restaurants, bathrooms, water fountains, hospitals, and cemeteries. the laws were called jim crow.\nseparate was never equal. the black schools got less money, the black hospitals got fewer doctors. and on top of it, black southerners were kept from voting by tests and taxes and violence.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_segregation_in_the_United_States","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"segregation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["montgomery bus boycott","the boycott","bus boycott"],"key":"montgomerybusboycott","kind":"event","lines":"from december 1955 to december 1956, black people in montgomery, alabama refused to ride the city's segregated buses. they walked, carpooled, and shared rides for more than a year, some of them miles a day.\nit started when rosa parks was arrested for not giving up her seat. it ended when the supreme court said bus segregation was illegal. a twenty six year old pastor named martin luther king was picked to lead it, and it made him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montgomery_bus_boycott","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the montgomery bus boycott","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["rosa parks"],"key":"rosaparks","kind":"person","lines":"a black seamstress and longtime activist in montgomery, alabama, who on december 1, 1955 refused to give her bus seat to a white man and was arrested.\nshe's often told as a tired old lady who just sat down. she was forty two, not especially tired, and had been organizing with the naacp, the big civil rights group, for over a decade. she knew exactly what she was doing.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosa_Parks","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rosa parks","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["letter from birmingham jail","birmingham jail"],"key":"birminghamjail","kind":"book","lines":"the open letter martin luther king wrote in april 1963 from a jail cell in birmingham, alabama, after being arrested at a protest. he wrote it in the margins of a newspaper and on scraps his lawyers smuggled in and out.\nit answers eight white clergymen who'd said he should wait and let the courts handle things. he explains why wait almost always means never. it's the best thing he wrote, and it takes about twenty minutes to read.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letter_from_Birmingham_Jail","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/letter-birmingham-jail","why":"stanford's page on the letter"}],"name":"letter from birmingham jail","status":"approved","threads":["prison","nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["riverside church"],"key":"riverside","kind":"place","lines":"a huge gothic church on the upper west side of manhattan, built in the 1920s with rockefeller money. it's been a pulpit for big liberal speeches for a century.\non april 4, 1967, martin luther king stood there and came out against the vietnam war in the speech beyond vietnam. a year later to the day he was dead.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riverside_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"riverside church","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["beyond vietnam"],"key":"beyondvietnam","kind":"book","lines":"martin luther king's speech at riverside church on april 4, 1967, where he came out fully against the vietnam war and called his own government the greatest purveyor of violence in the world.\nit cost him the white house, most of the big newspapers, and a lot of his allies in the civil rights movement, who thought he should stay in his lane. he said it anyway.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyond_Vietnam:_A_Time_to_Break_Silence","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/beyond-vietnam","why":"stanford's page on the speech"}],"name":"beyond vietnam","status":"approved","threads":["war","nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["memphis sanitation strike","sanitation strike","garbage workers"],"key":"sanitationstrike","kind":"event","lines":"in february 1968 about thirteen hundred black garbage workers in memphis walked off the job after two of them were crushed to death in a broken truck. they carried signs that said i am a man.\nking came to support them. he gave the mountaintop speech there on april 3 and was shot on the balcony of the lorraine motel the next evening.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memphis_sanitation_strike","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the memphis sanitation strike","status":"approved","threads":["work"]},{"aliases":["i've been to the mountaintop","been to the mountaintop"],"key":"mountaintop","kind":"book","lines":"the last speech martin luther king gave, in memphis on the night of april 3, 1968, in a thunderstorm. he told the story of the stabbing ten years before, and then said he'd been to the mountaintop and seen the promised land, and might not get there with them.\nhe was shot the next evening. people who were in the room say it sounded like he knew.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I've_Been_to_the_Mountaintop","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"i've been to the mountaintop","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["fbi","the fbi"],"key":"fbi","kind":"group","lines":"the federal bureau of investigation, america's national police force. from 1924 to 1972 it was run by one man, j. edgar hoover, who used it to spy on anyone he thought was a threat, civil rights leaders very much included.\nthe fbi bugged king's hotel rooms, tapped his phones, and in 1964 mailed him an anonymous letter, with a tape, that he read as telling him to kill himself. it came out in the seventies, when congress investigated.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Bureau_of_Investigation","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COINTELPRO","why":"the secret program that went after king"}],"name":"the fbi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["j. edgar hoover","edgar hoover"],"key":"hoover","kind":"person","lines":"the man who ran the fbi for almost fifty years, from 1924 until he died in 1972. he kept secret files on presidents, senators, movie stars, and activists, and everybody in washington was a little afraid of him.\nhe hated martin luther king, and in 1964 called him the most notorious liar in the country, in public. he's the reason there are tapes.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._Edgar_Hoover","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"j. edgar hoover","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["selma","selma to montgomery"],"key":"selma","kind":"event","lines":"a small city in alabama where the fight for black voting rights came to a head in 1965. on march 7, bloody sunday, state troopers beat peaceful marchers on the edmund pettus bridge on live television.\ntwo weeks later, martin luther king led thousands, rabbi abraham heschel in the front row, on a five day march from selma to the state capital in montgomery. that summer congress passed the voting rights act.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selma_to_Montgomery_marches","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"selma","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["black power"],"key":"blackpower","kind":"group","lines":"a movement of young black activists in the late sixties who were done with king's patient nonviolence and integration. they wanted black pride, black-run communities, and the right to defend themselves.\nthe phrase took off in 1966. a lot of them thought king was a relic by 1967. he took it seriously and argued with it rather than dismissing it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_power","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"black power","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["coretta scott king","coretta"],"key":"coretta","kind":"person","lines":"martin luther king's wife, a trained singer from alabama who studied music in boston, where they met. she was an activist before she married him and after he died.\nshe was at home with their baby when the house was bombed in 1956. after 1968 she spent decades building the king center in atlanta and fighting to make his birthday a national holiday, which it became in 1986.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coretta_Scott_King","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"coretta scott king","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ebony"],"key":"ebony","kind":"book","lines":"a glossy monthly magazine for black american readers, started in 1945 in chicago, like life magazine but about black life. for decades it was on the coffee table in millions of black homes.\nking wrote about his trip to india for it in 1959.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebony_(magazine)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ebony","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["i have a dream"],"key":"ihaveadream","kind":"book","lines":"the speech martin luther king gave at the march on washington on august 28, 1963, to about two hundred and fifty thousand people in front of the lincoln memorial. the most famous part wasn't in his notes. a singer behind him, mahalia jackson, called out tell them about the dream, and he did.\nit's the speech everybody knows. it's also the one most often used to make him sound safer than he was.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Have_a_Dream","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"i have a dream","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["strength to love"],"key":"strengthtolove","kind":"book","lines":"a 1963 collection of martin luther king's sermons, some of which he drafted in jail cells. it's him as a preacher, not a politician.\nif you want to hear what he sounded like on an ordinary sunday, not at the big march, this is it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strength_to_Love","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"strength to love","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nobel peace prize","nobel prize","nobel committee"],"key":"nobelpeaceprize","kind":"event","lines":"a prize given every year since 1901 from the fortune of alfred nobel, the swedish chemist who invented dynamite. a committee in norway picks the peace winner. it's the most famous award in the world.\nking won it in 1964, at thirty five. in 1967 he nominated thich nhat hanh for it, which he wasn't supposed to say out loud, and no peace prize was given that year. the dalai lama won it in 1989.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Peace_Prize","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the nobel site","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1964/king/facts/","why":"king's 1964 prize"}],"name":"the nobel peace prize","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["a.j. muste","a. j. muste"],"key":"muste","kind":"person","lines":"an american minister and pacifist who lived from 1885 to 1967 and spent his life organizing against every war america fought, and for workers' rights. people called him the american gandhi.\nthe line there is no way to peace, peace is the way is usually credited to him. thich nhat hanh used it and said so.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A._J._Muste","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a.j. muste","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["brave new world"],"key":"bravenewworld","kind":"book","lines":"aldous huxley's 1932 novel about a future where babies are made in factories and sorted into castes, everyone is kept content with sex, shopping, and a happy pill called soma, and nobody wants anything real.\norwell's 1984 is the future where they control you by fear. brave new world is the one where they control you with pleasure. a lot of people think huxley's was the better guess.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brave_New_World","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"brave new world","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["soma"],"key":"soma","kind":"word","lines":"the happy pill in brave new world. one dose and your worries go away, no hangover. the government hands it out so nobody ever gets upset enough to change anything.\nhuxley took the name from an old ritual drink in the hindu scriptures. think of it as a cross between an antidepressant and a phone you can't put down.","links":[],"name":"soma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the doors of perception","doors of perception"],"key":"doorsofperception","kind":"book","lines":"aldous huxley's short 1954 book about taking mescaline in his living room the spring before, and seeing a vase of flowers, some books, and the folds in his pants as if for the first time.\nit made the case that drugs could show ordinary people what the mystics had been describing. the sixties counterculture read it like a manual. jim morrison named his band the doors after it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Doors_of_Perception","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the doors of perception","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["william blake"],"key":"blake","kind":"person","lines":"an english poet and painter who lived from 1757 to 1827, mostly poor and mostly ignored. he saw angels in trees as a kid and never stopped seeing visions.\nhe wrote that if the doors of perception were cleansed, everything would appear as it is, infinite. huxley took his title from that, and it's how a lot of people first heard of blake.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Blake","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"william blake","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["eton","eton college"],"key":"eton","kind":"place","lines":"england's most famous private boarding school for boys, near windsor castle, founded in 1440. it's trained twenty british prime ministers and a lot of royalty.\nhuxley taught french there for a while around 1917 and 1918. one of his students was a kid named eric blair, who later wrote as george orwell.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eton_College","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eton","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["george orwell","orwell"],"key":"orwell","kind":"person","lines":"the pen name of eric blair, an english writer who lived from 1903 to 1950 and wrote 1984 and animal farm. big brother, thought police, and doublespeak all come from him.\nas a teenager at eton he had aldous huxley as his french teacher. so the two most famous nightmares of the future in english came out of the same classroom.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Orwell","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"george orwell","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ends and means"],"key":"endsandmeans","kind":"book","lines":"a 1937 book of essays by aldous huxley about how to make a better world without using the violence that makes it worse. it was his pacifist book.\na young, not yet religious thomas merton read it at columbia and it was the first thing that made him think mysticism was real and worth wanting. he says so in his memoir.","links":[],"name":"ends and means","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kpfa"],"key":"kpfa","kind":"place","lines":"a radio station in berkeley, california, started in 1949, paid for by its listeners instead of by ads. it was the first of its kind in the country.\nalan watts had a weekly show on it through the fifties and sixties, talking about zen and taoism to whoever tuned in. old tapes of those shows are a lot of what you hear on youtube now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KPFA","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kpfa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kqed"],"key":"kqed","kind":"place","lines":"the public tv and radio station in san francisco, started in 1954. it's the local pbs station.\nin 1959 and 1960 alan watts did a series on it called eastern wisdom and modern life, just him in a jacket explaining zen and the tao to the camera.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KQED_(TV)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kqed","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the way of zen","way of zen"],"key":"wayofzen","kind":"book","lines":"alan watts's 1957 book on where zen came from, india, china, the taoists, and what it's actually about. it was one of the first books in english to explain it clearly for regular readers.\nreal zen teachers had complaints about it. it still sells, and it's still a good first book.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Way_of_Zen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the way of zen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the joyous cosmology","joyous cosmology"],"key":"joyouscosmology","kind":"book","lines":"alan watts's 1962 book about what he saw on lsd and other psychedelics. timothy leary and richard alpert wrote the foreword.\nhe said later that once you get the message, you hang up the phone, and after a while he mostly did.","links":[],"name":"the joyous cosmology","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["the book on the taboo against knowing who you are","the taboo against knowing who you are"],"key":"thebookwatts","kind":"book","lines":"alan watts's 1966 book, full title the book on the taboo against knowing who you are. the taboo is the unspoken rule that you're a separate little self stuck inside your skin, when watts thinks you're the whole universe doing a person for a while.\nit's the book he wanted to hand his kids before they went out into the world. most people think it's his best.","links":[],"name":"the book","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ego","egos"],"key":"ego","kind":"word","lines":"in spiritual talk, ego means the sense of being a separate little me, the one that wants, worries, compares, and keeps score. it's not the same as being full of yourself, though that's part of it.\na lot of the traditions on these pages say that little me is more of a habit than a thing, and that seeing through it is freedom. watts called it a hoax. freud, who made the word popular, meant something more technical.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ego_death","why":"what the spiritual traditions mean by losing it"}],"name":"ego","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mount tamalpais","tamalpais"],"key":"tamalpais","kind":"place","lines":"the big mountain just north of the golden gate bridge in marin county, california, about twenty five hundred feet. mount tam, locals call it.\nthe beat poets hiked around it, and alan watts spent his last years in a cabin on its slopes. he died there in his sleep in 1973.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Tamalpais","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mount tamalpais","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sausalito"],"key":"sausalito","kind":"place","lines":"a small town on the water just north of san francisco, across the golden gate. in the fifties and sixties its houseboat community was full of artists and bohemians.\nalan watts lived there on an old ferryboat, the vallejo.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sausalito,_California","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sausalito","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["beat generation","the beat generation","the beats"],"key":"beatgeneration","kind":"group","lines":"a group of american writers in the late forties and fifties, jack kerouac, allen ginsberg, and william burroughs at the center, who wrote about drugs, jazz, sex, and road trips, and went looking for god in buddhism.\nthey were the first big rebellion against fifties america. the hippies came out of them ten years later. alan watts knew them and wrote an essay called beat zen, square zen, and zen, which gently teased them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beat_Generation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the beat generation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["creeds","creed"],"key":"creed","kind":"word","lines":"a short official statement of what a religion believes, often said out loud together. the christian ones start with i believe in god, the father almighty. sikhs open their holy book with one.\nthe big christian creeds were hammered out by church councils in the 300s. watts's complaint was that people ended up believing in the creed instead of looking at what it was pointing to.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creed","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"creed","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["areopagus","the areopagus"],"key":"areopagus","kind":"place","lines":"a rocky hill in athens, right below the acropolis, where the city's old council met. in paul's day it was where you went to argue ideas in front of the city's leaders.\npaul gave a speech there around the year 50 and quoted greek poets to greek philosophers. you can still climb it. there's a bronze plaque with the speech on it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areopagus","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areopagus_sermon","why":"the speech paul gave there"}],"name":"the areopagus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["epimenides"],"key":"epimenides","kind":"person","lines":"a greek poet and seer from crete, from around 600 bc, half legend. the story goes he fell asleep in a cave for fifty seven years.\npaul quoted a line usually credited to him, in him we live and move and have our being, to the philosophers in athens.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epimenides","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"epimenides","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["aratus"],"key":"aratus","kind":"person","lines":"a greek poet from the 200s bc who wrote a long poem about the stars and the weather. it was hugely popular in the ancient world.\nits opening has the line we are his offspring, about zeus, which paul borrowed in athens to talk about his own god.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aratus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"aratus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["unknown god"],"key":"unknowngod","kind":"word","lines":"an altar paul saw in athens with the inscription to an unknown god. the greeks sometimes put these up to cover any god they might have missed, like a spare key.\npaul used it as his opener. you already worship this one, he said. let me tell you who it is.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unknown_God","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the unknown god","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shams of tabriz","shams tabrizi","shams"],"key":"shams","kind":"person","lines":"a wandering muslim mystic who turned up in konya in 1244 and turned rumi, then a respectable scholar, upside down. nobody knows much about him before that, and nobody knows for sure what happened to him after.\nrumi's students were jealous and drove him off. he came back, then vanished for good around 1248. some say he was murdered. rumi wrote thousands of poems to him and signed many of them with shams's name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shams_Tabrizi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"shams of tabriz","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["konya"],"key":"konya","kind":"place","lines":"a city in central turkey, then the capital of a turkish kingdom. rumi lived and taught there most of his life, and his tomb, under a green tiled dome, is there.\nit's where the whirling dervishes, the order started by rumi's followers, still spin every december on the anniversary of his death.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konya","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"konya","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["robert bly"],"key":"bly","kind":"person","lines":"an american poet from minnesota who lived from 1926 to 2021. besides his own poems he made loose, lively english versions of kabir, rumi, rilke, and others that got a lot of americans reading them.\nhe's also the iron john guy, who started the men's movement of the nineties with drumming in the woods.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Bly","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"robert bly","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["be here now"],"key":"beherenow","kind":"book","lines":"ram dass's 1971 book, a square brown thing that's part memoir and part hand-drawn pages of sayings you have to turn sideways to read. it's sold something like two million copies.\nfor a whole generation it was the first spiritual book that sounded like them, funny and a little stoned and completely sincere. it's still in print.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Be_Here_Now_(book)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"his foundation","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/","why":"ram dass's own foundation, with talks and books"}],"name":"be here now","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["fierce grace"],"key":"fiercegrace","kind":"book","lines":"a 2001 documentary about ram dass after his stroke, by the filmmaker mickey lemle. it goes from the harvard days through india to him in a wheelchair, struggling for words and grinning.\nthe title is his phrase for the stroke, a gift that nearly kills you. it's the best way to meet him if you don't want to read.","links":[],"name":"fierce grace","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["still here"],"key":"stillhere","kind":"book","lines":"ram dass's 2000 book on aging, changing, and dying. he'd started writing it before the stroke in 1997 and finished it after, so it turned into a different book than he planned.","links":[],"name":"still here","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stephen levine"],"key":"levine","kind":"person","lines":"an american poet and meditation teacher who lived from 1937 to 2016 and spent decades sitting with the dying, with his wife ondrea. his book who dies? is a classic for people facing death or grieving.\nhe and ram dass worked with dying people together for years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Levine_(author)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"stephen levine","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yoga journal"],"key":"yogajournal","kind":"book","lines":"an american magazine about yoga and the spiritual scene around it, started in 1975. in the seventies it was where the new american seekers talked to each other.\nram dass published his confession about being conned, egg on my beard, there in 1976.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoga_Journal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"yoga journal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["center for action and contemplation","the center for action and contemplation"],"key":"cac","kind":"group","lines":"the teaching and retreat center richard rohr started in 1987 in albuquerque, new mexico. action means working for justice. contemplation means sitting quietly with god. the name says you have to do both.\nit sends out a free daily email meditation to hundreds of thousands of people and runs a two year school. it's become a home for a lot of people who are halfway out of church.","links":[{"label":"the center","url":"https://cac.org/","why":"rohr's own center, with the free daily meditations"}],"name":"the center for action and contemplation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["falling upward"],"key":"fallingupward","kind":"book","lines":"richard rohr's 2011 book about the two halves of life. the first half is building the container, a name, a career, a set of rules. the second half starts when something cracks it, and you didn't ask for it.\nit's short and easy to read. a lot of people pick it up in the middle of a divorce, a firing, or a diagnosis, and say it's the first thing that made sense.","links":[],"name":"falling upward","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the universal christ","universal christ"],"key":"universalchrist","kind":"book","lines":"richard rohr's 2019 book arguing that christ isn't jesus's last name but the name for god showing up in all of creation, with jesus as the clearest case.\nit was a bestseller. a lot of traditional christians think it goes too far. rohr is fine with that.","links":[],"name":"the universal christ","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["islam","islamic","muslim","muslims"],"key":"islam","kind":"group","lines":"the religion started in arabia in the early 600s by muhammad, who said god had begun speaking to him through the angel gabriel. the word means surrender, as in handing yourself over to god. a muslim is someone who does that.\nabout two billion people are muslim today, roughly a quarter of the world. the basics are simple to say and hard to do. one god, pray five times a day, give to the poor, fast in the month of ramadan, and go to mecca once in your life if you can.\non these pages it's the home of rumi and the sufis, and half of kabir's world. americans mostly meet islam on the news. the people on these pages met it at the kitchen table.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islam","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"islam","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["muhammad","the prophet muhammad","prophet muhammad"],"key":"muhammad","kind":"person","lines":"the founder of islam, a merchant from mecca, in arabia, who lived from about 570 to 632. muslims call him the prophet, the last messenger god sent. around the age of forty he started going up to a cave outside the city to pray alone, and he said that's where the words of the quran started coming to him.\nhe was run out of mecca, built a community in the city of medina, and by the time he died most of arabia had become muslim. muslims love him deeply and most of them don't picture him at all. that's why you won't find a drawing of him in this forest.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhammad","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"muhammad","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["quran","the quran","koran","qur'an"],"key":"quran","kind":"book","lines":"the holy book of islam. muslims believe it's god's own words, given to muhammad over about twenty three years and memorized by his followers before it was written down. it's 114 chapters, called suras, in arabic.\nit's meant to be heard more than read. millions of people have the whole thing by heart, and a good reciter can make a room go silent. a translation, muslims say, is only a commentary on it, because the real thing only exists in arabic.\nwhen the sufis call rumi's masnavi the quran in persian, that's about as big a compliment as a book can get.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quran","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the quran","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mecca","makkah"],"key":"mecca","kind":"place","lines":"a city in western saudi arabia, where muhammad was born. it's the holiest place in islam. in the middle of it is the kaaba, a big black cube-shaped shrine, and every muslim on earth faces toward it to pray, five times a day.\ngoing there once in your life is one of the basic duties of islam, if you can afford it and your health allows. millions go every year. only muslims are allowed in the city.\nin english the word got borrowed to mean any place people flock to, a mecca for surfers. the original is the one a quarter of the world turns toward every morning.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mecca","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mecca","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["allah"],"key":"allah","kind":"word","lines":"the arabic word for god. it's not the name of a different god. arabic-speaking christians and jews say allah too, in their own prayers and bibles.\nwhen rumi writes about the beloved, it's allah he means. the soft american versions of his poems tend to leave that out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_in_Islam","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"allah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mosque","mosques"],"key":"mosque","kind":"place","lines":"a muslim place of prayer. inside it's mostly one big carpeted room with no pews and no pictures, where people line up shoulder to shoulder and pray together. there's usually a tower, the minaret, where the call to prayer is sung out five times a day.\nfriday at midday is the big weekly prayer, the way sunday morning is for a lot of churches.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosque","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mosque","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mullah","mullahs"],"key":"mullah","kind":"word","lines":"a muslim cleric, someone trained in the quran and islamic law who leads prayers and tells people what the religion says about how to live. roughly the muslim version of the parish priest, or the local rabbi.\nkabir poked at them the same way he poked at the hindu priests. the building and the uniform aren't where god lives, he kept saying.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullah","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mullah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sufi","sufis","sufism"],"key":"sufi","kind":"group","lines":"the mystics of islam. sufis are muslims who want more than the rules. they want to meet god directly, the way you'd want to meet someone you're in love with and not only read their letters. they do it with prayer, chanting, poetry, music, and in some orders, dancing.\nthey usually gather around a master in an order, a kind of spiritual family passed down teacher to student. rumi is the most famous sufi there ever was. hafiz is another. the whirling dervishes are a sufi order.\na lot of stricter muslims have always been suspicious of them, and some still are. but for a thousand years they've been the side of islam that writes the love poems.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sufism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sufi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dervish","dervishes","whirling dervishes","whirling dervish"],"key":"dervish","kind":"group","lines":"a sufi who's taken the path of poverty, the word comes from persian and means something like a poor man, or one who stands at the door. some dervishes wandered and begged, the way shams did. others lived together in lodges under a master.\nthe whirling dervishes are the order rumi's son sultan walad organized after rumi died, called the mevlevi, after mevlana, our master, which is what turks call rumi. in white skirts and tall felt hats they spin slowly for a long time with one palm up and one palm down, and it's a prayer, not a show, though tourists now buy tickets to it in turkey.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dervish","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mevlevi_Order","why":"the whirling order rumi's followers started"}],"name":"dervish","status":"approved","threads":["wandering"]},{"aliases":["persian poetry","persian"],"key":"persianpoetry","kind":"word","lines":"persian is the language of iran, and of a lot of afghanistan and tajikistan too. for about a thousand years it was the language poetry was written in from turkey to india, the way french was the language of fancy europe.\nrumi, hafiz, attar, and omar khayyam all wrote in it. iranians still quote hafiz the way people quote song lyrics, and people open his book at random to get advice on what to do.\nmost of what americans read as rumi is english that's traveled a long way from the persian. the originals rhyme, and they're full of the quran.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_literature","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"persian poetry","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the masnavi","masnavi","mathnawi"],"key":"masnavi","kind":"book","lines":"rumi's big book, six volumes and about twenty five thousand rhyming couplets in persian, which he dictated over the last dozen or so years of his life to his friend husam al-din, who wrote it down as rumi walked around konya talking.\nit's stories inside stories. a king and a slave girl, a merchant and a parrot, dirty jokes, bits of the quran, and then all of a sudden a passage that stops you cold. it opens with a flute made from a reed crying because it was cut from the reed bed.\nsufis call it the quran in persian. the old full english translation is a hundred years old and stiff. there are newer ones that read much better.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masnavi","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/isl/masnavi/index.htm","why":"an old abridged english translation, public domain"}],"name":"the masnavi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bayazid","bayazid bastami","bayazid of bastam"],"key":"bayazid","kind":"person","lines":"a persian sufi from the 800s, one of the first of the wild ones. he's famous for saying things in a trance like glory be to me, how great is my majesty, which sounds like a man calling himself god.\nhis followers said it was god talking through him after he'd emptied himself out. his critics said it was blasphemy. either way, he's the one shams used to test rumi.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayazid_Bastami","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bayazid bastami","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["coleman barks","barks"],"key":"colemanbarks","kind":"person","lines":"an american poet from tennessee, born in 1937, who taught at the university of georgia and became the reason most americans have heard of rumi. his rumi books have sold millions of copies.\nhe doesn't read persian. he took stiff english translations by british scholars from a hundred years ago and reworked them into loose, lovely american free verse. the results are beautiful and not very close to the original, and most of the islam falls out along the way.\nthink of it like a cover version. you might love the song, and it's worth knowing somebody else wrote it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coleman_Barks","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"coleman barks","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the essential rumi","essential rumi"],"key":"essentialrumi","kind":"book","lines":"coleman barks's 1995 collection of rumi poems in his loose american versions. it's the book that made rumi a bestseller in the united states, the one on every yoga studio shelf.\nit's a good door in, if you know it's a door. the real thing is rougher, more muslim, and a lot longer.","links":[],"name":"the essential rumi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sultan walad"],"key":"sultanwalad","kind":"person","lines":"rumi's oldest son, who lived from 1226 to 1312. when shams ran off to damascus the first time, rumi sent sultan walad to bring him back, and he did.\nafter rumi died, sultan walad organized his father's followers into the mevlevi order, the whirling dervishes, and wrote poems of his own about his father's life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultan_Walad","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sultan walad","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mongols","the mongols","mongol"],"key":"mongols","kind":"group","lines":"the horse-riding armies from the grasslands north of china who, under genghis khan and his sons in the 1200s, built the biggest land empire the world had ever seen. they came west across central asia and wiped out whole cities that didn't surrender.\nrumi's family left balkh just ahead of them when he was a boy. a lot of the persian world spent that century as refugees, which is part of why its poems are so full of longing for home.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongol_Empire","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mongols","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["balkh"],"key":"balkh","kind":"place","lines":"an ancient city in what's now northern afghanistan, so old and important that people called it the mother of cities. rumi was born there, or nearby, in 1207.\nthe mongols wrecked it around 1220, soon after his family left. today it's a small town next to the ruins.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balkh","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"balkh","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["attar","attar of nishapur"],"key":"attar","kind":"person","lines":"a persian poet and sufi who died around 1221. his name means perfume seller, which may have been his trade, or pharmacist, which may have been too.\nhis most famous book is the conference of the birds. the story goes that he met the boy rumi on the road and handed him a book, and said this one will set the world on fire.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attar_of_Nishapur","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"attar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the conference of the birds","conference of the birds"],"key":"conferencebirds","kind":"book","lines":"attar's long persian poem from around 1177. all the birds of the world set out to find their king, a legendary bird called the simorgh. most of them quit along the way with excuses, the nightingale won't leave his rose, the duck won't leave the water.\nthirty birds make it. and in persian, thirty birds is si morgh. they look for the king and see themselves. it's one of the great punchlines in all of literature.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Conference_of_the_Birds","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the conference of the birds","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["caste","castes","caste system","low caste"],"key":"caste","kind":"word","lines":"the old hindu system that sorts every family into a rank at birth. there are four broad classes, priests on top, then warriors, then merchants and farmers, then laborers, plus thousands of smaller groups, and below all of them the people once called untouchable.\nyour caste used to decide your job, who you could marry, who you could eat with, even whose shadow you could let fall on you. you couldn't work your way out of it.\nindia outlawed discrimination by caste in 1950, but it's still very much alive in marriages, villages, and politics. think of it as the american class system, if you were born into your parents' job and it was written on you.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caste","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"caste","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["brahmin","brahmins","brahman priests"],"key":"brahmin","kind":"group","lines":"the top caste in hinduism, traditionally the priests and scholars. only brahmins could perform the big rituals and teach the old scriptures in sanskrit.\nboys from brahmin families are given a sacred thread at a ceremony in their childhood and wear it over one shoulder their whole lives. kabir's joke was to ask why, if they were born so special, they came out of the womb the same way as everybody else.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"brahmin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sacred thread"],"key":"sacredthread","kind":"word","lines":"a thin loop of cotton cord that boys in the upper castes of hinduism, especially brahmins, receive at a coming-of-age ceremony and wear across the chest from the left shoulder for life. it marks the start of their religious education, a kind of second birth.\nto kabir, a low caste weaver, it was the perfect symbol of religion as a badge.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upanayana","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sacred thread","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["weaver","weavers"],"key":"weaver","kind":"word","lines":"in kabir's india, weaving cloth on a hand loom was a caste job, passed down in families, and a low one. kabir came from a muslim weaver community in varanasi, people who'd likely been low caste hindus not long before and had become muslim.\nall day at the loom, feet on the pedals, the shuttle going back and forth. his songs are full of thread and cloth and the body as a garment. varanasi is still famous for its silk weaving, and a lot of the weavers are still muslim.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julaha","why":"the weaver community kabir came from"}],"name":"weaver","status":"approved","threads":["work"]},{"aliases":["hindi"],"key":"hindi","kind":"word","lines":"the main language of northern india, spoken by hundreds of millions of people today. it's a cousin of urdu, the language of pakistan. in everyday talk the two are nearly the same.\nin kabir's day serious religious writing was in sanskrit, the old priestly language most people couldn't understand. kabir sang in the plain speech of the street, which is why ordinary people could carry his songs around in their heads.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hindi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ram, ram","rama"],"key":"rama","kind":"word","lines":"ram, or rama, is one of the names of god in hinduism, the hero-king of the old epic the ramayana. saying ram, ram is how a lot of indians pray, and it's also what they blurt out when something startles them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rama","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rama","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ramananda"],"key":"ramananda","kind":"person","lines":"a hindu teacher in varanasi, probably in the 1400s, who taught devotion to the god rama and took students from any caste, which was radical then.\nthe story says he stepped on kabir in the dark on the river steps, cried out ram, and ended up with a muslim weaver for a disciple. historians argue over whether the two ever met. the story stuck anyway.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramananda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ramananda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ghat","ghats"],"key":"ghat","kind":"place","lines":"a flight of wide stone steps leading down to a river, where people bathe, pray, wash clothes, and in some places burn the dead. varanasi has dozens of them along the ganges, one after another for miles.\nat dawn they fill up with people going into the water to pray. at night some of them are lit by funeral fires. it's where a lot of indian life and death happens out in the open.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghat","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ghat","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sikh","sikhs","sikhism"],"key":"sikh","kind":"group","lines":"a religion born in punjab, in northern india, around 1500, with guru nanak. it teaches one god, equality of everyone regardless of caste, honest work, and sharing what you have. there are about twenty five million sikhs today.\nsikh men usually wear a turban and don't cut their hair. every sikh temple has a free kitchen that feeds anyone who walks in, no questions. the golden temple in amritsar feeds tens of thousands of people a day.\nin america sikhs have too often been mistaken for other people and attacked for it. they're their own tradition, with ten gurus and a holy book that includes kabir.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sikhism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sikh","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["guru granth sahib","the guru granth sahib","adi granth"],"key":"gurugranthsahib","kind":"book","lines":"the holy book of the sikhs. it's a huge collection of hymns by the sikh gurus, and also by hindu and muslim saints they admired, including kabir, who has more verses in it than anyone else who wasn't a sikh guru.\nthe tenth guru declared that after him there'd be no more human gurus. the book itself would be the guru. so sikhs treat it like a living teacher. it gets its own room, it's carried on the head, it's put to bed at night.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guru_Granth_Sahib","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the guru granth sahib","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rabindranath tagore","tagore"],"key":"tagore","kind":"person","lines":"an indian poet, songwriter, painter, and teacher from bengal who lived from 1861 to 1941. in 1913 he became the first person from outside europe to win the nobel prize for literature.\nhe wrote the national anthems of both india and bangladesh. in 1915 he put out a small english book, songs of kabir, which is how a lot of the west first heard kabir.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabindranath_Tagore","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rabindranath tagore","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["songs of kabir"],"key":"songsofkabir","kind":"book","lines":"a short book of a hundred kabir poems in english, translated by rabindranath tagore with the help of an english writer on mysticism, evelyn underhill, and published in 1915.\nit's old-fashioned in places, and the scholars have argued about which of the poems kabir really wrote. but it's where robert bly found him, and it's free.","links":[{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519","why":"tagore's 1915 translation, public domain"}],"name":"songs of kabir","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the bijak","bijak"],"key":"bijak","kind":"book","lines":"the collection of kabir's songs and sayings kept by the kabir panth, the religious group that grew up around him after he died. the name means something like a treasure map.\nit's the sharpest and rudest version of him, the one where he's laughing at every priest in town.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bijak","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the bijak","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the kabir book"],"key":"kabirbook","kind":"book","lines":"robert bly's short book of kabir poems in loose american english, first published in 1977. bly worked from tagore's english, not the original hindi, and made it sound like a man talking to you across a table.","links":[],"name":"the kabir book","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dominican","dominicans","dominican order"],"key":"dominican","kind":"group","lines":"a catholic religious order started in 1216 by a spanish priest named dominic. officially they're the order of preachers. they're friars, which means they take vows like monks but go out into the towns to preach and teach instead of staying behind walls.\nthey wear white robes with a black cloak. they built themselves around the universities, and thomas aquinas, the church's biggest thinker, was one. they also ran a lot of the inquisition, which is the dark side of the same zeal.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominican_Order","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dominican","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["friar","friars"],"key":"friar","kind":"word","lines":"a member of a catholic religious order who takes vows of poverty, chastity, and obedience like a monk, but works out in the world, begging, preaching, and teaching, instead of living behind monastery walls.\nthe franciscans and the dominicans are the two big friar orders. think of the monk as the one who stays home and prays, and the friar as the one out on the street.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"friar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["university of paris","the university of paris","sorbonne"],"key":"universityparis","kind":"place","lines":"one of the first universities in the world, up and running around 1200. in the middle ages it was the top place in europe to study theology, the study of god, and every serious scholar wanted to teach there.\neckhart held its top chair in theology twice, which almost nobody got once. the sorbonne, still one of its famous parts today, is named after a man who started a college there in the 1250s.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Paris","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the university of paris","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["heresy","heretic","heretics","heretical"],"key":"heresy","kind":"word","lines":"believing or teaching something the official church has ruled false. in the middle ages it wasn't a difference of opinion. it was a crime, and the punishment could be prison, losing everything, or being burned alive.\nthe church's reasoning was that a heretic was killing souls, which was worse than killing bodies. marguerite porete was burned in paris in 1310 for a book about the soul losing itself in god. eckhart knew exactly what he was up against.\ntoday the word gets used for anything that breaks the party line, in politics or at work. the old version had fire in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heresy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"heresy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["marguerite porete","porete"],"key":"porete","kind":"person","lines":"a french woman, a beguine, one of the laywomen who lived religious lives without being nuns, who wrote a book called the mirror of simple souls about the soul emptying itself until only god is left.\nshe refused to take it back or even answer her accusers. she was burned alive in paris in 1310. her book kept getting copied anyway, often with her name taken off, and it wasn't known to be hers until 1946.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marguerite_Porete","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"marguerite porete","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pope","popes","the pope"],"key":"pope","kind":"person","lines":"the head of the catholic church, who is also the bishop of rome. catholics believe he's the successor to the apostle peter, and he has the final say on what the church teaches. there have been about two hundred and sixty of them.\nin the middle ages the pope was also a political ruler with land, armies, and taxes, and kings fought with him constantly. now he runs vatican city, the tiny country inside rome, and speaks for about 1.4 billion catholics.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the pope","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["avignon"],"key":"avignon","kind":"place","lines":"a walled city in southern france, on the rhône river. from 1309 to 1377 the popes lived there instead of in rome, after a french king pushed the church around. seven popes in a row ran the church from a huge stone palace that's still standing.\nitalians called it the church's babylonian captivity. eckhart went there to defend himself, and died there or on the way, around 1328.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avignon_Papacy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"avignon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["archbishop of cologne","the archbishop of cologne","cologne"],"key":"archbishopcologne","kind":"person","lines":"cologne is a big old city on the rhine in western germany, with a cathedral you can see for miles. its archbishop, the top churchman in that part of germany, was also a prince with land and a vote in picking the emperor.\nthe one who went after eckhart in 1326 was henry of virneburg, who'd already been hard on the beguines, the religious laywomen eckhart preached to.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archdiocese_of_Cologne","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the archbishop of cologne","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sermon","sermons"],"key":"sermon","kind":"word","lines":"a talk given in church, usually by a priest or preacher, explaining a bit of the bible and what it means for how you live. the sunday sermon is still the center of most protestant services.\nin eckhart's day sermons for scholars were in latin. eckhart preached in german to nuns and townspeople, and that's where his wildest ideas came out, because the nuns wrote down what he said.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sermon","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sermon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["thomas aquinas","aquinas"],"key":"aquinas","kind":"person","lines":"an italian dominican friar who lived from 1225 to 1274 and became the biggest thinker in catholic history. his summa theologica tries to answer every question about god and the world, one objection at a time. it runs to thousands of pages and he never finished it.\nnear the end he had some kind of experience during mass and stopped writing. he said everything he'd written seemed like straw compared to what he'd seen.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Aquinas","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"thomas aquinas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["martha and mary","mary and martha"],"key":"marthamary","kind":"event","lines":"a short story in luke's gospel, chapter 10. jesus visits two sisters. martha is rushing around the kitchen getting dinner on. mary sits at his feet and listens. martha complains, and jesus tells her mary has chosen the better part.\nfor centuries it was read as prayer beats work. eckhart flipped it. martha, he said, was the grown-up, the one who could be busy and with god at the same time.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesus_at_the_home_of_Martha_and_Mary","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"martha and mary","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["beati pauperes spiritu","poverty of spirit"],"key":"beati","kind":"word","lines":"latin for blessed are the poor in spirit, the first line of jesus's sermon on the mount in matthew 5, and the name of eckhart's most famous sermon.\nin it he says the truly poor person wants nothing, knows nothing, and has nothing, not even a place in himself for god to work. it's the one where he prays god to rid him of god.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poor_in_spirit","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"beati pauperes spiritu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["marpa","marpa the translator"],"key":"marpa","kind":"person","lines":"a tibetan farmer, husband, and father who lived from about 1012 to 1097 and walked over the himalayas to india at least three times to study with buddhist masters there. he carried teachings back and translated them into tibetan, which is why he's called marpa the translator.\nhe was rich, short-tempered, and nobody's idea of a holy man. he's the one who worked milarepa half to death building stone towers before he'd teach him. his line of teachers became the kagyu school.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marpa_Lotsawa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"marpa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["karma","karmic"],"key":"karma","kind":"word","lines":"the idea from india, in hinduism and buddhism both, that what you do comes back around. the word just means action. every act, word, and thought leaves a mark, and those marks ripen later, in this life or another one.\nit isn't a cosmic judge handing out punishment. it's closer to habit and consequence, the way every drink makes the next one easier.\nin english it's become what goes around comes around, usually about someone else getting theirs. the old version is mostly about your own.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karma","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"karma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yogi","yogis","yogin"],"key":"yogi","kind":"word","lines":"in india and tibet, someone who does yoga in the old sense, meaning spiritual practice full time. meditation, breathing, living rough, often alone in caves or forests. it's not someone who does stretches at the gym.\nmilarepa is the most famous yogi tibet ever had. picture a hermit with a practice.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yogi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"yogi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the hundred thousand songs of milarepa","hundred thousand songs of milarepa","hundred thousand songs"],"key":"hundredthousandsongs","kind":"book","lines":"the big collection of milarepa's songs and the stories around them, put together in its present form in 1488 by tsangnyön heruka, the same wild yogi who wrote down his life story.\nit's not a hundred thousand songs. that's a way of saying a whole lot. each chapter is a scene, a hunter, a demon, a student, a village, and a song milarepa sang there. tibetans still sing them.","links":[],"name":"the hundred thousand songs of milarepa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the life of milarepa","life of milarepa"],"key":"lifemilarepa","kind":"book","lines":"the story of milarepa's life, told mostly in his own voice, written down in 1488 by tsangnyön heruka. it's the most loved book in tibet.\nit reads like a novel. the uncle who steals everything, the curse, the killings, the brutal teacher, the towers, the cave, the nettles. it's been in english since 1928.","links":[],"name":"the life of milarepa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tsangnyön heruka","tsangnyon heruka","madman of tsang"],"key":"tsangnyon","kind":"person","lines":"a tibetan yogi who lived from 1452 to 1507 and was known as the madman of tsang, tsang being his region. he wore bone ornaments, smeared himself with ashes, and acted outrageous on purpose, to shake people loose.\nhe's also the one who sat down and wrote milarepa's life and songs into the books tibet still reads. a crazy man with a great prose style.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsangnyön_Heruka","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tsangnyön heruka","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["geshe"],"key":"geshe","kind":"word","lines":"a title for a learned tibetan buddhist monk, today roughly a doctorate in buddhist philosophy that takes twelve to twenty years of study and debate to earn.\nin milarepa's story the geshe is the book-smart monk who resents the cave singer everybody loves. every tradition has that guy.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geshe","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"geshe","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gampopa"],"key":"gampopa","kind":"person","lines":"a tibetan doctor who lived from 1079 to 1153. after his wife died he became a monk, then went looking for milarepa and became his greatest student.\nhe combined milarepa's cave practice with orderly monastery life, and that's what turned a singing hermit's teachings into the kagyu school, which is still going. his book the jewel ornament of liberation is still a standard guide.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gampopa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gampopa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rechungpa"],"key":"rechungpa","kind":"person","lines":"one of milarepa's two closest students, who lived from 1084 to 1161. he was young, gifted, and restless, and kept wandering off, getting into trouble, and coming back.\nin the stories milarepa loves him like a son and teases him constantly. he's the student most people find easiest to relate to.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rechungpa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rechungpa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["repa"],"key":"repa","kind":"word","lines":"tibetan for cotton-clad, the name for yogis who wore only a thin white cotton cloth through the himalayan winter. they kept warm with an inner heat practice that works with the breath and the body.\nmilarepa means mila the cotton-clad. mila was his family's name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tummo","why":"the inner heat practice"}],"name":"repa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nalanda"],"key":"nalanda","kind":"place","lines":"a huge buddhist university in northeast india, in the state now called bihar, that ran for about seven hundred years, from around the 400s to around 1200. at its height thousands of monks lived and studied there, and students came from as far as china and korea.\nit had a library so big that, the story goes, it burned for months when raiders sacked it around 1200. the ruins are a world heritage site. think of it as oxford and harvard rolled together, a thousand years early.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nalanda_mahavihara","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nalanda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bodhisattva","bodhisattvas"],"key":"bodhisattva","kind":"word","lines":"in buddhism, someone who's on the road to waking up and has promised not to take the exit until everybody else gets out too. they could have their own peace. they stay in the mess for other people's sake.\nit's the big ideal of mahayana buddhism, the kind practiced in tibet, china, japan, and korea. the dalai lama is believed to be one.\nthink of the firefighter who keeps going back into the building. that's the job.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodhisattva","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bodhisattva","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the way of the bodhisattva","way of the bodhisattva","bodhicaryavatara","bodhicharyavatara"],"key":"bodhicaryavatara","kind":"book","lines":"shantideva's one big book, written in sanskrit around the year 700. ten chapters of verse about how to become a bodhisattva, someone who lives for everyone else's freedom, told as a man talking himself into it.\nit's practical to the point of being funny. how to handle anger, how to handle laziness, how to stop comparing yourself to other people. chapter 6, on patience, and chapter 8, on swapping yourself with others, are the famous ones.\nthe dalai lama calls it the book that taught him most of what he knows about compassion, and he's taught it more than anything else.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodhicaryavatara","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"an old translation, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bud/tpol/index.htm","why":"the path of light, l.d. barnett's 1909 english version, public domain"}],"name":"the way of the bodhisattva","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kshanti","the chapter on patience","chapter on patience"],"key":"kshanti","kind":"word","lines":"kshanti is the sanskrit word buddhists use for patience, and it means more than waiting in line. it's the ability to take harm, insults, and pain without striking back or falling apart. it's one of the six things a bodhisattva is supposed to perfect.\nshantideva's chapter 6 is the classic on it. anger, he says, is the worst thing you can carry, and the person who makes you angry is doing you a favor by giving you the practice. it's the chapter people read in the middle of a divorce.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kshanti","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"patience (kshanti)","status":"approved","threads":["anger"]},{"aliases":["padmakara translation","padmakara"],"key":"padmakara","kind":"group","lines":"the padmakara translation group is a team of western translators working with tibetan teachers at a center in the south of france. they've put a lot of tibetan buddhist classics into clear english.\ntheir version of the way of the bodhisattva is the one most english readers use.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padmakara_Translation_Group","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the padmakara translation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["no time to lose"],"key":"notimetolose","kind":"book","lines":"pema chödrön's 2005 book walking through shantideva's the way of the bodhisattva, verse by verse, like a teacher reading it with you. it's warm and plain.\nif the original feels too old and strict, this is the way in.","links":[],"name":"no time to lose","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bengal","bengali"],"key":"bengal","kind":"place","lines":"a region in the east of the indian subcontinent, on the delta where the ganges meets the sea. today it's split between the indian state of west bengal, whose capital is calcutta, and the country of bangladesh.\nbengali is its language, one of the most spoken in the world. in the 1800s calcutta was the capital of british india, and bengal was where a lot of india's modern religious and political ideas got started.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bengal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kali"],"key":"kali","kind":"person","lines":"a hindu mother goddess, the fierce one. she's shown black or dark blue, with her tongue out, wearing a necklace of cut-off heads and a skirt of arms, often standing on her husband shiva.\nshe's time and death and the destruction of everything that isn't real. to her devotees she's also the most loving mother there is, the one who kills your demons for you. ramakrishna called her ma and cried for her like a kid.\nfor a westerner it's a lot to take in. think of her as what a mother would look like if she'd do anything at all to protect you.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kali","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["temple priest","temple priests"],"key":"templepriest","kind":"word","lines":"in hinduism, a temple priest looks after the god in the temple, treating the statue like a living guest. wake it, bathe it, dress it, feed it, wave lamps in front of it, put it to bed. it's called puja, worship, and it happens every day, several times a day.\nit's a job, with a salary and a schedule, usually held by brahmins. ramakrishna took it so seriously he kept losing track of the schedule.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pujari","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"temple priest","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vivekananda","swami vivekananda"],"key":"vivekananda","kind":"person","lines":"ramakrishna's most famous disciple, a calcutta college kid named narendranath datta who lived from 1863 to 1902. he became a monk after ramakrishna died and took the name vivekananda.\nin 1893 he spoke at the parliament of the world's religions in chicago and became a sensation. he spent years lecturing in america and england, started the first vedanta societies in the west, and founded the ramakrishna mission back in india. he died at thirty nine.\nyoga and hindu ideas in america start, more or less, with him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Vivekananda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"vivekananda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["parliament of the world's religions","parliament of religions","parliament of the world's religions in chicago"],"key":"parliamentreligions","kind":"event","lines":"a gathering in chicago in september 1893, held alongside the world's fair, where leaders of religions from all over the world spoke to each other in public, most of them for the first time.\nit's usually counted as the start of interfaith conversation in the west. vivekananda's speech there, which opened with sisters and brothers of america, is the most famous thing that happened.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_World's_Religions","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the parliament of the world's religions","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sarada devi","holy mother"],"key":"saradadevi","kind":"person","lines":"ramakrishna's wife, who lived from 1853 to 1920. she was married to him as a small child, came to live with him at eighteen, and they lived together as celibates.\nafter he died she became the heart of the movement, a teacher and a mother to the monks. his followers call her the holy mother.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarada_Devi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sarada devi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ramakrishna mission","the ramakrishna mission","ramakrishna order"],"key":"ramakrishnamission","kind":"group","lines":"the organization vivekananda started in 1897 in ramakrishna's name. it's a monastic order that also runs schools, hospitals, and disaster relief across india and beyond, with vedanta centers around the world.\nit's the place ramakrishna's ideas ended up living, a mix of monks meditating and monks running clinics.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramakrishna_Mission","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ramakrishna mission","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bhairavi brahmani","the bhairavi brahmani"],"key":"bhairavi","kind":"person","lines":"a wandering woman teacher of tantra who showed up at ramakrishna's temple in 1861. she was learned, a brahmin, and she told him that what his family thought was madness was the state the old scriptures described.\nshe taught him tantra for several years. she's the first teacher who took him seriously.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhairavi_Brahmani","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the bhairavi brahmani","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tantra","tantric","tantrics"],"key":"tantra","kind":"word","lines":"a set of practices in hinduism and buddhism, going back more than a thousand years, that work through the body, the senses, ritual, sound, and visualization, instead of trying to get away from them. the idea is that the energy of ordinary life, even desire and fear, can be used to wake up.\nin the west the word got turned into a sex thing. that's a tiny, mostly invented slice of it. most tantra is ritual and meditation, and a lot of tibetan buddhism is tantric.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tantra","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tantra","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["totapuri"],"key":"totapuri","kind":"person","lines":"a wandering naked monk who stopped at ramakrishna's temple in 1864 and taught him advaita vedanta, the teaching that only the one reality exists.\nhe'd planned to stay a few days and stayed eleven months. the story goes that he got sick there, tried to drown himself in the ganges, and couldn't find water deep enough, and came out believing in the mother goddess he'd laughed at.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Totapuri","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"totapuri","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vedanta","advaita vedanta","advaita"],"key":"vedanta","kind":"word","lines":"one of the main schools of hindu philosophy, based on the upanishads, the old wisdom texts at the end of the vedas. vedanta literally means the end of the vedas.\nits most famous form, advaita, which means not two, says there's only one reality, and your deepest self and god are the same thing. the separate you is a misunderstanding, like mistaking a rope for a snake in the dark.\nit's the hinduism vivekananda brought to america, and the one huxley and a lot of californians fell in love with.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vedanta","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advaita_Vedanta","why":"the not two school"}],"name":"vedanta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["samadhi"],"key":"samadhi","kind":"word","lines":"in hindu and buddhist meditation, a state of complete absorption, where the mind goes so still that the line between you and what you're focused on disappears.\nramakrishna slipped into it constantly, sometimes in the middle of a conversation, going stiff and blank while people waited for him to come back. it can look alarming from the outside.\nthe closest ordinary thing is being so lost in something you love that you forget you exist. samadhi is that, all the way.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samadhi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"samadhi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tao","the tao","dao"],"key":"tao","kind":"word","lines":"chinese for the way, or the road. in lao tzu's book it means the way everything works underneath everything, the flow the whole world moves in, which you can follow but can't name or grab.\ntaoism, the chinese tradition that grew up around the idea, says the best life is lived going with it, like a boat going with the current. the first line of the tao te ching says the tao you can talk about isn't the real one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tao","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the tao","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tao te ching","the tao te ching","daodejing","tao teh king"],"key":"taoteching","kind":"book","lines":"the short chinese book credited to lao tzu, eighty one tiny chapters, about five thousand characters in all. the title means something like the book of the way and its power. it probably took shape over a couple of centuries before about 250 bc.\nit's about water, emptiness, not forcing, and how the soft outlasts the hard. it's also, oddly, advice to rulers, telling them the less they meddle the better things go.\nit's one of the most translated books in the world. you can read the whole thing in an evening, and people spend their whole lives on it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tao_Te_Ching","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216","why":"an old public domain english translation"}],"name":"the tao te ching","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wu wei","wu-wei"],"key":"wuwei","kind":"word","lines":"chinese for doing without doing, or effortless action. it's the heart of the tao te ching.\nit's not laziness. it's acting without forcing, the way a good carpenter follows the grain of the wood or a swimmer floats instead of thrashing. you do things, but you stop fighting the way things want to go.\nathletes call it being in the zone. musicians call it the song playing itself.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wu_wei","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"wu wei","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the zhou","zhou dynasty","zhou"],"key":"zhou","kind":"group","lines":"the family of kings that ruled china, at least in name, for about eight hundred years, from around 1046 bc to 256 bc, the longest dynasty in chinese history.\nby lao tzu's and confucius's time the kings had lost most of their real power and china was breaking into warring states. a lot of chinese philosophy was written by people trying to figure out how to live in a falling-apart country.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhou_dynasty","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the zhou","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["confucius"],"key":"confucius","kind":"person","lines":"a chinese teacher who lived from 551 to 479 bc and became the most influential thinker in chinese history. his students wrote down his sayings in a book called the analects.\nhe taught duty, respect for parents and elders, good manners, study, and fair government. for more than a thousand years chinese officials had to pass exams on his books to get a job. he's the rules and rituals guy, the father figure, and lao tzu is the one who laughs at the rules.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confucius","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"confucius","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sima qian"],"key":"simaqian","kind":"person","lines":"china's first great historian, who lived from about 145 to 86 bc. his records of the grand historian tell the story of china from the legends up to his own day.\nhe defended a disgraced general in front of the emperor, and was sentenced to death. he chose castration instead, which was the only way out, so he could finish the book. he finished it. his short biography of lao tzu is most of what we have.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sima_Qian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sima qian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tarsus"],"key":"tarsus","kind":"place","lines":"a city on the southern coast of what's now turkey, near the mediterranean. in paul's time it was a busy roman town and a university city, known for its philosophers.\npaul called it no ordinary city. it's still there, a town of a few hundred thousand people.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarsus,_Mersin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tarsus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pharisee","pharisees"],"key":"pharisee","kind":"group","lines":"a group of jewish teachers in jesus's and paul's time who tried to carry god's law into every part of daily life, not only the temple. they taught in the synagogues and were respected by ordinary people.\nthe gospels often make them the bad guys arguing with jesus, and pharisee became a word for a self-righteous hypocrite. that's unfair to them. modern judaism grew mostly out of the pharisees, and paul was proud to have been one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharisees","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pharisee","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["roman citizen","roman citizenship"],"key":"romancitizen","kind":"word","lines":"a legal status in the roman empire, something like a passport with privileges. most people in the empire didn't have it. citizens couldn't be whipped without a trial, could appeal their case to the emperor, and couldn't be crucified.\npaul had it from birth, and used it. it's why, when he was arrested, he got sent to rome for trial, and why, the tradition says, he was beheaded and not crucified.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_citizenship","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"roman citizen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["roman empire","the roman empire","the romans","roman occupiers"],"key":"romanempire","kind":"group","lines":"the empire ruled from rome that, in the time of jesus and paul, ran everything around the mediterranean, from spain to syria, from britain to egypt. it held maybe sixty million people.\nit built roads, kept order with soldiers, taxed everyone, and killed rebels in public. israel was an occupied territory, and the romans were the ones who crucified jesus. the same roads let paul and the first christians travel everywhere, and within three hundred years the empire itself went christian.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Empire","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the roman empire","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["damascus"],"key":"damascus","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of syria, one of the oldest cities on earth that's been lived in without a break. in paul's time it had a big jewish community, some of whom had started following jesus.\nrumi's son went there to bring shams back. paul was on his way there to arrest people when the light hit him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damascus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"damascus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["damascus road","road to damascus","way to damascus"],"key":"damascusroad","kind":"event","lines":"the story in acts, chapter 9, of paul, then called saul, on his way to damascus to arrest followers of jesus, when a light from heaven knocked him to the ground and a voice asked why he was persecuting him. he got up blind and came out on the other side.\nit's the most famous conversion story in history, and in english a damascus moment now means any sudden total change of mind.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversion_of_Paul_the_Apostle","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the damascus road","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["acts of the apostles","the book of acts","book of acts","acts 17","acts 17:28","acts 9"],"key":"acts","kind":"book","lines":"the fifth book of the new testament, written by the same person who wrote the gospel of luke, as a sequel. it picks up after jesus is gone and tells how his followers spread the word from jerusalem out across the roman empire.\nthe first half is mostly peter. the second half is mostly paul, the road, the shipwrecks, the riots, the jails, all the way to rome. it's the closest thing the bible has to an adventure novel.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acts_of_the_Apostles","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/act.htm","why":"the book of acts in the king james version"}],"name":"the book of acts","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["epistles","epistle","paul's letters","letters of paul"],"key":"epistles","kind":"book","lines":"the letters in the new testament, written to early churches or to individual christians. epistle is just an old word for letter.\nthirteen of them have paul's name on them. scholars are sure he wrote seven, and argue about the rest, which may have been written later by his followers under his name. they're the oldest christian writing there is, older than the gospels.\nthey're real mail. you can hear him losing his temper, missing people, fixing fights, and asking someone to bring the coat he left behind.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pauline_epistles","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the epistles","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["corinthians","1 corinthians","first corinthians","2 corinthians","1 corinthians 13"],"key":"corinthians","kind":"book","lines":"two letters paul wrote to the church he'd started in corinth, a rowdy rich port city in greece. the christians there were fighting over everything, who was the best teacher, who ate what, sex, lawsuits, who got to speak in church.\nchapter 13 of the first letter is the love chapter, love is patient, love is kind, the one read at nearly every church wedding. he wrote it to people who were being awful to each other.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Epistle_to_the_Corinthians","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"corinthians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["letter to the romans","the letter to the romans","epistle to the romans","romans 7","romans 7:15"],"key":"romansletter","kind":"book","lines":"paul's longest letter, written around the year 57 to the christians in rome, a church he hadn't started and hadn't visited yet. it's his big statement of what he believed.\nchapter 7 is where he admits he can't do the good he wants and keeps doing the thing he hates. chapter 8 ends with nothing can separate us from the love of god. it's the letter that set off martin luther and the whole protestant reformation fifteen hundred years later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epistle_to_the_Romans","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the letter to the romans","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["philippians","letter to the philippians"],"key":"philippians","kind":"book","lines":"a short letter paul wrote from prison to the church in philippi, a roman town in northern greece. it was the first church he started in europe, and he clearly loved them.\nfor a man in chains it's strangely happy. rejoice, he says, and again i say rejoice. it's also where he says he's learned to be content whether he has a lot or nothing. four chapters. read it in fifteen minutes.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epistle_to_the_Philippians","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"philippians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["galatians","letter to the galatians"],"key":"galatians","kind":"book","lines":"paul's angriest letter, to churches in galatia, in what's now central turkey. other preachers had come through telling his non-jewish converts they had to follow the jewish law and be circumcised to count. paul was furious.\nit's also got one of the most radical lines in the bible. there is neither jew nor greek, slave nor free, male nor female, for you are all one. and it's where he tells the story of calling peter a hypocrite to his face.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epistle_to_the_Galatians","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"galatians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["corinth"],"key":"corinth","kind":"place","lines":"a big, rich port city in greece, on the narrow strip of land between the mainland and the southern peninsula. in paul's day it was a crossroads full of sailors, traders, and temples, with a reputation for partying.\npaul lived there a year and a half making tents, and started a church that gave him more trouble than any other.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Corinth","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"corinth","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nero"],"key":"nero","kind":"person","lines":"the roman emperor from 54 to 68. he killed his own mother, fancied himself a singer and an actor, and is remembered as one of the worst emperors rome had.\nafter a great fire burned much of rome in 64, he blamed the christians and had them killed in public, some torn apart by dogs, some burned as torches at night. tradition says paul and peter both died in rome under him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nero","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nero","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["great fire of rome","the great fire of rome"],"key":"greatfire","kind":"event","lines":"a fire that started in july of the year 64 and burned for about six days, destroying much of the city of rome. rumors said nero started it to clear room for a new palace.\nhe needed someone to blame, and picked the christians, a small, odd, unpopular group. it's the first time rome went after them by name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_Rome","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the great fire of rome","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["philippi"],"key":"philippi","kind":"place","lines":"a roman town in northern greece, named after philip of macedon, alexander the great's father. acts says paul started the first church in europe there, in the house of a businesswoman named lydia who sold purple cloth.\nhe and his partner silas were beaten and jailed there, and sang hymns in the cell at midnight.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"philippi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["thorn in the flesh","thorn in my flesh"],"key":"thorn","kind":"word","lines":"paul's phrase, in his second letter to the corinthians, chapter 12, for something wrong with him that he begged god three times to take away. he never says what it was. an illness, bad eyes, epilepsy, a temptation, a person, people have guessed everything.\nthe answer he got was my grace is enough for you, my power is made perfect in weakness. the phrase is now ordinary english for a constant nagging problem.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thorn_in_the_flesh","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the thorn in the flesh","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["lystra"],"key":"lystra","kind":"place","lines":"a small roman town in what's now central turkey. in acts, chapter 14, paul healed a lame man there, and the crowd decided he and his partner barnabas were the greek gods hermes and zeus come down, and the priest brought bulls to sacrifice to them.\npaul tore his clothes and shouted that they were only men. a few days later a different crowd stoned him and dragged him out of town, thinking he was dead. he got up and walked back in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lystra","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lystra","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bible","the bible","bibles"],"key":"bible","kind":"book","lines":"the holy book of christianity, which is really a library, dozens of books written over about a thousand years by many different people, in hebrew, a bit of aramaic, and greek.\nthe first and bigger part is the hebrew bible, which christians call the old testament and jews call their scriptures. the second part is the new testament, about jesus and the first christians. protestant bibles have sixty six books. catholic ones have a few more.\nit's the best-selling book in history. it's got poetry, laws, history, war stories, love songs, letters, and nightmares in it, and a lot of people who swear by it have only read bits.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bible","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/index.htm","why":"the king james version, public domain"}],"name":"the bible","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hebrew bible","the hebrew bible","old testament","the old testament","jewish scriptures","tanakh"],"key":"hebrewbible","kind":"book","lines":"the scriptures of judaism, written mostly in hebrew over roughly a thousand years before jesus. jews call it the tanakh. christians kept it as the first part of their bible and call it the old testament.\nit opens with creation and the torah, the five books of moses, then histories of israel's kings, the prophets shouting at those kings, and books of poetry and wisdom like psalms and job.\nold doesn't mean replaced. for jews it's the whole thing. jesus and paul read it as their bible, because it was the only one there was.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebrew_Bible","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the hebrew bible","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["new testament","the new testament"],"key":"newtestament","kind":"book","lines":"the second, shorter part of the christian bible, twenty seven books written in greek in the first hundred years or so after jesus. four gospels about his life, the book of acts about the first christians, a pile of letters, most of them paul's, and revelation, the wild vision at the end.\nyou can read the whole thing in a few days. most of it is shorter than people expect.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Testament","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the new testament","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gospels","gospel","the four gospels"],"key":"gospels","kind":"book","lines":"the four books at the start of the new testament that tell the life of jesus, named matthew, mark, luke, and john. gospel is old english for good news.\nmark is the earliest, written around the year 70, fast and rough. matthew and luke copied a lot of mark and added their own material. john is different, later, slower, and full of long speeches. they don't agree on every detail, and never tried to.\nthey're not biographies in the modern sense. they're more like four friends each telling you who he was, the way they saw it, so you'd follow him too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the gospels","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gospel of mark","mark's gospel","mark 2","mark 2:27","mark 3","mark 15"],"key":"mark","kind":"book","lines":"the shortest of the four gospels, sixteen chapters, and probably the first written, around the year 70. it's fast. the word immediately shows up constantly. jesus heals, argues, gets misunderstood by everybody including his friends, and gets killed.\nthe oldest copies end with the women running from the empty tomb, scared, telling nobody. you can read the whole thing in about ninety minutes. start here.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Mark","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/mar.htm","why":"the king james version, public domain"}],"name":"the gospel of mark","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gospel of matthew","matthew's gospel","matthew 5","matthew 6","matthew 6:34","matthew 27"],"key":"matthew","kind":"book","lines":"the first book of the new testament, twenty eight chapters, written probably in the 80s for a mostly jewish audience. it keeps showing how jesus fits the old hebrew scriptures.\nit has the sermon on the mount, chapters 5 through 7, the three chapters where jesus lays out how to actually live. love your enemies, turn the other cheek, don't worry about tomorrow, the lord's prayer.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Matthew","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the gospel of matthew","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gospel of luke","luke's gospel","luke 15","luke 17","luke 17:21","luke 10"],"key":"luke","kind":"book","lines":"the third gospel, twenty four chapters, written by the same person who wrote the book of acts. it's the gospel most interested in the poor, women, outsiders, and people who've blown it.\nthe prodigal son and the good samaritan are only in luke. chapter 15 is three stories in a row about something lost and found, a sheep, a coin, and a son, and a party every time.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Luke","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the gospel of luke","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gospel of john","john's gospel","john 11","john 8"],"key":"john","kind":"book","lines":"the fourth gospel, twenty one chapters, written last, probably in the 90s. it's different from the other three. slower, more mystical, with long speeches, and it opens with in the beginning was the word.\nit has water turned into wine, lazarus raised from the dead, the woman caught in adultery, and the shortest verse in the bible, jesus wept.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_John","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the gospel of john","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["parable","parables"],"key":"parable","kind":"word","lines":"a short story with a point you have to find yourself. jesus taught mostly in parables, about farmers, seeds, lost sheep, bosses, debts, and parties.\nthey often don't end the way you'd expect, and he rarely explained them. the point is to leave you arguing with it on the walk home.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parables_of_Jesus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"parable","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["prodigal son","the prodigal son"],"key":"prodigalson","kind":"event","lines":"a story jesus tells in luke 15. a younger son asks for his inheritance early, which is like telling your father you wish he was dead, and blows it all partying in another country. broke and feeding pigs, he heads home to beg for a job as a hired hand.\nhis father sees him far off and runs down the road to him, an old man running, which rich men didn't do, and throws a party before the kid finishes his apology. the older brother who stayed and worked is furious and won't come in. the story ends with the father pleading with him. we never find out if he goes in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parable_of_the_Prodigal_Son","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the prodigal son","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness","family"]},{"aliases":["good samaritan","the good samaritan","samaritan"],"key":"goodsamaritan","kind":"event","lines":"a story jesus tells in luke 10, when a lawyer asks him who counts as my neighbor. a man gets beaten and left for dead on the road. a priest walks by on the other side. a temple worker does the same. then a samaritan, from a people the jews of that time despised, stops, bandages him, takes him to an inn, and pays for his stay.\nthe point was that the hero was the guy you hated. today good samaritan laws protect people who stop to help at an accident. the sting of the original is harder to feel. imagine the hero being whoever your side can't stand.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parable_of_the_Good_Samaritan","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samaritans","why":"the samaritans, who still exist"}],"name":"the good samaritan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kingdom of god","the kingdom of god","kingdom of heaven"],"key":"kingdomofgod","kind":"word","lines":"the main thing jesus talked about. mostly he meant god's way of doing things showing up here and now, where the last are first, the poor are blessed, and enemies get loved.\nhe said it was near, and that it was within you, or among you, the greek can go either way. tolstoy built a whole book on that line.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_God_(Christianity)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the kingdom of god","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sabbath","the sabbath","shabbat"],"key":"sabbath","kind":"word","lines":"the weekly day of rest in judaism, from sundown friday to sundown saturday, commanded in the ten commandments because god rested on the seventh day. no work at all. families eat together, pray, and rest.\nreligious rules grew up around it about what counted as work, and jesus got in trouble for healing on it. most christians later moved their day to sunday. observant jews still keep it, phones off, for twenty five hours.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shabbat","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sabbath","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["crucifixion","crucified","crucify"],"key":"crucifixion","kind":"event","lines":"the roman way of executing slaves, rebels, and the lowest criminals. they nailed or tied a person to a wooden cross and left them to die slowly in public, over hours or days, by exhaustion and suffocation. it was meant to terrify and humiliate.\nafter the slave revolt led by spartacus, the romans crucified six thousand men along one road. jesus was crucified outside jerusalem under the roman governor pontius pilate around the year 30. that's why the cross, an execution device, became the christian symbol. think of wearing a little electric chair around your neck.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crucifixion_of_Jesus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"crucifixion","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["resurrection","the resurrection","rose from the dead","came back from the dead"],"key":"resurrection","kind":"event","lines":"the christian belief that jesus came back to life on the third day after he was crucified. his friends said they found the tomb empty and then saw him, ate with him, and touched him.\nit's the center of christianity, and easter is the day it's celebrated. paul said if it didn't happen, the whole thing is pointless. people have been arguing about what happened that weekend ever since.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resurrection_of_Jesus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the resurrection","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["messiah","the messiah"],"key":"messiah","kind":"word","lines":"hebrew for the anointed one, meaning a king chosen by god, since kings in israel were anointed with oil. jews in jesus's time were waiting for a messiah to throw out the romans and set things right.\nchristians say jesus was the messiah. christ is just the greek word for the same thing. most jews say the messiah hasn't come yet, since the world is obviously still not fixed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messiah","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"messiah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rabbi","rabbis"],"key":"rabbi","kind":"word","lines":"hebrew for my teacher. today it's a trained jewish religious leader, who teaches, leads prayers, and answers questions of jewish law, roughly the jewish version of a pastor.\nin jesus's time it was a respectful title for any teacher, and people in the gospels call jesus rabbi.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rabbi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nazareth"],"key":"nazareth","kind":"place","lines":"the village in galilee, in the north of what's now israel, where jesus grew up. back then it was a few hundred people, a nowhere place nobody expected anything from.\ntoday it's a city of about eighty thousand, the biggest arab city in israel.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazareth","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nazareth","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["galilee","sea of galilee"],"key":"galilee","kind":"place","lines":"the hilly region in the north of what's now israel, around a big freshwater lake called the sea of galilee. farming and fishing country, far from the big city and the temple in jerusalem.\njesus did most of his teaching there, and several of his closest friends were fishermen from the lake. people in jerusalem looked down on galileans as country folk with funny accents.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galilee","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"galilee","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jordan river","the jordan river","the jordan"],"key":"jordanriver","kind":"place","lines":"the river that runs south from the hills of the north, through the sea of galilee, down to the dead sea. part of it is the border between israel and the country of jordan today.\nin the bible the israelites cross it into the promised land, and jesus is baptized in it. crossing jordan became a phrase in black spirituals for going to freedom, or to heaven.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jordan_River","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the jordan river","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john the baptist"],"key":"johnbaptist","kind":"person","lines":"a wild jewish preacher who lived out in the desert near the jordan river, wore camel hair, ate locusts and wild honey, and told everyone to turn their lives around before it was too late. he dunked people in the river as a sign they were starting over.\njesus came to him to be baptized, and that's where his public life starts. john criticized the local king, herod antipas, for marrying his brother's wife, got thrown in prison, and was beheaded at a party when the queen's daughter asked for his head on a platter.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Baptist","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john the baptist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["baptism","baptize","baptizing","baptized","baptised"],"key":"baptism","kind":"word","lines":"a ritual of washing with water that marks starting over, being cleaned, and joining the community. john the baptist dunked people in the river. christians made it the way you become a christian.\nsome churches sprinkle babies. others wait until you're old enough to choose and put you all the way under. either way it's the same picture, going under and coming up new.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baptism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"baptism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tax collector","tax collectors"],"key":"taxcollectors","kind":"group","lines":"in jesus's time, local jews who collected money for the roman occupiers and made their living by charging more than was owed and keeping the difference. collaborators and crooks, in most people's eyes.\njesus ate at their houses, which scandalized everybody, and one of his twelve closest followers, matthew, had been one. imagine a preacher having dinner at the house of the guy who repossesses cars for the mob.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publican","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tax collectors","status":"approved","threads":["money"]},{"aliases":["jerusalem"],"key":"jerusalem","kind":"place","lines":"the holy city of judaism, and holy to christians and muslims too. in jesus's time it was the capital, with the great temple where the priests offered sacrifices, and pilgrims poured in for the festivals.\njesus was arrested and crucified there, and the first church started there. the romans destroyed the temple in the year 70. all that's left is a piece of its outer wall, the western wall, where jews still pray.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerusalem","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jerusalem","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gethsemane","garden of gethsemane"],"key":"gethsemane","kind":"place","lines":"an olive grove at the foot of the mount of olives, just outside jerusalem's walls. the name means oil press. jesus went there to pray the night he was arrested, while his friends kept falling asleep.\nit's where he asked god to take the cup away, and where one of his own, judas, led the soldiers to him with a kiss. there's still an olive garden there, with trees that are very, very old.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gethsemane","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gethsemane","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lazarus"],"key":"lazarus","kind":"person","lines":"a friend of jesus, the brother of martha and mary, in a village called bethany just outside jerusalem. in john's gospel, chapter 11, he dies, and jesus arrives four days late.\njesus sees the sisters crying and cries too. jesus wept, the shortest verse in the bible. then he has them open the tomb and calls lazarus out, and he walks out wrapped in burial cloths.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazarus_of_Bethany","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lazarus","status":"approved","threads":["dying","grief"]},{"aliases":["woman caught in adultery","the woman caught in adultery","throw the first rock","cast the first stone"],"key":"adultery","kind":"event","lines":"a story in john's gospel, chapter 8. religious men drag a woman caught sleeping with a man who wasn't her husband in front of jesus. the law said she could be stoned to death. what should we do, they ask, trying to trap him.\nhe writes something in the dirt with his finger, nobody knows what, and says let whoever has never done wrong throw the first stone. they leave one by one, oldest first. the story isn't in the oldest copies of john, and it might have been added later. the churches kept it anyway, because it sounds like him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesus_and_the_woman_taken_in_adultery","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the woman caught in adultery","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["cana","wedding at cana","water into wine"],"key":"cana","kind":"event","lines":"a village in galilee where, in john's gospel, chapter 2, jesus went to a wedding with his mother. the wine ran out, a disaster for the family. his mother told him to do something. he told the servants to fill six big stone jars with water, and it came out wine, and good wine.\njohn calls it his first sign. the first miracle in the story is keeping a party going.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marriage_at_Cana","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["psalms","psalm","book of psalms","psalm 22"],"key":"psalms","kind":"book","lines":"a book of the bible made of a hundred and fifty songs and prayers, many credited to king david. they cover everything. praise, thanks, rage, grief, revenge fantasies, depression, and god why have you left me.\nthey were the prayer book of jesus, and they're still sung in synagogues and monasteries every day. monks go through all hundred and fifty every week or two.\npsalm 22, the one jesus quoted on the cross, starts with my god, my god, why have you forsaken me and ends in praise. psalm 23 is the lord is my shepherd, the one read at funerals.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psalms","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psalm_22","why":"the one jesus quoted on the cross"}],"name":"psalms","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["four noble truths","the four noble truths"],"key":"fournobletruths","kind":"word","lines":"the first thing the buddha taught after he woke up, the core of all buddhism. one, life involves suffering, things hurt and don't satisfy. two, the suffering comes from craving, from grabbing at things to be other than they are. three, it can stop. four, here's the path that stops it.\nit's set up like a doctor's visit. symptom, cause, prognosis, treatment. the fourth truth, the treatment, is the eightfold path, a set of practices for how you see, speak, act, work, and meditate.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Noble_Truths","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the four noble truths","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sutta","suttas","sutra","sutras"],"key":"sutta","kind":"word","lines":"one of the buddha's recorded talks. sutta is the pali word, sutra the sanskrit one, and it means thread, like a thread of teaching.\nthere are thousands of them. most start the same way, thus have i heard, and then say where the buddha was staying and who came to see him. they were memorized and chanted by monks for centuries before they were written down.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutta_Piṭaka","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sutta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pali canon","the pali canon","pali","tipitaka"],"key":"palicanon","kind":"book","lines":"the oldest complete collection of the buddha's teachings, kept in pali, an old indian language close to what he spoke. memorized and passed down by chanting for about four hundred years, then written on palm leaves in sri lanka around the first century bc.\nit's huge, dozens of volumes in english, split into three baskets, the rules for monks, the talks, and the philosophy. it's the scripture of theravada buddhism, the kind practiced in sri lanka, thailand, burma, and cambodia.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pali_Canon","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the texts, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/","why":"a big free library of translated talks from the pali canon"}],"name":"the pali canon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["anguttara nikaya","the anguttara nikaya","numbered talks"],"key":"anguttara","kind":"book","lines":"one of the big collections of the buddha's talks in the pali canon, called the numbered discourses. the talks are sorted by how many items they list, first the ones about one thing, then two things, and so on up to eleven.\nthe kalama sutta is in the book of threes. it sounds dry and it's full of good stuff.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aṅguttara_Nikāya","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the anguttara nikaya","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["majjhima nikaya","the majjhima nikaya","middle-length talks","middle length discourses"],"key":"majjhima","kind":"book","lines":"one of the big collections of the buddha's talks in the pali canon, a hundred and fifty two middle-length discourses. a lot of the famous stories are here, the poisoned arrow, the raft, the serial killer angulimala.\nif you want the buddha as a person, arguing, joking, and handling people, this is the book.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majjhima_Nikaya","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the majjhima nikaya","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kalama sutta","the kalama sutta","kalamas","the kalamas"],"key":"kalamasutta","kind":"book","lines":"a short talk the buddha gave to the kalamas, the people of a town called kesaputta. every teacher who comes through, they told him, praises his own ideas and trashes everybody else's. how are we supposed to know who's right?\nhe told them not to go by tradition, rumor, scripture, logic, a teacher's reputation, or even him. when you know for yourselves that something leads to harm, drop it. when you know it leads to good, keep doing it. it's been called the buddha's charter of free inquiry.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalama_Sutta","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/an/an03/an03.065.than.html","why":"the whole talk in english, a few minutes to read"}],"name":"the kalama sutta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["poisoned arrow","the poisoned arrow","malunkya sutta","shorter malunkya sutta"],"key":"poisonedarrow","kind":"event","lines":"a story the buddha told a monk named malunkyaputta, who said he'd quit unless the buddha answered the big questions. is the universe eternal? is it infinite? does a buddha exist after death?\nthe buddha said it's like a man shot with a poisoned arrow who won't let the doctor pull it out until he knows who shot it, what caste he was, how tall, what the bow was made of. he'll die first. i teach suffering and the end of suffering, he said. the rest doesn't help.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parable_of_the_Poisoned_Arrow","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/mn/mn.063.than.html","why":"the whole talk in english"}],"name":"the poisoned arrow","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the raft","raft simile","alagaddupama sutta"],"key":"raft","kind":"event","lines":"a picture the buddha used in a talk sometimes called the snake simile. a man comes to a big river with danger on this side and safety on the other. he ties a raft together from sticks and branches and paddles across.\nonce he's on the other side, does he carry the raft on his head for the rest of the trip? no. the teachings are the raft. they're for crossing, not for holding onto, not even the buddha's own.","links":[{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/mn/mn.022.than.html","why":"the snake talk with the raft in it, in english"}],"name":"the raft","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kushinagar","kusinara"],"key":"kushinagar","kind":"place","lines":"a small town in northern india, in the state of uttar pradesh, where the buddha died at about eighty, lying on his right side between two trees.\nit's one of the four big buddhist pilgrimage sites, with a temple holding a huge statue of the buddha lying down. mingyur rinpoche nearly died there too, of food poisoning, as a wandering beggar in 2011.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kushinagar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kushinagar","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["yasodhara"],"key":"yasodhara","kind":"person","lines":"the buddha's wife. they were married young, and she gave birth to their son the same night, or near it, that he left home to become a wandering monk.\nthe tradition says she later shaved her head and became a nun herself. she doesn't get many lines in the old texts. modern writers have started giving her some.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yasodhara","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"yasodhara","status":"approved","threads":["family"]},{"aliases":["rahula"],"key":"rahula","kind":"person","lines":"the buddha's son. the story is that when the buddha heard he'd been born, he said, a fetter has been born, which is what rahula means, something that ties you down.\nwhen he was about seven, his mother sent him to ask his father for his inheritance, and the buddha had him ordained as a novice monk instead. there's a talk the buddha gave him about not lying, using a water dipper to show him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rāhula","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rahula","status":"approved","threads":["family"]},{"aliases":["dhammapada","the dhammapada"],"key":"dhammapada","kind":"book","lines":"a short book of the buddha's sayings in verse, 423 of them, in twenty six little chapters. it's the most read buddhist text in the world and the one most people start with.\nit opens with the mind comes first, we are what we think. it's the kind of book you can open anywhere and get something. you could carry it in your back pocket.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhammapada","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/kn/dhp/index.html","why":"the whole book in english"}],"name":"the dhammapada","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sona sutta","the sona sutta"],"key":"sonasutta","kind":"book","lines":"a short talk to a monk named sona, who pushed so hard at walking meditation that his feet bled, and got nowhere, and thought about quitting.\nthe buddha asked him if he'd played the lute before he was a monk. when the strings were too tight, did it sound good? too loose? no. it's the same with effort, he said. tune it. it's the source of the middle way people talk about.","links":[{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/an/an06/an06.055.than.html","why":"the whole talk in english"}],"name":"the sona sutta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["in the buddha's words"],"key":"inthebuddhaswords","kind":"book","lines":"a 2005 book by bhikkhu bodhi, a single-volume collection of the buddha's talks from the pali canon, sorted by topic with short introductions.\nif you want to read the buddha himself and don't know where to start in a library of dozens of volumes, this is the book.","links":[],"name":"in the buddha's words","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bhikkhu bodhi"],"key":"bhikkhubodhi","kind":"person","lines":"an american buddhist monk, born in brooklyn in 1944, who was ordained in sri lanka in 1972 and has spent his life translating the buddha's talks from pali into clear english.\nhis translations are the standard ones. he also started buddhist global relief, a charity that fights hunger.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhikkhu_Bodhi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bhikkhu bodhi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mara"],"key":"mara","kind":"person","lines":"the demon of buddhist stories, the tempter. he stands for everything that keeps you stuck, desire, fear, doubt, distraction, death. under the tree he sent his armies and his daughters at the buddha, and the buddha didn't move.\nin the old stories he keeps showing up and the buddha keeps saying i see you, mara, and he leaves. a lot of teachers use that as practice. when the fear or the craving comes, you name it and don't feed it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mara_(demon)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mara","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["brahma"],"key":"brahma","kind":"person","lines":"in hinduism, the creator god, one of the big three with vishnu the preserver and shiva the destroyer. he's shown with four faces looking in four directions.\nin buddhist stories he's a powerful god, but still inside the world. he comes to the buddha and begs him to teach, because even the gods need it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahma","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"brahma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["angulimala"],"key":"angulimala","kind":"person","lines":"a serial killer in the buddha's time who lived on the roads and wore a necklace of his victims' fingers. that's what his name means.\nthe buddha walked straight toward him on the road. angulimala ran after him and couldn't catch him, and shouted stop. the buddha said, i've stopped. it's you who hasn't. angulimala threw down his sword and became a monk. he's the buddhist proof that nobody's too far gone, like milarepa for tibet.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aṅgulimāla","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"angulimala","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["devadatta"],"key":"devadatta","kind":"person","lines":"the buddha's cousin, who became a monk and then got jealous. he wanted to run the order. when the buddha said no, he tried to have him killed, once by rolling a boulder down a hill at him, once by setting a drunk elephant loose on him.\nthe elephant stopped in front of the buddha and knelt. devadatta split off with his own group of monks. he's the judas of buddhism.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devadatta","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"devadatta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shakyas","the shakyas","shakya"],"key":"shakya","kind":"group","lines":"the buddha's clan, a small ruling people in the foothills of the himalayas, on today's border of india and nepal. his father was one of their leaders. one of the buddha's names, shakyamuni, means the sage of the shakyas.\nthe story goes that late in the buddha's life a neighboring king wiped them out, and the buddha couldn't stop it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shakya","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the shakyas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ananda"],"key":"ananda","kind":"person","lines":"the buddha's cousin and his personal attendant for the last twenty five years of his life. he went everywhere with him and had an incredible memory.\nafter the buddha died, the monks gathered to recite everything he'd taught, and ananda recited the talks. that's why so many begin with thus have i heard. it was ananda talking.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ananda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ananda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bodhi tree","the bodhi tree","bo tree"],"key":"bodhitree","kind":"place","lines":"the fig tree at bodh gaya, in northeast india, that the buddha sat under the night he woke up. bodhi means awakening.\na tree grown from a descendant of it still stands at the same spot, and pilgrims come from all over the world. a cutting taken to sri lanka in the 200s bc is still alive, one of the oldest planted trees on earth with a known date.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodhi_Tree","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the bodhi tree","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nirvana","nibbana"],"key":"nirvana","kind":"word","lines":"the goal of buddhism, the end of suffering. the word means blowing out, like a candle. what gets blown out is the fire of craving, hatred, and confusion.\nit's not heaven and it's not being wiped out. the buddha mostly refused to describe it, because any description would turn it into one more thing to grab. in english it came to mean bliss, and then a band. the original is quieter.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nirvana_(Buddhism)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nirvana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["enlightenment","enlightened"],"key":"enlightenment","kind":"word","lines":"the english word usually used for what happened to the buddha under the tree. the old words mean waking up. you see how things actually are, and the grabbing and the fear let go.\nevery tradition on these pages has some version of it, and most of the teachers say not to make it a trophy. there's an old zen saying. before enlightenment, chop wood, carry water. after enlightenment, chop wood, carry water.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enlightenment_in_Buddhism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"enlightenment","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dharma","dhamma"],"key":"dharma","kind":"word","lines":"a big word from india with a few meanings. in buddhism it mostly means the teaching, what the buddha taught, and also the way things really are. in hinduism it means duty, your right way of living, the job that's yours.\nwhen krishna tells arjuna to fight, he's telling him to do his dharma. when a buddhist says they practice the dharma, they mean the whole path.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dharma","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dharma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sangha"],"key":"sangha","kind":"group","lines":"the buddhist community. originally it meant the monks and nuns. now it often means anyone you practice with, the people in the room.\nbuddhists take refuge in three things, the buddha, the dharma, and the sangha. the point is you don't do this alone. thich nhat hanh said the next buddha might be a sangha.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sangha","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sangha","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mahayana","mahayana buddhism"],"key":"mahayana","kind":"group","lines":"the great vehicle, one of the two big branches of buddhism, which grew up in india a few centuries after the buddha and spread to tibet, china, japan, korea, and vietnam. zen and tibetan buddhism are both mahayana.\nits big ideal is the bodhisattva, who puts off their own freedom to help everyone. it added a lot of new scriptures that the older branch doesn't accept.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahayana","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mahayana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["theravada","theravada buddhism"],"key":"theravada","kind":"group","lines":"the teaching of the elders, the oldest surviving branch of buddhism, practiced mainly in sri lanka, thailand, burma, laos, and cambodia. its scripture is the pali canon.\nit's simpler and closer to the old talks. monks in saffron robes, alms rounds at dawn, and the kind of mindfulness meditation that got carried to america in the seventies.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theravada","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"theravada","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bhagavad gita","the bhagavad gita","gita","the gita","bhagavadgita"],"key":"gita","kind":"book","lines":"the song of god, seven hundred verses of sanskrit poetry, eighteen chapters, maybe two thousand years old. it's a conversation on a battlefield between a prince named arjuna, who's frozen and won't fight, and his chariot driver, krishna, who turns out to be god.\nit's a small piece of the much bigger mahabharata, but it's the part everybody reads. the core of it is do your work, let go of how it turns out, and know that the real you can't be killed.\nit's the closest thing hinduism has to a single bible. gandhi read it every day. thoreau took it to walden pond. oppenheimer quoted it at the first atomic bomb test.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhagavad_Gita","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/gita/index.htm","why":"edwin arnold's old english verse version, public domain"}],"name":"the bhagavad gita","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mahabharata","the mahabharata"],"key":"mahabharata","kind":"book","lines":"india's giant old epic, around a hundred thousand verses, about ten times the length of the iliad and the odyssey together. it took shape over centuries and was finished maybe around 400.\nthe main story is a feud between two sets of cousins, the five pandava brothers and the hundred kaurava brothers, over a kingdom, which ends in a war that kills almost everyone. packed around that are hundreds of other stories, lessons, and arguments about how to live.\nindians say what's in it can be found elsewhere, and what's not in it can't be found anywhere. when it was made into a tv series in the late eighties, streets across india emptied on sunday mornings.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahabharata","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mahabharata","status":"approved","threads":["war","family"]},{"aliases":["arjuna"],"key":"arjuna","kind":"person","lines":"the third of the five pandava brothers in the mahabharata, the greatest archer of his time, and krishna's close friend and brother-in-law.\nin the gita he's the one who looks across the battlefield at his cousins, teachers, and elders, drops his bow, and says he can't do it. everybody who's ever frozen in front of something they had to do is arjuna.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arjuna","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"arjuna","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kurukshetra"],"key":"kurukshetra","kind":"place","lines":"the battlefield where the war in the mahabharata is fought and where the gita takes place. it's also a real town in the state of haryana, north of delhi, and pilgrims go there.\nthe war lasts eighteen days in the story. by the end almost everyone on both sides is dead.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurukshetra_War","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kurukshetra","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["hinduism","hindu","hindus"],"key":"hinduism","kind":"group","lines":"the main religion of india, and one of the oldest in the world, with roots going back more than three thousand years. it has no single founder and no single holy book. it's more like a huge family of traditions sharing some big ideas.\nthose ideas include karma, rebirth, dharma, and the hope of getting free of the cycle. there are many gods, and many hindus say they're all faces of one reality. about 1.2 billion people are hindu, most of them in india.\nyoga, meditation, and the word guru all came to the west from here.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hinduism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hinduism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["avatar","avatars"],"key":"avatar","kind":"word","lines":"sanskrit for descent. in hinduism it's a god, usually vishnu, coming down to earth in a body to set things right when the world goes bad. krishna and rama are the two most loved avatars of vishnu.\nthe internet borrowed it for your little picture online, and a movie borrowed it for blue aliens. the old meaning is god wearing a body.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avatar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"avatar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vishnu"],"key":"vishnu","kind":"person","lines":"one of the main gods of hinduism, the preserver, the one who keeps the world going. he's usually shown blue, with four arms, lying on a giant snake floating on the cosmic ocean.\nwhenever things fall apart, the story goes, he comes down in a new form to fix it. krishna is one of those forms.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vishnu","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"vishnu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lila","divine play"],"key":"lila","kind":"word","lines":"sanskrit for play. in hinduism it's the idea that the world is god playing, the way a kid plays, for the joy of it and not to get anything.\nkrishna stealing butter as a baby, dancing with the milkmaids, tricking everyone, is lila. it's a way of saying god isn't grim.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lila_(Hinduism)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lila","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gandhari"],"key":"gandhari","kind":"person","lines":"a queen in the mahabharata, the mother of the hundred kaurava brothers. her husband was born blind, and when she married him she tied a cloth over her own eyes and wore it for the rest of her life.\nall hundred of her sons died in the war. she blamed krishna, who could have stopped it, and cursed him and his whole clan. he accepted the curse.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gandhari","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gandhari","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yadavas","the yadavas","yadava"],"key":"yadava","kind":"group","lines":"krishna's own clan in the mahabharata. years after the war, at a drunken festival by the sea, they turned on each other and killed each other off, fulfilling gandhari's curse.\ntoday yadav is one of the biggest community names in northern india, and many trace their family back to krishna's clan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yadava","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the yadavas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["oppenheimer","robert oppenheimer","j. robert oppenheimer"],"key":"oppenheimer","kind":"person","lines":"an american physicist who lived from 1904 to 1967 and ran the lab at los alamos, in new mexico, that built the first atomic bombs during world war two.\nhe'd learned sanskrit to read the gita in the original. after the first test, in july 1945, he said a line from chapter 11 had come to him, now i am become death, the destroyer of worlds. he spent the rest of his life trying to control the thing he'd built. christopher nolan's 2023 movie oppenheimer is about him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._Robert_Oppenheimer","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"robert oppenheimer","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["eknath easwaran","easwaran"],"key":"easwaran","kind":"person","lines":"an indian english professor who came to america in 1959, taught meditation at berkeley in the sixties, and lived there till 1999. he started the blue mountain center of meditation.\nhis translations of the gita, the upanishads, and the dhammapada are warm, clear, and easy, with good introductions.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eknath_Easwaran","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eknath easwaran","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["barbara stoler miller","stoler miller"],"key":"stolermiller","kind":"person","lines":"an american scholar of sanskrit who taught at barnard and columbia in new york and died in 1993. her 1986 translation of the gita is spare and clean, and it's the one a lot of college classes use.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbara_Stoler_Miller","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"barbara stoler miller","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sanskrit"],"key":"sanskrit","kind":"word","lines":"the old classical language of india, the language of the gita, the vedas, and a lot of buddhist scripture. it's to india what latin was to europe, the language of priests and scholars long after people stopped speaking it at home.\nit's a cousin of english, way back. that's why the sanskrit for mother is matar.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanskrit","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sanskrit","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["paths to god"],"key":"pathstogod","kind":"book","lines":"ram dass's 2004 book on the bhagavad gita, built from talks he gave in the seventies. he walks through it chapter by chapter as a guide for living, full of stories from his own mess.","links":[],"name":"paths to god","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["satan","the satan","the accuser"],"key":"satan","kind":"person","lines":"in the hebrew of the book of job, ha-satan means the accuser, or the adversary. he's one of the beings in god's court, a kind of prosecutor whose job is to test people, and he answers to god.\nthe red devil with horns and a pitchfork, lord of hell and enemy of god, came much later. in job he's more like an internal affairs officer with a mean streak.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the satan (the accuser)","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["whirlwind","out of the whirlwind"],"key":"whirlwind","kind":"event","lines":"in the book of job, chapter 38, god finally answers job out of a whirlwind, a storm. he doesn't explain anything. he asks job where he was when the world was made, and walks him through the stars, the sea, the wild animals.\nit's four chapters of the biggest speech in the bible, and it's all questions.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Job","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/job.htm","why":"the book of job in the king james version"}],"name":"the whirlwind","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["behemoth"],"key":"behemoth","kind":"word","lines":"a huge beast god shows off to job in chapter 40, with bones like bronze pipes and a tail like a cedar tree. most scholars think the writer was picturing a hippo, or an elephant, blown up to mythic size.\nin english the word now means anything enormous, a behemoth of a truck.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Behemoth","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"behemoth","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leviathan"],"key":"leviathan","kind":"word","lines":"a sea monster in the bible, a giant snake or dragon that nobody can catch. in job, chapter 41, god says, can you pull in leviathan with a fishhook? the answer is no, and god can.\nit probably started as an old canaanite monster of chaos. later thomas hobbes named his famous book about government after it, and in modern hebrew the word just means whale.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leviathan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leviathan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["land of uz","the land of uz"],"key":"uz","kind":"place","lines":"where the book of job says job lived. nobody knows where it was, maybe east of israel in what's now jordan, or in arabia.\nthe point may be that job isn't an israelite. the bible's great story about suffering stars a foreigner.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_of_Uz","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the land of uz","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hebrew","hebrews"],"key":"hebrew","kind":"word","lines":"the language of the jewish people and of most of the hebrew bible. it stopped being a spoken language for most of two thousand years and lived on in prayer and study, until it was brought back to everyday life in the late 1800s. today it's the language of israel.\nin the old stories hebrews is also an early name for the israelites themselves.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebrew_language","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hebrew","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["israelites","the israelites","israelite"],"key":"israelites","kind":"group","lines":"the people of ancient israel, the twelve tribes the bible traces back to the twelve sons of jacob, whom god renamed israel. they're the ancestors of the jewish people.\nthe exodus story is theirs. slaves in egypt, freed, forty years in the desert, then into the land of canaan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israelites","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the israelites","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["exodus","book of exodus","the exodus","exodus 3","exodus 33","exodus 4"],"key":"exodus","kind":"book","lines":"the second book of the bible, forty chapters. the israelites are slaves in egypt, moses is born and hidden in a basket, god calls him from a burning bush, the plagues hit egypt, the sea splits, and at mount sinai god gives the ten commandments.\nexodus means going out. jews retell it every spring at passover, with a meal. enslaved black americans sang it, go down moses, let my people go. it's the biggest freedom story in the west.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Exodus","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/exo.htm","why":"the book of exodus in the king james version"}],"name":"exodus","status":"approved","threads":["exile","wandering"]},{"aliases":["pharaoh","pharaohs"],"key":"pharaoh","kind":"person","lines":"the title of the kings of ancient egypt, who were treated as gods. the word means great house, like saying the white house when you mean the president.\nthe bible never names the pharaoh of the exodus. people often guess ramesses ii, around 1250 bc, but there's no egyptian record of the story at all, and a lot of historians think it's a people's memory more than a history.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharaoh","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pharaoh","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["burning bush","the burning bush","a bush caught fire"],"key":"burningbush","kind":"event","lines":"the moment in exodus, chapter 3, when moses, a fugitive tending sheep in the desert, sees a bush on fire that doesn't burn up. a voice from it tells him to take off his sandals, because he's standing on holy ground, and to go back to egypt and free his people.\nmoses argues. what follows is the strangest job interview in history, with god as the boss and moses giving every excuse he's got.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burning_bush","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the burning bush","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["i am who i am","i am that i am"],"key":"iamwhoiam","kind":"word","lines":"what god answers in exodus 3 when moses asks for his name. in hebrew it's ehyeh asher ehyeh, which can also mean i will be what i will be.\nit's the root of the name of god jews write as four letters and don't say out loud. it isn't a name you can hold on to. it's more like, i'm the one who is. that's all you get.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Am_that_I_Am","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"i am who i am","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["aaron"],"key":"aaron","kind":"person","lines":"moses's older brother. when moses said he couldn't talk, god sent aaron to do the talking. he became israel's first high priest.\nhe's also the one who made the golden calf while moses was up the mountain, because the people asked for a god they could see. the man chosen to lead worship built the idol.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aaron","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"aaron","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["miriam"],"key":"miriam","kind":"person","lines":"moses's older sister. as a girl she watched the basket in the reeds and got their own mother hired as his nurse. after the sea crossing she led the women dancing with tambourines.\nlater she and aaron complained about moses's wife, and she was struck with a skin disease for a week. the people wouldn't move on until she came back.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miriam","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"miriam","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mount sinai","sinai"],"key":"mountsinai","kind":"place","lines":"the mountain in the desert where, in the bible, god gave moses the law and the ten commandments. nobody knows for sure which mountain it was.\nthe traditional one is a granite peak in egypt's sinai peninsula, with an old monastery at its foot. tourists climb it at night to watch the sun come up from the top.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Sinai","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mount sinai","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ten commandments","the ten commandments"],"key":"tencommandments","kind":"word","lines":"the ten basic rules god gives moses on two stone tablets at mount sinai, in exodus 20. one god, no idols, don't misuse god's name, keep the sabbath, honor your parents, don't murder, don't cheat on your spouse, don't steal, don't lie about people, don't want what's theirs.\nthey're the core of jewish and christian ethics. in america people still fight in court over putting them up in public buildings.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ten_Commandments","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ten commandments","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the sea split","red sea","parting of the red sea"],"key":"redsea","kind":"event","lines":"the story in exodus, chapter 14, where the israelites, running from egypt with pharaoh's army behind them, get trapped at the sea. moses holds out his staff, a wind blows all night, the water splits, and they walk across on dry ground. the egyptians follow and the sea comes back down on them.\nthe hebrew actually says sea of reeds, which may mean a marsh. either way, it's the escape every enslaved people since has pointed to.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossing_the_Red_Sea","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the parting of the sea","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["golden calf","the golden calf"],"key":"goldencalf","kind":"event","lines":"while moses was up mount sinai for forty days, the people got scared he wasn't coming back and asked aaron to make them a god they could see. he melted down their gold earrings and made a calf, and they partied around it.\nmoses came down, saw it, and smashed the stone tablets. golden calf still means anything people worship that isn't worth it, usually money.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_calf","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the golden calf","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["promised land","the promised land"],"key":"promisedland","kind":"place","lines":"the land god promised to abraham and his descendants in the bible, canaan, roughly modern israel and the palestinian territories. the whole exodus is a walk toward it.\nmoses led the people for forty years and died looking at it from a mountain. king used the same picture the night before he was killed. i may not get there with you.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Promised_Land","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the promised land","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["canaan","canaanite","canaanites"],"key":"canaan","kind":"place","lines":"the old name for the land along the eastern mediterranean, roughly today's israel, the palestinian territories, lebanon, and parts of jordan and syria. the canaanites were the peoples living there before and alongside the israelites.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canaan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"canaan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["torah","the torah"],"key":"torah","kind":"book","lines":"the first five books of the bible, genesis, exodus, leviticus, numbers, and deuteronomy, which tradition says moses wrote. torah means teaching, or instruction.\nit's the most sacred part of the jewish scriptures. every synagogue has it handwritten on a scroll, and they read through the whole thing aloud every year, a piece every week. when they finish, they dance with the scroll and start again.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torah","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the torah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["deuteronomy","deuteronomy 34"],"key":"deuteronomy","kind":"book","lines":"the fifth book of the bible, moses's farewell speeches at the edge of the promised land, retelling the whole story and the law to a new generation that didn't see egypt.\nit has the line jews say every morning and night, hear o israel, the lord is our god, the lord is one. chapter 34, the last, is moses's death on the mountain. nobody knows where he's buried to this day, it says.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Deuteronomy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"deuteronomy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["book of numbers","the book of numbers","numbers 11","numbers 12"],"key":"numbers","kind":"book","lines":"the fourth book of the bible, named for the censuses, the head counts of the tribes at the start and end. in between it's the forty years in the desert, full of complaining, rebellions, snakes, and a talking donkey.\nit's where moses tells god to just kill him because he can't carry these people anymore.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Numbers","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the book of numbers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["genesis","book of genesis"],"key":"genesis","kind":"book","lines":"the first book of the bible. creation in six days, adam and eve and the snake, cain killing abel, noah's flood, the tower of babel, and then the family stories of abraham, isaac, jacob, and joseph and his brothers.\ngenesis means beginning. it's where most of the stories everybody half knows come from.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Genesis","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"genesis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jethro"],"key":"jethro","kind":"person","lines":"moses's father-in-law, a priest of midian, a desert people, not an israelite. moses lived with him for years, tending his sheep, after running from egypt.\nlater he watched moses trying to settle everyone's disputes alone from dawn to dark and told him he'd burn out, and to appoint judges. moses listened. the druze, a religious community in lebanon, syria, and israel, honor him as their chief prophet.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jethro_(biblical_figure)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jethro","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["midian","midianite","midianites"],"key":"midian","kind":"place","lines":"a desert region in the bible, probably in the northwest of the arabian peninsula, east of the gulf of aqaba. moses ran there after killing the egyptian, married a local priest's daughter, and lived as a shepherd.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"midian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cush","kush"],"key":"cush","kind":"place","lines":"the ancient kingdom south of egypt along the nile, in what's now sudan, also called nubia. for a while its kings ruled egypt itself. it has more pyramids than egypt does.\nmoses's wife in numbers 12 is called a cushite woman, most likely a black african, and his brother and sister complain about her. god takes moses's side.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kush","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cush","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["missionaries of charity","the missionaries of charity"],"key":"missionariesofcharity","kind":"group","lines":"the order of nuns mother teresa started in calcutta. rome signed off on it in 1950 with a handful of women, most of them her old students.\nthey wear the white cotton sari with the blue stripes, own almost nothing, and run homes for the dying, orphanages, soup kitchens, and leprosy clinics. by the time she died in 1997 there were thousands of sisters in over a hundred countries.\npicture a charity with no fundraising gala and no ad budget that somehow ended up everywhere. the sisters are still at it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missionaries_of_Charity","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the missionaries of charity","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["calcutta","kolkata"],"key":"calcutta","kind":"place","lines":"a huge city in eastern india, on a river near the bay of bengal. the british ran their indian empire from it until 1911. since 2001 it's officially kolkata.\nin the forties and fifties it was also famous for its poverty. refugees poured in after the famine of 1943 and the split of india and pakistan in 1947, and a lot of them ended up on the street.\nthat's the calcutta mother teresa walked out into in 1948. the city has complicated feelings about being known mostly as the place she rescued people from.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolkata","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"calcutta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["darjeeling"],"key":"darjeeling","kind":"place","lines":"a hill town high in the himalayan foothills of northeast india, the place the tea is named for. the british built it as a summer escape from the heat of the plains.\nmother teresa was on the train up there in september 1946 for a retreat when she heard what she called the call within a call. her order still keeps that day.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darjeeling","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"darjeeling","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sisters of loreto","the sisters of loreto","loreto"],"key":"loreto","kind":"group","lines":"an order of catholic nuns based in ireland that runs schools for girls all over the world. mother teresa joined them at eighteen and taught at their school in calcutta for almost twenty years.\nit was a comfortable, respectable life behind a wall, and she was good at it. leaving it for the slums meant getting permission from rome to walk out of her vows to them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_of_the_Blessed_Virgin_Mary","why":"the loreto sisters' formal name"}],"name":"the sisters of loreto","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kalighat","nirmal hriday"],"key":"kalighat","kind":"place","lines":"a neighborhood of calcutta around the famous temple to kali, the hindu goddess. in 1952 the city gave mother teresa part of an old pilgrims' rest house next to the temple, and she turned it into a home for the dying.\nshe called it nirmal hriday, pure heart. people picked up off the street were brought there so they could die clean, fed, and with somebody next to them. it's also the place the critics wrote about, because of how little pain relief there was.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalighat_Home_for_the_Dying","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kalighat","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["come be my light"],"key":"comebemylight","kind":"book","lines":"a 2007 book of mother teresa's private letters to her confessors, the priests she told her inner life to, edited by brian kolodiejchuk, the priest who argued her case for sainthood.\nshe'd asked for the letters to be destroyed. the church kept them. they show that for almost fifty years she felt no presence of god at all when she prayed, and went to work anyway. it came out ten years after she died and surprised almost everybody.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother_Teresa","why":"her life, with a section on the letters"}],"name":"come be my light","status":"approved","threads":["doubt"]},{"aliases":["confessor","confessors"],"key":"confessor","kind":"word","lines":"a catholic priest you tell your sins to, regularly, in confession. for serious people it goes further. a confessor becomes the one person you tell your whole inner life to, doubts and all, and who's supposed to tell you honestly what he sees.\nsomewhere between a therapist and a sponsor, with a vow of silence. mother teresa, teresa of avila, and john of the cross all had confessors, and john of the cross was one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confessor","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"confessor","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jesuit","jesuits","society of jesus"],"key":"jesuit","kind":"group","lines":"an order of catholic priests started in 1540 by ignatius of loyola, a spanish soldier who got hit by a cannonball and found god while he recovered.\nthey became the church's teachers and scholars, famous for running schools and universities, georgetown and boston college among them, and for arguing well. pope francis, the first jesuit pope, was one. if a catholic priest is also a professor, there's a decent chance he's a jesuit.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesuits","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the jesuits","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["duvaliers","the duvaliers","duvalier"],"key":"duvalier","kind":"person","lines":"a father and son who ran haiti as dictators from 1957 to 1986. papa doc, the father, and baby doc, the son. their private militia, the tonton macoutes, tortured and killed tens of thousands of people.\nbaby doc and his wife lived in luxury while haiti stayed the poorest country in the americas. mother teresa accepted an honor from them in 1981 and praised the family, which is one of the things her critics never let go of.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/François_Duvalier","why":"the father, papa doc"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Claude_Duvalier","why":"the son, baby doc"}],"name":"the duvaliers","status":"approved","threads":["money"]},{"aliases":["charles keating","keating"],"key":"keating","kind":"person","lines":"an american banker whose savings and loan, a kind of neighborhood savings bank, collapsed in 1989 and took the savings of more than twenty thousand people with it, a lot of them retirees who'd been talked into risky bonds.\nhe gave mother teresa over a million dollars. when he was on trial for fraud she wrote the judge asking for mercy. the prosecutor wrote back asking her to give the money to the people it was taken from. she never answered.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Keating","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"charles keating","status":"approved","threads":["money"]},{"aliases":["christopher hitchens","hitchens","the missionary position"],"key":"hitchens","kind":"person","lines":"a british journalist and writer who moved to america and became one of the most famous atheists of his time, sharp, funny, and happy to pick a fight with anyone.\nin 1995 he wrote the missionary position, a short, furious book against mother teresa. it goes after the money she took, the dictators she praised, and the lack of pain relief in her homes. when the vatican looked into making her a saint, it called him in to argue the case against her, and he did. he died of cancer in 2011.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_Hitchens","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Missionary_Position:_Mother_Teresa_in_Theory_and_Practice","why":"the book against her"}],"name":"christopher hitchens","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["patna"],"key":"patna","kind":"place","lines":"an old city on the ganges in northern india, the capital of the state of bihar. mother teresa spent a few months there in 1948 learning basic nursing from the medical mission sisters, an american order of nuns who were doctors and nurses, before she went into the slums.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patna","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"patna","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["l'arche","l'arche daybreak","larche"],"key":"larche","kind":"group","lines":"a network of homes where people with intellectual disabilities and the people who help them live together as one household, cooking, eating, and praying at the same table. the name is french for the ark.\njean vanier, a canadian philosopher, started the first one in a village in france in 1964 by inviting two men from an institution to live with him. now there are homes in dozens of countries. henri nouwen spent his last ten years at daybreak, the one outside toronto.\nin 2020 an investigation found that vanier had sexually abused women who came to him for spiritual guidance. the homes, and the people in them, carried on.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%27Arche","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"l'arche","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jean vanier","vanier"],"key":"vanier","kind":"person","lines":"a canadian philosopher and former naval officer who started l'arche in france in 1964, homes where people with intellectual disabilities and their helpers share a life. for decades he was treated as something close to a living saint.\nafter he died in 2019, an investigation in 2020 found that he had sexually abused at least six women who came to him for spiritual guidance, and hid it for decades. he's not in this forest, and the people of l'arche have had to live with what he did.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Vanier","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jean vanier","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["adam arnett"],"key":"adamarnett","kind":"person","lines":"a young man at the l'arche daybreak home outside toronto who couldn't speak, had severe epilepsy, and needed help with everything. when henri nouwen arrived in 1986 they gave him adam to care for.\nevery morning nouwen bathed him, shaved him, dressed him, and fed him breakfast. nouwen said adam taught him more than any professor. adam died in february 1996 and nouwen wrote a short book about him, adam, god's beloved, and died that september before it came out.","links":[],"name":"adam arnett","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rembrandt","rembrandt's"],"key":"rembrandt","kind":"person","lines":"rembrandt van rijn, a dutch painter in the 1600s, one of the greatest who ever lived. famous for faces lit out of the dark, and for painting himself over and over as he got old and broke.\nnear the end of his life he painted the return of the prodigal son, the father holding his ragged kid against his chest. it hangs in the hermitage museum in st petersburg. henri nouwen sat in front of it for hours in 1986 and wrote a whole book about it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rembrandt","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Return_of_the_Prodigal_Son_(Rembrandt)","why":"the painting nouwen sat in front of"}],"name":"rembrandt","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the return of the prodigal son","return of the prodigal son"],"key":"returnprodigal","kind":"book","lines":"the name of rembrandt's painting of the story jesus tells about a son who blows his inheritance and comes home broke, and of henri nouwen's 1992 book about that painting.\nnouwen sees himself first as the kid who ran off, then as the older brother who stayed home and sulks, and ends up at the hardest one, the father, the one whose job is just to welcome people home. it's his best known book, and the one to start with.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Return_of_the_Prodigal_Son_(Rembrandt)","why":"the painting"},{"label":"his foundation","url":"https://henrinouwen.org/","why":"the henri nouwen society, with his writings and a free daily meditation"}],"name":"the return of the prodigal son","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness","family"]},{"aliases":["hermitage, the enormous art museum","hermitage museum"],"key":"hermitagemuseum","kind":"place","lines":"one of the biggest art museums in the world, in st petersburg, russia, which was called leningrad in soviet times. it started as catherine the great's private collection in the winter palace of the tsars.\nrembrandt's return of the prodigal son hangs there. henri nouwen got permission to sit in front of it for hours in 1986, and that afternoon became a book.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermitage_Museum","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the hermitage museum","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leningrad"],"key":"leningrad","kind":"place","lines":"the name st petersburg, russia's old capital on the baltic sea, went by from 1924 to 1991, while russia was the communist soviet union. it was named after lenin, the leader of the revolution. when the soviet union fell the city took its old name back.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Petersburg","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leningrad","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the wounded healer","wounded healer"],"key":"woundedhealer","kind":"book","lines":"henri nouwen's short 1972 book for ministers, and anyone who helps people for a living. its argument is that your own wounds, looked at honestly, are what you actually have to give, because that's where you can meet somebody else in theirs.\nit's why therapists and nurses and recovering drunks who sponsor other drunks recognize themselves in it. you don't have to be fixed to help. you have to have been hurt and be honest about it.","links":[],"name":"the wounded healer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the inner voice of love","inner voice of love"],"key":"innervoiceoflove","kind":"book","lines":"the private notes henri nouwen wrote to himself in 1987 and 1988, during his breakdown in winnipeg, one a day or so, each one an order from the part of him that still knew something to the part that didn't.\nhe kept them in a drawer for eight years and published them in 1996, the year he died. they're short and raw. it's the book people pass each other in a bad month.","links":[],"name":"the inner voice of love","status":"approved","threads":["loneliness"]},{"aliases":["life of the beloved"],"key":"lifeofthebeloved","kind":"book","lines":"a short 1992 book henri nouwen wrote for a jewish friend in new york, a journalist who didn't share his faith and asked him to write something for people like him. its one idea is that before anything else you're loved, and the other voices in your head are trying to talk you out of it.","links":[],"name":"life of the beloved","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["abbey of the genesee","the genesee diary","genesee diary","genesee"],"key":"genesee","kind":"place","lines":"a trappist monastery in upstate new york, near rochester, where the monks are famous for baking bread, monks' bread, sold in supermarkets. trappists are the strict, mostly silent catholic monks.\nhenri nouwen lived there as a monk for seven months in 1974, baking bread and hauling rocks out of the fields, and wrote the genesee diary about how bad he was at it. he wanted peace and found his own restlessness waiting for him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbey_of_the_Genesee","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the abbey of the genesee","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yale divinity school","yale"],"key":"yaledivinity","kind":"place","lines":"yale is one of the oldest and most famous universities in america, in new haven, connecticut. its divinity school trains ministers and teaches religion.\nhenri nouwen taught there for about ten years in the seventies and early eighties, one of the most popular teachers on campus, before moving on to harvard.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_Divinity_School","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"yale divinity school","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["notre dame"],"key":"notredame","kind":"place","lines":"the university of notre dame, the most famous catholic university in america, in indiana, better known to most people for its football team. henri nouwen taught psychology there in the late sixties, his first job in america.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Notre_Dame","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"notre dame","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["flying rodleighs","the flying rodleighs","rodleighs"],"key":"rodleighs","kind":"group","lines":"a south african trapeze act henri nouwen saw at a german circus in 1991 and got hooked on. he traveled with them and wanted to write a book about them.\ntheir leader, rodleigh stevens, told him the flyer never catches the catcher. the one flying lets go, stretches out his arms, and trusts the catcher to grab him. nouwen thought that was the whole spiritual life in one sentence. he died before he wrote the book.","links":[],"name":"the flying rodleighs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["grace"],"key":"grace","kind":"word","lines":"in christianity, grace is god's love that you don't earn and can't lose. it's a gift, not a paycheck. the word comes from the latin for free, as in on the house.\nthe whole fight of the protestant reformation was partly over it. can you work your way to god, or does it only come as a gift. most christians say it's a gift and then live like it's a paycheck anyway, which is exactly what brennan manning spent his life yelling about.\noutside church the word survived in saying grace before a meal and in a grace period on a bill, the extra time you didn't earn. same idea.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divine_grace","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"grace","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["the ragamuffin gospel","ragamuffin gospel","ragamuffin band","ragamuffin"],"key":"ragamuffin","kind":"book","lines":"brennan manning's 1990 book. a ragamuffin is a dirty, ragged kid. the book says god's love is for exactly those people first, the bedraggled, beat up, and burnt out, not for the ones who've got it together.\nit landed hard among evangelicals, a lot of whom had grown up being told to try harder and pray more. the singer rich mullins named his band after it. people still hand it to friends who think they've blown it with god.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brennan_Manning","why":"the man and the book"}],"name":"the ragamuffin gospel","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["all is grace"],"key":"allisgrace","kind":"book","lines":"brennan manning's memoir, from 2011, written near the end with a co-writer when he was old and sick. it admits he'd been drinking far more than anyone knew and lying about it while he preached grace to crowds.\nthe title is the last line of the diary of a country priest, a french novel he loved. he took the phrase and turned it on himself.","links":[],"name":"all is grace","status":"approved","threads":["addiction","forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["rich mullins"],"key":"richmullins","kind":"person","lines":"an american christian singer and songwriter, best known for awesome god, a worship song they still sing in churches everywhere. he gave most of his money away and lived for a while on a navajo reservation teaching music to kids.\nhe loved the ragamuffin gospel and named his group the ragamuffin band. he died in a car crash in 1997, at forty one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rich_Mullins","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rich mullins","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["little brothers of jesus","the little brothers of jesus","charles de foucauld"],"key":"littlebrothers","kind":"group","lines":"a small catholic order, started in 1933, that follows the example of charles de foucauld, a rich french cavalry officer turned hermit who lived among the tuareg people in the sahara and was killed there in 1916.\nthe brothers don't run schools or hospitals. they take ordinary jobs, in factories and fields, live in small groups among poor people, and pray. brennan manning spent time with them in the late sixties, including months alone in a cave in spain.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Brothers_of_Jesus","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_de_Foucauld","why":"the soldier who became a hermit"}],"name":"the little brothers of jesus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the diary of a country priest","diary of a country priest","georges bernanos","bernanos"],"key":"countrypriest","kind":"book","lines":"a 1936 french novel by georges bernanos. a young priest, sick and awkward, gets sent to a village that doesn't want him, fails at almost everything, and dies of stomach cancer, not knowing he'd done any good.\nhis last words are all is grace, meaning it doesn't matter, it's all a gift anyway. brennan manning took those words for the title of his memoir.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Diary_of_a_Country_Priest","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the diary of a country priest","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the catholic worker","catholic worker","catholic worker movement"],"key":"catholicworker","kind":"group","lines":"a newspaper and a movement dorothy day and peter maurin started in new york on may 1, 1933. the paper cost a penny and still does. it said the church belonged with the poor and the unions, and it said so in the middle of the great depression.\nthen came the soup lines, then houses where anyone could get a bed and a meal, then farms. today there are more than two hundred catholic worker houses, run by volunteers who live in them with the people who come, no salaries.\npicture a homeless shelter where the staff sleep down the hall, eat the same soup, and nobody fills out a form.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic_Worker_Movement","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the movement's site","url":"https://www.catholicworker.org/","why":"the catholic worker's own site, with her writings and a list of houses"}],"name":"the catholic worker","status":"approved","threads":["work"]},{"aliases":["houses of hospitality","house of hospitality"],"key":"hospitalityhouse","kind":"place","lines":"the catholic worker's shelters. anyone who came got a bed, a meal, and some dignity, and nobody made them earn it, get sober first, or sit through a sermon.\npeter maurin's idea was that every parish should have one, the way medieval monasteries kept a room for travelers. dorothy day lived in them for almost fifty years. they're still running in cities all over the world.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic_Worker_Movement","why":"the houses are part of the movement"}],"name":"houses of hospitality","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["peter maurin","maurin"],"key":"petermaurin","kind":"person","lines":"a french drifter from a peasant family who turned up at dorothy day's apartment in 1932 with a plan to rebuild the world. he'd worked as a laborer across canada and the us and read everything.\nhe talked in short rhyming verses he called easy essays, and he'd lecture anybody who stood still. his plan was newspapers to spread the idea, houses to feed people, and farms to get them back on the land. day made it happen. he died in 1949.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Maurin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"peter maurin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pacifist","pacifists","pacifism"],"key":"pacifist","kind":"word","lines":"someone who won't support any war, for any reason. not just the unjust ones. all of them.\nthat's easy to say about a war you don't like. dorothy day said it about the second world war, against hitler, when almost every american was behind it, and her paper lost most of its readers. that's what pacifism costs when you actually mean it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacifism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pacifist","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["anarchist","anarchists","anarchism"],"key":"anarchist","kind":"word","lines":"someone who thinks there shouldn't be governments at all, that people could run their lives together without bosses, police, or states.\nin the movies it means a guy throwing bombs, and some did. most were closer to dorothy day's man forster batterham, who loved the beach and fishing and thought marriage and the state were both cages. day called her own movement a kind of christian anarchism, doing it yourself instead of waiting for the government.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anarchism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"anarchist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["forster batterham","forster","batterham"],"key":"batterham","kind":"person","lines":"a biologist and anarchist dorothy day lived with on staten island in the twenties, the father of her daughter tamar. he loved the beach and didn't believe in marriage, god, or the state.\nwhen she became catholic he wouldn't marry and she wouldn't stay without the church, and they split in 1927. it broke her heart. decades later, at the end of his life, they were friends again, and she helped look after the woman he'd lived with when she was dying.","links":[],"name":"forster batterham","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["greenwich village"],"key":"greenwichvillage","kind":"place","lines":"a neighborhood in lower manhattan that was new york's artist and radical quarter in the 1910s. cheap rooms, bars, little magazines, free love, socialism, and every argument you could have.\ndorothy day was a young reporter there, drinking with playwrights and writing for socialist papers. picture brooklyn's coolest neighborhood before the money came, times ten.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"greenwich village","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["eugene o'neill","o'neill"],"key":"oneill","kind":"person","lines":"an american playwright, later the only american playwright to win the nobel prize for literature. long day's journey into night is his. in 1917 he was an unknown drunk in greenwich village, and dorothy day spent nights in bars with him.\nshe'd sit up with him after he drank, and he'd recite a poem about the hound of heaven, god chasing a man who runs. she said it stayed with her.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugene_O%27Neill","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eugene o'neill","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["suffragists","suffragist","suffragettes","suffrage"],"key":"suffragist","kind":"group","lines":"the women who fought for the right to vote. in america they won it in 1920.\nin 1917 some of them picketed the white house every day with banners. hundreds were arrested and sent to a workhouse in virginia, where the guards beat them. dorothy day, twenty and not really a suffragist herself, went along, got arrested, and joined a hunger strike.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silent_Sentinels","why":"the white house pickets she was arrested with"}],"name":"the suffragists","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["works of mercy","the works of mercy","matthew 25"],"key":"worksofmercy","kind":"word","lines":"an old church list of what you're supposed to actually do for people. feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, clothe the naked, shelter the homeless, visit the sick and the prisoner, bury the dead.\nmost of it comes from matthew 25 in the new testament, where jesus says whatever you did for the least of these, you did for me. the idea is you meet god in the person asking for soup. dorothy day built her whole life on that one chapter.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Works_of_mercy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the works of mercy","status":"approved","threads":["work"]},{"aliases":["the long loneliness","long loneliness"],"key":"longloneliness","kind":"book","lines":"dorothy day's autobiography, from 1952. it tells the radical years, the abortion, the common-law marriage, becoming catholic, losing the man she loved over it, and starting the catholic worker.\nthe title is her name for what everyone carries. the last pages say the only answer is love, and love comes with community. it's the place to start with her.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Long_Loneliness","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the long loneliness","status":"approved","threads":["loneliness"]},{"aliases":["loaves and fishes"],"key":"loavesandfishes","kind":"book","lines":"dorothy day's 1963 book about the catholic worker itself, the houses, the soup lines, and the difficult, funny, maddening people who came through the door.\nthe title is from the gospel story where jesus feeds five thousand people with five loaves and two fish. that's roughly how the catholic worker's budget felt.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feeding_the_multitude","why":"the gospel story the title comes from"}],"name":"loaves and fishes","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the eleventh virgin","eleventh virgin"],"key":"eleventhvirgin","kind":"book","lines":"dorothy day's only novel, from 1924, a thinly disguised story of her own wild years, including the affair and the abortion. she later wished she hadn't published it and tried to buy up the copies.","links":[],"name":"the eleventh virgin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["civil defense drills","civil defense","air raid drills"],"key":"civildefense","kind":"event","lines":"in the fifties new york held drills where sirens went off and the whole city had to get inside or underground, as if a soviet nuclear bomb were coming. anyone who stayed on the street could be arrested.\ndorothy day and a handful of others sat on benches in city hall park instead, year after year, saying the drills just got people used to the idea of nuclear war. they went to jail for it. by 1961 thousands had joined them, and the drills ended soon after.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Alert","why":"civil defense in the us, the drills and the protests against them"}],"name":"the civil defense drills","status":"approved","threads":["prison","nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["cesar chavez","chavez","farmworkers' union","united farm workers"],"key":"chavez","kind":"person","lines":"a mexican-american labor leader who organized the farmworkers of california, the people picking grapes and lettuce for almost nothing, into a union in the sixties. he used boycotts, marches, and long fasts, and he was a devout catholic.\nin 1973 dorothy day, seventy five, went out to support the union's strike and was arrested with them. there's a famous photograph of her on a folding stool facing two armed police officers.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cesar_Chavez","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Farm_Workers","why":"the union"}],"name":"cesar chavez","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence","work"]},{"aliases":["union square"],"key":"unionsquare","kind":"place","lines":"a plaza in manhattan where radicals and unions gave their speeches and held their rallies, especially on may day, the workers' holiday. dorothy day sold the first issue of the catholic worker there on may 1, 1933, for a penny, to a crowd of communists.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_Square,_Manhattan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"union square","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the confessions","confessions of augustine"],"key":"confessions","kind":"book","lines":"augustine's memoir, written around 397 when he was about forty three and a new bishop. it's addressed to god from start to finish, like one long prayer, and anyone can listen in.\nthe first nine books are his life. the stolen pears, the partying, the woman he lived with for fifteen years and sent away, his mother's tears, and the afternoon in a garden in milan when he finally gave in. the last four turn into philosophy about memory and time.\nit's usually called the first autobiography in the western world, and it's still the model for every memoir that tells on its own author. if you've read a recovery memoir, you've read its grandchildren.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confessions_(Augustine)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3296","why":"the old pusey translation on project gutenberg"}],"name":"the confessions","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["manichees","manichaeans","manichaeism","manichean"],"key":"manichees","kind":"group","lines":"a religion started in persia in the 200s by a prophet named mani. it taught that the universe is a war between two equal powers, good light and evil darkness, and that bits of light are trapped in our bodies, which belong to the dark.\nit spread from north africa all the way to china and lasted for centuries. augustine was one for nine years in his twenties. he said later it suited him because it meant the bad in him wasn't really him.\nwhen people call a view manichean today they mean it splits the world into pure good guys and pure bad guys.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manichaeism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the manichees","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rhetoric","rhetorician"],"key":"rhetoric","kind":"word","lines":"the art of persuasive speaking and arguing. in the roman world it was the master skill, what you studied to become a lawyer, a politician, or anyone who mattered.\na teacher of rhetoric was somewhere between a debate coach, a speechwriter, and a law professor. augustine was a star one, and landed the official job in milan, the city where the emperor lived. he gave it all up after his conversion.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhetoric","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rhetoric","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bishop","bishops"],"key":"bishop","kind":"word","lines":"in the catholic church, and the orthodox and anglican ones, a bishop is the senior priest in charge of a whole area, called a diocese, with all its churches and priests. the pope is technically the bishop of rome.\nin augustine's day a bishop was also a local judge, a fundraiser, and the man people ran to in a crisis. in teresa of avila's day he was the man you needed a signature from to open a convent, which is why she fought so many of them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bishop","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bishop","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vandals","the vandals"],"key":"vandals","kind":"group","lines":"a germanic tribe that swept through gaul and spain in the early 400s and crossed into north africa in 429, taking the rich roman provinces there one city at a time.\nthey were besieging hippo when augustine died in 430. in 455 they sacked rome itself, and their name turned into the word for anyone who wrecks things for no reason.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vandals","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the vandals","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["monica","saint monica"],"key":"monica","kind":"person","lines":"augustine's mother, a christian woman in north africa married to a pagan with a temper. she spent years crying and praying over her brilliant, wild son, and followed him across the sea to italy to keep at it.\na bishop told her the child of so many tears could not be lost. she lived to see him baptized, and died at ostia, rome's port, on the way home. the church later made her a saint, and the city of santa monica in california is named after her.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Monica","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"monica","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["adeodatus"],"key":"adeodatus","kind":"person","lines":"augustine's son with the woman he lived with for fifteen years and never married. the name means given by god. he was clever, and augustine was proud of him.\nhe was baptized alongside his father in milan at easter 387 and died a couple of years later, still a teenager, back in africa.","links":[],"name":"adeodatus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["concubine","concubines"],"key":"concubine","kind":"word","lines":"a live-in partner who isn't legally a wife. in the roman world it was a normal, recognized arrangement, usually a man with a woman from a lower class he wasn't allowed or didn't want to marry.\naugustine lived with his for about fifteen years and had a son with her. when his mother found him a proper bride, she was sent home to africa. he never tells us her name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concubinage","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"concubine","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["carthage"],"key":"carthage","kind":"place","lines":"the big city of roman north africa, on the coast of what's now tunisia. rome destroyed the old carthage in 146 bc and rebuilt it as its own, and by augustine's time it was one of the largest cities in the empire.\naugustine went there as a student at seventeen. he says it was a boiling pot of shameful loves, and he jumped right in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carthage","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"carthage","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["thagaste","tagaste"],"key":"thagaste","kind":"place","lines":"a small roman town in north africa, now souk ahras in algeria, where augustine was born in 354. nowhere special, which is why his parents scraped so hard to send him off to study.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Souk_Ahras","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"thagaste","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hippo","hippo regius"],"key":"hippo","kind":"place","lines":"a port town in roman north africa, now annaba in algeria. augustine went there in 391 to look into setting up a monastery, got grabbed by the congregation and made a priest more or less against his will, and was their bishop for thirty five years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippo_Regius","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hippo","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["milan"],"key":"milan","kind":"place","lines":"a city in northern italy that was, in augustine's day, where the western roman emperor actually lived. the job of the city's official teacher of rhetoric was a big one.\nit's where augustine heard ambrose preach, had his breakdown in a garden, and was baptized at easter 387.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"milan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ambrose","saint ambrose"],"key":"ambrose","kind":"person","lines":"the bishop of milan in the late 300s, a former roman governor who got made bishop by popular demand before he'd even been baptized. one of the most powerful men in the church, he once made an emperor do public penance.\naugustine went to hear him preach for the style and ended up listening to what he said. ambrose baptized him at easter 387. augustine also noticed he read silently, without moving his lips, which was unusual enough back then to write down.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambrose","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ambrose","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["take up and read","tolle lege","romans 13","romans 13:13"],"key":"tollelege","kind":"event","lines":"tolle lege in latin. in 386, crying under a fig tree in a garden in milan, augustine heard a child's voice from the next house chanting take up and read. he took it as an order, opened paul's letters at random, and landed on romans 13, lines telling him to stop the partying and sleeping around.\nthat was the moment he gave in. nobody knows what game the kid was playing.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tolle_lege","why":"augustine's life, the garden included"}],"name":"take up and read","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the city of god","city of god"],"key":"cityofgod","kind":"book","lines":"augustine's giant book, written over thirteen years after the goths sacked rome in 410. pagans were saying rome fell because it had dropped the old gods for christianity.\nhis answer was that there are two cities, the earthly one built on love of self and the heavenly one built on love of god, mixed together until the end. rome was never the point. it shaped how the west thought about history and politics for a thousand years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_City_of_God","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45304","why":"volume one on project gutenberg"}],"name":"the city of god","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["goths","the goths"],"key":"goths","kind":"group","lines":"germanic peoples who pushed into the roman empire in the 300s and 400s. in 410 the visigoths, one branch of them, sacked the city of rome, the first time in eight hundred years an enemy had taken it.\nit shocked the whole roman world, a bit like 9/11 did, the thing everyone assumed could never happen. augustine wrote the city of god in answer to it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sack_of_Rome_(410)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the goths","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["donatists","the donatists","donatist"],"key":"donatists","kind":"group","lines":"a rival christian church in north africa in augustine's time, big and popular. they said priests who had handed over scriptures or caved in during the roman persecution of the early 300s weren't real priests anymore, and neither were the ones they ordained.\naugustine argued with them for years, then backed using the roman state to force them back into the catholic church. his argument for it was used for centuries after to justify forcing people into line.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donatism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the donatists","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pagan","pagans"],"key":"pagan","kind":"word","lines":"a word christians used for people who kept the old gods, jupiter, venus, and the rest of the roman and greek crowd. it probably comes from the latin for country people, because the cities went christian first.\naugustine's father was one until near the end. the sailors in the book of jonah are pagans too, and come off better than the prophet.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paganism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pagan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cassiciacum"],"key":"cassiciacum","kind":"place","lines":"a friend's country house outside milan where augustine spent a few months in 386 and 387, after the garden and before his baptism, with his mother, his son, and some friends, talking philosophy and reading the psalms out loud. nobody's sure exactly where it was.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassago_Brianza","why":"the village thought to be the place"}],"name":"cassiciacum","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ostia"],"key":"ostia","kind":"place","lines":"the port of ancient rome, at the mouth of the tiber. augustine and his mother were waiting there for a ship home to africa in 387 when they had their talk at the window, and she died there days later. the ruins are still there, a short train ride from rome.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostia_Antica","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ostia","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["possidius"],"key":"possidius","kind":"person","lines":"a younger friend of augustine who lived with him in hippo and became a bishop himself. he wrote the first life of augustine soon after he died, and was there at the end.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Possidius","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"possidius","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["penitential psalms","the penitential psalms"],"key":"penitentialpsalms","kind":"book","lines":"seven of the psalms, the old hebrew prayer songs, that the church grouped together because they're all about being sorry. psalm 51, the one david is supposed to have written after bathsheba, is the most famous.\naugustine had them copied out and hung on the wall by his bed so he could read them while he was dying.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penitential_Psalms","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the penitential psalms","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["assisi"],"key":"assisi","kind":"place","lines":"a hill town in umbria, in the middle of italy, all pink stone. francis was born there, stripped naked in its square, and died just below it. now it's one of the great pilgrim towns of the world, full of friars and tour buses.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assisi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"assisi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["perugia","perugian"],"key":"perugia","kind":"place","lines":"the bigger city across the valley from assisi, and its old rival. in 1202 the two went to war, and francis, who wanted to be a knight, got captured and spent about a year in a perugian prison. he came home sick and changed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perugia","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"perugia","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leper","lepers","leprosy"],"key":"leper","kind":"word","lines":"leprosy is a bacterial disease that eats away at nerves and skin, so people lose feeling and then fingers, toes, and faces. in the middle ages it was the most feared disease there was, and people thought it was a punishment for sin.\nlepers had to live outside the town, carry a clapper or bell so people could get out of the way, and nobody touched them. francis getting off his horse and kissing one is the moment his life turned.\nit's curable now with antibiotics. calling someone a leper still means the person nobody will go near.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leprosy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leprosy","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["san damiano"],"key":"sandamiano","kind":"place","lines":"a little falling-down church outside assisi where, the story goes, the painted cross over the altar told francis to go and repair its house. he took it literally and started hauling stones.\nit became the home of clare, his friend, and her sisters, and it's where francis wrote the canticle of the sun near the end. the cross now hangs in the basilica of saint clare in town.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Damiano,_Assisi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"san damiano","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the lord's prayer","lord's prayer","our father who art in heaven"],"key":"lordsprayer","kind":"word","lines":"the prayer jesus teaches his followers in the gospels, the one that starts our father, who art in heaven. it's the one prayer almost every christian of every kind knows by heart.\nfrancis quoted its first line when he gave his clothes back to his dad in the town square. from now on, he said, the only father he had was that one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord%27s_Prayer","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the lord's prayer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["crusades","the crusades","crusade","crusaders","crusader"],"key":"crusades","kind":"event","lines":"a string of wars from 1095 to the late 1200s where european christian armies went to take the holy land, the area around jerusalem, from the muslims who ruled it. popes promised the fighters their sins would be forgiven.\nthey took jerusalem in 1099, massacred the people in it, and lost it again in 1187. there were atrocities on every side, including crusaders sacking christian constantinople in 1204. francis walked across the lines of one of them in 1219 to talk to the enemy.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crusades","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the crusades","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["the holy land","holy land"],"key":"holyland","kind":"place","lines":"what christians called the area where jesus lived and died, roughly today's israel and palestine, with jerusalem at the center. jews and muslims hold it sacred too, which is a lot of the reason it's been fought over for two thousand years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Land","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the holy land","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sultan","the sultan","al-kamil"],"key":"sultan","kind":"person","lines":"a sultan is a muslim ruler, roughly a king. the one francis met was al-kamil, the sultan of egypt, a nephew of saladin, in 1219, while a crusader army was besieging the egyptian city of damietta.\nfrancis walked across the battle lines with one companion. by the old accounts the sultan listened to him, treated him kindly, and sent him back alive. al-kamil later made a treaty that handed jerusalem to the christians for ten years without a fight.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Kamil","why":"the sultan himself"}],"name":"the sultan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stigmata","the stigmata"],"key":"stigmata","kind":"word","lines":"wounds that appear on a person's hands, feet, and side where jesus was nailed and speared on the cross. francis is the first person on record said to have had them, on the mountain of la verna in 1224, two years before he died.\nhis friars saw the wounds and wrote about them. nobody can prove what happened. hundreds of people since have claimed them, and the church is very suspicious of most.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stigmata","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the stigmata","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["la verna"],"key":"laverna","kind":"place","lines":"a mountain in tuscany a count gave francis as a place to pray. he went there in 1224 for a long fast and came down with the stigmata. there's a franciscan monastery on it now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Verna","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"la verna","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["portiuncula","the portiuncula"],"key":"portiuncula","kind":"place","lines":"a tiny old chapel in the plain below assisi, one of the churches francis rebuilt with his own hands. the name means the little portion. it's where the brothers started, and where he asked to be laid on the bare ground to die in 1226.\nit now sits inside a gigantic basilica built over it, which is the kind of thing francis would have hated.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porziuncola","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the portiuncula","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["canticle of the sun","the canticle of the sun","canticle of the creatures"],"key":"canticle","kind":"book","lines":"the praise song francis wrote in 1225, near the end, sick and almost blind in a hut by san damiano. brother sun, sister moon, brother wind, sister water, brother fire, mother earth. it's one page long.\nit's one of the first poems ever written in italian instead of latin. near the end he added a verse praising god for sister bodily death, and it's the reason the hymn all creatures of our god and king exists.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canticle_of_the_Sun","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the canticle of the sun","status":"approved","threads":["joy"]},{"aliases":["the little flowers of st francis","little flowers of st francis","the little flowers","fioretti"],"key":"littleflowers","kind":"book","lines":"a collection of stories about francis and his first brothers, put together about a century after he died. preaching to birds, taming the wolf of gubbio, perfect joy being turned away cold and hungry at your own door.\na lot of it is legend. it's the version of francis most people carry around, and it's a lovely book.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fioretti_di_San_Francesco","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the little flowers of st francis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wolf of gubbio","the wolf of gubbio","gubbio"],"key":"gubbio","kind":"event","lines":"the story, from the little flowers, of a wolf that was eating people and animals around the town of gubbio. francis walked out, made the sign of the cross, and struck a deal. the wolf would stop killing and the town would feed it. it lived among them for two years and died of old age.\nit's a legend. it's also a decent picture of how he dealt with anything frightening.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolf_of_Gubbio","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the wolf of gubbio","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["greccio"],"key":"greccio","kind":"place","lines":"a hill town south of assisi where, at christmas 1223, francis set up a manger with a real ox and donkey in a cave so people could see how poor the birth of jesus had been. every nativity scene on every lawn goes back to that night.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greccio","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"greccio","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["eager to love"],"key":"eagertolove","kind":"book","lines":"richard rohr's 2014 book about francis, subtitled the alternative way of francis of assisi. rohr, a franciscan priest himself, reads francis as a man who stayed inside the church and lived at its edge, and as a path anyone can walk.","links":[],"name":"eager to love","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["norwich"],"key":"norwich","kind":"place","lines":"a city in the east of england, near the coast, one of the biggest and richest in the country in the middle ages thanks to wool. it's full of old churches. julian lived in a room on the side of one of them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwich","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"norwich","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["anchoress","anchoresses","anchorite","anchorites","anchorhold"],"key":"anchoress","kind":"word","lines":"a woman who had herself walled into a small room on the side of a church for the rest of her life, to pray. a man who did it was an anchorite. the bishop held a service for it that borrowed from the funeral service, because as far as the world was concerned she was dead.\nthe room usually had one window into the church, to see the altar, and one onto the street, where people came for advice. a servant brought food. picture a lockdown you choose, with no end date, that turns you into the town's counselor.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchorite","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"anchoress","status":"approved","threads":["silence","loneliness"]},{"aliases":["black death","the black death"],"key":"blackdeath","kind":"event","lines":"the plague that swept through asia and europe from 1347 to about 1351 and killed somewhere between a third and half of everyone in europe. it was spread by fleas on rats, and nobody then had any idea why it was happening.\nwhole villages emptied out. it kept coming back in waves for centuries. julian lived through it as a small child and again later, so when she writes all shall be well, she's writing it in a town that had buried half its people.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Death","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the black death","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["crucifix","crucifixes"],"key":"crucifix","kind":"word","lines":"a cross with the figure of jesus on it, as opposed to a plain cross. catholics hang them in churches and homes and hold them up in front of the dying, which is what the priest did for julian in 1373.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crucifix","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"crucifix","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["revelations of divine love","the showings","showings","all shall be well"],"key":"revelations","kind":"book","lines":"julian of norwich's book about sixteen visions, she called them showings, that she had in may 1373 when she was so sick everyone thought she was dying. she wrote a short version soon after and a long one about twenty years later.\nit's the first book in english we know was written by a woman. it has the hazelnut, god as mother, and the line everyone knows, all shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well.\nit isn't a greeting card. she wrote it in a plague town, and she asks straight out how anything could be well with so much sin and pain around, and gets told to trust it anyway.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revelations_of_Divine_Love","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52958","why":"the book on project gutenberg"}],"name":"revelations of divine love","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hazelnut","the hazelnut"],"key":"hazelnut","kind":"word","lines":"the most famous image in julian of norwich's book. she was shown something the size of a hazelnut lying in her palm and understood it was everything that is made. she wondered how it could last, it was so small, and was told it lasts because god loves it.\nthe whole universe as a tiny nut in your hand. it's in chapter 5 of the long version, and it's where to start with her.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revelations_of_Divine_Love","why":"the book it's from"}],"name":"the hazelnut","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["margery kempe"],"key":"margerykempe","kind":"person","lines":"a townswoman from king's lynn in east england, born around 1373, a mother of fourteen who went on pilgrimage to jerusalem, rome, and spain and sobbed out loud in church so often people wanted her thrown out.\nshe dictated her life story, the book of margery kempe, which is often called the first autobiography in english. in it she visits julian at her window around 1413, and julian tells her to trust what she's been given.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margery_Kempe","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"margery kempe","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ancrene wisse","the ancrene wisse"],"key":"ancrenewisse","kind":"book","lines":"a rulebook for anchoresses written in english in the early 1200s, full of practical advice on how to live walled in. it famously says an anchoress may keep one animal, a cat. that's why every modern picture of julian has one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancrene_Wisse","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ancrene wisse","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["church of st julian","st julian's church"],"key":"stjulianchurch","kind":"place","lines":"a small, very old parish church in norwich. julian's cell was built against its side, and she's probably named after it. it was bombed in 1942 and rebuilt with a chapel where the cell stood, and people come to sit in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_of_Norwich","why":"her life, with the church and the cell"}],"name":"the church of st julian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["behovely","behoovely"],"key":"behovely","kind":"word","lines":"an old english word meaning necessary, or fitting, or needed for things to work out. julian writes that sin is behovely, but all shall be well. she doesn't mean sin is fine. she means it's somehow part of the road, and it doesn't get the last word.","links":[],"name":"behovely","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["carmelite","carmelites","carmelite order"],"key":"carmelite","kind":"group","lines":"a catholic order of friars and nuns that traces itself back to hermits living on mount carmel in the holy land around 1200. when the crusaders lost the holy land they came to europe and became an ordinary order.\nby the 1500s a lot of their houses had gone comfortable, with visitors, gossip, and nuns from rich families keeping their servants. teresa of avila and john of the cross dragged a branch of them back to the poor, silent life they started with, and got hated for it by the rest.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carmelites","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the carmelites","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mount carmel"],"key":"mountcarmel","kind":"place","lines":"a ridge of hills over the sea at haifa, in what's now northern israel. in the bible it's where the prophet elijah called down fire on a stone altar. christian hermits lived in its caves in the 1100s, and the carmelites are named for it.\njohn of the cross called his big prose book the ascent of mount carmel, meaning the climb to god.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Carmel","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mount carmel","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["discalced","discalced carmelites"],"key":"discalced","kind":"word","lines":"an old word that just means shoeless. teresa of avila's reformed nuns and friars went in rough rope sandals instead of shoes, as a sign they'd chosen to be poor.\nthe name stuck to the whole reform. the discalced carmelites are still a separate order today, with convents all over the world. the ones who didn't join the reform are sometimes called the calced, the shod ones.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discalced_Carmelites","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"discalced","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dark night of the soul","the dark night of the soul","the dark night"],"key":"darknight","kind":"book","lines":"the dark night is a poem by john of the cross, eight short stanzas, about a woman slipping out of a sleeping house at night to meet her lover. he wrote a long book afterward explaining it line by line, and that's where the phrase dark night of the soul comes from.\nhe meant something exact. a long stretch where god goes silent, prayer feels like nothing, and all the old comforts dry up, which he says is god working on you in a way you can't feel. it isn't a punishment and it isn't depression, though it can come with it.\npeople use the phrase now for any bad month. his version is what mother teresa lived in for almost fifty years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Night_of_the_Soul","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dark night of the soul","status":"approved","threads":["doubt","silence"]},{"aliases":["spiritual canticle","the spiritual canticle"],"key":"spiritualcanticle","kind":"book","lines":"a long love poem by john of the cross, a bride searching the countryside for her lover and finding him. he made most of it up in his head in 1578 while locked in a closet-sized cell in toledo, with no paper, and wrote it down after he escaped.\nit's modeled on the song of songs, the love poetry in the bible. it's considered one of the great poems in the spanish language.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiritual_Canticle","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the spiritual canticle","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["song of songs","the song of songs","song of solomon"],"key":"songofsongs","kind":"book","lines":"a short book of love poems in the middle of the bible, a woman and a man wanting each other, pretty frankly. god isn't mentioned in it once.\njews and christians have read it for two thousand years as a picture of god's love for his people, or for the soul. john of the cross built his whole spiritual canticle on it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_of_Songs","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the song of songs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["castile","castilian"],"key":"castile","kind":"place","lines":"the big central kingdom of old spain, high dry plains with walled towns like avila, toledo, and salamanca. by the 1500s it ran most of spain and its empire, and castilian became what the world calls spanish.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_of_Castile","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"castile","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["toledo"],"key":"toledo","kind":"place","lines":"an old walled city on a hill over a river in the middle of spain, once the capital. in december 1577 the carmelites who opposed the reform dragged john of the cross there and locked him in a tiny cell in their friary for nine months. he got out through a window on a rope of torn blankets.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toledo,_Spain","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"toledo","status":"approved","threads":["prison"]},{"aliases":["duruelo"],"key":"duruelo","kind":"place","lines":"a tiny hamlet in castile where john of the cross and another friar opened the first house of teresa's reform for men in 1568, in a run-down farmhouse. teresa visited and was moved by how poor it was.","links":[],"name":"duruelo","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["úbeda","ubeda"],"key":"ubeda","kind":"place","lines":"a town in andalusia, the south of spain, where john of the cross went for treatment when he was sick in 1591. the prior there, the man in charge of the house, made his last months miserable. he died there in december.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Úbeda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"úbeda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nicolás doria","nicolas doria","doria"],"key":"doria","kind":"person","lines":"an italian banker turned friar who took over the discalced carmelites after teresa of avila died. he ran them hard, pushed for more control, and stripped john of the cross of his posts in 1591, the year john died.","links":[],"name":"nicolás doria","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["matins"],"key":"matins","kind":"word","lines":"the night prayers of a monastery, sung in the dark hours before dawn. monks and nuns get up for them every night. john of the cross died just as the bell rang for matins on december 14, 1591.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matins","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"matins","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["salvador dalí","salvador dali","dalí","christ of saint john of the cross"],"key":"dali","kind":"person","lines":"a spanish surrealist painter, the one with the waxed, upturned mustache and the melting clocks. in 1951 he painted christ of saint john of the cross, a huge crucifixion seen from above, floating over a bay.\nhe based it on a small ink sketch john of the cross made around 1575 after a vision. it hangs in a museum in glasgow.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvador_Dalí","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christ_of_Saint_John_of_the_Cross","why":"the painting"}],"name":"salvador dalí","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the trinity"],"key":"trinity","kind":"word","lines":"the christian idea that there's one god who is also three, the father, the son, who is jesus, and the holy spirit. not three gods. one, in three.\nnobody claims to fully understand it. it's the belief that separates most christians from jews and muslims, and the one theologians have spent two thousand years trying to put into words.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trinity","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the trinity","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["philip ii","king philip"],"key":"philipii","kind":"person","lines":"the king of spain from 1556 to 1598, ruler of the biggest empire in the world, with most of the americas, and a very serious catholic. he sent the armada against england in 1588.\nhe liked teresa of avila and protected her reform. when john of the cross was kidnapped in 1577 she wrote to him begging him to step in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_II_of_Spain","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"philip ii of spain","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["converso","conversos"],"key":"converso","kind":"word","lines":"a spanish jew who converted to christianity, or someone descended from one. in 1391 and again in 1492 huge numbers of spanish jews were forced to choose between baptism, exile, or death.\nthe converts were never fully trusted. the inquisition hunted them for secretly keeping jewish ways, and a lot of spain cared about purity of blood, having no jewish ancestors. teresa of avila's grandfather was a converso who had to do public penance. her family spent years hiding it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Converso","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"converso","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["inquisition","the inquisition","spanish inquisition","the spanish inquisition"],"key":"inquisition","kind":"group","lines":"church courts set up to hunt down heresy, beliefs the church said were wrong. the spanish one, started in 1478 under the king and queen, went after conversos suspected of secretly still being jewish, and later protestants and mystics.\nit used informers, secret trials, and torture, and it burned people. it seized teresa of avila's autobiography and sat on it for years. spinoza's family fled the portuguese version. it wasn't officially shut down in spain until 1834.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_Inquisition","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the inquisition","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["old christians","old christian"],"key":"oldchristians","kind":"word","lines":"what spaniards in the 1500s called people with no jewish or muslim ancestors. it mattered. a lot of jobs, orders, and honors were closed to anyone who couldn't prove it. teresa of avila's family worked hard to pass as old christians.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Christian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"old christians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["martyr","martyrs","martyred","martyrdom"],"key":"martyr","kind":"word","lines":"someone killed for their faith who wouldn't take it back. the early christians believed martyrs went straight to heaven.\nteresa of avila, at seven, talked her brother into running off to the moors to get their heads cut off for exactly that reason. an uncle caught them on the road.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martyr","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"martyr","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the moors","moors"],"key":"moors","kind":"group","lines":"what europeans called the muslims of north africa and spain. muslims ruled parts of spain for almost eight hundred years, until 1492. in teresa of avila's childhood they were the obvious enemy just across the sea.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moors","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the moors","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["convent of the incarnation"],"key":"incarnation","kind":"place","lines":"a big carmelite convent just outside the walls of avila where teresa lived for almost thirty years, from twenty on. at its fullest it held nearly two hundred nuns, many from good families, with a busy parlor for visitors.\nlater she came back as its prioress, and brought john of the cross in as the nuns' confessor.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teresa_of_Ávila","why":"her life, with the convent"}],"name":"the convent of the incarnation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mental prayer"],"key":"mentalprayer","kind":"word","lines":"quiet prayer in your own words, or no words, as opposed to reciting set prayers. teresa of avila describes it as a close conversation with a friend you know loves you.\nshe gave it up for about a year because she felt too bad a person to do it, and called that one of the worst mistakes of her life.","links":[],"name":"mental prayer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the interior castle","interior castle","dwelling places"],"key":"interiorcastle","kind":"book","lines":"teresa of avila's book on prayer, written in a few months in 1577 in the middle of the worst fights over her reform. she pictures the soul as a castle made of a single diamond, with many rooms, and god in the innermost one.\nyou go in one room at a time, and humility is the floor of every room. she calls the rooms dwelling places. it's still one of the best maps of the inside of a person anyone has drawn.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior_Castle","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the interior castle","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the book of her life","book of her life"],"key":"bookofherlife","kind":"book","lines":"teresa of avila's autobiography, written in the 1560s at her confessors' orders to explain her visions. it tells the vain girlhood, the lukewarm years in the convent, and the day at thirty nine she broke down in front of a statue of christ.\nthe inquisition seized it and kept it for years. it's funny, honest, and wanders off topic, the way she did.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teresa_of_Ávila","why":"her life, and the book"}],"name":"the book of her life","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["book of her foundations","the book of her foundations","book of the foundations"],"key":"foundations","kind":"book","lines":"teresa of avila's account of starting her convents, one by one, on terrible roads with no money. it's full of practical detail, broken carts, bad inns, difficult bishops, and the line about god walking among the pots and pans.","links":[],"name":"the book of her foundations","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["breviary"],"key":"breviary","kind":"book","lines":"the prayer book priests, monks, and nuns carry, with the set prayers and psalms for each hour of each day. after teresa of avila died they found a short poem tucked in hers, the one that starts let nothing disturb you.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breviary","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"breviary","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["belfast"],"key":"belfast","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of northern ireland, a shipbuilding city, where the titanic was built. c.s. lewis was born there in 1898 to a protestant family and left for english boarding schools as a boy.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belfast","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"belfast","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["oxford","oxford university","oxford don"],"key":"oxford","kind":"place","lines":"the oldest university in the english speaking world, in a town about an hour northwest of london. it's made up of dozens of separate colleges, and a teacher there is called a don.\nc.s. lewis taught english literature at magdalen college, pronounced maudlin, for almost thirty years, and did most of his writing there. the inklings met in his rooms and at a pub in town.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Oxford","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"oxford","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["magdalen college","magdalen","addison's walk"],"key":"magdalen","kind":"place","lines":"one of the oxford colleges, pronounced maudlin, with a deer park and a path along the river called addison's walk. c.s. lewis taught there from 1925 to 1954.\non addison's walk on the night of september 19, 1931, he walked and argued with tolkien and hugo dyson until three in the morning, and that was the night that turned him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdalen_College,_Oxford","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"magdalen college","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["j.r.r. tolkien","tolkien","the lord of the rings","lord of the rings"],"key":"tolkien","kind":"person","lines":"an oxford professor of old english who invented middle earth and wrote the hobbit and the lord of the rings, the books the movies came from. he was a devout catholic.\nhe was c.s. lewis's close friend. on a night walk in 1931 he argued that the story of christ was a myth like the old ones lewis loved, except that it really happened, and a few days later lewis believed. lewis in turn pushed him for years to finish the lord of the rings. they drifted apart later, and tolkien never liked narnia much.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._R._R._Tolkien","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"j.r.r. tolkien","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hugo dyson"],"key":"dyson","kind":"person","lines":"an english literature teacher at reading and then oxford, a friend of c.s. lewis and tolkien and one of the inklings. he was the third man on addison's walk on the night in 1931 that turned lewis. he's also remembered for groaning out loud when tolkien read yet another bit about elves.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugo_Dyson","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hugo dyson","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["inklings","the inklings"],"key":"inklings","kind":"group","lines":"a group of oxford friends, men who liked stories, who met in the thirties and forties to read their work out loud to each other and argue. c.s. lewis, tolkien, charles williams, lewis's brother warnie, and others.\nthey met in lewis's rooms on thursday nights and at a pub called the eagle and child on tuesday mornings. the lord of the rings got its first hearing there, chapter by chapter. picture a writers' group where two of the members wrote books that sold hundreds of millions.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inklings","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the inklings","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the chronicles of narnia","chronicles of narnia","narnia"],"key":"narnia","kind":"book","lines":"seven children's books by c.s. lewis, from 1950 to 1956, about english kids who get into narnia, another world full of talking animals, through a wardrobe or a painting or a ring. the lion, the witch and the wardrobe is the first and most famous.\nthe lion, aslan, is lewis's picture of christ. he dies for someone else's betrayal and comes back. kids read it as an adventure and never notice, which is how lewis wanted it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Chronicles_of_Narnia","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the chronicles of narnia","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the screwtape letters","screwtape letters","screwtape"],"key":"screwtape","kind":"book","lines":"a 1942 book by c.s. lewis. it's letters from a senior devil, screwtape, to his nephew, a junior devil, coaching him on how to ruin one ordinary man. it's funny, and uncomfortably accurate about how people actually go wrong, mostly in small, boring ways.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Screwtape_Letters","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the screwtape letters","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mere christianity"],"key":"merechristianity","kind":"book","lines":"c.s. lewis's plain explanation of what christians believe, which started as bbc radio talks during the second world war, when people needed something to hold. it's still one of the most read christian books in the world.\nit's the book that made him the famous defender of the faith, the man with an answer for everything. that's why a grief observed hits so hard.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mere_Christianity","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mere christianity","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the problem of pain","problem of pain"],"key":"problemofpain","kind":"book","lines":"c.s. lewis's 1940 book on why a good god lets people suffer. it's clear and confident. twenty years later, when his wife died, he found out it hadn't prepared him at all, and wrote a grief observed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Problem_of_Pain","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the problem of pain","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["a grief observed","grief observed"],"key":"griefobserved","kind":"book","lines":"the notebooks c.s. lewis kept after his wife joy davidman died of cancer in 1960. the first line is no one ever told me that grief felt so like fear.\nhe writes that god feels like a door slammed in his face, bolted from the inside, and wonders whether god is a cosmic sadist. he published it in 1961 under a fake name, n.w. clerk, because the famous defender of christianity had come apart. it doesn't end with an answer. it's short, and you'll read it in one night.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Grief_Observed","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a grief observed","status":"approved","threads":["grief"]},{"aliases":["joy davidman"],"key":"joydavidman","kind":"person","lines":"an american poet and writer from new york, jewish-born, an ex-communist who became a christian partly through c.s. lewis's books. she wrote to him in 1950, came to england, and they became friends, then more.\nthey married in a registry office in 1956 so she could stay in britain, and again at her hospital bed in 1957 when she had cancer. she went into remission and they had a few happy years. she died in 1960. the film shadowlands tells it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joy_Davidman","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joy davidman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["janie moore","paddy moore"],"key":"janiemoore","kind":"person","lines":"the mother of paddy moore, c.s. lewis's friend from army training, who was killed in the first world war. the two young men had promised to look after each other's families.\nlewis kept the promise. he lived with mrs moore, twenty six years older than him, and her daughter for about thirty years, until she died in 1951. nobody knows for sure what the relationship was, and he never said.","links":[],"name":"janie moore","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["surprised by joy"],"key":"surprisedbyjoy","kind":"book","lines":"c.s. lewis's 1955 memoir of how he went from atheist to christian. joy there means a sharp longing he'd felt since childhood for something he couldn't name. it's also where he calls himself the most dejected and reluctant convert in all england.\nfunny timing, since joy davidman came into his life around then.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surprised_by_Joy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"surprised by joy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["apologist","apologists","apologetics"],"key":"apologist","kind":"word","lines":"someone who defends a belief with arguments, especially christianity. it has nothing to do with saying sorry. it's from the greek word for a defense in court.\nc.s. lewis was the most famous christian apologist of the twentieth century, on the radio, in books, in debates. the problem with being the man with the answers is what happens when your own life stops fitting them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_apologetics","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"apologist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["peter kreeft","between heaven and hell"],"key":"kreeft","kind":"person","lines":"an american catholic philosophy professor at boston college. in 1982 he wrote between heaven and hell, a short book imagining c.s. lewis, aldous huxley, and john kennedy, who all died on november 22, 1963, arguing about jesus somewhere after death.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Kreeft","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"peter kreeft","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["capernaum"],"key":"capernaum","kind":"place","lines":"a fishing village on the north shore of the sea of galilee, the lake in northern israel. peter lived there with his wife and his mother-in-law, and jesus made it his base.\narchaeologists found a house there that early christians turned into a church, and it's probably peter's. there's a modern church on stilts over it now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capernaum","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"capernaum","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["his brother andrew","brother andrew"],"key":"andrew","kind":"person","lines":"peter's brother, a fisherman on the sea of galilee, one of the twelve. in john's gospel he's the one who meets jesus first and goes to get his brother.\ntradition says he was crucified in greece on an x-shaped cross. that's why scotland, which took him as its patron saint, has a white x on its flag.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_the_Apostle","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"andrew","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cephas"],"key":"cephas","kind":"word","lines":"the aramaic nickname jesus gave simon the fisherman. it means rock. in greek it became petros, and in english peter. paul mostly calls him cephas in his letters.\ncall it rocky. the gospels then spend three years showing how little like a rock he was.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter","why":"the man the name belongs to"}],"name":"cephas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["aramaic"],"key":"aramaic","kind":"word","lines":"the everyday language of the middle east in jesus's time, a cousin of hebrew. jesus and his friends spoke it. the gospels were written in greek, but they keep a few of his aramaic words, like cephas, rabboni, and eloi, eloi, lema sabachthani on the cross.\na few communities in syria and iraq still speak forms of it today.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aramaic","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"aramaic","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the last supper","last supper"],"key":"lastsupper","kind":"event","lines":"the final meal jesus ate with his friends, the night before he was executed. he broke bread and passed wine and told them this is my body, this is my blood. every christian communion service goes back to that table.\nit's also the night peter swore he'd die before he'd deny him. leonardo da vinci's painting of it is the one everybody pictures.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_Supper","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the last supper","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the high priest","high priest","high priest's"],"key":"highpriest","kind":"person","lines":"the top religious official in jerusalem in jesus's day, in charge of the temple, the one great jewish temple where sacrifices were made. under roman rule he also dealt with the romans, so he was part priest and part politician.\nthe one who had jesus arrested was caiaphas. peter's denial happened in his courtyard, by a charcoal fire, while jesus was being questioned inside.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Priest_of_Israel","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caiaphas","why":"the one in the story"}],"name":"the high priest","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["malchus"],"key":"malchus","kind":"person","lines":"the high priest's servant whose ear peter cut off with a sword when the soldiers came for jesus in the olive grove. only john's gospel gives his name. luke says jesus touched the ear and healed it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malchus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"malchus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the denial","denied him","deny him","denial of peter"],"key":"denial","kind":"event","lines":"the night jesus was arrested, peter followed him to the high priest's house and waited in the courtyard by a fire. a servant girl said you were with him. he said he didn't know him. two more people asked. two more times he said no, the last time with curses.\nthen the rooster crowed, the way jesus had said it would, and peter went out and wept. all four gospels tell it, and none of them clean it up. it's the most human scene in the book, the loudest friend folding in front of a servant girl.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denial_of_Peter","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"peter's denial","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the rooster crowed","rooster crowed","the rooster","the cock crowed"],"key":"rooster","kind":"word","lines":"at the last supper jesus told peter that before the rooster crowed that night, peter would deny him three times. he did, and it did.\nthat's why you see roosters on top of church steeples all over europe. it's a reminder that even the rock folded, and got asked back.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denial_of_Peter","why":"the story"}],"name":"the rooster","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["antioch"],"key":"antioch","kind":"place","lines":"one of the biggest cities of the roman empire, in what's now southern turkey near the syrian border, a rich, mixed place of greeks, jews, and everyone else. it's where jesus's followers were first called christians.\nit's also where paul called peter a hypocrite to his face, around the year 49, for pulling back from eating with non-jewish christians. today the city is called antakya, and much of it was flattened in the 2023 earthquake.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antioch","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"antioch","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gentile","gentiles"],"key":"gentile","kind":"word","lines":"anyone who isn't jewish. that's all it means. the word comes from the latin for the nations, as in everybody else.\nthe first christians were all jews, and the big early fight was whether gentiles had to become jews first, get circumcised and keep the food laws, to follow jesus. paul said no. peter agreed, then wobbled. the answer they landed on is why christianity became its own religion.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gentile","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gentile","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ananias and sapphira","sapphira"],"key":"ananias","kind":"person","lines":"a married couple in the book of acts, in the very first church in jerusalem, where people pooled their money. they sold some land, kept part of the price, and said they'd given it all. when peter confronted them, each one dropped dead on the spot, one after the other.\nit's one of the most unsettling stories in the new testament, and peter doesn't come off gentle in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ananias_and_Sapphira","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ananias and sapphira","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["james, the brother of jesus","james, jesus's brother","jesus's brother"],"key":"jamesbrother","kind":"person","lines":"one of jesus's brothers, who didn't follow him during his life, by the gospels' account, and then became the leader of the church in jerusalem after he died. he was strict about jewish law and respected for it.\nwhen men from james came to antioch, peter stopped eating with gentiles. the jewish historian josephus says james was stoned to death in jerusalem in 62.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James,_brother_of_Jesus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"james, the brother of jesus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["barnabas"],"key":"barnabas","kind":"person","lines":"an early christian from cyprus who vouched for paul when nobody in jerusalem trusted the former persecutor, and then went on the road with him as his partner. at antioch even barnabas followed peter in eating apart from the gentiles, which stung paul.\nthe two later split over whether to bring along a young man who'd quit on them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnabas","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"barnabas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pentecost"],"key":"pentecost","kind":"event","lines":"a jewish harvest festival fifty days after passover. in acts 2 it's the day the holy spirit came down on jesus's followers in jerusalem like tongues of fire, and peter, who'd folded in front of a servant girl seven weeks earlier, stood up and preached to a crowd.\nchristians count it as the church's birthday. it's also where pentecostal churches get their name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pentecost","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pentecost","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["centurion","centurions"],"key":"centurion","kind":"word","lines":"an officer in the roman army, in charge of around eighty to a hundred men. in acts 10 a centurion named cornelius sends for peter, and peter goes into a gentile's house for the first time in his life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centurion","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"centurion","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["herod agrippa"],"key":"herodagrippa","kind":"person","lines":"a grandson of herod the great who ruled judea for rome from 41 to 44. in acts 12 he has james, the brother of john, executed and throws peter in prison, and peter walks out at night.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herod_Agrippa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"herod agrippa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the acts of peter","acts of peter","quo vadis"],"key":"actsofpeter","kind":"book","lines":"a book from the late second century, over a hundred years after peter, full of legends about him in rome. it isn't in the bible.\nit's where two famous stories come from. fleeing rome, peter meets jesus on the road and asks quo vadis, latin for where are you going, and jesus says to rome, to be crucified again, so peter turns back. and when they crucify him, he asks to go upside down.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acts_of_Peter","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the acts of peter","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the twelve","twelve disciples","the twelve disciples"],"key":"thetwelve","kind":"group","lines":"the inner circle of twelve followers jesus picked, peter first among them. fishermen, a tax collector, a political zealot, and judas, who betrayed him. twelve on purpose, one for each of the twelve tribes of old israel.\nafter judas died they picked someone to fill his seat, and they're mostly called the apostles from then on, the ones sent out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostles","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the twelve","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["apostle","apostles","apostle to the apostles"],"key":"apostle","kind":"word","lines":"someone sent out with a message. the word is greek. in the new testament it means the twelve jesus picked, plus paul, who insisted he counted too.\nmary magdalene's old title is apostle to the apostles, because on easter morning she was sent to tell them. the one who was sent to the ones who were sent.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostles","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"apostle","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["first pope"],"key":"firstpope","kind":"word","lines":"the catholic church says peter was the first bishop of rome, and that every pope since sits in his chair. the line comes from matthew 16, where jesus says you are peter, and on this rock i will build my church.\nprotestants and the orthodox read that verse differently. st peter's basilica in the vatican is built over the spot where tradition says he's buried, and bones were found there in the twentieth century.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter","why":"his life and what the church made of him"}],"name":"the pope as peter's successor","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["feed my sheep"],"key":"feedmysheep","kind":"word","lines":"what jesus tells peter on the beach in john 21, after asking him three times if he loves him, once for each denial. no forgiveness speech, no lecture. a job.\nsheep there means people, and a shepherd is someone who takes care of them. that's where the word pastor comes from. it's latin for shepherd.","links":[],"name":"feed my sheep","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["goliath"],"key":"goliath","kind":"person","lines":"the philistine giant in the book of 1 samuel who stood in a valley for forty days daring any israelite to fight him. david, a shepherd kid, walked out with a sling and five smooth stones and killed him with the first one.\ndavid and goliath is now what everyone calls any fight between a nobody and a giant.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goliath","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"goliath","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["philistines","the philistines","philistine"],"key":"philistines","kind":"group","lines":"a people who lived on the coast of what's now israel and gaza around 1000 bc, probably seafarers who came from the aegean. in the bible they're israel's enemy for generations, and goliath was their champion.\nbecause of a nineteenth century insult, philistine now means someone with no taste in art. the real ones made pretty nice pottery.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philistines","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the philistines","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the prophet samuel","prophet samuel"],"key":"samuelprophet","kind":"person","lines":"the last great judge and prophet of old israel. he made and unmade kings. when the people demanded one, he anointed saul, and when saul went bad, god sent him to bethlehem to find a new one, and he picked the youngest son, a shepherd named david.\ntwo books of the bible, 1 samuel and 2 samuel, are named after him, and they tell david's whole story, the worst parts included.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"samuel","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["1 samuel","2 samuel","books of samuel"],"key":"booksofsamuel","kind":"book","lines":"two books in the hebrew bible, the old testament, that tell the story of israel getting its first kings, saul and then david. they're some of the best storytelling in the bible, and they don't protect anybody.\n1 samuel has goliath and david on the run. 2 samuel has david as king, bathsheba, the prophet nathan, and his son absalom's revolt.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Books_of_Samuel","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the books of samuel","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["prophet","prophets","the prophets","hebrew prophets"],"key":"prophet","kind":"word","lines":"in the bible, a prophet isn't mainly someone who predicts the future. it's someone who speaks for god, and usually says what nobody in charge wants to hear. nathan to king david, amos to the rich, jonah, very unwillingly, to nineveh.\nthe hebrew prophets, isaiah, jeremiah, amos and the rest, spent most of their time telling people that god cares more about how you treat the poor than about your religious ceremonies. heschel wrote a big book called the prophets, and it's the thread that ran from them to martin luther king.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prophet","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nevi%27im","why":"the prophets' books in the hebrew bible"}],"name":"prophet","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["king saul"],"key":"kingsaul","kind":"person","lines":"israel's first king, tall and handsome and troubled. he took the young david into his court, then got jealous and threw a spear at him while he played music, and spent years hunting him through the hills.\nhe ended by falling on his own sword in a lost battle against the philistines. his son jonathan was david's closest friend.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saul","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"king saul","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lyre","lyres"],"key":"lyre","kind":"word","lines":"a small harp you hold in your arms, with strings stretched over a frame. david played one for king saul to calm him down, and it's why he's always drawn with a harp.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyre","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lyre","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hebron"],"key":"hebron","kind":"place","lines":"an old city in the hills south of jerusalem, now in the west bank. david was first made king there and ruled from it for seven years before taking jerusalem. tradition says abraham is buried there too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebron","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hebron","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ark of the covenant","the ark of the covenant"],"key":"ark","kind":"word","lines":"a gold-covered wooden chest that held the stone tablets of the ten commandments. it was the holiest thing israel had, the place god was thought to be present. only priests could carry it, on poles.\nwhen david brought it into jerusalem he danced in front of it so hard his wife said he'd made a fool of himself. it disappeared when the babylonians destroyed jerusalem in 587 bc, and nobody knows what happened to it. indiana jones has a theory.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ark_of_the_Covenant","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ark of the covenant","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["psalm 23","twenty third","the lord is my shepherd"],"key":"psalmtwentythree","kind":"book","lines":"the most famous psalm, six verses long, headed as one of david's. the lord is my shepherd, i shall not want. even though i walk through the valley of the shadow of death.\nit's the one they read at funerals, in hospitals, and in foxholes. a shepherd king writing about god as a shepherd.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psalm_23","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"psalm 23","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["psalm 51"],"key":"psalmfiftyone","kind":"book","lines":"the psalm headed as david's prayer after the prophet nathan confronted him about bathsheba and the murder of her husband. have mercy on me, o god. create in me a clean heart.\nit doesn't make one excuse. it's the model for every prayer of real sorrow since, and it's the most famous of the penitential psalms.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psalm_51","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"psalm 51","status":"approved","threads":["forgiveness"]},{"aliases":["psalm 31","psalm 31:5","into your hands i commit my spirit"],"key":"psalmthirtyone","kind":"book","lines":"one of the psalms headed as david's, a prayer from someone surrounded by enemies. its fifth verse, into your hands i commit my spirit, is what luke's gospel says jesus said as he died.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psalm_31","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"psalm 31","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["absalom","my son absalom"],"key":"absalom","kind":"person","lines":"david's handsome, charming son. after his half brother amnon raped his sister tamar and david did nothing, absalom waited two years and killed him. later he turned the country against his father and took the throne. david fled jerusalem barefoot.\nin the battle that followed, absalom's long hair caught in a tree and david's general killed him hanging there. david's cry when he heard, my son absalom, would i had died instead of you, is one of the saddest lines in any book.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absalom","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"absalom","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mount of olives","the mount of olives"],"key":"mountofolives","kind":"place","lines":"a hill just east of jerusalem across a small valley, once covered in olive trees. david walked up it barefoot and weeping when he fled his son absalom. a thousand years later jesus prayed at its foot the night he was arrested.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_of_Olives","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mount of olives","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bathsheba"],"key":"bathsheba","kind":"person","lines":"the wife of uriah, one of david's soldiers. david saw her bathing from his palace roof, had her brought to him, and got her pregnant, and then had her husband killed. he was the king, and the text never says she had any say.\nshe became his wife and later the mother of solomon, the next king. she fought to put solomon on the throne when david was dying, and won.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bathsheba","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bathsheba","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["uriah the hittite","uriah"],"key":"uriah","kind":"person","lines":"bathsheba's husband, one of david's best soldiers, a foreigner, a hittite. david called him home from the war so he'd sleep with his wife and cover up the pregnancy. uriah wouldn't go home while his comrades were in the field.\nso david sent him back carrying the letter that ordered his own death. he's the most honorable man in the story, and he's the one who dies.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uriah_the_Hittite","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"uriah the hittite","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nathan the prophet","the prophet nathan"],"key":"nathan","kind":"person","lines":"a prophet at king david's court. after david took bathsheba and had her husband killed, nathan came and told him a story about a rich man who stole a poor man's only lamb. david was furious and said the man deserved to die. nathan said, you are the man.\nit's the model for telling the truth to power. he didn't accuse. he let the king convict himself.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nathan_(prophet)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nathan the prophet","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["joab"],"key":"joab","kind":"person","lines":"the commander of david's army, and his nephew. hard and loyal and ruthless. he carried out david's order to leave uriah alone at the front, and later killed absalom against david's orders.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joab","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joab","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["en-gedi","en gedi","engedi"],"key":"engedi","kind":"place","lines":"an oasis of springs and waterfalls in the desert cliffs above the dead sea. david hid in its caves on the run from king saul, and when saul wandered into one alone, david cut off a corner of his robe instead of killing him. it's a nature reserve now, with ibex.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ein_Gedi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"en-gedi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jonathan"],"key":"jonathan","kind":"person","lines":"king saul's son and david's closest friend, who protected david from his own father. when jonathan was killed in battle with saul, david wrote a lament saying jonathan's love for him was more wonderful than the love of women.\npeople have argued for a long time about what that means. the text doesn't say more.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonathan_(1_Samuel)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jonathan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["a man after god's own heart","man after god's own heart"],"key":"godsownheart","kind":"word","lines":"a phrase from 1 samuel 13, where the prophet samuel tells king saul that god has found a man after his own heart to replace him. it means david, before he'd done anything wrong, and paul repeats it in acts 13.\npeople quote it as if it excused what david did later. the book kept both in, side by side, on purpose.","links":[],"name":"a man after god's own heart","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["magdala"],"key":"magdala","kind":"place","lines":"a fishing town on the west shore of the sea of galilee, famous for salting fish. mary magdalene means mary from magdala. archaeologists dug up a first century synagogue there in 2009.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdala","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"magdala","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["seven demons","the seven demons"],"key":"sevendemons","kind":"word","lines":"luke 8 says seven demons had gone out of mary magdalene. that's all it says. it could mean a terrible illness, madness, epilepsy, or something else. seven in the bible usually means complete, so whatever it was, it was as bad as it gets.\nit doesn't mean sin, and it definitely doesn't mean prostitute. that came later, from a pope.","links":[],"name":"the seven demons","status":"approved","threads":["madness"]},{"aliases":["herod antipas"],"key":"herodantipas","kind":"person","lines":"a son of herod the great who ruled galilee for rome during jesus's whole life. he beheaded john the baptist. jesus called him that fox.\nthe wife of his household manager, joanna, followed jesus and helped pay for his travels, which means money from the ruler's own house was funding the man he'd later mock.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herod_Antipas","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"herod antipas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the empty tomb","empty tomb","stone rolled away"],"key":"emptytomb","kind":"event","lines":"on the sunday after jesus was crucified, his women followers went to the rock-cut tomb where he'd been laid and found the stone rolled back and the body gone. that's where the story of the resurrection starts in all four gospels.\nin john's version mary magdalene is there first, alone, in the dark, and she's the first to see him. whatever you believe about it, the first witnesses in every gospel are women, at a time when a woman's testimony didn't count in court.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empty_tomb","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the empty tomb","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the disciple jesus loved","disciple jesus loved","beloved disciple"],"key":"belovedisciple","kind":"person","lines":"a follower of jesus who shows up only in john's gospel and is never named. he leans on jesus at the last supper, stands at the cross, and races peter to the tomb. tradition says he's john himself, the one the gospel is named for, but nobody knows.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disciple_whom_Jesus_loved","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the disciple jesus loved","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rabboni"],"key":"rabboni","kind":"word","lines":"aramaic for my teacher, a warmer form of rabbi. it's what mary magdalene says in john 20 when the man she took for the gardener says her name and she realizes who he is.","links":[],"name":"rabboni","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["good friday"],"key":"goodfriday","kind":"event","lines":"the friday jesus was crucified, remembered every year two days before easter. nobody's quite sure why it's called good. christians say it's because of what came after.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Good_Friday","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"good friday","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["pope gregory the great","gregory the great"],"key":"gregory","kind":"person","lines":"pope from 590 to 604, one of the most powerful popes of the early middle ages. he organized relief for rome's poor, sent missionaries to england, and gave gregorian chant its name.\nin a sermon in 591 he merged mary magdalene with the unnamed sinful woman in luke 7 and with mary of bethany. the sinful woman became a prostitute in later preaching, and for fourteen centuries so did mary magdalene.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Gregory_I","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pope gregory the great","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sinful woman","the sinful woman","luke 7"],"key":"sinfulwoman","kind":"person","lines":"an unnamed woman in luke 7 who comes into a dinner party, cries on jesus's feet, dries them with her hair, and pours perfume on them, while his host sniffs that if jesus were a real prophet he'd know what kind of woman she is.\njesus says her sins are forgiven because she loved much. she has no name, and she isn't mary magdalene. a pope later made her one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anointing_of_Jesus","why":"the stories of women anointing jesus, luke 7 included"}],"name":"the sinful woman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mary of bethany"],"key":"maryofbethany","kind":"person","lines":"the sister of martha and lazarus, in a village just outside jerusalem. she's the one who sits at jesus's feet listening while martha does the cooking, and in john 12 she pours expensive perfume on his feet. a different mary from mary magdalene, merged with her by a pope in 591.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_of_Bethany","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mary of bethany","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ephesus"],"key":"ephesus","kind":"place","lines":"a big greek port city on the coast of what's now western turkey, famous for its huge temple to the goddess artemis. paul lived there for a couple of years. an eastern tradition says mary magdalene died there. its ruins are some of the best preserved in the world.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephesus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ephesus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sainte-baume","sainte baume"],"key":"saintebaume","kind":"place","lines":"a cave high on a limestone cliff in provence, in the south of france. a french legend says mary magdalene sailed there after jesus died and spent her last thirty years in it praying. there's a chapel in it now, and dominican friars look after it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sainte-Baume","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sainte-baume","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tiberius","emperor tiberius"],"key":"tiberius","kind":"person","lines":"the roman emperor from 14 to 37, so during all of jesus's adult life. gloomy and suspicious, he spent his last years on the island of capri. an old legend has mary magdalene visiting him with an egg to tell him about the resurrection.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiberius","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tiberius","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["orthodox icons"],"key":"icon","kind":"word","lines":"a painted holy image, usually on wood, in the eastern orthodox churches of greece, russia, and the middle east. they're painted in a strict, flat old style with gold backgrounds, and people pray in front of them and kiss them.\nthe icons of mary magdalene show her holding a red egg, from the legend about her and the emperor. it's one reason orthodox christians dye eggs red at easter.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icon","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"icon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["latin"],"key":"latin","kind":"word","lines":"the language of ancient rome, which became the language of the western church for over fifteen hundred years. the catholic mass was said in latin until the sixties. italian, spanish, french, and portuguese all grew out of it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"latin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the gospel of mary","gospel of mary"],"key":"gospelofmary","kind":"book","lines":"a text from the second century, over a hundred years after jesus, found in egypt in pieces, with more than half the pages missing. it's not in the bible. in it mary magdalene teaches the other disciples something jesus told her privately.\npeter pushes back and asks if jesus really spoke with a woman and not openly to them. another disciple tells him he's always been hot-tempered. whatever it is as history, the argument sounds real.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Mary","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the gospel of mary","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["noli me tangere"],"key":"nolimetangere","kind":"word","lines":"latin for don't touch me, or don't hold on to me. it's what jesus says to mary magdalene in john 20 when she recognizes him in the garden.\nit's one of the most painted scenes in european art, a woman reaching and a man pulling back, and it's in half the museums in europe.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noli_me_tangere","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"noli me tangere","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["alabaster jar"],"key":"alabasterjar","kind":"word","lines":"a small jar carved from pale, soft stone, used for expensive perfumed oil. in the gospels a woman pours one over jesus. painters gave it to mary magdalene for centuries, but it belongs to other women in the story. every picture of her with a jar is a picture of the mix-up.","links":[],"name":"the alabaster jar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nineveh"],"key":"nineveh","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of the assyrian empire, on the tigris river across from modern mosul in northern iraq. for a while it was the biggest city in the world, with huge walls and palaces carved with scenes of the empire's wars.\nto the people of israel it was the enemy capital, the city of the people who destroyed them. the book of jonah is funny because god sends a jewish prophet there to save it. it was destroyed in 612 bc, and the islamic state blew up some of what was left in 2016.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nineveh","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nineveh","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["assyria","assyrian","assyrians"],"key":"assyria","kind":"place","lines":"an empire based in what's now northern iraq that was the terror of the middle east from the 800s to the 600s bc. it conquered by fear, deporting whole populations and bragging in stone carvings about skinning rebels.\nin 722 bc it wiped out the northern kingdom of israel and carried its people off. imagine being told to go help the country that did that to your people.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assyria","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"assyria","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["joppa","jaffa"],"key":"joppa","kind":"place","lines":"an old port on the coast of israel, now jaffa, part of tel aviv. jonah went down there to catch a ship going the other way from nineveh. peter was staying there when he had his vision of the sheet full of animals.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaffa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joppa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tarshish"],"key":"tarshish","kind":"place","lines":"a far-off port somewhere west across the mediterranean, probably in spain. to someone in israel it was about as far as a ship could take you. jonah bought a ticket there when god told him to go east.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarshish","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tarshish","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["casting lots","cast lots","casting of lots"],"key":"castinglots","kind":"word","lines":"a way of making a decision by chance, like drawing straws or throwing dice, which people in the bible believed god could steer. the sailors in jonah cast lots to find whose fault the storm was, and the soldiers at the cross cast lots for jesus's clothes.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleromancy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"casting lots","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sackcloth","sackcloth and ashes"],"key":"sackcloth","kind":"word","lines":"rough, scratchy cloth made of goat hair, the stuff sacks were made of. in the old middle east you wore it, and sat in ashes, to show you were grieving or sorry. the king of nineveh did it, and made the cattle wear it too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sackcloth","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sackcloth","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["northern kingdom of israel","northern kingdom","the twelve tribes","twelve tribes"],"key":"northernkingdom","kind":"place","lines":"after king solomon died, around 930 bc, the old kingdom split in two. ten of the twelve tribes, the family clans israel traced itself to, formed the northern kingdom of israel. the other two became the kingdom of judah, around jerusalem.\nassyria destroyed the northern kingdom in 722 bc and scattered its people. they're the ten lost tribes of legend.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Israel_(Samaria)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the northern kingdom of israel","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yom kippur","day of atonement"],"key":"yomkippur","kind":"event","lines":"the day of atonement, the holiest day of the jewish year, in the fall. jews fast for about twenty five hours, no food, no water, and spend most of it in synagogue asking to be forgiven, by god and by the people they've wronged.\nin the afternoon they read the whole book of jonah out loud, the story of a prophet who's furious that god forgave his enemies. even people who never go to synagogue often go on yom kippur.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yom_Kippur","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"yom kippur","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["king james bible","the king james bible","king james version"],"key":"kingjames","kind":"book","lines":"the english translation of the bible made for king james i and published in 1611. for about three hundred and fifty years it was the english bible, and a lot of everyday english comes from it, the salt of the earth, the powers that be, a fly in the ointment.\nit's the one that turned jonah's great fish into a whale.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_James_Version","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the king james bible","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sign of jonah","the sign of jonah","matthew 12"],"key":"signofjonah","kind":"word","lines":"in matthew 12 people ask jesus for a miraculous sign to prove himself. he says they'll get none except the sign of jonah. as jonah was three days in the belly of the fish, he says, he'll be three days in the heart of the earth, and nineveh repented at jonah's preaching and they won't.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sign_of_Jonah","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sign of jonah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hasidic","hasidism","hasid","hasidim"],"key":"hasidic","kind":"group","lines":"a movement inside judaism that started in poland and ukraine in the 1700s with a healer and teacher called the baal shem tov. it said god is everywhere, in everything, and can be served with joy, singing, dancing, and stories, not just by scholars poring over the law.\neach hasidic community gathers around its rebbe, a holy man whose job often passes from father to son. most of their world in eastern europe was wiped out in the holocaust. today the men in black coats, hats, and side curls in brooklyn and jerusalem are hasidim.\nheschel came from a long line of hasidic rebbes. he left that world for a berlin university and never stopped praying the way he'd been raised.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasidic_Judaism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hasidism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rebbe","rebbes"],"key":"rebbe","kind":"word","lines":"the leader of a hasidic community, a teacher, holy man, and counselor all at once. followers bring him their troubles and hang on his words, and the position often runs in families, father to son, for generations.\na rabbi is someone trained in jewish law. a rebbe is someone people follow. heschel was named after a famous one among his ancestors, and everyone expected him to become one too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rebbe","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rebbe","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["warsaw"],"key":"warsaw","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of poland. before the second world war it had the biggest jewish community in europe, about a third of the city. the nazis walled them into a ghetto, and almost all of them were murdered.\nheschel was born there in 1907 and got out six weeks before the germans invaded in 1939. his mother and sisters didn't.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warsaw","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Jews_in_Poland","why":"the jewish world he came from"}],"name":"warsaw","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jewish theological seminary","the jewish theological seminary"],"key":"jts","kind":"place","lines":"the main school in new york that trains conservative rabbis, the middle road of american judaism. heschel taught ethics and mysticism there from 1945 until he died in 1972.\nit's across the street from union theological seminary, the protestant school where heschel gave his famous lecture no religion is an island.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewish_Theological_Seminary_of_America","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the jewish theological seminary","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["conservative judaism","conservative rabbis"],"key":"conservativejudaism","kind":"group","lines":"the middle of the three big branches of american judaism. orthodox keeps the old law strictly. reform modernized a lot of it away. conservative keeps the law but says it can change over time.\nthe name has nothing to do with politics. heschel taught at its seminary for almost thirty years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Judaism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"conservative judaism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["reform judaism","reform rabbis","hebrew union college"],"key":"reformjudaism","kind":"group","lines":"the liberal, modern branch of judaism, started in germany in the 1800s. it dropped a lot of the old rules about food, dress, and prayer and says the law is a guide, not a set of commands. it's the biggest branch in america.\nhebrew union college in cincinnati trains its rabbis. heschel's first american job was there, and he was lonely, a hasid from warsaw among people who didn't pray the way he did.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_Judaism","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebrew_Union_College-Jewish_Institute_of_Religion","why":"the college in cincinnati"}],"name":"reform judaism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["second vatican council","the second vatican council","vatican council","vatican ii"],"key":"vaticanii","kind":"event","lines":"a huge meeting of all the catholic bishops of the world in rome, from 1962 to 1965, where the church rethought a lot of things. mass in local languages instead of latin, a friendlier stance toward other christians and other religions.\none of its documents finally said jews as a people were not to blame for jesus's death, and dropped the old contempt. heschel lobbied cardinals and met the pope to push it there, and said he'd rather go to auschwitz than give up his faith to be converted.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Vatican_Council","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nostra_aetate","why":"the document about the jews"}],"name":"the second vatican council","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["isaiah"],"key":"isaiah","kind":"person","lines":"one of the great hebrew prophets, in jerusalem in the 700s bc, and the longest prophet's book in the bible. he said god was sick of their holy days and sacrifices while the poor were crushed, and he pictured a day when nations would beat their swords into plowshares.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaiah","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"isaiah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["amos"],"key":"amos","kind":"person","lines":"a shepherd and fig farmer who became a prophet in israel in the 700s bc, the first whose words got written down as a book. he told the rich that god hated their religious festivals and wanted justice instead.\nlet justice roll down like waters, and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream. martin luther king quoted that line from amos again and again.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amos_(prophet)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"amos","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["god in search of man"],"key":"godinsearchofman","kind":"book","lines":"heschel's big 1955 book on jewish faith. its idea is that it isn't only people looking for god. god is looking for us. it starts with wonder, what he calls radical amazement, the plain shock that anything exists at all.","links":[],"name":"god in search of man","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["man is not alone"],"key":"manisnotalone","kind":"book","lines":"heschel's 1951 book on how faith starts, with awe and wonder at the world rather than with arguments. it made his name in america.","links":[],"name":"man is not alone","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the sabbaths are our great cathedrals","cathedrals in time"],"key":"thesabbathbook","kind":"book","lines":"heschel's short 1951 book about the jewish day of rest, friday sundown to saturday sundown. other people build cathedrals in space, he says, and the jews built theirs in time. the seventh day is a palace you walk into every week.\nit's short, and it's the best way in to him. anyone who's ever wanted a day with no phone can read it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Joshua_Heschel","why":"his life and books"}],"name":"the sabbath (heschel's book)","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["moral grandeur and spiritual audacity"],"key":"moralgrandeur","kind":"book","lines":"a collection of heschel's essays, edited by his daughter susannah heschel and published in 1996. it has the pieces on selma, vietnam, and the prophets, and his notes on feeling his legs praying.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susannah_Heschel","why":"his daughter, who edited it"}],"name":"moral grandeur and spiritual audacity","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["radical amazement"],"key":"radicalamazement","kind":"word","lines":"heschel's phrase for where faith starts. not with a proof or a doctrine, but with the plain shock that there's anything at all. he thought losing that shock was the real danger, getting so used to the world you stop noticing it.","links":[],"name":"radical amazement","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["flower leis","flower lei","leis"],"key":"leis","kind":"word","lines":"garlands of flowers from hawaii, worn around the neck as a sign of welcome and peace. a group from hawaii brought them to the selma march in 1965, and the front row, king, heschel, and the rest, wore them. the photograph with the lei and the big white beard is the one the country saw.","links":[],"name":"flower leis","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["national conference on religion and race","conference on religion and race"],"key":"religionandrace","kind":"event","lines":"a meeting in chicago in january 1963 that brought protestant, catholic, and jewish leaders together on civil rights. heschel opened by saying the first such conference had been between pharaoh and moses, and it still wasn't over. king spoke too. it's where they met.","links":[],"name":"the national conference on religion and race","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["clergy and laymen concerned about vietnam"],"key":"clalc","kind":"group","lines":"an antiwar group of ministers, priests, and rabbis started in 1965, with heschel among the founders. it organized the night at riverside church in new york in april 1967 where king gave his speech against the vietnam war.","links":[],"name":"clergy and laymen concerned about vietnam","status":"approved","threads":["war","nonviolence"]},{"aliases":["rabbinical assembly","the rabbinical assembly"],"key":"rabbinicalassembly","kind":"group","lines":"the association of conservative rabbis in america. in march 1968 it held its convention at a resort in the catskills and invited martin luther king to speak. heschel introduced him. king was killed ten days later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbinical_Assembly","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the rabbinical assembly","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["catskills","the catskills","catskill mountains","concord hotel"],"key":"catskills","kind":"place","lines":"low mountains a couple of hours north of new york city. from the twenties to the sixties they were full of huge jewish resort hotels, the borscht belt, where families came for the summer and young comedians learned their trade. the concord was one of the biggest.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catskill_Mountains","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borscht_Belt","why":"the old resort world"}],"name":"the catskills","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ralph abernathy","abernathy"],"key":"abernathy","kind":"person","lines":"a baptist minister from alabama who was martin luther king's closest friend and right hand from the montgomery bus boycott on. he marched in the front row at selma, and led the movement after king was killed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Abernathy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ralph abernathy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ralph bunche","bunche"],"key":"bunche","kind":"person","lines":"an american diplomat at the united nations and the first black person to win the nobel peace prize, in 1950, for negotiating peace between israel and its neighbors. he marched in the front row out of selma in 1965.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Bunche","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ralph bunche","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["herem","cherem"],"key":"herem","kind":"word","lines":"the hebrew word for a ban, the jewish community's version of excommunication. it cut you off completely. nobody could talk to you, do business with you, or stay under a roof with you.\nspinoza's, read out in the amsterdam synagogue in july 1656, was one of the harshest the community ever issued. it cursed him by day and by night, and never said exactly what he'd done.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herem_(censure)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"herem","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["portuguese jews","jews of amsterdam","amsterdam jews"],"key":"amsterdamjews","kind":"group","lines":"families who fled spain and portugal, where they'd been forced to live as christians and the inquisition hunted anyone who kept jewish ways in secret. in the 1600s amsterdam let them live openly as jews, one of the few places in europe that would.\nthey built a proud, rich community, with a grand synagogue that still stands. they were newcomers to their own religion, relearning it after generations in hiding, and nervous about anyone who might get them in trouble with their dutch hosts. that's the world that threw spinoza out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Jews_in_the_Netherlands","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the portuguese jews of amsterdam","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["synagogue","synagogues"],"key":"synagogue","kind":"place","lines":"a jewish house of prayer, study, and community, the jewish version of a church. services are led by a rabbi or by members, and the torah scroll is kept in a cabinet at the front.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synagogue","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"synagogue","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cubit","cubits","four cubits"],"key":"cubit","kind":"word","lines":"an old measure, the length of a forearm from the elbow to the tip of the middle finger, about a foot and a half. four cubits is about six feet, the same distance everyone learned in 2020. spinoza's herem said no one could come within it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubit","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cubit","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["grinding lenses","grinding glass lenses","ground lenses","lens grinding","grinding wheel"],"key":"lensgrinding","kind":"word","lines":"shaping and polishing glass into lenses for glasses, microscopes, and telescopes, by hand, on a wheel. in the 1600s it was cutting edge work, the tech of the scientific revolution. spinoza was very good at it, and scientists came to him for lenses.\nit was also quiet, solitary work that let him think. the glass dust he breathed for years probably made the lung disease that killed him at forty four worse.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baruch_Spinoza","why":"his life, including the lens work"}],"name":"lens grinding","status":"approved","threads":["work"]},{"aliases":["the ethics"],"key":"ethics","kind":"book","lines":"spinoza's big book, finished around 1675 and published only after he died, because he didn't dare. it's laid out like a geometry textbook, with definitions, axioms, and numbered proofs.\nit says there's one substance, one basic stuff that everything is, which you can call god or nature. no god outside the world making choices. the good life is understanding how everything fits, and that understanding is joy. it ends with the line all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethics_(Spinoza_book)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3800","why":"the elwes translation on project gutenberg"}],"name":"the ethics","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["deus sive natura","god or nature"],"key":"deussivenatura","kind":"word","lines":"latin for god, or nature. spinoza's phrase in the ethics for the one thing everything is. he means they're two names for the same thing, not that god made nature.\nthat's why people called him an atheist for a century, and why einstein said spinoza's god was the only one he believed in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deus_sive_Natura","why":"the ethics, where the phrase lives"}],"name":"deus sive natura","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rijnsburg"],"key":"rijnsburg","kind":"place","lines":"a village near leiden in holland where spinoza rented rooms in the early 1660s, grinding lenses and writing. the little house is a museum now, with a copy of his library.","links":[],"name":"rijnsburg","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leiden"],"key":"leiden","kind":"place","lines":"a university town in holland, then one of the great centers of learning in europe. spinoza lived in a village next to it for a few years, near enough to talk to the students and scientists.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leiden","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leiden","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["heidelberg","university of heidelberg"],"key":"heidelberg","kind":"place","lines":"a famous old university town in germany. in 1673 it offered spinoza a professorship of philosophy, with a promise he could teach freely as long as he didn't disturb the established religion. he turned it down, because that promise was exactly the catch.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heidelberg_University","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"heidelberg","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["on the improvement of the understanding","improvement of the understanding"],"key":"improvementunderstanding","kind":"book","lines":"an early, unfinished book by spinoza. it opens with one of the most honest passages in philosophy, saying experience had taught him that money, fame, and pleasure are empty, and that he went looking for something that would give lasting joy, like a sick man looking for a cure.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treatise_on_the_Emendation_of_the_Intellect","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"on the improvement of the understanding","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["de witt brothers","the de witt brothers","johan de witt"],"key":"dewitt","kind":"person","lines":"johan and cornelis de witt. johan ran the dutch republic for almost twenty years. in 1672, the disaster year, when france invaded, a mob in the hague lynched both brothers and tore their bodies apart.\nspinoza admired johan and was so furious he wanted to post a sign at the scene calling the killers the lowest of barbarians. his landlord locked him in the house to keep him alive.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johan_de_Witt","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the de witt brothers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dutch republic","the dutch republic"],"key":"dutchrepublic","kind":"place","lines":"the country that became the netherlands, a republic of provinces from 1581 to 1795, run by merchants rather than kings. in the 1600s it was the richest place in europe, with the freest press, which is why spinoza, descartes, and a lot of banned books ended up there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Republic","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dutch republic","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leibniz","gottfried leibniz"],"key":"leibniz","kind":"person","lines":"a german philosopher and mathematician who invented calculus at the same time as newton. he visited spinoza in the hague in 1676, a few months before spinoza died, and they talked for days. afterward he played the visit down, since being linked to spinoza was bad for a career.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leibniz","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["theological political treatise","theological-political treatise","tractatus theologico-politicus"],"key":"theologicalpolitical","kind":"book","lines":"spinoza's 1670 book arguing for freedom of thought and speech, and reading the bible as a book written by people at particular times, not dictated by god. it came out with no author's name and a fake publisher. it was banned in 1674.\nit's one of the founding books of modern bible scholarship and of free speech.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tractatus_Theologico-Politicus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the theological-political treatise","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["political treatise"],"key":"politicaltreatise","kind":"book","lines":"spinoza's last book, about how governments should work, unfinished when he died in 1677. he says in it that he tried not to mock people's actions, or mourn them, or hate them, only to understand them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tractatus_Politicus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the political treatise","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["einstein","albert einstein"],"key":"einstein","kind":"person","lines":"albert einstein, the physicist of relativity. in 1929 a new york rabbi cabled him asking if he believed in god. he cabled back that he believed in spinoza's god, who shows himself in the harmony of everything that exists, not in a god who concerns himself with what people do.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religious_and_philosophical_views_of_Albert_Einstein","why":"his views on god, spinoza included"}],"name":"einstein","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["copenhagen"],"key":"copenhagen","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of denmark. in kierkegaard's day it was a small, gossipy city of about a hundred and twenty thousand people where everyone knew everyone.\nhe walked its streets for hours a day talking to anybody, which is why the cartoons in the corsair hurt so much. there was nowhere to hide.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copenhagen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"copenhagen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jutland"],"key":"jutland","kind":"place","lines":"the peninsula that makes up the mainland of denmark, flat heath and farmland. kierkegaard's father grew up there as a poor shepherd boy, and the family story was that he'd cursed god on a hillside there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jutland","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jutland","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["regine olsen","regine"],"key":"regine","kind":"person","lines":"the girl kierkegaard got engaged to in 1840, when she was eighteen. a year later he broke it off in a way meant to make her hate him, so she'd be free. she begged him not to.\nshe married johan frederik schlegel in 1847, lived a long life, and died in 1904. kierkegaard left her everything in his will. she turned it down. she's in almost every book he wrote, under some other name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regine_Olsen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"regine olsen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pseudonyms","pseudonym","pseudonymous"],"key":"pseudonym","kind":"word","lines":"made-up author names. kierkegaard published most of his books under them, johannes de silentio, victor eremita, johannes climacus, and they disagree with each other.\nit wasn't to hide. everyone in copenhagen knew it was him. it was to make the reader think for themselves instead of just taking his word. picture a writer running five different accounts that argue with each other on purpose.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Søren_Kierkegaard","why":"his life and the pen names"}],"name":"pseudonyms","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["either/or"],"key":"eitheror","kind":"book","lines":"kierkegaard's first big book, from 1843, two huge volumes by made-up authors. the first half is a young man living for pleasure and beauty, ending with the diary of a seducer. the second half is letters from a married judge arguing for a life of commitment and choice.\nhe doesn't tell you who wins. you have to pick. after the corsair cartoons, kids shouted either/or at him in the street.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Either/Or_(Kierkegaard_book)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"either/or","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["fear and trembling"],"key":"feartrembling","kind":"book","lines":"kierkegaard's 1843 book about abraham, who god tells to take his son isaac up a mountain and kill him as a sacrifice, and who goes. written under the name johannes de silentio, john of silence.\nkierkegaard says he can't understand abraham and can't stop looking at him. faith, for him, isn't comfortable belief. it's something that scares the hell out of you. the title comes from a line of paul's about working out your salvation with fear and trembling.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fear_and_Trembling","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"fear and trembling","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["abraham","abraham and isaac","isaac"],"key":"abraham","kind":"person","lines":"the first father of the jewish people in the book of genesis, and claimed by christians and muslims too. god tells him to leave home for a land he'll be shown, and he goes, and god promises him descendants like the stars.\nthe story everyone argues about is in genesis 22. god tells him to take his son isaac up a mountain and sacrifice him, and abraham walks three days, builds the altar, and raises the knife before an angel stops him. when god calls, he answers hineni, here i am. kierkegaard wrote fear and trembling about it, and leonard cohen sang the word on his last album.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binding_of_Isaac","why":"the mountain and the knife"}],"name":"abraham","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the concept of anxiety","concept of anxiety","concept of dread"],"key":"conceptanxiety","kind":"book","lines":"kierkegaard's 1844 book about anxiety, or dread. he says it's the dizziness of freedom, the vertigo you feel looking down at all the things you could do. it's not a disorder in his book. it's what it feels like to be a person who has to choose.\na lot of modern psychology and the whole existentialist movement grew out of it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Concept_of_Anxiety","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the concept of anxiety","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the sickness unto death","sickness unto death"],"key":"sicknessuntodeath","kind":"book","lines":"kierkegaard's 1849 book about despair, which he says is the sickness unto death. not just feeling bad, but a wrong relation to yourself, either desperately not wanting to be yourself or desperately wanting to be yourself on your own terms. most people, he says, are in despair and don't know it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sickness_Unto_Death","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sickness unto death","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leap of faith","the leap of faith"],"key":"leapoffaith","kind":"word","lines":"the idea, from kierkegaard, that you can't argue or prove your way to faith. at some point you jump, without a net. he mostly just called it the leap.\nthe phrase got so famous that people now use it for quitting a job or moving across the country. his version is about staking your whole life on something you can't be sure of.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leap_of_faith","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the leap of faith","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the corsair","corsair"],"key":"corsair","kind":"book","lines":"a satirical weekly paper in copenhagen, a gossip and cartoon rag that mocked everybody. at the end of 1845 kierkegaard dared it to go after him, and it did, for months, with cartoons of his skinny legs and uneven trousers.\nit made him a laughingstock in a city where he walked the streets every day. picture being the target of a viral meme in a town of a hundred thousand people who all know your face.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corsaren","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the corsair","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["danish state church","state church","church of denmark"],"key":"statechurch","kind":"group","lines":"the lutheran church of denmark, run and paid for by the government. every dane was a member by birth and pastors were state officials.\nin his last year kierkegaard attacked it in pamphlets he paid for himself, saying the christianity of the new testament didn't exist in denmark. it still is the state church today.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Denmark","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the danish state church","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bishop mynster","mynster"],"key":"mynster","kind":"person","lines":"jakob mynster, the head of the danish church for over twenty years, a polished, respected preacher. he'd been kierkegaard's father's pastor and his own.\nwhen mynster died in 1854 and was praised at the funeral as a witness to the truth, kierkegaard exploded, and started his public attack on the whole church.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Peter_Mynster","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bishop mynster","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["johan frederik schlegel","schlegel"],"key":"schlegel","kind":"person","lines":"a danish civil servant who married regine olsen in 1847. when kierkegaard wrote asking to be allowed to write to her, he sent the letter back unopened. in 1855 he was made governor of the danish west indies, and they sailed away.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johan_Frederik_Schlegel","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"johan frederik schlegel","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stages on life's way","guilty? not guilty?"],"key":"stagesonlifesway","kind":"book","lines":"kierkegaard's 1845 book laying out three ways to live, the aesthetic, for pleasure, the ethical, for duty, and the religious, for god. one long section, guilty? not guilty?, is a diary of a man going over and over a broken engagement. anyone in copenhagen could tell whose.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stages_on_Life%27s_Way","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"stages on life's way","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["danish west indies"],"key":"danishwestindies","kind":"place","lines":"denmark's colony in the caribbean, the islands of st thomas, st john, and st croix, run on slave labor until 1848. the us bought them in 1917 and they're now the u.s. virgin islands.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danish_West_Indies","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the danish west indies","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["communion","holy communion","eucharist"],"key":"communion","kind":"word","lines":"the christian ritual of bread and wine, remembering jesus's last supper, where he said this is my body, this is my blood. catholics call it the eucharist or mass, and it's the center of every service.\nkierkegaard, dying, refused it from a priest because priests were officials of the crown. he'd take it from an ordinary person or not at all. he didn't get it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eucharist","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"communion","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["upbuilding discourse","upbuilding discourses","purity of heart is to will one thing"],"key":"upbuildingdiscourse","kind":"book","lines":"short religious talks kierkegaard published under his own name, alongside the pseudonymous books, written like sermons for a single reader. purity of heart is to will one thing, from 1847, is the best known. these are where he's warm.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upbuilding_Discourses_in_Various_Spirits","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the upbuilding discourses","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["works of love"],"key":"worksoflove","kind":"book","lines":"kierkegaard's 1847 book about love as a duty, love your neighbor, and what that actually asks. it's the gentle kierkegaard, the one who doesn't show up in the famous quotes.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Works_of_Love","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"works of love","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nation of islam","the nation of islam"],"key":"nationofislam","kind":"group","lines":"a black american religious movement started in detroit in 1930. it used the name of islam but taught its own things, including that white people were a race of devils created by an evil scientist, and that black people should live separately and build their own businesses, schools, and world.\nit was strict, no drinking, no drugs, no pork, suits and bow ties, and it pulled a lot of men out of prison and off the street. malcolm x was its most famous preacher for twelve years. most muslims around the world don't consider its teachings islam. it's still around, led since the late seventies by louis farrakhan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nation_of_Islam","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the nation of islam","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["elijah muhammad"],"key":"elijahmuhammad","kind":"person","lines":"the leader of the nation of islam from 1934 until he died in 1975. born elijah poole in georgia, the son of a sharecropper, he said the nation's founder had been god in person and that he himself was god's messenger.\nmalcolm x wrote to him from prison and worshipped him for years. in 1963 malcolm learned he'd fathered children with several young women who worked for him as secretaries, and the break between them started there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elijah_Muhammad","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"elijah muhammad","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hajj","the hajj"],"key":"hajj","kind":"event","lines":"the pilgrimage to mecca, in saudi arabia, that every muslim who can afford it and is healthy enough is supposed to make once in their life. millions go every year, all dressed alike in plain white cloth, rich and poor, every color, doing the same rituals side by side.\nthat sameness is what broke malcolm x open in april 1964. he wrote home that he'd eaten from the same plate as blue-eyed, blond muslims, and it had made him rethink everything he'd preached about race. someone who's made it gets the title hajj, which is where el-hajj malik el-shabazz, his new name, comes from.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hajj","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the hajj","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["audubon ballroom","the audubon ballroom"],"key":"audubon","kind":"place","lines":"a dance hall and meeting hall in washington heights, upper manhattan. malcolm x was shot to death on its stage on february 21, 1965, while speaking, with his pregnant wife and their daughters in the audience.\npart of the building is now a memorial and education center named for him and his wife betty shabazz.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audubon_Ballroom","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the audubon ballroom","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["alex haley","haley"],"key":"alexhaley","kind":"person","lines":"an american writer who spent twenty years in the coast guard before turning to journalism. he interviewed malcolm x for playboy and then spent two years taking down his life story, which became the autobiography of malcolm x in 1965.\nlater he wrote roots, the book and then the tv series about tracing his own family back to an african man sold into slavery, which about half of america watched in 1977.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alex_Haley","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"alex haley","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the autobiography of malcolm x","autobiography of malcolm x"],"key":"autobiographymalcolm","kind":"book","lines":"malcolm x's life story, told to alex haley over two years and published in 1965, months after he was killed. the hustling, prison, the dictionary, the nation of islam, the break, mecca, and his sense that he wouldn't live to read it.\nit's one of the most important american books of the century, and one of the great conversion stories, twice over. read it next to augustine and you'll see how much they have in common.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Autobiography_of_Malcolm_X","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the autobiography of malcolm x","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["marcus garvey","garvey"],"key":"marcusgarvey","kind":"person","lines":"a jamaican-born leader who built the biggest black mass movement in american history in the early 1920s, telling black people to be proud of africa and to build their own businesses, even their own shipping line, and one day go back.\nmalcolm x's father organized for him, and the family paid for it. the us government went after garvey and deported him in 1927.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Garvey","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"marcus garvey","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["white supremacists","white supremacist","white supremacy"],"key":"whitesupremacist","kind":"group","lines":"people who believe white people are superior and should rule over everyone else, and in malcolm x's childhood in the midwest, groups like the ku klux klan and the black legion who backed it up with arson and murder.\nhis family's house in lansing, michigan, was burned in 1929. when his father died under a streetcar in 1931, the family always believed the black legion killed him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_supremacy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"white supremacists","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["harlem"],"key":"harlem","kind":"place","lines":"a neighborhood in upper manhattan that became the capital of black america in the twentieth century, music, writing, politics, churches, hustlers. malcolm x ran numbers and sold drugs there as a teenager called detroit red, and later preached there at the nation's temple number seven.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"harlem","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["running numbers","ran numbers","numbers game","numbers racket"],"key":"numbersgame","kind":"word","lines":"an illegal street lottery. people bet small amounts on a three digit number, usually taken from something published every day, and runners carried the bets and the payouts around the neighborhood.\nit was a huge business in harlem, sort of the state lottery before states ran lotteries. malcolm x worked it as a teenager.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numbers_game","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"running numbers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["charlestown state prison","charlestown"],"key":"charlestown","kind":"place","lines":"an old massachusetts state prison in boston, built in 1805, where malcolm x started his burglary sentence in 1946. the cells had no plumbing, just a bucket. he was so angry and profane there the other inmates called him satan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlestown_State_Prison","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"charlestown state prison","status":"approved","threads":["prison"]},{"aliases":["uncle tom"],"key":"unclestom","kind":"word","lines":"an insult for a black person seen as bowing and scraping to white people. it comes from the loyal, long-suffering slave in uncle tom's cabin, the 1852 novel that fired up the fight against slavery.\nmalcolm x called martin luther king one. he took a lot of what he said in those years back.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uncle_Tom","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"uncle tom","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["chickens had come home to roost","chickens coming home to roost"],"key":"chickens","kind":"event","lines":"an old saying that the harm you do comes back on you. a week after president kennedy was killed in november 1963, malcolm x used it about the assassination, meaning america's violence had come home.\nthe country was grieving and it went over terribly. elijah muhammad used it to silence him for ninety days, and malcolm never really came back to the nation after.","links":[],"name":"the chickens coming home to roost","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["organization of afro-american unity"],"key":"ooau","kind":"group","lines":"a political group malcolm x started in june 1964, after leaving the nation of islam, modeled on the organization of african unity that african countries had formed the year before. it was open to all black americans, not just muslims, and meant to take america to the united nations over human rights.\nhe'd had it about eight months when he was killed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organization_of_Afro-American_Unity","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the organization of afro-american unity","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["civil rights act","the civil rights act"],"key":"civilrightsact","kind":"event","lines":"the 1964 law that outlawed segregation in public places like restaurants, hotels, and buses, and banned job discrimination by race, color, religion, sex, or national origin. southern senators filibustered it for two months. the only time malcolm x and martin luther king met was watching that fight in the capitol.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_Rights_Act_of_1964","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the civil rights act","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["manning marable","malcolm x: a life of reinvention"],"key":"marable","kind":"person","lines":"an american historian at columbia who spent about twenty years on malcolm x: a life of reinvention, the big 2011 biography that corrects a lot of the autobiography. he died just days before it came out. it won the pulitzer prize.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manning_Marable","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"manning marable","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sigmund freud","freud"],"key":"freud","kind":"person","lines":"the viennese doctor who invented psychoanalysis around 1900, the talking cure, lying on a couch saying whatever comes into your head. he said most of what drives us is unconscious, especially sex and old childhood stuff.\na lot of how people talk about themselves today, repression, denial, the ego, a freudian slip, comes from him, even from people who've never read him. viktor frankl wrote to him as a teenager and freud wrote back. frankl ended up saying freud had it wrong about what we most need.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigmund_Freud","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sigmund freud","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["psychoanalysis","psychoanalyst"],"key":"psychoanalysis","kind":"word","lines":"freud's method of treatment, and his theory of the mind. the patient talks, often lying down, for years, several times a week, and the analyst listens for what's buried underneath, dreams, slips, old family stuff.\nit's the grandfather of most talk therapy.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychoanalysis","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"psychoanalysis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["alfred adler","adler"],"key":"adler","kind":"person","lines":"a viennese doctor who was one of freud's first followers and broke away from him in 1911. he said what drives people is the need to feel capable and to overcome feeling small. he's where the phrase inferiority complex comes from.\nfrankl was his student for a while before going his own way.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Adler","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"alfred adler","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vienna","viennese"],"key":"vienna","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of austria, and around 1900 one of the great cities of the world, the home of freud, mahler, and a huge jewish middle class. in 1938 hitler's germany took over austria, cheered by crowds in the streets, and vienna's jews were robbed, driven out, and deported.\nfrankl went back after the war and lived there the rest of his life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"vienna","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["theresienstadt","terezín","terezin"],"key":"theresienstadt","kind":"place","lines":"an old fortress town in what's now the czech republic that the nazis turned into a ghetto and camp for jews, especially old people and well-known ones. they used it as a fake model settlement to fool the red cross.\nabout thirty three thousand people died there from hunger and disease, and most of the rest were sent on to auschwitz. frankl's father died there in 1943.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theresienstadt_Ghetto","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"theresienstadt","status":"approved","threads":["war","prison"]},{"aliases":["auschwitz","auschwitz-birkenau"],"key":"auschwitz","kind":"place","lines":"the biggest of the nazi death camps, in occupied poland. about a million people, nearly all of them jews, were murdered there, most of them in gas chambers within hours of getting off the train.\nfrankl was there only a few days in october 1944 before being sent on to a work camp. his mother was killed there. heschel said he'd rather go to auschwitz than give up his faith. the name has become shorthand for the worst thing people have done to each other.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auschwitz_concentration_camp","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"auschwitz","status":"approved","threads":["war","dying"]},{"aliases":["dachau","kaufering","türkheim","turkheim"],"key":"dachau","kind":"place","lines":"the first nazi concentration camp, opened near munich in 1933, the model for all the others. by the end of the war it had dozens of satellite work camps around bavaria.\nfrankl spent his last months in two of them, kaufering and türkheim, digging and then working as a doctor, sick with typhus. the americans freed türkheim in april 1945.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dachau_concentration_camp","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dachau","status":"approved","threads":["war","dying"]},{"aliases":["bergen-belsen","belsen"],"key":"bergenbelsen","kind":"place","lines":"a nazi camp in northern germany where tens of thousands died of starvation and typhus at the end of the war. anne frank died there. so did frankl's first wife, tilly, soon after the british freed it in april 1945.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bergen-Belsen_concentration_camp","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bergen-belsen","status":"approved","threads":["war","dying"]},{"aliases":["holocaust","the holocaust","holocaust testimony"],"key":"holocaust","kind":"event","lines":"the murder of about six million jews by nazi germany and its helpers from 1941 to 1945, along with roma, disabled people, and others. it was done on purpose, with trains, paperwork, and gas chambers. jews also call it the shoah, the catastrophe.\non these pages it's what frankl survived and his family didn't, and what took heschel's mother and sisters.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Holocaust","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the holocaust","status":"approved","threads":["war","dying"]},{"aliases":["ghetto","ghettos"],"key":"ghetto","kind":"place","lines":"originally the walled jewish quarter of venice, where jews were locked in at night. the nazis used the word for the sealed-off areas where they crammed jews before killing them, like warsaw's and theresienstadt.\nin america it came to mean any poor, segregated neighborhood.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazi_ghettos","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ghetto","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["typhus"],"key":"typhus","kind":"word","lines":"a disease spread by lice, with high fever, rash, and delirium. it thrives wherever people are packed together filthy and starving, and it killed huge numbers in the camps. frankl had it in the last camp and kept himself awake at night rebuilding his lost book so he wouldn't slip away.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epidemic_typhus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"typhus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["logotherapy"],"key":"logotherapy","kind":"word","lines":"the kind of therapy frankl built, from the greek word logos, meaning meaning. freud said people are driven by pleasure, adler said power. frankl said the deepest drive is the need for a reason, and a therapist's job is to help you find yours.\nit was never as big a school as freud's, but its idea is everywhere now. every talk about finding your why is in debt to it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logotherapy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"logotherapy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["man's search for meaning"],"key":"manssearch","kind":"book","lines":"frankl's short book, dictated in nine days in 1946. the first half is what happened in the camps and what he saw there. the second half lays out logotherapy.\nit has sold well over ten million copies and gets named in surveys as one of the most influential books in america. it's short enough to read in one sitting, and most people do.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27s_Search_for_Meaning","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"man's search for meaning","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nietzsche","friedrich nietzsche"],"key":"nietzsche","kind":"person","lines":"friedrich nietzsche, a german philosopher of the late 1800s, the one who said god is dead, meaning people had stopped really believing and hadn't noticed yet what that would cost.\nhe wrote he who has a why to live can bear almost any how. frankl quoted it in man's search for meaning, and that's how most people know it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Nietzsche","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nietzsche","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lawrence langer"],"key":"langer","kind":"person","lines":"an american scholar who spent decades studying holocaust survivors' testimony. he argued that survival in the camps came down mostly to luck, not attitude, and that books like frankl's can make it sound otherwise.","links":[],"name":"lawrence langer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["montreal","westmount"],"key":"montreal","kind":"place","lines":"the biggest city in quebec, french-speaking canada, on an island in the st lawrence river. westmount is the rich english-speaking neighborhood on the side of the mountain, where leonard cohen grew up in a prominent jewish family.\nhe kept a house in montreal all his life and he's buried there, in the family plot. his last album has the choir of the synagogue he grew up in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montreal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"montreal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hydra"],"key":"hydra","kind":"place","lines":"a small, rocky greek island a couple of hours by boat from athens, with no cars, just donkeys and steps. in the fifties and sixties it drew a little colony of foreign writers and painters living cheap.\nleonard cohen bought a house there in 1960 for about fifteen hundred dollars, lived there with marianne ihlen, and wrote his early books. he kept the house for the rest of his life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydra_(island)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hydra","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["marianne ihlen","so long, marianne","so long marianne"],"key":"marianne","kind":"person","lines":"a norwegian woman leonard cohen met on hydra in 1960 and lived with, on and off, for most of the decade, with her young son. so long, marianne is about her.\nwhen she was dying of leukemia in 2016 he wrote her a letter saying he was right behind her, close enough to take her hand. she died that summer. he died that november.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marianne_Ihlen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"marianne ihlen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["judy collins"],"key":"judycollins","kind":"person","lines":"an american folk singer, famous in the sixties. in 1966 she recorded leonard cohen's suzanne, and that's how most people first heard of him. she more or less pushed him onto a stage for the first time the next year.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judy_Collins","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"judy collins","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["suzanne"],"key":"suzanne","kind":"book","lines":"a leonard cohen song, first a poem, about a woman in montreal who feeds you tea and oranges by the river, and about jesus as a sailor who walked upon the water. judy collins recorded it in 1966 and made his name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suzanne_(Leonard_Cohen_song)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"suzanne","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["joshu sasaki","sasaki","sasaki roshi"],"key":"sasaki","kind":"person","lines":"a japanese teacher of rinzai zen who came to los angeles in 1962 and founded the mount baldy zen center. leonard cohen studied with him for decades, cooked for him, drove him around, and was ordained a monk under him in 1996.\nstarting in 2012, many former students came forward saying he'd sexually abused and harassed women for decades, and that the people around him knew. he died in 2014 at a hundred and seven. he's not in this forest.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshu_Sasaki","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joshu sasaki","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mount baldy zen center","mount baldy","zen center on mount baldy"],"key":"mountbaldy","kind":"place","lines":"a bare-bones zen monastery high in the san gabriel mountains above los angeles, at around six thousand feet, in an old boy scout camp. cold in winter, early mornings, long silent sittings.\nleonard cohen lived there in a small cabin from the mid nineties to 1999, cooking for his teacher and sitting in the cold. he said he was bad at it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Baldy_Zen_Center","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mount baldy zen center","status":"approved","threads":["silence"]},{"aliases":["rinzai zen","rinzai"],"key":"rinzai","kind":"group","lines":"one of the two main schools of zen, the strict one, known for long, hard retreats and for koans, riddles with no logical answer that a teacher gives you to wrestle with until something breaks. the other big school, soto, is mostly just sitting.\njoshu sasaki, leonard cohen's teacher, was a rinzai teacher.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rinzai_school","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rinzai zen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["koan","koans"],"key":"koan","kind":"word","lines":"a riddle a zen teacher gives a student that can't be solved by thinking. what is the sound of one hand clapping is the famous one. you sit with it for weeks or years, and the point is for your thinking mind to give up.","links":[],"name":"koan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jikan"],"key":"jikan","kind":"word","lines":"the buddhist name leonard cohen was given when he was ordained a monk on mount baldy in 1996. it's usually translated the silent one, or ordinary silence. a funny name for a man who made his living with words.","links":[],"name":"jikan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ramesh balsekar","balsekar"],"key":"balsekar","kind":"person","lines":"a retired indian banker who became a teacher of advaita, the indian teaching that there's no separate self doing anything. leonard cohen went to mumbai in 1999 after coming down from mount baldy and spent months going to his talks every day.\nbalsekar's teacher was nisargadatta maharaj, the bidi seller who wrote i am that.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramesh_Balsekar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ramesh balsekar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hallelujah"],"key":"hallelujah","kind":"book","lines":"the leonard cohen song, from 1984. he said he wrote dozens of verses for it over years. his label wouldn't release the album in the us. then john cale recorded it, then jeff buckley, then it turned up in shrek, and now it's sung at weddings, funerals, and on every talent show.\nthe word is hebrew for praise god. the song is about love and sex and failure and praising anyway, a cold and broken hallelujah.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hallelujah_(Leonard_Cohen_song)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hallelujah","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jeff buckley"],"key":"buckley","kind":"person","lines":"an american singer with an unreal voice who recorded hallelujah in 1994, slow and aching, on his only finished album, grace. it's the version most people know. he drowned in a river in memphis in 1997, at thirty.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeff_Buckley","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jeff buckley","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john cale","velvet underground"],"key":"johncale","kind":"person","lines":"a welsh musician who was a founding member of the velvet underground, the new york band lou reed led in the sixties. in 1991 he recorded hallelujah with just a piano, and that version is what started the song's second life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Cale","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john cale","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["you want it darker"],"key":"youwantitdarker","kind":"book","lines":"leonard cohen's last album, released in october 2016, seventeen days before he died. he recorded it mostly sitting in a medical chair in his living room, sick. the title song has hineni, here i am, sung with the choir of his old montreal synagogue.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You_Want_It_Darker","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"you want it darker","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["hineni"],"key":"hineni","kind":"word","lines":"hebrew for here i am. it's what abraham says when god calls him, just before being told to sacrifice his son, and what moses says at the burning bush. it doesn't mean here's my location. it means i'm ready, whatever it is.\nleonard cohen sang it at the end of his life on you want it darker.","links":[],"name":"hineni","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["book of longing"],"key":"bookoflonging","kind":"book","lines":"leonard cohen's 2006 collection of poems and drawings, a lot of it from his years on mount baldy. funny, sad, dirty, and prayerful, sometimes on the same page.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Longing","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"book of longing","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["let us compare mythologies"],"key":"letuscompare","kind":"book","lines":"leonard cohen's first book of poems, published in montreal in 1956 when he was twenty one, while he was still at mcgill university.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Let_Us_Compare_Mythologies","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"let us compare mythologies","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the favourite game","favourite game"],"key":"favouritegame","kind":"book","lines":"leonard cohen's first novel, from 1963, about a young jewish poet growing up in montreal and chasing women. it's pretty much him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Favourite_Game","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the favourite game","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the song anthem","there is a crack in everything"],"key":"anthem","kind":"book","lines":"a leonard cohen song from 1992. forget your perfect offering, there is a crack in everything, that's how the light gets in. it's probably the most quoted line he ever wrote.","links":[],"name":"anthem","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sylvie simmons","i'm your man"],"key":"simmons","kind":"person","lines":"a music journalist who wrote i'm your man, the big 2012 biography of leonard cohen.","links":[],"name":"sylvie simmons","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["eloi, eloi, lema sabachthani","lema sabachthani","why have you forsaken me"],"key":"eloi","kind":"word","lines":"aramaic for my god, my god, why have you forsaken me. mark's gospel says jesus cried it out from the cross, just before he died. it's the first line of psalm 22.\nthe people standing there misheard eloi as elijah. it's the rawest moment in the gospels, the son of god saying god has left him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayings_of_Jesus_on_the_cross","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eloi, eloi, lema sabachthani","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["2 peter"],"key":"secondpeter","kind":"book","lines":"a short letter in the new testament written in peter's name. most scholars think someone else wrote it, decades after he died. it calls paul our dear brother and admits some of his letters are hard to understand.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Epistle_of_Peter","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"2 peter","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["acts 15","council of jerusalem"],"key":"councilofjerusalem","kind":"event","lines":"a meeting of the first christian leaders in jerusalem, around the year 50, told in acts 15. the question was whether gentile followers of jesus had to become jews first. peter stood up and said god made no distinction between us and them. james ruled they didn't have to.\nit's the moment christianity stopped being a jewish sect and started becoming its own religion.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_Jerusalem","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the council of jerusalem","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["chögyam trungpa","chogyam trungpa","trungpa","trungpa rinpoche"],"key":"trungpa","kind":"person","lines":"a tibetan lama who fled when china took over tibet in 1959, studied at oxford, gave up his monk's robes, married a young englishwoman, and landed in america in 1970. he was brilliant at explaining buddhism to westerners in plain english, and he built a big network of meditation centers out of boulder, colorado.\nhe also drank heavily, slept with many of his students, and died at forty seven in 1987. that's why he has no page in this forest. he's here as a door because he's the teacher pema chödrön stayed with, and you can't understand her without knowing who he was.\nthink of the most gifted, most charismatic, most out of control person you've ever worked for. the students who stayed have spent decades arguing about what to do with that.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%B6gyam_Trungpa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"chögyam trungpa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shambhala","shambhala international","shambhala centers"],"key":"shambhala","kind":"group","lines":"the name of the network of meditation centers chögyam trungpa built in the west, and the name of his teaching about living bravely and decently in ordinary life. the word comes from an old tibetan legend about a hidden kingdom where everyone lives wisely.\nafter trungpa died it passed to his son, who went by the sakyong mipham. in 2018 reports came out that the son had sexually abused students, and the organization split and shrank. pema chödrön resigned as a senior teacher in 2020.\nit's also the name of a big buddhist publishing house, shambhala publications, which is a separate company.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shambhala_International","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"shambhala","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sakyong mipham","the sakyong","sakyong"],"key":"sakyong","kind":"person","lines":"chögyam trungpa's eldest son, who took over the shambhala meditation network after his father died. sakyong is a title that means earth protector.\nin 2018 former students published accounts of sexual abuse and heavy drinking by him. he stepped back from teaching. when it looked like he'd come back, pema chödrön quit as a senior teacher.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakyong_Mipham","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sakyong mipham","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ösel tendzin","osel tendzin"],"key":"oseltendzin","kind":"person","lines":"an american student of chögyam trungpa, born thomas rich in new jersey, whom trungpa named as his successor. he ran the shambhala network after trungpa died in 1987.\nhe knew he was hiv positive and kept having unprotected sex with students, and at least one young man was infected. it came out in 1988 and he died of aids in 1990. it's the ugliest part of the story pema chödrön lived inside.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%96sel_Tendzin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ösel tendzin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["w.s. merwin","w. s. merwin","merwin"],"key":"merwin","kind":"person","lines":"one of the best american poets of the last century. he won the pulitzer prize twice and was named poet laureate of the united states, which is the country's official poet, in 2010.\nin 1975 he and his partner, dana naone, went to a long retreat run by chögyam trungpa. when they refused to join a party and locked their door, trungpa had them dragged out and stripped in front of everyone. merwin wrote about it later. he lived his later decades in hawaii, planting thousands of palm trees on worn out farmland, and died in 2019.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._S._Merwin","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/w-s-merwin","why":"his poems and life"}],"name":"w.s. merwin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["root teacher","root guru"],"key":"rootteacher","kind":"word","lines":"in tibetan buddhism, the one teacher who matters most in your life. you might study with a dozen teachers, but the root teacher is the one who opened the door for you, and you treat them with a loyalty that's hard for westerners to picture.\nthat loyalty is what makes it so hard when a root teacher behaves badly. walking away can feel like walking away from the thing they showed you.","links":[],"name":"root teacher","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tonglen"],"key":"tonglen","kind":"word","lines":"a tibetan buddhist practice. the word means sending and taking. you breathe in the pain you or someone else is in, the fear or grief or rage, and breathe out relief, space, a bit of kindness, toward everybody who feels the same.\nit runs backwards from most meditation apps, which have you breathe in calm and breathe out stress. the idea is to stop running from the bad feeling and use it as the thing that connects you to everybody else who's got it.\npema chödrön teaches it more than anything else, and says start with your own pain, since it's right there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonglen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tonglen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shenpa"],"key":"shenpa","kind":"word","lines":"a tibetan word pema chödrön made famous. she translates it as the hook. it's the itch before you scratch, the little tightening in your gut right before you snap at someone, reach for the phone, or send the text you shouldn't.\nher teaching is that you can learn to catch that half second. you feel the hook, you don't bite, and it passes. her short book taking the leap is all about it.","links":[],"name":"shenpa","status":"approved","threads":["anger"]},{"aliases":["gampo abbey"],"key":"gampoabbey","kind":"place","lines":"a tibetan buddhist monastery on a cliff over the gulf of st lawrence, on cape breton island at the top of nova scotia in eastern canada. chögyam trungpa founded it in 1983 as a place for westerners to train as monks and nuns.\npema chödrön became its director in the mid eighties. she's said it was the first place she couldn't run from herself. when things fall apart grew out of talks she gave there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gampo_Abbey","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the abbey","url":"https://gampoabbey.org/","why":"the monastery's own site"}],"name":"gampo abbey","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cape breton","cape breton island"],"key":"capebreton","kind":"place","lines":"a big, rugged island at the northeast end of nova scotia, on canada's atlantic coast. fishing villages, cliffs, long winters, fiddle music, a lot of scottish and french family names.\nit's where gampo abbey sits, about as far from everything as you can get and still be in north america.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Breton_Island","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cape breton","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ordination","ordained","full ordination","fully ordained"],"key":"ordination","kind":"word","lines":"the ceremony where someone formally becomes a priest, minister, monk or nun. a bishop lays hands on a new catholic priest. a buddhist monk shaves his head and takes vows in front of other monks. it's the line between being a believer and being one of the people who serve full time.\nin buddhism there are steps. novice vows first, a few basic rules and a shaved head, then full ordination, the complete set of hundreds of vows a fully ordained monk or nun takes. tibetan buddhism lost the line of full ordination for women centuries ago, so a tibetan buddhist woman who wants it has to go to the chinese tradition, which kept it. that's what pema chödrön did in hong kong in 1981.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordination","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ordination","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["when things fall apart"],"key":"thingsfallapart","kind":"book","lines":"pema chödrön's 1997 book, edited from talks she gave at gampo abbey. short chapters about staying with fear, grief and rage instead of running from them.\nit's the book people hand each other in hospital hallways and after a divorce. it doesn't promise things will get better. it tells you how to sit in it while they don't.","links":[],"name":"when things fall apart","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the places that scare you","places that scare you"],"key":"placesthatscare","kind":"book","lines":"pema chödrön's 2001 book about using hard feelings to soften up instead of harden. it's where she lays out tonglen and the other practices she gives out most, step by step.","links":[],"name":"the places that scare you","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["taking the leap"],"key":"takingtheleap","kind":"book","lines":"a short 2009 book by pema chödrön about shenpa, the hook, the itch before you react. it's about catching that half second and not biting.","links":[],"name":"taking the leap","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["soto zen","soto","sōtō"],"key":"sotozen","kind":"group","lines":"one of the two big schools of japanese zen. the other one, rinzai, is known for koans, the riddles a teacher throws at you. soto is known for just sitting. no riddle to crack, no goal, just sit down, face the wall, and pay attention.\nit came to japan from china in the 1200s with a monk named dogen. shunryu suzuki was a soto priest, and most of the zen in america runs back through soto teachers like him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C5%8Dt%C5%8D","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"soto zen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["zazen"],"key":"zazen","kind":"word","lines":"zen meditation. literally seated zen. you sit on a cushion with your back straight, legs crossed, hands in your lap, eyes half open and looking at the floor or a wall, and you don't do anything on purpose. you just sit and notice.\nit sounds like nothing. try it for twenty minutes. that's the whole lesson.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zazen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"zazen","status":"approved","threads":["silence"]},{"aliases":["sokoji","sokoji temple"],"key":"sokoji","kind":"place","lines":"a japanese american soto zen temple in san francisco's japantown, in an old building that used to be a synagogue. the families there wanted a priest for funerals, weddings and holidays.\nshunryu suzuki came to run it in 1959. a few americans started showing up at dawn to sit with him, and that's how san francisco zen center started, in the same building, with the two congregations never quite getting along.","links":[],"name":"sokoji","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["japantown"],"key":"japantown","kind":"place","lines":"a neighborhood in san francisco where japanese american families lived and ran businesses. during world war two the government forced them all into internment camps, and many came back afterward to a neighborhood that had changed hands.\nsuzuki's temple, sokoji, was there, and so was the first home of san francisco zen center.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japantown,_San_Francisco","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"japantown","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["san francisco zen center"],"key":"sfzc","kind":"group","lines":"the zen community shunryu suzuki's american students formed in 1962. it grew into one of the biggest buddhist organizations in the west, with a city temple, a farm north of the city, and tassajara in the mountains.\na lot of american zen teachers trained there. it had its big crisis in 1983, when suzuki's successor, richard baker, was pushed out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Francisco_Zen_Center","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the center","url":"https://www.sfzc.org/","why":"the zen center's own site"}],"name":"san francisco zen center","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tassajara","tassajara zen mountain center"],"key":"tassajara","kind":"place","lines":"an old hot springs resort at the end of a long dirt road in the mountains inland from big sur, california. san francisco zen center bought it in 1967 and made it the first zen training monastery outside asia.\nin the winter it's closed to guests and the monks train. in the summer it takes paying guests, and the students cook and clean for them. the tassajara bread book, from 1970, came out of its kitchen.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tassajara_Zen_Mountain_Center","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tassajara","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["zen mind, beginner's mind","zen mind beginner's mind"],"key":"zenmind","kind":"book","lines":"shunryu suzuki's only book, from 1970, made from talks his students taped and edited. short, plain chapters about sitting, breathing, and not trying to get anywhere.\nit's still the book most people start zen with. it doesn't sound like a scripture. it sounds like a kind old man talking to you before breakfast.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen_Mind,_Beginner%27s_Mind","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"zen mind, beginner's mind","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["beginner's mind","beginners mind","shoshin"],"key":"beginnersmind","kind":"word","lines":"a zen idea, shoshin in japanese, that suzuki made famous. it means meeting everything as if for the first time, the way a beginner does, curious and open, instead of like an expert who's already sure.\nhis line is that the beginner sees many possibilities and the expert sees few. you've probably seen it on a slide in an office workshop. it's better than that.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoshin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"beginner's mind","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["richard baker","baker roshi"],"key":"richardbaker","kind":"person","lines":"an american student of shunryu suzuki whom suzuki named as his successor in 1971, a few weeks before he died. baker ran san francisco zen center and built it into a big operation, with a bakery, a restaurant, a farm, and a lot of money.\nin 1983 it came out that he was having an affair with a friend's wife, and students were already angry about how he ran the place and lived. he was pushed out. he went on teaching elsewhere, in colorado and germany.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zentatsu_Richard_Baker","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"richard baker","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["crooked cucumber"],"key":"crookedcucumber","kind":"book","lines":"the nickname shunryu suzuki's teacher gave him as a boy, meaning a cucumber too bent to be any use. it became the title of his biography, written by his student david chadwick and published in 1999.\nit's the best way into the man, the small funny priest behind the famous book.","links":[],"name":"crooked cucumber","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["trudy dixon"],"key":"trudydixon","kind":"person","lines":"a young student of shunryu suzuki who did most of the work of editing his talks into zen mind, beginner's mind. she had cancer, and died in 1969 at thirty, before the book came out. her introduction is still in it.","links":[],"name":"trudy dixon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["big sur"],"key":"bigsur","kind":"place","lines":"a wild stretch of the california coast south of monterey, where the mountains drop straight into the pacific. one road along cliffs, redwoods in the canyons, not much else.\nit's where tassajara is, up in the mountains behind the coast. it's also the title of jack kerouac's 1962 novel about cracking up in a borrowed cabin there, the least romantic book about fame ever written.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sur","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"big sur","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["big sur, from 1962","big sur, when you want the truth"],"key":"bigsurbook","kind":"book","lines":"jack kerouac's 1962 novel about going to a cabin at big sur in 1960 to dry out and find some peace, and falling apart instead. drink, paranoia, a breakdown, all written down in detail.\nit's the book about what fame did to him, from the man who wrote on the road. no one comes out of it looking cool.","links":[],"name":"big sur, the novel","status":"approved","threads":["addiction","madness"]},{"aliases":["arunachala","arunachala hill"],"key":"arunachala","kind":"place","lines":"a hill in south india, at the temple town of tiruvannamalai, that hindus have worshiped for centuries as the god shiva himself in the form of a mountain. pilgrims walk barefoot around the base of it, about eight miles.\nramana maharshi arrived at sixteen in 1896 and never left. he said the hill was his teacher.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arunachala","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"arunachala","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tiruvannamalai"],"key":"tiruvannamalai","kind":"place","lines":"a temple town in tamil nadu, in south india, at the foot of the holy hill arunachala. it has one of the biggest temples in india, dedicated to shiva.\nsince the 1930s it's also been full of westerners, because ramana maharshi's ashram is there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiruvannamalai","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tiruvannamalai","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shiva","lord shiva","siva"],"key":"shiva","kind":"person","lines":"one of the great gods of hinduism. he's the destroyer, the one who tears things down so they can start over, and also the great yogi, sitting still on a mountain in the himalayas with ashes on his skin and a snake around his neck.\nhundreds of millions of hindus worship him as the highest god. the hill arunachala is said to be him in the form of a mountain.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shiva","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"shiva","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tamil","tamils","tamil nadu"],"key":"tamil","kind":"word","lines":"the language and people of the far south of india and the north of sri lanka. tamil is one of the oldest living languages in the world, with poetry going back two thousand years, and it isn't related to hindi at all.\ntamil nadu is the indian state where they live. ramana maharshi was a tamil boy from there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_language","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tamil","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["madurai"],"key":"madurai","kind":"place","lines":"a big old temple city in tamil nadu, in the far south of india, built around a huge temple to the goddess meenakshi. ramana maharshi was living at his uncle's house there when he was sixteen and had his experience of death.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madurai","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"madurai","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["patala lingam","the patala lingam"],"key":"patalalingam","kind":"place","lines":"a dark underground shrine room beneath the great temple at tiruvannamalai. the young ramana sat there in his first weeks, so still that insects ate sores into his legs and he didn't notice.","links":[],"name":"the patala lingam","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["seshadri swamigal","seshadri"],"key":"seshadri","kind":"person","lines":"an older holy man at tiruvannamalai, thought of by the locals as a bit mad and a real saint. when the young ramana was sitting in the pit under the temple, he chased off the boys who were throwing stones at him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seshadri_Swamigal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"seshadri swamigal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["self-inquiry","self inquiry","the question who am i","who am i?, a short booklet","who am i?, which is about twenty pages"],"key":"selfinquiry","kind":"word","lines":"ramana maharshi's whole method, in a question. who am i? whenever a thought comes up, you don't argue with it. you ask who it's coming to. the answer is me. then you ask, who's that? and you follow the feeling of i back to where it starts.\nhe said if you keep at it, the question burns up every other thought and then burns itself up too. what's left is the thing you actually are. who am i? is also the title of a short booklet of his answers, about twenty pages, where he lays it all out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-enquiry_(Ramana_Maharshi)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"self-inquiry","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["be as you are"],"key":"beasyouare","kind":"book","lines":"a collection of ramana maharshi's talks, sorted by subject and edited by david godman, an englishman who lived at the ashram for decades. it came out in 1985.\nit's the easiest way in after the short booklet who am i?","links":[],"name":"be as you are","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["talks with sri ramana maharshi","talks with ramana maharshi"],"key":"talksramana","kind":"book","lines":"a big book of notes taken by visitors and his attendant in ramana maharshi's hall between 1935 and 1939. people ask him things, he answers, sometimes he says nothing.","links":[],"name":"talks with sri ramana maharshi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["henri cartier-bresson","cartier-bresson"],"key":"cartierbresson","kind":"person","lines":"a french photographer, one of the most famous of the twentieth century, known for catching the one perfect split second in a street. he was in india in 1950 and photographed ramana maharshi in his last days, and the story goes he saw the shooting star the night ramana died.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_Cartier-Bresson","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"henri cartier-bresson","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["somerset maugham","w. somerset maugham","maugham"],"key":"maugham","kind":"person","lines":"an english novelist and playwright who was one of the most popular writers in the world in the twenties and thirties. he visited ramana maharshi in 1938.\nhis 1944 novel the razor's edge is about a young american who comes back from the first world war shaken, goes looking for something real, and finds it in india. the holy man in it is at least partly ramana.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._Somerset_Maugham","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"somerset maugham","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the razor's edge","razor's edge"],"key":"razorsedge","kind":"book","lines":"a 1944 novel by somerset maugham about a young american who turns down money and a normal life after the first world war and goes looking for meaning, which he finds in india. it was a bestseller and has been filmed twice.\nthe holy man in it is partly modeled on ramana maharshi. for a lot of readers it was their first picture of an indian sage.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Razor%27s_Edge","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the razor's edge","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["paul brunton","brunton"],"key":"brunton","kind":"person","lines":"an english journalist who toured india in 1930 and 1931 hunting for holy men, a bit skeptical and a bit of a showman. at ramana maharshi's hall he sat down meaning to fire off questions and found he couldn't.\nhe wrote it up in a search in secret india, 1934, and it sold. that book is the reason westerners started turning up at the hill.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Brunton","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"paul brunton","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["a search in secret india","search in secret india"],"key":"secretindia","kind":"book","lines":"paul brunton's 1934 travel book about looking for real holy men in india. most of it is fakirs and fortune tellers. the chapters that mattered are the ones about sitting in ramana maharshi's hall.\nit was a bestseller in england, and it put ramana on the map for westerners.","links":[],"name":"a search in secret india","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["david godman"],"key":"davidgodman","kind":"person","lines":"an english writer who went to ramana maharshi's ashram in 1976 and stayed for decades, collecting and editing everything he could find about ramana and the people around him. be as you are is his.","links":[],"name":"david godman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kirtan","kirtans"],"key":"kirtan","kind":"word","lines":"singing the names of god in a group, usually call and response. one person sings a line and the room sings it back, over and over, to drums and cymbals and a harmonium, getting faster and louder.\nit's closer to a gospel revival than a church choir. people cry, dance, lose track of time. krishna das has been filling halls in america with it for decades, strangers singing sanskrit names they don't understand and walking out lighter.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirtan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kirtan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["paramahansa yogananda","yogananda"],"key":"yogananda","kind":"person","lines":"an indian monk who came to america in 1920 to talk at a religion conference in boston and stayed, teaching yoga and meditation out of a headquarters in los angeles. he was one of the first indian teachers to live in the west for good.\nhis memoir, autobiography of a yogi, from 1946, has sold millions and got a lot of americans started. he died in 1952.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paramahansa_Yogananda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"paramahansa yogananda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["autobiography of a yogi"],"key":"autobiographyyogi","kind":"book","lines":"paramahansa yogananda's 1946 memoir about growing up in india and meeting holy men, some of them working miracles if you take the book at its word. it has sold millions of copies and turned a lot of westerners toward india.\nsteve jobs read it every year, and had copies handed out at his memorial. chapter 45 is about anandamayi ma.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autobiography_of_a_Yogi","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7452","why":"the first edition, free at project gutenberg"}],"name":"autobiography of a yogi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["initiation","diksha"],"key":"initiation","kind":"word","lines":"in india, the ceremony where you formally take someone as your spiritual teacher and they take you as their student. usually the teacher gives you a mantra, a word or phrase to repeat, and from then on you're theirs. the word for it is diksha.\nit's a bit like a baptism and a bit like signing on as an apprentice.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diksha","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"initiation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dhaka","dacca"],"key":"dhaka","kind":"place","lines":"the capital of bangladesh, a huge, crowded city on the rivers of the ganges delta. when anandamayi ma was young it was a provincial city in british bengal. it's where her first followers gathered in the twenties.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhaka","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dhaka","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["east bengal"],"key":"eastbengal","kind":"place","lines":"the eastern half of bengal, the big region around the ganges delta. when india was split in 1947 it went to pakistan, and in 1971 it broke away after a brutal war and became bangladesh.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Bengal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"east bengal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bholanath"],"key":"bholanath","kind":"person","lines":"the name anandamayi ma's followers gave her husband, ramani mohan chakrabarti. they were married when she was about thirteen and never lived as husband and wife. he took initiation from her and followed her for the rest of his life. he died in 1938.","links":[],"name":"bholanath","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dehradun","dehra dun"],"key":"dehradun","kind":"place","lines":"a city in the foothills of the himalayas in north india, now the capital of the state of uttarakhand. anandamayi ma had an ashram there and died nearby in 1982.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dehradun","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dehradun","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kankhal"],"key":"kankhal","kind":"place","lines":"an old holy town right next to haridwar, on the ganges where it comes down out of the himalayas. anandamayi ma is buried there in a shrine.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kankhal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kankhal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["haridwar","hardwar"],"key":"haridwar","kind":"place","lines":"one of the holiest towns in india, where the ganges comes out of the mountains onto the plains. every evening crowds float little lamps on the river. every twelve years it hosts the kumbh mela, a festival where tens of millions of people come to bathe.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haridwar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"haridwar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kamala nehru"],"key":"kamalanehru","kind":"person","lines":"the wife of jawaharlal nehru, india's first prime minister, and a fighter in the independence movement herself. she was devoted to anandamayi ma. she died of tuberculosis in 1936, eleven years before india was free.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamala_Nehru","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kamala nehru","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["indira gandhi","indira"],"key":"indiragandhi","kind":"person","lines":"india's first and only woman prime minister, from 1966 to 1977 and again from 1980 until she was assassinated by her own bodyguards in 1984. she was nehru's daughter, and no relation to mohandas gandhi. she got the name by marrying a man called feroze gandhi.\ntough, feared, and in 1975 she suspended democracy for nearly two years. she also visited anandamayi ma for years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indira_Gandhi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"indira gandhi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kheyala","kheyal"],"key":"kheyala","kind":"word","lines":"a bengali word anandamayi ma used for the sudden impulse that decided where she'd go and what she'd do. not a plan, not a choice, just something that arose. when people asked her plans, she said this body has none, it goes where the kheyala takes it.","links":[],"name":"kheyala","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["beedi","beedis","bidi","bidis"],"key":"beedi","kind":"word","lines":"a thin little cigarette from india, a pinch of tobacco rolled up in a dried leaf and tied with a thread. it's the cheapest smoke in the country, the one laborers and rickshaw drivers smoke.\nnisargadatta maharaj sold them in his shop in bombay, and smoked them to the end.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beedi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"beedi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bombay","mumbai"],"key":"bombay","kind":"place","lines":"india's biggest city, on the west coast, called mumbai since 1995. money, movies, the stock market, skyscrapers next to slums, twenty million people.\nnisargadatta maharaj ran a cigarette shop there and taught in a tiny room upstairs.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mumbai","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bombay","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["khetwadi"],"key":"khetwadi","kind":"place","lines":"a crowded old neighborhood in the middle of bombay, narrow lanes and old apartment houses. nisargadatta maharaj's house, with the tiny loft room where he talked to visitors, was there.","links":[],"name":"khetwadi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["siddharameshwar maharaj","siddharameshwar"],"key":"siddharameshwar","kind":"person","lines":"the teacher nisargadatta maharaj met in 1933. he told him he wasn't the body and wasn't the mind, only the bare sense of existing, and to stay with that. nisargadatta did, for three years. siddharameshwar died in 1936.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddharameshwar_Maharaj","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"siddharameshwar maharaj","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["navnath","navnath sampradaya","the navnath line"],"key":"navnath","kind":"group","lines":"an old chain of teachers in western india, said to go back to nine legendary yogis, the nine naths. each teacher hands the teaching to the next. nisargadatta's teacher was in it, and so was he.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navnath","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the navnath line","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["i am that"],"key":"iamthat","kind":"book","lines":"the big book of nisargadatta maharaj's conversations with visitors, taped and translated from marathi by maurice frydman and published in 1973. no chapters on doctrine, just people asking hard questions and a cranky old man answering.\nit made him famous in the west. people read one conversation a day, anywhere in the book.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Am_That","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"i am that","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["maurice frydman","frydman"],"key":"frydman","kind":"person","lines":"a polish jewish engineer who came to india in the thirties and stayed. he took monk's vows for a while, worked with gandhi's people on village industry, sat with ramana maharshi, and helped draft a constitution for a small indian princely state.\nlate in life he found nisargadatta in his loft in bombay, taped the talks, and turned them into i am that. he died in 1976.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurice_Frydman","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"maurice frydman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["marathi"],"key":"marathi","kind":"word","lines":"the language of maharashtra, the state around bombay on india's west coast. around eighty million people speak it. nisargadatta taught in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marathi_language","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"marathi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kainchi dham","kainchi"],"key":"kainchi","kind":"place","lines":"neem karoli baba's ashram in the himalayan foothills near the hill town of nainital, a cluster of temples by a stream in a narrow valley. ram dass and the other westerners sat with him there.\nevery june a huge crowd comes for the anniversary of the temple. since steve jobs and other tech people talked about it, the traffic jams on the mountain road have made the news.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kainchi_Dham","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kainchi dham","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nainital"],"key":"nainital","kind":"place","lines":"a hill town in the himalayan foothills of north india, built by the british around a lake as a place to escape the summer heat. kainchi is about an hour's drive away.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nainital","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nainital","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vrindavan","brindavan","vrindaban"],"key":"vrindavan","kind":"place","lines":"a town in north india where the god krishna is said to have grown up as a cowherd boy, flirting with the milkmaids and playing his flute. it's packed with thousands of temples and pilgrims singing his name.\nmirabai walked there after leaving the palace. neem karoli baba had an ashram there and died there in 1973. it's also known for its widows, who used to be sent there by their families to live out their lives in prayer.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vrindavan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"vrindavan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["neeb karori"],"key":"neebkarori","kind":"place","lines":"a small village near farrukhabad in uttar pradesh, north india, where the story says a train wouldn't start until the old man with no ticket was let back on. he stayed there a while, and the village name became his.","links":[],"name":"neeb karori","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ramayana","the ramayana"],"key":"ramayana","kind":"book","lines":"one of india's two great old epics, the story of prince rama, who's sent into the forest for fourteen years, loses his wife sita to a demon king, and gets her back with the help of an army of monkeys and bears led by hanuman.\nevery indian kid knows it, the way american kids know star wars. it's acted out every autumn in towns all over the country, and in 1987 a tv version stopped the whole nation on sunday mornings.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramayana","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ramayana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["steve jobs"],"key":"stevejobs","kind":"person","lines":"the man who started apple with steve wozniak in a garage in 1976 and later made the mac, the ipod and the iphone. he died in 2011.\nin 1974, at nineteen, he went to india to find neem karoli baba, and got there after the old man had died. he stayed months anyway, came home with a shaved head, and studied zen for years after. he read autobiography of a yogi every year and had copies handed out at his memorial.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Jobs","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"steve jobs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["larry brilliant","doctor america"],"key":"larrybrilliant","kind":"person","lines":"an american doctor who went to india in the early seventies, ended up at neem karoli baba's feet, and was told again and again he'd help rid the world of smallpox. he joined the world health organization's smallpox campaign in india and did.\nlater he co-founded seva, a charity that has paid for millions of eye operations, and ran google's charity arm. his memoir is sometimes brilliant.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larry_Brilliant","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"larry brilliant","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["smallpox"],"key":"smallpox","kind":"word","lines":"a virus that killed people for thousands of years, covering them in pus-filled blisters and killing about three in ten of the people it infected. survivors were often scarred or blind. it killed hundreds of millions of people in the twentieth century alone.\na world campaign in the sixties and seventies hunted down every last case, village by village, and vaccinated everyone around it. the last natural case of the deadly kind was a little girl in bangladesh in 1975. in 1980 the world was declared free of it. it's the only human disease ever wiped out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smallpox","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"smallpox","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["world health organization","the world health organization"],"key":"who","kind":"group","lines":"the united nations' health agency, set up in 1948 and based in geneva. it coordinates the world's fights against disease, from smallpox to covid.\nits smallpox campaign, which larry brilliant worked on in india, is the biggest thing it ever pulled off.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Health_Organization","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the world health organization","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["seva","seva foundation"],"key":"seva","kind":"group","lines":"a word from india meaning selfless service. it's also the name of a charity started in 1978 by larry brilliant, his wife girija, ram dass and a few others, mostly paying for cataract operations that give people their sight back.\nit has helped millions of people see again, most of them in poor countries, for the cost of a cheap dinner out per operation.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seva_Foundation","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"seva","url":"https://www.seva.org/","why":"the charity's own site"}],"name":"seva","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sometimes brilliant"],"key":"sometimesbrilliant","kind":"book","lines":"larry brilliant's 2016 memoir, about the hippie years, neem karoli baba, and the smallpox campaign. it's where his side of the story is.","links":[],"name":"sometimes brilliant","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["krishna das"],"key":"krishnadas","kind":"person","lines":"an american singer, born jeffrey kagel on long island, who sat with neem karoli baba in the early seventies. after the old man died he came home and fell apart for years, with drugs among other things.\nhe came back by leading kirtan, the call and response singing of god's names. he's been filling halls with strangers singing along for decades, and was nominated for a grammy in 2013.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krishna_Das_(singer)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"krishna das","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["miracle of love"],"key":"miracleoflove","kind":"book","lines":"a collection of stories about neem karoli baba from the people who knew him, indians and westerners, put together by ram dass and published in 1979. mind reading, feeding people, disappearing, a lot of tears.\nit's the closest thing to a book of his teachings, because he wrote nothing.","links":[],"name":"miracle of love","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the hog farm","hog farm"],"key":"hogfarm","kind":"group","lines":"a hippie commune that started on an actual pig farm in california in the sixties and became famous for running the free kitchen and the calm-down tent at woodstock in 1969. later a bunch of them drove buses to india, and larry brilliant went along.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hog_Farm","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the hog farm","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sub ek","sab ek"],"key":"subek","kind":"word","lines":"hindi for all one. neem karoli baba said it constantly, meaning everything and everyone is the same one thing, and every religion is pointing at it.","links":[],"name":"sub ek","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["butterfly dream","the butterfly dream","dreamed he was a butterfly","dreaming he was a butterfly"],"key":"butterflydream","kind":"event","lines":"the most famous story in the zhuangzi, at the end of chapter 2. zhuangzi dreams he's a butterfly, fluttering around happy, not knowing he's zhuangzi. then he wakes up, and he's zhuangzi. and now he can't tell whether he's a man who dreamed he was a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming he's a man.\nit's not a riddle to solve. it's a joke about how sure you are of who you are. twenty three hundred years before the matrix.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuangzi_(book)","why":"the book, with a section on the butterfly dream"}],"name":"the butterfly dream","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["huizi","hui shi"],"key":"huizi","kind":"person","lines":"zhuangzi's best friend and favorite sparring partner, a logician who loved arguments and paradoxes. he served as a minister to the king of wei.\nin the book he's always the straight man. zhuangzi wins the argument and then misses him terribly when he dies. none of huizi's own writings survive.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hui_Shi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"huizi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hao river","happy fish","the happiness of fish"],"key":"happyfish","kind":"event","lines":"a famous argument in the zhuangzi. zhuangzi and huizi are on a bridge over the hao river. zhuangzi says look how happy the fish are. huizi says you're not a fish, how do you know what makes fish happy. zhuangzi says you're not me, how do you know i don't know.\nit goes around once more, and zhuangzi ends it by saying he knew it standing right there on the bridge. philosophers still argue about it. it's two friends messing with each other.","links":[],"name":"the happy fish","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["useless tree","the useless tree"],"key":"uselesstree","kind":"event","lines":"a story zhuangzi tells more than once. a carpenter walks past a huge old tree without looking. its wood is no good, too knotted for boards, it makes coffins that rot. so nobody's ever cut it down, and it's grown enormous.\nthat night the tree comes to the carpenter in a dream and asks what he thinks usefulness got the fruit trees, which get stripped and broken every year. being useless to the saw is how it got to live. the patron saint of everyone who turned down the promotion.","links":[],"name":"the useless tree","status":"approved","threads":["work"]},{"aliases":["cook ding","cook ting","a cook named ding"],"key":"cookding","kind":"person","lines":"a cook in the zhuangzi, chapter 3, who carves up an ox for a king so smoothly it looks like a dance. he's had the same knife for nineteen years and never sharpened it.\nhe explains that a bad cook hacks and breaks the blade every month. he just feels for the gaps between the joints and lets the knife slide where the ox is already open. the king says he's learned how to live from it.","links":[],"name":"cook ding","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the inner chapters","inner chapters"],"key":"innerchapters","kind":"book","lines":"the first seven chapters of the zhuangzi, the ones scholars think zhuangzi mostly wrote himself. the other twenty six were added by followers and imitators over the next century or two.\nthey're the best part. the butterfly, the cook, the useless tree, the happy fish are all in there or close.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuangzi_(book)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the inner chapters","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["king of chu","the king of chu","kingdom of chu"],"key":"kingofchu","kind":"person","lines":"chu was the biggest kingdom in south china in zhuangzi's time. the story goes its king sent two officials to hire zhuangzi to run the state. zhuangzi, fishing, asked whether the sacred tortoise the king kept wrapped in silk in his temple, dead three thousand years, would rather be honored and dead or alive dragging its tail in the mud. alive, they said. then go, he said. i'll drag my tail in the mud.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chu_(state)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the king of chu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["empty boat","the empty boat"],"key":"emptyboat","kind":"event","lines":"a story from the zhuangzi, chapter 20. if an empty boat drifts into yours on the river, you don't get mad, even if you've got a temper. if there's a man in it, you shout at him. so make yourself the empty boat, and nobody will have anything to hit.\nthomas merton put it in his book the way of chuang tzu and it's been passed around ever since.","links":[],"name":"the empty boat","status":"approved","threads":["anger"]},{"aliases":["the way of chuang tzu","way of chuang tzu"],"key":"wayofchuangtzu","kind":"book","lines":"thomas merton's 1965 book of loose versions of zhuangzi's stories. he couldn't read chinese, so he worked from several translations side by side with help from the scholar john wu.\nhe said he enjoyed writing it more than anything else he'd written. it's short and it's the easiest way in.","links":[],"name":"the way of chuang tzu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["chuang tzu","chuang-tzu","zhuang zhou"],"key":"chuangtzu","kind":"word","lines":"the older english spelling of zhuangzi, and zhuang zhou was his actual name. same man. the spelling changed when china's own system for writing chinese in the roman alphabet, pinyin, took over in the eighties.","links":[],"name":"chuang tzu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["burton watson"],"key":"burtonwatson","kind":"person","lines":"an american translator of chinese and japanese classics who taught at columbia. his translations of zhuangzi are clear, funny, and faithful, the ones most teachers hand out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burton_Watson","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"burton watson","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john wu","john c.h. wu","john c. h. wu"],"key":"johnwu","kind":"person","lines":"a chinese lawyer, judge and scholar who converted to catholicism and helped write the constitution of the republic of china. he wrote to thomas merton for years and helped him with the chinese for the way of chuang tzu.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Ching_Hsiung_Wu","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john wu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lao-zhuang","lao zhuang"],"key":"laozhuang","kind":"word","lines":"what the chinese call lao tzu and zhuangzi together, the two names run into one. it means the taoist tradition at its source. one wrote the short strange book of poems, the other wrote the one with the jokes.","links":[],"name":"lao-zhuang","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lao dan"],"key":"laodan","kind":"word","lines":"another name for lao tzu, the old master of the tao te ching. it's what he's called when he shows up as a character in zhuangzi's book, lecturing confucius.","links":[],"name":"lao dan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["punjab","the punjab","punjabi"],"key":"punjab","kind":"place","lines":"the land of five rivers, a big flat farming region in the northwest of the indian subcontinent. in 1947, when the british left and india and pakistan split, the punjab was cut in half, and millions of people fled across the new line in both directions. hundreds of thousands were killed.\nit's the homeland of the sikhs. guru nanak was born there, and most sikhs today have family roots there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punjab","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the punjab","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nankana sahib","talwandi"],"key":"nankanasahib","kind":"place","lines":"the village where guru nanak was born in 1469, then called talwandi. it's in pakistan now, near lahore, and sikh pilgrims cross the border to visit the gurdwara on the spot.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nankana_Sahib","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nankana sahib","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sultanpur lodhi"],"key":"sultanpurlodhi","kind":"place","lines":"a town in the punjab where young nanak worked at the government storehouse. the river bein, where the story says he disappeared for three days, runs past it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanpur_Lodhi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sultanpur lodhi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mardana","bhai mardana"],"key":"mardana","kind":"person","lines":"a muslim musician who went with guru nanak on all his long journeys, playing the rabab, a kind of lute, while nanak sang. he was a friend from the home village and they were together for decades.\na hindu born holy man and a muslim musician on the road together, singing that there's no hindu and no muslim. the partnership was the message.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhai_Mardana","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bhai mardana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rabab","rebab"],"key":"rabab","kind":"word","lines":"a lute from central asia and the middle east with a short neck, a round body covered in skin, and gut strings. bhai mardana played it for guru nanak.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubab_(instrument)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rabab","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kaaba","the kaaba","ka'ba"],"key":"kaaba","kind":"place","lines":"the cube shaped stone building covered in black cloth in the middle of the great mosque in mecca. it's the holiest place in islam. every muslim on earth faces it to pray, five times a day.\nthe story goes guru nanak fell asleep with his feet pointing at it. told to move them, he said, then turn my feet toward where god isn't.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaaba","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the kaaba","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kartarpur"],"key":"kartarpur","kind":"place","lines":"the village on the ravi river that guru nanak founded in his last years, where he farmed and ran the free kitchen. it's in pakistan now, a few miles from the indian border.\nin 2019 the two countries opened a corridor so indian sikhs could walk across to the gurdwara there without a visa. for two countries that barely speak, that's something.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kartarpur,_Pakistan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kartarpur","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["langar","langars"],"key":"langar","kind":"word","lines":"the free kitchen at every sikh temple. volunteers cook, and anybody who walks in gets fed, sitting on the floor in rows, rich next to poor, any religion, no questions asked.\nguru nanak started it to kill the idea of caste, the old indian rule about who can eat with whom. the one at the golden temple in amritsar feeds tens of thousands of people a day. sikh langars have shown up at floods, earthquakes and protests all over the world.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Langar_(Sikhism)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"langar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gurdwara","gurdwaras","sikh temple","sikh temples"],"key":"gurdwara","kind":"place","lines":"a sikh temple. the word means door of the guru. inside, the holy book sits on a raised platform under a canopy, people sing hymns from it, and there's always a free kitchen.\nanyone can walk in. you cover your head, take off your shoes, and you'll probably be fed.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gurdwara","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gurdwara","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["babur"],"key":"babur","kind":"person","lines":"a warlord from central asia, descended from both genghis khan and timur, who came down into india and won the battle of panipat in 1526. he founded the mughal empire, which ruled most of india for the next two hundred years.\nhe also wrote one of the great memoirs of the time, honest about his drinking and his homesickness. guru nanak wrote hymns about the slaughter when babur's army sacked the town of saidpur.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babur","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"babur","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["mughal empire","the mughal empire","mughals","the mughals","mughal"],"key":"mughal","kind":"group","lines":"the muslim dynasty that ruled most of india from 1526 into the 1700s, founded by babur. they built the taj mahal. at their peak they were one of the richest empires on earth.\nsome of the emperors were tolerant and some weren't. the sikh gurus lived under them, and two of the gurus were executed on mughal orders.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mughal_Empire","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mughal empire","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["saidpur"],"key":"saidpur","kind":"place","lines":"a town in the punjab, now called eminabad, in pakistan. babur's army sacked it around 1520. the tradition says guru nanak was there and taken prisoner, and the hymns he wrote about it are in the sikh scripture.","links":[],"name":"saidpur","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["guru angad","angad","lehna"],"key":"guruangad","kind":"person","lines":"the second sikh guru. his name was lehna, and he was a devoted follower who did the dirty work around nanak's farm and kitchen. nanak passed over his own sons and chose him, and renamed him angad, which means part of my own body.\nhe's remembered for the gurmukhi script, the alphabet the sikh scripture is written in.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guru_Angad","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"guru angad","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sri chand"],"key":"srichand","kind":"person","lines":"guru nanak's elder son. he became a wandering ascetic, a holy man who gave up home and family, and founded an order of them called the udasis. his father didn't pick him as successor.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Chand","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sri chand","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["janamsakhis","janamsakhi","the janamsakhis"],"key":"janamsakhis","kind":"book","lines":"collections of stories about guru nanak's life, written down in the generations after he died. the river, the twenty rupees, mecca, the flowers are all from them.\nthey're more like saints' legends than history. they grew in the telling. the hymns in the scripture are the solid ground.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Janamsakhis","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the janamsakhis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ik onkar","ek onkar"],"key":"ikonkar","kind":"word","lines":"the first words of the sikh scripture. it means there is one, or one god. it's written as a single symbol, a numeral one joined to a curling letter, and you'll see it on sikh temples, jewelry and car stickers everywhere.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ik_Onkar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ik onkar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mul mantar","mool mantar","the mul mantar"],"key":"mulmantar","kind":"word","lines":"the short creed that opens the sikh scripture. one god, truth is its name, the creator, without fear, without hate, timeless, never born, self existent, known by the guru's grace. sikhs say it every morning.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mul_Mantar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mul mantar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["japji sahib","japji"],"key":"japji","kind":"book","lines":"guru nanak's long morning hymn, the first thing in the sikh scripture. thirty eight verses about one god, truth, and how small we are next to it. sikhs recite it every morning.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japji_Sahib","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"japji sahib","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["raga","ragas","sri rag"],"key":"raga","kind":"word","lines":"in indian music, a raga is the scale, mood and set of melodic moves a piece is built on. each one has a feeling and often a time of day it belongs to, dawn or dusk or midnight.\nthe sikh scripture is arranged by raga, not by author or subject. sri rag is the first one. that tells you the thing was meant to be sung, not just read.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raga","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"raga","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["golden temple","the golden temple","harmandir sahib"],"key":"goldentemple","kind":"place","lines":"the holiest place for sikhs, in the city of amritsar in the indian punjab. a temple covered in gold, in the middle of a big square pool, with doors on all four sides so anyone from any direction can come in.\nits free kitchen feeds tens of thousands of people every day. in 1984 the indian army stormed it to root out armed separatists, and hundreds died. months later indira gandhi was killed by her sikh bodyguards.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Temple","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the golden temple","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["amritsar"],"key":"amritsar","kind":"place","lines":"a city in the indian punjab, near the pakistan border, built around the golden temple. it's the center of the sikh world.\nit's also where, in 1919, a british general had his troops fire on a crowd in a walled garden, the jallianwala bagh, and killed hundreds. that day turned a lot of indians against british rule for good.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amritsar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"amritsar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["guru arjan","guru arjan dev"],"key":"guruarjan","kind":"person","lines":"the fifth sikh guru. in 1604 he compiled the sikh holy book, gathering the hymns of the gurus before him plus poets from other faiths, like kabir. he also finished the golden temple.\nin 1606 he was arrested on the mughal emperor's orders and died under torture, the first sikh martyr.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guru_Arjan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"guru arjan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shiraz"],"key":"shiraz","kind":"place","lines":"a city in the south of iran known for poets, gardens and roses. two of persia's greatest poets, hafiz and saadi, lived and are buried there.\nhafiz's tomb is a garden where people still go at dusk to read him to each other. yes, the shiraz grape is named after it, though whether the grape actually came from there is doubtful.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shiraz","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"shiraz","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["persia","persians"],"key":"persia","kind":"place","lines":"the old name, in english, for iran. the people and their language are persian, farsi in the language itself. the country officially asked the world to call it iran in 1935.\nfor a thousand years persian was the language of poetry and government from turkey to india, the way french was in europe. rumi and hafiz wrote in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"persia","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["divan","the divan","diwan"],"key":"divan","kind":"book","lines":"a persian word for a poet's collected poems. rumi has a huge one, and so does hafiz. when someone says the divan, they mean the whole pile of a poet's short poems in one book.\nhafiz's divan is about five hundred ghazals, short love poems, and it's the most loved book in iran after the quran. families keep it next to the quran and open it at random to tell their fortunes, especially on yalda, the longest night of the year.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diwan_(poetry)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divan_of_Hafez","why":"the divan of hafiz itself"}],"name":"divan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ghazal","ghazals"],"key":"ghazal","kind":"word","lines":"a kind of short poem from arabic and persian. five to fifteen couplets, each one standing on its own, all ending on the same sound. the last couplet usually has the poet's own name in it, the poet talking to himself.\nghazals are about love and longing, and you often can't tell if the beloved is a person or god. hafiz is the master. in india and pakistan they're still sung as songs, the way we'd sing a ballad.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghazal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ghazal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mubariz al-din","mubariz"],"key":"mubariz","kind":"person","lines":"a harsh ruler who took shiraz in 1353, closed the taverns and wine shops, and enforced strict religious rules. hafiz wrote poems mocking the preachers who scolded in public and drank in private. mubariz was overthrown and blinded by his own son a few years later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mubariz_al-Din_Muhammad","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mubariz al-din","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["goethe","johann wolfgang von goethe"],"key":"goethe","kind":"person","lines":"germany's greatest writer, the man who wrote faust, the play about the scholar who sells his soul to the devil. he was also a scientist, a statesman, and a lifelong ladies' man. he died in 1832.\nin 1814, in his sixties, he read hafiz in german and it woke him up. he wrote a whole book of poems back to him, the west-eastern divan.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann_Wolfgang_von_Goethe","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"goethe","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["west-eastern divan","the west-eastern divan","west-östlicher divan"],"key":"westeasterndivan","kind":"book","lines":"goethe's 1819 book of poems written in answer to hafiz, a german poet playing persian. it's where the idea of world literature, reading across cultures as equals, really starts.\nin 1999 the conductor daniel barenboim and the writer edward said named an orchestra of young israeli and arab musicians after it.","links":[],"name":"the west-eastern divan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["joseph von hammer","hammer-purgstall","von hammer"],"key":"hammer","kind":"person","lines":"an austrian scholar of the middle east who made the first complete german translation of hafiz, in 1812 and 1813. that's the one goethe read.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_von_Hammer-Purgstall","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joseph von hammer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ralph waldo emerson","waldo emerson","emerson","emerson's"],"key":"emerson","kind":"person","lines":"the most famous american writer of the mid 1800s, an essayist and lecturer from concord, massachusetts. he quit being a minister because he couldn't believe the church's version anymore, and taught instead that god shows up in nature and in your own gut. his essay self-reliance is the root of a very american idea, trust yourself.\nhe took in thoreau like a younger brother and let him build the cabin on his land at walden. he wrote whitman the most famous fan letter in american history. he also translated hafiz, from german.\nhe was the center of the transcendentalists, and more or less the founding father of american spirituality outside the churches.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Waldo_Emerson","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/ralph-waldo-emerson","why":"his poems and life"}],"name":"ralph waldo emerson","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["daniel ladinsky","ladinsky","ladinsky's"],"key":"ladinsky","kind":"person","lines":"an american writer who in 1999 published the gift, poems by hafiz. they're his own poems, inspired by hafiz, not translations, and he's more or less said so. scholars of persian say they have almost nothing to do with the real ones.\nthey're also what almost everyone quotes. the sun never says to the earth you owe me, the posters and the instagram hafiz, that's ladinsky. read him if you like him, but know whose poems they are.","links":[],"name":"daniel ladinsky","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yalda","shab-e yalda"],"key":"yalda","kind":"event","lines":"the longest night of the year, the winter solstice, celebrated in iran with a party older than islam. families stay up late eating pomegranates and watermelon and nuts, and read hafiz out loud, opening his book at random to tell each other's fortunes.","links":[],"name":"yalda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["faces of love"],"key":"facesoflove","kind":"book","lines":"a 2012 book of translations of hafiz and two other poets of shiraz by dick davis, an english scholar of persian. it's the real hafiz in english, with notes, and it still reads like poetry.","links":[],"name":"faces of love","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["samarkand"],"key":"samarkand","kind":"place","lines":"an ancient city in what's now uzbekistan, on the silk road, famous for its blue tiled domes. timur made it his capital. with bukhara, it's the byword for the richest cities in central asia.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samarkand","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"samarkand","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bukhara"],"key":"bukhara","kind":"place","lines":"an ancient city in what's now uzbekistan, on the silk road, a center of islamic learning for a thousand years. in hafiz's most famous line he offers to give it and samarkand away for a mole on a beautiful face.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bukhara","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bukhara","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["timur","tamerlane","tamburlaine"],"key":"timur","kind":"person","lines":"a central asian warlord who conquered a huge empire from turkey to india in the late 1300s, famous for building towers out of his enemies' skulls. he took shiraz in 1387.\nthe story that he summoned hafiz and scolded him about samarkand is almost certainly made up. it's too good to drop.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timur","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"timur","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rajput","rajputs"],"key":"rajput","kind":"group","lines":"the warrior clans of northwest india who ruled rajasthan from hill forts for centuries. proud, touchy about honor, famous for fighting to the last man rather than surrender.\nmirabai was born into one rajput royal family and married into another, and the honor code is exactly what she wouldn't live by.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rajput","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rajputs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rajasthan"],"key":"rajasthan","kind":"place","lines":"the big desert state in the northwest of india, bordering pakistan. forts on hilltops, palaces, camels, the thar desert. it used to be a patchwork of rajput kingdoms.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rajasthan","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rajasthan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["merta","rathore","rathores"],"key":"merta","kind":"place","lines":"a town in rajasthan where mirabai was born, ruled by a branch of the rathore rajputs, her family. there's a temple and museum to her there now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merta_City","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"merta","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mewar"],"key":"mewar","kind":"place","lines":"the proudest of the rajput kingdoms, in the south of rajasthan, ruled from the hill fortress of chittor and later from udaipur. its rulers were called rana and boasted they never married their daughters to the mughal emperors.\nmirabai married its crown prince, and its court is what she walked out of.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mewar","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mewar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["chittor","chittorgarh"],"key":"chittor","kind":"place","lines":"a huge fortress on a long rocky hill in rajasthan, the old capital of mewar. it was besieged and sacked three times, and each time, the story goes, the women burned themselves rather than be captured and the men rode out to die. there's a temple to mirabai inside it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chittor_Fort","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"chittor","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bhoj raj"],"key":"bhojraj","kind":"person","lines":"the crown prince of mewar whom mirabai married around 1516. he died a few years later, probably of wounds from battle, before he could become king. that left her a widow at the court.","links":[],"name":"bhoj raj","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sati","commit sati"],"key":"sati","kind":"word","lines":"an old practice in parts of india where a widow climbed onto her husband's funeral fire and burned to death with him. it was held up as the highest loyalty, especially among rajput royal families. how voluntary it ever really was is a fair question.\nthe british banned it in 1829, pushed by indian reformers. it still happened now and then, and the last famous case, in rajasthan in 1987, caused an uproar. mirabai, the story goes, refused, saying her real husband, krishna, was alive.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sati_(practice)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sati","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["rana","the rana"],"key":"rana","kind":"word","lines":"the title of the king of mewar. in mirabai's songs the rana is the angry in-law king who wants her stopped, and who sends the poison.","links":[],"name":"rana","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dwarka","dwaraka"],"key":"dwarka","kind":"place","lines":"a temple town on the far western coast of india, in gujarat, where the god krishna is said to have ruled as a grown king. it's one of the four holiest pilgrimage places in hinduism.\nmirabai ended her life there. the story says she went into krishna's image in the temple and never came out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwarka","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dwarka","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lok laj"],"key":"loklaj","kind":"word","lines":"hindi for what people will say, the shame of public opinion. it runs all through mirabai's songs, and she keeps singing that she's thrown it off.","links":[],"name":"lok laj","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["giridhar gopal","giridhar","gopal"],"key":"giridhar","kind":"word","lines":"names for krishna. giridhar means mountain lifter, from the story where the boy krishna holds a whole hill up on one finger like an umbrella to shelter his village from a storm. gopal means cowherd. mirabai calls him that in song after song.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Govardhan_Hill","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"giridhar gopal","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["m.s. subbulakshmi","m. s. subbulakshmi","subbulakshmi"],"key":"subbulakshmi","kind":"person","lines":"the great classical singer from south india, one of the most loved voices the country ever had. she played mirabai in a 1945 film and sang her songs for the rest of her life. she was the first musician to get india's highest civilian honor. she died in 2004.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M._S._Subbulakshmi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"m.s. subbulakshmi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mirabehn","madeleine slade"],"key":"mirabehn","kind":"person","lines":"madeleine slade, the daughter of a british admiral, who read about gandhi, came to live at his ashram in 1925, and stayed with the movement for decades. gandhi named her mirabehn, sister mira, after mirabai. she spun cotton, went to prison, and later lived in austria, near vienna, drawn by her other great love, beethoven's music. she died in 1982.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirabehn","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mirabehn","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sabarmati ashram","sabarmati"],"key":"sabarmati","kind":"place","lines":"gandhi's community on the bank of the sabarmati river outside ahmedabad, in west india, from 1917 to 1930. people lived simply, spun cotton, prayed morning and evening, and cleaned their own latrines. the salt march started there in 1930. it's a museum now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabarmati_Ashram","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sabarmati ashram","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ravidas","raidas"],"key":"ravidas","kind":"person","lines":"a poet saint from varanasi around the fifteenth century who was a cobbler, from the people at the very bottom of the caste ranking, the ones called untouchable. he sang that god doesn't care what caste you're born in. dozens of his songs are in the sikh scripture.\nthe tradition makes him mirabai's guru. whether the dates work is doubtful. millions of dalits, the people once called untouchable, honor him today.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ravidas","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ravidas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["untouchable","untouchables","untouchability","dalit","dalits"],"key":"untouchable","kind":"word","lines":"the people at the very bottom of the old hindu caste order, so low they were outside it. higher castes wouldn't eat with them, touch them, or let them into temples. they did the work others thought dirty, leather, sweeping, handling the dead.\nthe practice was outlawed in india's constitution in 1950. the people themselves mostly use the word dalit now, meaning broken or crushed. the prejudice has not gone away.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dalit","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"untouchable","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ektara"],"key":"ektara","kind":"word","lines":"a one string instrument wandering singers in india carry, a gourd or can with a bamboo neck and a single string. you pluck it for a drone while you sing. mirabai is usually painted with one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ektara","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ektara","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["for love of the dark one"],"key":"fordarkone","kind":"book","lines":"a small book of mirabai's songs in english by the american poet andrew schelling. short, plain and alive. the dark one is krishna, who's blue-black in the paintings.","links":[],"name":"for love of the dark one","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bapu"],"key":"bapu","kind":"word","lines":"father, in gujarati. it's what gandhi's people called him, and what a lot of indians still call him.","links":[],"name":"bapu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["romain rolland"],"key":"romainrolland","kind":"person","lines":"a french novelist who won the nobel prize for literature in 1915, a pacifist who wrote books about gandhi, ramakrishna and vivekananda that brought them to european readers. his book on gandhi is what sent madeleine slade to india.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romain_Rolland","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"romain rolland","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stoicism","stoic","stoics","the stoics","stoic philosophy"],"key":"stoicism","kind":"group","lines":"a school of greek philosophy started in athens around 300 bc, named after the stoa, the painted porch where its first teacher, zeno, taught. it spread all over the roman world.\nthe core of it is short. some things are up to you, your judgments, your choices, how you act. everything else isn't, your body, your money, your reputation, what other people do. put your whole effort into the first list and let the second one go. do that and you can be free and decent even as a slave or an emperor.\nin plain english stoic has come to mean keeping a stiff upper lip and not showing feelings. that's not really it. modern cognitive behavioral therapy took its core straight from the stoics, and it's having a big revival now, sometimes in a macho, self-help version the old stoics would find odd.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoicism","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/","why":"the serious overview"}],"name":"stoicism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cognitive behavioral therapy","cognitive behavioural therapy","cbt"],"key":"cbt","kind":"word","lines":"the most common kind of talk therapy today, usually a short course of sessions. the idea is that what upsets you is often less the thing that happened than what you tell yourself about it, and you can learn to catch those thoughts and check them.\nits founders, albert ellis and aaron beck, said outright that they got the core idea from the stoics, and especially from epictetus's line that it isn't things that upset people, it's what they think about things.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_behavioral_therapy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cognitive behavioral therapy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["enchiridion","the enchiridion","the handbook"],"key":"enchiridion","kind":"book","lines":"a short handbook of epictetus's teaching, put together by his student arrian. the word means something held in the hand. fifty three short sections, twenty minutes to read.\nthe first line is the whole thing. some things are up to us and some are not. soldiers, prisoners and people in recovery have carried it around for two thousand years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enchiridion_of_Epictetus","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45109","why":"an old english translation at project gutenberg"}],"name":"the enchiridion","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the discourses of epictetus","discourses of epictetus","the discourses, book","discourses, book"],"key":"discourses","kind":"book","lines":"the notes epictetus's student arrian took in his classes, written down as close to word for word as he could. there were eight books, and four survive.\nthey're livelier than the handbook. you hear the old man arguing, teasing, losing his patience, telling a student to stop whining. it's closer to a transcript than a textbook.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discourses_of_Epictetus","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10661","why":"a selection from the discourses, with the handbook, at project gutenberg"}],"name":"the discourses of epictetus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["arrian"],"key":"arrian","kind":"person","lines":"a greek from what's now turkey who studied with epictetus as a young man and took down his classes almost word for word. those notes are the discourses and the enchiridion. without him we'd have nothing.\nhe went on to be a roman senator and governor and wrote the best surviving history of alexander the great.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"arrian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["musonius rufus","musonius"],"key":"musonius","kind":"person","lines":"the leading stoic teacher in rome in the first century, epictetus's teacher. nero exiled him, and later emperors did too.\nhe was unusual for his time. he said women should study philosophy the same as men, and that a husband should be faithful the same as a wife. some of his lectures survive, written down by students.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Musonius_Rufus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"musonius rufus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["domitian"],"key":"domitian","kind":"person","lines":"roman emperor from 81 to 96. paranoid, cruel to anyone he thought was plotting, and near the end he threw the philosophers out of rome, epictetus among them. his own household had him stabbed to death in 96.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domitian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"domitian","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["nicopolis"],"key":"nicopolis","kind":"place","lines":"a roman city on the coast of northwest greece, built by the emperor augustus to celebrate a naval victory there. it's in ruins now. epictetus opened his school there after he was thrown out of rome, and people came from all over to sit in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicopolis","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nicopolis","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["hierapolis","phrygia"],"key":"hierapolis","kind":"place","lines":"a greek and roman city in phrygia, a region of what's now western turkey, famous for its hot springs. the white stone terraces of pamukkale are right next to the ruins now. epictetus was born there a slave.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hierapolis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["epaphroditus"],"key":"epaphroditus","kind":"person","lines":"epictetus's owner in rome, a freed slave himself who rose to be one of nero's secretaries. when nero fell in 68 and killed himself, epaphroditus helped him do it. domitian later had him executed for it.","links":[],"name":"epaphroditus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["alexander the great"],"key":"alexander","kind":"person","lines":"the young king of macedonia who, in the 330s and 320s bc, conquered everything from greece to egypt to the edge of india before he died at thirty two. he's the model every conqueror since has measured himself against.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_the_Great","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"alexander the great","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["simplicius"],"key":"simplicius","kind":"person","lines":"a greek philosopher of the 500s who wrote a long commentary on epictetus's handbook. he's where the story comes from that epictetus took in an abandoned baby in old age.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplicius_of_Cilicia","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"simplicius","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lucian"],"key":"lucian","kind":"person","lines":"a greek satirist of the second century, the funniest writer of the roman empire, who made fun of philosophers, gods and con men. he's the source of the story about the man who paid a fortune for epictetus's clay lamp.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lucian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["drachma","drachmas"],"key":"drachma","kind":"word","lines":"the old greek silver coin. one was roughly a day's pay for a laborer, so three thousand drachmas was around ten years of wages. greece used the name for its money until it switched to the euro in 2002.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_drachma","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"drachma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the meditations","meditations of marcus aurelius","meditations, book"],"key":"meditations","kind":"book","lines":"the private notebook marcus aurelius kept, mostly in army camps on the danube in the 170s, while he was running a war and the roman empire. he wrote it in greek, to himself. twelve short books of notes, reminders, and pep talks.\nhe never meant anyone to read it. it survived by luck and was first printed in the 1550s. now it's on every nightstand from locker rooms to silicon valley, because it sounds like someone talking himself out of bed in the morning, which it is.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meditations","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2680","why":"an old english translation at project gutenberg"}],"name":"the meditations","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hadrian"],"key":"hadrian","kind":"person","lines":"roman emperor from 117 to 138, a restless traveler and builder. he built the wall across northern england and rebuilt the pantheon in rome. he picked out the boy marcus and arranged the adoptions that made him emperor one day.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hadrian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lucius verus"],"key":"luciusverus","kind":"person","lines":"marcus aurelius's adoptive brother and co-emperor from 161 until he died, probably of the plague, in 169. he led the war against parthia, and was known more for his parties than his work.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Verus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lucius verus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["parthia","parthian","parthians"],"key":"parthia","kind":"place","lines":"the rival empire to rome in the east, centered in what's now iran and iraq. they fought rome on and off for three hundred years. marcus's war with them in the 160s brought home the plague.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parthian_Empire","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"parthia","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["antonine plague","the antonine plague","a plague, probably smallpox"],"key":"antonineplague","kind":"event","lines":"an epidemic, probably smallpox, that soldiers brought back from the war with parthia in 165. it swept the roman empire for about fifteen years and killed millions, by some estimates a tenth of everyone. the famous doctor galen saw it and described it.\nit's the covid of marcus aurelius's reign, only far deadlier, and there was nothing anyone could do about it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Plague","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the antonine plague","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["danube","the danube"],"key":"danube","kind":"place","lines":"the second longest river in europe, running from germany to the black sea through vienna and budapest. for centuries it was rome's northern border, with the tribes on the other side. marcus spent most of his last decade in army camps along it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danube","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the danube","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["quadi","the quadi"],"key":"quadi","kind":"group","lines":"a germanic tribe north of the danube, in what's now slovakia and moravia, that marcus aurelius fought for years. book two of the meditations is headed among the quadi, on the river gran.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the quadi","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["carnuntum"],"key":"carnuntum","kind":"place","lines":"a big roman legion base and town on the danube, near what's now vienna. marcus used it as his headquarters. book three of the meditations is headed there. its ruins are an open air museum now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnuntum","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"carnuntum","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["faustina","faustina the younger"],"key":"faustina","kind":"person","lines":"faustina the younger, marcus aurelius's wife and the daughter of the emperor before him. she had at least thirteen children with him, and most died young. she went with him on campaign and was called mother of the camps.\nshe died in 175 at a village in cappadocia, in what's now turkey, and he renamed it after her. roman gossip said she had affairs. marcus, in his notebook, thanks the gods for her.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faustina_the_Younger","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"faustina","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cappadocia"],"key":"cappadocia","kind":"place","lines":"a region in the middle of what's now turkey, famous now for its strange rock chimneys and hot air balloons. faustina died there in 175.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cappadocia","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cappadocia","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["avidius cassius"],"key":"avidiuscassius","kind":"person","lines":"marcus aurelius's best general in the east. in 175, on a false rumor that marcus had died, he declared himself emperor. three months later his own officers killed him. marcus said he was sorry not to get the chance to pardon him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avidius_Cassius","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"avidius cassius","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["commodus"],"key":"commodus","kind":"person","lines":"marcus aurelius's son, made co-emperor as a teenager and sole emperor at eighteen when marcus died in 180. vain, cruel, and lazy, he fought in the arena as a gladiator for show and renamed rome after himself. he was strangled in his bath by a wrestler in 192.\nhe's the villain in the movie gladiator, loosely. marcus's choice to leave everything to him is the big mistake of his reign.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commodus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"commodus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the movie gladiator","movie gladiator"],"key":"gladiatorfilm","kind":"book","lines":"the 2000 ridley scott movie with russell crowe as a roman general turned gladiator. marcus aurelius is in it as a wise old emperor, and commodus as the villain who murders him. the murder is made up. the rest is loose.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gladiator_(2000_film)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gladiator, the film","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lyon","martyrs of lyon","blandina","pothinus"],"key":"lyon","kind":"event","lines":"in 177, in lyon, a roman city in gaul, what's now france, a mob and then the roman authorities turned on the local christians. dozens were tortured and killed in the arena. a slave girl named blandina was tortured from morning to night and finally thrown to a wild bull. the old bishop pothinus, over ninety, died in prison.\nthe governor wrote to marcus aurelius asking what to do with the ones who were roman citizens. the answer, as eusebius preserves it, was that those who denied being christian could go, and those who confessed should be executed. a survivor's letter describing it all is one of the oldest christian documents we have.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persecution_in_Lyon","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the martyrs of lyon","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["gaul","gauls"],"key":"gaul","kind":"place","lines":"the roman name for what's now mostly france and belgium. julius caesar conquered it in the 50s bc, and it stayed roman for five hundred years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaul","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gaul","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["eusebius","eusebius of caesarea"],"key":"eusebius","kind":"person","lines":"a bishop in what's now israel, writing around 320, who wrote the first big history of the church. without him we'd know almost nothing about the early christians. he copied old letters and documents straight into his book, including the one about the martyrs of lyon.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eusebius","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eusebius","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cassius dio"],"key":"cassiusdio","kind":"person","lines":"a roman senator and historian who lived through the end of marcus aurelius's reign and wrote a huge history of rome in greek. he's the one who tells us marcus burned avidius cassius's letters unread.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassius_Dio","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cassius dio","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["junius rusticus","rusticus"],"key":"rusticus","kind":"person","lines":"a stoic philosopher and senator who taught the young marcus aurelius. he lent him his own copy of epictetus's lectures, and marcus thanks him for it by name in the first book of the meditations. later marcus made him the city prefect, basically mayor and police chief of rome.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Junius_Rusticus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"junius rusticus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["constantine","constantine the great"],"key":"constantine","kind":"person","lines":"the roman emperor from 306 to 337 who became a christian and made the religion legal, then favored. he founded constantinople, now istanbul. after him christianity went from being hunted to running the empire.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_the_Great","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"constantine","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["caesar","a caesar"],"key":"caesar","kind":"word","lines":"the family name of julius caesar, the general who took over the roman republic and was stabbed to death in the senate in 44 bc. his adopted heir augustus became the first emperor, and after that caesar turned into a title that meant emperor.\nthe words kaiser and tsar come from it. when marcus warns himself not to turn into a caesar, he means don't let the job make you into one more emperor.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caesar_(title)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"caesar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["letters to a young poet"],"key":"letterstoayoungpoet","kind":"book","lines":"ten letters rilke wrote between 1903 and 1908 to franz kappus, a young military cadet who'd sent him poems and asked if they were any good. kappus published them in 1929, after rilke died.\nrilke doesn't really answer the question. he tells him to go inside and ask whether he'd die if he couldn't write, to be patient with everything unsolved in his heart, and to live the questions. every writing student gets handed it sooner or later. it's short, you can read it in an afternoon.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letters_to_a_Young_Poet","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"letters to a young poet","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["franz kappus","franz xaver kappus","kappus"],"key":"kappus","kind":"person","lines":"the young austrian military cadet who wrote to rilke in 1902 asking about his poems. he became an army officer, a journalist and a writer of popular novels, and published rilke's letters to him in 1929.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_Xaver_Kappus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"franz kappus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["duino elegies","the duino elegies","elegies"],"key":"duinoelegies","kind":"book","lines":"ten long poems rilke started in 1912 at duino castle on the adriatic and couldn't finish for ten years, then finished in a few weeks in 1922. an elegy is a poem of mourning. these are about being human, being mortal, and praising the world anyway.\nthey're hard, and they're thought of as some of the greatest poems of the twentieth century. the first line, who, if i cried out, would hear me among the angels, is the famous one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duino_Elegies","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the duino elegies","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["duino castle","duino"],"key":"duino","kind":"place","lines":"a castle on a cliff over the adriatic sea near trieste, in what's now the far northeast corner of italy. it belonged to rilke's patron, princess marie von thurn und taxis, and he stayed there as her guest in the winter of 1911 and 1912.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duino_Castle","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"duino castle","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["marie von thurn und taxis","thurn und taxis"],"key":"thurnundtaxis","kind":"person","lines":"a rich austrian princess, owner of duino castle, and one of rilke's most generous patrons. she's the source of the story that the first line of the elegies came to him on the cliffs in a wind.","links":[],"name":"princess marie von thurn und taxis","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sonnets to orpheus","the sonnets to orpheus"],"key":"sonnetstoorpheus","kind":"book","lines":"fifty five short poems rilke wrote in a rush in february 1922 at muzot, at the same time he finished the elegies. they're written to orpheus and to a young dancer who'd died at nineteen, the daughter of friends.\nlighter than the elegies, and full of praise.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonnets_to_Orpheus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sonnets to orpheus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["orpheus"],"key":"orpheus","kind":"person","lines":"a singer in greek myth whose music could charm animals and move stones. when his wife eurydice died, he went down to the land of the dead and sang until the gods let her go, on one condition, that he not look back at her on the way out. at the last moment he looked, and lost her.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orpheus","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"orpheus","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["muzot","château de muzot"],"key":"muzot","kind":"place","lines":"a small stone tower house from the 1200s in the swiss valais, the sunny valley of the rhône in the alps. a rich swiss businessman, werner reinhart, bought it so rilke could live there. in february 1922 rilke finished the elegies there in a few weeks.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Muzot","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"muzot","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["werner reinhart"],"key":"werner","kind":"person","lines":"a rich swiss businessman and patron of musicians and poets who bought the tower at muzot and let rilke live in it rent free.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Werner_Reinhart","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"werner reinhart","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["auguste rodin","rodin","rodin's"],"key":"rodin","kind":"person","lines":"the most famous sculptor in the world around 1900, the man who made the thinker and the kiss. he worked every day, whether he felt like it or not, and that's what rilke went to paris to learn.\nrilke wrote a book about him, became his secretary in 1905, and was fired in 1906. they made it up later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auguste_Rodin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"auguste rodin","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the thinker"],"key":"thethinker","kind":"word","lines":"rodin's most famous sculpture, a naked man sitting with his chin on his fist, lost in thought. you've seen it in a hundred cartoons. it was first made around 1880 for a huge doorway about dante's hell, and he's looking down at it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Thinker","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the thinker","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["meudon"],"key":"meudon","kind":"place","lines":"a town on a hill just outside paris where rodin had his house and studios. rilke lived in a cottage there while working as his secretary.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meudon","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"meudon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jardin des plantes"],"key":"jardindesplantes","kind":"place","lines":"the big botanical garden in paris, with a small old zoo in it. the story goes rodin sent rilke there to look at one animal until he really saw it, and he came back with the poem the panther.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jardin_des_plantes","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the jardin des plantes","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the panther"],"key":"panther","kind":"book","lines":"a short poem rilke wrote around 1902 or 1903 about a panther in the paris zoo, pacing behind bars until it seems there are a thousand bars and no world behind them. it's in his new poems. people read it as a poem about being trapped in your own head.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Panther_(poem)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the panther","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lou andreas-salomé","lou andreas-salome","andreas-salomé","lou salomé"],"key":"louandreassalome","kind":"person","lines":"a russian-born writer and later psychoanalyst, brilliant and fiercely independent, who turned down a marriage proposal from the philosopher nietzsche and later trained with freud. she was married her whole adult life, on her own terms.\nshe met rilke when he was twenty one and she was thirty six. she was his lover, then his closest friend for life. she got him to change his name from rené to rainer and took him to russia twice.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lou_Andreas-Salom%C3%A9","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lou andreas-salomé","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["clara westhoff","clara rilke-westhoff"],"key":"clarawesthoff","kind":"person","lines":"a german sculptor who studied with rodin and married rilke in 1901. their daughter ruth was born that december. within a year they were living mostly apart, and stayed that way, married, writing to each other, for the rest of his life. she kept working as an artist and died in 1954.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clara_Westhoff","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"clara westhoff","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["raron"],"key":"raron","kind":"place","lines":"a village in the swiss alps where rilke is buried, in the churchyard beside an old church on a rock. the epitaph he wrote himself is on the stone, starting rose, oh pure contradiction.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raron","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"raron","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["archaic torso of apollo"],"key":"archaictorso","kind":"book","lines":"a rilke poem from 1908 about an ancient greek statue that's lost its head, arms and legs. the stone still seems to be looking at you, and the poem ends with the line you must change your life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaic_Torso_of_Apollo","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"archaic torso of apollo","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stephen mitchell"],"key":"stephenmitchell","kind":"person","lines":"an american translator whose versions of rilke, the tao te ching, the book of job and the gita are the ones a lot of people read. clear and readable, sometimes loose.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Mitchell_(translator)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"stephen mitchell","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wrote on the road","typed on the road","written on the road","scroll of on the road","when on the road hit","on the road, part one","on the road, the book"],"key":"ontheroad","kind":"book","lines":"jack kerouac's 1957 novel about driving back and forth across america in the late forties with his friend neal cassady, called dean moriarty in the book. jazz, benzedrine, girls, cheap food, all night talk, and the feeling that something holy is right around the next bend.\nhe typed it in about three weeks in april 1951 on one long roll of taped together paper so he wouldn't have to stop. it took six years to find a publisher. when it came out it made him famous overnight and sent a generation onto the highway, and later onto the hippie trail to india.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_the_Road","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"on the road","status":"approved","threads":["wandering"]},{"aliases":["the dharma bums","dharma bums"],"key":"dharmabums","kind":"book","lines":"jack kerouac's 1958 novel about his friendship with the poet gary snyder, called japhy ryder in it, and climbing a mountain in the sierra with him. dharma is the buddhist word for the teaching, so the title means something like holy bums.\nit's the book where kerouac's buddhism is out front. japhy's speech about a rucksack revolution, young americans wandering around with packs looking for something real, more or less came true.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Dharma_Bums","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dharma bums","status":"approved","threads":["wandering"]},{"aliases":["gary snyder","snyder","japhy ryder","japhy"],"key":"garysnyder","kind":"person","lines":"an american poet, born in 1930, who studied chinese and japanese, worked as a logger and a fire lookout, and in 1956 went to kyoto to train in zen for years. he's the real buddhist of the beat crowd.\nkerouac turned him into japhy ryder in the dharma bums, which he didn't love. he won the pulitzer prize in 1975 for turtle island, and has lived for decades in the sierra foothills writing about wilderness and work.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Snyder","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/gary-snyder","why":"his poems and life"}],"name":"gary snyder","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["allen ginsberg","ginsberg"],"key":"ginsberg","kind":"person","lines":"an american poet from new jersey, the loudest voice of the beat generation. his long poem howl, from 1956, got its publisher put on trial for obscenity, and won.\nhe was openly gay decades before that was safe, went to india in the early sixties looking for teachers, and became a student of chögyam trungpa. he chanted om at protests and spent his life at the front of every march. he died in 1997.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allen_Ginsberg","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/allen-ginsberg","why":"his poems and life"}],"name":"allen ginsberg","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["howl"],"key":"howl","kind":"book","lines":"allen ginsberg's long, furious poem, first read aloud at the six gallery in san francisco in october 1955. it opens with i saw the best minds of my generation destroyed by madness, and goes on to rage about drugs, sex, madness, and a machine that eats people.\nafter city lights published it in 1956, police arrested the store's manager and its publisher, lawrence ferlinghetti, and ferlinghetti was put on trial for obscenity. the judge ruled in 1957 that it had real social importance. that ruling opened the door for a lot of books.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howl_(poem)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"howl","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["six gallery","the six gallery","six gallery reading"],"key":"sixgallery","kind":"event","lines":"a little art space in san francisco. on the night of october 7, 1955, six poets read there, and allen ginsberg read howl for the first time. kerouac passed around jugs of wine and yelled go.\npeople date the beat generation going public to that night. gary snyder read too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six_Gallery_reading","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the six gallery","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["william burroughs","william s. burroughs","burroughs"],"key":"burroughs","kind":"person","lines":"an american writer from a rich st louis family, a harvard graduate, and a heroin addict for much of his life. his novel naked lunch, from 1959, is a hallucinated cut up nightmare about addiction and control, and was banned in boston.\nhe was the older, darker figure of the beat crowd. in 1951 in mexico city he shot and killed his wife, joan vollmer, playing at william tell with a gun, and got away with it. he died in 1997.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_S._Burroughs","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"william burroughs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["naked lunch"],"key":"nakedlunch","kind":"book","lines":"william burroughs's 1959 novel, a series of nightmare scenes of addiction, control and sex with no real plot. it was put on trial for obscenity in boston and cleared in 1966.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naked_Lunch","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"naked lunch","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["neal cassady","cassady","dean moriarty"],"key":"cassady","kind":"person","lines":"a fast talking, car stealing, womanizing kid from denver, raised partly on skid row by his alcoholic father. kerouac made him dean moriarty, the hero of on the road, and ginsberg was in love with him.\nlater he drove the bus for ken kesey's merry pranksters in the sixties. he was found by the railroad tracks in mexico in 1968 and died at forty one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neal_Cassady","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"neal cassady","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lowell","lowell, massachusetts"],"key":"lowell","kind":"place","lines":"a mill town on the merrimack river north of boston, one of the first factory cities in america. by the time kerouac was a kid the mills were closing and the town was full of french canadian, irish and greek working families.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lowell,_Massachusetts","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lowell","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["french canadian","french canadians","french-canadian"],"key":"frenchcanadian","kind":"word","lines":"people from quebec, the french speaking part of canada. in the late 1800s and early 1900s hundreds of thousands moved to new england to work in the mills. they kept their french, their big catholic families and their parish schools.\nkerouac grew up speaking a quebec french at home and didn't speak much english until he was about six.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Americans","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"french canadian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["columbia university"],"key":"columbia","kind":"place","lines":"an old, expensive ivy league university in upper manhattan in new york. kerouac got in on a football scholarship, broke his leg, and drifted out. ginsberg met him around there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_University","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"columbia university","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lawrence ferlinghetti","ferlinghetti"],"key":"ferlinghetti","kind":"person","lines":"a poet who founded city lights, a bookstore and small publisher in san francisco, in 1953. he published howl, got arrested for it, and won. he lent kerouac his cabin at big sur. he lived to be a hundred and one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Ferlinghetti","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"lawrence ferlinghetti","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["city lights","city lights books","city lights bookstore"],"key":"citylights","kind":"place","lines":"a bookstore and publisher in san francisco's north beach, founded in 1953 by lawrence ferlinghetti. it published howl and a lot of the beats. it's still there, still open late.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Lights_Bookstore","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"city lights","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wake up: a life of the buddha","wake up, a life of the buddha","called wake up","or wake up"],"key":"wakeup","kind":"book","lines":"a short life of the buddha kerouac wrote in 1955, retelling the story out of the buddhist scriptures he'd been reading. nobody published it until 2008.","links":[],"name":"wake up: a life of the buddha","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["some of the dharma"],"key":"someofthedharma","kind":"book","lines":"kerouac's buddhist notebooks from 1953 to 1956, hundreds of pages of notes, prayers, poems and arguments with himself about the buddha and his own head. published in 1997.","links":[],"name":"some of the dharma","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["a buddhist bible","buddhist bible"],"key":"buddhistbible","kind":"book","lines":"a thick anthology of buddhist scriptures in english put together by dwight goddard, an american former missionary to china, in 1932. it was one of the only books like it for decades.\nkerouac found it in a library in 1954 and it took him over. he carried it around for years.","links":[],"name":"a buddhist bible","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dwight goddard","goddard","goddard's"],"key":"dwightgoddard","kind":"person","lines":"an american engineer and christian missionary to china who turned buddhist, and in 1932 put together a buddhist bible, an english anthology of the scriptures. that's the book kerouac read to pieces.","links":[],"name":"dwight goddard","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["desolation peak"],"key":"desolationpeak","kind":"place","lines":"a mountain in the north cascades of washington state, near the canadian border, with a fire lookout cabin on top. in the summer of 1956 kerouac spent two months alone up there watching for fires, on gary snyder's advice.\nhe'd expected to meet god. he mostly met himself and got bored. he wrote about it in desolation angels.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desolation_Peak_(Washington)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"desolation peak","status":"approved","threads":["silence","loneliness"]},{"aliases":["fire lookout","fire lookouts"],"key":"firelookout","kind":"word","lines":"a person, and the little glass walled cabin, posted on a mountaintop all summer to watch for forest fires and call them in. weeks of solitude with a radio and a pair of binoculars. gary snyder and kerouac both did it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_lookout","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"fire lookout","status":"approved","threads":["silence","loneliness"]},{"aliases":["matterhorn peak"],"key":"matterhornpeak","kind":"place","lines":"a granite peak in the sierra nevada of california, near yosemite. gary snyder took kerouac and a friend up it in october 1955, and it's the climb in the dharma bums.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matterhorn_Peak","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"matterhorn peak","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sierra nevada","the sierra nevada","the high sierra","high sierra"],"key":"sierranevada","kind":"place","lines":"the big granite mountain range along the east side of california, with yosemite in it. snowy peaks, lakes, and a lot of empty country.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierra_Nevada","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sierra nevada","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kyoto"],"key":"kyoto","kind":"place","lines":"japan's old capital for a thousand years, full of temples and zen monasteries. gary snyder trained in zen there off and on for about a decade starting in 1956.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyoto","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kyoto","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["firing line"],"key":"firingline","kind":"event","lines":"a political talk show on american tv hosted by william f. buckley from 1966 to 1999. in 1968 kerouac showed up on it drunk, rambling and bitter about the hippies.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firing_Line_(TV_program)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"firing line","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["jan kerouac","a daughter, jan"],"key":"jankerouac","kind":"person","lines":"jack kerouac's daughter, born in 1952, whom he denied for years. he met her twice. she became a writer herself, with a hard life, and died in 1996 at forty four.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_Kerouac","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"jan kerouac","status":"approved","threads":["family"]},{"aliases":["joan haverty"],"key":"joanhaverty","kind":"person","lines":"kerouac's second wife, married in 1950. they split while she was pregnant with their daughter jan, and he spent years saying the child wasn't his. she later wrote a memoir about it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_Haverty_Kerouac","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joan haverty","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stella sampas"],"key":"stellasampas","kind":"person","lines":"the sister of sammy sampas, kerouac's closest friend from his lowell boyhood, who was killed in world war two. kerouac married her in 1966, partly so someone would help him look after his mother.","links":[],"name":"stella sampas","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rucksack revolution","rucksack"],"key":"rucksack","kind":"word","lines":"japhy ryder's vision, in the dharma bums, of thousands of young americans wandering the world with packs on their backs, praying and being kind. ten years later it more or less happened.","links":[],"name":"the rucksack revolution","status":"approved","threads":["wandering"]},{"aliases":["beatific"],"key":"beatific","kind":"word","lines":"blissed out, blessed, full of holy happiness. kerouac said that's what beat really meant, not beaten down.","links":[],"name":"beatific","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["concord, massachusetts","concord jail","concord town hall","concord circle","in concord","near concord"],"key":"concord","kind":"place","lines":"a small town about twenty miles west of boston where the american revolution started, with the first shots at the north bridge in 1775. seventy years later it was the home of emerson, thoreau, and louisa may alcott, the most famous writing town in america.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concord,_Massachusetts","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"concord","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["transcendentalism","transcendentalist","transcendentalists","the transcendentalists"],"key":"transcendentalism","kind":"group","lines":"a movement of new england writers and ministers in the 1830s and 1840s, centered on emerson in concord. they believed god shows up in nature and in your own gut more than in any church or creed, and that ordinary people could know the truth directly.\nthey read the hindu scriptures when almost no americans did, started a commune, pushed against slavery, and pushed for women's rights. a lot of what americans now call being spiritual but not religious starts with them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transcendentalism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"transcendentalism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["walden","walden; or, life in the woods"],"key":"walden","kind":"book","lines":"henry david thoreau's 1854 book about living alone for two years in a cabin he built at walden pond, outside concord. it's about beans, ice, ants, loons, the seasons, and what a person actually needs, and it's also a full on attack on how most people waste their lives working for things they don't want.\nit's the book behind every tiny house, every fantasy of quitting and moving to the woods. it sold slowly at first. now it's one of the most read american books ever.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walden","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/205","why":"walden and civil disobedience at project gutenberg"}],"name":"walden","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["walden pond"],"key":"waldenpond","kind":"place","lines":"a deep, clear pond in the woods about a mile and a half south of concord, massachusetts. thoreau built his cabin on its shore on land emerson owned, and lived there from 1845 to 1847.\nit's a state park now, and a replica of the cabin stands near the parking lot. on a hot day it's full of swimmers.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walden_Pond","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"walden pond","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["civil disobedience","resistance to civil government"],"key":"civildisobedience","kind":"book","lines":"thoreau's 1849 essay, first called resistance to civil government, about why he went to jail rather than pay a tax to a government that backed slavery and was fighting a war with mexico. its argument is that when the law makes you do wrong, you break the law, openly, and take the punishment.\ngandhi read it in south africa and martin luther king read it in college. it's one of the most traveled essays ever written. the phrase itself now just means breaking an unjust law on purpose, in public, without violence.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_Disobedience_(Thoreau)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"civil disobedience","status":"approved","threads":["nonviolence","prison"]},{"aliases":["poll tax","back poll tax"],"key":"polltax","kind":"word","lines":"a flat tax everyone pays just for existing, the same amount rich or poor. in thoreau's massachusetts it was charged to every man over twenty.\nlater, in the american south, poll taxes were used to keep black people from voting, because you had to pay to vote. that was banned in the 1960s. thoreau's was a different use, but the name's the same.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poll_tax","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"poll tax","status":"approved","threads":["prison"]},{"aliases":["war with mexico","mexican-american war","mexican war"],"key":"mexicanwar","kind":"event","lines":"the war the united states fought against mexico from 1846 to 1848. the us won and took about half of mexico's land, including what's now california, texas and most of the southwest.\nplenty of northerners, thoreau among them, saw it as a land grab to spread slavery into new territory. a young congressman named abraham lincoln spoke against it too.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican%E2%80%93American_War","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the war with mexico","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["a week on the concord and merrimack rivers","week on the concord and merrimack rivers"],"key":"aweek","kind":"book","lines":"thoreau's first book, from 1849, about a boat trip he'd taken with his brother john in 1839, written after john died. he paid for the printing. it flopped, and the publisher sent back over seven hundred unsold copies.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Week_on_the_Concord_and_Merrimack_Rivers","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a week on the concord and merrimack rivers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john brown","john brown's"],"key":"johnbrown","kind":"person","lines":"a white abolitionist, someone fighting to end slavery, who believed god wanted it ended by force. in october 1859 he and about twenty men seized the federal armory at harpers ferry, virginia, hoping to arm enslaved people for an uprising. it failed within two days. he was hanged that december.\nmost northerners called him a madman. thoreau stood up in concord and defended him. eighteen months later the civil war started, and union soldiers marched singing john brown's body.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Brown_(abolitionist)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john brown","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["harpers ferry","harper's ferry"],"key":"harpersferry","kind":"place","lines":"a small town where two rivers meet in what's now west virginia, where the us government had an armory full of rifles. john brown's raid on it in 1859 is one of the sparks of the civil war.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Brown%27s_raid_on_Harpers_Ferry","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"harpers ferry","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["abolitionist","abolitionists"],"key":"abolitionist","kind":"word","lines":"someone who fought to abolish slavery, to end it outright, not just limit it. in the america of the 1840s and 1850s that was a radical position, even in the north.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abolitionism_in_the_United_States","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"abolitionist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["charles wilkins"],"key":"wilkins","kind":"person","lines":"an english printer and scholar in india who made the first english translation of the bhagavad gita, in 1785. that's the copy thoreau read at walden.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Wilkins","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"charles wilkins","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["indian opinion"],"key":"indianopinion","kind":"book","lines":"the newspaper gandhi started in south africa in 1903 to speak for the indians living there. in 1907 he printed extracts from thoreau's civil disobedience in it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Opinion","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"indian opinion","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["durban"],"key":"durban","kind":"place","lines":"a port city on the east coast of south africa, home to a big indian community. gandhi lived and worked there and nearby for years, and ran his newspaper from a farm outside it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durban","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"durban","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["morehouse college","morehouse"],"key":"morehouse","kind":"place","lines":"a college for black men in atlanta, founded in 1867. martin luther king started there at fifteen and read thoreau's civil disobedience there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morehouse_College","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"morehouse college","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["stride toward freedom"],"key":"stridetowardfreedom","kind":"book","lines":"martin luther king's 1958 book about the montgomery bus boycott. the chapter pilgrimage to nonviolence is where he tells how he got his ideas, thoreau and gandhi among them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stride_Toward_Freedom","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"stride toward freedom","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bronson alcott","amos bronson alcott"],"key":"alcott","kind":"person","lines":"a teacher, dreamer and talker in the concord circle, always broke, known for experimental schools and a commune that failed within a year. he's the father of louisa may alcott.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amos_Bronson_Alcott","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bronson alcott","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["louisa may alcott"],"key":"louisamayalcott","kind":"person","lines":"the concord writer who wrote little women, the 1868 novel about four sisters that's never been out of print. she supported her whole family with her writing.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louisa_May_Alcott","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"louisa may alcott","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["harrison blake"],"key":"harrisonblake","kind":"person","lines":"a schoolteacher in worcester, massachusetts, who wrote to thoreau in 1848 and kept up with him by letter until thoreau died. thoreau wrote him some of his best letters, including the one about meeting whitman.","links":[],"name":"harrison blake","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["leaves of grass"],"key":"leavesofgrass","kind":"book","lines":"walt whitman's book of poems, first printed at his own expense in 1855 with twelve poems, no author's name on the title page, and a picture of him in work clothes. it sounded like nothing before it, long loose lines, the body and sex and the whole country crammed in.\nhe spent the rest of his life adding to it and rewriting it. the last edition, which he approved from his deathbed in 1892, had about four hundred poems. it's the root of most american poetry since.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaves_of_Grass","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leaves of grass","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["song of myself"],"key":"songofmyself","kind":"book","lines":"the long poem at the heart of leaves of grass. whitman lies in the grass and celebrates himself, and then everybody, the slave, the prostitute, the president, the dying, the newborn, as if they were all him.\nit's where do i contradict myself, very well then i contradict myself, i am large, i contain multitudes, comes from. it ends with him telling you to look for him under your boot soles.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_of_Myself","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45477/song-of-myself-1892-version","why":"the poem, free online"}],"name":"song of myself","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["civil war","the civil war","american civil war"],"key":"civilwar","kind":"event","lines":"the war in the united states from 1861 to 1865 between the northern states, the union, and eleven southern states that broke away to keep slavery, the confederacy. somewhere between six hundred and seven hundred and fifty thousand soldiers died, more americans than in any other war.\nthe north won, slavery was ended, and lincoln was shot five days after the south surrendered. whitman spent the war in the army hospitals of washington.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Civil_War","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the civil war","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["fredericksburg"],"key":"fredericksburg","kind":"place","lines":"a town in virginia where, in december 1862, the union army threw wave after wave of men against confederates dug in on a hill, and lost badly. whitman's brother george was wounded there, and walt went down to find him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fredericksburg","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"fredericksburg","status":"approved","threads":["war","dying"]},{"aliases":["abraham lincoln","lincoln","lincoln's"],"key":"lincoln","kind":"person","lines":"president of the united states through the civil war, from 1861 until he was shot at a theater in washington in april 1865, days after the war ended. he freed the enslaved people in the rebel states in 1863 and pushed through the amendment that ended slavery.\nwhitman saw him ride past in the streets of washington often, and they'd nod to each other. when lincoln was killed whitman wrote two of his most famous poems about it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Lincoln","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"abraham lincoln","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["when lilacs last in the dooryard bloom'd","when lilacs last"],"key":"lilacs","kind":"book","lines":"whitman's long elegy for lincoln, from 1865. it follows the coffin as the funeral train carries it across the country, with lilacs, a star, and a bird singing in a swamp. it's one of the great grief poems in english.","links":[{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45480/when-lilacs-last-in-the-dooryard-bloomd","why":"the poem, free online"}],"name":"when lilacs last in the dooryard bloom'd","status":"approved","threads":["grief"]},{"aliases":["o captain! my captain!","o captain my captain","o captain! my captain"],"key":"ocaptain","kind":"book","lines":"whitman's short rhymed poem for lincoln, from 1865, the ship home safe and the captain dead on the deck. it's one of the very few whitman poems that rhymes, and schoolkids still recite it. it's the line the students shout in dead poets society.","links":[{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45474/o-captain-my-captain","why":"the poem, free online"}],"name":"o captain! my captain!","status":"approved","threads":["grief"]},{"aliases":["peter doyle"],"key":"peterdoyle","kind":"person","lines":"an irish born streetcar conductor in washington, a confederate veteran, whom whitman met in 1865 when doyle was about twenty one. they were close for years, and whitman's letters to him are tender. most people now think he was the great love of whitman's life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Doyle_(transit_worker)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"peter doyle","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["camden","camden, new jersey","mickle street"],"key":"camden","kind":"place","lines":"a city across the delaware river from philadelphia. whitman moved there after his stroke in 1873 and bought a small house on mickle street in 1884, where visitors came from all over to see the old poet in his cluttered front room. it's a museum now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walt_Whitman_House","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"camden","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["james harlan"],"key":"harlan","kind":"person","lines":"a senator from iowa who became secretary of the interior in 1865, found a copy of leaves of grass in whitman's desk, read it, and fired him for it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Harlan_(senator)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"james harlan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["good gray poet","the good gray poet"],"key":"goodgraypoet","kind":"word","lines":"the nickname a friend of whitman's, william o'connor, gave him in an angry 1866 pamphlet defending him after he was fired. it stuck, and turned the wild poet of the body into a kindly old grandfather in the public eye.","links":[],"name":"the good gray poet","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john addington symonds","symonds"],"key":"symonds","kind":"person","lines":"an english writer and scholar, and an early defender of gay men when that could put you in prison. he wrote to whitman for years asking what the love between comrades poems meant. in 1890 whitman answered by claiming six illegitimate children.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Addington_Symonds","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john addington symonds","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["specimen days"],"key":"specimendays","kind":"book","lines":"whitman's 1882 prose memoir, a loose collection of diary pieces. the best parts are his notes from the army hospitals in the civil war, and his recovery after the stroke, sitting by a creek in new jersey.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Specimen_Days","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8813","why":"his complete prose works, specimen days included, at project gutenberg"}],"name":"specimen days","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["deathbed edition","the deathbed edition"],"key":"deathbededition","kind":"book","lines":"the last edition of leaves of grass, from 1891 and 1892, which whitman approved from his bed in camden weeks before he died. about four hundred poems. it's the version most people read.","links":[],"name":"the deathbed edition","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["library of congress","the library of congress"],"key":"libraryofcongress","kind":"place","lines":"america's national library in washington, the biggest library in the world. it holds a huge collection of whitman's papers, including, as it turns out, the cardboard butterfly.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Congress","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the library of congress","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["boston common"],"key":"bostoncommon","kind":"place","lines":"the big old park in the middle of boston, the oldest city park in america. emerson walked whitman up and down it in 1860 trying to get him to cut the sex poems. whitman listened and said no.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boston_Common","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"boston common","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["provincetown"],"key":"provincetown","kind":"place","lines":"an old fishing town at the very tip of cape cod, massachusetts, where the pilgrims first landed in 1620, before plymouth. since the early 1900s it's been an artists' and writers' colony and a gay resort town.\nmary oliver lived there for about forty years with molly malone cook, and walked its woods, ponds and dunes nearly every morning.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provincetown,_Massachusetts","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"provincetown","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cape cod"],"key":"capecod","kind":"place","lines":"a long hooked peninsula sticking out into the atlantic from massachusetts, beaches, dunes, ponds and pine woods, summer houses and old fishing towns.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Cod","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cape cod","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["edna st. vincent millay","edna st vincent millay","millay","millay's"],"key":"millay","kind":"person","lines":"one of the most famous american poets of the twenties, a bohemian in new york's greenwich village, known for burning the candle at both ends, which is her line. she won the pulitzer in 1923 and died in 1950.\na teenage mary oliver wrote to millay's sister, and ended up living at the family place, steepletop, for years, helping sort the papers.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edna_St._Vincent_Millay","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/edna-st-vincent-millay","why":"her poems and life"}],"name":"edna st. vincent millay","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["steepletop"],"key":"steepletop","kind":"place","lines":"edna st. vincent millay's farmhouse in the hills of upstate new york, near the massachusetts line. mary oliver lived there as a young woman helping millay's sister with the papers, and met molly malone cook there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steepletop","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"steepletop","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["molly malone cook","molly"],"key":"mollymalonecook","kind":"person","lines":"an american photographer who opened one of the first photography galleries in the country, in provincetown. she was mary oliver's partner for over forty years, handled the business of the world so mary could write, and died in 2005.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molly_Malone_Cook","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"molly malone cook","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["pulitzer prize","the pulitzer prize","pulitzer","the pulitzer"],"key":"pulitzer","kind":"word","lines":"the best known american prize for writing, given every spring by columbia university in the name of joseph pulitzer, a newspaper owner. there are prizes for news reporting, novels, history, music and poetry.\nmary oliver won the poetry prize in 1984. gary snyder won it in 1975.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pulitzer_Prize","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the pulitzer prize","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["american primitive"],"key":"americanprimitive","kind":"book","lines":"mary oliver's 1983 book of poems, which won the pulitzer prize the next year. poems about animals, ponds, and the body in the woods.","links":[],"name":"american primitive","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vassar","vassar college"],"key":"vassar","kind":"place","lines":"a private college in poughkeepsie, new york, for women only until 1969. mary oliver went there for a year and didn't finish.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vassar_College","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"vassar","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the summer day"],"key":"summerday","kind":"book","lines":"mary oliver's most quoted poem, from 1990. she looks at a grasshopper eating sugar out of her hand, wonders what a prayer is, and ends asking what you plan to do with your one wild and precious life.\nit's on mugs, graduation cards and funeral programs. it's better than the mug.","links":[],"name":"the summer day","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wild geese"],"key":"wildgeese","kind":"book","lines":"a short mary oliver poem from her 1986 book dream work. it starts you do not have to be good, and tells you that the world offers itself to your imagination whatever you've done. a lot of people have had it read to them at the worst moment of their lives.","links":[],"name":"wild geese","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["when death comes"],"key":"whendeathcomes","kind":"book","lines":"a mary oliver poem about meeting death curious instead of scared, and not wanting to end up having simply visited this world.","links":[],"name":"when death comes","status":"approved","threads":["dying"]},{"aliases":["new and selected poems"],"key":"newandselected","kind":"book","lines":"mary oliver's collection of poems from her earlier books plus new ones, the first volume from 1992, which won the national book award. it's the easiest place to start.","links":[],"name":"new and selected poems","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dog songs"],"key":"dogsongs","kind":"book","lines":"mary oliver's 2013 book of poems and one essay about the dogs she loved, especially one named percy.","links":[],"name":"dog songs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["my friend walt whitman"],"key":"myfriendwalt","kind":"book","lines":"an essay by mary oliver about how, as a lonely kid in ohio, she took whitman into the woods and he was the friend she needed. it's in her books blue pastures and upstream.","links":[],"name":"my friend walt whitman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["blue pastures"],"key":"bluepastures","kind":"book","lines":"mary oliver's 1995 book of short essays on writing, nature and the poets she loved.","links":[],"name":"blue pastures","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lakota","lakotas","the lakota","sioux"],"key":"lakota","kind":"group","lines":"a native american nation of the northern plains, buffalo hunters on horseback, the people americans called the sioux. they're one of the three great branches of the sioux, and the lakota themselves are made up of seven bands.\nthey beat the us army more than once, and lost anyway. today most live on reservations in south and north dakota, pine ridge among them. sitting bull and crazy horse were lakota.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakota_people","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the lakota","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["oglala","oglalas","oglala lakota","oglala sioux"],"key":"oglala","kind":"group","lines":"one of the seven bands of the lakota. crazy horse and black elk were oglala. most oglala today live on the pine ridge reservation in south dakota.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oglala_Lakota","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the oglala","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["little bighorn","the little bighorn","battle of the little bighorn","custer's last stand","little big horn"],"key":"littlebighorn","kind":"event","lines":"the battle in june 1876 in montana where lakota, cheyenne and arapaho warriors wiped out five companies of the us seventh cavalry under george custer, over two hundred men. americans called it custer's last stand.\nit was the biggest win the plains nations ever had over the army, and it didn't save them. within a year most of them were forced onto reservations. black elk was there, at twelve.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Little_Bighorn","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the battle of the little bighorn","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["george custer","custer","custer's"],"key":"custer","kind":"person","lines":"a flashy, reckless us cavalry officer who became a union general in the civil war at twenty three. he attacked a huge native camp on the little bighorn river in 1876 without waiting for support, and he and every man with him died.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Armstrong_Custer","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"george custer","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["cheyenne"],"key":"cheyenne","kind":"group","lines":"a plains nation, allies of the lakota, who fought beside them at the little bighorn. today they live mostly in montana and oklahoma.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheyenne","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the cheyenne","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["six grandfathers","the six grandfathers"],"key":"sixgrandfathers","kind":"word","lines":"in black elk's great vision, the six powers of the world, the four directions, the sky above and the earth below, who show the nine year old boy the life of his people. it's also the lakota name for the mountain in the black hills that got carved into mount rushmore.","links":[],"name":"the six grandfathers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sacred hoop","the sacred hoop","the nation's hoop"],"key":"sacredhoop","kind":"word","lines":"in black elk's vision, the circle of his people's life, all the camps and families as one nation, with a flowering tree at the center. the hoop being broken is his picture of what happened to them.","links":[],"name":"the sacred hoop","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["buffalo bill","buffalo bill's wild west","buffalo bill cody","wild west show"],"key":"buffalobill","kind":"person","lines":"william cody, a former buffalo hunter and army scout who became the biggest showman in the world. his buffalo bill's wild west was a traveling show of cowboys, sharpshooters, stagecoach robberies and real native americans acting out battles, playing to crowds of thousands across america and europe.\nsitting bull toured with it for a season. black elk joined in 1886 to see the white world, and performed for queen victoria in london.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo_Bill","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"buffalo bill","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["queen victoria"],"key":"queenvictoria","kind":"person","lines":"the queen of britain from 1837 to 1901, the head of an empire that covered a quarter of the world. she came to see buffalo bill's show in 1887, her jubilee year. the lakota called her grandmother england.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Victoria","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"queen victoria","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wounded knee","wounded knee massacre","wounded knee creek"],"key":"woundedknee","kind":"event","lines":"on december 29, 1890, at wounded knee creek on the pine ridge reservation in south dakota, us soldiers disarming a band of lakota opened fire. somewhere around two hundred and fifty to three hundred lakota were killed, many of them women and children, some chased down miles away. twenty soldiers got medals of honor for it.\nit's taken as the end of the plains wars. black elk rode there as it was happening. in 1973 activists occupied the site for seventy one days to protest how native people were still being treated.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wounded_Knee_massacre","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"wounded knee","status":"approved","threads":["war","dying"]},{"aliases":["pine ridge","pine ridge reservation","the reservation"],"key":"pineridge","kind":"place","lines":"the pine ridge reservation in south dakota, the oglala lakota's land. a reservation is land the us government set aside for a native nation by treaty, usually after taking the rest, where the tribe has its own government. pine ridge is one of the poorest places in the united states.\nblack elk lived there most of his life, and wounded knee is on it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_Ridge_Indian_Reservation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pine ridge","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["catechist","catechists"],"key":"catechist","kind":"word","lines":"a lay teacher in the catholic church, not a priest, who teaches the faith to others and helps run things where there's no priest. black elk did it for decades, traveling from reservation to reservation.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catechesis","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"catechist","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["john neihardt","neihardt","neihardt's"],"key":"neihardt","kind":"person","lines":"a white poet from nebraska, the state's poet laureate, who went to pine ridge in 1931 and had black elk tell him his life through his son ben as interpreter. black elk speaks, from 1932, is the book.\nneihardt shaped it, and in places wrote lines black elk never said, including the most famous ones, and left out that black elk had been a catholic teacher for decades. it's a great book and it's partly his.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Neihardt","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"john neihardt","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["black elk speaks"],"key":"blackelkspeaks","kind":"book","lines":"the 1932 book of black elk's life and great vision, as told to john neihardt. it sold little at first, then in the sixties and seventies it became a bible of the counterculture and the native american revival.\nit's read now knowing neihardt's hand is on it. raymond demallie's the sixth grandfather prints the actual interview notes, so you can see which is which.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Elk_Speaks","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"black elk speaks","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the sixth grandfather","sixth grandfather"],"key":"sixthgrandfather","kind":"book","lines":"raymond demallie's 1984 book that prints the notes of black elk's interviews with neihardt, so you can see what black elk actually said and what neihardt added.","links":[],"name":"the sixth grandfather","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["raymond demallie","demallie"],"key":"demallie","kind":"person","lines":"an american scholar of the lakota who spent his career at indiana university. he's the one who showed which lines in black elk speaks are neihardt's.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_DeMallie","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"raymond demallie","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["joseph epes brown"],"key":"josephbrown","kind":"person","lines":"a young white scholar who lived with black elk in 1947 and wrote down his account of the seven sacred rites of the lakota. that became the sacred pipe, in 1953.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Epes_Brown","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"joseph epes brown","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the sacred pipe","sacred pipe"],"key":"sacredpipe","kind":"book","lines":"black elk's account of the seven sacred rites of the lakota, the sweat lodge, the vision quest, the sun dance and the rest, as told to joseph epes brown and published in 1953. it's closer to his own voice than black elk speaks.","links":[],"name":"the sacred pipe","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sun dance","the sun dance"],"key":"sundance","kind":"event","lines":"the great summer ceremony of the plains nations. people fast and dance for days around a sacred tree cut and set up in the middle of the camp, praying for the people, and some pierce their chests and tie themselves to the tree until they tear free.\nthe us government banned it for decades. it was danced in secret and came back in the open in the twentieth century.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Dance","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sun dance","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["sweat lodge","sweat lodges"],"key":"sweatlodge","kind":"word","lines":"a low dome of bent willow covered in hides or blankets, dark inside, with red hot stones in a pit. water on the stones makes steam, and people sit in the heat and pray. it's for cleansing, before other ceremonies or just to start over.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweat_lodge","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sweat lodge","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vision quest","vision quests"],"key":"visionquest","kind":"word","lines":"a lakota rite where a person goes alone to a hilltop and fasts, usually for a few days, praying for a vision to guide their life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vision_quest","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"vision quest","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["horse dance","the horse dance"],"key":"horsedance","kind":"event","lines":"the ceremony in which black elk, in his late teens, acted out part of his great vision in front of the whole camp, with riders on horses of different colors standing for the powers he'd seen. after it, he said, the fear left him.","links":[],"name":"the horse dance","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["harney peak","black elk peak"],"key":"harneypeak","kind":"place","lines":"the highest mountain in the black hills of south dakota, where in his vision black elk stood at the center of the world. he went back up it with neihardt in 1931 to pray. in 2016 it was officially renamed black elk peak.\nthe name harney came from an army general who'd led a massacre of lakota in 1855, which is why people pushed to change it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Elk_Peak","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"harney peak","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["black hills","the black hills"],"key":"blackhills","kind":"place","lines":"a small range of dark, pine covered mountains in western south dakota, the holiest ground of the lakota. a treaty in 1868 guaranteed it to them. then gold was found, and the us took it. mount rushmore is carved into it.\nin 1980 the supreme court ruled the land was taken illegally and awarded money. the lakota refused it, because they want the land back. the money is still sitting in an account, now over a billion dollars.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Hills","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the black hills","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["cause for sainthood","declaring him a saint","canonization"],"key":"sainthood","kind":"word","lines":"the long catholic process for officially declaring someone a saint. first the local bishop opens a cause and investigates their life, then rome reviews it, and usually two miracles have to be credited to prayers to them. it can take decades or centuries. black elk's opened in 2017.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonization","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cause for sainthood","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wakan tanka","wakȟáŋ tȟáŋka","great mystery","the great mystery","great spirit","the great spirit"],"key":"wakantanka","kind":"word","lines":"the lakota and dakota name for the holy, the sacred power in everything. it gets translated as the great spirit, and sometimes the great mystery, and the second is closer. it isn't one old man in the sky. it's more like the whole sacred order of things, all of it at once.\nwakan means holy, or mysterious, or powerful in a way you can't explain. tanka means great. when a lakota prayer says the name, it's pointing at something too big to picture.\ncharles eastman called it the great mystery in his books for white readers, on purpose, so they'd stop imagining a bearded god.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wakan_Tanka","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"wakan tanka","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["wičháša wakȟáŋ","wicasa wakan","holy man","holy men","holy woman"],"key":"wicasawakan","kind":"word","lines":"the lakota words for a holy man, someone who prays, dreams, and does ceremony on behalf of the people. wičháša means man, wakȟáŋ means holy or mysterious.\nit isn't a job you apply for. it usually starts with a vision, and the community recognizing it, and years of learning from an older holy man. black elk, lame deer and fools crow were all called this.\nwhite people have mostly said medicine man instead, which only gets at the healing part.","links":[],"name":"wičháša wakȟáŋ, holy man","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["medicine man","medicine men","medicine woman","medicine people"],"key":"medicineman","kind":"word","lines":"the outsider's word, the one white americans used for any native religious leader or healer. it lumps together very different things, a lakota holy man, a navajo singer, an ojibwe herbalist, as if they were all the same job.\neach nation has its own words and its own roles. on these pages we use the nation's word first and say what it means. native people sometimes use medicine man themselves in english, because it's the word everybody knows, and lame deer did.","links":[],"name":"medicine man","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hataałii","hatááłii","hataalii","navajo singer","navajo singers"],"key":"hataalii","kind":"word","lines":"the navajo word for a ceremonial healer, usually translated singer, because the heart of the work is long sacred chants, some of which go on for days. a hataałii learns them from an older singer over years, by memory.\nthe idea is that illness comes from being out of balance, and the singer's ceremony, held in a hogan, the round navajo house, brings the power of the holy people to the patient to restore harmony. it's a whole medical and religious tradition, not a guy with herbs.\nit's a good example of why medicine man is a lazy word. a lakota holy man and a navajo singer are about as alike as a pastor and a surgeon.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo_medicine","why":"navajo healing and the singers who do it"}],"name":"hataałii, navajo singer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["heyoka","heyókȟa","heyokas","sacred clown","sacred clowns"],"key":"heyoka","kind":"word","lines":"a lakota sacred clown. a person who dreams of the wakinyan, the thunder beings, has to become a heyoka whether they want to or not, and heyokas do everything backwards. they shiver in a heat wave and complain they're stuffed when there's no food.\nit's funny and it's meant to be. the laughing breaks people open, and the backwardness holds up a mirror. lame deer was one, and said a clown's work is sacred.\nthink of a holy fool, or the comic at a funeral who's the only one telling the truth.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heyoka","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"heyoka, the sacred clown","status":"approved","threads":["joy"]},{"aliases":["wakinyan","wakíŋyaŋ","thunder beings","the thunder beings"],"key":"wakinyan","kind":"word","lines":"the thunder beings of lakota belief, the powers in the storm, often pictured as great birds whose wings make thunder and whose eyes flash lightning. they live in the west.\nto dream of them is a big deal, and a hard one. it means you have to become a heyoka, a sacred clown, and act out your vision in public, backwards, so the people can see it.","links":[],"name":"wakinyan, the thunder beings","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hanbleceya","hanblečheya","hanblechia","crying for a vision"],"key":"hanbleceya","kind":"word","lines":"the lakota name for what english calls the vision quest. it means something like crying for a vision. a person, usually young, goes alone to a hilltop, often a pit dug there, with a holy man's guidance, and fasts and prays for days, waiting for a vision to guide their life.\nwhat happens up there is between the person, the holy man, and the powers. it's one of the seven sacred rites black elk described. lame deer went up for his at sixteen.\nthe english phrase vision quest has been borrowed by weekend retreats and summer camps. the real one isn't a retreat. people come down changed or they don't come down with anything.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vision_quest","why":"the plain overview, and why the english term is loose"}],"name":"hanbleceya, crying for a vision","status":"approved","threads":["silence","the body"]},{"aliases":["inipi","inípi"],"key":"inipi","kind":"word","lines":"the lakota name for the sweat lodge ceremony, a purification in a low dome of bent willow covered in blankets or hides, dark inside, with red hot stones in a pit. water on the stones makes steam, and people pray in the heat.\nit's for cleansing and starting over, and it often comes before other ceremonies, like the vision quest or the sun dance. it's led by someone who knows what they're doing.\nin 2009 a self-help guru named james arthur ray ran a fake sweat lodge in arizona for paying customers, and three people died. lakota leaders said plainly that it had nothing to do with their ceremony.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweat_lodge","why":"the plain overview, including the 2009 deaths"}],"name":"inipi, the sweat lodge","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["čhaŋnúŋpa","chanunpa","chanupa","the pipe","sacred pipes","ceremonial pipe","pipe ceremony"],"key":"chanunpa","kind":"word","lines":"the sacred pipe of the lakota and many other nations. it's not for smoking the way people smoke cigarettes. the smoke carries prayers up, and smoking it together is a promise, which is why treaties and truths were sealed with it.\nthe lakota say white buffalo calf woman, a holy woman who came to the people long ago, brought the pipe and taught them how to pray with it. the bowl is often red stone from the pipestone quarry in minnesota.\nblack elk's second book, the sacred pipe, is about the ceremonies that go with it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ceremonial_pipe","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the pipe, čhaŋnúŋpa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sacred tobacco","tobacco offering","tobacco offerings","tobacco ties"],"key":"tobacco","kind":"word","lines":"for a lot of native nations tobacco is a sacred plant, one of the medicines, and not the stuff in a pack of marlboros. you offer it when you ask an elder for help or teaching, you leave it when you take something from the land, and you put it in the pipe to carry prayers.\ntobacco ties are little bundles of tobacco tied in cloth, made in strings for ceremonies like the vision quest. commercial tobacco and all the addiction that came with it is a different thing, and a lot of native health programs say so plainly.","links":[],"name":"sacred tobacco","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mitákuye oyás'iŋ","mitakuye oyasin","all my relatives","all my relations"],"key":"mitakuyeoyasin","kind":"word","lines":"a lakota phrase, all my relatives. you say it at the end of a prayer, or going in and out of the sweat lodge, like an amen.\nrelatives means everybody. your family, your nation, other nations, the animals, the plants, the rocks, the sky. it's a one line theology. everything is kin, and you're answerable to all of it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitakuye_Oyasin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mitákuye oyás'iŋ, all my relatives","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["seven grandfather teachings","the seven grandfather teachings","seven grandfathers","the seven grandfathers"],"key":"sevengrandfathers","kind":"word","lines":"a set of seven teachings from the anishinaabe peoples of the great lakes, among them the ojibwe and the potawatomi. wisdom, love, respect, bravery, honesty, humility and truth, each tied to an animal, the beaver, the eagle, the buffalo, the bear, the raven, the wolf and the turtle.\nthey show up on posters in a lot of native schools and community centers now. not lakota, so don't confuse them with black elk's six grandfathers, which are the powers in his vision.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Grandfathers","why":"the seven teachings and their animals"}],"name":"the seven grandfather teachings","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["turtle island"],"key":"turtleisland","kind":"place","lines":"a name for north america, or for the whole earth, used by many native peoples of the east and the great lakes. it comes from creation stories where the world was made on the back of a great turtle, after a woman fell from the sky and animals brought up mud from the bottom of the water for her to stand on.\nrobin wall kimmerer tells that story at the start of braiding sweetgrass. activists use the name now to say this continent had a name before columbus. in the forest it's the name of the native grove.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turtle_Island_(North_America)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"turtle island","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["red road","the red road"],"key":"redroad","kind":"word","lines":"a phrase a lot of native people use in english for living a good life, the right path, sober, prayerful, and connected to your people and ceremonies. it comes from plains teaching, and black elk speaks uses it, where the good red road runs north and south.\nyou'll hear it a lot in native recovery circles, where walking the red road means getting and staying sober the native way. it's close to what someone in twelve step means by working the program.","links":[],"name":"the red road","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["seven council fires","the seven council fires","očhéthi šakówiŋ","oceti sakowin"],"key":"sevencouncilfires","kind":"group","lines":"the seven nations of the people americans called the sioux. in their own language, očhéthi šakówiŋ. four eastern dakota nations, the santee, two western dakota, the yankton and yanktonai, and the lakota, also called the teton, who are the biggest and are split into seven bands of their own, the oglala, hunkpapa and sicangu among them.\nso when a page says sitting bull was hunkpapa lakota, that's like saying someone is from a particular town in a particular state of one big country. dakota and lakota both mean friends, allies. at standing rock in 2016 one of the big protest camps took the name oceti sakowin.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sioux","why":"the whole people, the three branches and the seven fires"}],"name":"the seven council fires","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ghost dance","the ghost dance","ghost dancers","ghost shirts","ghost shirt"],"key":"ghostdance","kind":"event","lines":"a religious movement that swept the western reservations in 1889 and 1890, started by wovoka, a paiute holy man in nevada, after a vision during an eclipse. dance the round dance, live clean, don't fight, and the dead will come back and the old world will return.\namong the lakota it took on a harder edge. dancers wore ghost shirts they believed bullets couldn't pierce, and some said the whites would be swept away. the government saw a war coming. it sent troops, killed sitting bull during an arrest, and two weeks later massacred hundreds at wounded knee.\nthe dance went quiet after that, but it never fully stopped.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dance","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the ghost dance","status":"approved","threads":["grief"]},{"aliases":["peyote road","the peyote road","road man","roadman","road chief","peyote meeting","peyote meetings"],"key":"peyoteroad","kind":"word","lines":"the name for the all-night prayer meetings of the native american church, and the way of life that goes with them. people sit in a circle in a tipi around a fire and a crescent shaped altar from night to dawn, eat peyote, a small cactus with mescaline in it, sing, drum, and pray. the leader is called the road man, because he leads the people along the road through the night.\nfor the people in it, peyote isn't a drug trip. it's a sacrament, like communion wine, and the meeting is church. a lot of members say it helped them quit drinking. it's legal for members under federal law.\nquanah parker, the comanche chief, is the most famous early road man.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peyote","why":"the cactus itself, its legal protection, and why it's getting scarce"}],"name":"the peyote road","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["native american church","the native american church"],"key":"nativeamericanchurch","kind":"group","lines":"the church of the peyote road, the biggest native religion in the united states, with a few hundred thousand members in the us, canada and mexico. it was incorporated in oklahoma in 1918, to give the peyote meetings legal protection, and quanah parker is counted among its founders.\nit mixes native ceremony with christianity, some branches more than others. the half moon way keeps the bible out and uses tobacco. the cross fire way reads the bible and doesn't.\nthe government and a lot of christian reformers, and some native leaders like zitkala-ša and charles eastman, fought to ban peyote for decades. a supreme court case in 1990 went against the church, and congress protected it by law in 1994.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_American_Church","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the native american church","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["code of handsome lake","the code of handsome lake","gaiwiio","gaihwi:io","the good message"],"key":"gaiwiio","kind":"book","lines":"the teaching handsome lake, a seneca prophet, preached after his visions in 1799, called the gaiwiio, the good message. no whiskey, no witchcraft, no beating your wife or leaving your family, no selling land, and keep the old thanksgiving ceremonies.\nit's not a book in the usual sense. it's recited from memory by trained speakers in the longhouse, over three mornings, at the big midwinter ceremony and at a gathering of the six nations in the fall. a seneca anthropologist named arthur c. parker published an english version in 1913.\npicture the big book of alcoholics anonymous, if it were spoken aloud every year by the elders and also held your whole culture together.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longhouse_Religion","why":"the religion the code became"}],"name":"the code of handsome lake","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["longhouse","longhouses","the longhouse","longhouse religion","the longhouse religion"],"key":"longhouse","kind":"place","lines":"a longhouse was the traditional haudenosaunee home, a long bark building where several related families lived together along a central hall with fires down the middle. the haudenosaunee name for themselves means people who build the longhouse.\ntoday the longhouse also means the building where the traditional religion meets, and the religion itself, the one that grew from handsome lake's code. when someone says they're longhouse, they mean they follow the old way rather than a church.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longhouse","why":"the building"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longhouse_Religion","why":"the religion"}],"name":"the longhouse","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sun dance ban","banned the sun dance","code of indian offenses","religious crimes code","courts of indian offenses"],"key":"sundanceban","kind":"event","lines":"in 1883 the us government set up rules for the reservations, the code of indian offenses, that outlawed the sun dance and other ceremonies and let government agents punish people for practicing their religion. it was part of the plan to make native people give up being native.\nfor about fifty years, people who danced the sun dance or held other ceremonies could lose their rations or go to jail. they did it anyway, in secret, on remote parts of the reservations, or in cut down versions the agents would allow. the government started backing off in the 1930s, and the american indian religious freedom act of 1978 finally said out loud that native religion was protected.\nzitkala-ša wrote an opera about the sun dance in 1913, while it was still illegal.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Dance","why":"the ceremony that was banned"}],"name":"the sun dance ban","status":"approved","threads":["the body"]},{"aliases":["american indian religious freedom act","religious freedom act","airfa"],"key":"airfa","kind":"event","lines":"a law congress passed in august 1978 saying native americans had the right to practice their own religions, visit sacred sites, use sacred objects, and hold ceremonies. it sounds obvious. it took until 1978.\nit didn't have much teeth at first. in 1990 the supreme court ruled that oregon could deny unemployment benefits to two native american church members who'd been fired for using peyote in a ceremony, and in 1994 congress amended the law to protect peyote use in ceremonies outright.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Indian_Religious_Freedom_Act","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the american indian religious freedom act","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["powwow","powwows","pow wow","pow wows"],"key":"powwow","kind":"event","lines":"a big public gathering of dancing, drumming and singing, open to visitors, held by native communities all over north america. dancers in regalia, which is what you call it, not a costume, dance in a circle around big drum groups, and there are contests with prize money.\nit's a social thing and a family reunion and a celebration more than a religious ceremony, though prayer runs through it. richard twiss brought the powwow drum and dancing into christian worship, which a lot of churches hated.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powwow","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"powwow","status":"approved","threads":["joy"]},{"aliases":["yuwipi"],"key":"yuwipi","kind":"word","lines":"a lakota healing and finding ceremony, held at night in a completely dark room, led by a holy man, for someone who's sick or has lost something. frank fools crow was known for it.\nwhat happens inside belongs to the people who are there. it's for asking the spirits for help, and people come out of it saying they got some.","links":[],"name":"yuwipi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["counted coup","counting coup","count coup","coup stick"],"key":"coup","kind":"word","lines":"on the plains, the bravest thing you could do in a fight wasn't killing an enemy. it was riding up and touching him, with your hand or a stick, and getting away alive. that was counting coup, and warriors kept count and told their coups at gatherings.\nit's a way of war built around nerve and humiliation more than body count. sitting bull got his name for counting his first coup at fourteen.","links":[],"name":"counting coup","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["tipi","tipis","teepee","teepees"],"key":"tipi","kind":"word","lines":"the cone shaped tent of the plains nations, poles tied together at the top and covered in buffalo hides, later canvas. it could go up or come down in minutes and be dragged by horses, which is what you need if you follow the buffalo.\na hole at the top lets out the smoke, with flaps you adjust to the wind. peyote meetings are held in one.","links":[],"name":"tipi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["boarding schools","boarding school","indian boarding schools","indian boarding school"],"key":"boardingschools","kind":"event","lines":"for about a hundred years, starting in the 1870s, the us government and churches took native children, often by force, and sent them far from home to schools designed to erase who they were. hair cut, own name taken, own language forbidden and beaten out of them, ceremonies banned, and trained for manual work.\nhundreds of these schools ran across the country. lots of children died of disease and neglect, and many are buried in school cemeteries far from home. the whole point was the phrase richard henry pratt used, kill the indian, save the man.\nit's why so many native families lost their languages in a single generation. luther standing bear, zitkala-ša, and robin wall kimmerer's grandfather were all sent. canada did the same thing and called them residential schools.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Indian_boarding_schools","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the indian boarding schools","status":"approved","threads":["family","exile"]},{"aliases":["carlisle","carlisle indian school","carlisle indian industrial school"],"key":"carlisle","kind":"place","lines":"the first and most famous government boarding school for native children, opened in 1879 in an old army barracks in carlisle, pennsylvania, by an army officer named richard henry pratt. more than ten thousand children from about a hundred and forty nations went there before it closed in 1918.\nit was the model for all the others. uniforms, military drill, new english names, no native language. more than a hundred and eighty students are buried in its cemetery, and some families are still bringing their children home. its football team, with jim thorpe, beat the best colleges in the country.\nluther standing bear was in the first class. zitkala-ša taught music there and then wrote against it. robin wall kimmerer's grandfather was sent there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlisle_Indian_Industrial_School","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"carlisle indian industrial school","status":"approved","threads":["family","exile"]},{"aliases":["richard henry pratt","pratt","captain pratt"],"key":"pratt","kind":"person","lines":"an army officer who founded the carlisle indian industrial school in 1879 and ran it for twenty five years. he started by teaching native prisoners of war held in a fort in florida, and decided the answer to what he called the indian problem was to take the children and make them white.\nhe thought of himself as a friend of native people, against the ones who wanted them dead. his famous line is the one about killing the indian to save the man. he fired zitkala-ša for writing honestly about the schools, and was himself forced out in 1904 after attacking the indian bureau.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Henry_Pratt","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"richard henry pratt","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kill the indian, save the man","kill the indian and save the man","kill the indian in him","kill the indian"],"key":"killtheindian","kind":"word","lines":"the motto of the indian boarding schools, from a speech richard henry pratt, carlisle's founder, gave in 1892. his idea was that all the indian in a person should be killed off so the man could be saved, meaning turned into a white american, with english, a trade, a church, and no tribe.\nhe meant it as the kind alternative to killing native people outright. that's what makes it so chilling. it's the whole boarding school system in six words.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Henry_Pratt","why":"the man who said it"}],"name":"kill the indian, save the man","status":"approved","threads":["family","exile"]},{"aliases":["dawes act","the dawes act","general allotment act","allotment"],"key":"dawesact","kind":"event","lines":"an 1887 law that broke up reservation land, which tribes held in common, into private plots for individual families, usually a hundred and sixty acres, and sold off the rest to white settlers. it was sold as making native people into independent farmers.\nwhat it actually did was take land. native nations held about a hundred and thirty eight million acres in 1887 and about forty eight million by 1934, when it was stopped. two thirds of what was left, gone in a lifetime. think of a law that says you each get to own your apartment now, and then sells the building's lobby, roof and parking lot out from under you.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawes_Act","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dawes act","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["trail of tears","the trail of tears"],"key":"trailoftears","kind":"event","lines":"the forced removal, in the 1830s, of five nations of the southeast, the cherokee, muscogee (creek), seminole, chickasaw and choctaw, from their homelands in georgia, alabama, tennessee, florida and mississippi, marched west to indian territory, now oklahoma, so white settlers could take their land.\nthousands died on the way, of cold, disease and hunger. for the cherokee alone, the usual estimate is about four thousand dead in 1838 and 1839. andrew jackson pushed it through. joy harjo's people, the muscogee, were among those marched out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail_of_Tears","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the trail of tears","status":"approved","threads":["exile","grief"]},{"aliases":["indian territory"],"key":"indianterritory","kind":"place","lines":"the land, mostly what's now oklahoma, that the us government set aside in the 1830s as the place to dump native nations it had forced out of the east and later off the plains. it was promised to them forever.\nforever lasted until 1907, when it was folded into the new state of oklahoma. in 2020 the supreme court ruled that much of eastern oklahoma is still legally indian country. chief joseph's people were exiled there, and quanah parker surrendered there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Territory","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"indian territory","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["bureau of indian affairs","the bureau of indian affairs","indian affairs","indian bureau","indian office","indian agent","indian agents","indian service"],"key":"bia","kind":"group","lines":"the federal agency, started in 1824, that runs the government's relationship with native nations. for most of its history it ran the reservations, and each one had an indian agent, a government man who controlled rations, land, schools, and who got arrested.\nfor a century it was the main tool for making native people stop being native. the boarding schools were largely its work. today it's run by native people and does a lot of services, but the history is why activists occupied its headquarters in 1972.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bureau_of_Indian_Affairs","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the bureau of indian affairs","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["fort laramie treaty","treaty of fort laramie","the treaty of 1868","1868 treaty"],"key":"fortlaramie","kind":"event","lines":"the treaty that ended red cloud's war on the northern plains and set aside a great sioux reservation, all of western south dakota, black hills included, for the absolute and undisturbed use of the lakota.\nsix years later custer led an expedition into the black hills, found gold, miners poured in, and in 1877 congress simply took the hills. in 1980 the supreme court agreed they'd been taken illegally and awarded money. the lakota have refused it, and it's grown past a billion dollars sitting in an account, because they want the land.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Fort_Laramie_(1868)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the fort laramie treaty of 1868","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["great sioux reservation","the great sioux reservation"],"key":"greatsiouxreservation","kind":"place","lines":"the huge reservation the 1868 fort laramie treaty set aside for the lakota, all of south dakota west of the missouri river, black hills included.\nthe government took the black hills in 1877, and in 1889 broke what was left into five smaller reservations, pine ridge, rosebud, standing rock, cheyenne river and lower brule, and opened about half the land to white settlers at a dollar and a quarter an acre. sitting bull spoke against it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Sioux_Reservation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the great sioux reservation","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["dakota war","dakota war of 1862","the dakota war","1862 dakota war","sioux uprising"],"key":"dakotawar","kind":"event","lines":"a war in minnesota in the late summer of 1862. the dakota were starving, the government was months late with the money and food it owed them under treaty, and a trader named andrew myrick said let them eat grass. young dakota men killed settlers, and it became a war.\nit was crushed in six weeks. three hundred and three dakota men were sentenced to death, lincoln cut the list, and on december 26, 1862, thirty eight were hanged together at mankato, the biggest one day execution in american history. the dakota were expelled from minnesota. charles eastman's family fled to canada.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakota_War_of_1862","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dakota war of 1862","status":"approved","threads":["war","exile"]},{"aliases":["fetterman fight","the fetterman fight","fetterman"],"key":"fetterman","kind":"event","lines":"a battle on december 21, 1866, near fort phil kearny in wyoming. lakota and cheyenne warriors sent a few decoys, crazy horse among them, to lure captain william fetterman and his men out of the fort, then surrounded them. all eighty one soldiers died.\nit was the army's worst loss on the plains until the little bighorn ten years later, and it helped force the treaty of 1868.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fetterman_Fight","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the fetterman fight","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["adobe walls","battle of adobe walls"],"key":"adobewalls","kind":"event","lines":"a trading post for white buffalo hunters in the texas panhandle. on june 27, 1874, quanah parker led hundreds of comanche, kiowa and cheyenne warriors against it, trying to stop the hunters who were wiping out the buffalo. about twenty eight hunters with long range rifles held them off.\nit set off the red river war, which ended with quanah surrendering at fort sill a year later.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Battle_of_Adobe_Walls","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"adobe walls","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["red river war","the red river war"],"key":"redriverwar","kind":"event","lines":"the army's 1874 to 1875 campaign to force the comanche, kiowa, southern cheyenne and arapaho off the southern plains and onto reservations in indian territory. the soldiers mostly didn't win battles. they burned the villages and killed the horses and let winter do the rest.\nit ended in june 1875 when quanah parker's band surrendered at fort sill. it was the end of free native life on the southern plains.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_River_War","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the red river war","status":"approved","threads":["war"]},{"aliases":["bear paw","bears paw","the bear paw","bear paw mountains","bears paw mountains","battle of bear paw"],"key":"bearpaw","kind":"event","lines":"low mountains in northern montana, about forty miles from the canadian border, where the army caught chief joseph and the nez perce at the end of their flight in the fall of 1877. after five days of fighting in the snow, joseph surrendered on october 5.\nabout two hundred and thirty nez perce slipped away in the night and made it to sitting bull's camp in canada.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bear_Paw","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the bear paw mountains","status":"approved","threads":["war","exile"]},{"aliases":["standing rock 2016","dakota access pipeline","dakota access","no dapl","water protectors","water protector"],"key":"dakotaaccess","kind":"event","lines":"in 2016 an oil company started building the dakota access pipeline under lake oahe, on the missouri river, half a mile from the standing rock reservation and upstream of its water. the standing rock sioux said it threatened their water and crossed their sacred sites and treaty land.\nstarting that april, people set up camps to block it. by fall there were thousands, from over three hundred native nations and everywhere else, calling themselves water protectors, under the lakota phrase mni wiconi, water is life. private security guards used dogs on them, and police used rubber bullets, tear gas and water cannons in freezing weather. the camps were cleared in february 2017, and the oil flows.\nit was the biggest native gathering in a century, and a whole generation's wounded knee, without the massacre.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakota_Access_Pipeline_protests","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"standing rock 2016","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["wounded knee 1973","occupation of wounded knee","wounded knee occupation","second wounded knee"],"key":"woundedkneeoccupation","kind":"event","lines":"on february 27, 1973, members of the american indian movement and local oglala lakota took over the village of wounded knee on the pine ridge reservation, where the army had massacred hundreds of lakota in 1890. they were protesting a tribal chairman, dick wilson, whom they called corrupt and violent, and a century of broken treaties.\nfederal agents surrounded them for seventy one days. there was gunfire most nights. two occupiers were killed, a third man disappeared and was never found, and a federal marshal was paralyzed. it ended in may with a deal to talk about the 1868 treaty. frank fools crow, the old ceremonial chief, helped bring it to an end.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wounded_Knee_Occupation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"wounded knee, 1973","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["american indian movement","the american indian movement"],"key":"aim","kind":"group","lines":"a native activist group started in minneapolis in 1968, at first to watch the police, who were beating native people in the city. it went national fast, with big public protests, and a lot of attitude, braids, and jean jackets. think of it as the native counterpart to the black panthers.\nin 1972 its members occupied the bureau of indian affairs in washington, richard twiss among them. in 1973 they held wounded knee for seventy one days. its leaders fought each other and the fbi went after it hard, and it split by the nineties.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Indian_Movement","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the american indian movement","status":"approved","threads":["anger"]},{"aliases":["osage","the osage","osage nation"],"key":"osage","kind":"group","lines":"a native nation in oklahoma. oil was found under their land in the 1890s, and because they'd kept the mineral rights together, by the 1920s osage families were some of the richest people in the world per head.\nwhite people moved in to rob them, marrying into families, getting themselves appointed guardians over osage money, and killing. in the early 1920s dozens of osage were murdered. it was one of the fbi's first big cases, and it's the story of the book and film killers of the flower moon. zitkala-ša co-wrote a report on the robbery in 1923.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osage_Nation","why":"the plain overview, and the murders"}],"name":"the osage","status":"approved","threads":["money"]},{"aliases":["muscogee","muscogee (creek)","muscogee nation","mvskoke","creek nation"],"key":"muscogee","kind":"group","lines":"a nation of the southeast, from what's now alabama and georgia, whom the english called the creek, for all the rivers they lived along. they were forced out in the 1830s on the trail of tears to indian territory, now oklahoma.\ntoday the muscogee nation, with its capital in okmulgee, oklahoma, has over a hundred thousand citizens. joy harjo is muscogee.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscogee","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the muscogee","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kiowa","the kiowa","kiowas"],"key":"kiowa","kind":"group","lines":"a plains nation that came down from western montana through the rockies to the southern plains, and by the 1800s were horse people and buffalo hunters in what's now oklahoma and texas, allied with the comanche. they were forced onto a reservation in oklahoma in 1867.\nthe kiowa language has only a handful of speakers left. n. scott momaday traced his people's long migration in the way to rainy mountain.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiowa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the kiowa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["potawatomi","the potawatomi","citizen potawatomi","citizen potawatomi nation"],"key":"potawatomi","kind":"group","lines":"an anishinaabe people of the western great lakes, michigan, indiana, illinois and wisconsin. in the 1830s most were forced west, and in 1838 a march of indiana potawatomi was called the trail of death because so many died on it.\nthe citizen potawatomi nation is based in oklahoma. robin wall kimmerer is a citizen. her grandfather was sent to the carlisle boarding school.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potawatomi","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the potawatomi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nez perce","the nez perce","nimíipuu","nimiipuu"],"key":"nezperce","kind":"group","lines":"a people of the columbia plateau, the high country where idaho, oregon and washington meet, famous for their horses. nez perce means pierced nose in french, a name traders gave them. their own name is nimíipuu, the people.\nin 1805 they saved the starving lewis and clark expedition. in 1877 the government drove chief joseph's band out of their valley in oregon, and they fled more than eleven hundred miles toward canada before they were caught.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nez_Perce","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the nez perce tribe","url":"https://nezperce.org/","why":"the nation's own site"}],"name":"the nez perce","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["comanche","comanches","the comanche"],"key":"comanche","kind":"group","lines":"the horse nation of the southern plains. they split off from the shoshone around 1700, got horses early, and became the best riders on the continent, controlling a huge territory from texas to kansas called comanchería. for more than a century they held off spain, mexico and texas.\nthe red river war and the end of the buffalo broke them in the 1870s. quanah parker's band was one of the last to come in, in 1875. the comanche nation is based in lawton, oklahoma.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comanche","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the comanche","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kwahadi","quahadi","the kwahadi"],"key":"kwahadi","kind":"group","lines":"the most remote and last free band of the comanche, who lived out on the staked plains, the high flat tableland of west texas. quanah parker's band.\nthey held out longer than anybody, until june 1875.","links":[],"name":"the kwahadi","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["northern paiute","paiute","paiutes","the paiute"],"key":"paiute","kind":"group","lines":"a people of the great basin, the dry country of lakes and sagebrush in western nevada, eastern california and southeastern oregon. they lived in small bands tied to particular lakes and marshes, fishing, hunting rabbits and gathering pine nuts.\nwovoka, the prophet of the ghost dance, was northern paiute.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Paiute_people","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the northern paiute","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["seneca","the seneca","senecas","seneca nation"],"key":"seneca","kind":"group","lines":"the westernmost of the six nations of the haudenosaunee, in western new york, called the keepers of the western door. they call themselves onöndowa'ga:', the great hill people.\nthey fought for the british in the revolution and lost most of their land after it. handsome lake was seneca. about eleven thousand seneca live in new york, oklahoma and ontario today. in the 1960s the kinzua dam flooded a third of their allegany reservation.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seneca_people","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the seneca","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["haudenosaunee","iroquois","the iroquois","six nations","the six nations","iroquois confederacy"],"key":"haudenosaunee","kind":"group","lines":"a league of six nations in what's now new york and southern ontario, the mohawk, oneida, onondaga, cayuga, seneca and, from 1722, the tuscarora. the english called them the iroquois. their own name means the people who build the longhouse.\nthe league is very old, bound by a constitution called the great law of peace, and it was one of the great powers of the colonial northeast. some people argue it influenced the american founders. handsome lake's religion is theirs.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iroquois","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the haudenosaunee","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["hunkpapa","húŋkpapȟa","the hunkpapa"],"key":"hunkpapa","kind":"group","lines":"one of the seven bands of the lakota. the name means head of the circle, because when the bands camped together, the hunkpapa lodges stood at the entrance.\nsitting bull was hunkpapa. most hunkpapa live on the standing rock reservation now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunkpapa","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the hunkpapa","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["sicangu","sičháŋǧu","brulé","brule","sicangu lakota"],"key":"sicangu","kind":"group","lines":"one of the seven bands of the lakota, called brulé in french, which means burnt. their own name means burnt thighs, from a story about running through a prairie fire.\nmost live on the rosebud reservation in south dakota. lame deer and richard twiss were sicangu.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brul%C3%A9","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sicangu","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["santee","santee dakota","eastern dakota"],"key":"santee","kind":"group","lines":"the eastern dakota, four of the seven council fires, who lived in minnesota around the upper mississippi. they were expelled from minnesota after the dakota war of 1862 and scattered to reservations in nebraska, the dakotas and canada.\ncharles eastman was santee.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santee_Dakota","why":"the dakota people, eastern and western"}],"name":"the santee","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["yankton","yankton dakota","ihanktonwan","yankton sioux"],"key":"yankton","kind":"group","lines":"one of the seven council fires, the western dakota, whose reservation sits along the missouri river in southeastern south dakota. their name means people of the end village.\nthey protected the sacred pipestone quarry in minnesota under an 1858 treaty. zitkala-ša was yankton, and so was vine deloria jr.'s grandfather.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yankton_Sioux_Tribe","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the yankton","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["standing rock","standing rock reservation","standing rock sioux","standing rock sioux tribe"],"key":"standingrock","kind":"place","lines":"a reservation straddling the line between north and south dakota, home mostly to hunkpapa lakota and yanktonai dakota. sitting bull lived and was killed there. vine deloria jr. was a member.\nin 2016 it was the center of the protests against the dakota access pipeline.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standing_Rock_Indian_Reservation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"standing rock","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rosebud reservation","rosebud indian reservation","rosebud sioux"],"key":"rosebud","kind":"place","lines":"a reservation in south central south dakota, home of the sicangu lakota, the rosebud sioux tribe. it was carved out in 1889 when the government broke up the great sioux reservation.\nlame deer and richard twiss were born there. it has its own tribal college, sinte gleska university, and a big buffalo herd.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosebud_Indian_Reservation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the rosebud reservation","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["fort robinson"],"key":"fortrobinson","kind":"place","lines":"an army fort in northwest nebraska, near the red cloud agency, where many lakota came in to surrender in 1877. crazy horse surrendered there that may and was bayoneted there that september.\nit's a state park now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Robinson","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"fort robinson","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["fort sill"],"key":"fortsill","kind":"place","lines":"an army post near lawton, oklahoma, started in 1869 during the wars on the southern plains. quanah parker surrendered there in 1875, and he and his mother are buried in its cemetery. geronimo, the apache leader, was held there as a prisoner and died there in 1909.\nit's still an active army base.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Sill","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"fort sill","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["colville","colville reservation","colville indian reservation"],"key":"colville","kind":"place","lines":"a reservation in north central washington state, home to twelve bands, among them chief joseph's nez perce, who were sent there in 1885 after years of exile in kansas and oklahoma and were never allowed back to their own valley in oregon.\njoseph died there in 1904.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colville_Indian_Reservation","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the colville reservation","status":"approved","threads":["exile"]},{"aliases":["wallowa","wallowa valley","the wallowa"],"key":"wallowa","kind":"place","lines":"a green valley in the mountains of northeastern oregon, chief joseph's home. his father refused to sign it away in 1863, and when white settlers came in 1871 joseph asked them to leave.\nin 1877 the government forced his band out to give the valley to farmers and ranchers, and none of them were ever allowed back to live. there's a town there now named joseph.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallowa_Valley","why":"the river and the valley"}],"name":"the wallowa valley","status":"approved","threads":["the land","exile"]},{"aliases":["devils tower","devil's tower","tsoai","rock tree"],"key":"devilstower","kind":"place","lines":"a huge rock tower in northeastern wyoming, over eight hundred feet tall, with long vertical columns like it's been clawed. several plains nations hold it sacred. the kiowa call it tsoai, the rock tree.\nthe kiowa story is that seven sisters were chased by a bear, a rock rose into the sky to save them, the bear clawed the sides, and the girls became stars. momaday's kiowa name, tsoai-talee, means rock tree boy. it was also the landing site in close encounters of the third kind.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devils_Tower","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"devils tower, tsoai","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["kinzua dam","the kinzua dam","kinzua"],"key":"kinzua","kind":"place","lines":"a flood control dam on the allegheny river in pennsylvania, finished in 1965. it flooded about ten thousand acres of the seneca nation, a third of the allegany reservation, broke a 1794 treaty, and forced more than six hundred seneca out of their homes.\nit also drowned cornplanter's town, where handsome lake had his visions.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinzua_Dam","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the kinzua dam","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["institute of american indian arts","iaia"],"key":"institute","kind":"place","lines":"a native art college in santa fe, new mexico, founded in 1962. a lot of the best native artists and writers of the last sixty years went through it.\njoy harjo went there at sixteen to be a painter.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_of_American_Indian_Arts","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the institute of american indian arts","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["quaker","quakers","society of friends","religious society of friends"],"key":"quakers","kind":"group","lines":"a christian movement from seventeenth century england, officially the religious society of friends. no priests, no creeds, and in the old days silent meetings where anyone could speak if the spirit moved them. they refused to fight in wars and were early against slavery.\nin america they ran missions and schools among native people, some gentler than others. quakers were living at cornplanter's town when handsome lake had his visions, and zitkala-ša's first boarding school, in indiana, was a quaker school.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quakers","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the quakers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["society of american indians"],"key":"sai","kind":"group","lines":"the first national organization run by native people themselves, founded in 1911, mostly by doctors, lawyers and teachers who'd been through the white schools. charles eastman helped start it. zitkala-ša was its secretary.\nit fought for citizenship and against corruption, and tore itself apart over peyote, some members wanting it banned and others defending it as a native religion. it broke up in 1923.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Society_of_American_Indians","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the society of american indians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["national congress of american indians","ncai"],"key":"ncai","kind":"group","lines":"the biggest and oldest national organization of native nations, founded in 1944 to stand up for tribal rights against government plans to dissolve the tribes into everybody else. vine deloria jr. ran it from 1964 to 1967, before he wrote custer died for your sins.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Congress_of_American_Indians","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the national congress of american indians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["national council of american indians"],"key":"nationalcouncil","kind":"group","lines":"a lobbying group zitkala-ša and her husband raymond bonnin started in washington in 1926, to push congress on native voting rights, land and money. she ran it until she died in 1938. not to be confused with the national congress of american indians, which came later.","links":[],"name":"the national council of american indians","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["atlantic monthly","the atlantic monthly","the atlantic"],"key":"atlanticmonthly","kind":"book","lines":"a magazine started in boston in 1857, and around 1900 about the most respected place in america to publish an essay. picture the new yorker of its day. zitkala-ša's essays about her childhood and the boarding schools ran in it in 1900, while she was still teaching at carlisle.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Atlantic","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the atlantic monthly","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["american indian stories"],"key":"americanindianstories","kind":"book","lines":"zitkala-ša's 1921 book, gathering her essays about her childhood on the yankton reservation, being taken to boarding school at eight, and teaching at carlisle, with some dakota legends and an essay on native rights. it's short and it's free online.\nthe part where they cut her hair is the one people remember.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Indian_Stories","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376","why":"at project gutenberg"}],"name":"american indian stories","status":"approved","threads":["family"]},{"aliases":["the sun dance opera","sun dance opera"],"key":"sundanceopera","kind":"book","lines":"an opera zitkala-ša wrote with a white composer, william hanson, in utah, the first opera by a native american. it opened in vernal, utah, in february 1913, with ute performers from the local reservation, and it was about the sun dance, a ceremony the government had outlawed.\nwhen it went to broadway in 1938, hanson had rewritten it and left her name off.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sun_Dance_Opera","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the sun dance opera","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the soul of the indian","soul of the indian"],"key":"soulofindian","kind":"book","lines":"charles eastman's short 1911 book explaining native religion, especially dakota religion, to white readers. it's calm, plain, and a little idealized, and it's where a lot of americans first read a native person explaining the great mystery in his own words.\nyou can read it in an afternoon.","links":[{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340","why":"at project gutenberg"}],"name":"the soul of the indian","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["indian boyhood"],"key":"indianboyhood","kind":"book","lines":"charles eastman's 1902 memoir of growing up dakota in canada, hunting and learning the old ways, until he was fifteen. elaine goodale, his wife, helped shape it. it's gentle and full of detail.","links":[{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/337","why":"at project gutenberg"}],"name":"indian boyhood","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["from the deep woods to civilization","deep woods to civilization"],"key":"deepwoods","kind":"book","lines":"charles eastman's 1916 memoir of his life after fifteen, school, dartmouth, medical school, and the first job at pine ridge where he treated the wounded after wounded knee. the title sounds like a success story. the book is quieter and sadder than that, about what civilization turned out to mean.","links":[],"name":"from the deep woods to civilization","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["land of the spotted eagle"],"key":"landofspottedeagle","kind":"book","lines":"luther standing bear's 1933 book about the old lakota way of life, family, religion, the land, and schooling, and his argument that native people had it right and america had a lot to learn. it's the one with the line that only to the white man was nature a wilderness.","links":[],"name":"land of the spotted eagle","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["my people the sioux"],"key":"mypeoplethesioux","kind":"book","lines":"luther standing bear's 1928 memoir, from his lakota boyhood through the first class at carlisle, the wild west show and after. it's the plainest first hand account of going to carlisle that exists.","links":[],"name":"my people the sioux","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["lame deer, seeker of visions","seeker of visions"],"key":"seekerofvisions","kind":"book","lines":"the 1972 book that john fire lame deer, a lakota holy man, made with richard erdoes, a white writer. lame deer talked and erdoes wrote the english. it's funny, rude, sad, and wise, the rodeos and the women and the jail right next to the vision quest and the sacred clowns.\nread it the way you read black elk speaks, knowing a white writer's hands are on the words.","links":[],"name":"lame deer, seeker of visions","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["custer died for your sins"],"key":"custerdiedforyoursins","kind":"book","lines":"vine deloria jr.'s 1969 book, subtitled an indian manifesto. eleven sharp, funny essays that went after the government, anthropologists, and the missionaries, and told white america it had no idea what native people wanted. it was a bestseller and a manifesto for the red power movement.\ncuster, in the title, is the cavalry commander wiped out at the little bighorn.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custer_Died_for_Your_Sins","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"custer died for your sins","status":"approved","threads":["anger"]},{"aliases":["god is red"],"key":"godisred","kind":"book","lines":"vine deloria jr.'s 1973 book comparing native religion and christianity. his argument is that christianity is about time, history moving toward an end, and native religions are about place, particular land that's holy, and that a religion that forgot the land helped wreck it.\nit's the book to read if you were raised in church and want the argument from the other side, made by a man who went to seminary.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_Is_Red","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"god is red","status":"approved","threads":["doubt","the land"]},{"aliases":["house made of dawn"],"key":"housemadeofdawn","kind":"book","lines":"n. scott momaday's 1968 novel about abel, a young man from a new mexico pueblo who comes back from world war ii unable to fit anywhere, and his long way home. it won the pulitzer prize for fiction in 1969, the first time for a native american writer.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_Made_of_Dawn","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"house made of dawn","status":"approved","threads":["war","addiction"]},{"aliases":["the way to rainy mountain","way to rainy mountain","rainy mountain"],"key":"rainymountain","kind":"book","lines":"n. scott momaday's short 1969 book following the kiowa's migration from montana to oklahoma, told three ways at once, the old stories, the history, and his own memories, all built around his grandmother aho's death. rainy mountain is a hill in oklahoma where she's buried.\nyou can read it in an evening. the introduction alone is worth it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Way_to_Rainy_Mountain","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the way to rainy mountain","status":"approved","threads":["grief","the land"]},{"aliases":["native american renaissance"],"key":"nativerenaissance","kind":"event","lines":"the name critics gave to the wave of native american writers who broke through after momaday's house made of dawn won the pulitzer in 1969. leslie marmon silko, james welch, louise erdrich, sherman alexie and many more.\nsome native writers don't love the word, since native people had been writing all along. but something did open up, and momaday opened it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_American_Renaissance","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the native american renaissance","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["braiding sweetgrass"],"key":"braidingsweetgrass","kind":"book","lines":"robin wall kimmerer's 2013 book of essays, braiding together plant science, native teaching, and her own life. it starts with the skywoman creation story and goes through strawberries, pecans, and the honorable harvest, the old rules for taking from the land.\na small nonprofit press put it out, and it spread by word of mouth to millions of readers. the main idea is that the land is a gift, and gifts create obligations.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braiding_Sweetgrass","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the publisher","url":"https://milkweed.org/book/braiding-sweetgrass","why":"milkweed editions' page"}],"name":"braiding sweetgrass","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["sweetgrass"],"key":"sweetgrass","kind":"word","lines":"a tall grass that smells like vanilla when it dries, sacred to many native nations of north america, one of the four sacred medicines on the northern plains along with tobacco, sage and cedar. it's cut, dried, and braided, and burned to purify, or just kept for the smell.\nbraided sweetgrass is sometimes called the hair of mother earth, which is why kimmerer named her book after it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierochloe_odorata","why":"the plant"}],"name":"sweetgrass","status":"approved","threads":["the land"]},{"aliases":["poet laureate","u.s. poet laureate","united states poet laureate","poet laureate of the united states"],"key":"poetlaureate","kind":"word","lines":"the official poet of the united states, picked by the librarian of congress for a two year term. the job is mostly to get more people reading poetry, and each laureate picks a project.\njoy harjo was the twenty third, from 2019 to 2022, the first native american, and only the second laureate to serve three terms.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Poet_Laureate","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the poet laureate","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["macarthur fellowship","genius grant"],"key":"macarthur","kind":"word","lines":"a big no strings grant, several hundred thousand dollars over five years, that a foundation gives every year to twenty or thirty people for being unusually original. you can't apply. you get a phone call. everybody calls it the genius grant.\nrobin wall kimmerer got one in 2022.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacArthur_Fellows_Program","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the macarthur fellowship","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["richard erdoes","erdoes","erdoes's"],"key":"erdoes","kind":"person","lines":"an austrian-born illustrator and writer, 1912 to 2008, who came to america as a young man and in 1967 went to the pine ridge reservation on assignment for life magazine and never really left the lakota world.\nhe made lame deer, seeker of visions with john fire lame deer in 1972, and lakota woman with mary crow dog, a young lakota woman who'd been at wounded knee in 1973. like john neihardt with black elk, he wrote the english. the voices are theirs, through his hands.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Erdoes","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"richard erdoes","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cynthia ann parker","cynthia ann"],"key":"cynthiaannparker","kind":"person","lines":"a white girl from texas taken in a comanche raid on her family's fort in 1836, at about nine. she grew up comanche, married a war leader, peta nocona, and had three children, quanah the oldest.\nin 1860 texas rangers attacked her camp and took her back to her white relatives, with her baby daughter. she didn't want to go. she never adjusted, lost the baby to illness in 1864, and died in 1871. quanah later had her reburied near him, and they lie together at fort sill.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cynthia_Ann_Parker","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cynthia ann parker","status":"approved","threads":["family","exile","grief"]},{"aliases":["peta nocona"],"key":"petanocona","kind":"person","lines":"a comanche war leader of the kwahadi band, cynthia ann parker's husband and quanah parker's father. the texas rangers claimed they killed him at the pease river in 1860. quanah always said they didn't, and that his father died a few years later of old wounds and grief.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peta_Nocona","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"peta nocona","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["elaine goodale","elaine goodale eastman"],"key":"elainegoodale","kind":"person","lines":"a white poet and teacher from massachusetts, 1863 to 1953, published as a child, who went west to teach native children and by 1890 ran the government schools in the dakotas. she was at pine ridge for wounded knee, and married charles eastman there in 1891.\nthey had six children, and she helped write and shape his books. they separated in 1921. her own memoir of those years, sister to the sioux, came out long after she died.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elaine_Goodale_Eastman","why":"her life, with her sister dora"}],"name":"elaine goodale eastman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["no water"],"key":"nowater","kind":"person","lines":"an oglala lakota man, the husband of black buffalo woman. around 1870, when she left him and rode off with crazy horse, he followed them and shot crazy horse in the face. the elders settled it with gifts, and she went back to him.","links":[],"name":"no water","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["black buffalo woman"],"key":"blackbuffalowoman","kind":"person","lines":"an oglala lakota woman whom crazy horse had loved since they were young. she married no water instead, and around 1870 she left him for crazy horse. no water shot crazy horse for it, she went back to her husband, and crazy horse lost his title as a shirt wearer.","links":[],"name":"black buffalo woman","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["shirt wearer","shirt wearers"],"key":"shirtwearer","kind":"word","lines":"among the oglala lakota, one of four younger men chosen to keep order in the camp and look after the people, and given a special shirt to wear as the badge of it. a shirt wearer was supposed to put the people ahead of himself in everything.\ncrazy horse was one, and lost it over black buffalo woman.","links":[],"name":"shirt wearer","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["cornplanter","cornplanter's"],"key":"cornplanter","kind":"person","lines":"a seneca war chief, around 1750 to 1836, half brother of handsome lake. he fought for the british in the revolution and afterward made peace with the americans, who gave him land on the allegheny river in pennsylvania.\nhe invited quakers to his town in 1798 to teach farming. his brother's visions happened there. the town is under the kinzua dam's lake now.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornplanter","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"cornplanter","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["annie oakley","little sure shot"],"key":"annieoakley","kind":"person","lines":"the famous sharpshooter from ohio, 1860 to 1926, the star of buffalo bill's wild west, who could split a playing card edge on. in 1884 sitting bull saw her shoot, adopted her as a daughter and named her little sure shot, and she used the name the rest of her life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annie_Oakley","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"annie oakley","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["kicking bear"],"key":"kickingbear","kind":"person","lines":"a lakota holy man and fighter, around 1845 to 1904, who went to nevada in 1889 and 1890 with short bull to hear wovoka, and brought the ghost dance home to the lakota. he taught it at sitting bull's camp in october 1890.\nafter wounded knee he was held prisoner for a while, then released to tour europe with buffalo bill's wild west, of all things.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kicking_Bear","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"kicking bear","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["nelson miles","nelson a. miles","colonel miles","general miles","colonel nelson miles"],"key":"nelsonmiles","kind":"person","lines":"a us army officer who fought in the civil war and then spent years fighting native nations on the plains. chief joseph surrendered to him at the bear paw mountains in 1877, and he promised the nez perce they could go home. washington broke the promise.\nin 1890 he was in charge of the troops around the lakota reservations. after wounded knee he called it a criminal blunder and a horrible massacre of women and children, and later pushed for payments to survivors.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nelson_A._Miles","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"nelson miles","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["russell means"],"key":"russellmeans","kind":"person","lines":"an oglala lakota activist, 1939 to 2012, one of the loudest leaders of the american indian movement, a spokesman at wounded knee in 1973, and later an actor, in the last of the mohicans. he gave the eulogy at frank fools crow's funeral.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russell_Means","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"russell means","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["james mooney","mooney"],"key":"mooney","kind":"person","lines":"a smithsonian researcher, 1861 to 1921, who went west to study the ghost dance right after wounded knee, found wovoka in nevada in 1892, and interviewed him. his long 1896 report is still the main source, and it's fairer to the dancers than most of what the government said at the time.","links":[],"name":"james mooney","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["velada","veladas","the velada"],"key":"velada","kind":"word","lines":"spanish for a night vigil, staying up all night. in the mazatec mountains of southern mexico it's the all-night healing ceremony a curandera, a healer, holds with the mushrooms for someone who's sick or in trouble.\nit's done in the dark, at home, for a reason, to heal or to find out what's wrong. what goes on inside one belongs to the mazatec people, and the forest leaves it there.\nwhen outsiders started coming to huautla in the late fifties and sixties, most of them wanted the velada for the trip. maría sabina said that wasn't what it was for.","links":[],"name":"the velada","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["curandera","curanderas","curandero","curanderos"],"key":"curandera","kind":"word","lines":"spanish for a healer, a woman who cures. curandero is the man. across mexico and latin america they're the local healers people go to with sickness, bad luck, and grief, sometimes alongside the doctor and sometimes instead.\nthe outsider's old word was medicine woman or witch doctor. curandera is the word the people themselves use.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curandero","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"curandera","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mazatec","mazatecs","sierra mazateca"],"key":"mazatec","kind":"group","lines":"an indigenous people of the mountains in the north of oaxaca, in southern mexico, with their own language, a tonal one where the pitch of a word changes its meaning. there are a couple hundred thousand of them today.\nthey've used mushrooms and other plants in healing ceremonies since long before the spanish came. maría sabina was mazatec, and her whole story happened in their mountains.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mazatecs","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the mazatec","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["huautla","huautla de jiménez","huautla de jimenez"],"key":"huautla","kind":"place","lines":"a small town strung along a steep mountain ridge in the sierra mazateca, in oaxaca, southern mexico. maría sabina lived there her whole life.\nafter gordon wasson wrote about it in 1957, it became a pilgrimage stop for hippies, scientists, and the curious, and the town paid for it. it still gets psychedelic tourists.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huautla_de_Jim%C3%A9nez","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"huautla de jiménez","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["oaxaca"],"key":"oaxaca","kind":"place","lines":"a mountainous state in the far south of mexico, pronounced wah-hah-kah. it has more indigenous peoples and languages than almost anywhere else in mexico.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oaxaca","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"oaxaca","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["little saints","the little saints","saint children","the saint children"],"key":"littlesaints","kind":"word","lines":"what maría sabina and the mazatecs called the psilocybin mushrooms, in spanish niños santos, the holy children. they're not a drug in that way of talking. they're beings you ask for help, and they speak.\ncalling them saints comes from centuries of mazatec belief mixed with catholicism after the spanish came. sabina prayed to christ and the saints and to the mushrooms in the same night.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psilocybin_mushroom","why":"the mushrooms themselves"}],"name":"the little saints","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["álvaro estrada","alvaro estrada"],"key":"estrada","kind":"person","lines":"a mazatec writer from huautla who sat with maría sabina and recorded her life story in her own words. it came out in spanish in 1977 and in english a few years later as maría sabina: her life and chants.\nit's the only full account we have in her voice instead of a visitor's.","links":[],"name":"álvaro estrada","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["life magazine","seeking the magic mushroom"],"key":"wassonlife","kind":"book","lines":"life was the giant weekly picture magazine of mid-century america, on the coffee table in millions of homes. think of it as instagram in print, before tv took over.\non may 13, 1957, it ran gordon wasson's story seeking the magic mushroom, with photographs, about taking the mushrooms with a mazatec healer. it's the article that introduced magic mushrooms to america, and the seekers who went looking for huautla after it came from there.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Life_(magazine)","why":"the magazine"}],"name":"seeking the magic mushroom","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["j.p. morgan","jp morgan"],"key":"jpmorgan","kind":"group","lines":"one of the oldest and most powerful banks on wall street, in new york. for decades being a partner or vice president there put you near the top of american money.\ngordon wasson was its vice president for public relations, which is part of why people took his mushroom stories seriously.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J.P._Morgan_%26_Co.","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"j.p. morgan","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mushrooms, russia and history"],"key":"mushroomsrussiahistory","kind":"book","lines":"gordon and valentina wasson's two volume book about how different cultures love or fear mushrooms, printed in 1957 in a small, expensive edition of five hundred and twelve copies.\nthe second volume named maría sabina and her town, which the life magazine article had not.","links":[],"name":"mushrooms, russia and history","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["soma, from 1968","soma: divine mushroom of immortality"],"key":"somabook","kind":"book","lines":"gordon wasson's 1968 book arguing that soma, the holy drink praised all through the rig veda, the oldest hindu scriptures, was made from the red and white fly agaric mushroom.\nit's a guess scholars still argue about. nobody knows for sure what soma was.","links":[],"name":"soma: divine mushroom of immortality","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["rig veda","the rig veda","rigveda"],"key":"rigveda","kind":"book","lines":"the oldest of the hindu scriptures, a collection of more than a thousand hymns composed in northern india around three thousand years ago, and chanted from memory for centuries before anyone wrote them down.\na lot of the hymns praise soma, a holy drink that gave the drinker visions and strength. what was in it is one of the old mysteries.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rigveda","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the rig veda","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["mkultra","mk-ultra"],"key":"mkultra","kind":"event","lines":"the cia's secret mind control research program, running from 1953 into the sixties. it paid universities, hospitals, and prisons, often through fake foundations, to test drugs, lsd above all, sometimes on people who didn't know they were being drugged.\nat least one man died. most of the records were destroyed in 1973, and the program came out in congressional hearings in the seventies. it's the reason people don't trust the government on this subject.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MKUltra","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"mkultra","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["ergot"],"key":"ergot","kind":"word","lines":"a fungus that grows on rye and other grain. baked into bread, it poisoned whole villages in the middle ages, with burning pain, gangrene, and fits people called st anthony's fire.\ndrug companies later pulled useful medicines out of it, for childbirth and migraines. albert hofmann made lsd from one of its chemicals in 1938.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ergot","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ergot","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["sandoz"],"key":"sandoz","kind":"group","lines":"a big swiss drug company in basel, where albert hofmann worked for over forty years. it made lsd and psilocybin and sent them out to researchers in the fifties and sixties under brand names, delysid and indocybin.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandoz","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"sandoz","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["bicycle day"],"key":"bicycleday","kind":"event","lines":"april 19, 1943, the day albert hofmann took lsd on purpose for the first time, found it was far stronger than he'd guessed, and rode his bicycle home through basel sure he was losing his mind.\npeople who take psychedelics mark the date every year. it's the first acid trip on record, and it was a frightening one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bicycle_Day_(psychedelic_holiday)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"bicycle day","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["lsd: my problem child","my problem child","the problem child book"],"key":"problemchild","kind":"book","lines":"albert hofmann's 1979 book about the drug he made, from the lab accident to the sixties. problem child is the german sorgenkind, the kid who keeps you up at night.\nit's calm, precise, and honest about both sides. he thought lsd had real value in psychiatry and in the kind of looking the mystics do, and that careless use by young people had made it his problem child.","links":[{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://maps.org/images/pdf/books/lsdmyproblemchild.pdf","why":"the full text, hosted by maps"}],"name":"lsd: my problem child","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["eleusis","the road to eleusis","road to eleusis","eleusinian mysteries"],"key":"eleusis","kind":"place","lines":"a town near athens where for close to two thousand years ancient greeks went through a secret initiation called the mysteries. thousands came every year. it was a crime to reveal what happened, and nobody fully did. people came out saying they no longer feared death.\nthe road to eleusis, from 1978, by gordon wasson, albert hofmann, and a classics professor, carl ruck, argues that the drink everyone took there had ergot in it. it's a guess, and scholars still argue about it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleusinian_Mysteries","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"eleusis","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["dmt","5-meo-dmt","desert toad"],"key":"dmt","kind":"word","lines":"a very strong, very short acting psychedelic, found in many plants and in small amounts in animals. smoked, it hits in seconds and is over in minutes, and people describe it as being blasted into another world.\nit's the active part of ayahuasca, the amazon brew. a close cousin, 5-meo-dmt, comes from the venom of a desert toad and is even more overwhelming. both are illegal in the us, and both can be terrifying.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N,N-Dimethyltryptamine","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dmt","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["ayahuasca"],"key":"ayahuasca","kind":"word","lines":"a brown, bitter brew from the amazon, made from two plants cooked together. one has dmt in it. the other stops your gut from breaking the dmt down, so it works when you drink it.\nindigenous peoples of the amazon have used it in healing ceremonies for a long time, and some brazilian churches use it as a sacrament. now there's a whole tourist trade of westerners flying to peru for retreats, which has brought money, fakes, and some deaths.\nit usually makes you throw up. it doesn't mix with a lot of medications, including common antidepressants, and that can be dangerous.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayahuasca","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ayahuasca","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["ibogaine","iboga"],"key":"ibogaine","kind":"word","lines":"a psychedelic from the root bark of a shrub in west central africa, iboga, used in initiation ceremonies in the bwiti religion of gabon.\npeople with opioid addiction have reported it wiping out their withdrawal and cravings, and there's serious research on that now. it's also hard on the heart and has killed people. it's illegal in the us, and people go to clinics in mexico for it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ibogaine","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ibogaine","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics","addiction"]},{"aliases":["mdma"],"key":"mdma","kind":"word","lines":"a drug first made by the german company merck in 1912 and forgotten, then brought back by sasha shulgin in the seventies. it's ecstasy, or molly, on the street. it's not a classic psychedelic. it mostly makes you feel open, warm, and unafraid, especially toward other people.\ntherapists used it quietly in the seventies and early eighties to help people talk about trauma. it was banned in 1985 once it hit the dance clubs. recent trials for ptsd looked strong, but in 2024 the us food and drug administration turned down the application and asked for more study.\nthe risks are real. overheating, drinking so much water your blood salt crashes, heart strain, a hard crash in mood afterward, and street pills that aren't what they say.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MDMA","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the national institute on drug abuse","url":"https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/mdma-ecstasy-molly","why":"the plain facts and risks, from the us government's drug research agency"}],"name":"mdma","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["ketamine"],"key":"ketamine","kind":"word","lines":"an anesthetic, used in surgery and in emergency rooms since the sixties. at lower doses it makes you feel detached from your body and your thoughts, and it's sold on the street as special k.\nsince 2019 a version of it, a nasal spray, has been approved in the us for depression that other drugs haven't helped, and ketamine clinics have popped up everywhere. it can be addictive, and heavy use damages the bladder.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ketamine","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ketamine","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["set and setting"],"key":"setandsetting","kind":"word","lines":"the idea that what happens on a psychedelic depends less on the drug than on the set, your mindset, mood, and expectations going in, and the setting, where you are and who's with you.\ntimothy leary made the phrase famous in the early sixties. it's still the first rule in every serious study. prepare people, make the room safe, have a sober guide there. the same drug at a festival alone and in a quiet room with a guide can be two completely different nights.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_and_setting","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"set and setting","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["mystical experience questionnaire","the mystical experience questionnaire"],"key":"meq","kind":"word","lines":"a checklist researchers use after a psychedelic session to measure whether someone had what they'd call a mystical experience. it asks things like whether you felt unity with everything, whether it felt more real than ordinary life, whether time and space fell away, and whether it was beyond words.\nit goes back to walter pahnke's good friday experiment in 1962, and behind that to the philosopher w. t. stace and to william james, the harvard psychologist who described the marks of mystical states in 1902. roland griffiths's team at johns hopkins used a version of it, and people who scored high on it tended to get better results later.","links":[],"name":"the mystical experience questionnaire","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["bad trip","bad trips","a bad trip"],"key":"badtrip","kind":"word","lines":"a psychedelic experience that turns frightening. terror, paranoia, the feeling you're dying or going insane or will never come back. it can last hours. people sometimes hurt themselves trying to get away from it.\nthe careful research is built around preventing and getting through them, with preparation, a safe room, and a sober guide. researchers now call them challenging experiences, and some people say later that the worst ones taught them the most. that's not a reason to go looking for one.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bad_trip","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"a bad trip","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["hallucinogen persisting perception disorder","hppd"],"key":"hppd","kind":"word","lines":"a rare condition where some of the visual effects of a psychedelic keep happening long after the drug is gone, for weeks, months, or years. trails behind moving things, halos, patterns, static in the air.\nfor most people who have it it's mild and fades. for a few it's constant and distressing, and there's no reliable treatment. nobody can predict who'll get it. it's one of the plain risks of these drugs, and it's why flashbacks weren't just a sixties joke.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hallucinogen_persisting_perception_disorder","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"hallucinogen persisting perception disorder","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["controlled substances act","the controlled substances act"],"key":"csa","kind":"event","lines":"the 1970 us law, signed by richard nixon, that sorted drugs into five schedules by how dangerous and how medically useful the government said they were, and set the penalties.\nlsd, psilocybin, mescaline, and marijuana went into schedule i, the strictest, which made them hard even to study. that's a big reason psychedelic research in the us stopped for about thirty years.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Controlled_Substances_Act","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the controlled substances act","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics","prison"]},{"aliases":["schedule i","schedule 1","schedule one"],"key":"scheduleone","kind":"word","lines":"the strictest category in the us drug law, for drugs the government says have a high potential for abuse and no accepted medical use. heroin is there. so are lsd, psilocybin, mdma, and, for decades, marijuana.\nscientists can study schedule i drugs only with special licenses from the dea, which is why research on them moved so slowly.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Schedule_I_controlled_substances_(U.S.)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"schedule i","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["good friday experiment","the good friday experiment","marsh chapel experiment"],"key":"goodfridayexperiment","kind":"event","lines":"on good friday, april 20, 1962, walter pahnke, a harvard grad student in timothy leary's circle, gave twenty divinity students either psilocybin or a placebo that made them flush and tingle, during a service at marsh chapel at boston university. most who got the drug said they had a real religious experience. most who got the placebo didn't.\nit was sloppy by today's standards. a follow up by rick doblin, published in 1991, found things the write up had left out, including a volunteer who panicked and had to be sedated. roland griffiths's 2006 study at johns hopkins was, in part, the careful version.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh_Chapel_Experiment","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the good friday experiment","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["walter pahnke","pahnke"],"key":"pahnke","kind":"person","lines":"a doctor and minister who ran the good friday experiment in 1962 for his harvard doctorate. he went on to do lsd therapy research with dying cancer patients in maryland, alongside stanislav grof. he drowned while diving in 1971, at forty.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_N._Pahnke","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"walter pahnke","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["huston smith"],"key":"hustonsmith","kind":"person","lines":"an american scholar of religion, the son of missionaries in china, whose book the world's religions was the textbook for generations of college students. he was in the chapel for the good friday experiment in 1962, took psilocybin that day, and said afterward it was the most powerful religious experience of his life.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huston_Smith","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"huston smith","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["millbrook"],"key":"millbrook","kind":"place","lines":"a town in the hudson valley north of new york city, where the hitchcocks, rich young heirs, lent timothy leary and richard alpert a sixty four room mansion on a big estate after harvard fired them in 1963.\nfor about five years it was a psychedelic commune, part research center, part party, part experiment in living. the local assistant district attorney, g. gordon liddy, raided it in 1966.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitchcock_Estate","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"millbrook","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["g. gordon liddy","gordon liddy","liddy"],"key":"liddy","kind":"person","lines":"a former fbi agent and county prosecutor in new york who raided timothy leary's millbrook commune in 1966. a few years later he helped plan the watergate break-in for nixon's reelection campaign and went to prison for it. in the eighties he and leary toured together debating each other, for money.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G._Gordon_Liddy","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"g. gordon liddy","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["watergate","the watergate"],"key":"watergate","kind":"event","lines":"the 1972 break-in at the democratic party's offices in the watergate building in washington, run by people working for richard nixon's reelection, and the cover-up that followed. it ended with nixon resigning in 1974, the only us president ever to quit.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watergate_scandal","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"watergate","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["richard nixon","nixon"],"key":"nixon","kind":"person","lines":"president of the united states from 1969 until he resigned over watergate in 1974. he declared the war on drugs, signed the controlled substances act in 1970, and called timothy leary the most dangerous man in america.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Nixon","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"richard nixon","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["human be-in","the human be-in"],"key":"humanbein","kind":"event","lines":"a huge free gathering in golden gate park, san francisco, on january 14, 1967, with poets, bands, and tens of thousands of young people. it was a kickoff for the summer of love.\nit's where timothy leary told the crowd to turn on, tune in, drop out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Be-In","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the human be-in","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["turn on, tune in, drop out"],"key":"turnon","kind":"word","lines":"timothy leary's slogan from 1966 and 1967. he said it meant turn on to what's inside you, tune in to the world, and drop out of the fake commitments. the country heard get high and quit your job.\nhe said later that marshall mcluhan, the media theorist, helped him with the phrasing. it was probably the most effective ad copy of the decade, and the backlash to it helped get these drugs banned.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turn_on,_tune_in,_drop_out","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"turn on, tune in, drop out","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["weather underground","the weather underground","weathermen","the weathermen"],"key":"weatherunderground","kind":"group","lines":"a small radical left wing group, grown out of the sixties student movement, that went underground in 1970 and set off bombs at government buildings, including the capitol and the pentagon, to protest the vietnam war. they usually phoned in warnings first. three of their own members died when a bomb they were building went off in a new york townhouse.\nin september 1970 they smuggled timothy leary out of the country after he escaped from prison, paid by a hippie drug ring.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather_Underground","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the weather underground","status":"approved","threads":["prison"]},{"aliases":["black panthers","the black panthers","black panther party"],"key":"blackpanthers","kind":"group","lines":"a black political party founded in oakland, california, in 1966 to protect black neighborhoods from police violence. they carried guns openly, ran free breakfast programs for kids, and were targeted hard by the fbi.\nsome of their leaders, including eldridge cleaver, ended up in exile in algeria, which is where timothy leary landed after his prison escape, and where they fell out with him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Panther_Party","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the black panthers","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["esalen","esalen institute","the esalen institute"],"key":"esalen","kind":"place","lines":"a retreat center on the cliffs of big sur, california, founded in 1962, with hot springs over the ocean. it became the home of the human potential movement, the sixties and seventies scene of encounter groups, massage, meditation, and experiments in consciousness.\nstanislav grof lived there for fourteen years, and terence mckenna gave workshops there. it still runs, and still costs a lot.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esalen_Institute","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"esalen","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["holotropic breathwork","the breathwork","breathwork"],"key":"holotropicbreathwork","kind":"word","lines":"a practice stanislav grof and his wife christina developed in the seventies after lsd was banned. you lie down and breathe faster and deeper than normal for a long time, with loud music and a partner sitting beside you, and people report experiences a lot like psychedelic ones.\nit's legal and widely taught, but it isn't nothing. breathing that hard changes your blood chemistry, can cause cramps and panic, and can bring up old trauma. people with heart problems, epilepsy, serious mental illness, or a pregnancy are told not to do it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holotropic_Breathwork","why":"the breathwork page, which covers holotropic breathwork and its risks"}],"name":"holotropic breathwork","status":"approved","threads":["the body","psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["spiritual emergency","spiritual emergence network"],"key":"spiritualemergency","kind":"word","lines":"stanislav and christina grof's term for a crisis where a spiritual opening overwhelms someone, with visions, terror, feeling you've died or merged with everything, sometimes looking a lot like a psychotic break.\ntheir argument is that some of these crises are a breakthrough trying to happen, and the person needs support more than sedation. that's a real idea and a risky one, because some of these states are simply illness and need a doctor.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiritual_emergency","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"spiritual emergency","status":"approved","threads":["madness"]},{"aliases":["bill wilson","bill w."],"key":"billwilson","kind":"person","lines":"the new york stockbroker who drank himself nearly to death, saw a white light in a detox bed in december 1934, and with an ohio surgeon named dr bob started alcoholics anonymous in 1935. he wrote most of the big book in 1939 and the twelve steps in it. he stayed sober thirty six years, through a long depression in the forties and fifties, and died of emphysema in 1971.\nin 1956 he took lsd in los angeles with gerald heard and aldous huxley's circle, and said it brought back something like the white light. he hoped it could help drunks who couldn't get a spiritual experience on their own. aa was appalled, and he dropped it.\nhe doesn't get a page in this forest. he had affairs, one of them about fifteen years long with a woman he met through aa, and there are long standing accounts of him pursuing women who were new to the program, people who'd come to him raw and frightened for help. that's the line. what he built keeps millions of people alive, and it's here in the doors. he isn't.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_W.","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"aa.org","url":"https://www.aa.org/","why":"the fellowship's own site"}],"name":"bill wilson","status":"approved","threads":["addiction","psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["towns hospital","the towns hospital"],"key":"townshospital","kind":"place","lines":"a private hospital in manhattan for drying out drinkers and drug users who could pay, run by charles towns. bill wilson was a patient there four times, and it's where, in december 1934, he had the white light experience that got him sober.\nits medical director, william silkworth, was one of the first doctors to treat alcoholism as an illness instead of a moral failure.","links":[],"name":"towns hospital","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["belladonna cure","the belladonna cure","belladonna"],"key":"belladonnacure","kind":"word","lines":"the treatment at towns hospital in the early 1900s, sold as a cure for drink and drugs. it was a mix of drugs that included belladonna, a poisonous plant also called deadly nightshade, and it purged patients hard.\nbelladonna can cause hallucinations, which is why some people have wondered what bill wilson's white light really was. he never doubted it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atropa_belladonna","why":"the plant"}],"name":"the belladonna cure","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["alcoholics anonymous"],"key":"alcoholicsanonymous","kind":"group","lines":"the fellowship bill wilson and dr bob smith started in 1935, where people who can't stop drinking meet and help each other stay sober by telling the truth about themselves. no dues, no leaders in charge, no professionals. it's in almost every town in the world, usually in a church basement, usually with bad coffee.\nit's where the twelve steps come from, and every twelve step group after it, for drugs, gambling, food, and family members, is built on its model. it doesn't work for everyone. for millions it's the reason they're alive.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcoholics_Anonymous","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"aa's own site","url":"https://www.aa.org/","why":"aa's own site, with meeting finders"}],"name":"alcoholics anonymous","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["the big book","big book"],"key":"bigbook","kind":"book","lines":"the book alcoholics anonymous, published in 1939, written mostly by bill wilson. everyone calls it the big book because the first printing was on thick paper and came out huge.\nthe first part explains the program, including the twelve steps in chapter 5. the second part is stories from members. it's sold tens of millions of copies, and aa puts the chapters online free.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Big_Book_(Alcoholics_Anonymous)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"aa's page for it","url":"https://www.aa.org/the-big-book","why":"the chapters as free pdfs and audio"}],"name":"the big book","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["twelve steps","the twelve steps","twelve step","twelve-step"],"key":"twelvesteps","kind":"word","lines":"the program at the heart of alcoholics anonymous. roughly, admit you can't control it, come to believe something bigger than you can help, hand your life over to that, take an honest inventory of yourself, admit it to someone, get ready to change, ask for help changing, make a list of the people you've hurt, make it right with them, keep taking inventory, keep praying or meditating, and carry it to the next person.\nthe higher power is left to you. lots of atheists work the steps with the group itself as their higher power.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-step_program","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"aa's own page","url":"https://www.aa.org/the-twelve-steps","why":"the steps as written"}],"name":"the twelve steps","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["appendix ii"],"key":"spiritualexperience","kind":"book","lines":"a two page appendix at the back of the big book, added soon after the first printing, called spiritual experience. it says that bill wilson's sudden flash of white light was not the usual thing, and that for most people the change is slow, what it calls the educational variety, noticed by friends before you notice it yourself.\nit ends with a line about contempt prior to investigation keeping people in ignorance. it's the kindest two pages in the book for anyone who's waiting for a light that isn't coming.","links":[],"name":"appendix ii, spiritual experience","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["dr bob","dr. bob","bob smith"],"key":"drbob","kind":"person","lines":"robert smith, a surgeon in akron, ohio, and a drunk, who met bill wilson in may 1935. his last drink, on june 10, 1935, is counted as the day alcoholics anonymous began. he spent the rest of his life helping drunks at an akron hospital, and died in 1950.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Smith_(doctor)","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dr bob","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["ebby thacher","ebby"],"key":"ebby","kind":"person","lines":"an old drinking buddy of bill wilson's who showed up at his kitchen table in november 1934 sober, having found religion through a christian group, and told bill how. that visit started everything. ebby himself kept relapsing for years, and died sober in 1966.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebby_Thacher","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"ebby thacher","status":"approved","threads":["addiction"]},{"aliases":["al-anon"],"key":"alanon","kind":"group","lines":"the fellowship for the families and friends of drinkers, started in 1951 by lois wilson, bill's wife, and a friend of hers. it uses the twelve steps for the people who love an alcoholic and can't fix them.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Anon/Alateen","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"al-anon","status":"approved","threads":["addiction","family"]},{"aliases":["carl jung","jung"],"key":"jung","kind":"person","lines":"the swiss psychiatrist who broke with freud and wrote about the unconscious, dreams, and religion. in a 1961 letter to bill wilson he said that a craving for alcohol was, at bottom, a thirst for wholeness, a spiritual thirst.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Jung","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"carl jung","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["gerald heard"],"key":"geraldheard","kind":"person","lines":"an english writer on religion and mysticism, a friend of aldous huxley's who came to california with him in 1937. he ran a small religious retreat in the hills near los angeles and was one of the first people to guide others through lsd in the fifties. bill wilson took it with him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerald_Heard","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"gerald heard","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["william james"],"key":"williamjames","kind":"person","lines":"an american psychologist and philosopher at harvard, born in 1842, the brother of the novelist henry james. he more or less founded american psychology.\nin the varieties of religious experience, from 1902, he took mystical experience seriously as data, and laid out its marks. you can't put it into words, it feels like knowing, it passes, and it happens to you rather than being done by you. he'd tried nitrous oxide, laughing gas, and wrote that it convinced him our normal waking consciousness is just one kind, with whole other forms of consciousness right next to it, separated by the thinnest film.\nbill wilson read the book after his white light and it helped him make sense of it. every psychedelic researcher since has quoted him.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_James","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"william james","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["the varieties of religious experience","varieties of religious experience"],"key":"varieties","kind":"book","lines":"william james's 1902 book, from lectures he gave in scotland, about religion as people actually live it, the conversions, mystical states, and saints, studied like a scientist would. no preaching, lots of case studies.\nit's where the idea that you can study spiritual experience without deciding whether god is real starts. bill wilson read it in 1934. the chapter on mysticism is the one to read.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Varieties_of_Religious_Experience","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/621","why":"public domain"}],"name":"the varieties of religious experience","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["johns hopkins","hopkins"],"key":"johnshopkins","kind":"place","lines":"one of the top medical schools and research hospitals in the world, in baltimore, maryland. when johns hopkins publishes something, other doctors pay attention.\nthat's why roland griffiths's psilocybin study there in 2006 mattered so much. in 2019 hopkins opened a whole center for psychedelic research.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johns_Hopkins_University","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"johns hopkins","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["dea","the dea","drug enforcement administration"],"key":"dea","kind":"group","lines":"the drug enforcement administration, the us federal police force for drugs, set up in 1973. it enforces the controlled substances act and licenses the few scientists allowed to work with schedule i drugs.\nit licensed sasha shulgin for years, used him as an expert, and then raided his lab in 1994 after his book came out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drug_Enforcement_Administration","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the dea","status":"approved","threads":["prison"]},{"aliases":["food and drug administration","the fda"],"key":"fda","kind":"group","lines":"the us government agency that decides which drugs can be sold as medicine. in 2024 it turned down the first application to approve mdma therapy for ptsd, asking for another large study.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Food_and_Drug_Administration","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the food and drug administration","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["ptsd","post-traumatic stress disorder"],"key":"ptsd","kind":"word","lines":"post-traumatic stress disorder. after something terrible, a war, an assault, a crash, some people's nervous systems stay stuck on alarm, with nightmares, flashbacks, jumpiness, going numb, avoiding anything that reminds them. it can last decades.\nit's one of the main things the new psychedelic research is aimed at, mdma above all.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-traumatic_stress_disorder","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the national institute of mental health","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/post-traumatic-stress-disorder-ptsd","why":"the plain facts, from the us government"}],"name":"ptsd","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["multidisciplinary association for psychedelic studies"],"key":"maps","kind":"group","lines":"maps, the multidisciplinary association for psychedelic studies, a nonprofit rick doblin founded in 1986 to get psychedelics, mdma above all, through the medical system as legal medicine. it paid for most of the mdma therapy trials for ptsd.\nit spun off a company, later called lykos therapeutics, to take mdma to the fda. the fda turned it down in 2024.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multidisciplinary_Association_for_Psychedelic_Studies","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"maps","url":"https://maps.org/","why":"their own site"}],"name":"maps","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["rick doblin","doblin"],"key":"doblin","kind":"person","lines":"the founder of maps, the multidisciplinary association for psychedelic studies, who has spent almost forty years trying to make mdma a legal prescription medicine. he also tracked down the good friday experiment volunteers in the late eighties and wrote up what the original report had left out.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rick_Doblin","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"rick doblin","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["leo zeff"],"key":"leozeff","kind":"person","lines":"a california psychotherapist who, near retirement in the late seventies, tried mdma from sasha shulgin and spent the rest of his working life quietly teaching it to other therapists across the country. he died in 1988. he used the name adam for it.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_Zeff","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"leo zeff","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["pihkal","pihkal: a chemical love story"],"key":"pihkal","kind":"book","lines":"sasha and ann shulgin's 1991 book. the title stands for phenethylamines i have known and loved, a family of chemicals that includes mescaline and mdma. the first half is their love story, told in turns, and it's a good one. the second half is lab notes, which this forest stays out of.\nit's a big part of why the dea raided sasha's lab in 1994.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PiHKAL","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"pihkal","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics","love"]},{"aliases":["tihkal","tihkal: the continuation"],"key":"tihkal","kind":"book","lines":"the shulgins' 1997 follow up to pihkal, the same shape, for tryptamines, the chemical family that includes psilocybin and dmt. more of their life together in the first half, more chemistry in the second.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TiHKAL","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"tihkal","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["default mode network","the default mode network"],"key":"dmn","kind":"word","lines":"a set of brain regions that are busiest when you're not doing anything in particular, daydreaming, remembering, worrying, thinking about yourself. it's roughly where the running story of me seems to live.\nbrain scans on psilocybin and lsd seem to show it going quiet and the rest of the brain talking to itself in unusual ways. researchers think that might be part of why people report losing the sense of a separate self, and why some depressions, stuck in the same thought loops, loosen. it's a working theory from brain pictures, not a settled fact.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Default_mode_network","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the default mode network","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["how to change your mind"],"key":"howtochangeyourmind","kind":"book","lines":"michael pollan's 2018 book on psychedelics, with a subtitle that sums it up, what the new science of psychedelics teaches us about consciousness, dying, addiction, depression, and transcendence. part history, part science reporting, part his own trips.\nit's the book most people read first, and netflix made it into a four part series in 2022.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/How_to_Change_Your_Mind","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the publisher","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/529343/how-to-change-your-mind-by-michael-pollan/","why":"penguin's page for the book"}],"name":"how to change your mind","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["shamanism","shaman","shamans"],"key":"shamanism","kind":"word","lines":"a word from the tungus people of siberia for a healer who goes into trance, by drumming, dancing, fasting, or plants, to deal with the spirit world for the sake of the community. anthropologists stretched it to cover similar healers all over the world.\nit gets used loosely now, by a lot of people selling retreats. the traditions it describes are real and specific, and each has its own name for the person.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamanism","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"shamanism","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["la chorrera"],"key":"lachorrera","kind":"place","lines":"a mission village on the igaraparaná river in the colombian amazon, where terence and dennis mckenna went with friends in 1971 looking for a plant drug and ended up doing a strange experiment with mushrooms that sent dennis into a breakdown for days. terence built most of his later ideas on what he said happened there.","links":[],"name":"la chorrera","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["dennis mckenna","his brother dennis","brother dennis"],"key":"dennismckenna","kind":"person","lines":"terence's younger brother, born 1950, who became a serious scientist of plant drugs, an ethnopharmacologist, and worked on the chemistry of ayahuasca. his 2012 memoir, the brotherhood of the screaming abyss, tells the two brothers' story straight, including the bad trip terence never talked about.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennis_McKenna","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"dennis mckenna","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics","family"]},{"aliases":["heroic dose","the heroic dose","heroic doses"],"key":"heroicdose","kind":"word","lines":"terence mckenna's phrase for taking a large amount of mushrooms, alone, in silent darkness, to go as far as possible. he preached it for years.\nit's close to the opposite of how careful research does it, with preparation, a safe room, and a sober guide present. alone in the dark is exactly where a bad trip has nobody to catch you. and by his brother's account he'd stopped doing it himself.","links":[],"name":"the heroic dose","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["stoned ape","the stoned ape idea","stoned ape theory","stoned ape hypothesis","food of the gods"],"key":"stonedape","kind":"word","lines":"terence mckenna's idea, from his 1992 book food of the gods, that early humans on the african plains ate psilocybin mushrooms and that this gave us language, religion, and the leap to being human.\nit's not accepted science. there's no fossil or archaeological evidence for it, and it's never been taken seriously by the people who study human evolution. it's a great campfire story, and that's all it is.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoned_ape_theory","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the stoned ape idea","status":"approved","threads":["psychedelics"]},{"aliases":["surendranath mitra","surendra mitra"],"key":"surendranathmitra","kind":"person","lines":"a calcutta householder, meaning a man with a job and a family and not a monk, who was one of ramakrishna's close followers. he lived in the same neighborhood as the datta family, and it was at his house in november 1881 that the kid next door got brought in to sing when the singer he'd lined up didn't show. that's how vivekananda and ramakrishna first laid eyes on each other.\nhe's also the money behind a lot of what came next. he paid for the garden house at cossipore where ramakrishna spent his last months, and after the old man died he rented the house at baranagar that became the first monastery and covered part of what it cost the boys to live there. he died in 1890.\nthink of the guy who never gets on stage and keeps paying for the hall. every movement has one, and nobody remembers his name.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surendra_Nath_Mitra","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"surendranath mitra","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["upanishads","upanishad","katha upanishad"],"key":"upanishads","kind":"book","lines":"the old hindu wisdom texts, written down roughly between 800 and 200 bc, that sit at the end of the vedas, india's oldest scriptures. upanishad means something like sitting down near, as in near a teacher. they're mostly conversations.\nthe big idea in them is that your deepest self, atman, and the ground of everything, brahman, are one thing. that's the root of vedanta, and of most of what vivekananda taught in america. the katha upanishad, a story about a boy who goes to the house of death and asks him what happens after, is where his line arise, awake, and stop not comes from.\nschopenhauer said they were the consolation of his life. emerson and thoreau read them. they're short and strange and worth an afternoon.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upanishads","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katha_Upanishad","why":"the one about the boy and death"}],"name":"the upanishads","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["vedanta society","vedanta society of new york","vedanta societies"],"key":"vedantasociety","kind":"group","lines":"the organization vivekananda started in new york in november 1894 so the people who'd been coming to his lectures would have somewhere to keep going after he left. it's the first hindu teaching center in america.\nthere are vedanta societies in most big american cities now, run by monks of the ramakrishna order sent from india. the hollywood one is where huxley, isherwood and a stretch of 1940s california went to sit. the teaching is vedanta, the hindu philosophy that says you and god are not two.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vedanta_Society","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the vedanta society","status":"approved"},{"aliases":["world's fair","columbian exposition","world's columbian exposition","chicago world's fair"],"key":"worldsfair","kind":"event","lines":"the world's columbian exposition, chicago, may to october 1893, held to mark four hundred years since columbus. a fake white city of enormous plaster buildings on the lakefront, the first ferris wheel, electric light everywhere, twenty seven million visitors.\nthe parliament of the world's religions was one of the congresses held alongside it, which is how a wandering monk from calcutta ended up giving the most famous speech of the fair.","links":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World%27s_Columbian_Exposition","why":"the plain overview"}],"name":"the 1893 world's fair","status":"approved"}]};
